window.GIQ={"meta":{"lede":"The clicker, interactive and TA-review questions made for the GI anatomy lectures (2019–2026), in learning order: the basic plan first, then each organ's cells and slides, then the diseases that build on them. Before every few questions, a short teaching paragraph explains what you need to work them out. Similar questions sit together in a shaded box. Tap an answer to see why it is right, why the others are wrong, and the slides that teach it."},"lectures":[{"id":"ANAT-G1","t":"GI development (embryology)"},{"id":"ANAT-G2","t":"Anterior abdominal wall, inguinal canal, spermatic cord, scrotum & testes"},{"id":"ANAT-G3","t":"Peritoneum, esophagus, stomach & celiac trunk"},{"id":"ANAT-G4","t":"Small & large intestines, SMA & IMA"},{"id":"ANAT-G5","t":"Liver, biliary tree, gallbladder, portal vein, pancreas & spleen"},{"id":"ANAT-G6","t":"Autonomic nervous system"},{"id":"ANAT-G7","t":"Radiology of thorax & abdomen"}],"groups":[{"l":"ANAT-G1","u":0,"label":"Head fold encloses the foregut","slides":[{"src":"sl/G002-2.jpg","cap":"Primordial gut: foregut at head end, hindgut at tail, midgut open to yolk sac","where":"2026 Middletown clicker, slide 2"},{"src":"sl/D045-126.jpg","cap":"Early gut tube: foregut, midgut, hindgut with their arteries and derivatives","where":"2025 Montana TA review, slide 126"}],"q":[{"id":"D042-37-1","s":"A 6-week-old embryo has abnormal development of the head fold during sagittal folding. Disruption of this process primarily interferes with formation of which structure?","o":["A. Appendix","B. Cloaca","C. Foregut","D. Mesentery proper","E. Distal duodenum"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Foregut","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Foregut.</b> In head-to-tail (sagittal) folding, the head fold tucks the front end of the endoderm under the embryo and closes it into the foregut. The tail fold makes the hindgut; the middle stays open to the yolk sac as the midgut.<br>A. Appendix: grows off the cecum, a midgut part.<br>B. Cloaca: end of the hindgut, made by the tail fold.<br>D. Mesentery proper: dorsal mesentery of the midgut small intestine.<br>E. Distal duodenum: past the bile duct opening, so midgut.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 3 (Trilaminar Germ Disc)","GI Development slide 9 (Foregut)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI module TA review","sl":37,"f":"D042"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G1","u":0,"label":"Lateral folding closes the gut tube","slides":[{"src":"sl/G001-4.jpg","cap":"Lateral folding A to C: gut closes, yolk sac link narrows","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 4"},{"src":"sl/D045-122.jpg","cap":"Lateral folding: gut tube enclosed, joined to yolk sac by vitelline duct","where":"2025 Montana TA review, slide 122"}],"q":[{"id":"D045-120-1","s":"A 4-week-old embryo is observed during a developmental study. Lateral folding brings the endodermal layer toward the midline, enclosing part of the yolk sac. Which structure results from this process?","o":["A. The gut tube enclosed within the body cavity","B. The notochordal canal","C. The neural tube","D. The allantois"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"The gut tube enclosed within the body cavity","a":"A","why":"<b>A. The gut tube, enclosed in the body cavity.</b> Lateral (side-to-side) folding swings both edges of the disc down and together, so the endoderm rolls into a closed gut tube inside the new body cavity. It stays linked to the yolk sac only by the thin vitelline duct.<br>B. Notochordal canal: forms from the primitive node, not by folding.<br>C. Neural tube: ectoderm folding on the back (neurulation).<br>D. Allantois: small outpouch of the yolk sac into the connecting stalk.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 4 (Lateral Folding Review)","GI Development slide 5 (Lateral Folding)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":120,"f":"D045"}]},{"id":"D041-73-1","s":"A 4-week embryo fails to undergo normal lateral (transverse) folding. Which of the following abnormalities is most likely to result?","o":["A. Failure of the yolk sac to pinch off, leaving the midgut open to the exterior","B. Absence of a foregut segment","C. Malrotation of the midgut","D. Premature obliteration of the vitelline duct","E. Early formation of the cloacal membrane"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Failure of the yolk sac to pinch off, leaving the midgut open to the exterior","a":"A","why":"<b>A. The yolk sac does not pinch off; the midgut stays open.</b> Lateral folding brings the two body-wall edges together and narrows the gut's wide opening into the yolk sac down to the thin vitelline duct. If it fails, the front body wall and the midgut stay open.<br>B. No foregut: a head-fold problem.<br>C. Midgut malrotation: happens weeks later, during herniation.<br>D. Early closure of the vitelline duct: the opposite of what happens.<br>E. Cloacal membrane: a tail-end structure, not affected.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 4 (Lateral Folding Review)","GI Development slide 5 (Lateral Folding)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":73,"f":"D041"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G1","u":1,"label":"Dorsal mesentery: double layer anchoring gut, carrying vessels","slides":[{"src":"sl/G001-5.jpg","cap":"Cross-section: gut hung by dorsal mesentery of visceral lateral plate mesoderm","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 5"},{"src":"sl/G001-7.jpg","cap":"Aortic branches running through the dorsal mesentery to the gut","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 7"},{"src":"sl/D042-48.jpg","cap":"Table: dorsal mesentery has two layers and carries arteries","where":"2025 TA review, slide 48"}],"q":[{"id":"F1252-123-1","s":"In the abdominal region, the gut tube is suspended within the intraembryonic cavity by what structure?","o":["A. Ventral mesentery","B. Dorsal mesentery","C. Somatic mesoderm","D. Paraxial mesoderm","E. Yolk sac"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Dorsal mesentery","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Dorsal mesentery.</b> The whole gut tube, from lower esophagus to rectum, hangs from the back body wall by the dorsal mesentery, a double layer of peritoneum.<br>A. Ventral mesentery: only along the foregut (stomach, top of duodenum, liver).<br>C. Somatic mesoderm: lines the body wall, not the gut.<br>D. Paraxial mesoderm: forms the somites (bone, muscle, skin).<br>E. Yolk sac: joined to the midgut by the vitelline duct; it holds nothing up.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 6 (Mesenteries)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":123,"f":"F1252"}]},{"id":"D042-41-1","s":"During formation of the primitive gut tube, the embryo undergoes lateral folding, which brings the splanchnic lateral plate mesoderm into close association with the endoderm. Which of the following correctly describes the structure that results from this relationship?","o":["A. A single-layered peritoneal membrane anchoring the gut tube to the posterior abdominal wall","B. The dorsal mesentery, which serves as a pathway for blood vessels between the aorta and gut","C. The ventral mesentery, which derives from somatic mesoderm and forms the falciform ligament","D. The greater omentum, formed by folding of the ventral mesentery on itself","E. The epithelial lining of the small intestine, formed by splanchnic mesoderm proliferation"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"The dorsal mesentery, which serves as a pathway for blood vessels between the aorta and gut","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Dorsal mesentery, a pathway for vessels.</b> Lateral folding wraps splanchnic (visceral) lateral plate mesoderm around the endoderm. At the back the two sides meet as the dorsal mesentery: two layers of peritoneum that anchor the gut and carry aortic branches to it.<br>A. Single layer: no, a mesentery is a double layer.<br>C. Ventral mesentery: comes from the septum transversum, not somatic mesoderm.<br>D. Greater omentum: folded dorsal (not ventral) mesentery.<br>E. Gut lining (epithelium): endoderm, not mesoderm.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 6 (Mesenteries)","GI Development slide 5 (Lateral Folding)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI module TA review","sl":41,"f":"D042"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G1","u":1,"label":"Ventral mesentery exists only along the foregut","slides":[{"src":"sl/G001-6.jpg","cap":"Mesenteries: dorsal along whole gut, ventral only at the foregut","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 6"},{"src":"sl/D042-48.jpg","cap":"Table: ventral mesentery in foregut only; dorsal in all three parts","where":"2025 TA review, slide 48"}],"q":[{"id":"D042-71-1","s":"During development, the gut tube is suspended by mesenteries. Which of the following correctly describes their distribution?","o":["A. The foregut is suspended by dorsal mesentery only","B. The ventral mesentery is present throughout the entire gut","C. The ventral mesentery exists only in the foregut region","D. The midgut and hindgut are suspended by both ventral and dorsal mesenteries"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"The ventral mesentery exists only in the foregut region","a":"C","why":"<b>C. The ventral mesentery exists only around the foregut.</b> The dorsal mesentery runs the full length of the gut. A ventral mesentery forms only along the foregut (stomach, top of duodenum, liver) and later becomes the lesser omentum and falciform ligament.<br>A. Foregut with dorsal only: no, the foregut has both.<br>B. Ventral along the whole gut: no, foregut only.<br>D. Midgut and hindgut with both: no, they have only a dorsal mesentery.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 6 (Mesenteries)","GI Development slide 19 (Ventral Mesentery)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI module TA review","sl":71,"f":"D042"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G1","u":1,"label":"Dorsal mesentery parts: mesogastrium to mesocolon","slides":[{"src":"sl/G001-6.jpg","cap":"Dorsal mesentery parts: mesogastrium, mesoduodenum, mesentery proper, mesocolon","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 6"},{"src":"sl/D045-126.jpg","cap":"Dorsal mesentery parts listed beside a labelled early gut tube","where":"2025 Montana TA review, slide 126"}],"q":[{"id":"D045-124-1","s":"A researcher identifies a double layer of peritoneum that attaches the developing gut to the posterior body wall. Which of the following correctly describes the structure and its adult derivatives?","o":["A. Ventral mesentery → mesoduodenum and mesocolon","B. Dorsal mesentery → lesser omentum and falciform ligament","C. Ventral mesentery → mesogastrium and mesocolon","D. Dorsal mesentery → mesogastrium, mesoduodenum, mesentery proper, mesocolon"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Dorsal mesentery → mesogastrium, mesoduodenum, mesentery proper, mesocolon","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Dorsal mesentery: mesogastrium, mesoduodenum, mesentery proper, mesocolon.</b> The dorsal mesentery ties the gut to the back wall and is named for the organ it holds: stomach (dorsal mesogastrium), duodenum (mesoduodenum), small intestine (mesentery proper), colon (mesocolon).<br>A and C. Ventral mesentery: attaches to the front wall and holds only the foregut.<br>B. Lesser omentum and falciform ligament: these come from the ventral mesentery, not the dorsal.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 6 (Mesenteries)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":124,"f":"D045"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G1","u":2,"label":"Intraperitoneal = still hangs on a mesentery","slides":[{"src":"sl/G001-8.jpg","cap":"Cross-section of gut hanging on dorsal mesentery; retroperitoneal definitions","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 8"},{"src":"sl/G004-13.jpg","cap":"Fist in balloon: intraperitoneal organ wrapped by visceral peritoneum","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 13"},{"src":"sl/D042-51.jpg","cap":"Table: intraperitoneal, primary, secondary retroperitoneal and subperitoneal organs","where":"2025 TA review, slide 51"}],"q":[{"id":"D041-80-1","s":"An organ is found to be suspended from the posterior body wall by a mesentery throughout development. In adulthood, it is completely surrounded by visceral peritoneum. This organ is best classified as:","o":["A. Primarily retroperitoneal","B. Secondarily retroperitoneal","C. Intraperitoneal","D. Extraperitoneal","E. Subperitoneal (infraperitoneal)"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Intraperitoneal","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Intraperitoneal.</b> An organ that keeps its mesentery and hangs into the peritoneal cavity is wrapped almost fully in visceral peritoneum (stomach, jejunum, ileum).<br>A. Primarily retroperitoneal: never had a mesentery (kidneys, ureters).<br>B. Secondarily retroperitoneal: had a mesentery, then lost it against the back wall.<br>D. Extraperitoneal: lies outside the peritoneal sac.<br>E. Subperitoneal: below the peritoneum, covered only on top (bladder).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 8 (Retroperitoneal Organs)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 13 (Peritoneal organs)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":80,"f":"D041"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G1","u":2,"label":"Secondary retroperitoneal: duodenum, pancreas, ascending/descending colon","slides":[{"src":"sl/G001-8.jpg","cap":"Secondary retroperitoneal: duodenum parts 2-4, pancreas except tail, ascending/descending colon","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 8"},{"src":"sl/G001-26.jpg","cap":"Ascending and descending colon mesenteries fuse to the posterior wall","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 26"},{"src":"sl/D042-51.jpg","cap":"Table sorting organs into intra-, primary and secondary retroperitoneal","where":"2025 TA review, slide 51"}],"q":[{"id":"F1228-282-1","s":"Which of the following organs are Secondary Retroperitoneal?","o":["A. Kidneys","B. Liver","C. Tail of pancreas","D. Ureters","E. Ascending colon"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Ascending colon","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Ascending colon.</b> Secondary retroperitoneal organs start with a mesentery, then fuse to the back wall as the gut rotates: duodenum (second to fourth parts), pancreas except the tail, ascending and descending colon.<br>A. Kidneys and D. Ureters: primarily retroperitoneal; they never had a mesentery.<br>B. Liver: intraperitoneal.<br>C. Tail of pancreas: stays intraperitoneal, in the splenorenal ligament.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 8 (Retroperitoneal Organs)"],"src":[{"t":"2019 review","n":"old Exam 2 review: abdomen part only","sl":282,"f":"F1228"},{"t":"? tutor review","n":"old Exam 2: abdomen part only","sl":178,"f":"F1243"}]},{"id":"F3903-269-1","s":"Which of the following organs is secondary retroperitoneal?","o":["A. Kidney","B. Tail of the pancreas","C. Appendix","D. Ascending colon","E. Spleen"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Ascending colon","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Ascending colon.</b> It starts on a mesentery; once the midgut returns and rotates, that mesentery fuses to the back wall, so it is secondary retroperitoneal. Same group: duodenum parts two to four, pancreas except the tail, descending colon.<br>A. Kidney: primarily retroperitoneal.<br>B. Tail of pancreas: intraperitoneal, in the splenorenal ligament.<br>C. Appendix: keeps its mesentery (mesoappendix).<br>E. Spleen: intraperitoneal.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 8 (Retroperitoneal Organs)","GI Development slide 26 (Mesenteries)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":269,"f":"F3903"}]},{"id":"D045-128-1","s":"A 10-week-old embryo shows the duodenum fused to the posterior abdominal wall. This organ is now classified as:","o":["A. Intraperitoneal","B. Primary Retroperitoneal","C. Secondary Retroperitoneal","D. Extraperitoneal"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Secondary Retroperitoneal","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Secondary retroperitoneal.</b> The duodenum starts with a mesentery (mesoduodenum). Stomach rotation swings it right, and it fuses to the back wall, losing the peritoneum behind it. Had a mesentery, then lost it = secondary.<br>A. Intraperitoneal: it would still hang on a mesentery.<br>B. Primary retroperitoneal: never had a mesentery (kidneys, ureters).<br>D. Extraperitoneal: a general word for lying outside the peritoneal sac; it does not describe this history.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 8 (Retroperitoneal Organs)","GI Development slide 26 (Mesenteries)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":128,"f":"D045"}],"t":1},{"id":"D042-49-1","s":"A surgeon is performing an abdominal procedure and notes that a particular organ is only partially covered by peritoneum on its anterior surface and lies directly against the posterior abdominal wall. Based on this anatomical relationship, which of the following organs is MOST likely being examined?","o":["A. Stomach","B. Jejunum","C. Spleen","D. Pancreas (excluding the tail)","E. Transverse colon"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Pancreas (excluding the tail)","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Pancreas (not the tail).</b> Peritoneum only on the front surface, with the back lying flat on the posterior wall, means retroperitoneal. The pancreas, except its tail, is secondary retroperitoneal.<br>A. Stomach, B. Jejunum, C. Spleen and E. Transverse colon: all intraperitoneal. Each hangs on a mesentery or ligament and is covered nearly all the way around.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 8 (Retroperitoneal Organs)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 13 (Peritoneal organs)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI module TA review","sl":49,"f":"D042"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3905-43-1","s":"23 year old handsome and jacked male lacrosse player comes into your office complaining of pain in the upper left quadrant. He states that he was hit with a lacrosse ball in that area earlier today. Thinking he is a sissy you palpate his abdomen and he screams in pain. Which of the following is true about the pancreas?","o":["A. The entire pancreas is retroperitoneal","B. All parts, except the tail of the pancreas are retroperitoneal","C. The entire pancreas is secondary retroperitoneal","D. All parts, except the tail of the pancreas are secondary retroperitoneal","E. It is a part of the midgut"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"All parts, except the tail of the pancreas are secondary retroperitoneal","a":"D","why":"<b>D. All of the pancreas except the tail is secondary retroperitoneal.</b> The pancreas forms inside the dorsal mesentery; then the head, neck and body fuse to the back wall. The tail stays in the splenorenal ligament, so it is intraperitoneal.<br>A. Entire pancreas retroperitoneal: the tail is not.<br>B. Plain 'retroperitoneal' in the lecture means it never had a mesentery; the pancreas had one.<br>C. Entire pancreas secondary: the tail is not.<br>E. Midgut: the pancreas is a foregut organ.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 8 (Retroperitoneal Organs)","Pancreas & Spleen slide 4 (Pancreas)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA weekly","n":"09-22-2022 TA session (abdominal wall, inguinal)","sl":43,"f":"F3905"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G1","u":3,"label":"Pylorus and first duodenal part come from foregut","slides":[{"src":"sl/G001-9.jpg","cap":"Foregut list: stomach and upper (first) part of the duodenum","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 9"},{"src":"sl/G001-16.jpg","cap":"Duodenum drawing: foregut-midgut junction at the bile duct opening","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 16"},{"src":"sl/D045-126.jpg","cap":"Foregut derivatives include stomach and proximal duodenum","where":"2025 Montana TA review, slide 126"}],"q":[{"id":"F1156-111-1","s":"Which of the following will give rise to the Upper portion of the duodenum (1st part)?","o":["A. Foregut","B. Midgut","C. Hindgut"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Foregut","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Foregut.</b> The foregut makes the first (upper) part of the duodenum. The midgut takes over just past the opening of the bile duct.<br>B. Midgut: duodenum past the bile duct opening down to two-thirds of the transverse colon.<br>C. Hindgut: last third of the transverse colon to the upper anal canal.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 9 (Foregut)","GI Development slide 16 (Duodenum and Recanalization)"],"src":[{"t":"? TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":111,"f":"F1156"}]},{"id":"F3900-188-1","s":"A 7-year-old child presents with chronic abdominal pain and is scheduled for an upper gastrointestinal endoscopy. During the procedure, the gastroenterologist notes inflammation in the superior duodenal mucosa. Which of the following embryonic gut tube portions is the primarily responsible for the development of the inflamed duodenal regain?","o":["A. Foregut","B. Midgut","C. Hindgut","D. Allantois"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Foregut","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Foregut.</b> The superior (first) part of the duodenum comes from the foregut and is fed by the celiac trunk. The duodenum past the bile duct opening comes from the midgut.<br>B. Midgut: lower duodenum to two-thirds of the transverse colon.<br>C. Hindgut: last third of the transverse colon to the upper anal canal.<br>D. Allantois: small outpouch toward the umbilical cord; it becomes the urachus.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 9 (Foregut)","GI Development slide 16 (Duodenum and Recanalization)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":188,"f":"F3900"}]},{"id":"D041-85-1","s":"A 3-week-old infant has persistent nonbilious vomiting. Imaging reveals an anatomic obstruction at the level of the pylorus. The affected region arises embryologically from which portion of the gut tube?","o":["A. Midgut","B. Foregut","C. Hindgut","D. Vitelline duct remnant","E. Cloacal membrane"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Foregut","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Foregut.</b> The pylorus is the outlet of the stomach, and the stomach comes from the foregut. Vomit without bile also puts the block above the bile duct opening.<br>A. Midgut: duodenum past the bile duct opening onward.<br>C. Hindgut: distal colon and rectum.<br>D. Vitelline duct remnant: Meckel diverticulum on the ileum.<br>E. Cloacal membrane: covers the future anus.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 9 (Foregut)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":85,"f":"D041"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G1","u":3,"label":"Duodenum: foregut-midgut and celiac-superior mesenteric junction","slides":[{"src":"sl/G001-16.jpg","cap":"Duodenum: transition foregut to midgut and celiac trunk to superior mesenteric","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 16"},{"src":"sl/G001-7.jpg","cap":"Gut arteries: celiac trunk, superior and inferior mesenteric to each region","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 7"}],"q":[{"id":"D042-84-1","s":"You are shadowing in the NICU and the physician that you are following decides to test your embryology. They ask “between what two points does the duodenum serve as a transition” ?","o":["A. Foregut and hindgut","B. Foregut and midgut","C. Midgut and hindgut","D. Celiac trunk and SMA"],"g":["B","D"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Foregut and midgut","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Foregut and midgut, and D. Celiac trunk and SMA (superior mesenteric artery).</b> The lecture lists both: the duodenum is where foregut changes to midgut, and where celiac trunk supply changes to superior mesenteric supply, just past the bile duct opening.<br>A. Foregut and hindgut: the midgut lies between them.<br>C. Midgut and hindgut: that change happens in the transverse colon, at its last third.","ex":"","fl":["The source keyed only B, but the 2026 lecture (GI Development slide 16) says the duodenum is the transition point both between foregut and midgut and between the celiac trunk and superior mesenteric artery, so D is also correct."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 16 (Duodenum and Recanalization)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI module TA review","sl":84,"f":"D042"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G1","u":4,"label":"Foregut organs: esophagus, stomach, liver, gallbladder, pancreas","slides":[{"src":"sl/G001-9.jpg","cap":"Foregut list: esophagus, stomach, liver, gallbladder, pancreas, first duodenum","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 9"},{"src":"sl/D045-126.jpg","cap":"Foregut, midgut and hindgut derivatives beside a labelled embryo","where":"2025 Montana TA review, slide 126"},{"src":"sl/G001-22.jpg","cap":"Midgut list: distal duodenum to two-thirds of transverse colon","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 22"}],"q":[{"id":"F1228-285-1","s":"Which of the following does the foregut NOT give rise to?","o":["A. Pancreas","B. Esophagus","C. Bile duct","D. Jejunum","E. Stomach"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Jejunum","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Jejunum.</b> The jejunum comes from the midgut. The foregut makes the esophagus, stomach, first part of the duodenum, liver, gallbladder and bile duct, and pancreas.<br>A. Pancreas, B. Esophagus, C. Bile duct and E. Stomach: all foregut.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 9 (Foregut)"],"src":[{"t":"2019 review","n":"old Exam 2 review: abdomen part only","sl":285,"f":"F1228"},{"t":"? tutor review","n":"old Exam 2: abdomen part only","sl":181,"f":"F1243"}]},{"id":"D045-113-1","s":"Failure of differentiation of the individual organs derived from the embryonic foregut would cause malformation of which of the following organs?","o":["A. Spleen","B. Gallbladder","C. Ascending colon","D. Ileum","E. Descending colon"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Gallbladder","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Gallbladder.</b> It forms from the hepatic diverticulum, a bud off the foregut. Foregut organs: esophagus, stomach, first part of duodenum, liver, gallbladder, bile ducts, pancreas.<br>A. Spleen: mesoderm in the dorsal mesogastrium, not gut tube.<br>C. Ascending colon: midgut.<br>D. Ileum: midgut.<br>E. Descending colon: hindgut.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 9 (Foregut)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":113,"f":"D045"},{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":230,"f":"F1178"},{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":291,"f":"F1180"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3903-261-1","s":"A new mother is worried about her son’s health. At 3-weeks-old he still has not gained much weight. A set of exams reveal that his stomach is underdeveloped. Which of the following organs would you expect to also be underdeveloped if the underlying problem was the improper development of the neonate’s foregut?","o":["A. Spleen","B. Jejunum","C. Descending colon","D. Gallbladder","E. Anus"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Gallbladder","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Gallbladder.</b> The stomach and gallbladder both come from the foregut; the gallbladder buds off it as part of the hepatic diverticulum.<br>A. Spleen: mesoderm in the dorsal mesogastrium, not a gut-tube organ.<br>B. Jejunum: midgut.<br>C. Descending colon: hindgut.<br>E. Anus: upper anal canal is hindgut; lower part is ectoderm.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 9 (Foregut)","GI Development slide 15 (Spleen)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":261,"f":"F3903"}]},{"id":"D045-132-1","s":"A newborn presents with bile-stained vomiting due to duodenal atresia. Which of the following organs shares the same embryologic origin as the affected structure?","o":["A. Jejunum","B. Cecum","C. Descending Colon","D. Pancreas"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Pancreas","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Pancreas.</b> Bile in the vomit puts the atresia just past the bile duct opening, the same stretch of duodenum where both pancreatic buds sprout, at the end of the foregut. So the pancreas grows out of the same piece of gut.<br>A. Jejunum and B. Cecum: midgut loop, beyond the duodenum.<br>C. Descending colon: hindgut.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 9 (Foregut)","GI Development slide 17 (Duodenal Stenosis and Atresia)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":132,"f":"D045"}],"t":1},{"id":"D042-52-1","s":"A 57-year-old man presents with severe epigastric pain that radiates to the back. Imaging reveals a perforated duodenal ulcer located just distal to the site where the common bile duct empties into the gastrointestinal tract. Because this ulcer lies within a region derived from the same embryological division of the gut that gives rise to the liver and pancreas, which of the following additional structures is also derived from that same embryologic region?","o":["A. Descending colon","B. Jejunum","C. Spleen","D. Upper esophagus","E. Appendix"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Upper esophagus","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Upper esophagus.</b> The stem says the ulcer sits in the gut division that makes the liver and pancreas: the foregut. The esophagus is foregut too. (By the lecture, duodenum just past the bile duct opening is midgut, so read the ulcer as just before that opening.)<br>A. Descending colon: hindgut.<br>B. Jejunum: midgut.<br>C. Spleen: mesoderm in the dorsal mesogastrium; fed by the celiac trunk but not from the gut tube.<br>E. Appendix: midgut.","ex":"","fl":["Key D fits the stem's stated division (the one that forms liver and pancreas = foregut). But the location given ('just distal to' the bile-duct opening) is midgut territory (G001:22: midgut = duodenum distal to the opening of the bile duct); the TA's own note says 'proximal to the major papilla'. Read it as proximal to the papilla."],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 9 (Foregut)","GI Development slide 22 (Midgut)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI module TA review","sl":52,"f":"D042"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G1","u":5,"label":"Blocked fetal gut: can't swallow, polyhydramnios","slides":[{"src":"sl/G001-11.jpg","cap":"Fetus swallows amniotic fluid; esophageal or duodenal block gives polyhydramnios","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 11"},{"src":"sl/F3900-247.jpg","cap":"Polyhydramnios: impaired swallowing or more fetal urine; gut causes table","where":"2023 TA review, slide 247"}],"q":[{"id":"F3900-245-1","s":"What best describes polyhydramnios?","o":["A. A condition characterized by a deficiency of amniotic fluid, commonly resulting from fetal gastrointestinal tract malformations.","B. An abnormality in the placental attachment, leading to increased amniotic fluid levels.","C. A condition that often results from impaired fetal swallowing or increased fetal urine production.","D. A condition that occurs due to a deficiency of fetal surfactant, resulting in improper regulation of amniotic fluid levels.","E. A condition in which maternal hormonal imbalances affect amniotic fluid production."],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"A condition that often results from impaired fetal swallowing or increased fetal urine production.","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Impaired fetal swallowing or more fetal urine.</b> Fetal urine adds amniotic fluid and fetal swallowing removes it. Less swallowing (esophageal or duodenal block) or more urine (for example, maternal diabetes) raises the volume.<br>A. Deficiency of fluid: that is oligohydramnios.<br>B. Placental attachment problem: not a cause.<br>D. Surfactant deficiency: premature lungs (respiratory distress syndrome), unrelated to fluid volume.<br>E. Maternal hormone imbalance: not the mechanism.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 11 (Polyhydramniose)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":245,"f":"F3900"}]},{"id":"D042-65-1","s":"A fetus is found to have polyhydramnios on prenatal ultrasound. Which GI anomaly is the most likely cause?","o":["A. Imperforate anus","B. Esophageal atresia","C. Hirschsprung disease","D. Umbilical hernia"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Esophageal atresia","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Esophageal atresia.</b> The fetus normally swallows amniotic fluid. A closed esophagus stops swallowing, so fluid builds up (polyhydramnios).<br>A. Imperforate anus: swallowed fluid is absorbed in the upper intestine long before the anus, so volume stays normal.<br>C. Hirschsprung disease: missing nerve cells in the distal colon; swallowing and absorption are normal.<br>D. Umbilical hernia: a body-wall weakness; no gut block.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 11 (Polyhydramniose)","GI Development slide 10 (Esophagus)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI module TA review","sl":65,"f":"D042"}],"t":1},{"id":"D045-155-1","s":"A pregnant patient’s ultrasound shows excessive amniotic fluid. The fetus is found to have esophageal atresia. What explains this finding?","o":["A. Blockage of maternal lymphatic drainage","B. Over-secretion of amniotic fluid by the amnion","C. Increased fetal urine production","D. Failure of the esophageal recanalization"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Failure of the esophageal recanalization","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Failure of esophageal recanalization.</b> The esophagus fills with cells (weeks five to eight), then reopens by cell death. If it stays closed, the fetus cannot swallow amniotic fluid, so fluid builds up.<br>A. Maternal lymphatic blockage: unrelated to amniotic fluid.<br>B. Over-secretion by the amnion: not the cause here.<br>C. More fetal urine: does raise amniotic fluid (for example, maternal diabetes), but it does not explain esophageal atresia.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 11 (Polyhydramniose)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":155,"f":"D045"}],"t":1},{"id":"D041-109-1","s":"A 20-week fetus is noted on ultrasound to have increased amniotic fluid. The obstetrician suspects polyhydramnios. A follow-up ultrasound shows a blind-ending, fluid-filled upper esophageal pouch. Failure of which embryological process most likely led to this condition?","o":["A. Recanalization of the esophagus","B. Failure of neural crest cell migration","C. Rotation of the stomach","D. Proliferation of mesenchyme in the dorsal mesogastrium","E. Closure of the pleuroperitoneal canals"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Recanalization of the esophagus","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Recanalization of the esophagus.</b> A blind, fluid-filled upper pouch is esophageal atresia. The esophagus becomes a solid rod and must reopen by about week eight; if not, the fetus can't swallow and amniotic fluid builds up.<br>B. Neural crest migration: Hirschsprung disease.<br>C. Stomach rotation: sets organ positions.<br>D. Dorsal mesogastrium mesenchyme: forms the spleen.<br>E. Pleuroperitoneal canals: diaphragmatic hernia.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 11 (Polyhydramniose)","GI Development slide 10 (Esophagus)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":109,"f":"D041"}]},{"id":"F1156-118-1","s":"A 30 year old pregnant woman with no significant past medical history comes to your clinic during her 25th week of gestation with shortness of breath, heartburn, and the feeling of tightness in her abdomen. You perform an ultrasound and find that there is excess amniotic fluid present. Referral to OBGYN is made. Which of the following is the most likely cause of the excess amniotic fluid?","o":["A. Failure of the pleuroperitoneal folds to form","B. There was an embryologic issue with the 5th aortic arch","C. The gut tube did not recalalize appropriately","D. Idiopathic","E. Improper closure of the anterior abdominal wall"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"The gut tube did not recalalize appropriately","a":"C","why":"<b>C. The gut tube did not recanalize.</b> A blocked esophagus or duodenum stops the fetus from swallowing and absorbing amniotic fluid, so it builds up. This is the developmental cause the lecture teaches.<br>A. Pleuroperitoneal folds fail: diaphragmatic hernia; its main harm is small lungs.<br>B. Fifth aortic arch: it normally disappears; no link to fluid.<br>D. Idiopathic: many mild real cases have no found cause, but the question asks for the embryologic one.<br>E. Open abdominal wall: gastroschisis or omphalocele, not the classic cause.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 11 (Polyhydramniose)","GI Development slide 10 (Esophagus)"],"src":[{"t":"? TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":118,"f":"F1156"}]},{"id":"D042-82-1","s":"You are reviewing GI development for the upcoming exam. You are trying to remember the unique process of cell proliferation and recanalization of the duodenum. What clinical correlate can occur if partial recanalization?","o":["A. Atresia","B. Polyhydramnios","C. Fistula","D. Nothing happens it will fix itself"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Polyhydramnios","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Polyhydramnios.</b> Partial recanalization leaves a narrow duodenum (stenosis). Swallowed amniotic fluid can't pass easily to the intestine to be absorbed, so fluid builds up.<br>A. Atresia: comes from no recanalization at all, not partial.<br>C. Fistula: an abnormal channel between two organs (such as trachea and esophagus), not a recanalization defect.<br>D. Fixes itself: a narrowed duodenum stays narrow and may need surgery.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 17 (Duodenal Stenosis and Atresia)","GI Development slide 11 (Polyhydramniose)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI module TA review","sl":82,"f":"D042"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G1","u":5,"label":"No kidneys, no urine: oligohydramnios","slides":[{"src":"sl/G001-11.jpg","cap":"Fetus swallows amniotic fluid, so fluid volume depends on the fetus","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 11"},{"src":"sl/F3900-247.jpg","cap":"Amniotic volume: swallowing removes fluid, fetal urine adds it","where":"2023 TA review, slide 247"}],"q":[{"id":"F1178-225-1","s":"A 20 year old woman delivers a stillborn infant with bilateral agenesis of the kidneys. During the later stages of pregnancy, the fetus likely also had which of the following conditions?","o":["A. Polyhydramnios","B. Oligohydramnios","C. Renal hypoplasia","D. Pelvic kidneys","E. Polycystic kidneys"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Oligohydramnios","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Oligohydramnios (too little amniotic fluid).</b> In later pregnancy most amniotic fluid is fetal urine. No kidneys means no urine, so fluid is very low (Potter sequence: flat face, limb defects, small lungs).<br>A. Polyhydramnios: too much fluid; from poor swallowing, not absent kidneys.<br>C. Renal hypoplasia, D. Pelvic kidneys and E. Polycystic kidneys: all need kidneys to exist; agenesis means none formed.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 11 (Polyhydramniose)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":225,"f":"F1178"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G1","u":6,"label":"Newborn drooling and choking at feeds: esophageal atresia","slides":[{"src":"sl/F3900-239.jpg","cap":"Table of esophageal atresia and fistula types with their symptoms","where":"2023 TA review, slide 239"},{"src":"sl/G001-10.jpg","cap":"Esophagus plugs then recanalizes; failure gives atresia or stenosis","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 10"}],"q":[{"id":"F3900-235-1","s":"A 1 week old baby in admitted to the NICU for excessive drooling despite suctioning, vomiting after feeding, and abdominal distention. What could be the cause of these symptoms?","o":["A. Tracheoesophageal fistula","B. Respiratory distress syndrome","C. Diaphragmatic hernia","D. Polyhydramnios"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Tracheoesophageal fistula","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Tracheoesophageal fistula.</b> Drooling (saliva can't pass a closed esophagus), vomiting with feeds and a swollen belly (air enters the stomach through a fistula from the trachea) fit esophageal atresia with a fistula to the lower esophagus.<br>B. Respiratory distress syndrome: premature lungs lacking surfactant; breathing trouble, no drooling.<br>C. Diaphragmatic hernia: breathing trouble with a sunken (scaphoid) belly.<br>D. Polyhydramnios: excess amniotic fluid in pregnancy; a clue to atresia, not the baby's diagnosis.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 10 (Esophagus)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":235,"f":"F3900"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3901-58-1","s":"You are called to the bedside of a new mother who’s upset because her newborn daughter isn’t feeding. You notice the baby is faintly blue, and when she starts to feed she immediately starts choking and coughing. On review of the chart, you see the mother suffered from polyhydramnios during her pregnancy. What is true about the newborn’s condition?","o":["A. The condition is caused by incomplete canalization of the duodenum","B. The cause of the condition is due to an anterior deviation of the tracheoesophageal septum","C. The condition is frequently associated with another fetal GI abnormality","D. The condition is often due to inability of the esophagus to recanalize correctly during the 11th week of development"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"The condition is frequently associated with another fetal GI abnormality","a":"C","why":"<b>C. It often comes with another anomaly.</b> Choking and blue spells with each feed plus polyhydramnios mean esophageal atresia. It usually comes with a tracheoesophageal fistula (about eighty-five percent) and often with other defects such as anal or duodenal atresia.<br>A. Incomplete duodenal canalization: duodenal atresia; bilious vomiting, not choking.<br>B. Anterior deviation: wrong; the tracheoesophageal septum deviates posteriorly.<br>D. Week eleven: wrong timing; the esophagus recanalizes by about week eight.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 10 (Esophagus)","GI Development slide 11 (Polyhydramniose)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":58,"f":"F3901"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G1","u":6,"label":"Air in stomach? Tells the fistula type","slides":[{"src":"sl/F3900-239.jpg","cap":"Atresia and fistula types: which ones put air in the stomach","where":"2023 TA review, slide 239"},{"src":"sl/G001-10.jpg","cap":"Esophageal occlusion and recanalization, weeks five to nine","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 10"}],"q":[{"id":"F3900-237-1","s":"Which of the following developmental malformations can result in air in the stomach? (may be more than one correct answer)","o":["A. Esophageal atresia and tracheoesophageal fistula at distal end","B. Esophageal atresias at the proximal and distal end","C. H-type tracheoesophageal fistula","D. Esophageal atresia and tracheoesophageal fistula at proximal end","E. Tracheoesophageal fistulas at the proximal and distal ends"],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"A, C, E: Esophageal atresia and tracheoesophageal fistula at distal end; H-type tracheoesophageal fistula; Tracheoesophageal fistulas at the proximal and distal ends","a":null,"why":"<b>A, C and E.</b> Air reaches the stomach only if the trachea connects to the lower esophagus. A (fistula at the distal end), C (H-type fistula with an open esophagus) and E (fistulas at both ends) all have that link.<br>B. Atresia at both ends, no fistula: the lower esophagus is sealed, so the stomach has no air.<br>D. Fistula only from the upper pouch: the lower esophagus ends blind, so no air reaches the stomach.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 10 (Esophagus)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":237,"f":"F3900"}]},{"id":"F1156-127-1","s":"You examine a newborn patient who has excess saliva in the mouth, gagging and cyanosis after feeding, and reflux of stomach contents into lungs. You scope the patient and find esophageal atresia that lacks direct connection to the trachea(refer to the picture). What type of TEF is present?","o":["A. A","B. B","C. C","D. D","E. Elongation factor 2","F. A guy who makes memes"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"A","a":"A","why":"<b>A.</b> In diagram A the upper esophagus is a blind pouch (the arrow curls back up, so feeds return) and the lower esophagus opens off the trachea (two-way arrow), so stomach acid reaches the lungs: atresia with a distal fistula.<br>B. Long open esophagus with a side channel to the trachea: H-type fistula, no atresia.<br>C. Upper esophagus opens into the trachea; the lower piece is a separate blind stump.<br>D. Both upper and lower esophagus open into the trachea.<br>E and F. Joke options.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F1156-127.jpg","in":"Four TEF diagrams A-D (labels blanked): A = blind upper esophageal pouch with the lower esophagus joined to the trachea (atresia with distal fistula); B = H-type fistula without atresia; C = atresia without fistula; D = upper pouch joined to trachea","tg":["GI Development slide 10 (Esophagus)"],"src":[{"t":"? TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":127,"f":"F1156"}],"t":1},{"id":"F0003-11-1","s":"A mother of a few hours old neonate is alarmed when upon the first feeding the baby vomits and has a frothy residue around his mouth. Physical examination of the baby does not show cyanosis or an extended abdomen. An ultrasound confirms there is no air in the stomach. Which of the following congenital defects would you expect to see in this neonate?","o":["A. Proximal blind-end esophagus with distal esophagus joined to the trachea by a tracheoesophageal fistula","B. Proximal and distal esophagus both end blindly, with no communication with the trachea (pure esophageal atresia)","C. Continuous esophagus with an H-type communication of esophagus with trachea","D. Proximal esophagus joined to the trachea by a fistula, distal esophagus ends blindly","E. Both proximal and distal esophageal segments communicate with the trachea"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Proximal and distal esophagus both end blindly, with no communication with the trachea (pure esophageal atresia)","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Pure esophageal atresia (both ends blind, no fistula).</b> Vomiting and froth at the first feed mean a closed esophagus. No air in the stomach means no fistula to the lower esophagus; no cyanosis means feeds are not entering the trachea.<br>A. Distal fistula: air fills the stomach; the belly swells.<br>C. H-type: esophagus is open; air in the stomach.<br>D. Upper-pouch fistula: feeds pour into the trachea, causing choking and cyanosis.<br>E. Fistulas at both ends: air in the stomach plus aspiration.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0003-11.jpg","in":"Options are 5 diagrams (A-E) of tracheoesophageal anomalies; the option texts here describe each diagram. B = both esophageal segments blind, no fistula (explains no air in stomach).","tg":["GI Development slide 10 (Esophagus)","GI Development slide 11 (Polyhydramniose)"],"src":[{"t":"? Middletown TA review","n":"MT Abdomen Review","sl":11,"f":"F0003"}]},{"id":"F3901-213-1","s":"You are on your Neonatal Intensive Care Unit rotation and one of the nurses reports to you that a baby delivered 26 hours ago seems to cough up saliva, all liquids he is fed, and is blue in the lips (cyanotic). You perform a series of tests which show the stomach is filled only with air and the lungs have some stomach acid in them. You go back to Embryology and decide that this baby most likely has:","o":["A. H-type tracheoesophageal fistula","B. Tracheoesophageal fistulas at the proximal and distal ends","C. Esophageal atresia with tracheoesophageal fistula at the distal end","D. Esophageal atresia with tracheoesophageal fistula at the proximal end","E. Esophageal atresias at the proximal and distal ends"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Esophageal atresia with tracheoesophageal fistula at the distal end","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Esophageal atresia with a fistula at the distal end.</b> The blind upper esophagus sends saliva and feeds back up. The lower esophagus opens off the trachea, so air fills the stomach and acid refluxes into the lungs. Most common type (about eighty-five percent).<br>A. H-type: the esophagus is open, so feeds would go down.<br>B. Fistulas at both ends: the upper pouch drains into the airway, so feeds are inhaled, not coughed back.<br>D. Upper-pouch fistula only: no air in the stomach.<br>E. Atresia at both ends: no air, no acid in the lungs.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 10 (Esophagus)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":213,"f":"F3901"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G1","u":7,"label":"Faster dorsal wall becomes greater curvature","slides":[{"src":"sl/G001-12.jpg","cap":"Dorsal stomach wall grows faster, forms greater curvature, then rotates left","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 12"},{"src":"sl/D045-137.jpg","cap":"Stomach rotation: greater curvature labelled as former dorsal wall","where":"2025 Montana TA review, slide 137"}],"q":[{"id":"D045-135-1","s":"During a dissection of a 7-week embryo, it is noted that the dorsal part of the stomach wall has expanded faster than the ventral. What anatomical feature does this difference produce after a 90° rotation to the left?","o":["A. Lesser Curvature of the stomach","B. Greater Curvature of the stomach","C. Hepatic Diverticulum","D. Mesenteric fusion"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Greater Curvature of the stomach","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Greater curvature.</b> The dorsal (back) wall of the stomach grows faster than the ventral wall. After the stomach turns ninety degrees to the left, that fast-growing back wall faces left and becomes the greater curvature.<br>A. Lesser curvature: the slower ventral (front) wall.<br>C. Hepatic diverticulum: the liver bud off the ventral foregut.<br>D. Mesenteric fusion: how organs such as the duodenum become secondary retroperitoneal.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 12 (Stomach and Stomach Rotation)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":135,"f":"D045"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G1","u":7,"label":"Greater omentum = folded dorsal mesogastrium","slides":[{"src":"sl/G001-13.jpg","cap":"Dorsal mesogastrium folds down into the greater omentum around omental bursa","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 13"},{"src":"sl/D042-60.jpg","cap":"Dorsal mesogastrium expands into the future greater omentum","where":"2025 TA review, slide 60"},{"src":"sl/D042-48.jpg","cap":"Table: greater omentum is folded dorsal mesogastrium, four layers","where":"2025 TA review, slide 48"}],"q":[{"id":"D042-78-1","s":"As the stomach rotates what structure does the dorsal mesentery become in the adult?","o":["A. Lesser omentum","B. Greater omentum","C. Omental bursa"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Greater omentum","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Greater omentum.</b> As the stomach rotates, its dorsal mesentery (dorsal mesogastrium) is pulled left and down and folds on itself as the greater omentum.<br>A. Lesser omentum: from the ventral mesentery (liver to stomach).<br>C. Omental bursa: the space behind the stomach (lesser sac), not a mesentery.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 13 (Greater Omentum)","GI Development slide 12 (Stomach and Stomach Rotation)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI module TA review","sl":78,"f":"D042"}],"t":1},{"id":"F1252-125-1","s":"What part of the dorsal mesentery gives rise to the greater omentum?","o":["A. Dorsal mesogastrium","B. Mesoduodenum","C. Mesentery proper","D. Mesocolon"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Dorsal mesogastrium","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Dorsal mesogastrium.</b> This is the part of the dorsal mesentery attached to the stomach. It balloons out and hangs down as the greater omentum.<br>B. Mesoduodenum: mostly disappears as the duodenum fuses to the back wall.<br>C. Mesentery proper: holds the jejunum and ileum.<br>D. Mesocolon: holds the colon (transverse and sigmoid mesocolon).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 13 (Greater Omentum)","GI Development slide 19 (Ventral Mesentery)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":125,"f":"F1252"}]},{"id":"D042-44-1","s":"During development, the dorsal mesentery suspends the gut tube from the posterior abdominal wall. Which of the following best explains why the greater omentum contains four layers of peritoneum?","o":["A. It develops when the dorsal mesentery folds on itself during foregut rotation","B. It forms from two separate ventral mesenteries that fuse during stomach rotation","C. It arises from intermediate mesoderm during formation of the spleen","D. It results from thickening of the somatic mesoderm overlying the stomach","E. It forms when the vitelline duct regresses and pulls the peritoneum with it"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"It develops when the dorsal mesentery folds on itself during foregut rotation","a":"A","why":"<b>A. The dorsal mesentery folds on itself.</b> The dorsal mesogastrium (two layers) hangs down from the greater curvature and folds back up, so the greater omentum has four layers.<br>B. Fusing ventral mesenteries: the ventral mesentery forms the lesser omentum and falciform ligament.<br>C. Intermediate mesoderm: forms kidneys and gonads; the spleen comes from dorsal mesogastrium mesenchyme.<br>D. Somatic mesoderm: lines the body wall.<br>E. Vitelline duct: links midgut to yolk sac; no role.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 13 (Greater Omentum)","GI Development slide 12 (Stomach and Stomach Rotation)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI module TA review","sl":44,"f":"D042"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G1","u":8,"label":"Spleen: mesenchyme in dorsal mesogastrium behind stomach","slides":[{"src":"sl/G001-15.jpg","cap":"Spleen forms from mesenchyme between dorsal mesogastrium layers, week five","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 15"},{"src":"sl/D042-60.jpg","cap":"Spleen developing inside the dorsal mesogastrium behind the stomach","where":"2025 TA review, slide 60"},{"src":"sl/D045-153.jpg","cap":"Spleen from dorsal mesogastrium mesenchyme in week five; fetal blood-forming organ","where":"2025 Montana TA review, slide 153"}],"q":[{"id":"D042-61-1","s":"Which of the following best describes the embryonic origin of the spleen?","o":["A. Endoderm from foregut","B. Neural crest cells","C. Mesenchyme of dorsal mesogastrium","D. Mesoderm of the septum transversum"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Mesenchyme of dorsal mesogastrium","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Mesenchyme of the dorsal mesogastrium.</b> In week five, mesoderm cells gather between the two layers of the dorsal mesentery behind the stomach and form the spleen. It is not a gut-tube (endoderm) organ.<br>A. Foregut endoderm: forms gut lining, liver, pancreas.<br>B. Neural crest: forms the nerve cells of the gut wall.<br>D. Septum transversum mesoderm: forms the liver's fibrous tissue, Kupffer cells and the central tendon of the diaphragm.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 15 (Spleen)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI module TA review","sl":61,"f":"D042"}],"t":1},{"id":"D045-151-1","s":"A medical student notes that the spleen develops within the dorsal mesogastrium during week 5. Which of the following correctly describes its embryologic tissue of origin?","o":["A. Neural Crest Cells","B. Intermediate Mesoderm","C. Mesenchyme","D. Endodermal lining of the foregut"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Mesenchyme","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Mesenchyme.</b> The spleen comes from mesenchymal (mesoderm) cells that collect between the two layers of the dorsal mesogastrium in week five.<br>A. Neural crest: forms the gut's nerve cells.<br>B. Intermediate mesoderm: forms kidneys and gonads.<br>D. Foregut endoderm: forms gut lining, liver and pancreas; the spleen is not a gut derivative.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 15 (Spleen)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":151,"f":"D045"}],"t":1},{"id":"F1228-288-1","s":"Which of the following is true about the spleen?","o":["A. Spleen develops anterior to the stomach","B. Spleen rotation affects the stomach position","C. Mesenchymal cells collect between the two layers of the dorsal mesogastrium","D. Starts to form during the 15th week of development","E. Main hematopoietic center for most of adolescence"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Mesenchymal cells collect between the two layers of the dorsal mesogastrium","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Mesenchymal cells collect between the two layers of the dorsal mesogastrium.</b> That is how the spleen starts.<br>A. Anterior to the stomach: no, it forms behind (posterior to) it.<br>B. Backwards: stomach rotation moves the spleen, not the other way.<br>D. Fifteenth week: no, it starts in week five.<br>E. Adolescence: it is a big blood-forming organ in the fetus, not in adolescence.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 15 (Spleen)"],"src":[{"t":"2019 review","n":"old Exam 2 review: abdomen part only","sl":288,"f":"F1228"},{"t":"? tutor review","n":"old Exam 2: abdomen part only","sl":184,"f":"F1243"}]},{"id":"D041-105-1","s":"A developmental defect causes the spleen to form in an abnormally anterior position relative to the stomach. Based on normal embryologic development, which of the following best describes where the spleen is supposed to develop?","o":["A. Within the ventral mesentery adjacent to the liver","B. Between the layers of the falciform ligament near the anterior abdominal wall","C. There is no abnormality, the spleen is supposed to develop anterior to the stomach","D. In the dorsal mesentery immediately posterior to the stomach","E. In the mesenchyme surrounding the dorsal pancreatic bud"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"In the dorsal mesentery immediately posterior to the stomach","a":"D","why":"<b>D. In the dorsal mesentery, just behind the stomach.</b> The spleen forms between the layers of the dorsal mesogastrium, posterior to the stomach; stomach rotation then carries it left.<br>A. Ventral mesentery by the liver: forms the lesser omentum and falciform ligament.<br>B. Falciform ligament: ventral mesentery to the front wall.<br>C. Anterior is normal: no, posterior is normal.<br>E. Around the dorsal pancreatic bud: the pancreas grows into the mesoduodenum; the spleen forms in the mesogastrium.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 15 (Spleen)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":105,"f":"D041"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G1","u":8,"label":"Stomach rotation sets the spleen's position","slides":[{"src":"sl/G001-15.jpg","cap":"Spleen in dorsal mesogastrium; stomach rotation decides its final position","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 15"},{"src":"sl/D042-76.jpg","cap":"Transverse views: rotation swings dorsal mesogastrium and spleen to the left","where":"2025 TA review, slide 76"}],"q":[{"id":"D041-103-1","s":"A researcher is studying abdominal organ development. She examines a fetus in which the spleen has failed to migrate to its normal position in the left upper quadrant. This anomaly most likely results from abnormal rotation of which embryological structure?","o":["A. Gut tube","B. Stomach","C. Hepatic diverticulum","D. Ventral pancreatic bud","E. Midgut upper arm"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Stomach","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Stomach.</b> The spleen forms in the dorsal mesogastrium behind the stomach. When the stomach rotates ninety degrees to the left, it carries the dorsal mesogastrium, and the spleen, to the left upper quadrant.<br>A. Gut tube: duodenal rotation moves the ventral pancreatic bud, not the spleen.<br>C. Hepatic diverticulum: forms the liver and biliary tree.<br>D. Ventral pancreatic bud: forms the uncinate process.<br>E. Midgut upper arm: forms the small-intestine loops.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 15 (Spleen)","GI Development slide 12 (Stomach and Stomach Rotation)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":103,"f":"D041"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G1","u":9,"label":"Projectile non-bilious vomiting + olive mass: pyloric stenosis","slides":[{"src":"sl/G001-14.jpg","cap":"Pyloric stenosis: thickened pylorus and projectile vomiting","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 14"}],"q":[{"id":"F1228-291-1","s":"A month old baby is brought to the emergency room because of projectile vomiting after feeding. On examination, a small knot is palpated at the right costal margin. Which of the following disorders does the baby have?","o":["A. Esophageal stenosis","B. Annular pancreas","C. Hypertrophic pyloric stenosis","D. Extrahepatic biliary atresia","E. Duodenal atresia"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Hypertrophic pyloric stenosis","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Hypertrophic pyloric stenosis.</b> Projectile vomiting after feeds at a few weeks old, plus a small firm 'olive' at the right costal margin, is a thickened pyloric muscle.<br>A. Esophageal stenosis: trouble swallowing and spitting up, no mass.<br>B. Annular pancreas: a pancreatic ring narrows the duodenum; signs start at birth, with no olive mass.<br>D. Extrahepatic biliary atresia: jaundice, pale stool, dark urine.<br>E. Duodenal atresia: bilious vomiting on the first day.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 14 (Pyloric Stenosis)"],"src":[{"t":"2019 review","n":"old Exam 2 review: abdomen part only","sl":291,"f":"F1228"},{"t":"? tutor review","n":"old Exam 2: abdomen part only","sl":187,"f":"F1243"}]},{"id":"F3903-283-1","s":"A 14-day-old male is brought to the physician because he has been projectile vomiting. The mother explains that he has had some small regurgitation issues but nothing to this effect. Examination shows a hard bump at the right costal margin of the infant. What anomaly would you suspect?","o":["A. Esophageal atresia","B. Pyloric stenosis","C. Duodenal atresia","D. Tracheoesophageal fistula","E. Esophageal stenosis"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Pyloric stenosis","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Pyloric stenosis.</b> Vomiting that turns projectile at about two weeks old, with a firm lump at the right costal margin, is a thickened pylorus.<br>A. Esophageal atresia: drooling and choking from the first feed.<br>C. Duodenal atresia: bilious vomiting on day one.<br>D. Tracheoesophageal fistula: coughing and cyanosis with feeds.<br>E. Esophageal stenosis: trouble swallowing, no abdominal mass.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 14 (Pyloric Stenosis)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":283,"f":"F3903"}],"t":1},{"id":"D041-88-1","s":"A 14-day-old male is brought to the physician because he has been progressively vomiting and recently the vomiting has been projectile. The infant has failed to gain weight and the mother reports no green color in the vomitus. Examination shows a hard bump at the right costal margin of the infant. What anomaly would you suspect?","o":["A. Esophageal atresia","B. Pyloric stenosis","C. Duodenal atresia","D. Tracheoesophageal fistula","E. Esophageal stenosis"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Pyloric stenosis","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Pyloric stenosis.</b> Vomit without bile puts the block above the bile duct opening. Projectile vomiting, poor weight gain and a hard lump at the right costal margin (the thick pylorus) clinch it.<br>A. Esophageal atresia: drooling and choking from the first feed.<br>C. Duodenal atresia: usually bilious vomiting on day one.<br>D. Tracheoesophageal fistula: coughing and cyanosis with feeds.<br>E. Esophageal stenosis: trouble swallowing, no mass.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 14 (Pyloric Stenosis)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":88,"f":"D041"},{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":77,"f":"F0008"}],"t":1},{"id":"G002-5-1","s":"A 3-week-old male is brought to the physician due to progressive, non-bilious projectile vomiting after feeding over the past 4 days. The infant appears dehydrated and has failed to gain weight since birth. Physical examination reveals an olive-shaped, firm mass palpable at the right costal margin. Which of the following embryological anomalies is the most likely cause of this patient's condition?","o":["A. Esophageal atresia","B. Hypertrophic pyloric stenosis","C. Duodenal atresia","D. Tracheoesophageal fistula","E. Annular pancreas"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Hypertrophic pyloric stenosis","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Hypertrophic pyloric stenosis.</b> At about three weeks, non-bilious vomiting that becomes projectile, poor weight gain and a firm olive-shaped mass mean a thickened pyloric muscle blocking the stomach outlet.<br>A. Esophageal atresia: drooling and choking from birth.<br>C. Duodenal atresia: bilious vomiting on day one.<br>D. Tracheoesophageal fistula: coughing and cyanosis with feeds.<br>E. Annular pancreas: pancreatic ring narrows the duodenum from birth; no olive mass.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 14 (Pyloric Stenosis)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown clicker","n":"","sl":5,"f":"G002"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G1","u":10,"label":"Duodenal atresia/stenosis = failed recanalization","slides":[{"src":"sl/G001-17.jpg","cap":"Duodenal plug: partial recanalization gives stenosis, none gives atresia","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 17"},{"src":"sl/D042-64.jpg","cap":"Gut tube occlusion and recanalization, weeks five to nine; stenosis, atresia","where":"2025 TA review, slide 64"}],"q":[{"id":"D045-139-1","s":"An infant is diagnosed with duodenal stenosis. This condition most likely results from which developmental error?","o":["A. Persistence of the vitelline duct","B. Incomplete recanalization following epithelial proliferation","C. Over-rotation of the midgut","D. Cloacal membrane rupture failure"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Incomplete recanalization following epithelial proliferation","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Incomplete recanalization after epithelial overgrowth.</b> The duodenal lining overgrows and plugs the lumen, then reopens by cell death by week eight. Partial reopening leaves a narrow lumen (stenosis); no reopening leaves a plug (atresia).<br>A. Persistent vitelline duct: Meckel diverticulum or umbilical fistula.<br>C. Midgut over-rotation: not a cause.<br>D. Cloacal membrane fails to rupture: imperforate anus.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 17 (Duodenal Stenosis and Atresia)","GI Development slide 16 (Duodenum and Recanalization)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":139,"f":"D045"}],"t":1},{"id":"D041-91-1","s":"A newborn develops bilious vomiting shortly after birth. Imaging shows double bubble sign consistent with proximal duodenal atresia. This condition most commonly results from failure of which embryologic process?","o":["A. Rotation of foregut","B. Regression of vitelline duct","C. Migration of neural crest cells","D. Fusion of pancreatic ducts","E. Recanalization of endodermal epithelium"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Recanalization of endodermal epithelium","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Recanalization of the endodermal lining.</b> The duodenal endoderm overgrows and closes the lumen, then must reopen by cell death. No reopening gives atresia, seen as a 'double bubble' (air in the stomach and first part of the duodenum).<br>A. Foregut rotation: sets position, not the lumen.<br>B. Vitelline duct regression: failure gives Meckel diverticulum.<br>C. Neural crest migration: failure gives Hirschsprung disease.<br>D. Pancreatic duct fusion: failure gives pancreas divisum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 16 (Duodenum and Recanalization)","GI Development slide 17 (Duodenal Stenosis and Atresia)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":91,"f":"D041"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G1","u":10,"label":"Bilious vomiting + polyhydramnios: duodenal atresia/stenosis","slides":[{"src":"sl/G001-17.jpg","cap":"Duodenal stenosis and atresia with a dilated proximal duodenum","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 17"},{"src":"sl/G001-11.jpg","cap":"Failure of the duodenum to recanalize also causes polyhydramnios","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 11"},{"src":"sl/F3900-247.jpg","cap":"Polyhydramnios plus bile in vomit: block below the bile duct opening","where":"2023 TA review, slide 247"}],"q":[{"id":"F3901-56-1","s":"A baby born to a woman who suffered with polyhydramnios during pregnancy is admitted to the neonatal intensive care unit because he continues to vomit bile. He has a distended abdomen and has not been able to pass much meconium. What is his most likely diagnosis?","o":["A. Esophageal fistula","B. Duodenal atresia","C. Pyloric stenosis","D. Esophageal atresia"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Duodenal atresia","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Duodenal atresia.</b> Polyhydramnios points to a gut block. Bile in the vomit puts the block past the bile duct opening, in the duodenum. Little meconium and a swollen upper belly fit.<br>A. Esophageal fistula: coughing and cyanosis with feeds; no bile.<br>C. Pyloric stenosis: non-bilious projectile vomiting at two to six weeks.<br>D. Esophageal atresia: drooling and choking; the vomit has no bile.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 17 (Duodenal Stenosis and Atresia)","GI Development slide 11 (Polyhydramniose)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":56,"f":"F3901"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3903-249-1","s":"A 28-year-old female is diagnosed with polyhydramnios during her pregnancy. After birth, her son is placed in the NICU due to repeating episodes of vomiting. Examination of the contents indicates presence of bile on all occasions. The neonate has also failed to gain any significant weight. What congenital anomaly explains these symptoms?","o":["A. Pyloric stenosis","B. Esophageal stenosis","C. Duodenal atresia","D. Meckel’s diverticulum","E. Biliary duct atresia"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Duodenal atresia","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Duodenal atresia.</b> Polyhydramnios plus vomit that always contains bile means a complete block in the duodenum past the bile duct opening (failed recanalization).<br>A. Pyloric stenosis: block above the bile duct, so no bile; starts at two to six weeks.<br>B. Esophageal stenosis: no bile; trouble swallowing.<br>D. Meckel diverticulum: painless rectal bleeding, not vomiting at birth.<br>E. Biliary atresia: jaundice and pale stool, because bile can't reach the gut.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 17 (Duodenal Stenosis and Atresia)","GI Development slide 11 (Polyhydramniose)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":249,"f":"F3903"}],"t":1},{"id":"F1228-294-1","s":"A 40 year old woman arrives at the Emergency Room with active labor at 33 weeks gestation. Her prenatal course was notable for increased nuchal translucency at a 12 week ultrasound. She gives birth to a girl weighing 1800g (normal is > 2,500g). The baby later presents with bilious vomiting and abdominal distension. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["A. Pyloric stenosis","B. Tracheoesophageal fistula","C. Duodenal stenosis","D. Esophageal atresia"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Duodenal stenosis","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Duodenal stenosis.</b> Bilious vomiting with a swollen belly means a block past the bile duct opening. Thick nuchal translucency and a forty-year-old mother suggest Down syndrome, which goes with duodenal atresia or stenosis.<br>A. Pyloric stenosis: above the bile duct, so no bile; starts weeks later.<br>B. Tracheoesophageal fistula and D. Esophageal atresia: above the stomach, so no bile in the vomit.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 17 (Duodenal Stenosis and Atresia)","GI Development slide 16 (Duodenum and Recanalization)"],"src":[{"t":"2019 review","n":"old Exam 2 review: abdomen part only","sl":294,"f":"F1228"},{"t":"? tutor review","n":"old Exam 2: abdomen part only","sl":190,"f":"F1243"}]},{"id":"F1228-297-1","s":"In the previous case, which of the following would have been present during the mom’s pregnancy prior to childbirth?","o":["A. Placental rupture","B. Polyhydramnios","C. Breech position","D. Eclampsia","E. Gestational diabetes"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Polyhydramnios","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Polyhydramnios.</b> The previous case (just above) is the baby with duodenal stenosis. A narrowed duodenum stops swallowed amniotic fluid from reaching the intestine to be absorbed, so fluid builds up during pregnancy.<br>A. Placental rupture (abruption): bleeding; not caused by the baby's gut.<br>C. Breech position: how the baby lies.<br>D. Eclampsia: maternal high blood pressure with seizures.<br>E. Gestational diabetes: a maternal sugar problem, not caused by duodenal stenosis.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 11 (Polyhydramniose)","GI Development slide 17 (Duodenal Stenosis and Atresia)"],"src":[{"t":"2019 review","n":"old Exam 2 review: abdomen part only","sl":297,"f":"F1228"},{"t":"? tutor review","n":"old Exam 2: abdomen part only","sl":193,"f":"F1243"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G1","u":11,"label":"Hepatic diverticulum: liver, bile ducts, gallbladder","slides":[{"src":"sl/G001-18.jpg","cap":"Hepatic diverticulum grows into septum transversum: bile duct, gallbladder, cystic duct","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 18"},{"src":"sl/D042-56.jpg","cap":"Hepatic diverticulum from foregut into septum transversum; gallbladder beside pancreatic buds","where":"2025 TA review, slide 56"}],"q":[{"id":"D042-57-1","s":"Which embryonic structure gives rise to the liver?","o":["A. Dorsal mesogastrium","B. Hepatic diverticulum","C. Ventral pancreatic bud","D. Cloacal membrane"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Hepatic diverticulum","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Hepatic diverticulum.</b> In week four an endoderm bud grows from the ventral wall of the lower foregut into the septum transversum and forms the liver, bile ducts and gallbladder.<br>A. Dorsal mesogastrium: forms the spleen and greater omentum.<br>C. Ventral pancreatic bud: forms the uncinate process and lower head of the pancreas.<br>D. Cloacal membrane: covers the future anus.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 18 (Liver and Gallbladder)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI module TA review","sl":57,"f":"D042"}],"t":1},{"id":"D045-147-1","s":"A 6-week-old embryo shows a hepatic diverticulum sprouting from the ventral aspect of the foregut. Which adult structures arise from this diverticulum?","o":["A. Liver parenchyma, bile ducts, gallbladder, cystic duct","B. Pancreatic tail and accessory duct","C. Dorsal mesogastrium and spleen","D. Duodenal submucosal glands"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Liver parenchyma, bile ducts, gallbladder, cystic duct","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Liver parenchyma, bile ducts, gallbladder, cystic duct.</b> The hepatic diverticulum gives the hepatic duct (which forms liver cells and bile capillaries), the bile duct, the gallbladder and the cystic duct.<br>B. Pancreatic tail and accessory duct: from the dorsal pancreatic bud.<br>C. Dorsal mesogastrium and spleen: mesoderm of the dorsal mesentery.<br>D. Duodenal submucosal glands: from the duodenal lining, not the diverticulum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 18 (Liver and Gallbladder)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":147,"f":"D045"}],"t":1},{"id":"D041-97-1","s":"A researcher is studying congenital anomalies of the biliary system. She notes that improper development of the hepatic diverticulum early in gestation has led to absence of the gallbladder in a fetus. Failure of which embryological process most directly explains this finding?","o":["A. Rotation of the stomach causing the diverticulum to move from ventral to lateral","B. Outgrowth of the hepatic diverticulum into the septum transversum","C. Partitioning of the cloaca into urogenital and anorectal regions","D. Fusion of the dorsal and ventral pancreatic buds","E. Recanalization of the duodenum after epithelial proliferation"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Outgrowth of the hepatic diverticulum into the septum transversum","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Outgrowth of the hepatic diverticulum into the septum transversum.</b> The diverticulum must grow into the septum transversum and branch; its small cystic branch becomes the gallbladder and cystic duct. If that fails, there is no gallbladder.<br>A. Stomach rotation: moves the diverticulum from ventral to lateral; position only.<br>C. Cloacal partitioning: hindgut.<br>D. Pancreatic bud fusion: pancreas only.<br>E. Duodenal recanalization: causes atresia or stenosis.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 18 (Liver and Gallbladder)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":97,"f":"D041"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G1","u":11,"label":"Kupffer cells: septum transversum mesoderm","slides":[{"src":"sl/G001-18.jpg","cap":"Septum transversum mesoderm forms liver fibrous tissue and Kupffer cells","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 18"},{"src":"sl/G002-18.jpg","cap":"Liver bud in septum transversum; Kupffer cells lining liver sinusoids","where":"2026 Middletown clicker, slide 18"}],"q":[{"id":"F3903-293-1","s":"A patient is having his blood work done. The test reveals he is not having proper breakdown of his red blood cells. Further investigation shows he has issues with his Kupffer cells. Embryologically, these cells derive from which of the following germ layers?","o":["A. Endoderm","B. Mesoderm","C. Ectoderm","D. Neuroectoderm"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Mesoderm","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Mesoderm.</b> Kupffer cells, the liver macrophages that break down old red blood cells, come from mesoderm (lecture: septum transversum mesoderm forms the liver's fibrous tissue and Kupffer cells).<br>A. Endoderm: forms liver cells and bile ducts from the hepatic diverticulum.<br>C. Ectoderm: skin and nervous system.<br>D. Neuroectoderm: brain and spinal cord.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 18 (Liver and Gallbladder)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":293,"f":"F3903"},{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":87,"f":"F0008"}]},{"id":"G002-17-1","s":"A researcher studying the development of the hepatic sinusoidal architecture tracks the lineage of specialized tissue macrophages responsible for clearing cellular debris and spent erythrocytes from portal circulation. These cells, known as Kupffer cells, originate from which of the following embryological germ layers?","o":["A. Endoderm","B. Mesoderm","C. Surface ectoderm","D. Neuroectoderm","E. Neural crest"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Mesoderm","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Mesoderm.</b> Liver cells and bile ducts come from endoderm (the hepatic bud), but Kupffer cells, the macrophages lining the sinusoids, come from mesoderm.<br>A. Endoderm: liver cells and bile ducts only.<br>C. Surface ectoderm: skin and lens.<br>D. Neuroectoderm: brain, spinal cord, retina.<br>E. Neural crest: ganglia, melanocytes and others; not liver macrophages.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 18 (Liver and Gallbladder)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown clicker","n":"","sl":17,"f":"G002"}],"t":1},{"id":"D041-99-1","s":"A fetus is found to have reduced liver size at week 9 of development. A defect in which of the following structures would most directly impair formation of the fibrous tissue and Kupffer cells of the liver?","o":["A. Hepatic duct","B. Ventral mesentery","C. Septum transversum mesoderm","D. Dorsal mesentery","E. Proctodeum"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Septum transversum mesoderm","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Septum transversum mesoderm.</b> The hepatic diverticulum grows into this mesoderm. The endoderm makes liver cells and bile ducts; the septum transversum mesoderm makes the fibrous tissue and Kupffer cells.<br>A. Hepatic duct: forms liver parenchyma and bile capillaries.<br>B. Ventral mesentery: forms the lesser omentum and falciform ligament.<br>D. Dorsal mesentery: forms mesogastrium, mesoduodenum, mesentery proper, mesocolon.<br>E. Proctodeum: the future anal opening.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 18 (Liver and Gallbladder)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":99,"f":"D041"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G1","u":12,"label":"Ventral mesentery becomes falciform ligament + lesser omentum","slides":[{"src":"sl/G001-19.jpg","cap":"Ventral mesentery becomes falciform ligament and lesser omentum","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 19"},{"src":"sl/F3900-224.jpg","cap":"Liver splits the ventral mesentery into falciform ligament and lesser omentum","where":"2023 TA review, slide 224"}],"q":[{"id":"F1156-113-1","s":"What does the Ventral Mesentery later become?","o":["A. Lesser omentum + Dorsal mesentery","B. Lesser omentum + Septum transversum","C. Septum transversum + Ventral pancreatic bud","D. Falciform ligament + Lesser omentum"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Falciform ligament + Lesser omentum","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Falciform ligament + lesser omentum.</b> The growing liver divides the ventral mesentery: liver to front body wall = falciform ligament; liver to stomach and duodenum = lesser omentum.<br>A. Dorsal mesentery: a separate mesentery on the back side.<br>B. Septum transversum: the source of the ventral mesentery, not its product.<br>C. Septum transversum and ventral pancreatic bud: neither comes from the ventral mesentery.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 19 (Ventral Mesentery)"],"src":[{"t":"? TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":113,"f":"F1156"}]},{"id":"F3900-222-1","s":"What adult structure does the ventral mesentery become?","o":["A. Falciform ligament","B. Greater omentum","C. Coronary ligament","D. Diaphragm","E. Ligamentum teres hepatis"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Falciform ligament","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Falciform ligament.</b> The liver splits the ventral mesentery in two: front part = falciform ligament (liver to front wall), back part = lesser omentum (liver to stomach and duodenum).<br>B. Greater omentum: dorsal mesogastrium.<br>C. Coronary ligament: peritoneum folded around the liver's bare area against the diaphragm; not one of the two ventral-mesentery derivatives the lecture lists.<br>D. Diaphragm: its central tendon comes from the septum transversum.<br>E. Ligamentum teres: the closed umbilical vein, lying in the falciform ligament's free edge.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 19 (Ventral Mesentery)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":222,"f":"F3900"}],"t":1},{"id":"F1180-283-1","s":"Which of the following structures is a remnant of the embryonic ventral mesentery?","o":["A. Sigmoid mesocolon","B. Mesentery of the small intestine","C. Greater omentum","D. Falciform ligament","E. Transverse mesocolon"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Falciform ligament","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Falciform ligament.</b> It is the part of the ventral mesentery between the liver and the front abdominal wall; the umbilical vein runs in its free edge.<br>A. Sigmoid mesocolon, B. Mesentery of the small intestine, C. Greater omentum and E. Transverse mesocolon: all from the dorsal mesentery.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 19 (Ventral Mesentery)","GI Development slide 6 (Mesenteries)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":283,"f":"F1180"},{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":222,"f":"F1178"}]},{"id":"D042-46-1","s":"A defect in development of the ventral mesentery would most directly disrupt which of the following adult structures?","o":["A. Mesentery of the jejunum","B. Mesocolon","C. Dorsal mesogastrium","D. Mesoduodenum","E. Falciform ligament"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Falciform ligament","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Falciform ligament.</b> Only the foregut has a ventral mesentery, and it becomes the falciform ligament (liver to front wall) and lesser omentum (liver to stomach).<br>A. Mesentery of the jejunum, B. Mesocolon, C. Dorsal mesogastrium and D. Mesoduodenum: all parts of the dorsal mesentery.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 19 (Ventral Mesentery)","GI Development slide 6 (Mesenteries)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI module TA review","sl":46,"f":"D042"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3900-220-1","s":"What structure connects the liver to the anterior abdominal wall in the developing embryo?","o":["A. Dorsal mesentery","B. Ventral mesentery","C. Gallbladder","D. Septum transversum"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Ventral mesentery","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Ventral mesentery.</b> In the embryo the liver sits inside the ventral mesentery. The part between the liver and the front body wall becomes the falciform ligament.<br>A. Dorsal mesentery: ties the gut to the back wall.<br>C. Gallbladder: an organ, not an attachment.<br>D. Septum transversum: the mesoderm block the liver grows into; most of it becomes the central tendon of the diaphragm, and its thin part near the liver forms the ventral mesentery.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 19 (Ventral Mesentery)","GI Development slide 18 (Liver and Gallbladder)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":220,"f":"F3900"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3901-209-1","s":"Peritoneal ligaments are not true ligaments; however, they consist of a double layer of peritoneum that connects an organ with another organ or to the abdominal wall. The liver originates from the hepatic diverticulum, which sprouts from the foregut into the ventral mesentery. Which part of the ventral mesentery connects the liver to the anterior abdominal wall?","o":["A. Falciform ligament","B. Lesser omentum","C. Hepatogastric ligament","D. Hepatoduodenal ligament"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Falciform ligament","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Falciform ligament.</b> It is the part of the ventral mesentery that runs from the liver to the front abdominal wall.<br>B. Lesser omentum: the other part, from the liver to the stomach and duodenum.<br>C. Hepatogastric ligament: part of the lesser omentum, liver to stomach.<br>D. Hepatoduodenal ligament: part of the lesser omentum, liver to duodenum; carries the portal triad.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 19 (Ventral Mesentery)","GI Development slide 18 (Liver and Gallbladder)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":209,"f":"F3901"}]},{"id":"D041-78-1","s":"A researcher notes that a certain organ develops within the septum transversum and divides a mesentery into two layers. Which of the following adult structures arises from this mesentery?","o":["A. Greater omentum","B. Mesentery proper","C. Sigmoid mesocolon","D. Falciform ligament","E. Gastrosplenic ligament"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Falciform ligament","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Falciform ligament.</b> The organ is the liver: it grows into the septum transversum and splits the ventral mesentery into the falciform ligament and lesser omentum.<br>A. Greater omentum: dorsal mesogastrium.<br>B. Mesentery proper: dorsal mesentery of the small intestine.<br>C. Sigmoid mesocolon: dorsal mesentery of the sigmoid colon.<br>E. Gastrosplenic ligament: dorsal mesogastrium between stomach and spleen.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 19 (Ventral Mesentery)","GI Development slide 6 (Mesenteries)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":78,"f":"D041"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G1","u":13,"label":"Duodenal rotation swings ventral bud behind and below","slides":[{"src":"sl/G001-20.jpg","cap":"Pancreatic buds at days 30 to 42: ventral bud swings behind duodenum","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 20"},{"src":"sl/D042-87.jpg","cap":"Four steps: ventral bud rotates clockwise around duodenum to meet dorsal bud","where":"2025 TA review, slide 87"}],"q":[{"id":"D042-88-1","s":"Rotation of what two organs aids in the formation of the pancreas?","o":["A. Stomach and liver","B. Stomach and duodenum","C. Duodenum and liver"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Stomach and duodenum","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Stomach and duodenum.</b> The stomach's rotation swings the duodenum into a C-shape to the right. That carries the <b>ventral pancreatic bud</b> behind and below the dorsal bud, where the two fuse.<br>A. Stomach and liver: the liver does not rotate the pancreas; it grows into the septum transversum.<br>C. Duodenum and liver: the duodenum is right, but the turning that drives it comes from the stomach, not the liver.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 20 (Pancreas)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI module TA review","sl":88,"f":"D042"}],"t":1},{"id":"D042-90-1","s":"In what position does the ventral bud move in relation to the dorsal bud to form the pancreas as the stomach and duodenum rotate?","o":["A. Superior and Inferior","B. Inferior and Posterior","C. Superior and Anterior","D. Anterior and Inferior"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Inferior and Posterior","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Inferior and posterior.</b> As the stomach and duodenum rotate, the ventral bud swings around the back of the duodenum and settles <b>below and behind</b> the dorsal bud. It becomes the uncinate process and lower part of the head.<br>A. Superior and inferior: two opposite directions, not a real position.<br>C. Superior and anterior: the reverse of the true path.<br>D. Anterior and inferior: below is right, but the bud ends up behind the dorsal bud, not in front.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 20 (Pancreas)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI module TA review","sl":90,"f":"D042"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G1","u":13,"label":"Pancreatic islet cells come from endoderm","slides":[{"src":"sl/G001-20.jpg","cap":"Dorsal and ventral pancreatic buds sprouting from the foregut tube","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 20"},{"src":"sl/G002-3.jpg","cap":"Germ-layer table: endoderm makes gut epithelium and gland parenchyma","where":"2026 Middletown clicker, slide 3"}],"q":[{"id":"F3902-149-1","s":"The given T1-weighted gradient echo of the abdomen demonstrates a malignant pancreatic islet cell tumor of the pancreas near the junction of the pancreatic body and tail. The pancreatic islet cells involved with this tumor are derived from which of the following embryonic sources?","o":["A) Ectoderm","B) Endoderm","C) Somatic mesoderm","D) Visceral mesoderm","E) Neural crest"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Endoderm","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Endoderm.</b> Both pancreatic buds grow out of the endodermal lining of the foregut. The ducts, the enzyme-making acini and the hormone-making <b>islet cells</b> all come from this endoderm.<br>A. Ectoderm: makes skin and the nervous system.<br>C. Somatic mesoderm: makes the body wall.<br>D. Visceral (splanchnic) mesoderm: makes the connective tissue and smooth muscle around the gland, not its cells.<br>E. Neural crest: makes gut nerve cells and the adrenal medulla, not islets.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Axial T1 MRI of the upper abdomen with arrows on a mass at the pancreatic body-tail junction; not needed to answer.","tg":["GI Development slide 20 (Pancreas)","GI Development slide 9 (Foregut)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":149,"f":"F3902"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G1","u":13,"label":"Bilobed ventral bud: annular pancreas","slides":[{"src":"sl/G001-21.jpg","cap":"Bilobed ventral bud wraps around the duodenum: annular pancreas","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 21"},{"src":"sl/D045-145.jpg","cap":"Bilobed ventral bud encircling the duodenum to form annular pancreas","where":"2025 Montana TA review, slide 145"}],"q":[{"id":"D041-94-1","s":"A newborn has duodenal obstruction caused by an annular pancreas. Which embryologic abnormality is responsible?","o":["A. Bifid ventral pancreatic bud encircling duodenum","B. Failure of dorsal bud to form","C. Dorsal and ventral bud fusion failure","D. Over rotation of stomach","E. Midgut volvulus"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Bifid ventral pancreatic bud encircling duodenum","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Bifid (two-lobed) ventral pancreatic bud.</b> When the duodenum rotates, one lobe swings behind it and the other in front, so pancreatic tissue rings the duodenum and narrows or blocks it.<br>B. No dorsal bud: missing pancreatic neck, body and tail.<br>C. Buds fail to fuse: pancreas divisum (two separate duct systems).<br>D. Stomach over-rotation: not a cause.<br>E. Midgut volvulus: a twisted, malrotated midgut; a different cause of bilious vomiting.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 20 (Pancreas)","GI Development slide 21 (Clinical Correlate)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":94,"f":"D041"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G1","u":13,"label":"Annular pancreas: bilobed ventral bud encircles duodenum","slides":[{"src":"sl/G001-21.jpg","cap":"Bilobed ventral bud wrapping around duodenum to form annular pancreas","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 21"},{"src":"sl/D045-145.jpg","cap":"Normal single ventral bud versus bilobed bud encircling the duodenum","where":"2025 Montana TA review, slide 145"}],"q":[{"id":"D045-143-1","s":"A neonate presents with nonbilious vomiting. Imaging reveals the duodenum encircled by pancreatic tissue. What is the most likely cause?","o":["A. Failure of dorsal pancreatic bud to form","B. Annular pancreas due to a bi-lobed ventral pancreatic bud","C. Absence of ventral pancreatic bud","D. Overgrowth of septum transversum"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Annular pancreas due to a bi-lobed ventral pancreatic bud","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Annular pancreas from a two-lobed ventral bud.</b> When the gut rotates, one lobe of the ventral bud swings behind the duodenum and the other in front. They meet and make a <b>ring of pancreas</b> that squeezes the second part of the duodenum.<br>A. Missing dorsal bud: no body or tail, but no ring.<br>C. Missing ventral bud: no uncinate process, but no ring.<br>D. Septum transversum overgrowth: affects the diaphragm and liver region, not the pancreas.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 21 (Clinical Correlate)","GI Development slide 20 (Pancreas)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":143,"f":"D045"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G1","u":14,"label":"Midgut derivatives: lower duodenum to two-thirds transverse colon","slides":[{"src":"sl/G001-22.jpg","cap":"Midgut list: lower duodenum to two-thirds transverse colon; superior mesenteric artery","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 22"},{"src":"sl/D041-135.jpg","cap":"Foregut, midgut and hindgut derivative lists with celiac, superior and inferior mesenterics","where":"2025 TA review, slide 135"}],"q":[{"id":"D041-113-1","s":"Which of the following organs or structures is derived from the midgut?","o":["A. Descending colon","B. Cecum","C. Urinary bladder","D. Liver","E. Pancreas"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Cecum","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Cecum.</b> It forms from the cecal swelling on the lower (caudal) limb of the midgut loop, so it is <b>midgut</b> and is fed by the superior mesenteric artery.<br>A. Descending colon: hindgut.<br>C. Urinary bladder: from the urogenital sinus, the front part of the cloaca (hindgut).<br>D. Liver: foregut (hepatic diverticulum).<br>E. Pancreas: foregut (dorsal and ventral buds).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 22 (Midgut)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":113,"f":"D041"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3901-64-1","s":"Which of the following is NOT part of the midgut?","o":["A. Jejunum","B. Ileum","C. Appendix","D. Cecum","E. Ascending colon","F. ⅔ of transverse colon","G. First part (upper portion) of duodenum"],"g":["G"],"k":["G"],"rv":false,"at":"First part (upper portion) of duodenum","a":"G","why":"<b>G. First part of the duodenum.</b> The duodenum above the bile duct opening is <b>foregut</b>, fed by the celiac trunk. Everything else listed is midgut, fed by the superior mesenteric artery.<br>A. Jejunum: midgut (upper limb of the loop).<br>B. Ileum: midgut.<br>C. Appendix: midgut, budding off the cecum.<br>D. Cecum: midgut (lower limb).<br>E. Ascending colon: midgut.<br>F. Two-thirds of transverse colon: midgut; only the last third is hindgut.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 22 (Midgut)","GI Development slide 9 (Foregut)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":64,"f":"F3901"}]},{"id":"D041-133-1","s":"Which correctly describes the derivative of the midgut and its arterial supply?","o":["A. Liver - superior mesenteric artery","B. Rectum - celiac trunk","C. Ileum - super mesenteric artery","D. Liver - inferior mesenteric artery","E. Distal ⅓ of transverse colon - superior mesenteric artery"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Ileum - super mesenteric artery","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Ileum and the superior mesenteric artery (SMA).</b> The ileum is midgut, and every midgut organ is fed by the SMA, the same artery the midgut loop rotates around.<br>A. Liver and SMA: the liver is foregut, fed by the celiac trunk.<br>B. Rectum and celiac trunk: the rectum is hindgut, fed by the inferior mesenteric artery (IMA).<br>D. Liver and IMA: wrong region and wrong artery.<br>E. Last third of transverse colon and SMA: that part is hindgut, fed by the IMA.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 22 (Midgut)","GI Development slide 7 (Blood supply)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":133,"f":"D041"}],"t":1},{"id":"D045-158-1","s":"A newborn develops an obstruction in the distal ileum. This region originates from which portion of the embryonic gut?","o":["A. Midgut","B. Foregut","C. Hindgut","D. Proctodeum"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Midgut","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Midgut.</b> The midgut makes the duodenum below the bile duct opening, the jejunum, the <b>ileum</b>, cecum, appendix, ascending colon and first two-thirds of the transverse colon. All are fed by the superior mesenteric artery.<br>B. Foregut: ends at the bile duct opening in the duodenum.<br>C. Hindgut: starts at the last third of the transverse colon.<br>D. Proctodeum: the ectodermal anal pit that forms the lower third of the anal canal.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 22 (Midgut)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":158,"f":"D045"}],"t":1},{"id":"D042-67-1","s":"A newborn presents with bilious vomiting within the first day of life. Imaging shows a duodenal obstruction distal to the bile duct entry. The affected region is derived from which embryological portion of the gut tube?","o":["A. Foregut","B. Midgut","C. Hindgut","D. Cloacal membrane"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Midgut","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Midgut.</b> The duodenum is split at the <b>bile duct opening</b>: above it is foregut, below it is midgut. A block below that opening makes the vomit bilious, because bile enters above the block.<br>A. Foregut: only the duodenum above the bile duct opening.<br>C. Hindgut: starts at the last third of the transverse colon.<br>D. Cloacal membrane: the membrane closing the end of the hindgut, not a gut region.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 22 (Midgut)","GI Development slide 16 (Duodenum and Recanalization)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI module TA review","sl":67,"f":"D042"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G1","u":15,"label":"Midgut loop: herniate week 6, return week 10","slides":[{"src":"sl/G001-24.jpg","cap":"Herniated loop turns ninety degrees, then one hundred eighty more on return","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 24"},{"src":"sl/D041-121.jpg","cap":"Rotation stages by gestational week: herniated at six to nine, back by eleven","where":"2025 TA review, slide 121"}],"q":[{"id":"D041-119-1","s":"Which of the following correctly describes the normal sequence of midgut development during weeks 6–10 of gestation?","o":["A. Herniation → 90° clockwise rotation → retraction → 180° clockwise rotation","B. Herniation → 90° counterclockwise rotation → retraction → 180° counterclockwise rotation","C. Herniation → 180° clockwise rotation → retraction → 90° counterclockwise rotation","D. Retraction → 270° clockwise rotation → fixation"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Herniation → 90° counterclockwise rotation → retraction → 180° counterclockwise rotation","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Herniation, ninety degrees counterclockwise, retraction, one hundred eighty degrees counterclockwise.</b> In week six the loop herniates into the umbilical cord and turns ninety degrees counterclockwise (seen from the front) around the superior mesenteric artery. In week ten it returns and turns another one hundred eighty, for <b>two hundred seventy</b> in total.<br>A. Clockwise: wrong direction; clockwise turning is reversed rotation.<br>C. The ninety-degree turn comes first, not the one hundred eighty.<br>D. Retraction comes after herniation, and the turning is counterclockwise.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 24 (Herniation and Rotation)","GI Development slide 23 (Herniation and Rotation)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":119,"f":"D041"}],"t":1},{"id":"D045-161-1","s":"A fetus at 8 weeks has loops of intestine within the umbilical cord. This finding is normal. Which of the following processes has not yet occurred?","o":["A. Elongation of the small intestine","B. 90 degree counterclockwise rotation","C. Retraction of the herniated midgut loop in the abdominal cavity","D. Herniation of the primary intestinal loop"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Retraction of the herniated midgut loop in the abdominal cavity","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Retraction of the herniated loop.</b> The midgut herniates into the cord at the start of week six and stays there until <b>week ten</b>, when it returns and makes its last one hundred eighty degrees of turning. At week eight it is still outside.<br>A. Elongation: the gut lengthens fast while herniated, so it has happened.<br>B. Ninety-degree counterclockwise rotation: happens during herniation, so it is done by week eight.<br>D. Herniation: began at week six.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 23 (Herniation and Rotation)","GI Development slide 24 (Herniation and Rotation)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":161,"f":"D045"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G1","u":15,"label":"Cranial (upper) limb of loop becomes small intestine","slides":[{"src":"sl/G001-24.jpg","cap":"Upper arm becomes small intestine; lower arm becomes cecum and large intestine","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 24"}],"q":[{"id":"F0003-2-1","s":"A rotational defect causes the cranial limb of the midgut loop to undergo ischemic necrosis during physiologic herniation. Which of the organs might be affected in the growing fetus?","o":["A. Stomach","B. Gallbladder","C. Jejunum","D. Cecum","E. Appendix"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Jejunum","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Jejunum.</b> The cranial (upper) limb of the midgut loop becomes the lower duodenum, <b>jejunum</b> and upper ileum. The caudal (lower) limb becomes the lower ileum, cecum, appendix, ascending colon and two-thirds of the transverse colon.<br>A. Stomach: foregut, not in the loop.<br>B. Gallbladder: foregut.<br>D. Cecum: caudal limb.<br>E. Appendix: caudal limb, off the cecum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 24 (Herniation and Rotation)","GI Development slide 22 (Midgut)"],"src":[{"t":"? Middletown TA review","n":"MT Abdomen Review","sl":2,"f":"F0003"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G1","u":15,"label":"Midgut loop rotates around the superior mesenteric artery","slides":[{"src":"sl/G001-24.jpg","cap":"Midgut loop wrapped around the superior mesenteric artery, its rotation axis","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 24"},{"src":"sl/G001-23.jpg","cap":"Herniation at week six; superior mesenteric artery is the rotation axis","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 23"}],"q":[{"id":"F3903-265-1","s":"During the herniation of the midgut of a 6-week-old embryo, faulty cell signaling causes the upper arm to rotate 180° counterclockwise instead of the usual 90°. Blood flow of which of the following would be impeded by the extra rotation of the midgut?","o":["A. Celiac trunk","B. Renal arteries","C. Superior mesenteric artery","D. Inferior mesenteric artery","E. Common iliac arteries"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Superior mesenteric artery","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Superior mesenteric artery (SMA).</b> The midgut loop turns around the SMA as its axis. Too much turning <b>twists the mesentery around the SMA</b> and kinks it, like a volvulus.<br>A. Celiac trunk: supplies foregut, which is not in the herniated loop.<br>B. Renal arteries: go to the kidneys, behind the peritoneum.<br>D. Inferior mesenteric artery: supplies hindgut, which stays in the abdomen.<br>E. Common iliac arteries: go to the pelvis and legs.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 23 (Herniation and Rotation)","GI Development slide 7 (Blood supply)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":265,"f":"F3903"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G1","u":16,"label":"Ascending colon becomes secondarily retroperitoneal","slides":[{"src":"sl/G001-8.jpg","cap":"Primary versus secondary retroperitoneal organs, including ascending and descending colon","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 8"},{"src":"sl/G001-26.jpg","cap":"Ascending and descending colon mesenteries fuse with posterior abdominal wall","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 26"}],"q":[{"id":"D041-82-1","s":"A surgeon notes that the visceral peritoneum covers only the anterior surface of an organ. Developmental review shows this organ initially had a mesentery but later fused to the posterior body wall. Which of the following organs matches this description?","o":["A. Kidney","B. Spleen","C. Stomach","D. Ascending colon","E. Liver"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Ascending colon","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Ascending colon.</b> It starts with a mesentery, which fuses to the back wall once the midgut is in place. That makes it <b>secondarily retroperitoneal</b>, covered by peritoneum only in front.<br>A. Kidney: primarily retroperitoneal; never had a mesentery.<br>B. Spleen: stays intraperitoneal in the dorsal mesogastrium.<br>C. Stomach: intraperitoneal; keeps its mesenteries (the omenta).<br>E. Liver: intraperitoneal, apart from its bare area.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 26 (Mesenteries)","GI Development slide 8 (Retroperitoneal Organs)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":82,"f":"D041"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G1","u":16,"label":"Nonrotation: small bowel right, colon left","slides":[{"src":"sl/G001-24.jpg","cap":"Normal two-hundred-seventy-degree rotation ends with cecum low on the right","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 24"},{"src":"sl/G002-21.jpg","cap":"Nonrotation (small bowel right, colon left) versus reversed clockwise rotation","where":"2026 Middletown clicker, slide 21"}],"q":[{"id":"G002-19-1","s":"A 15-year-old male undergoes surgery for an unrelated acute abdominal condition. Intraoperatively, the surgeon observes that the entire small intestine is located on the right side of the abdominal cavity, the entire large intestine is located on the left side, and the cecum is positioned in the lower midline suprapubic region. Which of the following developmental aberrations best accounts for these findings?","o":["A. Failure of the midgut loop to rotate after returning to the abdomen (Nonrotation)","B. Clockwise rotation of the midgut loop around the superior mesenteric artery (Reversed rotation)","C. Hypermobility of the hepatic flexure","D. Failure of the lateral body folds to fuse","E. Incomplete recanalization of the duodenal lumen"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Failure of the midgut loop to rotate after returning to the abdomen (Nonrotation)","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Nonrotation.</b> The loop made only its first ninety-degree turn and skipped the last one hundred eighty on return. So the <b>small bowel stays on the right, the colon on the left</b>, and the cecum ends low in the midline.<br>B. Reversed (clockwise) rotation: the transverse colon ends up behind the superior mesenteric artery.<br>C. Hepatic flexure mobility: cannot move all the bowel.<br>D. Failed lateral body folds: gastroschisis.<br>E. Failed duodenal recanalization: duodenal stenosis or atresia.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 24 (Herniation and Rotation)","GI Development slide 27 (Clinical Correlates)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown clicker","n":"","sl":19,"f":"G002"}],"t":1},{"id":"F0008-89-1","s":"A 15-year-old male was treated surgically for a strangulated indirect inguinal hernia. The surgeon noticed that the small intestine was located in the right half of the abdominal cavity and the large intestine on the left side. The cecum and appendix were suprapubic in position. Which of the following would cause this anomaly?","o":["A. nonrotation of the midgut","B. reversed rotation of the midgut","C. midgut volvulus","D. mixed rotation of the midgut","E. duplication of the intestine"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"nonrotation of the midgut","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Nonrotation of the midgut.</b> Normally the loop turns two hundred seventy degrees counterclockwise, ending with the cecum low on the right. If it stops after the first ninety, the <b>small bowel lies on the right and the colon on the left</b>.<br>B. Reversed rotation: clockwise turn; transverse colon passes behind the superior mesenteric artery.<br>C. Midgut volvulus: a twist that cuts off blood, not a layout.<br>D. Mixed rotation: cecum sits just below the pylorus, tied down by bands.<br>E. Duplication: an extra cyst or tube of bowel.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 24 (Herniation and Rotation)","GI Development slide 23 (Herniation and Rotation)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":89,"f":"F0008"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G1","u":17,"label":"Omphalocele: midgut fails to return to abdomen","slides":[{"src":"sl/G001-27.jpg","cap":"Omphalocele photo: bowel kept in an amnion sac at the umbilicus","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 27"},{"src":"sl/F3903-255.jpg","cap":"Side by side: omphalocele sac at umbilicus versus uncovered gastroschisis bowel","where":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker, slide 255"}],"q":[{"id":"F1228-310-1","s":"Failure of the intestinal loops to return to the abdominal cavity results in a:","o":["A. Meckel’s diverticulum","B. Gastroschisis","C. Omphalocele","D. Pyloric stenosis","E. Imperforate anus"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Omphalocele","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Omphalocele.</b> The midgut normally returns to the abdomen in week ten. If it stays in the cord, the bowel sits in a <b>sac of amnion</b> (and peritoneum) at the umbilicus.<br>A. Meckel's diverticulum: leftover vitelline duct on the ileum.<br>B. Gastroschisis: body wall fails to close; bare bowel.<br>D. Pyloric stenosis: thick pyloric muscle; projectile vomiting.<br>E. Imperforate anus: anal membrane fails to rupture.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 27 (Clinical Correlates)"],"src":[{"t":"2019 review","n":"old Exam 2 review: abdomen part only","sl":310,"f":"F1228"},{"t":"? tutor review","n":"old Exam 2: abdomen part only","sl":206,"f":"F1243"}],"t":1},{"id":"G002-15-1","s":"During the 6th week of embryonic development, the midgut loop normally herniates through the primitive umbilical ring. By the 11th week, these intestinal loops fail to return to the abdominal cavity, remaining covered by a sac of peritoneum and amnion. Which of the following anomalies has occurred?","o":["A. Omphalocele","B. Gastroschisis","C. Umbilical hernia","D. Meckel's diverticulum","E. Duodenal malrotation"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Omphalocele","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Omphalocele.</b> The loops herniated normally but never came back. They stay at the umbilicus inside a sac of <b>peritoneum and amnion</b>, often with a trisomy.<br>B. Gastroschisis: wall gap beside the umbilicus; bowel bare, no sac.<br>C. Umbilical hernia: loops returned, then pushed out later through a weak ring; covered by skin.<br>D. Meckel's diverticulum: leftover vitelline duct.<br>E. Duodenal malrotation: abnormal turning, not failure to return.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 27 (Clinical Correlates)","GI Development slide 23 (Herniation and Rotation)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown clicker","n":"","sl":15,"f":"G002"},{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":291,"f":"F3903"},{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":85,"f":"F0008"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G1","u":17,"label":"Omphalocele: membrane sac at the umbilicus","slides":[{"src":"sl/G001-27.jpg","cap":"Omphalocele photo: shiny amnion sac at umbilicus; gastroschisis bowel uncovered","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 27"},{"src":"sl/F3903-255.jpg","cap":"Omphalocele covered by peritoneum and amnion; gastroschisis has no covering","where":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker, slide 255"}],"q":[{"id":"F1252-187-1","s":"While on rotations you attending asks you... “what is distinguishes an omphalocele from a gastroschisis?” You respond by saying….","o":["A. “Location of the esophagus”","B. “Amniotic membrane”","C. “Closure of the fossa ovalis”","D. “Fusion of the sternum”","E. “Synovial membrane”"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"“Amniotic membrane”","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Amniotic membrane.</b> An omphalocele is covered by a sac of <b>amnion</b> (and peritoneum). Gastroschisis bowel is bare.<br>A. Esophagus location: unrelated to wall defects.<br>C. Fossa ovalis closure: a heart (atrial septum) event.<br>D. Sternum fusion: chest wall, not abdomen.<br>E. Synovial membrane: lines joints.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 27 (Clinical Correlates)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":187,"f":"F1252"}]},{"id":"F3902-235-1","s":"You are doing an OBGYN rotation at Garnet Hospital and your attending asks you to evaluate a patient that was born just a few days ago with a malformity. Which of the following defects is this pt suffering from?","o":["A. Omphalocele","B. Gastroschisis","C. Polyhydramnios","D. Meckel's Diverticulum","E. Pyloric stenosis"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Omphalocele","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Omphalocele.</b> The photo shows a large <b>midline sac at the umbilicus</b> with a shiny, see-through covering of amnion; no bowel is exposed. That covering marks omphalocele.<br>B. Gastroschisis: bare red bowel loops beside the umbilicus, no sac.<br>C. Polyhydramnios: too much amniotic fluid; not visible on a baby.<br>D. Meckel's diverticulum: inside the abdomen.<br>E. Pyloric stenosis: projectile vomiting and an olive-shaped mass; no wall defect.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F3902-235.jpg","in":"Photo of a newborn with a large midline sac at the umbilicus, covered by a shiny translucent membrane (bowel not exposed).","tg":["GI Development slide 27 (Clinical Correlates)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":235,"f":"F3902"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G1","u":18,"label":"Gastroschisis: lateral body folds fail to fuse","slides":[{"src":"sl/G001-5.jpg","cap":"Lateral folding: somatic lateral plate mesoderm wraps around to close body wall","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 5"},{"src":"sl/G001-27.jpg","cap":"Gastroschisis photo and cause: bilateral somatic lateral plate mesoderm fails to fuse","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 27"},{"src":"sl/G002-9.jpg","cap":"Prenatal ultrasound: bowel floating free outside the fetal abdomen","where":"2026 Middletown clicker, slide 9"}],"q":[{"id":"G002-7-1","s":"Ultrasound examination of a pregnant woman at 32 weeks gestation reveals fetal bowel loops freely floating in the amniotic cavity without a covering membrane. This condition is diagnosed as gastroschisis, which results from a defect in the formation and fusion of which of the following embryonic structures?","o":["A. Head fold","B. Tail fold","C. Neural folds","D. Lateral body folds","E. Primitive umbilical ring"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Lateral body folds","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Lateral body folds.</b> They carry the somatic lateral plate mesoderm forward to close the front abdominal wall. If they fail to fuse, bowel spills out beside the umbilicus with <b>no covering membrane</b>: gastroschisis.<br>A. Head fold: closes the upper chest wall; failure can leave the heart outside the chest (ectopia cordis).<br>B. Tail fold: closes the lower wall; failure is linked to bladder exstrophy.<br>C. Neural folds: form the neural tube.<br>E. Primitive umbilical ring: midgut failing to return through it gives omphalocele.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 27 (Clinical Correlates)","GI Development slide 5 (Lateral Folding)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown clicker","n":"","sl":7,"f":"G002"},{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":79,"f":"F0008"}],"t":1},{"id":"D042-34-1","s":"A researcher is studying congenital abdominal wall defects in a 5-week-old embryo. He notes a failure of the somatic lateral plate mesoderm to migrate and fuse at the midline during development. Which of the following additional findings would most likely be present in this embryo?","o":["A. Persistence of an open connection between the midgut and yolk sac","B. Intestinal loops herniating lateral to the umbilicus without a protective membrane","C. Failure of the cloacal membrane to rupture","D. Formation of a blind-ending esophageal pouch due to failure of recanalization","E. Loss of peritoneal covering over the ascending colon"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Intestinal loops herniating lateral to the umbilicus without a protective membrane","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Bowel herniating beside the umbilicus with no membrane.</b> Failure of the somatic lateral plate mesoderm to fuse leaves a full-thickness wall gap: <b>gastroschisis</b>. There is no amnion sac, so the bowel lies bare.<br>A. Open midgut to yolk sac link: vitelline fistula (Meckel family).<br>C. Cloacal membrane not rupturing: imperforate anus.<br>D. Blind esophageal pouch: esophageal atresia.<br>E. Ascending colon losing its back peritoneum: normal secondary retroperitoneal fusion.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 27 (Clinical Correlates)","GI Development slide 5 (Lateral Folding)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI module TA review","sl":34,"f":"D042"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G1","u":18,"label":"Bare bowel beside umbilicus means gastroschisis","slides":[{"src":"sl/G001-27.jpg","cap":"Gastroschisis photo: bare bowel beside umbilicus; omphalocele sac above for contrast","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 27"},{"src":"sl/F3903-255.jpg","cap":"Omphalocele (sac, midline, trisomy) versus gastroschisis (no sac, lateral)","where":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker, slide 255"}],"q":[{"id":"F3901-98-1","s":"A baby was born with its abdominal viscera protruding out from its abdomen, the viscera covered by nothing and completely exposed to the outside world. Name the condition.","o":["A. Congenital Omphalocele","B. Umbilical Hernia","C. Meckel’s Diverticulum","D. Gastroschisis","E. Midgut Volvulus"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Gastroschisis","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Gastroschisis.</b> Viscera covered by <b>nothing</b> means a full-thickness gap in the body wall, from failed fusion of the lateral folds.<br>A. Omphalocele: covered by amnion and peritoneum.<br>B. Umbilical hernia: covered by skin.<br>C. Meckel's diverticulum: ileal pouch inside the abdomen.<br>E. Midgut volvulus: bowel twisted inside the abdomen; bilious vomiting.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 27 (Clinical Correlates)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":98,"f":"F3901"}]},{"id":"F1156-125-1","s":"A baby is born and the firs thing the doctors notice is bowel loops on the outside of the baby, specifically herniated through the anterior abdominal wall. There is no seal around the intestines. This malformation is known as what?","o":["A. TEF","B. Gastroschisis","C. Omphalocele","D. Umbilical hernia","E. Indirect hernia","F. Cushing’s syndrome"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Gastroschisis","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Gastroschisis.</b> Bowel outside the front wall with <b>no seal or sac</b> is gastroschisis, from failed fusion of the lateral body folds.<br>A. TEF (tracheoesophageal fistula): an abnormal link between trachea and esophagus.<br>C. Omphalocele: bowel sealed in an amnion sac at the umbilicus.<br>D. Umbilical hernia: skin-covered.<br>E. Indirect hernia: through the deep inguinal ring into the inguinal canal.<br>F. Cushing's syndrome: too much cortisol.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 27 (Clinical Correlates)"],"src":[{"t":"? TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":125,"f":"F1156"}]},{"id":"F3902-233-1","s":"You are doing an peds rotation at Garnet Hospital and your attending asks you to evaluate a patient that was born just a few days ago with a malformity. You see the pts intestines are herniated through the abdominal wall and you see the intestines are situated lateral to the umbilicus. What is the pt suffering with ?","o":["A. Omphalocele","B. Gastroschisis","C. Polyhydramnios","D. Meckel's Diverticulum","E. Pyloric stenosis"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Gastroschisis","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Gastroschisis.</b> Bowel that comes out <b>beside (lateral to) the umbilicus</b>, usually on the right, is gastroschisis. The cord itself is normal.<br>A. Omphalocele: bowel comes out through the umbilicus, inside a sac.<br>C. Polyhydramnios: too much amniotic fluid in pregnancy, not a wall defect.<br>D. Meckel's diverticulum: ileal pouch inside the abdomen.<br>E. Pyloric stenosis: thick pyloric muscle; projectile vomiting.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 27 (Clinical Correlates)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":233,"f":"F3902"}]},{"id":"F3903-253-1","s":"A plastic surgeon is performing reconstructive surgery on the anterior abdominal wall of a female neonate who was born with gastroschisis. The loops of intestines are not severely strangulated and can be fully saved. The surgeon asks one of the residents which of the following statements is true about gastroschisis:","o":["A. The intestines herniate into the umbilical cord","B. It is usually associated with a trisomy","C. It is caused due to improper resorption of the midgut","D. The intestines are covered by a membrane","E. The intestines herniate lateral to the umbilicus"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"The intestines herniate lateral to the umbilicus","a":"E","why":"<b>E. The intestines herniate lateral to the umbilicus.</b> Gastroschisis is a wall gap beside the umbilicus, usually on the right, from failed fusion of the lateral folds.<br>A. Into the umbilical cord: omphalocele.<br>B. Trisomy: goes with omphalocele, not gastroschisis.<br>C. Improper 'resorption' of the midgut: not a real cause; failed return of the midgut is omphalocele.<br>D. Covered by a membrane: omphalocele.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 27 (Clinical Correlates)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":253,"f":"F3903"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3900-251-1","s":"You finally get some sleep, but wake up in a cold sweat after a nightmare where Professor Rosenthal asks you a question in front of the class and you get it wrong. If she asked for a fact about gastroschisis, what was your wrong answer?","o":["A. Gastroschisis is a congenital anomaly where abdominal organs herniate through a defect in the abdominal wall with no covering.","B. Gastroschisis is typically associated with a herniation of the liver and intestines.","C. Gastroschisis is caused by a failure of the anterior abdominal wall to close properly.","D. Unlike omphalocele, gastroschisis is not associated with trisomies.","E. Gastroschisis is apparent at birth and has a 5-10% mortality rate.."],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Gastroschisis is typically associated with a herniation of the liver and intestines.","a":"B","why":"<b>B is the false statement.</b> In gastroschisis only bowel spills out; the <b>liver stays inside</b>. A liver in the sac points to omphalocele.<br>A. True: no peritoneum or skin covers the bowel.<br>C. True: the front wall fails to close because the lateral plate mesoderm does not fuse.<br>D. True: trisomies go with omphalocele, not gastroschisis.<br>E. True: seen at birth; the lecture gives five to ten percent mortality.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 27 (Clinical Correlates)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":251,"f":"F3900"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G1","u":19,"label":"Umbilical hernia is skin-covered; omphalocele is not","slides":[{"src":"sl/G003-24.jpg","cap":"Umbilical hernia: weak wall plus raised abdominal pressure; also in adults","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 24"},{"src":"sl/F3901-100.jpg","cap":"Umbilical hernia covered by skin; gastroschisis has no covering","where":"2021 Middletown TA review, slide 100"},{"src":"sl/G001-27.jpg","cap":"Omphalocele: amnion-covered sac with no skin or muscle","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 27"}],"q":[{"id":"F3900-253-1","s":"In order to redeem your dream self, you find Professor Rosenthal the next day and ask her to pimp you on some GI embryo. She asks what differentiates an umbilical hernia from an omphalocele. You (correctly) answer:","o":["A. Umbilical hernias are covered by subcutaneous tissue and skin, whereas omphaloceles are covered by amnion without skin or muscle.","B. Umbilical hernias are often caused by weakness of the lateral abdominal muscles.","C. Umbilical hernias are a result of improper return of the intestines during  physiological herniation.","D. Adults cannot develop umbilical hernias, bu obesity is a risk factor for developing an omphalocele as an adult."],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Umbilical hernias are covered by subcutaneous tissue and skin, whereas omphaloceles are covered by amnion without skin or muscle.","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Skin versus amnion.</b> An umbilical hernia pushes through a weak umbilical ring after the bowel has returned, so it is covered by <b>subcutaneous tissue and skin</b>. An omphalocele is bowel that never returned, covered only by <b>amnion</b> (no skin, no muscle).<br>B. Lateral muscles: the weak spot is the umbilical ring, plus raised abdominal pressure.<br>C. Failed return of the intestines: that is omphalocele.<br>D. Reversed: adults do get umbilical hernias (women, obesity); omphalocele is only congenital.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 27 (Clinical Correlates)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 24 (Clinical Correlates)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":253,"f":"F3900"}],"t":1},{"id":"D041-75-1","s":"A 3-month-old boy is admitted to the hospital with an abnormal mass of tissue protruding from his abdomen. An MRI examination reveals that the mass contains some greater omentum and some small intestine. The abnormal mass protrudes when the infant cries, strains, and coughs. Which of the following conditions will most likely explain the symptoms?","o":["A. Umbilical hernia","B. Omphalocele","C. Gastroschisis","D. Epigastric hernia","E. Indirect inguinal hernia"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Umbilical hernia","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Umbilical hernia.</b> A three-month-old whose omentum and small bowel bulge when he cries or strains has a weak umbilical ring. The bulge is covered by <b>skin and subcutaneous tissue</b>.<br>B. Omphalocele: present at birth, in an amnion sac with no skin.<br>C. Gastroschisis: bare bowel beside the umbilicus at birth.<br>D. Epigastric hernia: through the linea alba above the umbilicus, mostly in adults.<br>E. Indirect inguinal hernia: through the deep inguinal ring toward the scrotum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 24 (Clinical Correlates)","GI Development slide 27 (Clinical Correlates)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":75,"f":"D041"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G1","u":20,"label":"Meckel's diverticulum: leftover vitelline duct on the ileum","slides":[{"src":"sl/G001-28.jpg","cap":"Ileal diverticulum: free pouch, fibrous cord, or open umbilical fistula","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 28"},{"src":"sl/G001-24.jpg","cap":"Panel A: omphaloenteric (vitelline) duct joins midgut loop to yolk sac","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 24"}],"q":[{"id":"F3902-220-1","s":"After explaining to the parent of the previous patient what a “Meckel’s Diverticulum” is, your attending takes it a step further and decides to pimp you on what causes it. As the Touro educated physician you are, you proudly answer saying, “it’s a remnant of the …”","o":["A. Vitelline duct or yolk sac","B. Rathke's pouch","C. Round ligament","D. Umbilical vein"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Vitelline duct or yolk sac","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Vitelline duct (yolk stalk).</b> The midgut loop is tied to the yolk sac by the vitelline duct. If its gut end does not disappear, it leaves an outpouching of the ileum: <b>Meckel's diverticulum</b>.<br>B. Rathke's pouch: pocket in the roof of the primitive mouth; makes the front pituitary.<br>C. Round ligament: of the liver, it is the leftover umbilical vein; of the uterus, the leftover gubernaculum.<br>D. Umbilical vein: becomes the round ligament of the liver.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 23 (Herniation and Rotation)","GI Development slide 28 (Meckel’s Diverticulum)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":220,"f":"F3902"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3900-227-1","s":"How does meckel’s diverticulum form?","o":["A. Failure of the gut tube to recanalize","B. Failure of midgut to return into the body cavity","C. Failure of the anterior abdominal wall to properly fuse","D. Outgrowth of the ilium forms a fistula with the umbilicus","E. Blockage of the GI tract which renders the fetus incapable of swallowing the amniotic fluid"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Outgrowth of the ilium forms a fistula with the umbilicus","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Outgrowth of the ileum that forms a fistula with the umbilicus.</b> Meckel's diverticulum is a pouch of the ileum left by the vitelline duct. If the duct stays open to the umbilicus, it is a <b>fistula</b> that can carry stool there. (The option's 'ilium' means ileum.)<br>A. Failed recanalization: atresia or stenosis.<br>B. Midgut failing to return: omphalocele.<br>C. Wall failing to fuse: gastroschisis.<br>E. Fetus unable to swallow amniotic fluid: polyhydramnios.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 28 (Meckel’s Diverticulum)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":227,"f":"F3900"}],"t":1},{"id":"F1180-279-1","s":"A 12-year-old boy was admitted to the hospital with massive rectal bleeding. Upon inspection, the color of the blood ranged from bright to dark red. The child appeared to be free of any pain. Radiographic examination revealed an ileal (Meckel's) diverticulum. Which of the following is the underlying embryologic cause of this condition?","o":["A. Failure of yolk stalk to regress","B. Duplication of the intestine","C. Malrotation of the cecum and appendix","D. Nonrotation of the midgut","E. Herniation of the intestines"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Failure of yolk stalk to regress","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Failure of the yolk stalk to regress.</b> A leftover vitelline duct (yolk stalk) forms Meckel's diverticulum on the ileum. It often holds stomach lining, whose acid ulcerates nearby ileum and causes <b>painless rectal bleeding</b> in children.<br>B. Duplication: a separate cyst or tube of bowel, not a stalk remnant.<br>C. Malrotation of cecum and appendix: wrong position, not a pouch.<br>D. Nonrotation: small bowel right, colon left.<br>E. Herniation: the normal week-six event.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 28 (Meckel’s Diverticulum)","GI Development slide 23 (Herniation and Rotation)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":279,"f":"F1180"},{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":218,"f":"F1178"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G1","u":21,"label":"Stool or discharge from umbilicus means Meckel's fistula","slides":[{"src":"sl/G001-28.jpg","cap":"Panel C: open omphaloenteric fistula from ileal diverticulum to umbilicus","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 28"}],"q":[{"id":"F1156-101-1","s":"Meckel's diverticulum is which of the following?","o":["A. The stomach wall not fully closing","B. Fecal matter through the umbilical through a fistula","C. Perforation of the umbilical cord during birth","D. Descending large intestine perforation and spilling of fecal matter into abdominal cavity"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Fecal matter through the umbilical through a fistula","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Stool reaching the umbilicus through a fistula.</b> This is the fistula form of Meckel's diverticulum: the vitelline duct stays open from the ileum to the umbilicus. Other forms are a free ileal pouch or a fibrous cord.<br>A. Stomach wall not closing: not a described defect.<br>C. Cord perforation at birth: unrelated.<br>D. Descending colon perforation: stool spills into the belly (peritonitis), not out the umbilicus.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 28 (Meckel’s Diverticulum)"],"src":[{"t":"? TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":101,"f":"F1156"}]},{"id":"F3903-289-1","s":"A 3-month-old baby girl presents with a swollen umbilicus that has failed to heal normally. The umbilicus drains secretions, and there is passage of fecal material through the umbilicus at times. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["A. Omphalocele","B. Gastroschisis","C. Anal agenesis","D. Meckel's diverticulum","E. Intestinal stenosis"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Meckel's diverticulum","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Meckel's diverticulum.</b> When the vitelline duct stays open from the ileum to the umbilicus (a vitelline fistula), stool and secretions drain from an <b>umbilicus that will not heal</b>.<br>A. Omphalocele: bowel in a sac at birth.<br>B. Gastroschisis: bare bowel beside the umbilicus at birth.<br>C. Anal agenesis: no anus; stool may leave by a fistula to the perineum or urinary tract, not the umbilicus.<br>E. Intestinal stenosis: a narrowing; vomiting and bloating.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 28 (Meckel’s Diverticulum)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":289,"f":"F3903"},{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":83,"f":"F0008"},{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":218,"f":"F3902"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":31,"f":"F3901"},{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":185,"f":"F1252"},{"t":"2019 review","n":"old Exam 2 review: abdomen part only","sl":307,"f":"F1228"},{"t":"? tutor review","n":"old Exam 2: abdomen part only","sl":203,"f":"F1243"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3900-225-1","s":"The mother of a 3 month old boy notices foul smelling drainage coming from his umbilicus. Which developmental defect could be present?","o":["A. Omphalocele","B. Gastroschisis","C. Meckel’s Diverticulum","D. Duodenal stenosis","E. Imperforate anus"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Meckel’s Diverticulum","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Meckel's diverticulum.</b> Foul drainage from the umbilicus in an infant points to a vitelline duct still open between the <b>ileum and the umbilicus</b>.<br>A. Omphalocele: sac of bowel at birth.<br>B. Gastroschisis: bare bowel at birth.<br>D. Duodenal stenosis: narrow duodenum; vomiting.<br>E. Imperforate anus: no anal opening; no meconium.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 28 (Meckel’s Diverticulum)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":225,"f":"F3900"}]},{"id":"F3903-257-1","s":"While feeding her one month old neonate, a mother observes a small lump in the baby’s lower right hand quadrant. When she gently palpates it, there seems to be some discharge from the umbilicus. She rushes the baby to the pediatrician. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["A. Meckel’s diverticulum","B. Megacolon","C. Gastroschisis","D. Colic atresia","E. Omphalocoele"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Meckel’s diverticulum","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Meckel's diverticulum.</b> A lump in the right lower quadrant plus <b>discharge from the umbilicus</b> fits an ileal pouch still joined to the umbilicus by the vitelline duct.<br>B. Megacolon (Hirschsprung disease): constipation and a swollen belly.<br>C. Gastroschisis: bare bowel at birth.<br>D. Colic (colon) atresia: blocked colon; no stool.<br>E. Omphalocoele: bowel in a sac at the umbilicus at birth.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 28 (Meckel’s Diverticulum)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":257,"f":"F3903"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G1","u":22,"label":"Hindgut derivatives: last third transverse colon to rectum","slides":[{"src":"sl/G001-29.jpg","cap":"Hindgut list: distal third transverse colon to upper anal canal; inferior mesenteric artery","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 29"},{"src":"sl/D041-135.jpg","cap":"Foregut, midgut and hindgut derivative lists with their arteries","where":"2025 TA review, slide 135"}],"q":[{"id":"D041-123-1","s":"Which of the following structures is derived from the hindgut portion of the embryonic gut tube?","o":["A. Duodenum","B. Jejunum","C. Transverse colon (proximal two-thirds)","D. Descending colon"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Descending colon","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Descending colon.</b> Hindgut makes the last third of the transverse colon, the <b>descending colon</b>, sigmoid colon, rectum, upper anal canal and urogenital sinus. It is fed by the inferior mesenteric artery.<br>A. Duodenum: foregut above the bile duct opening, midgut below.<br>B. Jejunum: midgut.<br>C. First two-thirds of transverse colon: midgut.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 29 (Hindgut)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":123,"f":"D041"}],"t":1},{"id":"F1252-134-1","s":"Which of the following structures is derived from the hindgut?","o":["A. Duodenum","B. Cecum","C. ⅔ of transverse colon","D. Sigmoid colon","E. Ascending colon"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Sigmoid colon","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Sigmoid colon.</b> It lies between the descending colon and rectum; all three are <b>hindgut</b>, fed by the inferior mesenteric artery.<br>A. Duodenum: foregut and midgut.<br>B. Cecum: midgut.<br>C. Two-thirds of transverse colon: midgut; only the last third is hindgut.<br>E. Ascending colon: midgut.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 29 (Hindgut)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":134,"f":"F1252"}]},{"id":"F3900-186-1","s":"A 45-year patient presents with rectal bleeding and changes in bowel habits. Colonoscopy reveals a polyp in the descending colon. Which embryonic gut tube portion primarily contributes to the development of the affected area of the colon.","o":["A. Foregut","B. Midgut","C. Hindgut","D. Allantois"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Hindgut","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Hindgut.</b> The <b>descending colon</b> is hindgut, along with the last third of the transverse colon, sigmoid colon, rectum and upper anal canal.<br>A. Foregut: esophagus to the bile duct opening in the duodenum.<br>B. Midgut: lower duodenum to two-thirds of the transverse colon.<br>D. Allantois: a pouch off the cloaca into the cord; becomes the urachus (median umbilical ligament).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 29 (Hindgut)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":186,"f":"F3900"}]},{"id":"D042-69-1","s":"A patient with ulcerative colitis has inflammation limited to rectum and colon up to the splenic flexure. These regions share a common embryologic origin from the","o":["A. Foregut","B. Midgut","C. Hindgut","D. Allantois"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Hindgut","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Hindgut.</b> Ulcerative colitis from the rectum up to the <b>splenic flexure</b> covers the rectum, sigmoid and descending colon and the far end of the transverse colon. All are hindgut, fed by the inferior mesenteric artery.<br>A. Foregut: esophagus to upper duodenum.<br>B. Midgut: stops at two-thirds of the transverse colon.<br>D. Allantois: becomes the urachus, not colon.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 29 (Hindgut)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI module TA review","sl":69,"f":"D042"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3901-40-1","s":"The mother of an 8 month old noticed that as soon as she started feeding her child solid food he seemed to have problems with defecation. The pediatrician determined that the child had underdevelopment of rectum. What other organs could possibly be underdeveloped as well?","o":["A. Liver","B. Spleen","C. Ascending colon","D. Descending colon","E. Cecum"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Descending colon","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Descending colon.</b> The rectum is hindgut, so look for another <b>hindgut</b> organ. Only the descending colon is hindgut in this list.<br>A. Liver: foregut.<br>B. Spleen: from mesoderm in the dorsal mesogastrium, not part of the gut tube.<br>C. Ascending colon: midgut.<br>E. Cecum: midgut.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 29 (Hindgut)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":40,"f":"F3901"}]},{"id":"D045-165-1","s":"A 2-month-old presents with chronic constipation and dilated colon proximal to a narrowed segment. The affected portion derives from which region of the gut tube?","o":["A. Foregut","B. Midgut","C. Hindgut","D. Cloacal membrane"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Hindgut","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Hindgut.</b> Chronic constipation with a dilated colon above a narrow segment is Hirschsprung disease. The narrow segment is usually the <b>rectum and sigmoid colon</b>, which are hindgut.<br>A. Foregut: esophagus to upper duodenum.<br>B. Midgut: small bowel to two-thirds of the transverse colon.<br>D. Cloacal membrane: the membrane that closes the end of the hindgut; not a gut region.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 32 (Clinical Correlates)","GI Development slide 29 (Hindgut)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":165,"f":"D045"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G1","u":23,"label":"Midgut-hindgut junction: both mesenteric arteries meet","slides":[{"src":"sl/G001-22.jpg","cap":"Midgut runs to two-thirds of transverse colon; superior mesenteric artery","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 22"},{"src":"sl/G001-29.jpg","cap":"Hindgut starts at distal third of transverse colon; inferior mesenteric artery","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 29"}],"q":[{"id":"D045-176-1","s":"A 35-year-old male undergoes surgery for a tumor at the junction of the midgut and hindgut derivatives. Which of the following vessels supplies this region?","o":["A. Superior mesenteric artery","B. Inferior mesenteric artery","C. Celiac trunk","D. Internal iliac artery"],"g":["A","B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Inferior mesenteric artery","a":"B","why":"<b>A and B. Both arteries.</b> The midgut-hindgut junction is in the <b>last third of the transverse colon</b>, near the splenic flexure. The midgut side gets the superior mesenteric artery (SMA) through its middle colic branch; the hindgut side gets the inferior mesenteric artery (IMA) through its left colic branch. They meet here. If asked only for the distal third, pick the IMA.<br>C. Celiac trunk: foregut.<br>D. Internal iliac artery: pelvis; gives the middle rectal artery.","ex":"","fl":["The source keyed only the IMA (inferior mesenteric artery), but the midgut-hindgut junction in the distal transverse colon is where the SMA's (superior mesenteric artery's) middle colic and the IMA's left colic branches meet, so the SMA is equally right."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 29 (Hindgut)","GI Development slide 7 (Blood supply)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":176,"f":"D045"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G1","u":23,"label":"Midgut sympathetics: splanchnics to superior mesenteric ganglion","slides":[{"src":"sl/G005-54.jpg","cap":"Jejunum and ileum sympathetics: thoracic eight to ten via greater, lesser, least splanchnics","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 54"},{"src":"sl/D042-99.jpg","cap":"Table: midgut sympathetics are lesser splanchnic to superior mesenteric ganglion","where":"2025 TA review, slide 99"}],"q":[{"id":"D042-97-1","s":"A patient undergoes surgical removal of a tumor located in the jejunum. During the procedure, the surgeon needs to ligate arteries near the root of the mesentery. To prevent excessive visceral pain signals, a targeted nerve block is performed at the ganglion that supplies this region. Targeting which of the following would be help block pain signals through the jejunum’s sympathetic fibers.","o":["A. Greater splanchnic nerve","B. Lesser splanchnic nerve","C. Lumbar splanchnic nerves","D. Pelvic splanchnic nerves","E. Vagus nerves"],"g":["A","B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Lesser splanchnic nerve","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Lesser splanchnic nerve (A also accepted).</b> The jejunum is midgut. The classic rule is midgut = <b>lesser splanchnic</b> (thoracic segments ten and eleven) to the superior mesenteric ganglion. The 2026 lecture says jejunal sympathetics come from thoracic segments eight to ten through the greater, lesser and least splanchnic nerves, so A also counts.<br>C. Lumbar splanchnic: hindgut.<br>D. Pelvic splanchnic: parasympathetic; hindgut.<br>E. Vagus: parasympathetic, not sympathetic.","ex":"","fl":["Key B follows the midgut = lesser splanchnic (T10-T11) rule the TA table uses. Note the 2026 small-intestine lecture says jejunum/ileum sympathetics come from T8-T10 'carried by greater, lesser and least splanchnic nerves' (G005:54), so A is not strictly wrong there; B is the best single answer."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 54 (Innervation)","ANS slide 27 (| Splanchnic Nerves | Autonomic Fiber Type | System | Origin | Destination |)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI module TA review","sl":97,"f":"D042"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G1","u":24,"label":"Urorectal septum divides cloaca; defect causes fistula","slides":[{"src":"sl/G001-30.jpg","cap":"Urorectal septum splits cloaca into bladder in front, rectum behind","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 30"},{"src":"sl/G001-32.jpg","cap":"Urorectal fistula diagram from abnormal urorectal septum","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 32"},{"src":"sl/G002-14.jpg","cap":"Normal cloacal partition by urorectal septum versus rectovaginal fistula","where":"2026 Middletown clicker, slide 14"}],"q":[{"id":"D041-126-1","s":"During embryonic development, the cloaca is divided into the urogenital sinus and the anorectal canal by the growth of which structure?","o":["A. Allantois","B. Urorectal septum","C. Vitelline duct","D. Mesonephric ridge"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Urorectal septum","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Urorectal septum.</b> This wedge of mesoderm grows down between the allantois and the hindgut. It splits the cloaca into the <b>urogenital sinus</b> in front (bladder, urethra) and the <b>anorectal canal</b> behind.<br>A. Allantois: pouch into the cord; becomes the urachus.<br>C. Vitelline duct: joins the midgut to the yolk sac.<br>D. Mesonephric ridge: makes the embryonic kidney and male genital ducts.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 30 (Cloaca and Cloacal Division)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":126,"f":"D041"}]},{"id":"D045-168-1","s":"A newborn has a rectovaginal fistula. This abnormality most likely results from defective development of which embryonic structure?","o":["A. Allantois","B. Urorectal septum","C. Proctodeum","D. Cloacal Membrane"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Urorectal septum","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Urorectal septum.</b> If the septum does not fully divide the cloaca, the rectum stays joined to the front urogenital part: a <b>rectovaginal fistula</b> in girls, a urorectal (rectourethral) one in boys.<br>A. Allantois: becomes the urachus; defects give a urachal fistula or cyst.<br>C. Proctodeum: the anal pit; defects give anal stenosis or agenesis.<br>D. Cloacal membrane: failure to rupture gives imperforate anus.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 32 (Clinical Correlates)","GI Development slide 30 (Cloaca and Cloacal Division)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":168,"f":"D045"}],"t":1},{"id":"G002-13-1","s":"A newborn female is examined in the neonatal intensive care unit shortly after delivery. The delivery nurse notes that the infant has passed meconium, but it appears to be emerging entirely from the vaginal vestibule. Physical examination confirms the absence of a normal anal opening at the perineum. Which of the following developmental processes most likely failed during embryogenesis to cause this presentation?","o":["A. Recanalization of the anorectal canal lumen","B. Fusion of the lateral mesonephric ducts","C. Partitioning of the cloaca by the urorectal septum","D. Migration of neural crest cells into the hindgut wall","E. Invagination of the proctodeum surface ectoderm"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Partitioning of the cloaca by the urorectal septum","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Partitioning of the cloaca by the urorectal septum.</b> Meconium coming out of the vaginal vestibule with no anus means the rectum still opens into the front (urogenital) part. That is a <b>rectovestibular fistula</b> from incomplete septum growth.<br>A. Anorectal recanalization: not how the anus opens; the anal membrane ruptures.<br>B. Mesonephric ducts: male genital ducts; they do not fuse.<br>D. Neural crest migration: Hirschsprung disease; anus normal.<br>E. Proctodeum invagination: explains a missing anus, but not a rectum opening into the vestibule.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 32 (Clinical Correlates)","GI Development slide 30 (Cloaca and Cloacal Division)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown clicker","n":"","sl":13,"f":"G002"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G1","u":25,"label":"Anal membrane must rupture; else imperforate anus","slides":[{"src":"sl/G001-30.jpg","cap":"Panel E labels the anal membrane closing the anal canal","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 30"},{"src":"sl/G001-32.jpg","cap":"Imperforate anus: anal membrane stays between rectum and anal pit","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 32"},{"src":"sl/D045-170.jpg","cap":"Urorectal septum splits cloacal membrane into urogenital and anal membranes","where":"2025 Montana TA review, slide 170"}],"q":[{"id":"D042-93-1","s":"A newborn is found to have an imperforate anus. Which embryologic event most likely failed?","o":["A. Rotation of the midgut","B. Recanalization of the esophagus","C. Rupture of the cloacal membrane","D. Failure of the vitelline duct to close"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Rupture of the cloacal membrane","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Rupture of the cloacal membrane.</b> The anal part of the cloacal membrane must break down around week eight so the rectum opens to the outside. If it stays intact, the anus is <b>imperforate</b>.<br>A. Midgut rotation: malrotation or volvulus.<br>B. Esophageal recanalization: esophageal atresia; polyhydramnios.<br>D. Vitelline duct not closing: Meckel's diverticulum or fistula.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 32 (Clinical Correlates)","GI Development slide 30 (Cloaca and Cloacal Division)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI module TA review","sl":93,"f":"D042"}],"t":1},{"id":"D045-173-1","s":"A neonate is born with an imperforate anus. This results from failure of which process?","o":["A. Rupture of the cloacal membrane","B. Migration of neural crest cells","C. Partitioning of the cloaca","D. Rotation of the hindgut"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Rupture of the cloacal membrane","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Rupture of the cloacal membrane.</b> After the urorectal septum divides it, the back part (the <b>anal membrane</b>) must rupture around week eight. If it does not, the anus has no opening.<br>B. Neural crest migration: Hirschsprung disease; the anus is open.<br>C. Cloacal partitioning: fistula between rectum and urinary tract or vagina.<br>D. Hindgut rotation: the hindgut does not rotate.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 32 (Clinical Correlates)","GI Development slide 30 (Cloaca and Cloacal Division)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":173,"f":"D045"}],"t":1},{"id":"D041-130-1","s":"A patent anal opening is formed during development when which embryonic structure ruptures?","o":["A. Cloacal membrane","B. Anal membrane","C. Urorectal septum","D. Proctodeum"],"g":["A","B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Anal membrane","a":"B","why":"<b>A or B. The anal membrane, the back part of the cloacal membrane.</b> The urorectal septum splits the cloacal membrane into a front urogenital membrane and a back <b>anal membrane</b>, which ruptures around week eight to open the anus. The 2026 lecture just says the cloacal membrane ruptures, so both are accepted.<br>C. Urorectal septum: divides the cloaca; never ruptures.<br>D. Proctodeum: the ectodermal pit that meets the membrane; it does not rupture.","ex":"","fl":["Key B (anal membrane = posterior part of the cloacal membrane after the urorectal septum divides it) is the textbook answer. But the 2026 lecture words it as 'The cloacal membrane must rupture' (G001:30) and the lower 1/3 of the anal canal arises 'from the rupture of the cloacal membrane' (G001:31), so on this year's exam an option 'cloacal membrane' could be what is keyed if 'anal membrane' is absent."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 30 (Cloaca and Cloacal Division)","GI Development slide 31 (Anal Canal)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":130,"f":"D041"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3903-278-1","s":"A 3-day-old neonate has not excreted any meconium (first feces). Feeding has been normal and there has been no regurgitation or projectile vomiting. His appetite is normal but his abdomen seems slightly distended. A manual examination would reveal which of the following anomalies?","o":["A. Urorectal fistula","B. Anal stenosis","C. Imperforate anus","D. Appendicitis"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Imperforate anus","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Imperforate anus.</b> No meconium at all, normal feeding, no vomiting and a mildly swollen belly point to a <b>closed anal opening</b>. A finger exam finds no anus, or only a membrane.<br>A. Urorectal fistula: meconium passes out through the urethra.<br>B. Anal stenosis: some meconium would pass through the narrow opening.<br>D. Appendicitis: fever and pain; rare in newborns.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 32 (Clinical Correlates)","GI Development slide 30 (Cloaca and Cloacal Division)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":278,"f":"F3903"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G1","u":26,"label":"Anal canal: upper two-thirds endoderm, lower third ectoderm","slides":[{"src":"sl/G001-31.jpg","cap":"Anal canal: hindgut above pectinate line, proctodeum (ectoderm) below","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 31"},{"src":"sl/F3903-277.jpg","cap":"Upper two-thirds endoderm with autonomic nerves; lower third ectoderm, somatic","where":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker, slide 277"}],"q":[{"id":"D042-95-1","s":"The inferior one-third of the anal canal develops from which structure?","o":["A. Endoderm of the hindgut","B. Mesoderm of the cloaca","C. Ectoderm of the proctodeum","D. Allantois"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Ectoderm of the proctodeum","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Ectoderm of the proctodeum.</b> The lower third of the anal canal, below the pectinate line, comes from the anal pit (proctodeum), which is <b>ectoderm</b>. The upper two-thirds comes from hindgut endoderm.<br>A. Hindgut endoderm: upper two-thirds.<br>B. Cloacal mesoderm: the cloaca is lined by endoderm; mesoderm makes muscle and connective tissue.<br>D. Allantois: becomes the urachus.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 31 (Anal Canal)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI module TA review","sl":95,"f":"D042"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3903-285-1","s":"The simple columnar and stratified columnar epithelia lining the lower part of the anal canal above the pectinate line is derived from","o":["A. Mesoderm","B. Endoderm","C. Ectoderm","D. Neuroectoderm","E. Neural crest cells"],"g":["B"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Ectoderm","a":"C","why":"<b>B. Endoderm.</b> Lining <b>above the pectinate line</b> is hindgut endoderm, which is why it is columnar like the rest of the gut. (The old deck keyed ectoderm; that is the zone below the pectinate line.)<br>A. Mesoderm: muscle and connective tissue.<br>C. Ectoderm: below the pectinate line, from the proctodeum.<br>D. Neuroectoderm: brain and spinal cord.<br>E. Neural crest cells: gut nerve cells.","ex":"","fl":["Above the pectinate line the anal canal is hindgut (cloacal) ENDODERM, lined by columnar epithelium; ectoderm (proctodeum) is only below the pectinate line (G001:31: superior 2/3 endodermal, inferior 1/3 ectodermal; Langman/Moore)."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 31 (Anal Canal)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":285,"f":"F3903"}],"t":1},{"id":"F1228-300-1","s":"Which of the following is FALSE?","o":["A. The stomach dilates ventral-dorsal and rotates 90 degrees to the left","B. The ventral bud gives rise to the uncinate process and inferior aspect of the head of the pancreas","C. The superior ⅔ of the anal canal is ectodermal in origin","D. Ventral mesentery is the falciform ligament and lesser omentum","E. The intestines rotate a total of 270 degrees as it herniates and returns into the abdomen."],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"The superior ⅔ of the anal canal is ectodermal in origin","a":"C","why":"<b>C is false.</b> The superior two-thirds of the anal canal is <b>endodermal</b> (hindgut). Only the inferior third is ectodermal.<br>A. True as the lecture words it: the stomach widens front-to-back and rotates ninety degrees.<br>B. True: the ventral bud makes the uncinate process and lower head.<br>D. True: the ventral mesentery becomes the falciform ligament and lesser omentum.<br>E. True: total midgut rotation is two hundred seventy degrees.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 31 (Anal Canal)","GI Development slide 12 (Stomach and Stomach Rotation)","GI Development slide 20 (Pancreas)"],"src":[{"t":"2019 review","n":"old Exam 2 review: abdomen part only","sl":300,"f":"F1228"},{"t":"? tutor review","n":"old Exam 2: abdomen part only","sl":196,"f":"F1243"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G1","u":27,"label":"Hemorrhoid pain: endoderm painless above, ectoderm painful below","slides":[{"src":"sl/G001-31.jpg","cap":"Pectinate line divides hindgut endoderm (above) from proctodeum ectoderm (below)","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 31"},{"src":"sl/F3903-277.jpg","cap":"Above pectinate: endoderm, autonomic nerves; below: ectoderm, somatic nerves","where":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker, slide 277"}],"q":[{"id":"F3903-273-1","s":"A 48-year-old male rushes to the ER after noticing blood in his fecal matter. A physical exam reveals no portal hypertension and no history of localized pain. The patient does mention feeling slight discomfort while sitting. A rectal examination confirms the diagnosis of internal hemorrhoids. Which of the following explains the lack of localized pain in this diagnosis?","o":["A. Hemorrhoids are located in the endodermal aspect of the anal canal","B. Hemorrhoids are located in the ectodermal aspect of the anal canal","C. The area where internal hemorrhoids can develop is innervated by somatic nerves","D. The hindgut failed to properly fuse with the surface ectoderm","E. Hemorrhoids are located in the mesodermal aspect of the anal canal"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Hemorrhoids are located in the endodermal aspect of the anal canal","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Endodermal part of the anal canal.</b> Internal hemorrhoids lie above the pectinate line, in hindgut endoderm. It has <b>autonomic nerves only</b>, so there is no sharp, localized pain.<br>B. Ectodermal part: below the line; external hemorrhoids, painful.<br>C. Somatic nerves: supply below the line; they would cause pain.<br>D. Hindgut not fusing with ectoderm: imperforate anus.<br>E. Mesodermal part: the lining is endoderm or ectoderm, not mesoderm.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 31 (Anal Canal)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":273,"f":"F3903"}],"t":1},{"id":"F1156-122-1","s":"A 67 year old female with a past history of HTN, hyperlipidemia, DM and constipation, presents to her GI for the concern of small amounts of rectal bleeding that she notices after she strains to use the bathroom. When asked if she has any pain from the bleeding she states that it does not hurt. You believe that the patient is experiencing a flare of her hemorrhoids. What is the most likely embryologic origin of the location of the patients symptoms?","o":["A. Endoderm","B. Ectoderm","C. Mesoderm","D. Neural crest cells","E. Lateral splanchnic mesoderm"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Endoderm","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Endoderm.</b> Painless bleeding means internal hemorrhoids, above the pectinate line. That part of the anal canal is hindgut <b>endoderm</b> with only autonomic (visceral) nerves, which do not carry sharp pain.<br>B. Ectoderm: below the pectinate line; painful.<br>C. Mesoderm: muscle and vessels, not the lining.<br>D. Neural crest cells: nerve cells.<br>E. Lateral splanchnic mesoderm: smooth muscle and connective tissue of the gut wall.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 31 (Anal Canal)"],"src":[{"t":"? TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":122,"f":"F1156"}]},{"id":"F3901-75-1","s":"A 30-year-old male presents to your office with the complaint of blood on the toilet paper after defecation,  but claims that he does not feel any pain. You diagnose him with hemorrhoids. Which of the following is true about his diagnosis?","o":["A. He has external hemorrhoids, which is why he does not experience any pain","B. He has internal hemorrhoids, which are below the pectinate line","C. He has external hemorrhoids, which are at a part of the rectum that is endodermal in origin","D. He has internal hemorrhoids, which are at a part of the rectum that is endodermal in origin","E. He has external hemorrhoids, which are above the pectinate line"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"He has internal hemorrhoids, which are at a part of the rectum that is endodermal in origin","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Internal hemorrhoids, in endodermal tissue.</b> Painless bleeding means <b>internal</b> hemorrhoids, above the pectinate line, in endoderm with visceral (autonomic) nerves.<br>A. External hemorrhoids are painful, not painless.<br>B. Internal hemorrhoids are above, not below, the line.<br>C. External hemorrhoids are in ectoderm.<br>E. External hemorrhoids are below the line.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 31 (Anal Canal)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":75,"f":"F3901"}]},{"id":"F3900-248-1","s":"A stressed med student has been studying so hard for anatomy that they forgot to drink water for several days. They have been straining to force bowel movements, and noted blood on the toilet paper the last time they were in the bathroom. Luckily, their studying has paid off and they know that since there was no pain, the following is true about their hemorrhoids:","o":["A. The blood supply to that region is provided by the inferior rectal artery.","B. They have external hemorrhoids.","C. Their hemorrhoids originate from the midgut above the pectinate line","D. Their hemorrhoids will become painful in the next couple of days","E. Their hemorrhoids are endodermal in origin"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Their hemorrhoids are endodermal in origin","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Endodermal in origin.</b> No pain means internal hemorrhoids above the pectinate line. That lining is hindgut <b>endoderm</b> with visceral nerves, and its blood comes from the superior rectal artery, a branch of the inferior mesenteric artery.<br>A. Inferior rectal artery: supplies below the line (external).<br>B. External hemorrhoids: painful.<br>C. Midgut: the anal canal is hindgut, not midgut.<br>D. They stay painless unless they prolapse or clot.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 31 (Anal Canal)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":248,"f":"F3900"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3902-238-1","s":"A 57 year old male comes into your clinic complaining of pain around his anal region. He has no significant past medical history and said he just came from a vacation and tried a variety of cultural entrees. You suspect a diagnosis of hemorrhoids. What is the tissue origin and innervation of the region where he is feeling pain?","o":["A. Motor nerves , mesoderm","B. Autonomic nerves, endoderm","C. Autonomic nerves, ectoderm","D. Somatic nerves, ectoderm","E. Somatic nerves, endoderm"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Somatic nerves, ectoderm","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Somatic nerves, ectoderm.</b> Painful hemorrhoids are external, below the pectinate line. That part comes from the ectodermal proctodeum and has <b>somatic sensory nerves</b> (inferior rectal nerve), which feel sharp pain.<br>A. Motor nerves, mesoderm: wrong nerve type and wrong layer.<br>B. Autonomic nerves, endoderm: the painless area above the line.<br>C. Autonomic nerves, ectoderm: mismatched pair.<br>E. Somatic nerves, endoderm: mismatched pair.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 31 (Anal Canal)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":238,"f":"F3902"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G1","u":28,"label":"Hirschsprung: neural crest fails, ganglion cells absent","slides":[{"src":"sl/G001-32.jpg","cap":"Megacolon: no postganglionic parasympathetic cell bodies; poor neural crest migration","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 32"},{"src":"sl/G002-11.jpg","cap":"Hirschsprung disease: narrow distal segment with dilated colon above","where":"2026 Middletown clicker, slide 11"},{"src":"sl/G002-12.jpg","cap":"Gut wall layers showing submucosal (Meissner) and myenteric (Auerbach) plexuses","where":"2026 Middletown clicker, slide 12"}],"q":[{"id":"F3902-231-1","s":"You are on rotations for general surgery and a residents starts quizzing you so you are prepared for your first surgery. The resident tells you the patient has a dysfunction in the enteric nervous system since birth. What embryological tissue may have contributed to this pt’s dysfunction?","o":["A. Endoderm","B. Dorsal Mesentery","C. Mesoderm","D. Neural crest cells"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Neural crest cells","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Neural crest cells.</b> The enteric nervous system (the gut wall's own nerve plexuses) is built by <b>neural crest cells</b> that migrate down the gut. Faulty migration gives Hirschsprung disease.<br>A. Endoderm: lining and glands.<br>B. Dorsal mesentery: holds the gut and carries its vessels.<br>C. Mesoderm: smooth muscle and connective tissue.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 32 (Clinical Correlates)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":231,"f":"F3902"}],"t":1},{"id":"F1252-138-1","s":"Megacolon, or Hirschsprung's disease, is characterized by poor migration of neural crest cells causes dilation of the colon. What type of cell bodies/fibers are absent in this presentation?","o":["A. Preganglionic parasympathetic","B. Preganglionic sympathetic","C. Postganglionic parasympathetic","D. Postganglionic sympathetic"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Postganglionic parasympathetic","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Postganglionic parasympathetic.</b> The missing ganglion cells of the myenteric and submucosal plexuses are the <b>postganglionic parasympathetic</b> neuron cell bodies. Without them the segment cannot relax, so the colon above it dilates.<br>A. Preganglionic parasympathetic: come from the vagus and pelvic splanchnic nerves; present.<br>B. Preganglionic sympathetic: from the spinal cord; present.<br>D. Postganglionic sympathetic: sit in ganglia outside the gut wall; present.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 103 (Hirschsprung’s disease)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":138,"f":"F1252"}]},{"id":"G002-10-1","s":"A 4-day-old newborn male has not passed meconium since birth despite normal feeding toleration. Digital rectal examination reveals normal anal anatomy, but a tight anal canal. Upon withdrawal of the examiner's finger, there is an immediate, forceful release of flatus and liquid stool. This condition is caused by a failure of neural crest cells to migrate into which of the following structures?","o":["A. Internal anal sphincter","B. Urachus","C. Ventral mesodermal folds","D. Linea alba","E. Submucosal and myenteric plexuses (Hirschsprung disease)"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Submucosal and myenteric plexuses (Hirschsprung disease)","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Submucosal and myenteric plexuses.</b> Neural crest cells must reach the submucosal (Meissner) and myenteric (Auerbach) plexuses. If they stop short, the end segment cannot relax: no meconium, a tight canal and an explosive 'squirt' of stool after the exam.<br>A. Internal anal sphincter: smooth muscle from mesoderm; it fails to relax because its plexus lacks neurons.<br>B. Urachus: leftover allantois.<br>C. Ventral mesodermal folds: body wall.<br>D. Linea alba: midline tendon of the abdominal wall.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 32 (Clinical Correlates)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 Middletown clicker","n":"","sl":10,"f":"G002"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3903-287-1","s":"A 4-day-old baby boy has not defecated since coming home from the hospital even though feeding has been normal without any excessive vomiting. Rectal examination reveals a normal anus, anal canal, and rectum. However, a large fecal mass is found in the colon, and a large release of flatus and feces follows the rectal examination. Which of the following conditions would be suspected?","o":["A. Imperforate anus","B. Anal agenesis","C. Anorectal agenesis","D. Rectal atresia","E. Agangliononic megacolon"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Aganglionic megacolon","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Aganglionic megacolon (Hirschsprung disease).</b> A normal anus, anal canal and rectum, a big stool mass in the colon, and a gush of gas and stool after the exam fit a <b>narrow segment without nerve cells</b>.<br>A. Imperforate anus: no anal opening.<br>B. Anal agenesis: no anal canal.<br>C. Anorectal agenesis: rectum ends high; no anus.<br>D. Rectal atresia: anus present, but the finger hits a blind end.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["GI Development slide 32 (Clinical Correlates)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":287,"f":"F3903"},{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":81,"f":"F0008"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":29,"label":"Quadrants: median plane + transumbilical plane","slides":[{"src":"sl/G003-10.jpg","cap":"Median and transumbilical planes split the abdomen into four quadrants","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 10"},{"src":"sl/D045-9.jpg","cap":"Four quadrants labelled with median sagittal and transumbilical planes","where":"2025 Montana TA review, slide 9"}],"q":[{"id":"D041-5-1","s":"Which pair of anatomical reference lines is used to divide the abdomen into the four abdominal quadrants?","o":["A. Midclavicular lines and the subcostal plane","B. Transpyloric plane and the intertubercular plane","C. Median (midsagittal) plane and the transumbilical plane","D. Midaxillary lines and the transtubercular plane","E. Midsternal line and the interspinous plane"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Median (midsagittal) plane and the transumbilical plane","a":"C","why":"<b>Median plane + transumbilical plane.</b> The vertical median (midsagittal) plane splits right from left. The horizontal transumbilical plane, through the umbilicus, splits upper from lower. Together they make the four quadrants.<br>A. Midclavicular lines + subcostal plane: lines of the nine-region system.<br>B. Transpyloric + intertubercular planes: horizontal planes of the nine-region system.<br>D. Midaxillary lines + transtubercular plane: not quadrant lines.<br>E. Midsternal line + interspinous plane: the interspinous plane is a nine-region option.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 10 (Quadrants)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":5,"f":"D041"}],"t":1},{"id":"D045-5-1","s":"A patient complains of pain localized to the Right Lower Quadrant (RLQ). Which of the following best describes the surface landmark intersection that defines this quadrant?","o":["A. Right of the midsagittal plane and above the transpyloric plane","B. Left of the midsagittal plane and below the transumbilical plane","C. Right of the midsagittal plane and below the transumbilical plane","D. Right of the midclavicular line and above the subcostal plane"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Right of the midsagittal plane and below the transumbilical plane","a":"C","why":"<b>Right of the midsagittal plane and below the transumbilical plane.</b> The RLQ (right lower quadrant) is the patient's right half, below the line through the umbilicus. It holds the cecum and appendix.<br>A. The transpyloric plane is not a quadrant line, and 'above' would be upper.<br>B. Left and below: that is the LLQ (left lower quadrant).<br>D. Midclavicular line and subcostal plane belong to the nine-region system.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 10 (Quadrants)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":5,"f":"D045"}],"t":1},{"id":"D045-7-1","s":"A 25-year-old female presents with left lower abdominal pain. The physician suspects an ovarian cyst and documents the pain as “LLQ tenderness.” Which of the following surface planes is used to define this area?","o":["A. Midclavicular and subcostal planes","B. Median and transumbilical planes","C. Intertubercular and transpyloric planes","D. Transpyloric and sagittal planes through midclavicular lines"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Median and transumbilical planes","a":"B","why":"<b>Median and transumbilical planes.</b> Every quadrant, including the LLQ (left lower quadrant), is defined by the same two planes. The median plane splits left from right. The transumbilical plane splits upper from lower.<br>A. Midclavicular and subcostal planes: nine-region lines.<br>C. Intertubercular and transpyloric planes: nine-region horizontal planes.<br>D. Transpyloric and midclavicular sagittal planes: nine-region lines.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 10 (Quadrants)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":7,"f":"D045"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":30,"label":"Which organ lies in which quadrant","slides":[{"src":"sl/G003-11.jpg","cap":"Organs by quadrant: gallbladder right upper, spleen left upper, ovary left lower","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 11"},{"src":"sl/F1252-51.jpg","cap":"Referred pain: gallbladder to right shoulder, spleen to left side","where":"2020 TA review, slide 51"}],"q":[{"id":"D041-8-1","s":"A patient comes to your office complaining of right upper abdominal pain that radiates to the tip of their right shoulder. What organ is typically located in this quadrant?","o":["A. Urinary Bladder","B. Appendix","C. Gallbladder","D. Spleen","E. Sigmoid Colon"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Gallbladder","a":"C","why":"<b>Gallbladder.</b> It sits in the <b>RUQ</b> (right upper quadrant) under the liver. When inflamed it irritates the diaphragm. The phrenic nerve (C3–C5, third to fifth cervical) shares spinal levels with shoulder skin, so pain is felt at the right shoulder tip.<br>A. Urinary bladder: midline, low in the pelvis.<br>B. Appendix: RLQ (right lower quadrant).<br>D. Spleen: LUQ (left upper quadrant).<br>E. Sigmoid colon: LLQ (left lower quadrant).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 11 (Organs in Quadrants)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 10 (Quadrants)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":8,"f":"D041"}],"t":1},{"id":"D045-13-1","s":"A patient presents with acute pain localized to the Right Upper Quadrant (RUQ) that radiates to the right shoulder. Which organ is most likely affected?","o":["A. Stomach","B. Gallbladder","C. Sigmoid colon","D. Appendix"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Gallbladder","a":"B","why":"<b>Gallbladder.</b> It lies in the <b>RUQ</b> (right upper quadrant) under the liver. When inflamed it irritates the diaphragm. The phrenic nerve (C3–C5, third to fifth cervical) shares spinal levels with shoulder skin, so pain is referred to the right shoulder.<br>A. Stomach: mostly LUQ (left upper quadrant) and epigastric.<br>C. Sigmoid colon: LLQ (left lower quadrant).<br>D. Appendix: RLQ (right lower quadrant).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 11 (Organs in Quadrants)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":13,"f":"D045"}],"t":1},{"id":"D045-15-1","s":"A 60-year-old male presents with dull, constant pain in the Left Upper Quadrant (LUQ) after a car accident. On exam, the patient is pale and hypotensive. Which organ injury should you most suspect?","o":["A. Spleen","B. Gallbladder","C. Cecum","D. Urinary bladder"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Spleen","a":"A","why":"<b>Spleen.</b> It lies in the <b>LUQ</b> (left upper quadrant), deep to <b>ribs 9–11</b>. It is the abdominal organ most often torn in blunt trauma. Bleeding from it explains the pallor and low blood pressure.<br>B. Gallbladder: RUQ (right upper quadrant).<br>C. Cecum: RLQ (right lower quadrant).<br>D. Urinary bladder: midline, low in the pelvis.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 11 (Organs in Quadrants)","Pancreas & Spleen slide 28 (Ribs 9-11)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":15,"f":"D045"}]},{"id":"D045-17-1","s":"A 25-year-old woman presents with cramping pain in the Left Lower Quadrant (LLQ) during menstruation. Ultrasound shows an enlarged structure in that region. Which organ is most likely involved?","o":["A. Liver","B. Gallbladder","C. Ascending colon","D. Left ovary"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Left ovary","a":"D","why":"<b>Left ovary.</b> The <b>LLQ</b> (left lower quadrant) holds the left ovary and uterine tube, the sigmoid colon, and the lower descending colon. Cyclic pain plus an enlarged structure there points to the ovary.<br>A. Liver: mainly RUQ (right upper quadrant); left lobe in the LUQ (left upper quadrant).<br>B. Gallbladder: RUQ.<br>C. Ascending colon: right side, RLQ (right lower quadrant) and RUQ.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 11 (Organs in Quadrants)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":17,"f":"D045"}],"t":1},{"id":"D042-4-1","s":"Charlie was running throughout New York City for his marathon prep. He was bored with his usual route and decided to go down a dark lit ally. Unfortunately, this decision caused him to receive a stab wound to the left lower quadrant. Which of the following organs did he most likely injure?","o":["A. Spleen","B. Right kidney","C. Left kidney","D. Left lobe of the liver","E. Spermatic Cord"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Spermatic Cord","a":"E","why":"<b>Spermatic cord.</b> The abdominal part of the left spermatic cord runs through the <b>LLQ</b> (left lower quadrant) to the deep inguinal ring. The LLQ also holds the sigmoid colon and, in women, the left ovary and uterine tube.<br>A. Spleen: LUQ (left upper quadrant).<br>B. Right kidney: RUQ (right upper quadrant).<br>C. Left kidney: LUQ.<br>D. Left lobe of the liver: LUQ.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 11 (Organs in Quadrants)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 31 (Inguinal canal)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI module TA review","sl":4,"f":"D042"}]},{"id":"D045-19-1","s":"A 45-year-old male presents with epigastric pain that worsens after meals. The pain localizes at the midline but sometimes radiates to the back. Which of the following quadrants contains the organ most likely responsible?","o":["A. Right Upper Quadrant","B. Left Upper Quadrant","C. Right Lower Quadrant","D. Left Lower Quadrant"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Left Upper Quadrant","a":"B","why":"<b>Left upper quadrant.</b> Epigastric pain that worsens after meals fits a gastric ulcer, and pain to the back fits the pancreas. The <b>stomach</b> and the <b>body and tail of the pancreas</b> lie in the LUQ (left upper quadrant).<br>A. RUQ (right upper quadrant): liver, gallbladder, pylorus, pancreatic head; pain sits more to the right.<br>C. RLQ (right lower quadrant): cecum and appendix.<br>D. LLQ (left lower quadrant): sigmoid colon and left ovary.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 11 (Organs in Quadrants)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 7 (Referred Pain and Palpations)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":19,"f":"D045"}]},{"id":"D045-11-1","s":"During an abdominal exam, a medical student incorrectly places the horizontal plane above the umbilicus while identifying quadrants. Which organ’s quadrant designation might be most affected by this error?","o":["A. Spleen","B. Gallbladder","C. Urinary bladder","D. Appendix"],"g":["B"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Appendix","a":"D","why":"<b>Gallbladder.</b> Raising the horizontal line makes the lower quadrants bigger. So only an organ sitting just above the true line can be wrongly called 'lower'. The gallbladder fundus reaches the front wall at the right 9th costal cartilage, the lowest of the upper-quadrant choices.<br>A. Spleen: stays hidden behind left ribs 9–11; still upper.<br>C. Urinary bladder: deep in the pelvis; stays lower.<br>D. Appendix: already below the umbilicus; a higher line keeps it in the RLQ (right lower quadrant).","ex":"","fl":["The source keyed Appendix, but raising the line above the umbilicus cannot move the appendix (already below it) out of the right lower quadrant; only an upper-quadrant organ near the line, the gallbladder, could be misplaced."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 10 (Quadrants)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 11 (Organs in Quadrants)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":11,"f":"D045"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":31,"label":"Referred pain map: stomach → epigastrium and mid-back","slides":[{"src":"sl/G003-7.jpg","cap":"Epigastric = foregut pain; ulcer epigastric, cholecystitis right hypochondrium, appendicitis right inguinal","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 7"},{"src":"sl/F1252-51.jpg","cap":"Referred pain map: stomach to epigastrium and mid-upper back; gallbladder to right shoulder","where":"2020 TA review, slide 51"},{"src":"sl/G003-24.jpg","cap":"Incisions placed over the region giving best access to the target organ","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 24"}],"q":[{"id":"F1252-49-1","s":"A 37 year old man walks into the urgent care office complaining of pain. You ask him to point to where the pain is coming from. He indicates the epigastric region. What is on your differential?","o":["A. Gallstones","B. Gastric ulcer","C. Appendicitis","D. Splenomegaly","E. Kidney stone"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Gastric ulcer","a":"B","why":"<b>Gastric ulcer.</b> The <b>epigastric region</b> receives referred pain from the <b>foregut</b> (stomach, first part of the duodenum, liver, gallbladder, pancreas). Peptic ulcer pain is felt and palpated in the epigastrium.<br>A. Gallstones: right hypochondrium, spreading to the right shoulder.<br>C. Appendicitis: starts around the umbilicus (midgut), then moves to the right inguinal region.<br>D. Splenomegaly (big spleen): left hypochondrium.<br>E. Kidney stone: flank pain spreading down to the groin.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 7 (Referred Pain and Palpations)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":49,"f":"F1252"}],"t":1},{"id":"F1228-149-1","s":"Which of the following associations is NOT correctly matched?","o":["A. Appendicitis: pain in right inguinal region","B. Cholecystitis: pain in right hypochondriac region","C. Peptic ulcer: pain in epigastric pain","D. Cholecystitis: pain in right lateral region"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Cholecystitis: pain in right lateral region","a":"D","why":"<b>Cholecystitis – right lateral region</b> is the wrong pair. Cholecystitis (gallbladder inflammation) is felt in the <b>right hypochondrium</b>, just under the right costal margin. The right lateral (flank) region lies below that.<br>A. Appendicitis – right inguinal region: correct pair.<br>B. Cholecystitis – right hypochondriac region: correct pair.<br>C. Peptic ulcer – epigastric region: correct pair; foregut pain is epigastric.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 7 (Referred Pain and Palpations)"],"src":[{"t":"2019 review","n":"old Exam 2 review: abdomen part only","sl":149,"f":"F1228"}]},{"id":"F1252-47-1","s":"A 35 year old woman comes into your office complaining of back pain. You ask her to point to where her pain is coming from, and she indicates the middle of her upper back. What are you concerned she might be suffering from?","o":["A. Gallstones","B. Gastric ulcer","C. Appendicitis","D. Splenomegaly","E. Kidney stone"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Gastric ulcer","a":"B","why":"<b>Gastric ulcer.</b> Stomach pain is referred to the epigastric region in front and to the <b>middle of the upper back</b> (between the shoulder blades) behind.<br>A. Gallstones: right upper quadrant pain spreading to the right shoulder and right shoulder blade.<br>C. Appendicitis: pain around the umbilicus that moves to the right lower quadrant.<br>D. Splenomegaly (big spleen): left upper quadrant pain; spleen irritation refers to the left shoulder.<br>E. Kidney stone: flank pain spreading down to the groin.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 7 (Referred Pain and Palpations)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":47,"f":"F1252"}]},{"id":"F0008-56-1","s":"You were asked to assist in a surgical operation on a young patient to treat an ulcer in the posterior wall of the stomach. You would expect that the surgeon will approach the ulcer by doing an anterior abdominal wall incision in the following region:","o":["a. Epigastric","b. Left inguinal","c. Left lumbar","d. Right hypochondrial","e. Hypogastric"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Epigastric","a":"A","why":"<b>Epigastric.</b> The stomach lies mainly in the epigastric region. Incisions are made over the region that gives the best access to the target organ, so a posterior gastric ulcer is still reached from the front through the epigastric region.<br>B. Left inguinal: lower left groin; sigmoid colon area.<br>C. Left lumbar: left flank; descending colon area.<br>D. Right hypochondrial: liver and gallbladder area.<br>E. Hypogastric: pubic region above the bladder; hindgut pain area.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 7 (Referred Pain and Palpations)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 6 (9 regions of the Abdomen)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":56,"f":"F0008"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":32,"label":"Transpyloric plane = L1 and its contents","slides":[{"src":"sl/G003-9.jpg","cap":"Transpyloric plane at L1 with its list of structures","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 9"},{"src":"sl/G003-8.jpg","cap":"Transpyloric plane against vertebral levels; subcostal plane lies lower","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 8"}],"q":[{"id":"F1252-101-1","s":"The transpyloric plane is at the level of:","o":["A. T11","B. T12","C. L1","D. L2","E. L3"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"L1","a":"C","why":"<b>L1 (first lumbar vertebra).</b> The transpyloric plane lies halfway between the jugular notch (top of the manubrium) and the pubic symphysis. It passes through the pylorus when the patient lies down.<br>A. T11 (eleventh thoracic): too high.<br>B. T12 (twelfth thoracic): level of the celiac trunk origin, one level too high.<br>D. L2 (second lumbar): one level too low.<br>E. L3 (third lumbar): about where the subcostal plane lies; too low.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 9 (Transpyloric planes)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":101,"f":"F1252"}],"t":1},{"id":"F1252-103-1","s":"Which of the following is not transected by the transpyloric plane?","o":["A. Origin of the celiac trunk","B. Fundus of the gallbladder","C. Neck of the pancreas","D. Origin of the hepatic portal vein","E. Hilum of the kidneys"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Origin of the celiac trunk","a":"A","why":"<b>Origin of the celiac trunk.</b> The celiac trunk leaves the aorta at <b>T12</b> (twelfth thoracic vertebra), one level above the transpyloric plane at L1 (first lumbar vertebra). The SMA (superior mesenteric artery), just below it, is on the plane.<br>B. Fundus of the gallbladder: on the plane, at the right 9th costal cartilage.<br>C. Neck of the pancreas: on the plane.<br>D. Origin of the hepatic portal vein: on the plane, behind the pancreatic neck.<br>E. Hila of the kidneys: on the plane.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 9 (Transpyloric planes)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":103,"f":"F1252"}]},{"id":"F3902-145-1","s":"What structures are found at the transpyloric plane? (List as many as you can think of)","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"L1 vertebra, pylorus, pancreatic neck, duodenojejunal flexure, fundus of gall bladder, 9th costal cartilage, hila of kidneys, origin of portal vein, transverse mesocolon, 2nd part of duodenum, superior mesenteric artery origin, hilum of spleen, termination of spinal cord","a":null,"why":"<b>Transpyloric plane = L1 (first lumbar vertebra).</b> It lies halfway between the top of the manubrium (jugular notch) and the pubic symphysis. On it: pylorus (lying down), gallbladder fundus, pancreatic neck, origins of the SMA (superior mesenteric artery) and hepatic portal vein, root of the transverse mesocolon, duodenojejunal junction, hila of the kidneys, splenic hilum, and the 9th costal cartilage tips.<br>Common mix-up: the celiac trunk starts higher, at T12 (twelfth thoracic vertebra), so it is not on this plane.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 9 (Transpyloric planes)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":145,"f":"F3902"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":32,"label":"Gallbladder fundus at right 9th costal cartilage","slides":[{"src":"sl/G006-39.jpg","cap":"Gallbladder fundus lies at the tip of the ninth costal cartilage","where":"2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary & Portal Vein, slide 39"},{"src":"sl/G003-8.jpg","cap":"Right linea semilunaris meets the costal margin at the transpyloric plane","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 8"}],"q":[{"id":"F3903-246-1","s":"A 47-year-old man came into the emergency room with a complaint of severe pain felt on the right upper quadrant of his abdominal wall. On examination, the physician noted that the patient experienced pain while inhaling and the pain was localized at the junction of the linea semilunaris and the right ninth costal cartilage. Which of the following organs is the most likely involved in the origination of the pain?","o":["A. Duodenum","B. Gall bladder","C. Kidney","D. Liver","E. Transverse colon"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Gall bladder","a":"B","why":"<b>Gall bladder.</b> The <b>fundus</b> of the gallbladder touches the front wall where the right <b>linea semilunaris</b> (lateral edge of rectus abdominis) meets the <b>right 9th costal cartilage</b>. Pain there when breathing in is Murphy sign of an inflamed gallbladder.<br>A. Duodenum: deeper, near the midline.<br>C. Kidney: on the back wall; felt in the flank and back.<br>D. Liver: mostly under the ribs; pain is not focused at this point.<br>E. Transverse colon: hangs lower, across the umbilical region.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 9 (Transpyloric planes)","Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 39 (Gallbladder)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":246,"f":"F3903"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":33,"label":"Abdominal wall layers, superficial to deep","slides":[{"src":"sl/G003-13.jpg","cap":"Wall layers from skin, Camper, Scarpa, three muscles, to parietal peritoneum","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 13"},{"src":"sl/D045-24.jpg","cap":"Numbered layers: skin, Camper, Scarpa, three muscles, transversalis fascia, peritoneum","where":"2025 Montana TA review, slide 24"}],"q":[{"id":"F3903-220-1","s":"Camper's and Scarpa's fascia refer to the:","o":["A) fascial layers of the abdominal wall that are deep to the peritoneum.","B) superficial and deep (respectively) fascial layers of the posterior abdominal wall.","C) superficial and deep layers (respectively) of the rectus sheath.","D) fatty and membranous layers (respectively) of the superficial fascia of the inferior anterior abdominal wall.","E) fatty and membranous fascial layers (respectively) of the perineum."],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"fatty and membranous layers (respectively) of the superficial fascia of the inferior anterior abdominal wall","a":"D","why":"<b>Fatty (Camper) and membranous (Scarpa) layers of the superficial fascia</b> (subcutaneous tissue) of the lower anterior abdominal wall. Both lie between the skin and the external oblique. Scarpa is distinct mainly below the umbilicus.<br>A. They are superficial, not deep to the peritoneum.<br>B. They belong to the anterior wall, not the posterior wall.<br>C. The rectus sheath is made of muscle aponeuroses, not these.<br>E. In the perineum the membranous layer is called Colles fascia, not Scarpa.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 13 (Anterolateral Abdominal Wall)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":220,"f":"F3903"}]},{"id":"D045-22-1","s":"Which of the following correctly lists the layers of the anterior abdominal wall from superficial to deep?","o":["A. Skin → Camper’s fascia → Scarpa’s fascia → external oblique → internal oblique → transversus abdominis → transversalis fascia → parietal peritoneum","B. Skin → deep fascia → transversus abdominis → internal oblique → external oblique → parietal peritoneum","C. Skin → superficial fascia → internal oblique → external oblique → transversus abdominis → transversalis fascia → peritoneum","D. Skin → external oblique → internal oblique → transversus abdominis → parietal peritoneum","E. Skin → Camper’s fascia → Scarpa’s fascia → transversus abdominis → internal oblique → external oblique → transversalis fascia → parietal peritoneum"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Skin → Camper’s fascia → Scarpa’s fascia → external oblique → internal oblique → transversus abdominis → transversalis fascia → parietal peritoneum","a":"A","why":"<b>Skin → Camper → Scarpa → external oblique → internal oblique → transversus abdominis → transversalis fascia → parietal peritoneum.</b> Two subcutaneous layers (fatty Camper, membranous Scarpa), then three flat muscles from outside in, then the deep fascia and peritoneum.<br>B. Skips Camper and Scarpa and reverses the muscles.<br>C. Puts internal oblique outside external oblique.<br>D. Skips Camper, Scarpa and transversalis fascia.<br>E. Reverses the three muscles.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 13 (Anterolateral Abdominal Wall)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":22,"f":"D045"}],"t":1},{"id":"D041-11-1","s":"A patient presents with an anterior abdominal stab wound just lateral to the linea alba, above the level of the arcuate line. From superficial to deep, which sequence correctly represents the layers the knife would penetrate?","o":["A. Skin → Camper’s fascia → Scarpa’s fascia → Transversalis Fascia → External oblique m. → Internal oblique m. → Transversus abdominis m. → Parietal peritoneum","B. Skin → Scarpa’s fascia → Camper’s fascia → External oblique m. → Transversalis fascia → Internal oblique m. → Transversus abdominis m. → Parietal peritoneum","C. Skin → Camper’s fascia → Scarpa’s fascia → External oblique m. → Internal oblique m. → Transversus abdominis m. → Transversalis fascia → Parietal peritoneum","D. Skin → Deep fascia → External oblique → Internal oblique → Transversalis fascia → Transversus abdominis → Parietal peritoneum","E. Skin → Camper’s fascia → External oblique → Internal oblique → Transversalis fascia → Transversus abdominis → Parietal peritoneum"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Skin → Camper’s fascia → Scarpa’s fascia → External oblique m. → Internal oblique m. → Transversus abdominis m. → Transversalis fascia → Parietal peritoneum","a":"C","why":"<b>Skin → Camper → Scarpa → external oblique → internal oblique → transversus abdominis → transversalis fascia → parietal peritoneum.</b> Next to the linea alba the three muscles are aponeuroses (rectus sheath), but the order is the same.<br>A. Puts transversalis fascia outside the muscles; it is the deepest fascia.<br>B. Swaps Camper and Scarpa; transversalis fascia sits between muscles.<br>D. Skips Camper and Scarpa; transversalis fascia is out of place.<br>E. Skips Scarpa; puts transversalis fascia above transversus abdominis.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 13 (Anterolateral Abdominal Wall)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":11,"f":"D041"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":34,"label":"External oblique fibers run inferomedially","slides":[{"src":"sl/G003-14.jpg","cap":"External oblique fibers run down and medially; aponeurosis forms inguinal ligament","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 14"}],"q":[{"id":"F3903-222-1","s":"Most muscle fibers of the external oblique muscle:","o":["A) Run transversely.","B) Run inferomedially from their superior attachment.","C) Run inferolaterally from their superior attachment.","D) Pass deep to the linea alba.","E) Pass deep to the inguinal ligament."],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Run inferomedially from their superior attachment","a":"B","why":"<b>Inferomedially.</b> External oblique fibers run down and toward the midline, like hands slid into front pockets. They end in an aponeurosis that joins the linea alba; its free lower edge folds over to form the inguinal ligament.<br>A. Transverse fibers belong to transversus abdominis.<br>C. Inferolateral is the internal oblique's slope, at right angles to the external oblique.<br>D. The aponeurosis inserts into the linea alba, not deep to it.<br>E. The aponeurosis itself forms the inguinal ligament.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 14 (External Oblique)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":222,"f":"F3903"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":34,"label":"Abdominal wall muscles: raise pressure, move trunk","slides":[{"src":"sl/G003-15.jpg","cap":"Internal oblique flexes and rotates trunk, raises intra-abdominal pressure","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 15"},{"src":"sl/G003-14.jpg","cap":"External oblique actions: trunk flexion and rotation, intra-abdominal pressure","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 14"}],"q":[{"id":"D041-25-1","s":"Which of the following actions is performed by the internal oblique?","o":["A. Extends the trunk","B. Flexes the hip","C. Flexes and rotates the trunk","D. Controls pelvis tilt"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Flexes and rotates the trunk","a":"C","why":"<b>Flexes and rotates the trunk.</b> The internal oblique also raises intra-abdominal pressure and supports the viscera. Its nerves are T6–T12 and L1 (sixth thoracic to first lumbar).<br>A. Extends the trunk: back muscles (erector spinae) do this.<br>B. Flexes the hip: iliopsoas does this.<br>D. Controls pelvic tilt: the lecture lists this for rectus abdominis.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 15 (Internal OBlique)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":25,"f":"D041"}],"t":1},{"id":"D045-44-1","s":"A 32-year-old woman is performing a Valsalva maneuver during a clinical exam. She contracts her abdominal wall muscles to increase intra-abdominal pressure. Which of the following correctly describes the functional importance of the abdominal wall in this context?","o":["A. The abdominal wall muscles stabilize the trunk, increase intra-abdominal pressure for activities like coughing or defecation, and assist in trunk movements.","B. The abdominal wall muscles primarily produce forceful leg movements and prevent hip flexion.","C. The abdominal wall muscles serve mainly to protect the kidneys and maintain respiration only.","D. The abdominal wall muscles prevent blood flow through the inferior vena cava during Valsalva.","E. The abdominal wall muscles are only involved in bending the spine laterally and do not affect intra-abdominal pressure."],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"The abdominal wall muscles stabilize the trunk, increase intra-abdominal pressure for activities like coughing or defecation, and assist in trunk movements.","a":"A","why":"<b>A.</b> The abdominal wall muscles <b>raise intra-abdominal pressure</b> (coughing, defecation, childbirth, Valsalva), <b>stabilize the trunk</b>, and <b>move the trunk</b> (flexion, side-bending, rotation).<br>B. They do not move the legs or block hip flexion; iliopsoas flexes the hip.<br>C. Too narrow; protecting the kidneys is not their main job.<br>D. They do not block the IVC (inferior vena cava); any drop in venous return is a side effect.<br>E. Wrong; raising pressure is a main job, not only side-bending.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 14 (External Oblique)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 15 (Internal OBlique)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":44,"f":"D045"}]},{"id":"D045-46-1","s":"A 40-year-old man is lifting a heavy box. He is instructed to brace his core to protect his spine and improve lifting efficiency. Which of the following best explains the functional role of the abdominal wall during this activity?","o":["A. It increases intra-abdominal pressure to stabilize the spine and supports trunk movements while resisting external forces.","B. It primarily contracts to move the arms and shoulders during lifting.","C. It only serves to protect the kidneys and visceral organs without affecting spine stability.","D. It decreases intra-abdominal pressure to allow the diaphragm to move more freely.","E. It is only active during respiration and does not contribute to lifting or trunk stability."],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"It increases intra-abdominal pressure to stabilize the spine and supports trunk movements while resisting external forces.","a":"A","why":"<b>A.</b> Bracing contracts the abdominal wall muscles, which <b>raises intra-abdominal pressure</b>. The pressurized abdomen acts like a stiff cylinder that <b>stabilizes the spine</b> and supports trunk movement against the load.<br>B. Arm and shoulder movement comes from limb and shoulder muscles.<br>C. Wrong; spinal stability is a key role.<br>D. Contraction raises, not lowers, the pressure.<br>E. They act in lifting and posture, not only in breathing.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 14 (External Oblique)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 15 (Internal OBlique)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 16 (Transversus Abdominis)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":46,"f":"D045"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":35,"label":"Rectus sheath encloses rectus abdominis","slides":[{"src":"sl/G003-18.jpg","cap":"Cross sections: rectus sheath wraps rectus abdominis in front and behind","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 18"}],"q":[{"id":"F3901-193-1","s":"You start a new rigorous work out regime this week (anything to procrastinate studying right?) which involves 200 crunches to build up that “6-pack” muscle. What covers this muscle anteriorly and posteriorly?","o":["A. Rectus Sheath","B. Linea Alba","C. Transversus Abdominis","D. Adipose"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Rectus Sheath","a":"A","why":"<b>Rectus sheath.</b> It is made from the aponeuroses of the three flat muscles plus transversalis fascia, and it wraps rectus abdominis in front and behind.<br>B. Linea alba: the midline seam between the two recti, not a covering.<br>C. Transversus abdominis: a flat lateral muscle; only its aponeurosis joins the sheath.<br>D. Adipose: the subcutaneous fat (Camper fascia) lies far superficial to the sheath.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 18 (rectus sheath)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":193,"f":"F3901"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":35,"label":"Below arcuate line: rectus lies on transversalis fascia","slides":[{"src":"sl/G003-19.jpg","cap":"Below arcuate line: no posterior sheath; rectus lies on transversalis fascia","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 19"},{"src":"sl/G003-20.jpg","cap":"Sagittal view: arcuate line; lower posterior wall is transversalis fascia only","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 20"}],"q":[{"id":"F0008-60-1","s":"The inferior border of the rectus sheath posteriorly is called the:","o":["a. Falx inguinalis","b. Inguinal ligament","c. Internal inguinal ring","d. Arcuate line","e. Linea alba"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Arcuate line","a":"D","why":"<b>Arcuate line.</b> It is the curved lower edge of the posterior rectus sheath, about one-third of the way from the umbilicus to the pubic crest. Below it, rectus abdominis lies directly on transversalis fascia.<br>A. Falx inguinalis (conjoint tendon): inserts on the pubic crest.<br>B. Inguinal ligament: folded lower edge of the external oblique aponeurosis.<br>C. Internal inguinal ring: opening in transversalis fascia.<br>E. Linea alba: midline seam between the two recti.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 18 (rectus sheath)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 19 (rectus sheath)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":60,"f":"F0008"}]},{"id":"F1180-241-1","s":"After a mastectomy, a musculocutaneous flap is used to restore the thoracic contour in a 34-year-old female patient. The ipsilateral (same side) rectus abdominis muscle was detached carefully from the surrounding structures and transposed to the thoracic wall. Which of the following landmarks is most often used to locate the inferior end of the posterior, tendinous layer of the rectus sheath?","o":["A. Intercristal line","B. Linea alba","C. Arcuate line","D. Pectineal line","E. Semilunar line"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Arcuate line","a":"C","why":"<b>Arcuate line.</b> The posterior (tendinous) layer of the rectus sheath stops at the arcuate line, a little below the umbilicus. Below it, only transversalis fascia lies behind the muscle.<br>A. Intercristal line: joins the tops of the iliac crests, at L4 (fourth lumbar vertebra).<br>B. Linea alba: midline seam.<br>D. Pectineal line: a ridge on the pubic bone.<br>E. Semilunar line: lateral edge of the rectus sheath.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 18 (rectus sheath)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 19 (rectus sheath)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":241,"f":"F1180"},{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":185,"f":"F1178"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3903-238-1","s":"During abdominal surgery through the anterior abdominal wall it is important to maintain the integrity of the rectus sheath. Which of the following is in contact with the posterior aspect of the rectus abdominis muscle – below the arcuate line?","o":["A. External oblique","B. Fascia transversalis","C. Inguinal ligament","D. Internal oblique","E. Transverse abdominis"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Fascia transversalis","a":"B","why":"<b>Fascia transversalis.</b> Below the arcuate line, all three flat-muscle aponeuroses pass in front of rectus abdominis. There is no posterior sheath, so the back of the muscle touches transversalis fascia.<br>A. External oblique: its aponeurosis is in front of the rectus.<br>C. Inguinal ligament: in the groin, not behind the rectus.<br>D. Internal oblique: its aponeurosis is in front below the line.<br>E. Transversus abdominis: its aponeurosis is also in front below the line.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 19 (rectus sheath)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":238,"f":"F3903"}],"t":1},{"id":"F0008-58-1","s":"An obstetrician decides to do a Caesarean section on a 25-year-old pregnant woman. A transverse suprapubic incision is chosen for that purpose. All of the following abdominal wall layers will be encountered during the incision EXCEPT the:","o":["a. Skin and subcutaneous tissue","b. Anterior rectus sheath","c. Rectus abdominus muscle","d. Posterior rectus sheath","e. Transversalis fascia, extraperitoneal fat, and peritoneum"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Posterior rectus sheath","a":"D","why":"<b>Posterior rectus sheath.</b> A suprapubic incision is below the arcuate line. There all three aponeuroses run in front of the rectus, so there is <b>no posterior sheath</b>. Behind the muscle lie only transversalis fascia, extraperitoneal fat and peritoneum.<br>A. Skin and subcutaneous tissue: always cut.<br>B. Anterior rectus sheath: present and cut.<br>C. Rectus abdominis: met, then split or pulled aside.<br>E. Transversalis fascia, fat and peritoneum: crossed to enter the abdomen.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 19 (rectus sheath)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":58,"f":"F0008"}]},{"id":"F3905-12-1","s":"A 60 y/o patient was prepped for abdominal surgery to remove a premalignant left colon polyp. The physician makes an incision into the left umbilical region, and dislocates the rectus abdominis that was lying directly superficial to the transversalis fascia. Where did the physician make their incision?","o":["A. Inferior to the arcuate ligament","B. Inferior to the inguinal ligament","C. At the linea alba","D. Superior to the linea alba","E. Superior to the arcuate ligament"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Inferior to the arcuate ligament","a":"A","why":"<b>Inferior to the arcuate line</b> (the stem's 'arcuate ligament' means the arcuate line). Only below this line does rectus abdominis lie directly on transversalis fascia, because the posterior sheath is missing there.<br>B. Below the inguinal ligament is the thigh, not the rectus.<br>C. At the linea alba there is no muscle, only the midline seam.<br>D. The linea alba is vertical, so 'superior to' it means nothing here.<br>E. Above the line, the posterior sheath separates the rectus from transversalis fascia.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 19 (rectus sheath)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA weekly","n":"09-22-2022 TA session (abdominal wall, inguinal)","sl":12,"f":"F3905"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":36,"label":"Rectus sheath layers above vs below arcuate line","slides":[{"src":"sl/G003-19.jpg","cap":"Anterior and posterior sheath layers above and below the arcuate line","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 19"},{"src":"sl/D045-27.jpg","cap":"Sheath layers above versus below arcuate line, transverse sections","where":"2025 Montana TA review, slide 27"},{"src":"sl/D042-11.jpg","cap":"Above and below arcuate line: epigastric vessels lie just behind rectus","where":"2025 TA review, slide 11"}],"q":[{"id":"D045-25-1","s":"During an abdominal wall reconstruction, a surgeon points out to the resident that the composition of the rectus sheath changes depending on its position relative to the arcuate line. At a point just below the umbilicus but above the arcuate line, which of the following correctly describes the layers forming the posterior portion of the rectus sheath?","o":["A. External oblique aponeurosis only","B. Internal oblique aponeurosis and transversus abdominis aponeurosis","C. Internal oblique aponeurosis only","D. Transversus abdominis aponeurosis only","E. No posterior rectus sheath is present at this level"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Internal oblique aponeurosis and transversus abdominis aponeurosis","a":"B","why":"<b>Internal oblique and transversus abdominis aponeuroses.</b> Above the arcuate line, the internal oblique aponeurosis splits around the rectus. Its posterior leaflet joins the transversus abdominis aponeurosis to form the posterior sheath.<br>A. External oblique: always in the anterior sheath.<br>C. Internal oblique only: leaves out transversus abdominis.<br>D. Transversus abdominis only: leaves out the internal oblique leaflet.<br>E. No posterior sheath: true only below the arcuate line.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 19 (rectus sheath)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":25,"f":"D045"}],"t":1},{"id":"F1228-125-1","s":"Which of the following is false regarding the rectus sheath:","o":["A. Aponeurosis of internal oblique splits to enclose rectus abdominis to form rectus sheath above the arcuate line","B. Above the arcuate line, the aponeurosis of external oblique, internal oblique, and transverse abdominis make the anterior layer of the rectus sheath","C. Below the arcuate line, rectus abdominis lies on fascia transversalis","D. Above the arcuate line, rectus abdominis is only in contact with the internal oblique aponeurosis"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Above the arcuate line, the aponeurosis of external oblique, internal oblique, and transverse abdominis make the anterior layer of the rectus sheath","a":"B","why":"<b>B is false.</b> Above the arcuate line, the anterior layer is only the <b>external oblique aponeurosis plus the anterior leaflet of the internal oblique</b>. Transversus abdominis joins the posterior layer there.<br>A. True: the internal oblique aponeurosis splits to enclose the rectus.<br>C. True: below the arcuate line the rectus lies on transversalis fascia.<br>D. True: the muscle touches only the two internal oblique leaflets; the other aponeuroses fuse outside them.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 19 (rectus sheath)"],"src":[{"t":"2019 review","n":"old Exam 2 review: abdomen part only","sl":125,"f":"F1228"}]},{"id":"D042-7-1","s":"A 27 year-old medical student is out a bar celebrating after a mod exam. They get into a fight and end up getting stab wound in the anterior abdominal wall, above the arcuate line. The wound is examined, and it is determined that the knife pieced all the layers of the anterior abdominal wall up until the rectus abdominis muscle. What is the deepest layer that has been damaged?","o":["A. Aponeurosis of the external oblique","B. Transversalis fascia","C. Posterior division of the internal oblique aponeurosis","D. Anterior Division of the internal oblique aponeurosis"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Anterior Division of the internal oblique aponeurosis","a":"D","why":"<b>Anterior division of the internal oblique aponeurosis.</b> Above the arcuate line, the anterior rectus sheath is the external oblique aponeurosis on top of the internal oblique's anterior leaflet. That leaflet touches the muscle, so it is the deepest layer cut.<br>A. External oblique aponeurosis: cut, but more superficial.<br>B. Transversalis fascia: behind the rectus; not reached.<br>C. Posterior division of the internal oblique: behind the rectus; not reached.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 19 (rectus sheath)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 18 (rectus sheath)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI module TA review","sl":7,"f":"D042"}]},{"id":"D042-9-1","s":"After he got fixed up, he (not so wisley) decided to get revenge on his stabber. He goes back to bar, and is stabbed again. This time below the arcuate line. It is determined that the inferior epigastric artery was spared, but all layers superficial to that were cut. What is the deepest layer that has been damaged?","o":["A. Aponeurosis of the external oblique","B. Transversus abdominis aponeurosis","C. Posterior division of the internal oblique aponeurosis","D. Anterior Division of the transversalis fascia"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Transversus abdominis aponeurosis","a":"B","why":"<b>Transversus abdominis aponeurosis.</b> Below the arcuate line, all three aponeuroses lie in front of rectus abdominis, transversus deepest. The inferior epigastric artery lies behind the rectus, on transversalis fascia. Of the choices, the transversus aponeurosis is the deepest layer cut (the rectus itself was also cut).<br>A. External oblique aponeurosis: most superficial.<br>C. Posterior internal oblique leaflet: absent below the line.<br>D. Transversalis fascia has no 'anterior division'; it lies deep to the artery.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 19 (rectus sheath)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 20 (rectus sheath)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI module TA review","sl":9,"f":"D042"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":37,"label":"Superior epigastric: internal thoracic branch, upper wall","slides":[{"src":"sl/G003-21.jpg","cap":"Internal thoracic gives musculophrenic and superior epigastric; external iliac gives inferior epigastric","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 21"},{"src":"sl/G003-20.jpg","cap":"Superior epigastric artery entering the rectus sheath from the internal thoracic","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 20"}],"q":[{"id":"F3900-148-1","s":"Which artery primarily supplies the upper rectus abdominis muscle with blood?","o":["A. Internal thoracic artery","B. Superior epigastric artery","C. Inferior epigastric artery","D. Subcostal artery"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Superior epigastric artery","a":"B","why":"<b>Superior epigastric artery.</b> It is the end branch of the internal thoracic artery. It enters the rectus sheath behind the upper rectus abdominis and supplies it.<br>A. Internal thoracic: the parent; it ends at the sixth intercostal space by splitting into superior epigastric and musculophrenic.<br>C. Inferior epigastric: supplies the lower rectus.<br>D. Subcostal: runs below rib 12 to the lateral wall.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 21 (vasculature)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 20 (rectus sheath)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":148,"f":"F3900"}]},{"id":"D042-12-1","s":"The abdominal cavity has blood supply that originates all the way from the thoracic cavity as well as from the abdominal cavity itself. Where does the superior epigastric originate from, and from what major artery does this arise?","o":["A. Lateral thoracic artery, axillary artery","B. Lateral thoracic artery, subclavian artery","C. Inferior thoracic artery, subclavian artery","D. Internal thoracic artery, axillary artery","E. Inferior thoracic artery, subclavian artery","F. Internal thoracic artery, subclavian artery"],"g":["F"],"k":["F"],"rv":false,"at":"Internal thoracic artery, subclavian artery","a":"F","why":"<b>Internal thoracic artery, from the subclavian artery.</b> The internal thoracic runs down behind the costal cartilages. At the sixth intercostal space it splits into the superior epigastric and musculophrenic arteries.<br>A. Lateral thoracic, axillary: supplies the lateral chest wall, not the abdomen.<br>B. Lateral thoracic, subclavian: wrong artery and wrong parent.<br>C. 'Inferior thoracic' artery: does not exist.<br>D. Internal thoracic, axillary: right branch, wrong parent.<br>E. 'Inferior thoracic' artery: does not exist (same as C).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 20 (rectus sheath)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 21 (vasculature)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI module TA review","sl":12,"f":"D042"}],"t":1},{"id":"D045-29-1","s":"A 28-year-old man is undergoing a midline abdominal incision for surgery. The surgeon is careful to avoid major vessels supplying the anterior abdominal wall. Which of the following vessels primarily supplies and drains the upper anterior abdominal wall?","o":["A. Superior epigastric artery and vein","B. Inferior epigastric artery and vein","C. Deep circumflex iliac artery and vein","D. Superficial epigastric artery and vein","E. Posterior intercostal artery and vein"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Superior epigastric artery and vein","a":"A","why":"<b>Superior epigastric artery and vein.</b> They continue from the internal thoracic vessels and run inside the rectus sheath, supplying and draining the wall above the umbilicus.<br>B. Inferior epigastric: lower wall, below the umbilicus.<br>C. Deep circumflex iliac: lower lateral wall along the iliac crest.<br>D. Superficial epigastric: subcutaneous lower wall, from the femoral vessels.<br>E. Posterior intercostal: lateral wall, not the upper front.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 20 (rectus sheath)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 21 (vasculature)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":29,"f":"D045"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":37,"label":"Abdominal wall arteries; lateral thoracic is not one","slides":[{"src":"sl/G003-21.jpg","cap":"Wall arteries: musculophrenic, epigastrics, circumflex iliacs, intercostals; no lateral thoracic","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 21"}],"q":[{"id":"F1228-110-1","s":"The vascular supply to the anterior abdominal wall arises from all of the following EXCEPT :","o":["A. The superior epigastric artery","B. The 10th posterior intercostal artery","C. The lateral thoracic artery","D. The superficial epigastric artery","E. The musculophrenic artery"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"The lateral thoracic artery","a":"C","why":"<b>Lateral thoracic artery.</b> It is an axillary artery branch to the lateral chest wall and breast. It does not supply the abdominal wall.<br>A. Superior epigastric: upper wall, from the internal thoracic.<br>B. 10th posterior intercostal: lateral wall, from the aorta.<br>D. Superficial epigastric: lower wall skin, from the femoral.<br>E. Musculophrenic: upper lateral wall along the costal margin, from the internal thoracic.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 21 (vasculature)"],"src":[{"t":"2019 review","n":"old Exam 2 review: abdomen part only","sl":110,"f":"F1228"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":37,"label":"Laceration near ASIS → superficial circumflex iliac","slides":[{"src":"sl/G003-21.jpg","cap":"Superficial circumflex iliac artery running toward the anterior superior iliac spine","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 21"}],"q":[{"id":"D041-15-1","s":"A patient suffers a laceration to the lower anterior abdominal wall just medial to the anterior superior iliac spine (ASIS). Which artery is most likely to be injured at this location?","o":["A. Superior epigastric artery","B. Internal thoracic artery","C. Inferior epigastric artery","D. Superficial circumflex iliac artery","E. Deep circumflex iliac artery"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Superficial circumflex iliac artery","a":"D","why":"<b>Superficial circumflex iliac artery.</b> It branches from the femoral artery below the inguinal ligament and runs in the subcutaneous fat toward the ASIS (anterior superior iliac spine). A skin laceration there hits it first.<br>A. Superior epigastric: upper wall, inside the rectus sheath.<br>B. Internal thoracic: in the chest, behind the costal cartilages.<br>C. Inferior epigastric: medial, behind the rectus.<br>E. Deep circumflex iliac: also heads to the ASIS, but lies deep to the muscles.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 21 (vasculature)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":15,"f":"D041"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":38,"label":"Inferior epigastric: behind rectus, enters at arcuate line","slides":[{"src":"sl/G003-20.jpg","cap":"Inferior epigastric artery rising behind rectus to the arcuate line","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 20"},{"src":"sl/D042-11.jpg","cap":"Epigastric vessels just deep to rectus, above and below arcuate line","where":"2025 TA review, slide 11"}],"q":[{"id":"F3902-168-1","s":"The inferior epigastric artery enters the rectus sheath inferior to what arbitrary structure in the abdomen?","o":["A. Inguinal ligament","B. Lienorenal ligament","C. Arcuate line","D. Linea alba","E. Linea semilunaris"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Arcuate line","a":"C","why":"<b>Arcuate line.</b> The inferior epigastric artery rises from the external iliac artery, climbs on transversalis fascia below the arcuate line, and passes into the rectus sheath at the arcuate line. Its peritoneal ridge (lateral umbilical fold) ends there.<br>A. Inguinal ligament: the artery starts just above it, far below its entry into the sheath.<br>B. Lienorenal ligament: holds the spleen; unrelated.<br>D. Linea alba: midline seam.<br>E. Linea semilunaris: lateral edge of the rectus.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 20 (rectus sheath)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 18 (rectus sheath)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":168,"f":"F3902"}],"t":1},{"id":"F1252-120-1","s":"Below the arcuate line, the inferior epigastric vessels run _____ to the ______, respectively.","o":["A. Anterior, transversalis fascia","B. Posterior, transversalis fascia","C. Anterior, posterior leaflet of the internal oblique aponeurosis","D. Anterior, transverse abdominis","E. Posterior, transverse abdominis"],"g":["A","E"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Anterior, transversalis fascia","a":"A","why":"<b>Anterior to the transversalis fascia</b> (lecture wording). Below the arcuate line there is no posterior sheath, so the inferior epigastric vessels lie on transversalis fascia, right behind rectus abdominis. E is also true: there the transversus aponeurosis passes in front of the rectus, so the vessels are behind it. If forced to pick one, choose A.<br>B. Lecture places them in front of, not behind, transversalis fascia.<br>C. The posterior internal oblique leaflet is absent below the line.<br>D. They lie behind, not in front of, transversus abdominis.","ex":"","fl":["The source keyed only A, but E is also true: below the arcuate line the transversus abdominis aponeurosis passes in front of the rectus, so the inferior epigastric vessels lie posterior to it."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 20 (rectus sheath)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":120,"f":"F1252"}]},{"id":"F3903-232-1","s":"A transverse incision through the rectus abdominis muscle and both layers of the rectus sheath at the arcuate line would transect the:","o":["A) inferior epigastric artery.","B) thoracoepigastric vein.","C) first lumbar artery.","D) ilioinguinal nerve.","E) T12 intercostal nerve."],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"inferior epigastric artery","a":"A","why":"<b>Inferior epigastric artery.</b> It runs vertically behind rectus abdominis and enters the rectus sheath at the arcuate line. A transverse cut through the muscle and both sheath layers there crosses it.<br>B. Thoracoepigastric vein: in the subcutaneous fat of the lateral wall.<br>C. First lumbar artery: on the posterior wall.<br>D. Ilioinguinal nerve: lower, in the inguinal canal.<br>E. T12 (twelfth thoracic) is the subcostal nerve; wall nerves run roughly sideways, parallel to a transverse cut.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 20 (rectus sheath)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 18 (rectus sheath)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":232,"f":"F3903"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3902-166-1","s":"A surgeon plans to use a portion of the suprapubic rectus abdominis muscle as a graft. While transecting the graft, the incision pools with blood. Which vessel needed to be isolated before transection?","o":["A. Superficial Epigastric","B. Musculophrenic","C. Deep Circumflex iliac","D. Inferior Mesenteric","E. Inferior Epigastric"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Inferior Epigastric","a":"E","why":"<b>Inferior epigastric.</b> It runs up directly behind the lower rectus abdominis, so cutting a suprapubic rectus graft cuts it. It branches from the external iliac artery just above the inguinal ligament.<br>A. Superficial epigastric: in the subcutaneous fat, not the muscle.<br>B. Musculophrenic: along the costal margin, far above.<br>C. Deep circumflex iliac: runs laterally along the iliac crest.<br>D. Inferior mesenteric: supplies the hindgut inside the abdomen.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 20 (rectus sheath)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 21 (vasculature)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":166,"f":"F3902"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":39,"label":"Superior and inferior epigastric anastomose in rectus sheath","slides":[{"src":"sl/G003-20.jpg","cap":"Superior epigastric (internal thoracic) meets inferior epigastric (external iliac) behind rectus","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 20"},{"src":"sl/G003-21.jpg","cap":"Abdominal wall arteries: epigastric pair joining along the rectus","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 21"}],"q":[{"id":"F3901-195-1","s":"The rectus sheath that envelops the rectus abdominis changes composition below the bellybutton to allow for an anastomosis of:","o":["A. Superior Mesenteric and Inferior Mesenteric arteries","B. Sigmoid and Superior Rectal arteries","C. Superior Epigastric and Inferior Epigastric arteries","D. Left Gastric and Common Hepatic arteries"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Superior Epigastric and Inferior Epigastric arteries","a":"C","why":"<b>Superior and inferior epigastric arteries.</b> The posterior rectus sheath changes at the arcuate line, below the umbilicus. There the inferior epigastric artery (from the external iliac) enters the sheath and meets the superior epigastric artery (from the internal thoracic) behind rectus abdominis.<br>A. SMA and IMA (superior and inferior mesenteric arteries): gut arteries.<br>B. Sigmoid and superior rectal: colon arteries.<br>D. Left gastric and common hepatic: celiac trunk branches.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 20 (rectus sheath)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":195,"f":"F3901"},{"t":"? TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":74,"f":"F1156"}],"t":1},{"id":"F1180-239-1","s":"What are the anastomoses found within the rectus abdominis?","o":["A. Superficial circumflex with deep circumflex artery","B. Muculophrenic with deep circumflex iliac artery","C. Superior epigastric artery with inferior epigastric artery","D. Superficial epigastric artery with inferior epigastric artery"],"g":["C"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Superficial epigastric artery with inferior epigastric artery","a":"D","why":"<b>Superior epigastric with inferior epigastric.</b> The superior epigastric (end branch of the internal thoracic artery) and the inferior epigastric (from the external iliac artery) meet inside the rectus sheath, on the back of rectus abdominis. An old deck keyed D; that is wrong.<br>A. Superficial and deep circumflex iliac: both head to the iliac crest, outside the rectus.<br>B. Musculophrenic and deep circumflex iliac: these join in the lateral wall.<br>D. Superficial epigastric runs in the subcutaneous fat, not in the rectus.","ex":"","fl":["The anastomosis within the rectus sheath/rectus abdominis is superior epigastric (from internal thoracic) with inferior epigastric (from external iliac) (G003:18, G003:20); the superficial epigastric runs in the subcutaneous tissue, not in the rectus muscle.","G003:20 (and Moore) - the superior epigastric (from internal thoracic) and inferior epigastric (from external iliac) arteries anastomose within the rectus sheath/rectus abdominis; the superficial epigastric artery runs in the subcutaneous tissue, not in the muscle. The TA's own concept slide 170 carries the error, which the 2026 G003:21 no longer states."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 18 (rectus sheath)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 20 (rectus sheath)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":239,"f":"F1180"},{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":183,"f":"F1178"}],"t":1},{"id":"F0008-62-1","s":"The internal thoracic artery is sometimes surgically cut near the caudal end of the sternum and used to supply blood to a region of the heart. In these cases, maintenance of adequate blood flow to the rectus abdominis may be dependent on increased flow through which artery?","o":["a. Superficial circumflex iliac","b. Superficial epigastric","c. Umbilical","d. Inferior epigastric","e. Deep circumflex iliac"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Inferior epigastric","a":"D","why":"<b>Inferior epigastric.</b> Cutting the internal thoracic artery removes the source of the superior epigastric artery. The rectus then relies on the inferior epigastric artery (from the external iliac), which feeds the superior epigastric through their anastomosis in the rectus sheath.<br>A. Superficial circumflex iliac: subcutaneous, toward the hip bone.<br>B. Superficial epigastric: subcutaneous lower wall.<br>C. Umbilical: pelvic artery whose far end is obliterated.<br>E. Deep circumflex iliac: lateral wall along the iliac crest.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 18 (rectus sheath)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 20 (rectus sheath)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 21 (vasculature)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":62,"f":"F0008"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":40,"label":"Lymph: above umbilicus axillary, below superficial inguinal","slides":[{"src":"sl/G003-23.jpg","cap":"Above umbilicus drains to axillary nodes; below to superficial inguinal nodes","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 23"}],"q":[{"id":"D045-34-1","s":"A 45-year-old man presents with skin infections over his anterior abdominal wall. The physician wants to understand the lymphatic drainage pattern to determine which lymph nodes may be involved. Which of the following best describes the general pattern of lymphatic drainage of the anterior abdominal wall?","o":["A. Lymph from the anterior abdominal wall drains directly into the thoracic duct.","B. Superior to the umbilicus drains mainly to axillary lymph nodes; inferior to the umbilicus drains mainly to superficial inguinal lymph nodes.","C. Superior to the umbilicus drains to superficial inguinal nodes; inferior to the umbilicus drains to axillary nodes.","D. Lymph from the anterior abdominal wall drains primarily to internal iliac nodes, regardless of location.","E. Entire anterior abdominal wall drains only to superficial inguinal lymph nodes."],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Superior to the umbilicus drains mainly to axillary lymph nodes; inferior to the umbilicus drains mainly to superficial inguinal lymph nodes.","a":"B","why":"<b>Above the umbilicus → axillary nodes; below → superficial inguinal nodes.</b> Superficial lymph follows the superficial vessels, which split at the umbilicus.<br>A. Skin lymph passes through regional nodes first, not straight to the thoracic duct.<br>C. Reversed.<br>D. Internal iliac nodes drain pelvic organs.<br>E. Only the lower half goes to the superficial inguinal nodes.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 23 (Lymphatics)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":34,"f":"D045"}]},{"id":"F0008-64-1","s":"The normal pattern of venous and lymphatic drainage of the superficial tissues of the anterior abdominal wall is arranged around a horizontal plane. Above that plane, drainage is in a cranial direction; below the plane drainage is in a caudal direction. This reference plane corresponds to:","o":["a. Level of umbilicus","b. Level of anterior superior iliac spines","c. Transtubercular line","d. Level of arcuate line","e. Transpyloric plane"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Level of umbilicus","a":"A","why":"<b>Level of the umbilicus.</b> Superficial veins and lymph above the umbilicus run up toward the axilla. Below it they run down toward the femoral vein and superficial inguinal nodes.<br>B. ASIS (anterior superior iliac spine) level: too low.<br>C. Transtubercular line: through the iliac tubercles at L5 (fifth lumbar); too low.<br>D. Arcuate line: rectus sheath landmark below the umbilicus.<br>E. Transpyloric plane: L1 (first lumbar); too high.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 23 (Lymphatics)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 21 (vasculature)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":64,"f":"F0008"}]},{"id":"D041-19-1","s":"A superficial skin infection develops on the anterior abdominal wall just above the level of the umbilicus. To which group of lymph nodes will lymph from this region primarily drain?","o":["A. Superficial inguinal lymph nodes","B. Cisterna Chyli","C. Axillary lymph nodes","D. Para-aortic (lumbar) lymph nodes","E. Deep inguinal lymph nodes"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Axillary lymph nodes","a":"C","why":"<b>Axillary lymph nodes.</b> The upper quadrants drain upward to the axillary nodes; the lower quadrants drain down to the superficial inguinal nodes. The umbilicus is the dividing line.<br>A. Superficial inguinal: below the umbilicus, and the scrotum.<br>B. Cisterna chyli: gathers deep lymph from below the diaphragm, not skin lymph directly.<br>D. Para-aortic (lumbar): testes and posterior wall organs.<br>E. Deep inguinal: glans and deeper perineal lymph.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 23 (Lymphatics)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":19,"f":"D041"}],"t":1},{"id":"D045-36-1","s":"A 50-year-old woman presents with a skin infection just above the umbilicus. The physician wants to know which superficial lymph nodes are most likely to be involved. Which of the following is correct?","o":["A. Axillary lymph nodes","B. Superficial inguinal lymph nodes","C. Internal iliac lymph nodes","D. Cervical lymph nodes","E. Popliteal lymph nodes"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Axillary lymph nodes","a":"A","why":"<b>Axillary lymph nodes.</b> Superficial lymph from above the umbilicus flows up to the axillary nodes. Below the umbilicus it flows down to the superficial inguinal nodes.<br>B. Superficial inguinal: skin below the umbilicus.<br>C. Internal iliac: pelvic organs.<br>D. Cervical: head and neck.<br>E. Popliteal: lower leg and foot.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 23 (Lymphatics)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":36,"f":"D045"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":40,"label":"Thoracoepigastric vein links axillary and femoral veins","slides":[{"src":"sl/G003-21.jpg","cap":"Superficial epigastric artery from femoral; matching veins follow these arteries","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 21"}],"q":[{"id":"D045-31-1","s":"A 40-year-old man presents with a large midline abdominal wall hernia. During surgery, the surgeon notes prominent superficial veins along the lateral chest and abdominal wall, indicating the presence of collateral venous pathways. Which of the following correctly describes the venous collateral pathway connecting the abdominal wall to the systemic circulation?","o":["A. Superior epigastric vein anastomoses with inferior epigastric vein only","B. Inferior epigastric vein anastomoses with posterior intercostal veins","C. Superficial epigastric vein anastomoses with deep circumflex iliac vein only","D. Thoracoepigastric vein anastomoses only with internal thoracic vein","E. Thoracoepigastric vein anastomoses with both axillary and femoral veins, providing a collateral pathway"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Thoracoepigastric vein anastomoses with both axillary and femoral veins, providing a collateral pathway","a":"E","why":"<b>Thoracoepigastric vein.</b> This superficial vein runs up the side of the trunk. It links the superficial epigastric vein (to the femoral vein) with the lateral thoracic vein (to the axillary vein), so it enlarges as a bypass when the IVC (inferior vena cava) is blocked.<br>A. Superior–inferior epigastric veins: deep, inside the rectus sheath.<br>B. Not the main superficial bypass.<br>C. Wrong partner, and 'only' is false.<br>D. It joins the axillary and femoral veins, not only the internal thoracic.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 21 (vasculature)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":31,"f":"D045"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":41,"label":"Wall nerves run between internal oblique and transversus","slides":[{"src":"sl/G003-16.jpg","cap":"Neurovascular bundles lie between internal oblique and transversus abdominis","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 16"},{"src":"sl/G003-20.jpg","cap":"Thoracoabdominal nerves crossing the lateral wall to the rectus sheath","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 20"}],"q":[{"id":"F3903-224-1","s":"The nerve supply to the muscles of the anterior abdominal wall:","o":["A) pierces the peritoneum immediately prior to entering the deep surface of the muscle.","B) is derived from the sympathetic trunk.","C) travels between the internal oblique and transverse abdominis muscles.","D) also innervates the quadriceps femoris.","E) is derived from sacral ventral rami."],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"travels between the internal oblique and transverse abdominis muscles","a":"C","why":"<b>Travels between the internal oblique and transversus abdominis.</b> The lower thoracic (thoracoabdominal and subcostal) nerves and the L1 (first lumbar) nerves run in this plane, just as intercostal nerves run between the inner muscle layers of the chest.<br>A. They stay outside the peritoneum.<br>B. They are spinal nerves, not sympathetic trunk branches.<br>D. Quadriceps is supplied by the femoral nerve (L2–L4, second to fourth lumbar).<br>E. They come from thoracic and first lumbar ventral rami, not sacral.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 16 (Transversus Abdominis)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 22 (Neurovasculature)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":224,"f":"F3903"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":41,"label":"Dermatomes: T7 xiphoid, T10 umbilicus, L1 groin","slides":[{"src":"sl/G003-22.jpg","cap":"Dermatomes: T7 xiphoid, T10 umbilicus, L1 iliohypogastric above pubis and groin","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 22"}],"q":[{"id":"F3903-236-1","s":"A 35-year-old woman has acute pain, localized around the umbilical region. The dermatome level involved in the origination of this pain is:","o":["A. T7","B. T8","C. T10","D. L1","E. S1"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"T10","a":"C","why":"<b>T10 (tenth thoracic).</b> The umbilicus lies in the T10 dermatome (the skin strip supplied by one spinal nerve). Midgut pain is referred to the umbilical region at this level.<br>A. T7 (seventh thoracic): xiphoid process.<br>B. T8 (eighth thoracic): upper abdomen, above the umbilicus.<br>D. L1 (first lumbar): groin and pubic region.<br>E. S1 (first sacral): lateral foot and sole.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 22 (Neurovasculature)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":236,"f":"F3903"}]},{"id":"D041-22-1","s":"Which one of the following correctly matches a spinal dermatome with its key abdominal surface landmark?","o":["A. T10 – Inguinal region","B. T5 – Umbilicus","C. L1 – Suprapubic region","D. T7 – Xiphoid process"],"g":["C","D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"T7 – Xiphoid process","a":"D","why":"<b>T7 (seventh thoracic) – xiphoid process</b> and <b>L1 (first lumbar) – suprapubic region</b> are both correct. The L1 iliohypogastric nerve supplies the skin just above the pubis (shown on the lecture slide), and L1 also covers the groin. If forced to pick one, D matches the lecture slide word for word.<br>A. T10 (tenth thoracic) is the umbilicus, not the groin.<br>B. T5 (fifth thoracic) is on the chest; the umbilicus is T10.","ex":"","fl":["The source keyed only D, but C is also true: the L1 dermatome (iliohypogastric nerve) supplies the suprapubic skin, as the lecture's dermatome slide shows."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 22 (Neurovasculature)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":22,"f":"D041"}],"t":1},{"id":"D045-41-1","s":"A patient presents with numbness around the umbilicus after an abdominal procedure. The physician wants to identify the affected dermatome and the nerve supplying that region. Which of the following correctly identifies the dermatome and its innervating nerve?","o":["A. T6 dermatome; iliohypogastric nerve","B. T10 dermatome; intercostal nerve","C. T12 dermatome; subcostal nerve","D. L1 dermatome; lateral femoral cutaneous nerve","E. T8 dermatome; ilioinguinal nerve"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"T10 dermatome; intercostal nerve","a":"B","why":"<b>T10 (tenth thoracic) dermatome; intercostal nerve.</b> The umbilicus is in the T10 dermatome. The tenth intercostal (thoracoabdominal) nerve runs between internal oblique and transversus abdominis to reach this skin.<br>A. T6 (sixth thoracic) is near the xiphoid; iliohypogastric is L1 (first lumbar).<br>C. T12 (twelfth thoracic, subcostal): skin below the umbilicus.<br>D. L1 is the groin; the lateral femoral cutaneous nerve supplies the lateral thigh.<br>E. T8 (eighth thoracic) is above the umbilicus; ilioinguinal supplies the groin.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 22 (Neurovasculature)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":41,"f":"D045"}],"t":1},{"id":"D042-15-1","s":"While trying out a new pilates class, you were doing so many oblique exercises on the reformer. The following morning your obliques were so sore, and knew this feeling would go away. However, the pain has been persisting for weeks and is radiating to the inguinal region. What dermatome is this associated with?","o":["A. T5","B. T6","C. T7","D. T8","E. T9","F. L1"],"g":["F"],"k":["F"],"rv":false,"at":"L1","a":"F","why":"<b>L1 (first lumbar).</b> The L1 dermatome covers the <b>inguinal region</b>. Its nerves, the iliohypogastric and ilioinguinal, run between the oblique muscles, so irritation there can spread pain to the groin.<br>A. T5 (fifth thoracic): chest, below the nipples.<br>B. T6 (sixth thoracic): near the xiphoid.<br>C. T7 (seventh thoracic): xiphoid process.<br>D. T8 (eighth thoracic): upper abdomen.<br>E. T9 (ninth thoracic): just above the umbilicus.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 22 (Neurovasculature)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI module TA review","sl":15,"f":"D042"}]},{"id":"D045-39-1","s":"A 32-year-old woman is scheduled for a procedure that involves a transverse incision along her anterior abdominal wall. To minimize postoperative sensory deficits, the surgeon reviews the innervation of the abdominal wall and the corresponding dermatomal landmarks. Which of the following correctly describes the innervation of the anterior abdominal wall and the dermatomes corresponding to key landmarks?","o":["A. T1–T6 intercostal nerves provide sensory innervation only; xiphoid process corresponds to T4, umbilicus to T8, pubic symphysis to T12.","B. T5–T11 intercostal nerves provide motor and sensory innervation; xiphoid process corresponds to T5, umbilicus to T9, pubic symphysis to T12.","C. T7–T12 intercostal nerves provide motor and sensory innervation; xiphoid process corresponds to T6, umbilicus to T10, pubic symphysis to L1.","D. T7–T12 intercostal nerves provide motor innervation only; xiphoid process corresponds to T7, umbilicus to T9, pubic symphysis to T11.","E. L1–L5 spinal nerves provide motor and sensory innervation; xiphoid process corresponds to L1, umbilicus to L3, pubic symphysis to L5."],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"T7–T12 intercostal nerves provide motor and sensory innervation; xiphoid process corresponds to T6, umbilicus to T10, pubic symphysis to L1.","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> The thoracoabdominal nerves (T7–T11, seventh to eleventh thoracic) and subcostal nerve (T12, twelfth thoracic) carry <b>motor and sensory</b> fibers. Landmarks: <b>umbilicus T10</b> (tenth thoracic), <b>pubic region L1</b> (first lumbar). The lecture puts the xiphoid at T7; some books say T6 (sixth thoracic). Only C gets T10 and L1 right.<br>A. T1–T6 (first to sixth thoracic) supply the chest.<br>B. Umbilicus T9 (ninth thoracic) and pubis T12 are wrong.<br>D. Not motor only; umbilicus is not T9.<br>E. L2–L5 (second to fifth lumbar) do not supply the abdominal wall.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 22 (Neurovasculature)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 14 (External Oblique)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":39,"f":"D045"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":42,"label":"Inguinal ligament: ASIS to pubic tubercle","slides":[{"src":"sl/G003-30.jpg","cap":"Inguinal ligament from anterior superior iliac spine to pubic tubercle","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 30"},{"src":"sl/G003-7.jpg","cap":"Surface marks: anterior superior iliac spine, inguinal ligament, pubic tubercle","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 7"}],"q":[{"id":"D045-49-1","s":"A medical student is learning to perform a proper inguinal hernia examination. The instructor asks the student to identify the inguinal ligament using palpable surface landmarks. Which of the following landmarks are used to identify the inguinal ligament?","o":["A. Greater trochanter and ischial tuberosity","B. Anterior superior iliac spine (ASIS) and pubic tubercle","C. Umbilicus and pubic symphysis","D. Iliac crest and sacral promontory","E. Anterior inferior iliac spine (AIIS) and coccyx"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Anterior superior iliac spine (ASIS) and pubic tubercle","a":"B","why":"<b>ASIS (anterior superior iliac spine) and pubic tubercle.</b> Both are palpable. The inguinal ligament runs between them as the folded lower edge of the external oblique aponeurosis.<br>A. Greater trochanter and ischial tuberosity: hip landmarks.<br>C. Umbilicus and pubic symphysis: vertical midline points.<br>D. Iliac crest and sacral promontory: higher and posterior.<br>E. AIIS (anterior inferior iliac spine) and coccyx: wrong points.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 29 (Intro to Inguinal region/recap)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 30 (Inguinal ligament)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":49,"f":"D045"}],"t":1},{"id":"D042-18-1","s":"A 57 year old male is complaining of left groin pain and decreased flexibility of his hip joint. Following palpation, the patient was diagnosed with hypertonicity of the inguinal ligament. The inguinal ligament extends from this (BLANK) landmark to this (BLANK) landmark.","o":["A. Anterior inferior iliac spine, pubic tubercle","B. Anterior superior iliac spine, pubic tubercle","C. Anterior inferior iliac spine, pubic symphysis","D. Anterior superior iliac spine, pubic symphysis"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Anterior superior iliac spine, pubic tubercle","a":"B","why":"<b>ASIS (anterior superior iliac spine) to pubic tubercle.</b> The inguinal ligament is the folded lower edge of the external oblique aponeurosis between these two bony points.<br>A. AIIS (anterior inferior iliac spine): lower; origin of rectus femoris.<br>C. AIIS and pubic symphysis: both wrong.<br>D. Pubic symphysis: the midline joint; the ligament ends on the pubic tubercle.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 29 (Intro to Inguinal region/recap)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 30 (Inguinal ligament)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI module TA review","sl":18,"f":"D042"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":42,"label":"Femoral vessels pass under the inguinal ligament","slides":[{"src":"sl/G003-30.jpg","cap":"Femoral artery and vein passing under the inguinal ligament","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 30"}],"q":[{"id":"D041-28-1","s":"What structure passes inferior to the inguinal ligament to enter the lower limb?","o":["A. Testicular artery","B. Femoral artery and vein","C. Obturator nerve","D. Inferior epigastric vessels"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Femoral artery and vein","a":"B","why":"<b>Femoral artery and vein.</b> The external iliac vessels pass under the inguinal ligament and become the femoral vessels in the thigh.<br>A. Testicular artery: goes through the inguinal canal, above the ligament.<br>C. Obturator nerve: leaves through the obturator canal.<br>D. Inferior epigastric vessels: run up the inside of the anterior wall.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 29 (Intro to Inguinal region/recap)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 30 (Inguinal ligament)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":28,"f":"D041"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":43,"label":"Deep ring: hole in transversalis fascia","slides":[{"src":"sl/G003-31.jpg","cap":"Internal spermatic fascia starts from transversalis fascia at the deep ring","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 31"},{"src":"sl/D045-58.jpg","cap":"Deep ring in transversalis fascia; superficial ring in external oblique aponeurosis","where":"2025 Montana TA review, slide 58"}],"q":[{"id":"D042-20-1","s":"A 45-year-old male was admitted to the hospital with groin pain and a palpable mass just superior to the inguinal ligament. The patient was diagnosed with an inguinal hernia and a surgical repair was performed. During the operation the surgeon found a loop of intestine passing through the deep inguinal ring. Which of the following best describes the structures that form the deep and superficial inguinal rings?","o":["A. The deep inguinal ring is an opening in the external oblique aponeurosis; the superficial ring is formed by the transversalis fascia","B. The deep inguinal ring is an opening in the transversalis fascia; the superficial ring is an opening in the external oblique aponeurosis","C. The deep inguinal ring is formed by the internal oblique muscle; the superficial ring is formed by the transversus abdominis","D. Both the deep and superficial inguinal rings are openings in the transversus abdominis muscle","E. The deep inguinal ring is located medial to the inferior epigastric vessels; the superficial ring is located lateral to the pubic tubercle"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"The deep inguinal ring is an opening in the transversalis fascia; the superficial ring is an opening in the external oblique aponeurosis","a":"B","why":"<b>Deep ring = opening in the transversalis fascia; superficial ring = opening in the external oblique aponeurosis.</b> The canal starts at the innermost layer and ends at the outermost.<br>A. Swaps the two rings.<br>C. Internal oblique and transversus abdominis arch over the canal as its roof; they form neither ring.<br>D. Transversus abdominis forms neither ring.<br>E. The deep ring lies lateral, not medial, to the inferior epigastric vessels.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 36 (Deep Inguinal ring)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 31 (Inguinal canal)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI module TA review","sl":20,"f":"D042"}]},{"id":"D045-56-1","s":"A 26-year-old man presents with a painless swelling in the right groin that increases when he coughs. The surgeon explains that the hernia sac is passing through the deep inguinal ring, which is the entrance to the inguinal canal. Which of the following best describes the anatomical structure and location of the deep inguinal ring?","o":["A. A triangular opening in the external oblique aponeurosis superior to the pubic tubercle","B. A slitlike defect in the transversus abdominis muscle lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels","C. An oval defect in the internal oblique muscle through which the ilioinguinal nerve exits","D. A circular opening in the transversalis fascia lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels","E. A fascial window formed by splitting fibers of Scarpa’s fascia near the inguinal ligament"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"A circular opening in the transversalis fascia lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels","a":"D","why":"<b>A circular opening in the transversalis fascia, lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels.</b> It is the entrance of the inguinal canal.<br>A. Triangular opening in the external oblique aponeurosis: that is the superficial ring, the exit.<br>B. Transversus abdominis arches over the canal; it does not form the ring.<br>C. Internal oblique gives the cremaster, not the ring; the ilioinguinal nerve enters the canal from the side.<br>E. Scarpa's fascia lies under the skin and forms no ring.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 36 (Deep Inguinal ring)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":56,"f":"D045"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3903-226-1","s":"Which of the following is incorrect pertaining to the deep (internal) inguinal ring?","o":["A) It is an opening in the peritoneum.","B) It is located lateral to the inferior epigastric artery.","C) It transmits the spermatic cord in males.","D) It transmits the round ligament of the uterus in females.","E) It forms the deep entrance to the inguinal canal."],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"It is an opening in the peritoneum (it is an opening in the transversalis fascia)","a":"A","why":"<b>A is the false statement.</b> The deep ring is an opening in the <b>transversalis fascia</b>, not in the peritoneum. Peritoneum covers it from inside.<br>B. True: it lies lateral to the inferior epigastric artery.<br>C. True: the spermatic cord enters here in males.<br>D. True: the round ligament enters here in females.<br>E. True: it is the inner (deep) entrance of the canal.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 36 (Deep Inguinal ring)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 29 (Intro to Inguinal region/recap)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":226,"f":"F3903"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":43,"label":"Superficial ring = gap in external oblique aponeurosis","slides":[{"src":"sl/G003-31.jpg","cap":"Superficial inguinal ring in external oblique aponeurosis, with intercrural fibers","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 31"},{"src":"sl/D045-58.jpg","cap":"Superficial ring in external oblique aponeurosis; deep ring in transversalis fascia","where":"2025 Montana TA review, slide 58"}],"q":[{"id":"F0008-66-1","s":"The superficial inguinal ring is an opening in which structure?","o":["a. Scarpa's fascia","b. Falx inguinalis","c. Internal abdominal oblique muscle","d. External abdominal oblique aponeurosis","e. Transversalis fascia"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"External abdominal oblique aponeurosis","a":"D","why":"<b>External abdominal oblique aponeurosis.</b> The superficial inguinal ring is a triangular gap in this aponeurosis, just above and lateral to the pubic tubercle. It is the exit of the inguinal canal.<br>A. Scarpa fascia: subcutaneous; it has no ring.<br>B. Falx inguinalis (conjoint tendon): medial part of the posterior wall.<br>C. Internal oblique: forms part of the anterior wall and roof, and the cremaster.<br>E. Transversalis fascia: holds the deep (internal) ring.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 14 (External Oblique)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 34 (Inguinal canal)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":66,"f":"F0008"}]},{"id":"D045-54-1","s":"During a physical exam on a 30-year-old man with a suspected inguinal hernia, the physician palpates the area just superior and lateral to the pubic tubercle. She explains to the medical student that this location corresponds to the exit of the inguinal canal. Which of the following best describes the anatomical structure and boundaries of the superficial inguinal ring?","o":["A. A circular opening in the transversalis fascia bordered by the medial and lateral crura","B. A slitlike opening in the transversus abdominis muscle lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels","C. A triangular opening in the external oblique aponeurosis formed by the medial and lateral crura of the external oblique","D. An oval defect in the internal oblique muscle through which the ilioinguinal nerve exits","E. A fascial window formed by splitting fibers of Scarpa’s fascia near the pubic crest"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"A triangular opening in the external oblique aponeurosis formed by the medial and lateral crura of the external oblique","a":"C","why":"<b>A triangular opening in the external oblique aponeurosis</b>, bounded by its medial and lateral crura and bridged by intercrural fibers. It sits above and lateral to the pubic tubercle and is the exit of the canal.<br>A. An opening in transversalis fascia is the deep ring, which has no crura.<br>B. Transversus abdominis forms neither ring.<br>D. Internal oblique has no ring; it gives rise to the cremaster.<br>E. Scarpa fascia has no ring.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 31 (Inguinal canal)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":54,"f":"D045"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":43,"label":"Deep ring sits above the mid-inguinal point","slides":[{"src":"sl/G003-30.jpg","cap":"Midclavicular line crossing the inguinal ligament marks the deep inguinal ring","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 30"},{"src":"sl/G003-6.jpg","cap":"Midclavicular line meets the inguinal line at the midinguinal point","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 6"}],"q":[{"id":"F3903-242-1","s":"A patient comes into the clinic with a bulge in the groin, which his physician diagnosed as an inguinal hernia. To determine whether it is an indirect or direct inguinal hernia, the physician applies pressure over the deep inguinal ring, with his left thumb. The patient is then asked to cough. The physician notes that the hernia does not push out of the abdomen and concludes that it is an indirect inguinal hernia. Where did the physician apply pressure?","o":["A. Immediately lateral to the pubic tubercle","B. Immediately lateral to the lacunar ligament","C. Half an inch above the mid inguinal point","D. One inch below the mid inguinal ligament","E. Immediately medial to the femoral vein"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Half an inch above the mid inguinal point","a":"C","why":"<b>Half an inch above the mid-inguinal point.</b> The deep inguinal ring lies just above the middle of the inguinal ligament, where the midclavicular line crosses it. Pressing there blocks an indirect hernia, which starts at the deep ring.<br>A. Just lateral to the pubic tubercle: the superficial ring.<br>B. Lateral to the lacunar ligament: the femoral ring.<br>D. Below the ligament: the thigh.<br>E. Medial to the femoral vein: the femoral canal.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 30 (Inguinal ligament)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 36 (Deep Inguinal ring)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":242,"f":"F3903"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":44,"label":"Posterior wall (conjoint tendon) weakness → direct hernia","slides":[{"src":"sl/G003-34.jpg","cap":"Canal walls table: posterior wall transversalis fascia, conjoint tendon medially","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 34"},{"src":"sl/G003-37.jpg","cap":"Direct hernia pushes through peritoneum and transversalis fascia in Hesselbach triangle","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 37"}],"q":[{"id":"F3903-240-1","s":"During surgical repair of inguinal hernias it is important to recognize the boundaries of the inguinal canal. Which of the following structures forms part of the posterior boundary of the inguinal canal?","o":["A. Aponeurosis of external oblique","B. Conjoint tendon","C. Inguinal ligament","D. Lacunar ligament","E. Pectineal ligament"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Conjoint tendon","a":"B","why":"<b>Conjoint tendon.</b> The posterior wall of the inguinal canal is transversalis fascia, reinforced medially by the conjoint tendon (internal oblique plus transversus abdominis aponeuroses, inserting on the pubic crest).<br>A. External oblique aponeurosis: the anterior wall.<br>C. Inguinal ligament: the floor.<br>D. Lacunar ligament: floor of the medial third.<br>E. Pectineal ligament: on the pubic bone, below the canal.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 34 (Inguinal canal)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":240,"f":"F3903"}],"t":1},{"id":"F1180-237-1","s":"A 45-year-old man is admitted to the hospital with a palpable and painful mass at his groin that is exacerbated when he stands erect or physically exerts himself. Physical examination indicates the probability of a direct inguinal hernia, a diagnosis that is confirmed laparoscopically. Which of the following is the most likely cause of this type of inguinal hernia?","o":["A. Defective transversalis fascia around the deep inguinal ring","B. Defective peritoneum around the deep inguinal ring","C. Defective aponeurosis of external abdominal oblique muscle","D. Defective extraperitoneal connective tissue","E. Defective aponeurosis of transversus abdominis muscle"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Defective aponeurosis of transversus abdominis muscle","a":"E","why":"<b>Defective aponeurosis of transversus abdominis.</b> A direct hernia pushes through a weak posterior wall in Hesselbach triangle. That wall is transversalis fascia backed by the transversus abdominis aponeurosis (with internal oblique, the conjoint tendon).<br>A. A defect around the deep ring gives an indirect hernia.<br>B. Open peritoneum at the deep ring also gives an indirect hernia.<br>C. External oblique aponeurosis: anterior wall; not the defect.<br>D. Extraperitoneal tissue: not a supporting layer.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 34 (Inguinal canal)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 37 (Direct Vs. Indirect Inguinal Hernias)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":237,"f":"F1180"},{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":181,"f":"F1178"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":45,"label":"Inguinal canal contents: spermatic cord / round ligament","slides":[{"src":"sl/G003-35.jpg","cap":"Canal contents: spermatic cord (male) or round ligament (female), plus ilioinguinal nerve","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 35"},{"src":"sl/G003-42.jpg","cap":"Spermatic cord contents: ductus deferens, testicular artery, pampiniform plexus","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 42"}],"q":[{"id":"D041-34-1","s":"In males, which structure passes through the inguinal canal and continues into the scrotum?","o":["A. Femoral nerve","B. Round ligament","C. Obturator nerve","D. Spermatic cord"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Spermatic cord","a":"D","why":"<b>Spermatic cord.</b> It enters the deep ring, runs through the inguinal canal, leaves at the superficial ring and descends into the scrotum. The ilioinguinal nerve also runs through the canal.<br>A. Femoral nerve: passes under the inguinal ligament into the thigh.<br>B. Round ligament: the female content, ending in the labia majora.<br>C. Obturator nerve: leaves through the obturator canal.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 35 (Inguinal Canal Contents)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 31 (Inguinal canal)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":34,"f":"D041"}],"t":1},{"id":"D045-60-1","s":"A 22-year-old man is undergoing an open inguinal hernia repair. During the procedure, the surgeon identifies a structure emerging from the deep inguinal ring and running within the inguinal canal toward the superficial inguinal ring. In a female patient, this structure would be replaced by a homologous structure. Which of the following correctly lists the major contents of the inguinal canal in males and females?","o":["A. Male: vas deferens; Female: ovarian ligament","B. Male: testicular artery only; Female: round ligament of the uterus only","C. Male: spermatic cord; Female: round ligament of the uterus","D. Male: pampiniform plexus only; Female: suspensory ligament of the ovary","E. Male: ilioinguinal nerve; Female: iliohypogastric nerve"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Male: spermatic cord; Female: round ligament of the uterus","a":"C","why":"<b>Male: spermatic cord; female: round ligament of the uterus.</b> The ilioinguinal nerve runs through the canal in both sexes.<br>A. Vas deferens is only one part of the cord; the ovarian ligament stays in the pelvis.<br>B. Testicular artery is only one part of the cord.<br>D. Pampiniform plexus is only one part; the suspensory ligament of the ovary stays in the pelvis.<br>E. Ilioinguinal runs in both sexes; the iliohypogastric nerve does not go through the canal.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 35 (Inguinal Canal Contents)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":60,"f":"D045"}]},{"id":"D042-23-1","s":"A 32-year-old patient is undergoing surgical repair of an inguinal hernia. During the procedure, the surgeon visualizes structures within the inguinal canal. Which of the following correctly identifies the major contents of the inguinal canal in males and females?","o":["A. Males: ovarian ligament; Females: round ligament of uterus","B. Males: pampiniform plexus and ductus deferens; Females: round ligament of uterus","C. Males: inferior epigastric vessels; Females: superior epigastric vessels","D. Males: iliohypogastric nerve; Females: ilioinguinal nerve only","E. Males: obturator nerve; Females: femoral branch of genitofemoral nerve"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Males: pampiniform plexus and ductus deferens; Females: round ligament of uterus","a":"B","why":"<b>Males: pampiniform plexus and ductus deferens (parts of the spermatic cord); females: round ligament of the uterus.</b> The ilioinguinal nerve also passes in both sexes.<br>A. The ovarian ligament stays in the pelvis.<br>C. Epigastric vessels run in the wall, not in the canal.<br>D. The iliohypogastric nerve does not pass through the canal.<br>E. The obturator nerve uses the obturator canal; the femoral branch of the genitofemoral nerve goes to the thigh.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 35 (Inguinal Canal Contents)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 42 (Spermatic Cord Contents)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI module TA review","sl":23,"f":"D042"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":45,"label":"Female inguinal canal: round ligament of uterus","slides":[{"src":"sl/G003-35.jpg","cap":"Canal contents: spermatic cord in males, round ligament of uterus in females","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 35"},{"src":"sl/D042-25.jpg","cap":"Female inguinal canal drawing: round ligament of uterus with ilioinguinal nerve","where":"2025 TA review, slide 25"}],"q":[{"id":"F0008-68-1","s":"Which structure passes through the deep inguinal ring?","o":["a. Round ligament of the uterus","b. Iliohypogastric nerve","c. Ilioinguinal nerve","d. Inferior epigastric artery","e. umbilical ligament"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Round ligament of the uterus","a":"A","why":"<b>Round ligament of the uterus.</b> It runs from the uterus through the deep inguinal ring, along the canal, and out the superficial ring to the labium majus.<br>B. Iliohypogastric nerve: pierces the muscles above the canal; it is not in it.<br>C. Ilioinguinal nerve: is in the canal, but enters from the side through the internal oblique, not through the deep ring.<br>D. Inferior epigastric artery: lies just medial to the deep ring, outside it.<br>E. Umbilical ligament: a fold on the inner abdominal wall near the midline.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 35 (Inguinal Canal Contents)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 31 (Inguinal canal)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":68,"f":"F0008"}]},{"id":"F1228-112-1","s":"Which of the following structures is a content of the inguinal canal and passes through the deep inguinal ring?","o":["A. Urachus","B. Medial Umbilical Ligament","C. Inferior epigastric artery","D. Round Ligament of the uterus","E. ilioinguinal nerve"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Round Ligament of the uterus","a":"D","why":"<b>Round ligament of the uterus.</b> It is the female content of the inguinal canal and the only choice that passes through the deep ring.<br>A. Urachus: runs in the midline from bladder to umbilicus.<br>B. Medial umbilical ligament: the closed-off umbilical artery on the inner wall; not in the canal.<br>C. Inferior epigastric artery: forms the medial edge of the deep ring; it does not pass through it.<br>E. Ilioinguinal nerve: lies in the canal but enters from the side, not through the deep ring.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 35 (Inguinal Canal Contents)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 31 (Inguinal canal)"],"src":[{"t":"2019 review","n":"old Exam 2 review: abdomen part only","sl":112,"f":"F1228"}]},{"id":"D045-62-1","s":"A 34-year-old woman comes to the clinic with a 3-month history of intermittent aching pain in her right groin that worsens when standing for long periods. She has also noticed a small, reducible bulge just superior to the pubic tubercle. An ultrasound confirms an inguinal hernia. During surgical repair, the surgeon identifies the structure that normally traverses the inguinal canal in females and notes it is mildly stretched by the hernia sac. Which of the following structures is being observed?","o":["A. Ovarian ligament","B. Round ligament of the uterus","C. Inferior epigastric artery","D. Genitofemoral nerve (genital branch)","E. Ureter"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Round ligament of the uterus","a":"B","why":"<b>Round ligament of the uterus.</b> In females it is the main structure in the inguinal canal. It enters at the deep ring and ends in the labium majus.<br>A. Ovarian ligament: joins the ovary to the uterus inside the pelvis; never enters the canal.<br>C. Inferior epigastric artery: runs just medial to the deep ring, outside the canal.<br>D. Genital branch of genitofemoral nerve: a small nerve that can run with the round ligament, not the main content.<br>E. Ureter: stays in the pelvis, far from the canal.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 35 (Inguinal Canal Contents)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 31 (Inguinal canal)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":62,"f":"D045"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":46,"label":"Umbilical folds: urachus, umbilical arteries, epigastric vessels","slides":[{"src":"sl/G003-25.jpg","cap":"Five folds: median (urachus), medial (umbilical arteries), lateral (inferior epigastric)","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 25"},{"src":"sl/G003-18.jpg","cap":"Below-arcuate section: median umbilical ligament (urachus) and medial umbilical fold","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 18"}],"q":[{"id":"F3905-14-1","s":"A 25-year-old woman visits her OB-GYN one year after a normal pregnancy and vaginal delivery of healthy twins. Following a physical examination, the physician informs the woman that she and her children are healthy and without any issues. At this time, the median umbilical folds contain which of the following structures in her newborn children?","o":["A. Obliterated umbilical vein","B. Obliterated urachus","C. Obliterated umbilical arteries","D. Obliterated ductus venosus","E. Obliterated inferior epigastric vessels"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Obliterated urachus","a":"B","why":"<b>Obliterated urachus.</b> The single median umbilical fold covers the urachus (allantois remnant), running from the top of the bladder to the umbilicus.<br>A. Obliterated umbilical vein: round ligament of the liver, in the falciform ligament.<br>C. Obliterated umbilical arteries: in the two medial umbilical folds.<br>D. Ductus venosus: becomes the ligamentum venosum on the liver.<br>E. Inferior epigastric vessels: in the lateral folds; they stay open.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 25 (Internal Surface of Abdominal Wall)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA weekly","n":"09-22-2022 TA session (abdominal wall, inguinal)","sl":14,"f":"F3905"}],"t":1},{"id":"F0008-72-1","s":"During a laproscopic examination of the deep surface of the lower anterior abdominal wall (using a lighted scope on a thin tube inserted through the wall), the attending physician noted something of interest and asked the young resident to look at the medial inguinal fossa. To do so, the young doctor would have to look at the area between the:","o":["a. obliterated umbilical artery and inferior epigastric artery","b. obliterated umbilical artery and urachus","c. inferior epigastric artery and lateral umbilical fold","d. inferior epigastric artery and urachus","e. median umbilical ligament and medial umbilical ligament"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"obliterated umbilical artery and inferior epigastric artery","a":"A","why":"<b>Between the obliterated umbilical artery and the inferior epigastric artery.</b> The medial inguinal fossa (Hesselbach triangle area) lies between the medial umbilical fold and the lateral umbilical fold. Direct hernias come through it.<br>B. Umbilical artery and urachus: the supravesical fossa.<br>C. The inferior epigastric artery is inside the lateral fold; same structure.<br>D. Inferior epigastric and urachus: spans two fossae.<br>E. Median and medial umbilical ligaments: the supravesical fossa.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 25 (Internal Surface of Abdominal Wall)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":72,"f":"F0008"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":46,"label":"Inferior epigastric vessels: medial edge of deep ring","slides":[{"src":"sl/G003-25.jpg","cap":"Inner abdominal wall: inferior epigastric vessels between deep ring and Hesselbach's triangle","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 25"},{"src":"sl/D045-73.jpg","cap":"Inferior epigastric vessels: deep ring lateral, Hesselbach's triangle (pink) medial","where":"2025 Montana TA review, slide 73"}],"q":[{"id":"D041-31-1","s":"To avoid uncontrolled bleeding, the surgeon clamps vessels just medial to the deep inguinal ring opening. Which vessels were clamped?","o":["A. Femoral vessels","B. Superficial epigastric vessels","C. Inferior epigastric vessels","D. External pudendal vessels"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Inferior epigastric vessels","a":"C","why":"<b>Inferior epigastric vessels.</b> They run up just medial to the deep inguinal ring, inside the lateral umbilical fold, so they are the vessels hugging the ring.<br>A. Femoral vessels: pass below the inguinal ligament into the thigh.<br>B. Superficial epigastric vessels: lie in the fat under the skin, not at the deep ring.<br>D. External pudendal vessels: thigh vessels that run to the scrotum or labia.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 36 (Deep Inguinal ring)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 25 (Internal Surface of Abdominal Wall)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":31,"f":"D041"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3902-67-1","s":"You are currently on your urology rotation when a patient presents to the clinic with groin pain. On exam, you notice a palpable mass and your attending tells you that it’s a hernia that is next to the spermatic cord. During the surgical repair, the surgeon notices that it does not go through the deep inguinal canal. What structure lies lateral to this hernia?","o":["A. Rectus abdominus","B. Medial umbilical fold","C. Inferior epigastric vessel","D. Ductus deferens"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Inferior epigastric vessel","a":"C","why":"<b>Inferior epigastric vessels.</b> A hernia that skips the deep ring is a direct hernia. It comes through Hesselbach's triangle, whose lateral border is the inferior epigastric vessels.<br>A. Rectus abdominis: forms the medial border, so it lies medial to the hernia.<br>B. Medial umbilical fold: lies medial to Hesselbach's triangle.<br>D. Ductus deferens: travels in the spermatic cord through the deep ring; it is not the lateral border.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 36 (Deep Inguinal ring)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 25 (Internal Surface of Abdominal Wall)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":67,"f":"F3902"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":47,"label":"Hesselbach triangle borders; direct hernias medial","slides":[{"src":"sl/G003-25.jpg","cap":"Posterior view: Hesselbach triangle medial to inferior epigastric vessels; deep ring lateral","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 25"},{"src":"sl/D045-73.jpg","cap":"Hesselbach triangle bounded by rectus, inferior epigastric vessels, inguinal ligament","where":"2025 Montana TA review, slide 73"},{"src":"sl/G003-37.jpg","cap":"Direct hernia medial to inferior epigastric vessels; indirect lateral via deep ring","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 37"}],"q":[{"id":"D042-28-1","s":"During an open repair of a direct inguinal hernia, the surgeon identifies the defect occurring within the inguinal (Hesselbach's) triangle. Which of the following correctly describes the boundaries of this anatomical region?","o":["A. Inguinal ligament, femoral vein, inferior epigastric vessels","B. Superior epigastric vessels, inguinal ligament, pubic tubercle","C. Lacunar ligament, pectineal line, femoral artery","D. Inguinal ligament, lateral border of rectus abdominis, inferior epigastric vessels","E. Deep inguinal ring, superficial inguinal ring, inguinal ligament"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Inguinal ligament, lateral border of rectus abdominis, inferior epigastric vessels","a":"D","why":"<b>Inguinal ligament, lateral border of rectus abdominis, inferior epigastric vessels.</b> Hesselbach (inguinal) triangle: inferior = inguinal ligament; medial = rectus edge; lateral = inferior epigastric vessels. Direct hernias push through it.<br>A. Femoral vein lies below the inguinal ligament.<br>B. Superior epigastric vessels are in the upper wall.<br>C. Lacunar ligament, pectineal line and femoral artery belong to the femoral region.<br>E. Deep and superficial rings are the two ends of the inguinal canal.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 25 (Internal Surface of Abdominal Wall)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 29 (Intro to Inguinal region/recap)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI module TA review","sl":28,"f":"D042"}],"t":1},{"id":"D041-44-1","s":"After diagnosing those two hernias your attending asks you the boundaries of the triangle of Hesselbach. Which of the following is matched incorrectly regarding these boundaries?","o":["A. Lateral border: inferior iliac artery and vein","B. Medial border: rectus abdominis","C. Lateral border: inferior epigastric vessels","D. Inferior: inguinal ligament"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Lateral border: inferior iliac artery and vein","a":"A","why":"<b>A is wrong.</b> There is no 'inferior iliac artery'. The lateral border of Hesselbach triangle is the <b>inferior epigastric vessels</b>.<br>B. Medial border: rectus abdominis (its lateral edge). Correct.<br>C. Lateral border: inferior epigastric vessels. Correct.<br>D. Inferior border: inguinal ligament. Correct.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 25 (Internal Surface of Abdominal Wall)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 29 (Intro to Inguinal region/recap)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":44,"f":"D041"}],"t":1},{"id":"D045-71-1","s":"A 55-year-old man presents with a bulge in the lower abdominal wall just above the inguinal ligament. He is diagnosed with a direct inguinal hernia. Through which anatomical boundaries does a direct inguinal hernia protrude?","o":["A. Lateral border: rectus abdominis; medial border: inferior epigastric vessels; inferior border: inguinal ligament","B. Lateral border: inferior epigastric vessels; medial border: rectus abdominis; inferior border: inguinal ligament","C. Lateral border: inguinal ligament; medial border: rectus abdominis; superior border: internal oblique","D. Lateral border: spermatic cord; medial border: inguinal ligament; superior border: transversalis fascia","E. Lateral border: inferior epigastric vessels; medial border: lateral border of rectus abdominis; inferior border: inguinal ligament"],"g":["B","E"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Lateral border: inferior epigastric vessels; medial border: rectus abdominis; inferior border: inguinal ligament","a":"B","why":"<b>Lateral: inferior epigastric vessels; medial: rectus abdominis (its lateral border); inferior: inguinal ligament.</b> B and E give the same three borders; E is just worded more exactly.<br>A. Swaps medial and lateral.<br>C. The inguinal ligament is the inferior border; internal oblique is not a border.<br>D. Spermatic cord and transversalis fascia are not borders.","ex":"","fl":["The source keyed only B, but E lists the same three borders (more precisely, the lateral border of rectus abdominis), so both are right."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 25 (Internal Surface of Abdominal Wall)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 29 (Intro to Inguinal region/recap)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":71,"f":"D045"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3905-17-1","s":"A 40 yo male patient presents to your office presenting with lower abdominal pain. Upon further examination, you realize the discomfort is localized inferolateral to the umbilicus. The patient states they are not physically active but they were helping their mother move into a new apartment and noticed the pain started later that night. You correctly diagnose them with an inguinal hernia through the Triangle of Hasselbach. Which of the following statements is correct: The hernia is…","o":["A. Direct - occurring medial to the inferior epigastric vessels and lateral to the abdominal wall","B. Indirect - occurring medial to the inferior epigastric vessels and lateral to the abdominal wall","C. Direct- occurring lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels and is due to a weakened abdominal wall","D. Indirect - occurring lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels and is due to a weakened abdominal wall"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Direct - occurring medial to the inferior epigastric vessels and lateral to the abdominal wall","a":"A","why":"<b>Direct, medial to the inferior epigastric vessels.</b> Hesselbach triangle lies medial to these vessels, so a hernia through it is direct, from a weak posterior wall (transversalis fascia). Choice A's 'lateral to the abdominal wall' means lateral to rectus abdominis.<br>B. Indirect hernias do not pass through the triangle.<br>C. Direct hernias are medial, not lateral.<br>D. Indirect hernias are lateral but go through the deep ring, not the triangle.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 29 (Intro to Inguinal region/recap)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 37 (Direct Vs. Indirect Inguinal Hernias)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA weekly","n":"09-22-2022 TA session (abdominal wall, inguinal)","sl":17,"f":"F3905"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":48,"label":"Direct hernia → through Hesselbach's triangle","slides":[{"src":"sl/G003-37.jpg","cap":"Direct hernia passes medial to inferior epigastric vessels through the inguinal triangle","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 37"},{"src":"sl/D045-73.jpg","cap":"Hesselbach's triangle bordered by rectus abdominis, inferior epigastric vessels, inguinal ligament","where":"2025 Montana TA review, slide 73"},{"src":"sl/F1228-124.jpg","cap":"Table: direct hernias are acquired, older, medial, rarely reach the scrotum","where":"2019 review, slide 124"}],"q":[{"id":"D041-37-1","s":"A patient presents to the ED with a mass located in the groin area. You are concerned about a potential hernia. Which of the following would classify the hernia as direct?","o":["A. It occurs through the deep inguinal ring","B. It is covered by the layers of the spermatic cord","C. Its lateral the inferior epigastric vessels","D. It occurs through the triangle of Hesselbach"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"It occurs through the triangle of Hesselbach","a":"D","why":"<b>It occurs through the triangle of Hesselbach.</b> This weak area lies medial to the inferior epigastric vessels, above the inguinal ligament and lateral to the rectus abdominis.<br>A. Through the deep ring: indirect.<br>B. Covered by spermatic cord layers: indirect. A direct sac is covered only by peritoneum and transversalis fascia.<br>C. Lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels: indirect.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 37 (Direct Vs. Indirect Inguinal Hernias)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 36 (Deep Inguinal ring)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":37,"f":"D041"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3903-244-1","s":"A 54-year-old man has pain in the groin. On examination, a palpable mass is felt in the region of the superficial inguinal ring which is diagnosed as a direct inguinal hernia. The hernial sac most likely:","o":["A. Is covered by all three layers of the spermatic fascia","B. Passes lateral to the inferior epigastric artery","C. Passes through linea alba of the anterior abdominal wall","D. Passes through the deep inguinal ring","E. Passes through the inguinal (Hesselbach’s) triangle"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Passes through the inguinal (Hesselbach’s) triangle","a":"E","why":"<b>Passes through the inguinal (Hesselbach's) triangle.</b> That is where every direct hernia comes through, medial to the inferior epigastric artery.<br>A. All three spermatic fasciae: an indirect sac, which runs inside the cord.<br>B. Lateral to the inferior epigastric artery: indirect.<br>C. Linea alba: the midline, site of epigastric and umbilical hernias.<br>D. Deep inguinal ring: indirect.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 37 (Direct Vs. Indirect Inguinal Hernias)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 36 (Deep Inguinal ring)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":244,"f":"F3903"}],"t":1},{"id":"F1228-122-1","s":"Which of the following is false about direct hernias?","o":["A. Occurs in older age","B. Lies lateral to inferior epigastric vessels","C. Does not descend into scrotum","D. Associated with weakness of posterior wall of inguinal canal","E. Acquired"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Lies lateral to inferior epigastric vessels","a":"B","why":"<b>B is false.</b> A direct hernia lies <b>medial</b> to the inferior epigastric vessels. Lateral means indirect.<br>A. True: direct hernias occur in older people.<br>C. True: they rarely descend into the scrotum.<br>D. True: they come through a weak back wall of the canal (Hesselbach's triangle).<br>E. True: they are acquired.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 37 (Direct Vs. Indirect Inguinal Hernias)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 29 (Intro to Inguinal region/recap)"],"src":[{"t":"2019 review","n":"old Exam 2 review: abdomen part only","sl":122,"f":"F1228"}]},{"id":"D045-74-1","s":"A 68-year-old man presents with a painless bulge in his lower abdomen. It becomes more prominent when he coughs or strains. Imaging shows a direct inguinal hernia. Which of the following statements about hernias passing through Hesselbach’s triangle is correct?","o":["A. They are most commonly associated with a patent processus vaginalis.","B. They are always bilateral.","C. They pass medial to the inferior epigastric vessels and rarely enter the scrotum.","D. They pass through the deep inguinal ring along the spermatic cord.","E. They are usually congenital and seen in young men."],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"They pass medial to the inferior epigastric vessels and rarely enter the scrotum.","a":"C","why":"<b>They pass medial to the inferior epigastric vessels and rarely enter the scrotum.</b> A direct sac pushes straight forward through Hesselbach's triangle, outside the cord, so it seldom goes down to the scrotum.<br>A. An open processus vaginalis causes indirect hernias.<br>B. They can be on one or both sides; not always both.<br>D. Following the cord through the deep ring is indirect.<br>E. Congenital and young describes indirect. Direct hernias are acquired, in older men.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 37 (Direct Vs. Indirect Inguinal Hernias)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 25 (Internal Surface of Abdominal Wall)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":74,"f":"D045"}],"t":1},{"id":"F1180-233-1","s":"A 6 year old boy comes to his pediatrician with a lump in the groin. On examination, the physician makes a diagnosis of direct inguinal hernia because the hernia tissue?","o":["A. Enters the Triangle of Hesselbach","B. Lies lateral to the inferior epigastric artery","C. Is covered by spermatic fascia","D. Descends into the scrotum","E. Develops after birth"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Enters the Triangle of Hesselbach","a":"A","why":"<b>It enters the triangle of Hesselbach.</b> Every direct hernia pushes through this weak area, medial to the inferior epigastric artery.<br>B. Lateral to the inferior epigastric artery: indirect.<br>C. Covered by the spermatic fasciae: indirect, because it runs inside the cord.<br>D. Descends into the scrotum: typical of indirect.<br>E. Develops after birth: true of many hernias of both types, so it cannot decide the type.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 36 (Deep Inguinal ring)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 37 (Direct Vs. Indirect Inguinal Hernias)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":233,"f":"F1180"},{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":177,"f":"F1178"}]},{"id":"D045-67-1","s":"A 65-year-old man notices a bulge in his lower abdomen just above the inguinal ligament that increases when he coughs or strains. The swelling does not extend into the scrotum. During surgery, the hernia is found to pass medial to the inferior epigastric vessels through a weakened area of the abdominal wall. Which of the following statements about this hernia is correct?","o":["A. It is an indirect inguinal hernia, medial to the inferior epigastric vessels, and rarely enters the scrotum.","B. It is an indirect inguinal hernia that passes through the deep inguinal ring.","C. It is a femoral hernia located lateral to the pubic tubercle.","D. It is a direct inguinal hernia, usually acquired, and passes through Hesselbach’s triangle.","E. It is a direct inguinal hernia, typically congenital, following the path of the spermatic cord."],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"It is a direct inguinal hernia, usually acquired, and passes through Hesselbach’s triangle.","a":"D","why":"<b>Direct inguinal hernia, usually acquired, through Hesselbach's triangle.</b> Medial to the inferior epigastric vessels, in an older man, and not reaching the scrotum.<br>A. A hernia medial to the vessels is direct, not indirect.<br>B. Indirect hernias use the deep ring, lateral to the vessels.<br>C. Femoral hernias lie below the inguinal ligament; this bulge is above it.<br>E. Direct hernias are acquired, not congenital, and do not follow the cord.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 37 (Direct Vs. Indirect Inguinal Hernias)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":67,"f":"D045"}],"t":1},{"id":"F1228-118-1","s":"A 56 y/o male is moving boxes up and down stairs. As he picks up a heavy box to move, he suddenly feels pain in his lower left region of his abdomen. A small mass is palpable 2 inches laterally to his pubic bone and half an inch superior to his inguinal ligament. What type of hernia is most likely, and where is the herniated sac found?","o":["A. indirect inguinal hernia, tunica vaginalis of the testes (where hydrocele occurs)","B. indirect inguinal hernia, superficial inguinal ring","C. indirect inguinal hernia, lateral inguinal fossa","D. direct inguinal hernia, medial to inferior epigastric arteries and superior to inguinal canal, and lateral to rectus abdominis","E. direct inguinal hernia, tunica vaginalis of the testes"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"direct inguinal hernia, medial to inferior epigastric arteries and superior to inguinal canal, and lateral to rectus abdominis","a":"D","why":"<b>Direct inguinal hernia, in Hesselbach's triangle.</b> A sudden bulge on lifting in an older man, just above the inguinal ligament and close to the pubis, lies medial to the inferior epigastric artery. Option D says 'superior to inguinal canal'; read it as 'superior to the inguinal ligament'.<br>A. and E. The tunica vaginalis surrounds the testis; fluid there is a hydrocele, not a hernia sac.<br>B. and C. An indirect hernia starts at the deep ring, in the lateral inguinal fossa, further from the pubis.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 37 (Direct Vs. Indirect Inguinal Hernias)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 29 (Intro to Inguinal region/recap)"],"src":[{"t":"2019 review","n":"old Exam 2 review: abdomen part only","sl":118,"f":"F1228"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":49,"label":"Older man, medial bulge → direct hernia","slides":[{"src":"sl/G003-37.jpg","cap":"Direct sac pushes medial to epigastric vessels, beside the cord, not into scrotum","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 37"},{"src":"sl/F1228-124.jpg","cap":"Table: indirect versus direct by age, scrotal descent, cause, vessel relation","where":"2019 review, slide 124"}],"q":[{"id":"F3900-64-1","s":"A 55-year-old male presents to his primary care physician with complaints of a bulge in his right groin that has gradually developed over the past several months. He reports discomfort in the area but no severe pain. On examination, there is a palpable, non-reducible mass in the right inguinal region. The mass appears to be more medial and posterior compared to the inguinal ligament. The patient's abdominal examination is unremarkable. What is the most likely diagnosis in this patient?","o":["A. Indirect inguinal hernia","B. Femoral hernia","C. Direct inguinal hernia","D. Testicular torsion","E. Epididymitis"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Direct inguinal hernia","a":"C","why":"<b>Direct inguinal hernia.</b> A bulge that grows slowly, over months, in an older man and sits medially points to a weak back wall of the canal (Hesselbach's triangle). That is a direct, acquired hernia.<br>A. Indirect: usually congenital, in younger people, and often reaches the scrotum.<br>B. Femoral: below the inguinal ligament, more common in women.<br>D. Testicular torsion: sudden, severe testicular pain.<br>E. Epididymitis: a painful, tender swelling behind the testis.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 37 (Direct Vs. Indirect Inguinal Hernias)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 29 (Intro to Inguinal region/recap)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":64,"f":"F3900"}]},{"id":"F3901-13-1","s":"After moving heavy equipment at a construction site, a 42- year-old man goes to his family physician with a bulge in his left groin that does not descend into the scrotum. The patient reports the bulge becomes more prominent when he is standing up, defecating, and coughing. On examination, the doctor places his finger in the location of the bulge and notes that his finger feels like it can pass into the abdominal cavity. Given his presentation and history, what is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["A. Direct inguinal hernia","B. Indirect inguinal hernia","C. Femoral hernia","D. Lymphadenitis of superficial inguinal nodes","E. Hydrocele testis"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Direct inguinal hernia","a":"A","why":"<b>Direct inguinal hernia.</b> Heavy lifting, an adult man, a bulge that does not reach the scrotum, and a finger that seems to go straight back into the abdomen through a weak spot all point to a direct hernia through Hesselbach's triangle.<br>B. Indirect: follows the cord and often goes into the scrotum.<br>C. Femoral: below the inguinal ligament, more often in women.<br>D. Swollen lymph nodes: firm, tender, and do not grow with coughing.<br>E. Hydrocele: fluid around the testis, inside the scrotum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 37 (Direct Vs. Indirect Inguinal Hernias)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 29 (Intro to Inguinal region/recap)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":13,"f":"F3901"},{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":99,"f":"D045"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3903-228-1","s":"In examining a male patient you place your finger in his superficial inguinal ring and ask him to cough. You then feel a sudden impulse medial to your finger. You conclude that your patient most likely has:","o":["A) Femoral hernia.","B) Direct inguinal hernia.","C) Enlarged lymph node.","D) Indirect inguinal hernia.","E) Testicular cancer."],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Direct inguinal hernia","a":"B","why":"<b>Direct inguinal hernia.</b> A direct hernia pushes straight forward through Hesselbach's triangle, which lies medial to the deep ring. So the cough impulse hits the side of your finger, medially, not its tip.<br>A. Femoral hernia: bulges below the inguinal ligament, not at the superficial ring.<br>C. Enlarged lymph node: has no cough impulse.<br>D. Indirect hernia: comes down the canal from the deep ring and hits the fingertip.<br>E. Testicular cancer: a hard scrotal mass with no cough impulse.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 36 (Deep Inguinal ring)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 37 (Direct Vs. Indirect Inguinal Hernias)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":228,"f":"F3903"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":50,"label":"Lateral to inferior epigastric vessels → indirect","slides":[{"src":"sl/G003-37.jpg","cap":"Indirect hernia passes lateral to inferior epigastric vessels into the deep ring","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 37"},{"src":"sl/D045-69.jpg","cap":"Cross-sections: indirect lateral to epigastric vessels, direct medial through the wall","where":"2025 Montana TA review, slide 69"}],"q":[{"id":"D042-26-1","s":"A 54-year-old man presents with a bulge in his right groin that becomes more noticeable when standing and coughing. During surgical evaluation, the hernia sac is found lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels and protrudes through the deep inguinal ring. Which type of inguinal hernia does this patient most likely have?","o":["A. Direct inguinal hernia","B. Indirect inguinal hernia","C. Femoral hernia","D. Ventral hernia"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Indirect inguinal hernia","a":"B","why":"<b>Indirect inguinal hernia.</b> Lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels plus through the deep inguinal ring means indirect.<br>A. Direct: medial to those vessels, through Hesselbach's triangle.<br>C. Femoral: below the inguinal ligament.<br>D. Ventral: through the front abdominal wall away from the groin, often at the midline or a scar.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 37 (Direct Vs. Indirect Inguinal Hernias)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 36 (Deep Inguinal ring)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI module TA review","sl":26,"f":"D042"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3901-11-1","s":"A 45-year-old male was admitted to the hospital with groin pain and a palpable mass just superior to the inguinal ligament. The patient was diagnosed with an inguinal hernia and a surgical repair was performed. During the operation the surgeon found a loop of intestine passing through the deep inguinal ring. Which of the following types of hernia was this?","o":["A. Direct inguinal","B. Umbilical","C. Femoral","D. Lumbar","E. Indirect inguinal"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Indirect inguinal","a":"E","why":"<b>Indirect inguinal hernia.</b> Any hernia that enters the deep inguinal ring is indirect. It then follows the canal, lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels.<br>A. Direct: pushes through Hesselbach's triangle, medial to those vessels, and never uses the deep ring.<br>B. Umbilical: at the navel.<br>C. Femoral: below the inguinal ligament.<br>D. Lumbar: in the back of the abdominal wall.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 36 (Deep Inguinal ring)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 37 (Direct Vs. Indirect Inguinal Hernias)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":11,"f":"F3901"}]},{"id":"F1156-44-1","s":"You palpate your patient’s hernia on the left flank area and realize the hernia is lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels. What type of hernia is it?","o":["A. Indirect hernia with superficial inguinal ring","B. Direct hernia with superficial inguinal ring","C. Indirect hernia with deep inguinal ring","D. Direct hernia with deep inguinal ring"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Indirect hernia with deep inguinal ring","a":"C","why":"<b>Indirect hernia, through the deep inguinal ring.</b> The deep ring is the opening lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels, so a hernia there is indirect.<br>A. It is indirect, but the superficial ring is only its exit. Lateral to the vessels identifies the deep ring, its entrance.<br>B. and D. Direct hernias are medial to the vessels and never use the deep ring.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 37 (Direct Vs. Indirect Inguinal Hernias)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 36 (Deep Inguinal ring)"],"src":[{"t":"? TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":44,"f":"F1156"}]},{"id":"F0008-74-1","s":"A man is moving into a new house and during the process lifts a large chest of drawers. As he lifts he feels a severe pain in the lower right quadrant of his abdomen. He finds that he can no longer lift without pain and the next day goes to see his physician. Surgery is indicated and during the surgery the surgeon opens the inguinal region and finds a hernial sac with a small knuckle of intestine projecting through the abdominal wall just above the inguinal ligament and lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels. The hernia was diagnosed as:","o":["a. An indirect inguinal hernia","b. A direct inguinal hernia","c. A femoral hernia","d. An incisional hernia","e. Umbilical hernia"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"An indirect inguinal hernia","a":"A","why":"<b>Indirect inguinal hernia.</b> The sac is above the inguinal ligament and lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels, which is exactly where the deep inguinal ring is.<br>B. Direct: would be medial to these vessels, in Hesselbach's triangle.<br>C. Femoral: would be below the inguinal ligament.<br>D. Incisional: comes through an old surgical scar.<br>E. Umbilical: at the navel.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 36 (Deep Inguinal ring)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 37 (Direct Vs. Indirect Inguinal Hernias)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":74,"f":"F0008"}]},{"id":"F3902-69-1","s":"A 65 year old male patient presents to the ER with a mass in the groin area. He states that the mass is painful upon palpation. The patient was diagnosed with an inguinal hernia requiring surgical intervention. During the surgery, the surgeon noticed that the loop of intestine lies lateral to the inferior epigastric/lateral umbilical fold. Which of the following type of hernia was this?","o":["A. Umbilical hernia","B. Femoral Hernia","C. Lumbar hernia","D. Direct inguinal hernia","E. Indirect inguinal hernia"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Indirect inguinal hernia","a":"E","why":"<b>Indirect inguinal hernia.</b> The inferior epigastric vessels raise the lateral umbilical fold. A hernia lateral to them enters the deep inguinal ring, which makes it indirect.<br>A. Umbilical hernia: at the navel.<br>B. Femoral hernia: below the inguinal ligament.<br>C. Lumbar hernia: in the back of the abdominal wall.<br>D. Direct hernia: medial to the inferior epigastric vessels.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 36 (Deep Inguinal ring)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 37 (Direct Vs. Indirect Inguinal Hernias)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":69,"f":"F3902"}],"t":1},{"id":"F1252-140-1","s":"A 5 y.o. Boy presents to the emergency room with pain in the lower abdominal region. You suspect his bowels herniated through the deep inguinal ring and traveled through the spermatic cord. Where is the hernia located?","o":["A. Medial to the femoral vein","B. Medial to inferior epigastric vessels","C. Lateral to inferior epigastric vessels","D. Through the umbilicus"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Lateral to inferior epigastric vessels","a":"C","why":"<b>Lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels.</b> The deep inguinal ring sits lateral to these vessels. So a hernia that enters the deep ring and follows the cord (indirect, typical in children) lies lateral to them.<br>A. Medial to the femoral vein: femoral hernia, below the inguinal ligament.<br>B. Medial to the vessels: direct hernia through Hesselbach's triangle.<br>D. Through the umbilicus: umbilical hernia.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 37 (Direct Vs. Indirect Inguinal Hernias)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 36 (Deep Inguinal ring)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":140,"f":"F1252"}]},{"id":"D045-65-1","s":"A 42-year-old man presents with a right-sided groin bulge that enlarges when he coughs. On physical exam, the bulge extends into the scrotum. During surgery, the hernia is found to pass lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels through the deep inguinal ring. Which of the following statements correctly describes the type of hernia and its anatomical relationship?","o":["A. Direct hernia; medial to inferior epigastric vessels; through Hesselbach’s triangle","B. Indirect hernia; lateral to inferior epigastric vessels; through deep inguinal ring","C. Direct hernia; lateral to inferior epigastric vessels; through superficial inguinal ring only","D. Indirect hernia; medial to inferior epigastric vessels; through Hesselbach’s triangle","E. Direct hernia; medial to inferior epigastric vessels; through deep inguinal ring"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Indirect hernia; lateral to inferior epigastric vessels; through deep inguinal ring","a":"B","why":"<b>Indirect; lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels; through the deep ring.</b> It follows the spermatic cord, which is why it can reach the scrotum.<br>A. That is the full description of a direct hernia, which rarely reaches the scrotum.<br>C. A direct hernia is never lateral to the vessels.<br>D. Indirect hernias are lateral, not medial, and do not use Hesselbach's triangle.<br>E. A direct hernia does not use the deep ring.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 37 (Direct Vs. Indirect Inguinal Hernias)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":65,"f":"D045"}],"t":1},{"id":"D045-51-1","s":"A 36-year-old man presents with a right-sided groin bulge that appears when he coughs. On exam, the physician palpates the inguinal ligament using the anterior superior iliac spine (ASIS) and pubic tubercle to determine the hernia type. The bulge is lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels, and the patient reports tenderness along the cremasteric reflex pathway. Which of the following statements best explains the anatomical reason why this hernia follows this pathway and which nerve is at risk during repair?","o":["A. The hernia passes medial to the inferior epigastric vessels because the deep inguinal ring is medial; the iliohypogastric nerve is at risk.","B. The hernia passes lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels because the deep inguinal ring is lateral; the genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve is at risk.","C. The hernia passes below the inguinal ligament into the femoral canal; the lateral femoral cutaneous nerve is at risk.","D. The hernia passes through Hesselbach’s triangle; the obturator nerve is at risk.","E. The hernia passes lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels but above the superficial ring; the femoral nerve is at risk."],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"The hernia passes lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels because the deep inguinal ring is lateral; the genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve is at risk.","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> The bulge is lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels because the <b>deep ring is lateral</b>, so this is an indirect hernia inside the spermatic cord. The cord carries the <b>genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve</b> (it moves the cremaster), so repair can injure it.<br>A. Medial is direct; the iliohypogastric nerve lies above the canal.<br>C. Below the ligament is femoral; that thigh nerve is far lateral.<br>D. Hesselbach's triangle is direct; the obturator nerve is in the pelvis.<br>E. The femoral nerve runs below the inguinal ligament.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 37 (Direct Vs. Indirect Inguinal Hernias)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 45 (Cremaster Muscle)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":51,"f":"D045"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":51,"label":"Child's hernia → indirect, open processus vaginalis","slides":[{"src":"sl/G003-41.jpg","cap":"Processus vaginalis follows the descending testis; if it stays open, congenital hernia","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 41"},{"src":"sl/F1228-124.jpg","cap":"Table: indirect hernias are congenital, tied to processus vaginalis, younger patients","where":"2019 review, slide 124"}],"q":[{"id":"F1180-245-1","s":"A 3-year-old boy is admitted to the pediatric clinic with a palpable mass in the right side of his scrotum, and a preliminary diagnosis is made of a congenital, indirect inguinal hernia. Which of the following is the most likely cause of an indirect inguinal hernia in this patient?","o":["A. The deep inguinal ring opens into an intact processus vaginalis","B. Congenital hydrocele","C. Ectopic testis","D. Epispadias","E. Rupture of the transversalis fascia"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"The deep inguinal ring opens into an intact processus vaginalis","a":"A","why":"<b>The deep ring opens into a processus vaginalis that never closed.</b> This peritoneal pouch follows the testis to the scrotum. It normally seals off. If it stays open, bowel can slide down it: a congenital indirect hernia.<br>B. Congenital hydrocele: fluid in that same open pouch; a sister condition, not the cause.<br>C. Ectopic testis: a testis off the normal path; it does not cause a hernia.<br>D. Epispadias: the urethra opens on the top of the penis.<br>E. A weak transversalis fascia causes direct hernias in adults.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 41 (Recap)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 37 (Direct Vs. Indirect Inguinal Hernias)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":245,"f":"F1180"},{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":189,"f":"F1178"}]},{"id":"F3900-62-1","s":"A 6-month-old male infant is brought to the pediatrician by his parents due to a noticeable swelling in his right groin. The parents report that they have noticed this swelling for a few weeks. On examination, a soft, non-tender mass is palpable in the right inguinal region. The mass is reducible and becomes less prominent when the infant is calm and relaxed. The physical examination is otherwise unremarkable. What is the most likely diagnosis in this infant?","o":["A. Direct inguinal hernia","B. Femoral hernia","C. Indirect inguinal hernia","D. Hydrocele","E. Testicular torsion"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Indirect inguinal hernia","a":"C","why":"<b>Indirect inguinal hernia.</b> Groin hernias in babies are almost always indirect. They are congenital: the processus vaginalis stays open, so bowel slides through the deep ring into the canal.<br>A. Direct: an acquired weakness in older adults.<br>B. Femoral: rare in infants; lies below the inguinal ligament.<br>D. Hydrocele: fluid around the testis or cord that lets light through; it does not push back into the abdomen like this mass.<br>E. Torsion: a sudden, very painful, swollen testis.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 37 (Direct Vs. Indirect Inguinal Hernias)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 33 (Development of inguinal Canal)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":62,"f":"F3900"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":51,"label":"Indirect hernia: wrapped in spermatic cord layers","slides":[{"src":"sl/G003-37.jpg","cap":"Indirect sac runs inside the spermatic cord, under all its coverings","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 37"},{"src":"sl/D042-31.jpg","cap":"Indirect sac wrapped by internal spermatic, cremasteric and external spermatic fasciae","where":"2025 TA review, slide 31"}],"q":[{"id":"F3905-21-1","s":"A 75 yo male presents to clinic with concerns about a mass in his scrotum. He says he noticed it a few months ago, but is now becoming a concern due to an increase in size and some discomfort. You correctly diagnose the patient with an indirect inguinal hernia. Which of the following is correct concerning this diagnosis:","o":["A. The proximal portion of the hernia is located at the deep inguinal ring","B. The proximal portion of the hernia is located at the superficial inguinal ring","C. The hernia is covered by the same layers as the spermatic cord","D. Both A and C","E. Both B and C"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Both A and C","a":"D","why":"<b>Both A and C.</b> An indirect hernia starts (its neck) at the <b>deep inguinal ring</b>. It runs inside the spermatic cord, so it is <b>covered by the same three layers</b> as the cord.<br>A. True, but only half the answer.<br>B. The superficial ring is where an indirect hernia leaves the canal, not where it starts.<br>C. True, but only half the answer.<br>E. Wrong, because B is false.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 37 (Direct Vs. Indirect Inguinal Hernias)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 36 (Deep Inguinal ring)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA weekly","n":"09-22-2022 TA session (abdominal wall, inguinal)","sl":21,"f":"F3905"}]},{"id":"D041-39-1","s":"Another patient presents with the same issues and you are able to identify the hernia as indirect. Which of the follow is not true regarding these hernias?","o":["A. Found lateral to inferior epigastric vessels","B. Covered by layers of spermatic cord","C. Occurs through Deep Inguinal Ring","D. Bowel pushes through peritoneum and transversalis fascia"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Bowel pushes through peritoneum and transversalis fascia","a":"D","why":"<b>D is not true of indirect hernias.</b> Pushing peritoneum and transversalis fascia out ahead of the bowel describes a <b>direct</b> hernia through Hesselbach's triangle. An indirect hernia slips through the hole already in the transversalis fascia, the deep ring.<br>A. True: indirect hernias are lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels.<br>B. True: they lie inside the cord, so they get its three coverings.<br>C. True: they enter the deep inguinal ring.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 37 (Direct Vs. Indirect Inguinal Hernias)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":39,"f":"D041"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":52,"label":"Below inguinal ligament in woman → femoral hernia","slides":[{"src":"sl/G003-30.jpg","cap":"Femoral artery and vein pass under inguinal ligament; lacunar ligament, pubic tubercle","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 30"},{"src":"sl/G003-37.jpg","cap":"Females are more prone to femoral hernias, below the inguinal ligament","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 37"}],"q":[{"id":"D045-101-1","s":"After spending 2 days moving heavy furniture out of her house, a 56-year-old woman goes to an emergency room in acute pain. The patient reports nausea, vomiting, and severe abdominal pain. On examination, the doctor discovers a painful, globular mass located inferior and lateral to the pubic tubercle. Given her presentation and history, what is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["A. Indirect inguinal hernia","B. Direct inguinal hernia","C. Femoral hernia","D. Lymphadenitis of superficial inguinal nodes","E. Fractured hip"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Femoral hernia","a":"C","why":"<b>Femoral hernia.</b> The lump is <b>below the inguinal ligament</b>, inferior and lateral to the pubic tubercle, in a woman. The femoral ring is narrow, with the sharp lacunar ligament at its edge, so the bowel often gets trapped. That causes pain and vomiting.<br>A. and B. Inguinal hernias sit above the inguinal ligament, superior and medial to the pubic tubercle.<br>D. Swollen lymph nodes do not cause bowel blockage and vomiting.<br>E. A hip fracture gives hip pain and a shortened leg, not a groin lump.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 37 (Direct Vs. Indirect Inguinal Hernias)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":101,"f":"D045"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":52,"label":"Most common hernia in women: indirect inguinal","slides":[{"src":"sl/G003-37.jpg","cap":"Most inguinal hernias occur in men; women are more prone to femoral hernias","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 37"}],"q":[{"id":"F1243-115-1","s":"A 39-year-old woman is admitted to the hospital with pain radiating to her inguinal region. Radiologic and physical examination reveal a herniation. Which of the following is the most common type of hernia in a female patient?","o":["A. Femoral hernia","B. Umbilical hernia","C. Direct inguinal hernia","D. Indirect inguinal hernia","E. Epigastric hernia"],"g":["D"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Femoral hernia","a":"A","why":"<b>Indirect inguinal hernia.</b> It is the most common hernia in both sexes, women included. Femoral hernias are more common in women than in men (wider pelvis), but even in women they are less common than inguinal hernias.<br>A. Femoral: the classic trap. Women get it more often than men do, but it is not their most common hernia.<br>B. Umbilical: less common in adults.<br>C. Direct: mostly in older men.<br>E. Epigastric: small midline hernias, uncommon.","ex":"","fl":["Key A (femoral) is wrong; correct D (indirect inguinal hernia). Femoral hernias are MORE common in women than in men, but the indirect inguinal hernia is still the most common hernia in women (and men). G003:37 says only that females are 'more prone' to femoral hernias than males."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 37 (Direct Vs. Indirect Inguinal Hernias)"],"src":[{"t":"? tutor review","n":"old Exam 2: abdomen part only","sl":115,"f":"F1243"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":52,"label":"Thin elderly woman, inner-thigh pain → obturator hernia","slides":[],"q":[{"id":"D045-103-1","s":"A 72-year-old woman, who lost 50 lb (22.7 kg) of weight in the past year, visited her physician complaining of abdominal cramps, pain, and vomiting. Physical examination detected a distended abdomen with no visible or palpable masses in the groin and upper thigh area. She also described a sharp cutting pain in the medial aspect of her left thigh, which was exacerbated by the physician extending and medially rotating her left thigh at the hip joint. An abdominal CT revealed small bowel obstruction. What is the most likely cause of the obstruction?","o":["A. Direct inguinal hernia","B. Indirect inguinal hernia","C. Umbilical hernia","D. Femoral hernia","E. Obturator hernia"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Obturator hernia","a":"E","why":"<b>Obturator hernia.</b> Bowel gets trapped in the obturator canal and presses on the obturator nerve. That gives sharp pain down the inner thigh, worse when the hip is extended and turned in. It is typical in thin, elderly women after weight loss, with no groin lump.<br>A. and B. Inguinal hernias bulge in the groin above the inguinal ligament.<br>C. Umbilical: a bulge at the navel.<br>D. Femoral: a lump below the inguinal ligament, without obturator nerve pain.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":103,"f":"D045"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":53,"label":"Spermatic cord contents","slides":[{"src":"sl/G003-42.jpg","cap":"Cord contents: ductus deferens, testicular artery, pampiniform plexus, genital nerve branch","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 42"},{"src":"sl/G003-43.jpg","cap":"Cut cord: artery, pampiniform plexus, ductus deferens inside three coverings","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 43"},{"src":"sl/G003-45.jpg","cap":"Labels both genitofemoral branches; only the genital branch runs with the cord","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 45"}],"q":[{"id":"D045-77-1","s":"During inguinal hernia surgery, a surgeon identifies the spermatic cord. Which of the following lists its main contents and fascial layers?","o":["A. Testicular artery, vas deferens, pampiniform plexus; dartos, tunica vaginalis, external oblique","B. Testicular artery, vas deferens, pampiniform plexus, lymphatics, genital nerve; internal spermatic, cremasteric, external spermatic","C. Testicular artery, epididymis, vas deferens; tunica albuginea, tunica vaginalis, dartos","D. Testicular artery, pampiniform plexus, ilioinguinal nerve; internal spermatic, external spermatic, tunica albuginea","E. Testicular artery, vas deferens, pampiniform plexus; internal oblique, transversalis, external iliac"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Testicular artery, vas deferens, pampiniform plexus, lymphatics, genital nerve; internal spermatic, cremasteric, external spermatic","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> The spermatic cord holds the <b>testicular artery, ductus (vas) deferens, pampiniform venous plexus, lymph vessels and the genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve</b>. Its three coats are the <b>internal spermatic, cremasteric and external spermatic fasciae</b>.<br>A. Dartos and tunica vaginalis are scrotal layers; external oblique is a muscle.<br>C. Epididymis sits on the testis; tunica albuginea is the testis capsule.<br>D. Ilioinguinal nerve runs outside the cord; tunica albuginea is not a cord coat.<br>E. These are abdominal wall layers and a vessel.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 42 (Spermatic Cord Contents)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 43 (Coverings of the Spermatic Cord)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 44 (Layers of the Cord and Layers of the abdominal  Wall)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":77,"f":"D045"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3905-26-1","s":"Which of the following is NOT a contained in the spermatic cord?","o":["A. Ductus deferens","B. Genitofemoral nerve, femoral branch","C. Cremasteric artery","D. Pampiniform plexus of veins"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Genitofemoral nerve, femoral branch","a":"B","why":"<b>Femoral branch of the genitofemoral nerve.</b> It passes under the inguinal ligament to the skin of the upper front thigh. Only the <b>genital branch</b> enters the cord.<br>A. Ductus deferens: a main cord content.<br>C. Cremasteric artery: in the cord (it comes from the inferior epigastric artery).<br>D. Pampiniform plexus: the vein network around the testicular artery in the cord.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 42 (Spermatic Cord Contents)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA weekly","n":"09-22-2022 TA session (abdominal wall, inguinal)","sl":26,"f":"F3905"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":54,"label":"Cord coverings ← abdominal wall layers","slides":[{"src":"sl/G003-44.jpg","cap":"Color-matched: each cord covering continues one abdominal wall layer","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 44"},{"src":"sl/D041-49.jpg","cap":"Table: each scrotal and cord layer with its abdominal wall origin","where":"2025 TA review, slide 49"}],"q":[{"id":"F1156-60-1","s":"Cremasteric muscle of spermatic cord comes from which layer of the abdomen?","o":["A. Internal oblique","B. External oblique","C. Scarpa's fascia","D. Transversalis fascia"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Internal oblique","a":"A","why":"<b>Internal oblique.</b> The cremaster muscle and its fascia come from the internal oblique, pulled down around the cord.<br>B. External oblique: gives the external spermatic fascia.<br>C. Scarpa's fascia: gives the dartos layer of the scrotum.<br>D. Transversalis fascia: gives the internal spermatic fascia.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 45 (Cremaster Muscle)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 44 (Layers of the Cord and Layers of the abdominal  Wall)"],"src":[{"t":"? TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":60,"f":"F1156"}]},{"id":"F3902-75-1","s":"A 40 year old man is scheduled to undergo vasectomy. During the procedure, the urologist begins to separate the different layers of the spermatic cord and asks you which structure of the spermatic cord is derived from the internal oblique muscle? Since you already reviewed the content, with confidence you answer","o":["A. External oblique","B. Cremaster muscle","C. Internal Spermatic Fascia","D. External Spermatic Fascia","E. Pampiniform Plexus"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Cremaster muscle","a":"B","why":"<b>Cremaster muscle.</b> It forms from the lowest fibers of the internal oblique, carried down around the cord.<br>A. External oblique: an abdominal muscle, not a cord layer; it gives the external spermatic fascia.<br>C. Internal spermatic fascia: from the transversalis fascia.<br>D. External spermatic fascia: from the external oblique aponeurosis.<br>E. Pampiniform plexus: veins inside the cord, not a coat.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 44 (Layers of the Cord and Layers of the abdominal  Wall)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 45 (Cremaster Muscle)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":75,"f":"F3902"}]},{"id":"F3902-73-1","s":"You are scheduled to assist your surgical attending during a hernia repair and you are trying to recall that the transversalis fascia contributes to the formation of which of the following structures?","o":["A. Inguinal ligament","B. Linea alba","C. Internal spermatic fascia","D. Cremaster muscle","E. Ductus deferens"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Internal spermatic fascia","a":"C","why":"<b>Internal spermatic fascia.</b> The deep ring is a hole in the transversalis fascia. As the cord passes through, it takes a sleeve of that fascia: the internal spermatic fascia.<br>A. Inguinal ligament: the rolled lower edge of the external oblique aponeurosis.<br>B. Linea alba: the midline seam of the flat muscles' aponeuroses.<br>D. Cremaster muscle: from the internal oblique.<br>E. Ductus deferens: a duct from the epididymis, not a fascial layer.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 44 (Layers of the Cord and Layers of the abdominal  Wall)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 43 (Coverings of the Spermatic Cord)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":73,"f":"F3902"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3900-104-1","s":"Match the spermatic cord layer to the layer of the abdominal wall: External Spermatic fascia / Cremasteric muscle / Internal spermatic fascia","o":["A. Muscular fibers of internal oblique muscle","B. Transversalis fascia","C. Continuation of external oblique aponeurosis"],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"External spermatic fascia -> C (Continuation of external oblique aponeurosis); Cremasteric muscle -> A (Muscular fibers of internal oblique muscle); Internal spermatic fascia -> B (Transversalis fascia)","a":null,"why":"<b>External spermatic fascia comes from the external oblique aponeurosis; cremaster from the internal oblique; internal spermatic fascia from the transversalis fascia.</b> Each cord coat comes from the wall layer at the same depth.<br>Common mix-up: expecting a coat from the transversus abdominis. Its lower fibers arch above the deep ring, so it gives none. The third coat comes from the transversalis fascia.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 44 (Layers of the Cord and Layers of the abdominal  Wall)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 45 (Cremaster Muscle)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":104,"f":"F3900"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":187,"f":"F3901"}]},{"id":"D041-47-1","s":"A 30 year old patient presents to the clinic with pain in his right testicle. You suspect he has necrosis of the cremaster muscle. What is the origin of this muscle?","o":["A. Scarpa’s fascia","B. External oblique muscle","C. Internal oblique muscle","D. Transversalis fascia","E. Dartos muscle"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Internal oblique muscle","a":"C","why":"<b>Internal oblique muscle.</b> The cremaster is made of internal oblique fibers pulled down around the cord as the testis descended.<br>A. Scarpa's fascia: gives the dartos muscle and fascia of the scrotum.<br>B. External oblique: gives the external spermatic fascia.<br>D. Transversalis fascia: gives the internal spermatic fascia.<br>E. Dartos muscle: smooth muscle in the scrotal wall, not the cremaster.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 44 (Layers of the Cord and Layers of the abdominal  Wall)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 45 (Cremaster Muscle)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":47,"f":"D041"}],"t":1},{"id":"F1178-179-1","s":"A newborn presents with a large left bulge in the inguinal canal and a swelling of the left scrotum. Ultrasound reveals a portion of small intestine within the scrotal swelling. What is the deepest layer of the abominable wall involved with this herniation?","o":["A. Transverse abdominal muscle","B. External oblique muscle","C. Internal oblique muscle","D. Rectus abdominis muscle","E. Transversalis fascia"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Transversalis fascia","a":"E","why":"<b>Transversalis fascia.</b> A newborn's scrotal hernia is indirect. It enters the deep ring, a hole in the transversalis fascia, which wraps the cord as the internal spermatic fascia. Only peritoneum lies deeper.<br>A. Transversus abdominis: arches above the deep ring and gives no cord coat.<br>B. External oblique: the outermost muscle; gives the external spermatic fascia.<br>C. Internal oblique: the middle layer; gives the cremaster.<br>D. Rectus abdominis: medial to the canal, not involved.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 43 (Coverings of the Spermatic Cord)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 44 (Layers of the Cord and Layers of the abdominal  Wall)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 36 (Deep Inguinal ring)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":179,"f":"F1178"},{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":235,"f":"F1180"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":55,"label":"Cord and scrotal layers, superficial to deep","slides":[{"src":"sl/G003-43.jpg","cap":"Coverings superficial to deep: external spermatic, cremasteric, internal spermatic fascia","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 43"},{"src":"sl/G003-46.jpg","cap":"Scrotum diagram: skin, dartos, external spermatic, cremaster, internal spermatic, tunica vaginalis","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 46"},{"src":"sl/F3902-72.jpg","cap":"Color-coded scrotal and cord layers matched to the wall layer each comes from","where":"2022 Middletown TA review, slide 72"}],"q":[{"id":"F3905-30-1","s":"Which of the following is the correct order of the layers of the spermatic cord from superficial to deep?","o":["A. External spermatic fascia → internal spermatic fascia → cremasteric muscle","B. Internal oblique → transversalis fascia → cremasteric muscle","C. External oblique → internal oblique → transversalis fascia","D. External spermatic fascia → cremasteric muscle → Internal spermatic fascia"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"External spermatic fascia → cremasteric muscle → Internal spermatic fascia","a":"D","why":"<b>External spermatic fascia, then cremasteric muscle, then internal spermatic fascia.</b> This copies the abdominal wall from outside in: external oblique, internal oblique, transversalis fascia.<br>A. Puts the internal spermatic fascia outside the cremaster; swapped.<br>B. Lists abdominal wall layers in the wrong order, not cord coats.<br>C. These are the wall layers the coats come from, not the coats themselves.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 43 (Coverings of the Spermatic Cord)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 44 (Layers of the Cord and Layers of the abdominal  Wall)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA weekly","n":"09-22-2022 TA session (abdominal wall, inguinal)","sl":30,"f":"F3905"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3900-102-1","s":"Number the spermatic cord coverings from superficial to deep:","o":["A. Internal spermatic fascia","B. External spermatic fascia","C. Vasculature, ducts, and nerves","D. Cremasteric muscle and fascia with genital branch of genitofemoral nerve"],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"1) External spermatic fascia; 2) Cremasteric muscle and fascia with genital branch of genitofemoral nerve; 3) Internal spermatic fascia; 4) Vasculature, ducts, and nerves","a":null,"why":"<b>External spermatic fascia, then cremasteric muscle and fascia, then internal spermatic fascia, then the contents.</b> The coats keep the abdominal wall's outside-to-inside order: external oblique, internal oblique, transversalis fascia.<br>Common mix-up: putting the cremaster outside the external spermatic fascia. The cremaster comes from the middle muscle (internal oblique), so it sits in the middle.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 43 (Coverings of the Spermatic Cord)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":102,"f":"F3900"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":185,"f":"F3901"}]},{"id":"F1228-114-1","s":"What are the layers of the scrotum in order from most superficial to deep?","o":["A. Skin, Colles fascia, dartos muscle, cremasteric fascia, external spermatic fascia, and internal spermatic fascia","B. Skin, dartos muscle, colles fascia, external spermatic fascia, cremasteric fascia, and internal spermatic fascia","C. Skin, dartos muscle, colles fascia, cremasteric fascia, external spermatic fascia, and internal spermatic fascia","D. Skin, colles fascia, dartos muscle, external spermatic fascia, cremasteric fascia, and internal spermatic fascia"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Skin, dartos muscle, colles fascia, external spermatic fascia, cremasteric fascia, and internal spermatic fascia","a":"B","why":"<b>Skin, dartos muscle, Colles fascia, external spermatic fascia, cremasteric fascia, internal spermatic fascia.</b> The dartos smooth muscle lies right under the skin. The three spermatic layers keep their outside-to-inside order: external, cremasteric, internal.<br>A. Puts Colles fascia above the dartos and the cremasteric above the external spermatic.<br>C. Swaps cremasteric and external spermatic fascia.<br>D. Puts Colles fascia above the dartos muscle.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 46 (Scrotum)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 43 (Coverings of the Spermatic Cord)"],"src":[{"t":"2019 review","n":"old Exam 2 review: abdomen part only","sl":114,"f":"F1228"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":55,"label":"Dartos muscle → sympathetic fibers","slides":[{"src":"sl/G003-46.jpg","cap":"Dartos muscle from Scarpa's fascia; sympathetic supply; wrinkles scrotal skin","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 46"}],"q":[{"id":"F1228-117-1","s":"Dartos muscle is supplied by?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Sympathetic fibers passing through the genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve","a":null,"why":"<b>Sympathetic fibers.</b> The dartos is smooth muscle in the scrotal fascia, which comes from Scarpa's fascia. Sympathetic fibers reach it inside the scrotal skin nerves, such as the genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve. Cold makes it contract and wrinkle the skin.<br>Common mix-up: the cremaster is skeletal muscle with a somatic motor nerve. The dartos is smooth muscle under sympathetic control.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 46 (Scrotum)"],"src":[{"t":"2019 review","n":"old Exam 2 review: abdomen part only","sl":117,"f":"F1228"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":56,"label":"Cremaster nerve: genital branch of genitofemoral","slides":[{"src":"sl/G003-45.jpg","cap":"Cremaster: from internal oblique, supplied by genital branch of genitofemoral nerve","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 45"},{"src":"sl/F1252-208.jpg","cap":"Cremasteric muscle is striated, supplied by genital branch of genitofemoral nerve","where":"2020 TA review, slide 208"}],"q":[{"id":"D041-52-1","s":"Your friend comes to you complaining about pain in his tesitcles. He says he doesn't want to go to the ED because you’re basically a doctor. You find he has no cremasteric reflex. You’re concerned about the innervation to the cremasteric muscle which is?","o":["A. Ilioinguinal nerve","B. Femoral branch of the genitofemoral nerve","C. Deep perineal nerve","D. Genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve","E. Perineal nerve"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve","a":"D","why":"<b>Genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve.</b> This nerve comes from the first and second lumbar spinal nerves (L1–L2), runs in the cord, and is the motor nerve of the cremaster. Losing it removes the reflex.<br>A. Ilioinguinal nerve: senses the thigh stroke (the input side) but does not move the muscle.<br>B. Femoral branch: skin of the upper thigh only.<br>C. Deep perineal nerve: a pudendal branch to perineal muscles.<br>E. Perineal nerve: a pudendal branch to the perineum and back of the scrotum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 45 (Cremaster Muscle)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":52,"f":"D041"}],"t":1},{"id":"D045-82-1","s":"A 20-year-old male presents for evaluation after noticing that one of his testes retracts toward the inguinal canal when exposed to cold. Which of the following correctly describes the function and innervation of the cremaster muscle?","o":["A. Elevates the testis; innervated by the genitofemoral nerve (L1–L2)","B. Lowers the testis; innervated by the ilioinguinal nerve (L1)","C. Elevates the testis; innervated by the pudendal nerve (S2–S4)","D. Contracts the scrotal skin; innervated by the posterior femoral cutaneous nerve","E. Relaxes the testis; innervated by the sympathetic fibers from T12"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Elevates the testis; innervated by the genitofemoral nerve (L1–L2)","a":"A","why":"<b>Elevates the testis; genitofemoral nerve (L1–L2, the first and second lumbar spinal nerves).</b> Its genital branch makes the cremaster pull the testis up in the cold, to keep it warm.<br>B. The cremaster raises, not lowers, the testis; the ilioinguinal nerve only senses the stroke.<br>C. Pudendal nerve (second to fourth sacral, S2–S4): perineum, not the cremaster.<br>D. Wrinkling scrotal skin is the dartos muscle's job.<br>E. The cremaster is skeletal muscle with a somatic nerve, not sympathetic fibers from the twelfth thoracic level.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 45 (Cremaster Muscle)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":82,"f":"D045"}],"t":1},{"id":"D045-105-1","s":"During a physical examination of a 35-year-old man with a spinal cord injury, the neurologist lightly strokes the right upper medial side of the thigh to test the integrity of the L1-2 spinal cord segments. He notes the cremasteric reflex is absent. Which of the following nerves carries the efferent limb of this reflex arc?","o":["A. Iliohypogastric","B. Genital branch of the genitofemoral","C. Femoral branch of the genitofemoral","D. Femoral","E. Ilioinguinal"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Genital branch of the genitofemoral","a":"B","why":"<b>Genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve.</b> It runs in the spermatic cord and supplies the cremaster, so it is the motor (efferent) limb.<br>A. Iliohypogastric: skin above the pubis and abdominal muscles.<br>C. Femoral branch of the genitofemoral: skin of the upper thigh only.<br>D. Femoral nerve: front thigh muscles; it carries the knee jerk.<br>E. Ilioinguinal: senses the thigh stroke, so it is the sensory (afferent) limb, not the motor one.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 45 (Cremaster Muscle)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":105,"f":"D045"}]},{"id":"D042-32-1","s":"A 22-year-old male presents after striking the inner part of his thigh during soccer practice. On examination, the clinician lightly strokes the patient’s superomedial thigh, causing the ipsilateral testis to elevate. Which of the following best describes the muscle responsible for this response and the innervation involved?","o":["A. Dartos muscle; afferent and efferent limbs via the pudendal nerve","B. Cremaster muscle; afferent limb via the ilioinguinal nerve and efferent limb via the genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve","C. Cremaster muscle; both afferent and efferent limbs via the iliohypogastric nerve","D. Dartos muscle; afferent limb via the femoral nerve and efferent limb via the obturator nerve","E. Internal oblique muscle; efferent limb via the ilioinguinal nerve only"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Cremaster muscle; afferent limb via the ilioinguinal nerve and efferent limb via the genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve","a":"B","why":"<b>Cremaster; sensory (afferent) limb ilioinguinal nerve, motor (efferent) limb genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve.</b> The ilioinguinal nerve (first lumbar spinal nerve, L1) feels the inner-thigh stroke. The genital branch (L1–L2) contracts the cremaster and lifts the testis.<br>A. Dartos only wrinkles skin, under sympathetic control; the pudendal nerve is not the path.<br>C. Iliohypogastric nerve: skin above the pubis.<br>D. Dartos, femoral and obturator nerves are not this reflex.<br>E. The internal oblique does not lift the testis.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 45 (Cremaster Muscle)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI module TA review","sl":32,"f":"D042"}]},{"id":"F1252-206-1","s":"18 y/o male presents to the clinic with numbness over the anterior scrotum and diminished cremasteric reflex on the right side. What nerve is responsible for these symptoms?","o":["A. Dorsal nerve of the penis","B. Peri prostatic nerve plexus","C. Genital branch of genitofemoral","D. Posterior scrotal nerves"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Genital branch of genitofemoral","a":"C","why":"<b>Genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve.</b> It moves the cremaster and senses the skin of the front of the scrotum, so one lesion explains both signs.<br>A. Dorsal nerve of the penis: a pudendal branch to the skin of the penis.<br>B. Periprostatic plexus: autonomic nerves to the prostate and erectile tissue.<br>D. Posterior scrotal nerves: pudendal branches to the back of the scrotum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 45 (Cremaster Muscle)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 43 (Coverings of the Spermatic Cord)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":206,"f":"F1252"}]},{"id":"D045-84-1","s":"During a sports physical exam, a physician observes that one testis does not elevate when the inner thigh is stroked, although scrotal skin wrinkles normally. Which of the following best explains the underlying anatomy and physiology?","o":["A. Dartos muscle dysfunction; autonomic fibers; responsible for wrinkling scrotal skin and elevating the testis","B. Cremaster muscle intact; somatic fibers via ilioinguinal nerve; elevates testis and contributes to temperature regulation","C. Cremaster muscle injury or denervation; genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve (L1–L2); elevates testis as part of protective and thermoregulatory reflex","D. Bulbospongiosus atrophy; pudendal nerve (S2–S4); elevates testis and aids ejaculation","E. Internal oblique disruption; iliohypogastric nerve; contracts scrotal wall to regulate testicular temperature"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Cremaster muscle injury or denervation; genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve (L1–L2); elevates testis as part of protective and thermoregulatory reflex","a":"C","why":"<b>Cremaster injury or denervation; genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve (L1–L2, the first and second lumbar spinal nerves).</b> The testis does not rise, so the cremaster or its nerve has failed. The skin still wrinkles, so the dartos (smooth muscle, sympathetic fibers) works.<br>A. The dartos works here, and it does not lift the testis.<br>B. 'Intact' cannot explain a missing reflex.<br>D. Bulbospongiosus is a perineal muscle for ejaculation.<br>E. The iliohypogastric nerve is not part of this reflex.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 45 (Cremaster Muscle)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 46 (Scrotum)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":84,"f":"D045"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":57,"label":"Cremasteric reflex: testis rises; tests L1–L2","slides":[{"src":"sl/G003-45.jpg","cap":"Cremaster tested by stroking the upper inner thigh; it lifts the testis","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 45"}],"q":[{"id":"F3903-234-1","s":"One of the standard tests of a newborn infant is to stroke the inside of its thigh to elicit the cremaster reflex. What would be the response you would expect to see?","o":["A) twitching of the skin in the hypogastric region","B) closing of the labia","C) contraction of the dartos muscle","D) penile rigidity","E) testicular retraction"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"testicular retraction","a":"E","why":"<b>Testicular retraction.</b> Stroking the inner thigh makes the cremaster contract and pull the testis on that side upward.<br>A. Skin twitch over the lower abdomen: that is the abdominal reflex.<br>B. Closing of the labia: no such reflex.<br>C. Dartos contraction: a slow smooth-muscle response to cold, run by sympathetic fibers, not this reflex.<br>D. Penile rigidity: erection, a parasympathetic response.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 45 (Cremaster Muscle)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":234,"f":"F3903"}],"t":1},{"id":"D045-109-1","s":"A 36-year-old man arrives at the ER unconscious and with a suspected spinal cord injury after being involved in a motor vehicle accident. If the physician wants to localize the spinal cord lesion, what reflex would test the integrity of the L1-2 spinal cord segment?","o":["A. Anocutaneous reflex","B. Patellar reflex","C. Plantar reflex","D. Calcaneal reflex","E. Cremasteric reflex"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Cremasteric reflex","a":"E","why":"<b>Cremasteric reflex.</b> It tests cord segments L1–L2 (first and second lumbar): ilioinguinal nerve in, genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve out.<br>A. Anocutaneous reflex ('anal wink'): S2–S4, the second to fourth sacral segments.<br>B. Patellar reflex (knee jerk): second to fourth lumbar segments (L2–L4).<br>C. Plantar reflex: fifth lumbar and first sacral segments (L5–S1).<br>D. Calcaneal reflex (ankle jerk): first and second sacral segments (S1–S2).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 45 (Cremaster Muscle)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":109,"f":"D045"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":58,"label":"Tunica vaginalis over tunica albuginea","slides":[{"src":"sl/G003-48.jpg","cap":"Parietal and visceral layers of tunica vaginalis over the tunica albuginea","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 48"}],"q":[{"id":"F3905-33-1","s":"The __________________ is a double lined serous sac that surrounds the testes. The visceral aspect of this sac is in direct contact with the __________________ .","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"tunica vaginalis; tunica albuginea","a":null,"why":"<b>Tunica vaginalis; tunica albuginea.</b> The tunica vaginalis is a two-layer sac of peritoneum left over from the processus vaginalis. Its visceral layer sticks to the tunica albuginea, the tough white capsule of the testis.<br>Common mix-up: fluid in the tunica vaginalis is a hydrocele (light shines through). Blood there is a hematocele (light does not shine through).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 48 (Testis)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA weekly","n":"09-22-2022 TA session (abdominal wall, inguinal)","sl":33,"f":"F3905"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":58,"label":"Ductus deferens starts at epididymis tail","slides":[{"src":"sl/G003-49.jpg","cap":"Epididymis head, body and tail; ductus deferens leaves the tail","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 49"},{"src":"sl/G003-48.jpg","cap":"Testis: tunica albuginea, rete testis, efferent ductules to epididymis head","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 48"}],"q":[{"id":"D041-55-1","s":"Which of the following relationships is correct?","o":["A. The epididymis is outside of the testes","B. The ductus deferens begins in the tail of the epididymis","C. The epididymis is where seminal fluid is produced","D. The ductus deferens descends from the prostate to join the urethra"],"g":["A","B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"The ductus deferens begins in the tail of the epididymis","a":"B","why":"<b>A and B are both correct.</b> The ductus deferens <b>begins at the tail of the epididymis</b>, the intended answer. The epididymis also lies <b>outside</b> the testis, on its back and outside the tunica albuginea, so A is true too.<br>C. Seminal fluid comes mostly from the seminal glands and prostate; the epididymis stores and matures sperm.<br>D. The ductus runs the other way: up the cord into the pelvis, then joins the seminal gland duct to form the ejaculatory duct inside the prostate.","ex":"","fl":["Key B is correct, but option A is also literally true: the epididymis lies on the posterior surface of the testis, outside the tunica albuginea (G003:49 'found on the posterior aspect of the testis'). Pick B as 'best' (it is the relationship the TA intended)."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 49 (Epididymis)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":55,"f":"D041"}],"t":1},{"id":"D045-87-1","s":"A 27-year-old man presents for evaluation of infertility. During surgical exploration, the surgeon traces the pathway of sperm from the testis to the ejaculatory ducts. Which of the following correctly describes the gross structure of the testis, epididymis, and vas (ductus) deferens?","o":["A. Testis covered by tunica vaginalis; epididymis lies along the anterior border; vas deferens arises from the head of the epididymis and enters the inguinal canal","B. Testis covered by tunica albuginea; epididymis lies along the posterior border; vas deferens arises from the tail of the epididymis and travels through the spermatic cord","C. Testis covered by dartos fascia; epididymis surrounds the medial aspect of the testis; vas deferens arises directly from the testicular artery","D. Testis covered by tunica albuginea; epididymis lies along the lateral border; vas deferens arises from the body of the epididymis","E. Testis covered by tunica vaginalis; epididymis lies along the posterior border; vas deferens arises from the head of the epididymis and enters the perineum"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Testis covered by tunica albuginea; epididymis lies along the posterior border; vas deferens arises from the tail of the epididymis and travels through the spermatic cord","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> The testis has a tough capsule, the <b>tunica albuginea</b>. The <b>epididymis lies along its back</b>, and the ductus deferens starts at the <b>tail</b> and runs up in the spermatic cord.<br>A. The epididymis is posterior; the ductus starts at the tail, not the head.<br>C. Dartos is a scrotal layer; the ductus is a duct, not an artery branch.<br>D. The ductus starts at the tail, not the body.<br>E. The ductus starts at the tail and goes up the cord, not to the perineum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 48 (Testis)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 49 (Epididymis)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":87,"f":"D045"}]},{"id":"D045-89-1","s":"A 32-year-old man presents with painful swelling of the posterior scrotum after a urinary tract infection. Ultrasound shows an elongated, inflamed structure along the posterior border of the testis. Which of the following structures is most likely affected, and what is its anatomical relationship?","o":["A. Head of epididymis; superior and lateral to the testis, receiving sperm from the rete testis","B. Body of epididymis; lateral surface of testis, connecting head and tail","C. Tail of epididymis; posteroinferior aspect of testis, continuous with the vas deferens","D. Vas deferens; medial and anterior to the testis, running with the testicular artery","E. Rete testis; within the mediastinum testis, distributing sperm to the epididymis"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Tail of epididymis; posteroinferior aspect of testis, continuous with the vas deferens","a":"C","why":"<b>Tail of the epididymis: at the back and bottom of the testis, continuous with the vas deferens.</b> Infection from the urinary tract climbs up the vas and reaches the tail first.<br>A. Head: sits on top of the testis.<br>B. Body: runs down the back of the testis, between head and tail.<br>D. Vas deferens: rises from the tail into the cord; it does not lie in front of the testis.<br>E. Rete testis: inside the testis, not along its back border.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 49 (Epididymis)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":89,"f":"D045"}]},{"id":"F3903-230-1","s":"Which of the following associations is incorrect?","o":["A) rete testis—opening of the ductus deferens","B) testicular arteries—arise from abdominal aorta","C) varicocele—dilation of the pampiniform plexus","D) epididymis—sperm maturation","E) anterior scrotum—innervated by branches from the ilioinguinal and genitofemoral nerves"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"rete testis—opening of the ductus deferens","a":"A","why":"<b>A is wrong.</b> The <b>rete testis</b> is a network of channels inside the testis. It sends sperm through efferent ductules to the <b>head of the epididymis</b>. The ductus deferens starts at the <b>tail</b> of the epididymis.<br>B. True: the testicular arteries come off the abdominal aorta.<br>C. True: a varicocele is a widened pampiniform venous plexus.<br>D. True: sperm mature in the epididymis.<br>E. True: the ilioinguinal nerve and genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve supply the front of the scrotum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 48 (Testis)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 50 (Blood Supply)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 53 (Clinical Correlates)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":230,"f":"F3903"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":58,"label":"Fluid in tunica vaginalis → hydrocele","slides":[{"src":"sl/G003-53.jpg","cap":"Hydrocele: fluid in tunica vaginalis, lights up on transillumination; hematocele, torsion","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 53"},{"src":"sl/G003-48.jpg","cap":"Cavity of tunica vaginalis between its parietal and visceral layers","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 48"}],"q":[{"id":"D041-64-1","s":"A 25-year-old man is admitted to the hospital with testicular pain. Physical examination reveals a swollen and inflamed right testis. A computed tomography (CT) scan examination reveals abnormal accumulation of fluid in the cavity of the tunica vaginalis (Fig. 4-2). Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis for this patient?","o":["A. Varicocele","B. Rectocele","C. Cystocele","D. Hydrocele","E. Hypospadias"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Hydrocele","a":"D","why":"<b>Hydrocele.</b> Fluid collects in the cavity of the tunica vaginalis, between its two layers. A light shone through the scrotum passes through it.<br>A. Varicocele: widened pampiniform veins ('bag of worms').<br>B. Rectocele: rectum bulging into the vagina.<br>C. Cystocele: bladder bulging into the vagina.<br>E. Hypospadias: urethra opens on the underside of the penis.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"slide 64 shows a CT (Fig. 4-2) of the scrotal fluid collection; not needed to answer","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 53 (Clinical Correlates)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 48 (Testis)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":64,"f":"D041"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":59,"label":"Testicular artery from abdominal aorta","slides":[{"src":"sl/G003-52.jpg","cap":"Right and left testicular arteries arising from the abdominal aorta","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 52"},{"src":"sl/G003-50.jpg","cap":"Testis blood supply comes from the abdominal aorta via testicular artery","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 50"}],"q":[{"id":"D045-94-1","s":"During a laparoscopic procedure for an undescended testis, the surgeon identifies the artery supplying the testis. Which of the following best describes the origin and course of this artery?","o":["A. Branch of the internal iliac artery; descends behind the bladder to the scrotum","B. Branch of the inferior epigastric artery; passes through the deep inguinal ring to the testis","C. Direct branch of the abdominal aorta; passes through the inguinal canal within the spermatic cord","D. Branch of the external iliac artery; travels anterior to the deep inguinal ring","E. Branch of the inferior vesical artery; runs with the ductus deferens to the testis"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Direct branch of the abdominal aorta; passes through the inguinal canal within the spermatic cord","a":"C","why":"<b>A direct branch of the abdominal aorta, running in the spermatic cord.</b> The testis forms high on the back wall of the abdomen. Its artery leaves the aorta just below the renal arteries and is pulled down with it.<br>A. Internal iliac branches supply pelvic organs, not the testis.<br>B. The inferior epigastric artery gives only the small cremasteric artery.<br>D. The external iliac artery does not supply the testis.<br>E. The vesical arteries give only the small artery of the ductus deferens.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 50 (Blood Supply)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":94,"f":"D045"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":59,"label":"Pampiniform plexus cools the testis","slides":[{"src":"sl/G003-51.jpg","cap":"Pampiniform plexus wraps the testicular artery, drawing heat away","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 51"},{"src":"sl/G003-43.jpg","cap":"Cross-section: testicular artery surrounded by pampiniform plexus of veins","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 43"}],"q":[{"id":"D045-92-1","s":"A 28-year-old man presents with infertility. Physical exam reveals no scrotal swelling or tenderness. Testicular biopsy shows normal spermatogenesis. The physician explains that sperm function depends on maintaining testicular temperature below core body temperature. Which of the following structures primarily facilitates this temperature regulation?","o":["A. Tunica vaginalis","B. Cremasteric muscle","C. Dartos fascia","D. Pampiniform venous plexus","E. Tunica albuginea"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Pampiniform venous plexus","a":"D","why":"<b>Pampiniform venous plexus.</b> Its eight to twelve veins wrap the testicular artery. Cool blood leaving the testis takes heat from warm blood coming in (countercurrent cooling), so the testis stays a few degrees below body temperature.<br>A. Tunica vaginalis: a serous sac around the testis.<br>B. Cremaster: helps by raising or lowering the testis, but is not the main cooler.<br>C. Dartos: wrinkles the skin to hold heat; also only a helper.<br>E. Tunica albuginea: the testis capsule.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 51 (Thermal Regulation)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 50 (Blood Supply)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":92,"f":"D045"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":59,"label":"Left testicular vein → left renal vein","slides":[{"src":"sl/G003-50.jpg","cap":"Left testicular vein drains to left renal vein; right to inferior vena cava","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 50"},{"src":"sl/F1253-178.jpg","cap":"Left gonadal vein joins left renal vein, which passes under superior mesenteric artery","where":", slide 178"}],"q":[{"id":"D041-62-1","s":"A 38-year-old man was diagnosed with metastatic cancer of the left testes. Which of the following most likely represents the route by which the cancer spread from the left testes to the rest of the body?","o":["A. Left testicular vein; inferior vena cava; left renal vein","B. Right testicular vein; left testicular vein; inferior vena cava","C. Left testicular vein; left common iliac vein; inferior vena cava","D. Left testicular vein; median sacral vein; inferior vena cava","E. Left testicular vein; left renal vein; inferior vena cava"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Left testicular vein; left renal vein; inferior vena cava","a":"E","why":"<b>Left testicular vein, then left renal vein, then inferior vena cava (IVC).</b> Cancer cells in the veins follow the blood. The left testicular vein empties into the left renal vein, which crosses to the IVC.<br>A. The renal vein comes before the IVC, not after.<br>B. Each testis drains on its own side; the right vein is not involved.<br>C. The testicular vein does not join the common iliac vein.<br>D. The median sacral vein drains the sacrum, not the testis.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 50 (Blood Supply)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":62,"f":"D041"}],"t":1},{"id":"D045-79-1","s":"A 28-year-old man presents with scrotal swelling that worsens when standing. Ultrasound shows dilated veins of the pampiniform plexus. Which of the following best explains the anatomical reason this varicocele is usually left-sided?","o":["A. Left spermatic cord has fewer lymphatics, leading to venous dilation","B. Left vas deferens is shorter, increasing venous pressure","C. Left testicular vein drains into the left renal vein at a right angle, while the right drains into the inferior vena cava","D. Left cremaster muscle is weaker, allowing reflux of blood","E. Left testicular artery is longer than the right, causing venous congestion"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Left testicular vein drains into the left renal vein at a right angle, while the right drains into the inferior vena cava","a":"C","why":"<b>The left testicular vein joins the left renal vein at a right angle; the right one drains straight into the inferior vena cava.</b> The right-angle join and longer blood column raise pressure on the left, so the pampiniform plexus widens. (A varicocele is widened veins, not inflamed ones.)<br>A. Lymph vessels do not drain venous blood.<br>B. Vas deferens length does not affect vein pressure.<br>D. The cremaster does not control vein valves.<br>E. Artery length does not cause venous congestion.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 50 (Blood Supply)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 53 (Clinical Correlates)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":79,"f":"D045"}]},{"id":"F1228-120-1","s":"Young Drake goes off to college to run cross country. Young Drake’s body fat dwindles down to a dangerous 4%. One day, he notices pain and swelling of his left testicle. What’s going on, and what’s the mechanism?","o":["A. Testicular Torsian: external force causing twisting of the spermatic cord","B. Nutcracker syndrome: loss of mesenteric fat causing clamping down of the L testicular vein","C. Nutcracker syndrome: rigorous motion from running causing disruption of blood flow back to L renal vein/aorta","D. Hydrocele: infection with bacteria","E. Elephantiasis: parasitic infection"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Nutcracker syndrome: loss of mesenteric fat causing clamping down of the L testicular vein","a":"B","why":"<b>Nutcracker syndrome.</b> Losing fat removes the cushion around the left renal vein, so it gets pinched between the superior mesenteric artery and the aorta. The left testicular vein drains into that vein, so blood backs up into the left testis. (Strictly, the renal vein is pinched, not the testicular vein.)<br>A. Torsion: a twist with sudden severe pain, not caused by fat loss.<br>C. Running does not block venous return.<br>D. A hydrocele is fluid, not an infection.<br>E. Elephantiasis: worms block lymph vessels.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 50 (Blood Supply)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 53 (Clinical Correlates)"],"src":[{"t":"2019 review","n":"old Exam 2 review: abdomen part only","sl":120,"f":"F1228"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":60,"label":"Testis → para-aortic (lumbar) nodes","slides":[{"src":"sl/G003-52.jpg","cap":"Solid arrow: testis lymph along testicular vessels to lumbar (para-aortic) nodes","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 52"},{"src":"sl/D041-58.jpg","cap":"Testis summary: lymph drains to lumbar (para-aortic) nodes","where":"2025 TA review, slide 58"}],"q":[{"id":"F1228-129-1","s":"Where would the testicular cancer spread to first?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Paraaortic (lumbar) lymph nodes","a":null,"why":"<b>Para-aortic (lumbar) lymph nodes.</b> Lymph follows the testicular vessels back up to where the testis formed, beside the aorta near the kidneys.<br>Common mix-up: answering superficial inguinal nodes. Those drain the scrotal skin, not the testis.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 52 (Lymphatics)"],"src":[{"t":"2019 review","n":"old Exam 2 review: abdomen part only","sl":129,"f":"F1228"}]},{"id":"F1252-72-1","s":"A 46 year old man comes to your office with testicular cancer. You are worried there might have been lymphatic spread. Which lymph nodes should you check?","o":["A. Lumbar","B. Superficial inguinal","C. Deep inguinal","D. Sacral","E. Internal iliac"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Lumbar","a":"A","why":"<b>Lumbar nodes.</b> Testicular lymph follows the testicular vessels up to the lumbar (para-aortic) nodes beside the aorta and inferior vena cava, near where the testis formed.<br>B. Superficial inguinal: scrotal skin.<br>C. Deep inguinal: deep lower limb and glans of the penis.<br>D. Sacral: rectum and back of the pelvis.<br>E. Internal iliac: pelvic organs.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 52 (Lymphatics)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":72,"f":"F1252"}]},{"id":"D045-107-1","s":"A 26-year-old medical student noticed a lump on his left testis, which was later diagnosed as testicular cancer. To rule out metastases of the testicular cancer, what group of lymph nodes should be checked in the first instance?","o":["A. Superficial inguinal","B. Deep inguinal","C. Lumbar","D. External iliac","E. Internal iliac"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Lumbar","a":"C","why":"<b>Lumbar nodes.</b> The testis drains straight to the lumbar (para-aortic) nodes beside the aorta, because it formed on the back abdominal wall and pulled its vessels down with it.<br>A. Superficial inguinal: scrotal skin.<br>B. Deep inguinal: deep lower limb and glans of the penis.<br>D. External iliac: lower limb and part of the bladder.<br>E. Internal iliac: pelvic organs.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 52 (Lymphatics)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":107,"f":"D045"}],"t":1},{"id":"D045-96-1","s":"A 30-year-old man presents with a painless testicular mass. Ultrasound confirms a solid intratesticular tumor. Which group of lymph nodes would most likely show metastatic spread of this cancer?","o":["A. Superficial inguinal nodes","B. Deep inguinal nodes","C. External iliac nodes","D. Internal iliac nodes","E. Para-aortic (lumbar) nodes"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Para-aortic (lumbar) nodes","a":"E","why":"<b>Para-aortic (lumbar) nodes.</b> A tumor inside the testis spreads along the testicular vessels to the nodes beside the aorta, near the kidneys, where the testis first formed.<br>A. Superficial inguinal: scrotal skin, not the testis.<br>B. Deep inguinal: deep thigh and glans of the penis.<br>C. External iliac: lower limb and some pelvic organs.<br>D. Internal iliac: pelvic organs.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 52 (Lymphatics)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":96,"f":"D045"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3902-205-1","s":"A 36-year old male comes into your office and explains that he has been recently diagnosed with testicular cancer. He heard that testicular cancer can spread to the lymph nodes, and asks how this is possible. You explain to the patient that this can occur since the lymph from testes drains into the _________ .","o":["A. Superficial inguinal lymph nodes","B. Aortic lymph nodes","C. Lumbar lymph nodes","D. Pre-aortic lymph nodes","E. Cisterna Chyli"],"g":["B","C"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Pre-aortic lymph nodes","a":"D","why":"<b>Aortic (B) or lumbar (C) nodes: the same group.</b> The lecture calls them <b>para-aortic</b> nodes, beside the aorta and inferior vena cava. Testicular lymph runs along the testicular vessels to them.<br>A. Superficial inguinal: scrotal skin.<br>D. Pre-aortic: in front of the aorta; they mainly drain the gut. Moore says some testicular lymph reaches them, but the lecture's answer is para-aortic.<br>E. Cisterna chyli: a later collecting sac, not the first nodes.","ex":"","fl":["Disputed key: the 2026 lecture (G003:52) says testicular lymph drains to the PARA-aortic nodes = lateral aortic/lumbar nodes, i.e. option B (or its synonym C, 'lumbar'); pre-aortic nodes are the gut-draining celiac/SMA/IMA nodes. Gray's also lists pre-aortic nodes, so D is defensible but is not the lecture's answer, and B/C/D overlap."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 52 (Lymphatics)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":205,"f":"F3902"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":60,"label":"Scrotum → superficial inguinal nodes","slides":[{"src":"sl/G003-52.jpg","cap":"Dashed arrow: scrotal lymph to superficial inguinal nodes; solid arrow: testis to lumbar","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 52"}],"q":[{"id":"D041-60-1","s":"In a routine visit to the outpatient clinic for his annual checkup, a 42-year-old man is informed that ultrasound examination has given strong evidence that he has a tumor of his scrotum that appears not to be in a testes. Which of the following nodes are the first lymph nodes that drain the affected area?","o":["A. Superficial inguinal","B. Internal iliac","C. Lumbar","D. Presacral","E. Axillary"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Superficial inguinal","a":"A","why":"<b>Superficial inguinal nodes.</b> The scrotum is skin, and skin below the umbilicus drains to these groin nodes. (The testis inside is different: it drains to the lumbar nodes.)<br>B. Internal iliac: pelvic organs and deep perineum.<br>C. Lumbar: testis, kidney, ovary; not scrotal skin.<br>D. Presacral: rectum and pelvic organs.<br>E. Axillary: abdominal wall skin above the umbilicus.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 52 (Lymphatics)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 46 (Scrotum)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":60,"f":"D041"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3902-207-1","s":"The previous pts cancer spread to his scrotum. Is the lymphatic drainage of the scrotum the same as the testes?","o":["A. True","B. False"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"False","a":"B","why":"<b>False.</b> The scrotum drains to the <b>superficial inguinal nodes</b>. The testis drains along its vessels to the <b>para-aortic (lumbar) nodes</b>, because it began high on the back abdominal wall.<br>Common mix-up: assuming the testis shares the groin drainage of the scrotal skin around it.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 52 (Lymphatics)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":207,"f":"F3902"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":61,"label":"Undescended testis: cryptorchidism, along descent path","slides":[{"src":"sl/G003-53.jpg","cap":"Cryptorchid testes: failed to fully descend into the scrotum","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 53"},{"src":"sl/G003-32.jpg","cap":"Testis descent path: abdomen, deep ring, canal, superficial ring, scrotum","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 32"}],"q":[{"id":"F1180-243-1","s":"Testicles are absent from the scrotum of a 1-year old male admitted to the pediatric clinic. The pediatrician examined the infant and palpated the testes in the inguinal canal. Which of the following terms is used to describe this condition?","o":["A. Pseudohermaphroditism","B. True hermaphroditism","C. Cryptorchism","D. Congenital adrenal hyperplasia","E. Chordee"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Cryptorchism","a":"C","why":"<b>Cryptorchism (cryptorchidism).</b> The testis did not finish descending and stopped on its normal path, here in the inguinal canal.<br>A. Pseudohermaphroditism: gonads of one sex with outer genitals that look like the other sex.<br>B. True hermaphroditism: both ovarian and testicular tissue.<br>D. Congenital adrenal hyperplasia: an adrenal enzyme defect that can masculinize girls.<br>E. Chordee: a downward bend of the penis.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 53 (Clinical Correlates)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":243,"f":"F1180"},{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":187,"f":"F1178"}],"t":1},{"id":"F0008-70-1","s":"A pediatrician has diagnosed a newborn baby of having right-sided cryptorchidism (undescended testis). The testis may have been trapped in any site EXCEPT:","o":["a. Perineum","b. Just outside the superficial inguinal ring","c. Pelvic brim","d. At the deep inguinal ring","e. Somewhere in the inguinal canal"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Perineum","a":"A","why":"<b>Perineum.</b> An undescended testis stops somewhere on its normal path: abdomen, pelvic brim, deep ring, inguinal canal, or just past the superficial ring. A testis in the perineum is off that path; that is an ectopic testis, not cryptorchidism.<br>B, C, D and E. All are points on the normal path of descent, so a testis can get stuck at each.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 53 (Clinical Correlates)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 32 (Development of inguinal Canal)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":70,"f":"F0008"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G2","u":61,"label":"Undescended testes → testicular cancer risk","slides":[{"src":"sl/G003-53.jpg","cap":"Clinical correlates: cryptorchid testes; testis cancer traced by lymph drainage","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 53"}],"q":[{"id":"F1228-127-1","s":"A 24 y/o female patient comes in with her husband with trouble conceiving. After performing genetic testing, you find that she has a blind vagina and is void of a uterus and ovaries. Karyotyping shows XY, suggestive of Androgen Insensitivity Syndrome. Imaging shows undescended testes. What are you most worried about?","o":["A. Cryptorchidism causing testicular cancer","B. Breast cancer","C. High testosterone causing male pattern baldness","D. Testicular torsion","E. Indirect hernias"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Cryptorchidism causing testicular cancer","a":"A","why":"<b>Cryptorchidism causing testicular cancer.</b> In androgen insensitivity syndrome, the person has male chromosomes (XY) but body cells ignore testosterone, so the testes stay in the abdomen or groin. Undescended testes have a much higher cancer risk, so they are usually removed.<br>B. Breast cancer: no special risk here.<br>C. Testosterone has no effect, since the body cannot respond to it.<br>D. Torsion: possible, but far less worrying than cancer.<br>E. Indirect hernias: possible, but not the main danger.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 53 (Clinical Correlates)"],"src":[{"t":"2019 review","n":"old Exam 2 review: abdomen part only","sl":127,"f":"F1228"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G3","u":62,"label":"Parietal lines wall, sharp pain; visceral covers organs","slides":[{"src":"sl/G004-8.jpg","cap":"Sagittal diagram labelling parietal peritoneum, visceral peritoneum and peritoneal cavity","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 8"},{"src":"sl/G004-10.jpg","cap":"Parietal peritoneum lines the wall; pain-sensitive and well localized","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 10"},{"src":"sl/D041-140.jpg","cap":"Summary: parietal pain well localized, visceral pain poorly localized","where":"2025 TA review, slide 140"}],"q":[{"id":"D045-180-1","s":"The parietal peritoneum differs from the visceral peritoneum primarily because","o":["A. Parietal lines organs; visceral lines the abdominal wall","B. Parietal is insensitive to pain; visceral is highly sensitive","C. Parietal lines the abdominal wall; visceral covers organs","D. Parietal produces bile; visceral produces mucus"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Parietal lines the abdominal wall; visceral covers organs","a":"C","why":"<b>Parietal lines the abdominal wall; visceral covers the organs.</b><br>A. Reversed.<br>B. Reversed. Parietal is the sensitive layer, with somatic nerves and sharp, well-localized pain. Visceral lacks sensory innervation, so its pain is vague.<br>D. Peritoneum is a serous membrane that makes only a thin film of fluid. Bile comes from the liver and mucus from gut glands.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 10 (Parietal Peritoneum)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 11 (Visceral Peritoneum)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":180,"f":"D045"}],"t":1},{"id":"D042-102-1","s":"The parietal peritoneum lines ____, and pain is ____. The visceral peritoneum lines ___ and pain is ___.","o":["A. Internal abdominal-pelvic wall, well localized; Organs, vaguely/poorly localized","B. Organs, well localized; Internal abdominal and pelvic wall, vaguely/poorly localized","C. Internal abdominal and pelvic wall, vaguely/poorly localized; Organs, well localized","D. Organs, well localized; Internal abdominal and pelvic wall, vaguely/poorly localized"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Internal abdominal-pelvic wall, well localized; Organs, vaguely/poorly localized","a":"A","why":"<b>Parietal lines the abdominal and pelvic wall, and its pain is well localized. Visceral covers the organs, and its pain is poorly localized.</b> Parietal peritoneum shares the somatic nerves of the wall it lines, so it feels pain, heat, cold and cutting sharply. Visceral peritoneum lacks those sensory nerves, so pain is vague.<br>B and D (the same choice twice): swap both the linings and the pain.<br>C. Right linings, but the pain pattern is reversed.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 10 (Parietal Peritoneum)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 11 (Visceral Peritoneum)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI module TA review","sl":102,"f":"D042"}]},{"id":"D041-138-1","s":"Which of the following statements best describes the relationship between the parietal peritoneum, visceral peritoneum, and the peritoneal cavity?","o":["A. The parietal peritoneum covers abdominal organs, the visceral peritoneum lines the abdominal wall, and the peritoneal cavity is located inside the organs themselves.","B. The parietal peritoneum lines the abdominal wall, the visceral peritoneum covers the abdominal organs, and the peritoneal cavity is the potential space between the two layers.","C. The parietal peritoneum attaches the intestines to the posterior abdominal wall, the visceral peritoneum forms ligaments around the liver, and the peritoneal cavity is located behind the peritoneum.","D. The parietal peritoneum surrounds the kidneys, the visceral peritoneum surrounds the spleen only, and the peritoneal cavity separates the abdominal cavity from the pelvic cavity."],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"The parietal peritoneum lines the abdominal wall, the visceral peritoneum covers the abdominal organs, and the peritoneal cavity is the potential space between the two layers.","a":"B","why":"<b>Parietal lines the abdominal wall, visceral covers the organs, and the cavity is the potential space between them.</b><br>A. Swaps the two layers, and the cavity is never inside organs.<br>C. Holding intestine to the posterior wall is the job of a mesentery. The space behind the peritoneum is the retroperitoneum, not the peritoneal cavity.<br>D. Kidneys are retroperitoneal, visceral peritoneum covers many organs, and the cavity runs on into the pelvis without a divider.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 10 (Parietal Peritoneum)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 11 (Visceral Peritoneum)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 8 (Peritoneum)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":138,"f":"D041"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G3","u":62,"label":"Peritoneal cavity: potential space between the layers","slides":[{"src":"sl/G004-9.jpg","cap":"Sagittal diagram: peritoneal cavity between parietal and visceral peritoneum","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 9"}],"q":[{"id":"D042-104-1","s":"The peritoneal cavity is located ___","o":["A. Superficial to parietal peritoneum,","B. Space within visceral peritoneum,","C. In between parietal and visceral peritoneum","D. Retroperitoneal location"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"In between parietal and visceral peritoneum","a":"C","why":"<b>Between the parietal and visceral peritoneum.</b> The peritoneal cavity is a thin potential space between the layer lining the wall and the layer covering the organs. It holds only a film of peritoneal fluid.<br>A. Superficial to parietal peritoneum: that is extraperitoneal fat and the body wall, outside the cavity.<br>B. Within visceral peritoneum: under that layer is the organ itself.<br>D. Retroperitoneal: that space lies behind the parietal peritoneum, outside the cavity.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 8 (Peritoneum)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 31 (Peritoneal cavity)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI module TA review","sl":104,"f":"D042"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3903-330-1","s":"Technically, the peritoneal cavity:","o":["A) contains all the abdominal organs.","B) is a closed cavity in both sexes.","C) is the potential space between the parietal and visceral peritoneum.","D) is typically open to the testis in the male.","E) contains lymph nodes."],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"is the potential space between the parietal and visceral peritoneum","a":"C","why":"<b>It is the potential space between parietal and visceral peritoneum.</b> It holds only a thin film of fluid, no organs.<br>A. Contains the organs: no; organs lie outside it, wrapped in visceral peritoneum.<br>B. Closed in both sexes: closed in males only. In females it opens to the outside through the uterine tubes, uterus and vagina.<br>D. Open to the testis: the processus vaginalis normally seals, so the male cavity is closed.<br>E. Lymph nodes: these sit in mesenteries and behind the peritoneum, not in the cavity.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 9 (Between parietal and visceral layers)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":330,"f":"F3903"},{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":100,"f":"F0008"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G3","u":62,"label":"Parietal from somatic, visceral from splanchnic mesoderm","slides":[{"src":"sl/G001-5.jpg","cap":"Lateral folding: somatic lateral plate mesoderm on wall, visceral layer around gut","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 5"},{"src":"sl/G001-4.jpg","cap":"Embryo cross-sections: somatic and splanchnic mesoderm around the forming coelom","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 4"}],"q":[{"id":"D042-100-1","s":"The parietal peritoneum is derived from____ ; The visceral peritoneum is derived from ____.","o":["A. Ectoderm ; endoderm","B. Endoderm ; somatic lateral plate mesoderm","C. Somatic lateral plate mesoderm ; splanchnic lateral plate mesoderm","D. Somatic lateral plate mesoderm ; endoderm"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Somatic lateral plate mesoderm ; splanchnic lateral plate mesoderm","a":"C","why":"<b>Somatic lateral plate mesoderm forms parietal peritoneum; splanchnic lateral plate mesoderm forms visceral peritoneum.</b> Lateral plate mesoderm splits into a somatic layer that lines the body wall and a splanchnic (visceral) layer that wraps the gut tube. The gap between them becomes the peritoneal cavity.<br>A. Ectoderm makes skin and nerves; endoderm makes the gut lining, not peritoneum.<br>B. Endoderm does not make peritoneum, and the layers are swapped.<br>D. Visceral peritoneum is mesoderm, not endoderm.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 10 (Parietal Peritoneum)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 11 (Visceral Peritoneum)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI module TA review","sl":100,"f":"D042"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G3","u":63,"label":"Intraperitoneal vs retroperitoneal: meaning and examples","slides":[{"src":"sl/G004-14.jpg","cap":"Sagittal section marking stomach, aorta, pancreas and uterus by peritoneal relation","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 14"},{"src":"sl/G004-13.jpg","cap":"Fist-in-balloon model: organ invaginates the peritoneal sac","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 13"},{"src":"sl/D045-186.jpg","cap":"Intraperitoneal vs retroperitoneal definitions with example organs","where":"2025 Montana TA review, slide 186"}],"q":[{"id":"F3112-40-1","s":"What does intraperitoneal mean? What does retroperitoneal mean? Do retroperitoneal organs have any mesentery associated with them at all?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Intraperitoneal: surrounded by peritoneum (mesentery) on all sides. Retroperitoneal: located posterior to the peritoneum. Retroperitoneal organs have peritoneum covering only their anterior surfaces, with the posterior surface pressed against the posterior abdominal wall.","a":null,"why":"<b>Intraperitoneal</b> organs are covered by visceral peritoneum almost all the way round and usually hang on a mesentery (stomach, jejunum, ileum, transverse colon). <b>Retroperitoneal</b> organs lie behind the parietal peritoneum, pressed on the posterior wall. They have no mesentery; peritoneum covers only their front surface.<br>Common mix-up: intraperitoneal does not mean the organ sits inside the peritoneal cavity. The cavity holds only fluid; the organ pushes into the sac like a fist into a balloon.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 13 (Peritoneal organs)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"Peritoneum & celiac trunk interactive (JL)","sl":40,"f":"F3112"}],"t":1},{"id":"D045-182-1","s":"Which organ is intraperitoneal?","o":["A. Stomach","B. Kidney","C. Bladder","D. Pancreas"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Stomach","a":"A","why":"<b>Stomach.</b> It is wrapped by visceral peritoneum and hangs from the lesser and greater omenta, so it is intraperitoneal.<br>B. Kidney: primarily retroperitoneal, with peritoneum only on its front.<br>C. Bladder: subperitoneal, with peritoneum only on its upper surface.<br>D. Pancreas: secondarily retroperitoneal (except its tail).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 13 (Peritoneal organs)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 14 (Peritoneal organs)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":182,"f":"D045"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3114-48-1","s":"Make sure you are comfortable with which organs are intraperitoneal and which ones are retroperitoneal. Intraperitoneal = covered by mesentery on all sides. Retroperitoneal = outside of the peritoneum, usually covered by mesentery only on anterior surface. Name as many retroperitoneal organs as you can!","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"SAD PUCKER: Suprarenal (adrenal) glands; Aorta and IVC (inferior vena cava); Duodenum (second to fourth parts); Pancreas (except the tail); Ureters; Colon (ascending and descending); Kidneys; Esophagus (abdominal part); Rectum (upper two-thirds; the lower third is below the peritoneum).","a":null,"why":"<b>SAD PUCKER</b> lists them: Suprarenal (adrenal) glands, Aorta and IVC (inferior vena cava), Duodenum (second to fourth parts), Pancreas (except the tail), Ureters, Colon (ascending and descending), Kidneys, Esophagus (abdominal part), Rectum (upper two-thirds).<br>Primarily retroperitoneal (never had a mesentery): kidneys, ureters, suprarenal glands, aorta, IVC. Secondarily retroperitoneal (lost their mesentery): duodenum, pancreas, ascending and descending colon.<br>Common mix-up: the first two centimetres of the duodenum and the pancreatic tail are intraperitoneal.","ex":"","fl":["The source listed the duodenum as parts 2-3 and the rectum as its lower two-thirds; the retroperitoneal duodenum is parts 2-4 (all but its first 2 cm), and it is the upper two-thirds of the rectum that is retroperitoneal, while the lower third is subperitoneal."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 13 (Peritoneal organs)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 14 (Peritoneal organs)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"Small & large intestines interactive","sl":48,"f":"F3114"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G3","u":63,"label":"Secondarily retroperitoneal: pancreas, duodenum, ascending/descending colon","slides":[{"src":"sl/G001-8.jpg","cap":"Retroperitoneal vs secondarily retroperitoneal organs, each with its organ list","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 8"},{"src":"sl/D041-148.jpg","cap":"SAD PUCKER list; secondary: duodenum 2-4, pancreas except tail, colon","where":"2025 TA review, slide 148"},{"src":"sl/G005-63.jpg","cap":"Ascending colon on posterior wall: secondarily retroperitoneal","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 63"}],"q":[{"id":"D041-146-1","s":"Which of the following are a correct match","o":["A. Kidneys - intraperitoneal","B. Rectum - secondary retroperitoneal","C. Tail of the pancreas - retroperitoneal","D. Aorta - intraperitoneal","E. Pancreas - secondary retroperitoneal"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Pancreas - secondary retroperitoneal","a":"E","why":"<b>Pancreas: secondarily retroperitoneal.</b> It forms inside a mesentery, then is pressed against the posterior wall and loses that mesentery. Only its tail stays intraperitoneal, in the splenorenal ligament.<br>A. Kidneys: primarily retroperitoneal (never had a mesentery).<br>B. Rectum: retroperitoneal (the R in SAD PUCKER, the memory word for retroperitoneal organs), but not on the lecture's secondarily retroperitoneal list (duodenum, pancreas, ascending and descending colon).<br>C. Tail of pancreas: intraperitoneal.<br>D. Aorta: primarily retroperitoneal.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 13 (Peritoneal organs)","Pancreas & Spleen slide 11 (Pancreas)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":146,"f":"D041"}]},{"id":"F1252-136-1","s":"Which of the following are secondary retroperitoneal (choose all that apply) ?","o":["A. A. Kidneys","B. B. Tail of the pancreas","C. C. Uterine cervix","D. D. Gonads","E. E. Pancreas (except tail)","F. F. Ascending colon","G. G. Descending colon","H. H. 1st part of duodenum","I. I. Duodenum parts 2-4","J. J. Uterine cervix","K. K. Gonads"],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Select all: E. Pancreas (except tail), F. Ascending colon, G. Descending colon, I. Duodenum parts 2-4","a":null,"why":"<b>Pancreas (except tail), ascending colon, descending colon, and duodenum parts two to four.</b> These began inside a mesentery, then fused to the posterior abdominal wall and lost it.<br>A. Kidneys: primarily retroperitoneal (never had a mesentery).<br>B. Tail of pancreas: stays intraperitoneal, in the splenorenal ligament.<br>C and J. Uterine cervix: primarily retroperitoneal, listed with the kidneys in the GI (gastrointestinal) development lecture.<br>D and K. Gonads: form in the posterior wall without a gut mesentery, so they are not secondarily retroperitoneal.<br>H. First part of duodenum: its first two centimetres are intraperitoneal.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 13 (Peritoneal organs)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":136,"f":"F1252"}]},{"id":"F3112-39-1","s":"While exploring the open abdominal cavity in lab, you notice that some organs are easily movable while others are stationary. You remember this has something to do with organs either having mesenteries or being retroperitoneal. Which of the following organs would you not be able to easily move about?","o":["A. Transverse colon","B. Ascending colon","C. Appendix","D. Jejunum","E. Sigmoid colon"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Ascending colon","a":"B","why":"<b>Ascending colon.</b> It is secondarily retroperitoneal. Its mesentery fused to the posterior abdominal wall, so it is fixed in place and has peritoneum only on its front.<br>A. Transverse colon: hangs on the transverse mesocolon, so it moves.<br>C. Appendix: has its own mesoappendix.<br>D. Jejunum: hangs on the mesentery proper.<br>E. Sigmoid colon: hangs on the sigmoid mesocolon.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 13 (Peritoneal organs)","Small & Large Intestines slide 63 (Ascending colon)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"Peritoneum & celiac trunk interactive (JL)","sl":39,"f":"F3112"},{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":316,"f":"F3903"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G3","u":64,"label":"Ligament, omentum, mesentery: double-layer definitions","slides":[{"src":"sl/G004-48.jpg","cap":"Table defining omenta, mesenteries and peritoneal ligaments as double layers","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 48"},{"src":"sl/G004-23.jpg","cap":"Mesentery joins an intraperitoneal organ to the posterior wall","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 23"}],"q":[{"id":"D045-189-1","s":"A peritoneal ligament is defined as","o":["A. A double layer of peritoneum attaching organs to the posterior abdominal wall","B. A double layer of peritoneum attaching an organ to another organ or the body wall","C. A four-layer fold of peritoneum from the stomach","D. A single layer of peritoneum containing blood vessels"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"A double layer of peritoneum attaching an organ to another organ or the body wall","a":"B","why":"<b>A double layer of peritoneum joining an organ to another organ or to the body wall.</b> Example: the falciform ligament, liver to anterior abdominal wall.<br>A. Joining intestine to the posterior abdominal wall describes a mesentery.<br>C. A four-layer fold from the stomach is the greater omentum (its double layer folds back on itself).<br>D. Peritoneal ligaments are double, not single, layers.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 17 (1. Peritoneal ligament)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":189,"f":"D045"}],"t":1},{"id":"D042-106-1","s":"Which of the following statements is true?","o":["A. The hepatogastric ligament is the free border of the lesser omentum","B. Mesenteries connect retroperitoneal organs to the posterior abdominal wall","C. Peritoneal ligaments consist of a single layer of peritoneum connecting viscera together or to the abdominal wall","D. The Omenta and Mesenteries are both derived from visceral peritoneum","E. The Omenta are derived from parietal peritoneum, but the Mesenteries are derived from visceral peritoneum"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"The Omenta and Mesenteries are both derived from visceral peritoneum","a":"D","why":"<b>Omenta and mesenteries are both double folds of visceral peritoneum.</b> They form as the gut tube and its mesenteries carry visceral peritoneum with them. At their roots they become continuous with parietal peritoneum.<br>A. The free border of the lesser omentum is the hepatoduodenal ligament, not the hepatogastric.<br>B. Mesenteries hold intraperitoneal organs; retroperitoneal organs have none.<br>C. Peritoneal ligaments are double layers, not single.<br>E. Omenta are not parietal; both come from visceral peritoneum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 17 (1. Peritoneal ligament)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 23 (3. Mesenteries)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 22 (Parts of lesser omentum)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI module TA review","sl":106,"f":"D042"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G3","u":64,"label":"Falciform ligament: liver to anterior abdominal wall","slides":[{"src":"sl/G004-17.jpg","cap":"Falciform ligament on the front of the liver: example peritoneal ligament","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 17"},{"src":"sl/G004-50.jpg","cap":"Table: falciform ligament runs from liver to anterior abdominal wall","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 50"}],"q":[{"id":"D041-166-1","s":"Which statement correctly describes the falciform ligament?","o":["A. Connects the lesser curvature of the stomach to the liver","B. Connects the anterior liver surface to the anterior abdominal wall and diaphragm","C. Connects the transverse colon to the posterior abdominal wall","D. Contains the gastroduodenal artery","E. Is continuous with the greater omentum"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Connects the anterior liver surface to the anterior abdominal wall and diaphragm","a":"B","why":"<b>It joins the front of the liver to the anterior abdominal wall and diaphragm.</b> Its free lower edge carries the round ligament of the liver (the closed-off umbilical vein).<br>A. Lesser curvature to liver: the hepatogastric ligament of the lesser omentum.<br>C. Transverse colon to posterior wall: the transverse mesocolon.<br>D. The gastroduodenal artery runs behind the first part of the duodenum, not in the falciform ligament.<br>E. It is separate from the greater omentum; it is a remnant of the ventral mesentery.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 17 (1. Peritoneal ligament)","Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 11 (Anterior surface of liver)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":166,"f":"D041"}],"t":1},{"id":"D041-142-1","s":"A 43 year old man has intense abdominal pain, he has a right subphrenic abscess that extends to the midline. Which structure attaches the liver to the anterior abdominal wall, describing a peritoneal ligament?","o":["A. Greater omentum","B. Falciform ligament","C. Mesentery proper","D. Lesser omentum","E. Gastroduodenal ligament"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Falciform ligament","a":"B","why":"<b>Falciform ligament.</b> A peritoneal ligament is a double layer of peritoneum joining an organ to another organ or to the wall. The falciform ligament joins the liver to the anterior abdominal wall and diaphragm, and it splits the subphrenic space into right and left.<br>A. Greater omentum: stomach to transverse colon.<br>C. Mesentery proper: small intestine to posterior wall.<br>D. Lesser omentum: liver to stomach and duodenum.<br>E. Gastroduodenal ligament: does not join the liver to the anterior wall.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 17 (1. Peritoneal ligament)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":142,"f":"D041"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G3","u":64,"label":"Mesentery root: duodenojejunal junction to right sacroiliac joint","slides":[{"src":"sl/G004-25.jpg","cap":"Root of mesentery (green) running obliquely down to the right","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 25"},{"src":"sl/G004-26.jpg","cap":"Posterior wall: root of mesentery, duodenojejunal junction, transverse mesocolon root","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 26"}],"q":[{"id":"F3903-373-1","s":"While assisting in a bowel resection the surgeon asked you to extend the small bowel from its root. Where do you find the origin of the root of the mesentery?","o":["A. Directed obliquely to the left from the approximately the left side of the L2 vertebra to the left sacroiliac joint","B. Directed obliquely to the right from the duodenojejunal junction to the right (ileocolic junction)","C. Directed obliquely to the right from approximately the right side of the L2 vertebra to the right side of the sacroiliac joint","D. Directed approximately vertical from the lateral aspect of the left paracolic gutter to the rectum.","E. Directed approximately horizontally from the right to the left paracoloic gutters at the L2 vertebra"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Directed obliquely to the right from the duodenojejunal junction to the right (ileocolic junction)","a":"B","why":"<b>It runs from the duodenojejunal junction, on the left of the second lumbar vertebra (L2), obliquely down and to the right to the ileocolic junction and right sacroiliac joint.</b> The root is about fifteen centimetres long.<br>A. Wrong direction; it runs to the right, not to the left sacroiliac joint.<br>C. It starts on the left side of L2, not the right.<br>D. No mesentery runs vertically from the left paracolic gutter to the rectum.<br>E. A roughly horizontal root is the transverse mesocolon, along the lower border of the pancreas.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 25 (Root of Mesentery)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":373,"f":"F3903"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G3","u":65,"label":"Greater omentum: greater curvature to transverse colon","slides":[{"src":"sl/G004-21.jpg","cap":"Greater omentum parts starred: gastrophrenic, gastrosplenic, gastrocolic ligaments","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 21"},{"src":"sl/G004-20.jpg","cap":"Greater omentum from greater curvature and duodenum to transverse colon","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 20"}],"q":[{"id":"D045-191-1","s":"Where does the Greater Omentum attach","o":["A. Holds the Intestines to posterior wall of the abdomen","B. Lesser Curvature of the Stomach to the Liver","C. Runs from the greater curvature and first part of duodenum to the anterior aspect of transverse colon."],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Runs from the greater curvature and first part of duodenum to the anterior aspect of transverse colon.","a":"C","why":"<b>From the greater curvature of the stomach and first part of the duodenum to the front of the transverse colon.</b> It then hangs down like an apron over the small intestine.<br>A. Holding the intestines to the posterior wall is the mesentery.<br>B. Lesser curvature of the stomach to the liver is the lesser omentum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 20 (Greater omentum)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 21 (Parts of greater omentum)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":191,"f":"D045"}],"t":1},{"id":"F1156-56-1","s":"Which of the following ligaments extends from the greater curvature of the stomach to the transverse colon?","o":["A. Gastrophrenic","B. Gastrosplenic","C. Gastrointestinal","D. Gastrocolic"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Gastrocolic","a":"D","why":"<b>Gastrocolic.</b> The name gives both ends: gastro (stomach, greater curvature) and colic (transverse colon). It is the apron part of the greater omentum.<br>A. Gastrophrenic: fundus of stomach to diaphragm.<br>B. Gastrosplenic: greater curvature to the hilum of the spleen.<br>C. Gastrointestinal: not a real ligament.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 20 (Greater omentum)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 21 (Parts of greater omentum)"],"src":[{"t":"? TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":56,"f":"F1156"}]},{"id":"F3902-22-1","s":"Which ligaments make up the greater omentum?","o":["A. Gastrocolic ligament, gastrosplenic ligament, gastrophrenic ligament","B. Hepatogastric ligament, hepatoduodenal ligament, hepatorenal ligament","C. Splenorenal ligament, gastrosplenic ligament, hepatorenal ligament","D. Falciform ligament, ovarian ligament, round ligament of uterus"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Gastrocolic ligament, gastrosplenic ligament, gastrophrenic ligament","a":"A","why":"<b>Gastrocolic, gastrosplenic and gastrophrenic ligaments.</b> All three start on the greater curvature of the stomach, where the greater omentum attaches.<br>B. Hepatogastric and hepatoduodenal make the lesser omentum; the hepatorenal ligament joins liver to right kidney.<br>C. Splenorenal (spleen to left kidney) and hepatorenal are not parts of the greater omentum.<br>D. Falciform is a liver ligament; the ovarian ligament and round ligament of the uterus are pelvic remnants of the gubernaculum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 20 (Greater omentum)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 21 (Parts of greater omentum)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":22,"f":"F3902"}],"t":1},{"id":"F1252-65-1","s":"As a third year medical student, you take the history of woman with jaundice. Your attending physician takes this opportunity to ask you about the abdominal “ligaments.” Which ligament forms the apron-like part of the greater omentum?","o":["A. Hepatogastric ligament","B. Hepatoduodenal ligament","C. Gastrophrenic ligament","D. Gastrosplenic ligament","E. Gastrocolic ligament"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Gastrocolic ligament","a":"E","why":"<b>Gastrocolic ligament.</b> It runs from the greater curvature to the transverse colon and hangs down as the apron in front of the small intestine.<br>A. Hepatogastric: liver to lesser curvature (lesser omentum).<br>B. Hepatoduodenal: liver to first part of duodenum; holds the portal triad.<br>C. Gastrophrenic: fundus to diaphragm (upper part of the greater omentum).<br>D. Gastrosplenic: greater curvature to spleen (left part of the greater omentum).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 20 (Greater omentum)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 21 (Parts of greater omentum)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":65,"f":"F1252"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G3","u":65,"label":"Pancreatic tail lies in splenorenal ligament","slides":[{"src":"sl/G004-43.jpg","cap":"Lesser sac opened: tail of pancreas lies in the splenorenal ligament","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 43"},{"src":"sl/G007-11.jpg","cap":"Pancreatic tail passes between splenorenal ligament layers with the splenic vessels","where":"2026 lecture: Pancreas & Spleen, slide 11"}],"q":[{"id":"F3903-304-1","s":"Due to his fluctuating levels of insulin, a 62 year old male undergoes a series of tests. A tumor is detected in the tail of the pancreas. The tumor’s definite borders would make it easy to excise. However, before the surgeon can reach the tumor, which of the following ligaments needs to be dissected?","o":["A. Splenorenal","B. Gastrosplenic","C. Gastrocolic","D. Hepatorenal","E. Hepatogastric"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Splenorenal","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Splenorenal.</b> The pancreatic tail runs between the two layers of the splenorenal ligament, with the splenic vessels, to the splenic hilum. To reach a tail tumor, that ligament must be opened.<br>B. Gastrosplenic: joins the spleen to the stomach's greater curvature.<br>C. Gastrocolic: greater omentum from stomach to transverse colon.<br>D. Hepatorenal: joins the liver to the right kidney.<br>E. Hepatogastric: lesser omentum from liver to lesser curvature.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Pancreas & Spleen slide 11 (Pancreas)","Pancreas & Spleen slide 23 (Spleen)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":304,"f":"F3903"},{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":106,"f":"F0008"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G3","u":66,"label":"Lesser omentum: hepatogastric plus hepatoduodenal ligaments","slides":[{"src":"sl/G004-22.jpg","cap":"Lesser omentum parts circled: hepatogastric and hepatoduodenal ligaments","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 22"},{"src":"sl/G006-16.jpg","cap":"Lesser omentum: hepatogastric and hepatoduodenal parts, portal triad in free edge","where":"2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary & Portal Vein, slide 16"},{"src":"sl/G004-49.jpg","cap":"Table: hepatogastric to lesser curvature, hepatoduodenal to first duodenum","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 49"}],"q":[{"id":"F1252-63-1","s":"As a third year medical student, you take the history of woman with jaundice. Your attending physician takes this opportunity to ask you about the abdominal “ligaments.” What two ligaments are continuous parts of the lesser omentum?","o":["A. Hepatogastric and hepatoduodenal ligaments","B. Gastrophrenic and gastrosplenic ligaments","C. Gastrosplenic and gastrocolic ligaments","D. Gastrophrenic and gastrocolic ligaments","E. Hepatogastric and gastrophrenic ligaments"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Hepatogastric and hepatoduodenal ligaments","a":"A","why":"<b>Hepatogastric and hepatoduodenal.</b> Both start on the liver; they are the two continuous parts of the lesser omentum.<br>B, C and D. Gastrophrenic, gastrosplenic and gastrocolic all leave the greater curvature, so they are parts of the greater omentum.<br>E. Gastrophrenic belongs to the greater omentum, not the lesser.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 22 (Parts of lesser omentum)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":63,"f":"F1252"}]},{"id":"D041-163-1","s":"The hepatogastric ligament is:","o":["A. Part of the lesser omentum connecting lesser curvature of the stomach to the liver","B. Part of the greater omentum connecting stomach to transverse colon","C. Part of the lesser omentum connecting liver to duodenum","D. A ligament connecting the liver to the diaphragm","E. The structure containing the portal triad"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Part of the lesser omentum connecting lesser curvature of the stomach to the liver","a":"A","why":"<b>Part of the lesser omentum, from the lesser curvature of the stomach to the liver.</b> It is the thin, membranous part of the lesser omentum.<br>B. Stomach to transverse colon: gastrocolic ligament (greater omentum).<br>C. Liver to duodenum: hepatoduodenal ligament.<br>D. Liver to diaphragm: coronary and triangular ligaments.<br>E. The portal triad (proper hepatic artery, portal vein, bile duct) lies in the hepatoduodenal ligament.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 22 (Parts of lesser omentum)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 19 (Omenta: Greater and Lesser)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":163,"f":"D041"}],"t":1},{"id":"D042-118-1","s":"A third-year student is on surgery rotation and is observing their first cholecystectomy. The surgeon courses through the abdominal wall, entering the lesser omentum to visualize the gallbladder and the surrounding vasculature. Which of the following ligaments are contained within the lesser omentum? (multiple answers may be selected)","o":["A. Hepatoduodenal","B. Splenorenal","C. Hepatogastric","D. Gastrocolic","E. Falciform"],"g":["A","C"],"k":["A","C"],"rv":false,"at":"Hepatoduodenal and Hepatogastric","a":"A,C","why":"<b>Hepatoduodenal and hepatogastric ligaments.</b> Together they make the lesser omentum, which joins the liver to the lesser curvature (hepatogastric) and to the first part of the duodenum (hepatoduodenal).<br>B. Splenorenal: spleen to left kidney; carries splenic vessels and pancreatic tail.<br>D. Gastrocolic: part of the greater omentum.<br>E. Falciform: liver to anterior abdominal wall.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 22 (Parts of lesser omentum)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 19 (Omenta: Greater and Lesser)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI module TA review","sl":118,"f":"D042"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G3","u":67,"label":"Portal triad runs in the hepatoduodenal ligament","slides":[{"src":"sl/G004-22.jpg","cap":"Hepatoduodenal ligament circled, labelled as containing the portal triad","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 22"},{"src":"sl/G006-16.jpg","cap":"Hepatoduodenal ligament (enclosing portal triad) vs hepatogastric ligament","where":"2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary & Portal Vein, slide 16"}],"q":[{"id":"F1180-333-1","s":"The hepatogastric ligament encloses the portal triad","o":["a. True","b. False"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"False, hepatoduodenal ligament","a":"B","why":"<b>False.</b> The portal triad (proper hepatic artery, portal vein, bile duct) runs in the <b>hepatoduodenal</b> ligament, the thick free edge of the lesser omentum. The hepatogastric ligament is the thin part to the lesser curvature and carries no triad.<br>Common mix-up: both are parts of the lesser omentum. Remember: duodenal is the edge with the triad.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 44 (Epiploic or Omental (Winslow’s) Foramen)","Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 16 (Review of lesser omentum)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":333,"f":"F1180"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3900-100-1","s":"The lesser omentum connects the lesser curvature of the stomach to the liver. It also contains ligaments within it. Which of these ligaments contains the portal triad?","o":["A. Falciform ligament","B. Lesser omentum","C. Hepatogastric ligament","D. Hepatoduodenal ligament"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Hepatoduodenal ligament","a":"D","why":"<b>Hepatoduodenal ligament.</b> It is the thick free edge of the lesser omentum and carries the portal triad (proper hepatic artery, portal vein, bile duct).<br>A. Falciform: liver to anterior abdominal wall.<br>B. Lesser omentum: the whole sheet; the question asks which of its ligaments.<br>C. Hepatogastric: the thin part to the lesser curvature; no triad.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 22 (Parts of lesser omentum)","Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 16 (Review of lesser omentum)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":100,"f":"F3900"}],"t":1},{"id":"F1252-61-1","s":"As a third year medical student, you take the history of woman with jaundice. Your attending physician takes this opportunity to ask you about the abdominal “ligaments.” The ligament that conducts the portal triad connects which two organs?","o":["A. The liver and the stomach","B. The liver and the duodenum","C. The stomach and the diaphragm","D. The stomach and the spleen","E. The stomach and the transverse colon"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"The liver and the duodenum","a":"B","why":"<b>The liver and the duodenum.</b> The portal triad runs in the hepatoduodenal ligament, which joins the liver to the first part of the duodenum.<br>A. Liver and stomach: hepatogastric ligament.<br>C. Stomach and diaphragm: gastrophrenic ligament.<br>D. Stomach and spleen: gastrosplenic ligament.<br>E. Stomach and transverse colon: gastrocolic ligament.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 22 (Parts of lesser omentum)","Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 16 (Review of lesser omentum)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":61,"f":"F1252"}]},{"id":"F3902-158-1","s":"During a cholecystectomy procedure (gallbladder removal), the surgical resident must use two fingers to clamp the free edge of the lesser omentum. What portion of the lesser omentum was clamped?","o":["A. Lienorenal ligament","B. Hepatogastric ligament","C. Hepatoduodenal ligament","D. Greater omentum","E. Gastroduodenal ligament"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Hepatoduodenal ligament","a":"C","why":"<b>Hepatoduodenal ligament.</b> It is the thick free edge of the lesser omentum, holding the portal triad. Pinching it between two fingers (Pringle maneuver) controls bleeding.<br>A. Lienorenal (splenorenal): spleen to left kidney, far to the left.<br>B. Hepatogastric: the thin part of the lesser omentum, not its free edge.<br>D. Greater omentum: hangs from the greater curvature.<br>E. Gastroduodenal ligament: not the free edge of the lesser omentum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 44 (Epiploic or Omental (Winslow’s) Foramen)","Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 16 (Review of lesser omentum)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":158,"f":"F3902"}]},{"id":"D045-194-1","s":"you are on your third year surgery rotation and the surgeon points to a structure under the liver that consists of 3 vessels. He calls is the Portal Triad, What is the name of the structure they are contained within?","o":["A. Falciform Ligament","B. Round Ligament of the Liver","C. Hepatoduodenal Ligament","D. Gastrohepatic Ligament"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Hepatoduodenal Ligament","a":"C","why":"<b>Hepatoduodenal ligament.</b> It is the thick free right edge of the lesser omentum, from the liver to the first part of the duodenum, and it carries the portal triad.<br>A. Falciform: liver to anterior abdominal wall; carries the round ligament.<br>B. Round ligament of the liver: the closed-off umbilical vein in the falciform ligament's free edge.<br>D. Gastrohepatic (hepatogastric): the thin left part of the lesser omentum; no triad.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 22 (Parts of lesser omentum)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 44 (Epiploic or Omental (Winslow’s) Foramen)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":194,"f":"D045"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G3","u":68,"label":"Portal triad: hepatic artery, portal vein, bile duct","slides":[{"src":"sl/G006-16.jpg","cap":"Portal triad labelled: proper hepatic artery, portal vein, bile duct","where":"2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary & Portal Vein, slide 16"},{"src":"sl/G004-46.jpg","cap":"Axial section: portal triad in hepatoduodenal ligament, in front of omental foramen","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 46"}],"q":[{"id":"D045-196-1","s":"After impressing the surgeon getting that question correct he tells you he will give you his job if you can name the three vessels in the Portal Triad","o":["A. Hepatic Artery, Hepatic Portal Vein, Bile Duct","B. Gastric Artery, Cystic Duct, Cystic Artery","C. Common Hepatic Artery, Celiac Trunk, Splenic Artery","D. My eyes hurt just looking at that word salad so tell me the answer mr TA"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Hepatic Artery, Hepatic Portal Vein, Bile Duct","a":"A","why":"<b>Hepatic artery (proper), hepatic portal vein and bile duct.</b> These three run together in the hepatoduodenal ligament.<br>B. The cystic duct and cystic artery serve the gallbladder, and no gastric artery runs in the triad.<br>C. Common hepatic artery, celiac trunk and splenic artery are the celiac trunk and its branches, lying behind and below the ligament.<br>D. Joke option.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 22 (Parts of lesser omentum)","Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 33 (Biliary ducts)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":196,"f":"D045"}],"t":1},{"id":"D041-164-1","s":"BONUS: What is the portal triad?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Bile duct, proper hepatic artery, portal vein","a":null,"why":"<b>Proper hepatic artery, hepatic portal vein and common bile duct.</b> They run together in the free right edge of the lesser omentum (the hepatoduodenal ligament), in front of the epiploic foramen. The bile duct is on the right, the artery on the left, and the portal vein behind both.<br>Common mix-up: the hepatic veins are not part of the triad. They drain the liver straight into the IVC (inferior vena cava).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 22 (Parts of lesser omentum)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 44 (Epiploic or Omental (Winslow’s) Foramen)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":164,"f":"D041"}]},{"id":"F1252-83-1","s":"While studying for the practical portion of your Anatomy exam, you decide to review some written material as well by relating the vessels indicated to the ligament that conducts them. Which statement is correct?","o":["A. The gastrophrenic, gastrosplenic, gastrocolic ligaments combine to form the lesser omentum and contain the hepatic artery, hepatic vein, and bile duct","B. The gastrophrenic, gastrosplenic, gastrocolic ligaments combine to form the lesser omentum and contain the hepatic artery, portal vein, and bile duct","C. The hepatoduodenal and hepatogastric ligaments form the lesser omentum and contain the hepatic artery, hepatic vein, and bile duct","D. The hepatoduodenal and hepatogastric ligaments form the lesser omentum and contain the hepatic artery, portal vein, and bile duct","E. The hepatoduodenal and hepatogastric ligaments form the lesser omentum and contain the hepatic artery, IVC, and bile duct"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"The hepatoduodenal and hepatogastric ligaments form the lesser omentum and contain the hepatic artery, portal vein, and bile duct","a":"D","why":"<b>Hepatoduodenal and hepatogastric ligaments form the lesser omentum and carry the hepatic artery, portal vein and bile duct.</b><br>A and B. Gastrophrenic, gastrosplenic and gastrocolic form the greater omentum.<br>C. The hepatic veins are not in the triad; they drain into the IVC (inferior vena cava) at the back of the liver.<br>E. The IVC lies behind the epiploic foramen, not inside the ligament.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Axial cadaver section at the level of the omental bursa; white arrows on the portal-triad structures in the free edge of the lesser omentum","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 22 (Parts of lesser omentum)","Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 16 (Review of lesser omentum)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":83,"f":"F1252"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3903-326-1","s":"A 47-year-old Asian man is diagnosed with carcinoma of the stomach. During a gastric resection, the surgeon wants to remove the lesser omentum because of tumor extension into it. Which of the following structures lie in the free edge of the lesser omentum and consequently must be dissected out in order to be preserved?","o":["A. Common bile duct, cystic duct, and hepatic artery","B. Cystic duct, hepatic artery, and hepatic vein","C. Hepatic vein and cystic duct","D. Portal vein, common bile duct, and hepatic artery","E. Portal vein, hepatic artery, and hepatic vein"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Portal vein, common bile duct, and hepatic artery","a":"D","why":"<b>Portal vein, common bile duct and hepatic artery.</b> This is the portal triad in the free edge of the lesser omentum (the hepatoduodenal ligament).<br>A. Leaves out the portal vein, the largest structure there. The cystic duct belongs to the gallbladder and is not part of the triad.<br>B, C and E. The hepatic veins leave the back of the liver into the IVC (inferior vena cava); they are never in the lesser omentum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 44 (Epiploic or Omental (Winslow’s) Foramen)","Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 16 (Review of lesser omentum)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":326,"f":"F3903"},{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":96,"f":"F0008"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3112-29-1","s":"What structures make up the hepatic portal triad? What ligament is the portal triad located in? What opening is found immediately inferior to this ligament? This opening allows for communication between what two spaces?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Proper hepatic artery, common bile duct, hepatic portal vein; the lesser omentum, specifically the hepatoduodenal ligament; the epiploic (omental) foramen; the greater and lesser sacs.","a":null,"why":"<b>Triad:</b> proper hepatic artery, common bile duct, hepatic portal vein. <b>Ligament:</b> hepatoduodenal, the free edge of the lesser omentum. <b>Opening:</b> the epiploic (omental) foramen. <b>It joins</b> the greater sac to the lesser sac (omental bursa).<br>Common mix-up: the foramen sits directly behind the free edge of the ligament (the ligament is its front wall), with the caudate lobe above and the first part of the duodenum below.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 22 (Parts of lesser omentum)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 44 (Epiploic or Omental (Winslow’s) Foramen)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"Peritoneum & celiac trunk interactive (JL)","sl":29,"f":"F3112"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G3","u":69,"label":"Free-edge vessels: proper hepatic artery, portal vein","slides":[{"src":"sl/G006-16.jpg","cap":"Proper hepatic artery and portal vein inside the hepatoduodenal ligament","where":"2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary & Portal Vein, slide 16"},{"src":"sl/G007-11.jpg","cap":"Portal triad in right free margin of lesser omentum, above the pancreas","where":"2026 lecture: Pancreas & Spleen, slide 11"}],"q":[{"id":"F3902-160-1","s":"The free edge of the lesser omentum houses which arterial supply to the gallbladder and liver?","o":["A. Left gastric","B. Splenic","C. Vagus","D. Proper hepatic","E. SMA."],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Proper hepatic","a":"D","why":"<b>Proper hepatic artery.</b> It runs in the free edge (hepatoduodenal ligament) and splits into right and left hepatic arteries; the right hepatic gives the cystic artery to the gallbladder.<br>A. Left gastric: runs along the lesser curvature.<br>B. Splenic: goes left along the pancreas to the spleen.<br>C. Vagus: a nerve, not an artery.<br>E. SMA (superior mesenteric artery): supplies the midgut; not in the lesser omentum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 44 (Epiploic or Omental (Winslow’s) Foramen)","Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 16 (Review of lesser omentum)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":160,"f":"F3902"}]},{"id":"D042-120-1","s":"Follow-up: Which of the following vessels are contained within the hepatoduodenal ligament and must be considered during this procedure?","o":["A. Splenic a.","B. Right gastric a.","C. Proper hepatic a.","D. Gastroduodenal a.","E. Right gastroepiploic a."],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Proper hepatic a.","a":"C","why":"<b>Proper hepatic artery.</b> It runs in the hepatoduodenal ligament with the portal vein and common bile duct (the portal triad), so it is at risk in gallbladder surgery.<br>A. Splenic artery: runs left along the upper border of the pancreas.<br>B. Right gastric: usually branches off the proper hepatic artery but runs to the lesser curvature; it is not a triad structure.<br>D. Gastroduodenal: leaves the common hepatic artery and goes down behind the first part of the duodenum.<br>E. Right gastroepiploic: runs along the greater curvature in the greater omentum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 22 (Parts of lesser omentum)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 44 (Epiploic or Omental (Winslow’s) Foramen)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI module TA review","sl":120,"f":"D042"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3903-308-1","s":"A liver is finally available for a 47-year-old patient on the transplant list. During the transplant procedure, the resident compresses the hepatoduodenal ligament to control the bleeding in the surgical field. Which artery is the resident compressing?","o":["A. Gastroduodenal","B. Common hepatic","C. Right gastric","D. Proper hepatic","E. Celiac"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Proper hepatic","a":"D","why":"<b>Proper hepatic artery.</b> It is the artery in the hepatoduodenal ligament, beside the portal vein and bile duct. Squeezing the ligament (Pringle maneuver) stops its flow.<br>A. Gastroduodenal: leaves the common hepatic and runs behind the first part of the duodenum.<br>B. Common hepatic: runs along the top of the pancreas; it becomes the proper hepatic only after giving off the gastroduodenal.<br>C. Right gastric: runs along the lesser curvature.<br>E. Celiac trunk: leaves the aorta at the twelfth thoracic vertebra, behind the lesser sac.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 44 (Epiploic or Omental (Winslow’s) Foramen)","Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 16 (Review of lesser omentum)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":308,"f":"F3903"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3112-28-1","s":"A liver is finally available for a 47-year-old patient on the transplant list. During the transplant procedure, the resident compresses the hepatoduodenal ligament to control the bleeding in the surgical field. Which vein is the resident compressing?","o":["A. Gastroduodenal","B. Hepatic","C. Right gastric","D. Proper hepatic","E. Hepatic portal"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Hepatic portal","a":"E","why":"<b>Hepatic portal vein.</b> It is the only large vein in the hepatoduodenal ligament and lies behind the artery and bile duct. Squeezing this ligament (the Pringle maneuver) stops blood flowing into the liver.<br>A. Gastroduodenal: an artery behind the duodenum.<br>B. Hepatic veins: leave the back of the liver straight into the IVC (inferior vena cava), outside the ligament.<br>C. Right gastric: small vessels along the lesser curvature.<br>D. Proper hepatic: an artery, not a vein.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 22 (Parts of lesser omentum)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 44 (Epiploic or Omental (Winslow’s) Foramen)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"Peritoneum & celiac trunk interactive (JL)","sl":28,"f":"F3112"}],"t":1},{"id":"F1243-94-1","s":"During surgery to remove the gallbladder (cholecystectomy), a surgery resident damages the cystic artery before a clamp is properly placed. The attending physician applies pressure to the free edge of the hepatoduodenal ligament of the lesser omentum to control the bleeding until the damaged artery is clamped and ligated. Which of the following arteries was compressed by the attending physician?","o":["A. Celiac","B. Splenic","C. Gastroduodenal","D. Common Hepatic","E. Proper hepatic"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Proper hepatic","a":"E","why":"<b>Proper hepatic artery.</b> The cystic artery usually comes from the right hepatic artery, a branch of the proper hepatic. Squeezing the free edge of the hepatoduodenal ligament (Pringle maneuver) compresses the proper hepatic artery and portal vein and slows the bleeding.<br>A. Celiac trunk: leaves the aorta behind the lesser sac.<br>B. Splenic: goes left to the spleen.<br>C. Gastroduodenal: goes down behind the duodenum, below the ligament.<br>D. Common hepatic: runs along the pancreas before the ligament.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 44 (Epiploic or Omental (Winslow’s) Foramen)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 22 (Parts of lesser omentum)"],"src":[{"t":"? tutor review","n":"old Exam 2: abdomen part only","sl":94,"f":"F1243"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G3","u":70,"label":"Lesser sac: behind stomach, opens via epiploic foramen","slides":[{"src":"sl/G004-46.jpg","cap":"Axial section: omental bursa behind stomach, entered through omental foramen","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 46"},{"src":"sl/G004-47.jpg","cap":"Sagittal sections: omental bursa between stomach and pancreas","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 47"},{"src":"sl/G004-40.jpg","cap":"Lesser sac closed except for its epiploic foramen opening","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 40"}],"q":[{"id":"F1228-132-1","s":"which of the following connects greater peritoneal cavity to lesser peritoneal cavity?","o":["A. Hepatoduodenal ligament","B. Epiploic foramen","C. Intersigmoid recess","D. Mesoappendix","E. Morrison’s pouch"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Epiploic foramen","a":"B","why":"<b>Epiploic (omental) foramen.</b> It is the only opening between the greater sac and the lesser sac (omental bursa), lying behind the hepatoduodenal ligament.<br>A. Hepatoduodenal ligament: forms the front wall of the foramen but is not the opening.<br>C. Intersigmoid recess: a pocket behind the sigmoid mesocolon.<br>D. Mesoappendix: the mesentery of the appendix.<br>E. Morison pouch: a recess of the greater sac next to the foramen, not the connection itself.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 44 (Epiploic or Omental (Winslow’s) Foramen)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 45 (Is bounded superiorly by peritoneum on the caudate lobe of the liver, inferiorly)"],"src":[{"t":"2019 review","n":"old Exam 2 review: abdomen part only","sl":132,"f":"F1228"}]},{"id":"D041-150-1","s":"Which statement best describes the lesser sac (omental bursa) and its relationship to the greater sac?","o":["A. It lies anterior to the stomach and communicates freely with the greater sac","B. It lies posterior to the stomach and lesser omentum and communicates with the greater sac only through the epiploic foramen","C. It is part of the infracolic compartment and opens into both paracolic gutters","D. It is a space between the transverse colon and jejunum with no communication to the greater sac","E. It lies between the right kidney and liver and contains the inferior and paracolic recesses"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"It lies posterior to the stomach and lesser omentum and communicates with the greater sac only through the epiploic foramen","a":"B","why":"<b>It lies behind the stomach and lesser omentum and opens into the greater sac only through the epiploic foramen.</b><br>A. It is behind the stomach, not in front, and it is closed except for the foramen.<br>C. The infracolic compartment and paracolic gutters belong to the greater sac.<br>D. The space between transverse colon and jejunum is greater sac, and the lesser sac does communicate.<br>E. Between the right kidney and liver is Morison pouch (the hepatorenal recess), part of the greater sac.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 40 (Lesser Sac (Omental Bursa))","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 33 (Lesser Sac (aka Omental Bursa))","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 44 (Epiploic or Omental (Winslow’s) Foramen)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":150,"f":"D041"}],"t":1},{"id":"F1252-42-1","s":"The anterior abdominal wall of a 21-year-old woman impacts the steering wheel during a head-on car collision. The blunt trauma resulted in a lacerated pancreas with digestive enzyme spilling anterior. Which of the following locations would initially receive the contents of the lacerated pancreas?","o":["A. Supracolic part of greater peritoneal sac","B. Infracolic part of greater peritoneal sac","C. Subhepatic space","D. Omental bursa","E. Subphrenic space"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Omental bursa","a":"D","why":"<b>Omental bursa.</b> The lesser sac lies directly behind the stomach and in front of the pancreas. Fluid leaking from the front of the pancreas enters it first; this is how a pancreatic pseudocyst forms.<br>A. Supracolic greater sac: reached only later, through the epiploic foramen.<br>B. Infracolic greater sac: below the transverse mesocolon, away from the front of the pancreas.<br>C. Subhepatic space (Morison pouch): fluid gets there only after leaving the bursa.<br>E. Subphrenic space: above the liver, far away.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 40 (Lesser Sac (Omental Bursa))","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 64 (Fluid in omental bursa)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":42,"f":"F1252"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G3","u":70,"label":"Fluid in the lesser sac (omental bursa)","slides":[{"src":"sl/G004-46.jpg","cap":"Cross-section: omental bursa (dark blue) behind stomach, entered through omental foramen","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 46"},{"src":"sl/G004-64.jpg","cap":"Posterior gastric perforation or pancreatic fluid passes into the omental bursa","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 64"},{"src":"sl/G004-47.jpg","cap":"Sagittal view: omental bursa behind stomach with superior and inferior recesses","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 47"}],"q":[{"id":"F3903-324-1","s":"The omental bursa:","o":["A) is a completely sealed-off recess of the peritoneal cavity.","B) is located anterior to the stomach.","C) can contain pancreatic fluid following rupture of the pancreas.","D) has medial and lateral recesses.","E) contains the portal vein."],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"can contain pancreatic fluid following rupture of the pancreas","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Can contain pancreatic fluid.</b> The omental bursa (lesser sac) lies behind the stomach and in front of the pancreas. An inflamed or injured pancreas can leak fluid into it, forming a pancreatic pseudocyst.<br>A. Not sealed: it opens into the greater sac through the omental (epiploic) foramen.<br>B. It lies behind the stomach, not in front.<br>D. Its recesses are superior, inferior and splenic, not medial and lateral.<br>E. The portal vein runs in the hepatoduodenal ligament, the front edge of the omental foramen, not inside the bursa.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 64 (Fluid in omental bursa)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 33 (Lesser Sac (aka Omental Bursa))"],"src":[{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":324,"f":"F3903"},{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":94,"f":"F0008"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3914-4-1","s":"A 40 year old male with a past medical history of knee surgery 4 months ago is admitted for a perforating ulcer in the posterior wall of the stomach due to excessive NSAID use. Which recess is most at risk for infection?","o":["A. Right subhepatic space","B. Lesser sac","C. Right subphrenic space","D. Morrison’s pouch"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Lesser sac","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Lesser sac.</b> The lesser sac (omental bursa) lies directly behind the stomach, so a perforated ulcer of the posterior stomach wall spills gastric contents into it.<br>A. Right subhepatic space: another name for the hepatorenal recess; it collects fluid in a supine patient, not a posterior gastric leak.<br>C. Right subphrenic space: between the diaphragm and right liver lobe, far from the back of the stomach.<br>D. Morrison's pouch: the hepatorenal recess again, same as A.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 64 (Fluid in omental bursa)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 40 (Lesser Sac (Omental Bursa))"],"src":[{"t":"2023 Middletown TA weekly","n":"09-28-23 TA session","sl":4,"f":"F3914"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G3","u":70,"label":"Enter lesser sac through the gastrocolic ligament","slides":[{"src":"sl/G004-43.jpg","cap":"Omental bursa exposed after cutting the gastrocolic ligament","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 43"},{"src":"sl/G004-46.jpg","cap":"Axial section: lesser sac behind stomach, gastrosplenic and splenorenal ligaments","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 46"}],"q":[{"id":"F1180-253-1","s":"A 35-year-old man is admitted to the hospital with a small-caliber bullet wound to the left upper quadrant of the abdomen. Radiographic examination reveals profuse intraperitoneal bleeding. An emergency laparotomy is performed, and the source of bleeding appears to be a vessel within the lesser sac. Which of the following ligaments would most likely be transected to gain adequate entry to the lesser sac?","o":["A. Coronary","B. Gastrosplenic","C. Splenorenal","D. Gastrocolic","E. Hepatoduodenal"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Gastrocolic","a":"D","why":"<b>Gastrocolic ligament.</b> It is the apron part of the greater omentum, from the greater curvature to the transverse colon, and it forms the front of the lesser sac. Cutting it opens the sac widely and safely, the usual surgical route.<br>A. Coronary: liver to diaphragm; not a wall of the lesser sac.<br>B. Gastrosplenic: also borders the sac on the left, but it is narrow and carries short gastric vessels, so the opening is small.<br>C. Splenorenal: carries the splenic vessels and pancreatic tail.<br>E. Hepatoduodenal: holds the portal triad; never cut.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 20 (Greater omentum)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 40 (Lesser Sac (Omental Bursa))"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":253,"f":"F1180"},{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":197,"f":"F1178"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G3","u":71,"label":"Epiploic foramen: caudate lobe above, duodenum below","slides":[{"src":"sl/G004-44.jpg","cap":"Epiploic foramen borders listed; finger passed through the foramen","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 44"},{"src":"sl/G004-45.jpg","cap":"Finger in omental foramen: liver above, lesser omentum in front","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 45"}],"q":[{"id":"F1180-274-1","s":"The Epiploic Foramen is bound superiorly by..?","o":["A. Peritoneum covering the IVC","B. The first part of the duodenum","C. The lesser omentum","D. Peritoneum on the caudate lobe of the liver"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Peritoneum on the caudate lobe of the liver","a":"D","why":"<b>Peritoneum on the caudate lobe of the liver.</b> Borders of the foramen: caudate lobe above, first part of duodenum below, hepatoduodenal ligament in front, IVC (inferior vena cava) behind.<br>A. Peritoneum on the IVC: the posterior border.<br>B. First part of the duodenum: the inferior border.<br>C. Lesser omentum (its free edge): the anterior border.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 44 (Epiploic or Omental (Winslow’s) Foramen)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":274,"f":"F1180"}]},{"id":"F3903-322-1","s":"Which is not a boundary of the epiploic (omental) foramen?","o":["A. Aorta","B. Caudate lobe of the liver","C. First part of the duodenum","D. Hepatoduodenal ligament"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Aorta","a":"A","why":"<b>Aorta.</b> The aorta lies to the left, behind the lesser sac; it does not border the foramen. The back wall is peritoneum on the IVC (inferior vena cava).<br>B. Caudate lobe of the liver: superior boundary.<br>C. First part of the duodenum: inferior boundary.<br>D. Hepatoduodenal ligament: anterior boundary (free edge of the lesser omentum).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 44 (Epiploic or Omental (Winslow’s) Foramen)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":322,"f":"F3903"},{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":92,"f":"F0008"}],"t":1},{"id":"D042-108-1","s":"Which of the following is correct?","o":["A. The superior border of the lesser sac (omental bursa) is the quadrate lobe of liver","B. The inferior border of the lesser sac (omental bursa) is the 1st portion of the duodenum","C. The posterior border of the lesser sac (omental bursa) is the Hepatic Portal Vein","D. The anterior border of the lesser sac (omental bursa) is the Gastroduodenal Artery"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"The inferior border of the lesser sac (omental bursa) is the 1st portion of the duodenum","a":"B","why":"<b>Inferior border = first part of the duodenum.</b> These borders really belong to the epiploic foramen, the doorway into the lesser sac: caudate lobe above, first part of duodenum below, hepatoduodenal ligament in front, IVC (inferior vena cava) behind.<br>A. The superior border is the caudate lobe, not the quadrate lobe.<br>C. The posterior border is the IVC; the portal vein is in front, in the hepatoduodenal ligament.<br>D. The anterior border is the hepatoduodenal ligament (free edge of lesser omentum), not the gastroduodenal artery.","ex":"","fl":["The borders being tested (and the TA's corrections: superior = caudate lobe, inferior = 1st part of duodenum, posterior = IVC, anterior = hepatoduodenal ligament) are the boundaries of the epiploic (omental) foramen, the entrance to the lesser sac (G004:44), not of the lesser sac itself. B is right for the foramen."],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 44 (Epiploic or Omental (Winslow’s) Foramen)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI module TA review","sl":108,"f":"D042"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3902-78-1","s":"A 45 year old male is scheduled for a cholecystectomy. During the surgery, the scalpel of the surgical resident slips and punctures a structure that lies superior to the epiploic foramen. What structure did the scalpel puncture?","o":["A. Aorta","B. Portal vein","C. SMV","D. Caudate lobe of the liver","E. IVC"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Caudate lobe of the liver","a":"D","why":"<b>Caudate lobe of the liver.</b> Peritoneum on the caudate lobe forms the roof of the epiploic foramen.<br>A. Aorta: to the left, behind the lesser sac; not a border.<br>B. Portal vein: in front, inside the hepatoduodenal ligament.<br>C. SMV (superior mesenteric vein): far below, behind the neck of the pancreas.<br>E. IVC (inferior vena cava): behind the foramen, not above it.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 44 (Epiploic or Omental (Winslow’s) Foramen)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":78,"f":"F3902"}]},{"id":"F3900-96-1","s":"A patient presented to the ER with acute abdominal pain, nausea, and vomiting. The patient was taken to surgery for an exploratory laparotomy which revealed a small intestinal strangulation in the epiploic foramen. Which part of the small intestines forms the inferior border of the epiploic foramen?","o":["A. First part of duodenum","B. Second part of duodenum","C. Third part of duodenum","D. Transverse colon"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"First part of duodenum","a":"A","why":"<b>First part of the duodenum.</b> Peritoneum on the first (superior) part of the duodenum forms the floor of the epiploic foramen. A loop trapped here cannot be freed by cutting, because every border carries large vessels.<br>B. Second part: runs down beside the head of the pancreas, lower down.<br>C. Third part: crosses lower, in front of the aorta and IVC (inferior vena cava).<br>D. Transverse colon: large intestine, and not a border.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 44 (Epiploic or Omental (Winslow’s) Foramen)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 65 (Intestine in Omental Bursa)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":96,"f":"F3900"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G3","u":72,"label":"Behind the epiploic foramen: inferior vena cava","slides":[{"src":"sl/G004-46.jpg","cap":"Axial section: inferior vena cava directly behind omental foramen, aorta to its left","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 46"},{"src":"sl/G004-44.jpg","cap":"Posterior border of epiploic foramen: peritoneum covering the inferior vena cava","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 44"}],"q":[{"id":"F1243-92-1","s":"A 52-year-old woman presents with severe upper right quadrant abdominal pain. She is jaundiced, obese, and has a history of gallstones. Further examination indicate acute cholecystectomy (removal of gallbladder). During the surgery, the doctor inserts her index finger into the omental (epiploic) foramen to palpate the structures in the hepatoduodenal ligament. Which of the following structures lies immediately posterior to her finger?","o":["A. Bile duct","B. Liver","C. Inferior Vena Cava (IVC)","D. Abdominal aorta","E. Head of pancreas"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Inferior Vena Cava (IVC)","a":"C","why":"<b>IVC (inferior vena cava).</b> A finger in the epiploic foramen has the hepatoduodenal ligament in front of it and peritoneum over the IVC behind it.<br>A. Bile duct: in front of the finger, in the ligament.<br>B. Liver: the caudate lobe is above the finger.<br>D. Abdominal aorta: to the left of the IVC, not directly behind.<br>E. Head of pancreas: below, inside the C-shaped curve of the duodenum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 44 (Epiploic or Omental (Winslow’s) Foramen)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 45 (Is bounded superiorly by peritoneum on the caudate lobe of the liver, inferiorly)"],"src":[{"t":"? tutor review","n":"old Exam 2: abdomen part only","sl":92,"f":"F1243"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3901-7-1","s":"A 61-year-old woman had been scheduled for a cholecystectomy. During the operation the scissors of the surgical resident accidentally entered the tissues immediately posterior to the epiploic (omental) foramen. The surgical field was filled immediately by profuse bleeding. Which of the following vessels was the most likely source of bleeding?","o":["A. Aorta","B. Inferior vena cava","C. Portal vein","D. Right renal artery","E. Superior mesenteric vein"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Inferior vena cava","a":"B","why":"<b>Inferior vena cava.</b> The back wall of the epiploic foramen is peritoneum on the IVC (inferior vena cava), so a blade going just behind the foramen tears it.<br>A. Aorta: to the left of the IVC, not directly behind the foramen.<br>C. Portal vein: in front of the foramen, in the hepatoduodenal ligament.<br>D. Right renal artery: lower, and it passes behind the IVC.<br>E. Superior mesenteric vein: lower, behind the neck of the pancreas.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 44 (Epiploic or Omental (Winslow’s) Foramen)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 45 (Is bounded superiorly by peritoneum on the caudate lobe of the liver, inferiorly)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":7,"f":"F3901"},{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":94,"f":"F3900"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3902-80-1","s":"A 34 year old males was brought in by EMS to the trauma bay s/p stab wound to the stomach. The patient was rushed into surgery immediately and upon examination it was found that the knife had penetrated the epiploic foramen slightly on the right. Which structure posteriorly is most at risk for damage?","o":["A. Aorta","B. IVC","C. Portal triad","D. Renal artery","E. Superior mesenteric vein"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"IVC","a":"B","why":"<b>IVC (inferior vena cava).</b> Peritoneum covering the IVC is the back wall of the epiploic foramen, so a blade going through the foramen hits it.<br>A. Aorta: lies to the left of the IVC, not directly behind the foramen.<br>C. Portal triad: in front of the foramen.<br>D. Renal artery: lower, at about the first lumbar vertebra.<br>E. Superior mesenteric vein: lower, behind the neck of the pancreas.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 44 (Epiploic or Omental (Winslow’s) Foramen)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":80,"f":"F3902"}],"t":1},{"id":"F1156-16-1","s":"A surgeon is performing a procedure in the epigastric region of the abdomen. The patient’s scans came back for common bile duct stenosis and so the surgeon is careful to locate the hepatoduodenal ligament to carefully examine the area. After going just posterior to the ligament through the epiploic foramen of Winslow, the surgeon’s probe nicks something and suddenly the entire surgical field is flooded with blood. What did the surgeon most likely nick?","o":["A. Common Hepatic Artery","B. Inferior Vena Cava","C. Abdominal Artery","D. Celiac Trunk","E. SMA"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Inferior Vena Cava","a":"B","why":"<b>Inferior vena cava.</b> Passing just behind the hepatoduodenal ligament through the epiploic foramen leads onto the IVC (inferior vena cava), the foramen's back wall.<br>A. Common hepatic artery: runs along the top of the pancreas, below the foramen.<br>C. 'Abdominal artery' (the aorta): lies to the left, behind the lesser sac.<br>D. Celiac trunk: leaves the aorta behind the lesser sac.<br>E. SMA (superior mesenteric artery): lower, behind the neck of the pancreas.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 44 (Epiploic or Omental (Winslow’s) Foramen)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 45 (Is bounded superiorly by peritoneum on the caudate lobe of the liver, inferiorly)"],"src":[{"t":"? TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":16,"f":"F1156"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G3","u":73,"label":"Subphrenic recess: between liver and diaphragm","slides":[{"src":"sl/G004-36.jpg","cap":"Subphrenic recess between diaphragm and liver, split by falciform ligament","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 36"},{"src":"sl/G004-34.jpg","cap":"Perihepatic recesses: subphrenic above the liver, hepatorenal below","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 34"}],"q":[{"id":"F1228-134-1","s":"where is the location of subphrenic recess","o":["A. Peritoneal pocket between liver and diaphragm","B. Peritoneal pocket between liver and kidney","C. Between ascending colon liver","D. Between base of lungs and diaphragm","E. Between stomach and liver"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Peritoneal pocket between liver and diaphragm","a":"A","why":"<b>Between the liver and the diaphragm.</b> It is also called the suprahepatic recess, split into right and left by the falciform ligament.<br>B. Liver and kidney: hepatorenal recess (Morison pouch), the subhepatic space.<br>C. Beside the ascending colon: right paracolic gutter.<br>D. Lung base and diaphragm: the pleural costodiaphragmatic recess, not peritoneal.<br>E. Behind the stomach and lesser omentum: the superior recess of the lesser sac.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 36 (Subphrenic (Suprahepatic) Recess)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 34 (Greater Sac)"],"src":[{"t":"2019 review","n":"old Exam 2 review: abdomen part only","sl":134,"f":"F1228"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3902-24-1","s":"A 42-year-old female is found to have an accumulation of fluid in the subphrenic recess when lying supine. Which ligament is found in this space?","o":["A. Round ligament of uterus","B. Suspensory ligament of ovary","C. Falciform ligament","D. Coronary ligaments"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Falciform ligament","a":"C","why":"<b>Falciform ligament.</b> The subphrenic recess lies between the diaphragm and the front and top of the liver. The falciform ligament runs through it and splits it into right and left subphrenic recesses.<br>A. Round ligament of uterus: runs to the deep inguinal ring; pelvic.<br>B. Suspensory ligament of ovary: lateral pelvic wall to ovary.<br>D. Coronary ligaments: form the back edge of the recess at the bare area of the liver; they do not divide it.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 36 (Subphrenic (Suprahepatic) Recess)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":24,"f":"F3902"}],"t":1},{"id":"D045-199-1","s":"While climbing a mountain stickman here falls down due to faulty equipment and hits the side of the cliff hard causing internal bleeding, in the Greater Sac, while upside down where will the blood drain to?","o":["A. Rectovaginal Recess","B. Subphrenic Recess","C. Uterovesical Recess","D. Splenic Recess"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Subphrenic Recess","a":"B","why":"<b>Subphrenic recess.</b> Fluid runs to the lowest point. Upside down, the space between the diaphragm and the liver becomes the lowest part of the greater sac.<br>A. Rectovaginal (rectouterine) recess: the lowest point when upright, not when inverted.<br>C. Uterovesical recess: also a pelvic pocket, low only when upright.<br>D. Splenic recess: part of the lesser sac, not the greater sac.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"slide 199 has a picture; question answerable from text","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 36 (Subphrenic (Suprahepatic) Recess)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 61 (Spread of Pathological Fluids)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":199,"f":"D045"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G3","u":73,"label":"Hepatorenal pouch: lowest point when supine","slides":[{"src":"sl/G004-62.jpg","cap":"Hepatorenal recess is the lowest, most gravity-dependent space when supine","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 62"},{"src":"sl/G004-38.jpg","cap":"Morison pouch links to lesser sac via epiploic foramen and right gutter","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 38"},{"src":"sl/G004-63.jpg","cap":"Lying body: fluid pools in hepatorenal pouch and pelvic pouch","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 63"}],"q":[{"id":"F3903-328-1","s":"The hepatorenal recess (Morison pouch):","o":["A) is part of the omental bursa.","B) is in the subphrenic recess.","C) receives infected fluids draining from the omental bursa in the supine position.","D) is a potential space in the hepatorenal ligament.","E) is extraperitoneal."],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"receives infected fluids draining from the omental bursa in the supine position","a":"C","why":"<b>It receives infected fluid draining out of the omental bursa when supine.</b> Morison pouch (the hepatorenal recess) is the lowest part of the peritoneal cavity when lying down. It connects with the omental bursa through the epiploic foramen, so fluid leaving the bursa lands here.<br>A. It is part of the greater sac, not the omental bursa.<br>B. It is subhepatic (below the liver), not subphrenic (above it).<br>D. It is a recess, not a space inside a ligament.<br>E. It lies inside the peritoneal cavity, so it is not extraperitoneal.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 37 (Subhepatic Recess)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 38 (Subhepatic Recess)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 62 (Spread of Pathological Fluids)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":328,"f":"F3903"},{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":98,"f":"F0008"}],"t":1},{"id":"F1252-43-1","s":"A 50-year-old female with a long history of heartburn (self-treated with various over-the-counter medications) develops severe epigastric pain and is urgently admitted to the hospital. A gastroscopic examination reveals a small, perforated ulceration in the posterior wall of the stomach body. At surgery, with the patient in supine position, 150 ml of blood-tinged, frothy gray liquid is aspirated from the peritoneal cavity. Where in the peritoneal cavity would liquid most likely first collect when the patient is supine?","o":["A. Right subphrenic space","B. Hepatorenal pouch (of Morison)","C. Left paracolic gutter","D. Vesicouterine pouch","E. Rectouterine pouch (of Douglas)"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Hepatorenal pouch (of Morison)","a":"B","why":"<b>Hepatorenal pouch (of Morison).</b> A posterior gastric ulcer leaks first into the omental bursa, which drains through the epiploic foramen into the hepatorenal pouch. That pouch is the lowest point of the peritoneal cavity in a supine patient.<br>A. Right subphrenic space: same quadrant but higher, so fluid does not pool there.<br>C. Left paracolic gutter: not the first stop from the bursa.<br>D and E. Vesicouterine and rectouterine pouches: lowest only when upright.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 37 (Subhepatic Recess)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 62 (Spread of Pathological Fluids)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":43,"f":"F1252"}]},{"id":"F3902-188-1","s":"A 60 year old male with a history of alcoholism is admitted to the hospital with a perforated ulcer on the posterior wall of the stomach. He remains bedridden in the hospital for several days. Where will peritonitis initially develop and where will it spread to as the patient remains bedridden?","o":["A. Right subhepatic → Right hepatorenal space","B. Omental bursa → Right subphrenic space","C. Left subhepatic → Left paracolic","D. Omental bursa → Right hepatorenal space"],"g":["D"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Omental bursa → Right subphrenic space","a":"B","why":"<b>Omental bursa, then right hepatorenal space.</b> The back wall of the stomach faces the omental bursa, so the leak starts there. It exits through the epiploic foramen into the hepatorenal recess (Morison pouch), the lowest point when supine.<br>A. The leak starts in the bursa, not in the subhepatic space.<br>B. The subphrenic space is higher than Morison pouch when supine, so fluid does not settle there.<br>C. The bursa empties to the right through the foramen, not to the left.","ex":"","fl":["Fluid from a posterior gastric perforation collects first in the omental bursa and leaves it through the epiploic foramen straight into the hepatorenal recess (Morison pouch), which is the lowest part of the peritoneal cavity when supine (G004:38, G004:62); the subphrenic recess is not the lowest point. The same deck family keys the Morison-pouch item this way (F3903 slide 328, key C)."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 38 (Subhepatic Recess)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 62 (Spread of Pathological Fluids)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 44 (Epiploic or Omental (Winslow’s) Foramen)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":188,"f":"F3902"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G3","u":74,"label":"Supine fluid pools in hepatorenal recess","slides":[{"src":"sl/G004-62.jpg","cap":"Hepatorenal recess: lowest, gravity-dependent part of the peritoneal cavity when supine","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 62"},{"src":"sl/G004-63.jpg","cap":"Supine side view: hepatorenal pouch; rectovesical/rectouterine pouch down in the pelvis","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 63"},{"src":"sl/F3112-20.jpg","cap":"Hepatorenal recess when supine; rectouterine and rectovesical pouches in female and male pelvis","where":"2025 Middletown clicker, slide 20"}],"q":[{"id":"D045-201-1","s":"Now in the ambulance and no longer upside down where will blood go while lying supine?","o":["A. Hepatorenal pouch","B. Splenic Recess","C. Uterovesical Recess","D. Subphrenic Recess"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Hepatorenal pouch","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Hepatorenal pouch.</b> Lying flat on the back (supine), the hepatorenal recess (Morrison's pouch, between the liver and right kidney) is the lowest point of the peritoneal cavity, so blood pools there.<br>B. Splenic recess: a side pocket of the lesser sac at the spleen, not a low point.<br>C. Uterovesical recess: a shallow pelvic pouch between uterus and bladder; the pelvis is lowest only when upright.<br>D. Subphrenic recess: under the diaphragm, higher than the hepatorenal pouch when supine.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"slide 201 has a picture; question answerable from text","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 62 (Spread of Pathological Fluids)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 37 (Subhepatic Recess)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":201,"f":"D045"}]},{"id":"F3914-7-1","s":"A 55 year old male with a history of alcohol abuse develop ascites. You asked the patient to lay supine to ultrasound his abdomen. Where is the excess fluid most likely to accumulate?","o":["A. Hepatorenal recess","B. Right subphrenic recess","C. Paracolic recess","D. Splenic recess"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Hepatorenal recess","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Hepatorenal recess.</b> Supine, the hepatorenal recess (Morrison's pouch, between liver and right kidney) is the lowest point of the peritoneal cavity, so ultrasound finds ascites (free abdominal fluid) there first.<br>B. Right subphrenic recess: between diaphragm and liver; higher than the hepatorenal recess when lying down.<br>C. Paracolic recess: gutters beside the colon that carry fluid, not the lowest spot.<br>D. Splenic recess: part of the lesser sac at the spleen, not a dependent area.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 62 (Spread of Pathological Fluids)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 54 (Ascites)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 Middletown TA weekly","n":"09-28-23 TA session","sl":7,"f":"F3914"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3112-17-1","s":"A 50-year-old female heavy drinker is rushed to the ER due to a slightly distended abdomen and right upper quadrant diffuse pain. A diagnosis of mild ascites is made. In order to run a diagnostic test, you need to obtain a sample of the gathering fluid. If the patient is laying supine, where would the majority of the fluid be localized?","o":["A. Rectouterine pouch","B. Lesser sac","C. Right paracolic gutter","D. Rectovesical pouch","E. Hepatorenal recess"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Hepatorenal recess","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Hepatorenal recess.</b> Lying supine, the hepatorenal recess (Morrison's pouch, between liver and right kidney) is the lowest part of the peritoneal cavity, so ascites (free fluid in the belly) collects there.<br>A. Rectouterine pouch: lowest point in a standing woman.<br>B. Lesser sac: a closed space behind the stomach, open only through the omental foramen.<br>C. Right paracolic gutter: a channel linking the hepatorenal recess to the pelvis, not the low point.<br>D. Rectovesical pouch: lowest point in a standing man.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 62 (Spread of Pathological Fluids)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 37 (Subhepatic Recess)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"Peritoneum & celiac trunk interactive (JL)","sl":17,"f":"F3112"}],"t":1},{"id":"F1156-92-1","s":"A patient is supine for the duration of an abdominal surgery and post op they find an infection that was caused from a ruptured duodenal ulcer, where would this most likely collect?","o":["A. Rectouterine pouch","B. Right paracolic gutter","C. Rectovesical pouch","D. Hepatorenal recess"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Hepatorenal recess","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Hepatorenal recess.</b> The patient lay supine (flat on the back) through surgery. In that position the hepatorenal recess (Morrison's pouch, between liver and right kidney) is the lowest part of the peritoneal cavity, so the leaked duodenal contents collect there.<br>A. Rectouterine pouch: lowest point in a standing woman.<br>B. Right paracolic gutter: a channel carrying fluid toward the pelvis, not the low point.<br>C. Rectovesical pouch: lowest point in a standing man.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 62 (Spread of Pathological Fluids)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 37 (Subhepatic Recess)"],"src":[{"t":"? TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":92,"f":"F1156"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3112-18-1","s":"Where would infection gather in a patient who is laying supine? Where would infection gather in an ambulant female patient? Where would infection gather in an ambulant male patient? Why do infections gather in these spaces?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Supine: hepatorenal recess. Ambulant female: rectouterine pouch. Ambulant male: rectovesical pouch. They are the lowest (gravity-dependent) parts of the peritoneal cavity for that position.","a":null,"why":"<b>Gravity decides.</b> Lying flat on the back, the lowest point is the hepatorenal recess (between liver and right kidney). Standing or sitting, the lowest point is the pelvis: the rectouterine pouch (between rectum and uterus) in women and the rectovesical pouch (between rectum and bladder) in men.<br>Common mix-up: the right paracolic gutter is only the channel that links these spaces, not where fluid settles.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 62 (Spread of Pathological Fluids)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 37 (Subhepatic Recess)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"Peritoneum & celiac trunk interactive (JL)","sl":18,"f":"F3112"}]},{"id":"F3900-110-1","s":"A patient presents on a stretcher complaining of severe, upper abdominal pain. He has a history of peptic ulcer disease and recently stopped taking his medications. You suspect a perforated duodenal ulcer, and because you crushed your anatomy course in medical school, you tell your attending the fluid that inevitably is leaking into his peritoneal cavity is accumulating where?","o":["A. Hepatorenal recess","B. Rectouterine pouch","C. Rectovesical pouch","D. Right paracolic gutter"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Hepatorenal recess","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Hepatorenal recess.</b> A patient on a stretcher lies flat (supine). In that position the hepatorenal recess (Morrison's pouch, between liver and right kidney) is the lowest part of the peritoneal cavity, and it sits right beside the leaking duodenum.<br>B. Rectouterine pouch: lowest point in a standing woman.<br>C. Rectovesical pouch: lowest point in a standing man.<br>D. Right paracolic gutter: a channel that carries fluid toward the pelvis, not the low point when supine.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 62 (Spread of Pathological Fluids)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 37 (Subhepatic Recess)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":110,"f":"F3900"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":49,"f":"F3901"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G3","u":75,"label":"Upright fluid: rectovesical (male) or rectouterine (female) pouch","slides":[{"src":"sl/G004-63.jpg","cap":"Fluid pools in the pelvic rectovesical/rectouterine pouch and hepatorenal pouch","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 63"},{"src":"sl/G004-14.jpg","cap":"Female sagittal section: uterovesical and rectouterine (rectovaginal) pouches","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 14"}],"q":[{"id":"F3900-112-1","s":"The same patient from the previous question is in his hospital room waiting to discuss his next steps for management of his perforated duodenal ulcer. He is told to stay in bed, but 20 minutes later you find him standing and yelling at the television (Tom Brady threw ANOTHER touchdown pass). The fluid in his abdomen is likely collecting where now?","o":["A. Hepatorenal recess","B. Rectouterine pouch","C. Rectovesical pouch","D. Left paracolic gutter"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Rectovesical pouch","a":"C","why":"<b>Rectovesical pouch.</b> Standing makes the pelvis the lowest part, and in a man the pelvic pocket lies between rectum and bladder.<br>A. Hepatorenal recess: lowest only when lying flat.<br>B. Rectouterine pouch: only in women.<br>D. Left paracolic gutter: a channel fluid flows along, not the lowest point when standing.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 61 (Spread of Pathological Fluids)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 62 (Spread of Pathological Fluids)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":112,"f":"F3900"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":51,"f":"F3901"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3907-31-1","s":"Hepatorenal recess clinically important– is an area where fluid can build up in a patient who is laying. Do you remember which pouch would fill with fluid in an upright patient?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"The rectouterine pouch in females (the rectovesical pouch in males)","a":null,"why":"<b>Rectouterine pouch (of Douglas) in a woman; rectovesical pouch in a man.</b> Upright, the pelvis is the lowest part of the peritoneal cavity, so fluid gathers there. Lying supine, the lowest point is the hepatorenal recess (Morison pouch).<br>Common mix-up: the vesicouterine pouch, in front of the uterus, is shallower. The deepest female pouch is behind the uterus.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 61 (Spread of Pathological Fluids)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 62 (Spread of Pathological Fluids)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA weekly","n":"10-06-2022 TA session (foregut viscera)","sl":31,"f":"F3907"}]},{"id":"F3901-53-1","s":"A patient presents to your family medicine practice with severe upper abdominal pain. She was diagnosed with a bacterial infection (H. Pylori) in the past, but it is unclear if she took any medications for it. She is sitting on the exam table when you evaluate her - where is the lowest point the fluid is likely accumulating in the peritoneal cavity?","o":["A. Between the rectum and the bladder","B. Between the bladder and the uterus","C. Between the lower lobe of the liver and the right kidney","D. Between the rectum and the uterus"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Between the rectum and the uterus","a":"D","why":"<b>Between the rectum and the uterus.</b> She is sitting, so fluid sinks to the lowest pocket, the rectouterine pouch (of Douglas).<br>A. Rectum and bladder: the rectovesical pouch, the male equivalent.<br>B. Bladder and uterus: the vesicouterine pouch, which is shallower.<br>C. Liver and right kidney: the hepatorenal recess, lowest only when lying supine.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 61 (Spread of Pathological Fluids)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 62 (Spread of Pathological Fluids)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":53,"f":"F3901"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3903-296-1","s":"A 50-year-old male heavy drinker is rushed to the ER due to a slightly distended abdomen and right upper quadrant diffuse pain. A diagnosis of mild ascites is made. In order to run a diagnostic test, you need to obtain a sample of the gathering fluid. If the patient is sitting upright, where would the majority of the fluid be localized?","o":["A. Rectouterine pouch","B. Lesser sac","C. Right paracolic gutter","D. Rectovesical pouch","E. Hepatorenal recess"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Rectovesical pouch","a":"D","why":"<b>Rectovesical pouch.</b> When sitting or standing, fluid sinks to the lowest part of the peritoneal cavity, the pelvis. In a man that is the rectovesical pouch, between rectum and bladder.<br>A. Rectouterine pouch: the lowest point in a woman, but this patient is male.<br>B. Lesser sac: closed except for the epiploic foramen; not a low point.<br>C. Right paracolic gutter: a channel fluid flows along, not where it pools upright.<br>E. Hepatorenal recess: lowest only when lying supine.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 61 (Spread of Pathological Fluids)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 62 (Spread of Pathological Fluids)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":296,"f":"F3903"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G3","u":76,"label":"Recurrent laryngeal nerve between esophagus and trachea","slides":[{"src":"sl/G004-75.jpg","cap":"Cervical esophagus relations: recurrent laryngeal nerves ascend between it and trachea","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 75"},{"src":"sl/F1180-111.jpg","cap":"Recurrent laryngeal nerves loop under subclavian and aortic arch, ascend between trachea and esophagus","where":"2022 Harlem TA review, slide 111"}],"q":[{"id":"F1228-136-1","s":"Where is the recurrent nerve in relation to esophagus?","o":["A. Posterior to cervical esophagus, resting upon the vertebral column","B. Lateral to the cervical esophagus","C. Anterior to cervical esophagus, posterior to trachea","D. Between thoracic esophagus and thoracic duct","E. Between cervical esophagus and phrenic nerve"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Anterior to cervical esophagus, posterior to trachea","a":"C","why":"<b>C. In front of the cervical esophagus, behind the trachea.</b> The recurrent laryngeal nerves ascend in the groove between the esophagus and the trachea.<br>A. Behind the esophagus lie the vertebral column and longus colli muscle.<br>B. Beside the esophagus lie the common carotid arteries and thyroid lobes.<br>D. No nerve runs between the thoracic esophagus and the thoracic duct; the duct lies behind the esophagus.<br>E. The phrenic nerve is far lateral, on the anterior scalene muscle.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 75 (Cervical esophagus)"],"src":[{"t":"2019 review","n":"old Exam 2 review: abdomen part only","sl":136,"f":"F1228"}]},{"id":"D045-207-1","s":"You go to a fancy restaurant ad order fish which was not properly deboned causing the bone to pierce your esophagus injuring a nerve causing, hoarseness and a weakened voice. What Nerve was injured.","o":["A. Phrenic Nerve","B. Glossopharyngeal Nerve","C. Long thoracic Nerve","D. Recurrent Laryngeal nerve"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Recurrent Laryngeal nerve","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Recurrent laryngeal nerve.</b> In the neck, the recurrent laryngeal nerves (vagus branches) climb in the groove between the esophagus and trachea. A bone piercing the esophagus can injure one, weakening the larynx muscles and causing hoarseness.<br>A. Phrenic nerve: runs down on the anterior scalene to the diaphragm; injury affects breathing, not voice.<br>B. Glossopharyngeal nerve: serves the pharynx and back-of-tongue taste, not the vocal cords.<br>C. Long thoracic nerve: supplies serratus anterior; injury gives a winged scapula.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 75 (Cervical esophagus)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":207,"f":"D045"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G3","u":76,"label":"Esophagus arteries: inferior thyroid, aortic, left gastric","slides":[{"src":"sl/G004-88.jpg","cap":"Esophagus by thirds: inferior thyroid, aortic branches, left gastric artery","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 88"},{"src":"sl/G004-89.jpg","cap":"Esophageal arteries: inferior thyroid above, aortic in middle, left gastric below","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 89"}],"q":[{"id":"F3903-340-1","s":"Which of the following supplies blood to the lower third of the esophagus?","o":["A. Aortic branches","B. Left gastric artery","C. esophageal artery","D. Right gastric artery","E. Inferior thyroid artery"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Left gastric artery","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Left gastric artery.</b> Its esophageal branches climb through the esophageal hiatus to the lower third of the esophagus.<br>A. Aortic branches: middle third.<br>C. Esophageal arteries straight off the thoracic aorta also serve the middle third; the lower third's branches come from the left gastric.<br>D. Right gastric artery: right part of the lesser curvature.<br>E. Inferior thyroid artery: upper third.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 110 (Left gastric:)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":340,"f":"F3903"},{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":108,"f":"F0008"}],"t":1},{"id":"F1228-140-1","s":"Which artery supplies the abdominal portion of the esophagus?","o":["A. Thyrocervical trunk","B. Descending thoracic aorta","C. Splenic artery","D. Superior mesenteric artery","E. Left gastric"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Left gastric","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Left gastric.</b> The abdominal esophagus gets esophageal branches of the left gastric artery (helped by the left inferior phrenic artery).<br>A. Thyrocervical trunk: its inferior thyroid branch supplies the cervical esophagus.<br>B. Descending thoracic aorta: supplies the thoracic esophagus.<br>C. Splenic artery: spleen, pancreas and fundus.<br>D. Superior mesenteric artery: the midgut, from the lower duodenum onward.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 110 (Left gastric:)"],"src":[{"t":"2019 review","n":"old Exam 2 review: abdomen part only","sl":140,"f":"F1228"}]},{"id":"D045-209-1","s":"You Decide to go back to the same restaurant and again order the fish, which again is not properly cleaned causing a fish bone to pierce the upper esophagus, which lacerates an artery. As Gordon Ramsay yells at the chef you remember what artery was likely pierced which is?","o":["A. Left gastric Artery","B. Branches from the Descending Thoracic Aorta","C. Inferior Thyroid Artery","D. Splenic Artery"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Inferior Thyroid Artery","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Inferior thyroid artery.</b> The esophagus gets blood by thirds: upper (cervical) third from the inferior thyroid artery, middle third from branches of the descending thoracic aorta, lower third from the left gastric artery. A bone in the upper esophagus hits the inferior thyroid branches.<br>A. Left gastric artery: lower third.<br>B. Descending thoracic aorta branches: middle third.<br>D. Splenic artery: spleen, pancreas and fundus; no esophageal branches.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 75 (Cervical esophagus)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 72 (Esophagus)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":209,"f":"D045"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G3","u":77,"label":"Z-line: squamous-to-columnar mucosal junction","slides":[{"src":"sl/G004-86.jpg","cap":"Esophagogastric junction left of T11; Z-line where esophageal mucosa becomes gastric","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 86"},{"src":"sl/G017-7.jpg","cap":"Histology and endoscopy: Z-line between white squamous and tan columnar lining","where":"2026 digestive interactive, slide 7"}],"q":[{"id":"F3914-14-1","s":"Which of the following statements best describes the anatomical significance of the Z-line (gastroesophageal junction) in the esophagus?","o":["A. It marks the transition from the upper esophagus to the lower esophagus.","B. It serves as the attachment site for the diaphragm's muscular fibers.","C. It is the point where the esophagus pierces the diaphragm to enter the abdominal cavity.","D. It houses the primary sensory receptors for taste and temperature.","E. It is the region where the esophageal mucosa is most susceptible to ulcer formation."],"g":["E"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"It is the point where the esophagus pierces the diaphragm to enter the abdominal cavity.","a":"C","why":"<b>E. Most prone to ulcers.</b> The Z-line is the jagged line where the esophagus's stratified squamous lining abruptly meets the stomach's columnar lining, at the esophagogastric junction (left of T11, the eleventh thoracic vertebra). Refluxed acid attacks the squamous lining just above it, so ulcers form here.<br>A. It marks esophagus versus stomach, not upper versus lower esophagus.<br>B. Diaphragm fibers wrap the hiatus; they do not attach to the lining.<br>C. The esophagus pierces the diaphragm higher, at T10 (tenth thoracic vertebra).<br>D. Taste receptors are on the tongue.","ex":"","fl":["Key C is wrong; best answer E. The Z-line is the abrupt mucosal change from esophageal (stratified squamous) to gastric (columnar) mucosa at the esophagogastric junction, which lies left of T11 (G004:86) - below the esophageal hiatus at T10, so it is not where the esophagus pierces the diaphragm. Acid reflux injures the squamous mucosa just above this junction, making it the site of reflux esophagitis/ulceration (and Barrett change)."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 86 (Esophagogastric junction)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 Middletown TA weekly","n":"09-28-23 TA session","sl":14,"f":"F3914"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G3","u":77,"label":"Reflux first injures antrum cardiacum","slides":[{"src":"sl/G004-80.jpg","cap":"Abdominal esophagus: conical end on the stomach's upper opening is the antrum cardiacum","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 80"},{"src":"sl/G004-126.jpg","cap":"Pyrosis (heartburn): gastric fluid regurgitates into the lower esophagus","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 126"}],"q":[{"id":"F1228-138-1","s":"what structure is first affected by GERD?","o":["A. Cricopharyngeus muscle","B. Antrum cardiacum","C. Skeletal muscle of Lower esophageal sphincter","D. Upper esophageal sphincter","E. Pyloric sphincter"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Antrum cardiacum","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Antrum cardiacum.</b> This is the cone-shaped end of the abdominal esophagus that sits on the stomach's upper opening. In GERD (gastroesophageal reflux disease), acid washes up into the lower esophagus, so this lowest segment is burned first.<br>A. Cricopharyngeus: forms the upper sphincter in the neck, far from the acid.<br>C. The esophageal wall here is smooth muscle (the nearby skeletal muscle is the diaphragm's right crus), and reflux injures the lining first, not muscle.<br>D. Upper esophageal sphincter: at the top of the esophagus.<br>E. Pyloric sphincter: the stomach outlet, downstream of the acid.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 80 (Abdominal esophagus)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 126 (Pyrosis)"],"src":[{"t":"2019 review","n":"old Exam 2 review: abdomen part only","sl":138,"f":"F1228"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G3","u":77,"label":"Lower esophageal sphincter: physiological, formed by right crus","slides":[{"src":"sl/G004-87.jpg","cap":"Diaphragm at the esophageal hiatus acts as physiological inferior esophageal sphincter","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 87"},{"src":"sl/D042-116.jpg","cap":"Diaphragm from below: right crus encircles the esophageal hiatus","where":"2025 TA review, slide 116"},{"src":"sl/G004-121.jpg","cap":"Cross-section at T11: esophagogastric junction lies left of the vertebral body","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 121"}],"q":[{"id":"D041-158-1","s":"Which of the following best describes the lower esophageal sphincter (LES)?","o":["A. A true anatomic sphincter located at T8","B. A voluntary skeletal muscle sphincter","C. A physiologic sphincter compressed by the diaphragm at the level of T11","D. A thickened ring of smooth muscle similar to the pyloric sphincter","E. A sphincter located right of the vertebral column"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"A physiologic sphincter compressed by the diaphragm at the level of T11","a":"C","why":"<b>C. A physiologic sphincter squeezed by the diaphragm near T11 (eleventh thoracic vertebra).</b> The LES (lower esophageal sphincter) is not a true thick-muscle ring. Just above the esophagogastric junction (left of T11), diaphragm muscle around the esophageal hiatus clamps the esophagus.<br>A. It is not a true anatomic sphincter, and T8 (eighth thoracic vertebra) is the vena cava opening.<br>B. It is involuntary, not voluntary skeletal muscle.<br>D. A thick smooth-muscle ring describes the pyloric sphincter.<br>E. The junction lies left of the vertebral column.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 87 (Esophagogastric junction)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 86 (Esophagogastric junction)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":158,"f":"D041"}],"t":1},{"id":"D042-114-1","s":"A 52-year-old man presents with chronic gastroesophageal reflux disease. Imaging and endoscopy reveal incompetence of the lower esophageal sphincter. The surgeon reviews the anatomy of the esophageal hiatus prior to fundoplication. Which structure forms the primary anatomical component of the lower esophageal sphincter, and what is its main function?","o":["A. Cricopharyngeus muscle; prevents aspiration during swallowing","B. Right crus of the diaphragm; prevents reflux of gastric contents into the esophagus","C. Left crus of the diaphragm; initiates peristalsis of the esophagus","D. Myenteric plexus of distal esophagus; voluntary initiation of swallowing","E. Median arcuate ligament; directs chyle into the thoracic duct"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Right crus of the diaphragm; prevents reflux of gastric contents into the esophagus","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Right crus of the diaphragm; prevents reflux.</b> The esophageal hiatus (at T10, the tenth thoracic vertebra) is an opening in the right crus. Its fibers cross below the hiatus and pinch the esophagus when the diaphragm contracts, acting as the physiological lower esophageal sphincter.<br>A. Cricopharyngeus: upper esophageal sphincter in the neck.<br>C. The left crus does not form the sphincter or start peristalsis.<br>D. The myenteric plexus is involuntary; swallowing starts voluntarily in the mouth.<br>E. Median arcuate ligament: arches over the aorta; nothing to do with chyle.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 87 (Esophagogastric junction)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 86 (Esophagogastric junction)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI module TA review","sl":114,"f":"D042"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G3","u":78,"label":"Stomach bed includes the splenic artery","slides":[{"src":"sl/G004-104.jpg","cap":"Stomach bed circled: diaphragm, spleen, left kidney, splenic artery, pancreas, mesocolon","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 104"},{"src":"sl/G004-103.jpg","cap":"Stomach bed list from superior to inferior, ending at transverse mesocolon","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 103"}],"q":[{"id":"F1228-142-1","s":"what forms the stomach bed?","o":["A. Right kidney","B. Splenic artery","C. Greater omentum","D. Appendix","E. Splenic flexure"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Splenic artery","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Splenic artery.</b> The stomach bed is the back wall of the lesser sac behind the stomach. Top to bottom: left dome of the diaphragm, spleen, left kidney and suprarenal gland, splenic artery, pancreas, transverse mesocolon.<br>A. It is the left kidney, not the right.<br>C. Greater omentum: hangs from the greater curvature in front of the intestines.<br>D. Appendix: in the right lower quadrant.<br>E. Splenic flexure of the colon: below and to the left; the bed ends at the transverse mesocolon.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 103 (Posterior relations of stomach)"],"src":[{"t":"2019 review","n":"old Exam 2 review: abdomen part only","sl":142,"f":"F1228"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G3","u":78,"label":"Pyloric stenosis: slow emptying dilates stomach","slides":[{"src":"sl/G004-131.jpg","cap":"Hypertrophic pyloric stenosis: thick pyloric muscle, slow emptying, dilated proximal stomach","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 131"},{"src":"sl/G004-96.jpg","cap":"Pyloric part: pyloric antrum, canal and sphincter before the duodenum","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 96"}],"q":[{"id":"D041-160-1","s":"Delayed gastric emptying leads to which structural consequence?","o":["A. Collapse of the pyloric antrum","B. Expansion of the pyloric region due to food accumulation","C. Dilation of the distal duodenum","D. Compression of the fundus","E. Narrowing of the gastric body"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Expansion of the pyloric region due to food accumulation","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Expansion from food piling up.</b> If the pylorus empties too slowly (as in hypertrophic pyloric stenosis, a thickened pyloric muscle), food builds up and the stomach above the narrow outlet stretches. The lecture: slow emptying, dilated proximal stomach.<br>A. The antrum sits above the blockage, so it fills rather than collapses.<br>C. The duodenum lies past the blockage and gets less food.<br>D. The fundus fills too; it is not compressed.<br>E. The body widens rather than narrows.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 131 (Congenital hypertrophic pyloric stenosis)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 130 (Pylorospasm)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":160,"f":"D041"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G3","u":79,"label":"Celiac trunk arises at T12","slides":[{"src":"sl/G004-113.jpg","cap":"Table: celiac trunk leaves aorta at aortic hiatus; splenic supplies pancreas, spleen","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 113"},{"src":"sl/G004-112.jpg","cap":"Celiac angiogram: celiac trunk, splenic, common hepatic, left gastric, gastroduodenal labelled","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 112"},{"src":"sl/D041-182.jpg","cap":"Numbered celiac angiogram: tortuous splenic artery (9) runs to the patient's left","where":"2025 TA review, slide 182"}],"q":[{"id":"F1228-326-2","s":"Bonus: At what level is the Celiac Trunk located?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"T12","a":null,"why":"<b>T12 (the twelfth thoracic vertebra).</b> The celiac trunk leaves the front of the abdominal aorta just after the aorta passes through the aortic hiatus of the diaphragm at T12.<br>Common mix-up: L1 (first lumbar vertebra) is the superior mesenteric artery, T11 (eleventh thoracic) is the esophagogastric junction, and T10 (tenth thoracic) is the esophageal hiatus.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 109 (Blood supply to stomach)","Radiology Abd III slide 9 (Celiac Trunk)"],"src":[{"t":"2019 review","n":"old Exam 2 review: abdomen part only","sl":326,"f":"F1228"},{"t":"? tutor review","n":"old Exam 2: abdomen part only","sl":222,"f":"F1243"}]},{"id":"F3902-119-1","s":"At what level can you find the Celiac Trunk and what organs does it supply?","o":["a) L1; Kidney","b) T11: spleen","c) T12: spleen","d) T10: pancreas"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"T12: spleen","a":"C","why":"<b>C. T12 (twelfth thoracic vertebra); spleen.</b> The celiac trunk leaves the front of the aorta at T12, just as the aorta passes the diaphragm's aortic hiatus. Its splenic branch supplies the spleen.<br>A. L1 (first lumbar vertebra) is the superior mesenteric artery; kidneys get renal arteries.<br>B. T11 (eleventh thoracic) is the esophagogastric junction.<br>D. T10 (tenth thoracic) is the esophageal hiatus; the celiac trunk does feed the pancreas, but not from T10.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 109 (Blood supply to stomach)","Pancreas & Spleen slide 29 (Spleen)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":119,"f":"F3902"}],"t":1},{"id":"F1252-54-1","s":"As a third year medical student, your attending physician shows you the radiograph on the left. He points to the indicated artery and asks you to name the artery it originates from, the vertebral level the originating artery branches from, and an organ it supplies. Which choice is most correct?","o":["A. Celiac, L1, Spleen","B. Superior Mesenteric, T12, Spleen","C. Superior Mesenteric, L1, Spleen","D. Superior Mesenteric, L1, Pancreas","E. Celiac, T12, Pancreas"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Celiac, T12, Pancreas","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Celiac, T12 (twelfth thoracic vertebra), pancreas.</b> The arrow marks the wavy splenic artery heading to the patient's left. It comes from the celiac trunk, which leaves the aorta at T12, and it supplies the pancreas body and tail on its way to the spleen.<br>A. Right artery and organ, but the celiac trunk is at T12, not L1 (first lumbar vertebra).<br>B. Wrong parent: the splenic artery is not a superior mesenteric branch.<br>C. Wrong parent: superior mesenteric artery.<br>D. Wrong parent: superior mesenteric artery.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F1252-54.jpg","in":"Celiac angiogram; white arrow on the tortuous artery running to the patient's left from the celiac trunk (splenic artery)","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 109 (Blood supply to stomach)","Pancreas & Spleen slide 14 (Pancreas)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":54,"f":"F1252"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G3","u":79,"label":"Celiac trunk branches and sub-branches","slides":[{"src":"sl/G004-110.jpg","cap":"Celiac trunk: left gastric, splenic, common hepatic, with their stomach branches","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 110"},{"src":"sl/F3112-12.jpg","cap":"Line diagram of every celiac branch, from left gastric to cystic","where":"2025 Middletown clicker, slide 12"}],"q":[{"id":"F1228-144-1","s":"which of the follow is NOT correctly matched?","o":["A. Right gastric - common hepatic","B. Right gastroepiploic - gastroduodenal","C. Left gastroepiploic - splenic","D. Left gastric - superior mesenteric","E. Short gastric - splenic"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Left gastric - superior mesenteric","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Left gastric – superior mesenteric is wrong.</b> The left gastric artery is a direct branch of the celiac trunk.<br>A. Right gastric from the hepatic artery (common or proper): correct.<br>B. Right gastroepiploic (right gastro-omental) from the gastroduodenal artery: correct.<br>C. Left gastroepiploic from the splenic artery: correct.<br>E. Short gastrics from the splenic artery: correct.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 109 (Blood supply to stomach)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 110 (Left gastric:)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 111 (Gasroduodenal:)"],"src":[{"t":"2019 review","n":"old Exam 2 review: abdomen part only","sl":144,"f":"F1228"}],"t":1},{"id":"F1228-147-1","s":"Which of the following is not a branch off of the celiac trunk (or sub-branch of the branches off of celiac trunk)?","o":["A. Gastroduodenal","B. Superior pancreaticoduodenal","C. Hepatic","D. Cystic","E. Suprarenal"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Suprarenal","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Suprarenal.</b> The suprarenal (adrenal) arteries come from the inferior phrenic arteries, the aorta and the renal arteries, not the celiac trunk.<br>A. Gastroduodenal: branch of the common hepatic artery.<br>B. Superior pancreaticoduodenal: branch of the gastroduodenal artery.<br>C. Hepatic: the common hepatic is a main celiac branch; proper, right and left hepatic follow.<br>D. Cystic: branch of the right hepatic artery.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 109 (Blood supply to stomach)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 110 (Left gastric:)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 111 (Gasroduodenal:)"],"src":[{"t":"2019 review","n":"old Exam 2 review: abdomen part only","sl":147,"f":"F1228"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G3","u":80,"label":"Curvature arcades: gastrics lesser, gastro-omentals greater","slides":[{"src":"sl/G004-110.jpg","cap":"Gastric arteries on lesser curvature, gastro-omental arteries on greater curvature","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 110"},{"src":"sl/F3112-12.jpg","cap":"Line diagram: @ symbols mark the lesser- and greater-curvature anastomoses","where":"2025 Middletown clicker, slide 12"},{"src":"sl/G004-114.jpg","cap":"Table: right gastric joins left gastric; right gastro-omental from gastroduodenal","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 114"}],"q":[{"id":"D041-169-1","s":"Which pair of arteries runs along the lesser and greater curvatures of the stomach?","o":["A. Left and right gastroepiploic (lesser), left and right gastric (greater)","B. Left gastric and splenic (lesser), gastroduodenal (greater)","C. Left and right gastric (lesser), left and right gastroepiploic (greater)","D. Proper hepatic and left gastric (greater), splenic (lesser)","E. Celiac trunk only supplies the stomach"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Left and right gastric (lesser), left and right gastroepiploic (greater)","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Gastrics on the lesser curvature, gastroepiploics on the greater.</b> The left and right gastric arteries meet along the lesser curvature. The left and right gastroepiploic (gastro-omental) arteries meet along the greater curvature.<br>A. Reversed.<br>B. The splenic artery runs behind the stomach; the gastroduodenal runs behind the duodenum.<br>D. The proper hepatic artery goes to the liver.<br>E. Not a curvature pair, and the celiac trunk also feeds liver, spleen, pancreas and duodenum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 110 (Left gastric:)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":169,"f":"D041"}],"t":1},{"id":"D045-211-1","s":"you are stressed for your upcoming exam and begin to binge eat Sour Patch Kids, not only do you get a sugar rush but you also burn a hole along the greater curve of the stomach YAY! What artery is compromised?","o":["A. Splenic Artery","B. Gastro-omental Artery","C. Short Gastric Artery","D. Gastric Artery"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Gastro-omental Artery","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Gastro-omental artery.</b> The left and right gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) arteries run along the greater curvature, so an ulcer there hits them.<br>A. Splenic artery: runs behind the stomach along the pancreas.<br>C. Short gastric arteries: supply the fundus.<br>D. Gastric arteries (left and right): run along the lesser curvature.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 110 (Left gastric:)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 111 (Gasroduodenal:)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":211,"f":"D045"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3112-33-1","s":"During dissection lab, a student accidentally cuts the artery located on the right aspect of the lesser curvature of the stomach. which of the following arteries would keep blood flowing to the lesser curvature if this injury happened in a live patient?","o":["A. Esophageal","B. Right gastroepiploic","C. Gastroduodenal","D. Left gastric","E. Right gastric"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Left gastric","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Left gastric.</b> The artery on the right side of the lesser curvature is the right gastric. It joins the left gastric artery along the lesser curvature, so the left gastric keeps that curvature supplied.<br>A. Esophageal arteries: supply the esophagus.<br>B. Right gastroepiploic (gastro-omental): runs on the greater curvature.<br>C. Gastroduodenal: runs behind the duodenum; its branch feeds the greater curvature.<br>E. Right gastric: the cut artery itself.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 110 (Left gastric:)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 109 (Blood supply to stomach)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"Peritoneum & celiac trunk interactive (JL)","sl":33,"f":"F3112"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3903-312-1","s":"During dissection lab, a student accidentally cuts the artery located on the left aspect of the smaller curvature of the stomach. which of the following arteries would keep blood flowing to the small curvature if this injury happened in a live patient?","o":["A. Esophageal","B. Right gastroepiploic","C. Gastroduodenal","D. Left gastroepiploic","E. Right gastric"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Right gastric","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Right gastric.</b> The artery on the left side of the lesser curvature is the left gastric. It joins the right gastric artery along the lesser curvature, so the right gastric keeps the lesser curvature supplied.<br>A. Esophageal arteries: supply the esophagus.<br>B. Right gastroepiploic: runs on the greater curvature.<br>C. Gastroduodenal: behind the duodenum; gives the right gastroepiploic.<br>D. Left gastroepiploic: on the greater curvature, from the splenic artery.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 110 (Left gastric:)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":312,"f":"F3903"}]},{"id":"D042-122-1","s":"48 year old male presents to the ER with epigastric pain and hemoptysis. Endoscopy reveals a bleeder ulcer on the lesser curvature of the stomach. CT angiogram shows erosion of an artery that arises from a branch from the celiac trunk, runs along the lesser curvature of the stomach, and anastomosis with the left gastric artery. What is the vessel involved and which vein would blood from this region drain into?","o":["A. Right gastro-omental artery; splenic vein","B. Right gastric artery; hepatic portal vein","C. Short gastric artery; right gastro-omental vein","D. Proper hepatic artery; pancreaticoduodenal vein"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Right gastric artery; hepatic portal vein","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Right gastric artery; hepatic portal vein.</b> The right gastric artery comes off the hepatic artery (a celiac branch), runs along the lesser curvature and joins the left gastric artery. The right and left gastric veins drain straight into the hepatic portal vein.<br>A. Right gastro-omental artery: runs on the greater curvature.<br>C. Short gastric artery: supplies the fundus.<br>D. Proper hepatic artery: goes up to the liver, not along the stomach.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 110 (Left gastric:)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 115 (right and left gastric veins drain into the hepatic portal vein; the short gastr)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI module TA review","sl":122,"f":"D042"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3112-34-1","s":"What are the two pairs of anastomoses in regards to the stomach? Where do these anastomoses occur? Where do each of the anastomosing branches stem from?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Left gastric with right gastric (on the lesser curvature); left gastro-omental with right gastro-omental (on the greater curvature). Left gastric from the celiac trunk; right gastric variable (common hepatic, proper hepatic, or at the gastroduodenal origin); left gastro-omental from the splenic; right gastro-omental from the gastroduodenal.","a":null,"why":"<b>Two arcades.</b> Lesser curvature: left gastric (from the celiac trunk) meets right gastric (from the hepatic artery). Greater curvature: left gastro-omental (from the splenic artery) meets right gastro-omental (from the gastroduodenal artery).<br>Common mix-up: gastro-omental and gastroepiploic are the same arteries, and the gastric arteries never run on the greater curvature.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 110 (Left gastric:)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 111 (Gasroduodenal:)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"Peritoneum & celiac trunk interactive (JL)","sl":34,"f":"F3112"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G3","u":81,"label":"Splenic artery branches: short gastrics, left gastro-omental","slides":[{"src":"sl/G004-110.jpg","cap":"Splenic artery gives posterior gastric, short gastrics to fundus, left gastro-omental","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 110"},{"src":"sl/G004-113.jpg","cap":"Table: short gastrics run in gastrosplenic ligament to the fundus","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 113"},{"src":"sl/G004-96.jpg","cap":"Stomach regions: cardia, fundus, body and pyloric part","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 96"}],"q":[{"id":"D041-171-1","s":"Which of the following foregut organs is primarily supplied by a branch of the splenic artery?","o":["A. Pylorus","B. Fundus of the stomach","C. First part of the duodenum","D. Lower esophagus","E. Pancreatic head"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Fundus of the stomach","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Fundus of the stomach.</b> The splenic artery gives the short gastric arteries, which run in the gastrosplenic ligament to the fundus.<br>A. Pylorus: right gastric and right gastro-omental arteries.<br>C. First part of duodenum: gastroduodenal artery and its supraduodenal branch.<br>D. Lower esophagus: left gastric artery.<br>E. Pancreatic head: superior and inferior pancreaticoduodenal arteries; the splenic artery feeds the body and tail.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 110 (Left gastric:)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":171,"f":"D041"}],"t":1},{"id":"F1156-4-1","s":"A 45 year old male with a past history of hypertension complains of left abdominal pain, weakness, dizziness, and cramping. You order an angiogram as you expect abdominal bleeding and you want to locate the source. The results of the angiogram show that the short gastric artery is affected. What directly supplies the short gastric artery?","o":["A. Left gastric artery","B. Splenic artery","C. Left gastro-omental artery","D. Common hepatic artery","E. Right gastric artery"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Splenic artery","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Splenic artery.</b> The four to five short gastric arteries branch off the end of the splenic artery near the spleen and run to the fundus.<br>A. Left gastric artery: celiac branch on the lesser curvature.<br>C. Left gastro-omental artery: a sister branch of the splenic artery on the greater curvature, not the usual parent of the short gastrics.<br>D. Common hepatic artery: gives the gastroduodenal and proper hepatic arteries.<br>E. Right gastric artery: from the hepatic artery, on the lesser curvature.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 110 (Left gastric:)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 109 (Blood supply to stomach)"],"src":[{"t":"? TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":4,"f":"F1156"}]},{"id":"D042-110-1","s":"The tortuous splenic artery courses posterior to the stomach and anterior to the pancreas. On its course to the spleen, it supplies blood via short gastric arteries to which region of the stomach? And what is one of the functions of this region of the stomach?","o":["A. Antrum; gastrin secretion","B. Pylorus; regulates gastric emptying","C. Fundus; gas storage and receptive relaxation","D. Body; mechanical and chemical digestion","E. Cardia; allows passage food from the esophagus to stomach"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Fundus; gas storage and receptive relaxation","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Fundus; gas storage and receptive relaxation.</b> Short gastric arteries from the splenic artery supply the fundus, the dome above the cardia. It holds the swallowed-air bubble and relaxes to take in food.<br>A. The antrum makes gastrin, but its blood comes from right gastric and gastro-omental branches.<br>B. The pylorus controls emptying, but is fed by right gastric and gastroduodenal branches.<br>D. The body is fed by the curvature arcades.<br>E. The cardia is fed by the left gastric artery.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 110 (Left gastric:)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 96 (Stomach)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI module TA review","sl":110,"f":"D042"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3907-35-1","s":"You are in the middle of a surgery to resect a tumor located in the patient’s pancreas, and you accidently cut the artery that supplies blood to the fundus of the stomach. What artery supplies blood to the fundus of the stomach, and what is it typically a branch of?","o":["A. Long gastric artery, branch of superior mesenteric artery","B. Right gastric artery, branch of common hepatic artery","C. Left gastric artery, branch of celiac trunk","D. Gastroduodenal artery, branch of the common hepatic artery","E. Short gastric artery, branch of splenic artery"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Short gastric artery, branch of splenic artery","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Short gastric artery, from the splenic artery.</b> The short gastrics leave the splenic artery near the spleen and run to the fundus. The splenic artery runs along the pancreas, so it is exposed in pancreatic surgery.<br>A. There is no long gastric artery, and the superior mesenteric artery serves the midgut.<br>B. Right gastric artery: right part of the lesser curvature.<br>C. Left gastric artery: lesser curvature and lower esophagus.<br>D. Gastroduodenal artery: pylorus and first part of duodenum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 110 (Left gastric:)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA weekly","n":"10-06-2022 TA session (foregut viscera)","sl":35,"f":"F3907"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":133,"f":"F3901"}]},{"id":"F3903-332-1","s":"A 51 year-old woman is admitted to the hospital with an acutely painful abdomen Radiographic examination reveals penetration of the fundic region of the stomach by an ulcer, resulting in intraabdominal bleeding. Which of the following arteries is the most likely source of the bleeding?","o":["A. Common hepatic artery","B. Inferior phrenic artery","C. Left gastroepiploic artery","D. Short gastric artery","E. Right gastroepiploic artery"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Short gastric artery","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Short gastric artery.</b> Four to five short gastric arteries leave the splenic artery near the spleen and run in the gastrosplenic ligament to the fundus, so a fundic ulcer erodes them.<br>A. Common hepatic artery: runs right, toward the liver.<br>B. Inferior phrenic artery: supplies the underside of the diaphragm.<br>C. Left gastroepiploic artery: left part of the greater curvature, below the fundus.<br>E. Right gastroepiploic artery: right part of the greater curvature near the pylorus.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 110 (Left gastric:)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":332,"f":"F3903"},{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":102,"f":"F0008"},{"t":"? tutor review","n":"old Exam 2: abdomen part only","sl":113,"f":"F1243"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3112-44-1","s":"A surgeon is trying to fix a tear in the fundus of the stomach of a patient caused by an ulcer. He accidentally cuts the splenic artery superior to the body of the pancreas. Blood flow to which of the following arteries will be affected?","o":["A. Left gastroepiploic","B. Proper hepatic artery","C. Cystic artery","D. Right hepatic","E. Common hepatic artery"],"g":["A"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Right hepatic","a":"D","why":"<b>A. Left gastroepiploic.</b> The left gastroepiploic (left gastro-omental) artery is a branch of the splenic artery, so cutting the splenic artery stops its flow. The splenic artery also gives the short gastric and posterior gastric arteries.<br>B. Proper hepatic: from the common hepatic artery.<br>C. Cystic: from the right hepatic artery.<br>D. Right hepatic: from the proper hepatic artery.<br>E. Common hepatic: a sister celiac branch, not downstream of the splenic.","ex":"","fl":["The left gastroepiploic (gastro-omental) artery is a branch of the splenic artery (G004:110, and the source's own slide 35/46 diagram), so cutting the splenic artery along the body of the pancreas cuts off its flow. The right hepatic artery comes from the proper hepatic off the common hepatic artery, not the splenic; B, C, D, E are all common-hepatic territory and unaffected."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 110 (Left gastric:)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"Peritoneum & celiac trunk interactive (JL)","sl":44,"f":"F3112"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G3","u":82,"label":"Gastroduodenal artery branches","slides":[{"src":"sl/G004-111.jpg","cap":"Gastroduodenal branches: supraduodenal, superior pancreaticoduodenal, right gastro-epiploic","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 111"},{"src":"sl/G004-114.jpg","cap":"Table: right gastro-omental and superior pancreaticoduodenal arise from gastroduodenal","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 114"},{"src":"sl/G007-15.jpg","cap":"Pancreas arteries: gastroduodenal with superior and inferior pancreaticoduodenal arcades","where":"2026 lecture: Pancreas & Spleen, slide 15"}],"q":[{"id":"D041-173-1","s":"The proximal duodenum (foregut part) receives blood from:","o":["A. Superior mesenteric artery only","B. Gastroduodenal artery and superior pancreaticoduodenal artery","C. Inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery only","D. Celiac trunk directly via the splenic artery","E. Left gastric artery only"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Gastroduodenal artery and superior pancreaticoduodenal artery","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Gastroduodenal and superior pancreaticoduodenal arteries.</b> The gastroduodenal artery (from the common hepatic) gives the supraduodenal and superior pancreaticoduodenal arteries, which feed the duodenum down to the bile-duct opening. They are two different arteries; the second branches from the first.<br>A. Superior mesenteric artery: feeds only the distal duodenum.<br>C. Inferior pancreaticoduodenal alone: distal duodenum only.<br>D. Splenic artery: no duodenal branch.<br>E. Left gastric artery: lesser curvature and lower esophagus.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 111 (Gasroduodenal:)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":173,"f":"D041"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3903-320-1","s":"A surgeon is trying to fix a tear in the duodenum of a patient caused by an ulcerated gallstone. He accidentally cuts the gastroduodenal artery close to its origin. Blood flow to which of the following arteries will be affected?","o":["A. Proper hepatic artery","B. Cystic artery","C. Right hepatic","D. Right gastroepiploic","E. Common hepatic artery"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Right gastroepiploic","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Right gastroepiploic.</b> The gastroduodenal artery's branches are the supraduodenal, the superior pancreaticoduodenal and the right gastroepiploic (right gastro-omental). Cutting it near its origin stops flow to all three.<br>A. Proper hepatic artery: a sister branch of the common hepatic, upstream.<br>B. Cystic artery: from the right hepatic artery.<br>C. Right hepatic artery: from the proper hepatic artery.<br>E. Common hepatic artery: the parent, upstream of the cut.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 111 (Gasroduodenal:)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":320,"f":"F3903"}],"t":1},{"id":"F1228-146-1","s":"What happens if the gastroduodenal artery is blocked ?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Its branches lose flow: the supraduodenal, the anterior and posterior superior pancreaticoduodenal, and the right gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) arteries. Collaterals partly make up for it: the inferior pancreaticoduodenal arteries from the superior mesenteric artery (pancreatic head and duodenum), the left gastro-omental artery along the greater curvature, and pancreatic branches of the splenic artery.","a":null,"why":"<b>Its three branches lose flow.</b> The gastroduodenal artery gives the supraduodenal, superior pancreaticoduodenal and right gastro-omental arteries. Collaterals soften the loss: the inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery (from the superior mesenteric artery) to the pancreatic head and duodenum, and the left gastro-omental artery along the greater curvature.<br>Common mix-up: the right gastric artery comes from the hepatic artery, not the gastroduodenal.","ex":"","fl":["Source answer lists the right gastric artery as a GDA-dependent branch and omits the right gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery. Correct: the GDA's branches are the supraduodenal, anterior/posterior superior pancreaticoduodenal and right gastro-omental arteries (G004:111); the right gastric usually arises from the proper/common hepatic artery (G004:110).","The source listed the right gastric artery as a gastroduodenal branch and left out the right gastro-omental; the gastroduodenal artery's branches are the supraduodenal, superior pancreaticoduodenal and right gastro-omental arteries."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 111 (Gasroduodenal:)"],"src":[{"t":"2019 review","n":"old Exam 2 review: abdomen part only","sl":146,"f":"F1228"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G3","u":83,"label":"Major duodenal papilla in descending duodenum","slides":[{"src":"sl/G005-16.jpg","cap":"Bile and main pancreatic ducts open at the major duodenal papilla, second part","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 16"},{"src":"sl/G005-9.jpg","cap":"Parts of duodenum: only the start of the superior part is intraperitoneal","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 9"}],"q":[{"id":"D041-154-1","s":"Which statement is true regarding the duodenum?","o":["A. The entire duodenum is intraperitoneal","B. It lies entirely in the left upper quadrant","C. The duodenum’s primary function is mechanical digestion","D. Its epithelium lacks villi","E. The descending portion receives bile and pancreatic enzymes at the major duodenal papilla"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"The descending portion receives bile and pancreatic enzymes at the major duodenal papilla","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Major duodenal papilla in the descending part.</b> The bile duct and main pancreatic duct join as the hepatopancreatic ampulla, which opens on the major duodenal papilla in the back-inner wall of the second (descending) part.<br>A. Only the first two centimeters of the first part are intraperitoneal; the rest is retroperitoneal.<br>B. It curves around the pancreatic head, mostly right of the midline.<br>C. Its main jobs are neutralizing acid, chemical digestion and absorption.<br>D. It does have villi.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 16 (Descending (second) part of duodenum)","Small & Large Intestines slide 15 (Descending (second) part of duodenum)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":154,"f":"D041"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G3","u":83,"label":"Inferior vena cava behind duodenum parts 1–3","slides":[{"src":"sl/G005-12.jpg","cap":"Behind first part: bile duct, portal vein and inferior vena cava circled","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 12"},{"src":"sl/G005-18.jpg","cap":"Second part posterior relations: right kidney, psoas major, inferior vena cava","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 18"},{"src":"sl/G005-23.jpg","cap":"Behind third part: right ureter, inferior vena cava, aorta, right psoas","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 23"}],"q":[{"id":"D041-156-1","s":"Which of the following lies posterior to the superior, descending, and inferior parts of the duodenum?","o":["A. Gastroduodenal Artery","B. Transverse Colon","C. SMA and SMV","D. Head of pancreas","E. IVC"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"IVC","a":"E","why":"<b>E. IVC (inferior vena cava).</b> The inferior vena cava lies behind the first, second and third parts of the duodenum.<br>A. Gastroduodenal artery: behind the first part only.<br>B. Transverse colon: crosses in front of the second part.<br>C. SMA and SMV (superior mesenteric artery and vein): cross in front of the third part.<br>D. Head of pancreas: inside the duodenal C-curve, medial to the second part and above the third.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 13 (Superior (First) part of duodenum)","Small & Large Intestines slide 20 (Posterior relations of second part of duodenum)","Small & Large Intestines slide 23 (Posterior relations of third part of duodenum)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":156,"f":"D041"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G3","u":84,"label":"Posterior gastric ulcer erodes splenic artery","slides":[{"src":"sl/G004-134.jpg","cap":"Perforated posterior gastric ulcer can erode the large splenic artery","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 134"},{"src":"sl/G004-104.jpg","cap":"Stomach bed: splenic artery and vein run behind stomach along pancreas","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 104"}],"q":[{"id":"D045-213-1","s":"While studying for your exam you are drinking so much coffee and energy drinks that you burn of hole along the posterior wall of the stomach what artery is at risk of being melted by that delicious celsius?","o":["A. Gastro-omental Artery","B. Short Gastric Artery","C. Splenic Artery","D. Supraduodenal Artery"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Splenic Artery","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Splenic artery.</b> The large splenic artery runs along the top of the pancreas in the stomach bed, right behind the posterior stomach wall, so a perforating posterior ulcer can erode it.<br>A. Gastro-omental arteries: run along the greater curvature.<br>B. Short gastric arteries: run to the fundus.<br>D. Supraduodenal artery: small branch above the first part of the duodenum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 134 (Gastric ulcer)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 103 (Posterior relations of stomach)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":213,"f":"D045"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3112-23-1","s":"A 37-year-old female is rushed to the ER due to high fever and severe stomach pains. An endoscopy reveals a perforated ulcer in the posterior wall of the body of the stomach which has led to the damage of the artery running immediately posterior. Which of the following arteries has the gastric acid eroded?","o":["A. Gastroduodenal artery","B. Common hepatic artery","C. Splenic artery","D. Left gastric artery","E. Posterior pancreatic artery"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Splenic artery","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Splenic artery.</b> The splenic artery runs along the upper border of the pancreas, directly behind the posterior wall of the stomach body (the stomach bed). A perforating posterior gastric ulcer erodes it.<br>A. Gastroduodenal artery: behind the first part of the duodenum (posterior duodenal ulcers).<br>B. Common hepatic artery: runs right, toward the liver.<br>D. Left gastric artery: along the lesser curvature.<br>E. Posterior (dorsal) pancreatic artery: a small branch behind the pancreas, not against the stomach.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 134 (Gastric ulcer)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 103 (Posterior relations of stomach)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"Peritoneum & celiac trunk interactive (JL)","sl":23,"f":"F3112"},{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":300,"f":"F3903"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G3","u":84,"label":"Posterior duodenal ulcer erodes gastroduodenal artery","slides":[{"src":"sl/G005-87.jpg","cap":"Duodenal ulcers are common on the posterior wall of the first part","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 87"},{"src":"sl/G005-88.jpg","cap":"Stomach lifted: gastroduodenal artery runs behind the first part of duodenum","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 88"},{"src":"sl/G005-11.jpg","cap":"First part's posterior relations: bile duct, gastroduodenal artery, portal vein, vena cava","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 11"}],"q":[{"id":"D042-112-1","s":"A patient undergoes surgical repair of a bleeding posterior duodenal ulcer. During the procedure, the surgeon notes that the ulcer eroded through the posterior wall of the first part (superior portion) of the duodenum, exposing a major artery running directly posterior to it. Which of the following arteries was identified and injury to this region of the upper GI tract would compromise which of the following functions?","o":["A. Supraduodenal a.; mixing of gastric chyme","B. Right gastroepiploic a.; emulsification of dietary fats","C. Short gastric a.’s; secretion of intrinsic factor for B12 absorption","D. Gastroduodenal a.; neutralization of acidic chyme","E. Superior pancreaticoduodenal a.’s; rapid delivery of bile and enzymes"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Gastroduodenal a.; neutralization of acidic chyme","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Gastroduodenal artery; neutralizing acidic chyme.</b> The gastroduodenal artery runs right behind the first part of the duodenum, so a posterior ulcer there erodes it. The first part receives acid chyme from the stomach and neutralizes it.<br>A. Supraduodenal artery: small branch on top of the duodenum; mixing chyme is the stomach's job.<br>B. Right gastroepiploic artery: on the greater curvature; fat emulsification is bile's job.<br>C. Short gastric arteries: supply the fundus.<br>E. Superior pancreaticoduodenal arteries: on the pancreatic head beside the second part.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 87 (Duodenal ulcer)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 111 (Gasroduodenal:)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI module TA review","sl":112,"f":"D042"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G3","u":85,"label":"Gastric veins drain to hepatic portal vein","slides":[{"src":"sl/G004-115.jpg","cap":"Left and right gastric veins enter portal vein; short gastric veins enter splenic vein","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 115"},{"src":"sl/G004-116.jpg","cap":"Netter: gastric, splenic and superior mesenteric veins forming the hepatic portal vein","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 116"}],"q":[{"id":"D041-175-1","s":"The left and right gastric veins drain primarily into:","o":["A. Inferior vena cava","B. Splenic vein","C. Portal vein","D. Superior mesenteric vein","E. Hepatic vein"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Portal vein","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Portal vein.</b> The left and right gastric veins run along the lesser curvature and empty straight into the hepatic portal vein.<br>A. Inferior vena cava: receives the hepatic veins, not stomach veins.<br>B. Splenic vein: receives the short gastric and left gastro-omental veins.<br>D. Superior mesenteric vein: receives the right gastro-omental vein.<br>E. Hepatic veins: drain the liver into the inferior vena cava.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 115 (right and left gastric veins drain into the hepatic portal vein; the short gastr)","Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 49 (Portal vein)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":175,"f":"D041"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G3","u":85,"label":"Stomach lymph drains to celiac nodes, cisterna chyli","slides":[{"src":"sl/G007-18.jpg","cap":"Stomach and pancreas lymph: pyloric, pancreaticosplenic and celiac lymph nodes","where":"2026 lecture: Pancreas & Spleen, slide 18"},{"src":"sl/G004-117.jpg","cap":"Stomach lymph follows arteries to gastric, gastroepiploic, pancreaticosplenic, pyloric nodes","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 117"},{"src":"sl/D041-190.jpg","cap":"Flowchart: stomach nodes drain via celiac nodes and intestinal trunks to cisterna chyli","where":"2025 TA review, slide 190"}],"q":[{"id":"F3903-338-1","s":"A 54-year-old male is admitted to the emergency department with severe upper abdominal pain. Gastroscopy reveals a tumor in the antrum of the stomach. A CT scan is ordered to evaluate lymphatic drainage of the stomach. Which of the following lymph nodes is most likely to be involved in a malignancy of the stomach?","o":["A. Celiac","B. Superior mesenteric","C. Inferior mesenteric","D. Lumbar","E. Hepatic"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Celiac","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Celiac.</b> All stomach lymph, the antrum's included, passes through nodes along the gastric arteries and ends in the celiac nodes around the celiac trunk.<br>B. Superior mesenteric nodes: midgut.<br>C. Inferior mesenteric nodes: hindgut.<br>D. Lumbar nodes: kidneys, gonads, back wall and legs.<br>E. Hepatic nodes: get some pyloric lymph on the way, but all of it then goes to the celiac nodes, so celiac is most likely.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 117 (Lymphatics)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 68 (Objective)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":338,"f":"F3903"}]},{"id":"F1180-267-1","s":"An endoscopic examination is performed on a 49-year-old patient with a preliminary diagnosis of a gastrointestinal cancer, and a tissue sample is sent for histopathologic examination. Biopsy reveals a gastric adenocarcinoma, and a total gastrectomy is performed. Which of the following lymph nodes will most likely first receive metastatic cells?","o":["A. Celiac","B. Splenic","C. Suprapancreatic","D. Right gastric","E. Cisterna chyli"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Celiac","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Celiac.</b> Stomach lymph first passes small nodes along the curvatures, and which ones depends on where the tumor sits. No site is given, and every part of the stomach drains to the celiac nodes, so they are the nodes most likely to receive the cells.<br>B. Splenic nodes: only the fundus and left greater curvature.<br>C. Suprapancreatic nodes: mainly the pancreas, only part of the stomach.<br>D. Right gastric nodes: only the right lesser curvature.<br>E. Cisterna chyli: a lymph sac, not a node; it gets lymph after the celiac nodes.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 117 (Lymphatics)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 68 (Objective)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":267,"f":"F1180"},{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":210,"f":"F1178"}]},{"id":"D042-127-1","s":"A 54-year-old man is diagnosed with an ulcerated mass along the lesser curvature of the stomach. A CT scan shows enlarged lymph nodes surrounding the celiac trunk. Biopsy confirms metastatic spread. Which of the following best describes the normal lymphatic drainage pathway for this region of the stomach?","o":["A. Right gastroepiploic nodes → superior mesenteric nodes → thoracic duct","B. Left gastric nodes → celiac nodes → cisterna chyli → thoracic duct","C. Hepatic nodes → para-aortic nodes → azygos vein","D. Short gastric nodes → superior mesenteric nodes → cisterna chyli","E. Pyloric nodes → bronchomediastinal trunk → right lymphatic duct"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Left gastric nodes → celiac nodes → cisterna chyli → thoracic duct","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Left gastric nodes → celiac nodes → cisterna chyli → thoracic duct.</b> Lymph follows the arteries. The lesser curvature drains along the left gastric vessels to gastric nodes, then to the celiac nodes, cisterna chyli and thoracic duct.<br>A. Stomach lymph does not go to superior mesenteric nodes.<br>C. Lymph does not drain into the azygos vein.<br>D. Short gastric lymph goes to splenic nodes, then celiac nodes.<br>E. The right lymphatic duct drains only the right upper body.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 117 (Lymphatics)","Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 22 (Lymphatic drainage)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI module TA review","sl":127,"f":"D042"}]},{"id":"D041-187-1","s":"A patient with a gastric fundus carcinoma shows no enlarged left gastric nodes, but CT reveals enlarged nodes near the splenic hilum and along the inferior border of the pancreas, with further spread to a large midline lymphatic sac posterior to the right crus of the diaphragm.","o":["A. Pancreaticoduodenal nodes → SMA nodes → cisterna chyli","B. Superior mesenteric nodes → mediastinal nodes → thoracic duct","C. Left gastric nodes → hepatic nodes → thoracic duct","D. Splenic nodes → celiac nodes → cisterna chyli"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Splenic nodes → celiac nodes → cisterna chyli","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Splenic nodes → celiac nodes → cisterna chyli.</b> Fundus lymph follows the short gastric and left gastro-omental vessels to pancreaticosplenic nodes at the splenic hilum and along the pancreas. These drain to the celiac nodes, then the cisterna chyli (the sac behind the right crus that starts the thoracic duct).<br>A. Pancreaticoduodenal nodes: pancreatic head and duodenum.<br>B. Superior mesenteric nodes: midgut.<br>C. Left gastric nodes: lesser curvature, and they were normal.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 117 (Lymphatics)","Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 22 (Lymphatic drainage)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":187,"f":"D041"}],"t":1},{"id":"D041-185-1","s":"A tumor compresses the cisterna chyli. Which of the following patterns is most likely on lymphangiography?","o":["A. Diffuse dilation of lymphatics accompanying the SMA with mild stasis near the aortic root","B. Prominent enlargement of lymph nodes along the celiac axis with evidence of delayed forward flow","C. Isolated dilation of the thoracic duct above the diaphragm with normal abdominal lymphatics","D. Selective enlargement of right gastric lymph nodes with normal splenic and hepatic drainage"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Prominent enlargement of lymph nodes along the celiac axis with evidence of delayed forward flow","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Enlarged celiac nodes with slowed forward flow.</b> Foregut lymph goes to the celiac nodes, then through the intestinal trunk into the cisterna chyli (the lymph sac that starts the thoracic duct). Squeezing the cisterna backs lymph up into the celiac nodes.<br>A. The aortic root is at the heart, far above the cisterna.<br>C. A blocked cisterna sends less lymph up, so the thoracic duct above would not dilate.<br>D. A central block would not enlarge only the right gastric nodes.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 22 (Lymphatic drainage)","Small & Large Intestines slide 52 (Lymphatics)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 117 (Lymphatics)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":185,"f":"D041"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G3","u":86,"label":"Vagus: more peristalsis, relaxed pylorus","slides":[{"src":"sl/G004-118.jpg","cap":"Vagal trunks increase peristalsis, relax pylorus; sympathetics do the opposite","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 118"},{"src":"sl/G004-90.jpg","cap":"Anterior and posterior vagal trunks sending gastric branches over the stomach","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 90"}],"q":[{"id":"D042-129-1","s":"Which of the following is the parasympathetic nerve supply to the stomach ?","o":["A. Phrenic Nerve","B. Vagus Nerve","C. Musculoskeletal nerve","D. Right gastric nerve"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Vagus Nerve","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Vagus nerve.</b> The stomach's parasympathetic supply comes from the anterior and posterior vagal trunks; it increases peristalsis and relaxes the pyloric sphincter.<br>A. Phrenic nerve: motor nerve to the diaphragm.<br>C. There is no musculoskeletal nerve (the musculocutaneous nerve is in the arm).<br>D. There is no right gastric nerve; the right gastric is an artery.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 118 (Nerve supply to stomach)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI module TA review","sl":129,"f":"D042"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3900-92-1","s":"It's the night before an exam and you reach for your bag of Doritos to snack on while you study. Your stomach is happy and is moving the chyme from your stomach into your duodenum. Which of the following nerves innervate the muscles that are contracting in order to move your snack along?","o":["A. Greater splanchnic nerve","B. Celiac plexus","C. Lesser splanchnic nerve","D. Vagal trunk","E. Lumbar splanchnic nerve"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Vagal trunk","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Vagal trunk.</b> The anterior and posterior vagal trunks (parasympathetic) increase stomach peristalsis and relax the pyloric sphincter, pushing chyme into the duodenum.<br>A. Greater splanchnic nerve: sympathetic; slows peristalsis and tightens the pylorus.<br>B. Celiac plexus: a mostly sympathetic network; the drive for peristalsis comes from the vagus itself.<br>C. Lesser splanchnic nerve: sympathetic to midgut and kidneys.<br>E. Lumbar splanchnic nerve: sympathetic to hindgut and pelvis.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 118 (Nerve supply to stomach)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":92,"f":"F3900"}]},{"id":"F1252-97-1","s":"Patient is having intestinal issues and is in distress. Your attending placed the patient on a medication that increased parasympathetic innervation to the stomach. This medication would affect which innervation and provide what result and relief to the patient?","o":["A. Sympathetic trunk - decreases peristalsis and contracts pyloric sphincter","B. Vagus nerve - increases peristalsis and contracts pyloric sphincter","C. Greater splanchnic nerve - increases peristalsis and relaxes pyloric sphincter","D. Vagus nerve - increases peristalsis and relaxes pyloric sphincter"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Vagus nerve - increases peristalsis and relaxes pyloric sphincter","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Vagus nerve; more peristalsis, relaxed pyloric sphincter.</b> The vagus is the stomach's parasympathetic nerve: it speeds stomach movement and opens the outlet so chyme can leave.<br>A. That is the sympathetic effect: slower peristalsis and a contracted pylorus.<br>B. Right nerve, but the vagus relaxes the pylorus rather than contracting it.<br>C. The greater splanchnic nerve is sympathetic, so its effects are the opposite.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 118 (Nerve supply to stomach)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":97,"f":"F1252"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G3","u":86,"label":"Pancreas parasympathetic: posterior vagal trunk","slides":[{"src":"sl/G004-90.jpg","cap":"Anterior and posterior vagal trunks, celiac plexus and ganglia around the stomach","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 90"},{"src":"sl/G007-19.jpg","cap":"Pancreas nerves: parasympathetic from vagus, sympathetic from greater splanchnic nerves","where":"2026 lecture: Pancreas & Spleen, slide 19"},{"src":"sl/D041-196.jpg","cap":"Foregut autonomics: posterior vagal trunk to pancreas; sympathetics synapse in celiac ganglion","where":"2025 TA review, slide 196"}],"q":[{"id":"D041-192-1","s":"A pancreatic transplant patient develops impaired insulin secretion due to damaged autonomic fibers. Which parasympathetic pathway normally stimulates endocrine pancreatic secretion?","o":["A. Preganglionic fibers from the posterior vagal trunk synapsing in the pancreatic plexus","B. Preganglionic fibers from the anterior vagal trunk synapsing in the celiac ganglion","C. Postganglionic fibers from the lesser splanchnic nerves","D. Postganglionic fibers from the hepatic plexus"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Preganglionic fibers from the posterior vagal trunk synapsing in the pancreatic plexus","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Posterior vagal trunk, synapsing in the pancreas.</b> Parasympathetic supply to the pancreas is vagal. Preganglionic fibers from the posterior vagal trunk pass through the celiac plexus without stopping and synapse in small ganglia in and around the pancreas.<br>B. The anterior trunk mainly serves stomach and liver, and vagal fibers do not synapse in the celiac ganglion (a sympathetic relay).<br>C. Lesser splanchnic nerves carry preganglionic sympathetic fibers, which reduce secretion.<br>D. Hepatic plexus: serves the liver and gallbladder.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Pancreas & Spleen slide 19 (Pancreas)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 118 (Nerve supply to stomach)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":192,"f":"D041"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G3","u":87,"label":"Foregut sympathetics and pain: greater splanchnic, celiac ganglion","slides":[{"src":"sl/G004-119.jpg","cap":"Greater thoracic splanchnic nerve synapses in celiac ganglion; fibers reach stomach","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 119"},{"src":"sl/G004-118.jpg","cap":"Stomach sympathetics: T6–T9 via greater splanchnic nerve to celiac plexus","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 118"},{"src":"sl/D041-196.jpg","cap":"Foregut visceral pain returns via greater splanchnic nerves to T6–T9","where":"2025 TA review, slide 196"}],"q":[{"id":"D045-218-1","s":"While studying you forget to eat all day and notice your stomach is rumbling. What Ganglion does the sympathetic innervation of the stomach originate from?","o":["A. Dorsal Root Ganglion","B. Geniculate Ganglion","C. Celiac Ganglion","D. Trigeminal Ganglion"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Celiac Ganglion","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Celiac ganglion.</b> Sympathetic fibers start in cord segments T6 to T9 (sixth to ninth thoracic), travel in the greater splanchnic nerve and synapse in the celiac ganglion; postganglionic fibers then reach the stomach.<br>A. Dorsal root ganglion: sensory cell bodies, not autonomic motor neurons.<br>B. Geniculate ganglion: sensory ganglion of the facial nerve (taste).<br>D. Trigeminal ganglion: sensory ganglion for the face.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 118 (Nerve supply to stomach)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":218,"f":"D045"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3903-336-1","s":"A 41-year-old woman is admitted to the hospital with upper abdominal pain. A gastroscopic examination reveals multiple small ulcerations in the body of the stomach. Which of the following nerves transmits the sensation of pain from this region?","o":["A. Spinal nerves T5 to T12","B. Greater thoracic splanchnic nerves","C. Lesser thoracic splanchnic nerves","D. Lumbar splanchnic nerves","E. Spinal nerves T12 to L2"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Greater thoracic splanchnic nerves","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Greater thoracic splanchnic nerves.</b> Pain from the stomach body (foregut) travels back with sympathetic fibers in the greater splanchnic nerves to cord segments T6 to T9 (sixth to ninth thoracic).<br>A. Spinal nerves T5 to T12 (fifth to twelfth thoracic) carry pain from the body wall, not the stomach.<br>C. Lesser thoracic splanchnic nerves: midgut pain.<br>D. Lumbar splanchnic nerves: hindgut pain.<br>E. Spinal nerves T12 to L2 (twelfth thoracic to second lumbar): lower body wall and groin.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 118 (Nerve supply to stomach)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":336,"f":"F3903"},{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":271,"f":"F1180"},{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":214,"f":"F1178"}]},{"id":"D041-194-1","s":"A patient presents with epigastric pain radiating to the back, worsened by eating. Imaging shows inflammation of the pancreatic head, but no gallstone obstruction. Which autonomic pathway is responsible for transmitting the visceral pain fibers from this region?","o":["A. Vagal sensory fibers traveling to the nucleus solitarius","B. Greater splanchnic nerves carrying visceral afferents to T6–T9","C. Lesser splanchnic nerves carrying visceral afferents to T10–T11","D. Lumbar splanchnic nerves carrying visceral afferents to L1–L2"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Greater splanchnic nerves carrying visceral afferents to T6–T9","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Greater splanchnic nerves to T6–T9 (sixth to ninth thoracic segments).</b> Pain fibers from foregut organs (stomach, duodenum, liver, pancreas) run back with sympathetic fibers in the greater splanchnic nerves to those cord segments. So pancreatitis pain is epigastric and can reach the back.<br>A. Vagal sensory fibers carry reflex and fullness signals, not pain.<br>C. Lesser splanchnic nerves (tenth and eleventh thoracic): midgut pain.<br>D. Lumbar splanchnic nerves (first and second lumbar): hindgut pain.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Pancreas & Spleen slide 19 (Pancreas)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 118 (Nerve supply to stomach)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":194,"f":"D041"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":88,"label":"Mesentery suspends jejunum and ileum","slides":[{"src":"sl/G005-38.jpg","cap":"Mesentery: double peritoneal fold tying jejunum and ileum to back wall","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 38"},{"src":"sl/G004-23.jpg","cap":"Mesentery types: mesentery proper, transverse and sigmoid mesocolon, mesoappendix","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 23"}],"q":[{"id":"D045-277-1","s":"During surgery, the small intestine is seen suspended from the posterior abdominal wall by a double-layered fold of peritoneum. What structure is being observed?","o":["A. Mesoappendix","B. Mesocolon","C. Mesentery","D. Omentum"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Mesentery","a":"C","why":"<b>Mesentery</b> (the mesentery proper). It is the double fold of peritoneum that ties the <b>jejunum and ileum</b> to the posterior abdominal wall and carries their vessels.<br>A. Mesoappendix: the small fold for the appendix only.<br>B. Mesocolon: holds the transverse or sigmoid colon.<br>D. Omentum: a fold hanging from the <b>stomach</b> (greater and lesser omentum).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 23 (3. Mesenteries)","Small & Large Intestines slide 38 (Jejunum and ileum)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":277,"f":"D045"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":88,"label":"Twisted small-bowel loop → jejunum","slides":[{"src":"sl/G005-38.jpg","cap":"Small-intestine parts: duodenum, jejunum (left upper), ileum (right lower)","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 38"},{"src":"sl/G005-100.jpg","cap":"Volvulus: a bowel loop twisting on its mesentery","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 100"}],"q":[{"id":"F3900-190-1","s":"A 30-year-old patient presents to the emergency department with severe abdominal pain, vomiting and signs of intestinal obstruction. A contrast-enhanced abdominal CT scan reveals a twisted loop of the small intestine. Which of the following structures is most likely affected by this intestinal volvulus?","o":["A. Cecum","B. Appendix","C. Jejunum","D. Transverse colon"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Jejunum","a":"C","why":"<b>Jejunum.</b> The CT (computed tomography) shows a twisted loop of <b>small</b> intestine, and the jejunum is the only small-intestine choice. A volvulus is a bowel loop twisting on its own mesentery.<br>A. Cecum: the first part of the <b>large</b> intestine, in the right lower quadrant.<br>B. Appendix: a blind pouch off the cecum, so large bowel.<br>D. Transverse colon: large intestine hanging on the transverse mesocolon.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 6 (Small intestines)","Small & Large Intestines slide 100 (Volvulus)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":190,"f":"F3900"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":89,"label":"Posterior to first duodenal part → bile duct","slides":[{"src":"sl/G005-12.jpg","cap":"Behind first part: bile duct, portal vein, IVC circled; gastroduodenal artery","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 12"},{"src":"sl/G005-33.jpg","cap":"Common bile duct running behind the first part of duodenum","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 33"}],"q":[{"id":"F1156-54-1","s":"Which of the following lies posterior to the first part of the duodenum?","o":["A. Neck of the pancreas","B. Quadrate lobe of the liver","C. Bile duct","D. Cystic duct"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Bile duct","a":"C","why":"<b>Bile duct.</b> Behind the first part of the duodenum lie the <b>bile duct</b>, the gastroduodenal artery, the portal vein and the IVC (inferior vena cava).<br>A. Neck of the pancreas: lies <b>below</b> the first part.<br>B. Quadrate lobe of the liver: lies <b>in front of</b> the first part.<br>D. Cystic duct: lies <b>above</b> it, by the neck of the gallbladder.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 11 (Superior  (First) part of duodenum)"],"src":[{"t":"? TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":54,"f":"F1156"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":89,"label":"Anterior to first duodenal part → gallbladder","slides":[{"src":"sl/G005-9.jpg","cap":"Gallbladder lying in front of the first duodenal part at L1","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 9"},{"src":"sl/G005-10.jpg","cap":"Gallbladder and quadrate lobe resting on the first part of duodenum","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 10"}],"q":[{"id":"F1252-115-1","s":"This part of the duodenum lies anterolateral to the body of L1 vertebrae, what structure is found anterior to this part of the duodenum.","o":["A. Bile Duct","B. Neck of the gallbladder","C. Neck of the pancreas","D. Greater omentum","E. Gallbladder"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Gallbladder","a":"E","why":"<b>Gallbladder.</b> The part lying at the first lumbar vertebra (L1) is the <b>first (superior) part</b> of the duodenum. In front of it lie peritoneum, the <b>gallbladder</b> and the quadrate lobe of the liver.<br>A. Bile duct: runs <b>behind</b> the first part.<br>B. Neck of the gallbladder: lies <b>above</b> the first part.<br>C. Neck of the pancreas: lies <b>below</b> the first part.<br>D. Greater omentum: hangs from <b>below</b> the first part.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 8 (Superior (First part) of duodenum)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":115,"f":"F1252"},{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":108,"f":"F3900"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":25,"f":"F3901"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3903-371-1","s":"Your 64-year-old patient had an ulcer of the anterior wall of the 1st portion of the duodenum that resulted in an erosion into which of the following?","o":["A. Common hepatic artery","B. Portal vein","C. Gall bladder","D. Stomach","E. Common bile duct"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Gall bladder","a":"C","why":"<b>Gallbladder.</b> The gallbladder (with the quadrate lobe of the liver) lies directly <b>in front of the first part</b> of the duodenum, so an anterior-wall ulcer there can erode into it.<br>A. Common hepatic artery: runs <b>above</b> the first part, along the top of the pancreas.<br>B. Portal vein: lies <b>behind</b> the first part.<br>D. Stomach: joins the duodenum at the pylorus; it is not in front of it.<br>E. Common bile duct: lies <b>behind</b> the first part.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 8 (Superior (First part) of duodenum)","Small & Large Intestines slide 13 (Superior (First) part of duodenum)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":371,"f":"F3903"},{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":119,"f":"F0008"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3902-6-1","s":"34 y/o M reports to the ER with a stab wound to the abdomen. Radiological imaging reveals the stab wound has punctured the duodenum, went through the gastroduodenal artery, the portal vein and the IVC. What structure is anterior to the part of the duodenum that was punctured first?","o":["A. Pancreas","B. R. kidney","C. L lobe of liver","D. Gallbladder"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Gallbladder","a":"D","why":"<b>Gallbladder.</b> The gastroduodenal artery, portal vein and IVC (inferior vena cava) are the <b>posterior relations of the first part</b> of the duodenum, so the knife went through the first part. The gallbladder lies <b>in front</b> of that part.<br>A. Pancreas: its neck lies <b>below</b> the first part; its head sits inside the C-loop.<br>B. Right kidney: lies <b>behind the second</b> part.<br>C. Left lobe of liver: lies over the stomach; the <b>quadrate</b> lobe is the one over the first part.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 8 (Superior (First part) of duodenum)","Small & Large Intestines slide 13 (Superior (First) part of duodenum)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":6,"f":"F3902"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":90,"label":"Posterior duodenal ulcer → gastroduodenal artery","slides":[{"src":"sl/G005-87.jpg","cap":"Duodenal ulcers are commonest on the posterior wall of the first part","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 87"},{"src":"sl/G005-33.jpg","cap":"Duodenum reflected: gastroduodenal artery runs right behind the first part","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 33"}],"q":[{"id":"F1180-301-1","s":"You have a patient that presents with vomiting bright red blood. Imaging reveals a posterior duodenal ulcer. Which artery is causing the bloody vomit?","o":["a. Gastroduodenal artery","b. Hepatic artery","c. Superior mesenteric artery","d. Posterior superior pancreaticoduodenal artery"],"g":["A"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Posterior superior pancreaticoduodenal artery","a":"D","why":"<b>Gastroduodenal artery.</b> It runs straight down <b>behind the first part</b> of the duodenum, where most duodenal ulcers sit. A deep posterior ulcer erodes it and the patient vomits bright-red blood.<br>B. Hepatic artery: the common hepatic runs <b>above</b> the first part and gives off the gastroduodenal.<br>C. Superior mesenteric artery: much lower; it crosses the <b>third</b> part.<br>D. Posterior superior pancreaticoduodenal artery: a <b>smaller branch</b> of the gastroduodenal, lower on the back of the pancreatic head. The old deck keyed it, but the gastroduodenal is the classic answer.","ex":"","fl":["The gastroduodenal artery runs directly behind the first part of the duodenum (G005:11), so a bleeding posterior duodenal ulcer classically erodes it; the same deck keys the gastroduodenal for the posterior duodenal-cap ulcer (slides 264-265) and F0008 slide 121 keys it over the pancreaticoduodenal branches.","Duodenal ulcers are most common on the posterior wall of the first part (G005:87), whose posterior relation is the gastroduodenal artery (G005:11); a bleeding posterior duodenal ulcer classically erodes the gastroduodenal artery. The same deck keys the gastroduodenal artery for a posterior duodenal-cap ulcer (slides 207-208)."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 11 (Superior  (First) part of duodenum)","Small & Large Intestines slide 87 (Duodenal ulcer)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":301,"f":"F1180"},{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":240,"f":"F1178"}],"t":1},{"id":"F1243-72-1","s":"A 35-year-old man suffers sharp abdominal pain accompanied by hematemesis (vomiting of blood) for 2 days. He dies without seeking medical help. Autopsy findings reveal a perforated ulcer in the posterior wall of the first part duodenum, which damage the artery in close proximity. Which of the following arteries was most likely ruptured?","o":["A. Short gastric","B. Left gastroomental","C. Gastroduodenal","D. Splenic","E. Left gastric"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Gastroduodenal","a":"C","why":"<b>Gastroduodenal artery.</b> A perforated ulcer in the <b>posterior wall of the first part</b> of the duodenum erodes the artery lying right behind it: the gastroduodenal.<br>A. Short gastric arteries: run from the splenic artery to the gastric fundus.<br>B. Left gastro-omental artery: runs along the greater curvature of the stomach.<br>D. Splenic artery: runs along the top of the pancreas; eroded by <b>posterior gastric</b> ulcers.<br>E. Left gastric artery: runs along the lesser curvature; bleeds from <b>gastric</b> ulcers there.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 11 (Superior  (First) part of duodenum)","Small & Large Intestines slide 87 (Duodenal ulcer)"],"src":[{"t":"? tutor review","n":"old Exam 2: abdomen part only","sl":72,"f":"F1243"}]},{"id":"F1180-264-1","s":"A 32-year-old woman is admitted to the hospital for complaints of upper abdominal pain. A CT scan shows evidence of an ulcer in the posterior wall of the duodenal cap. Which structure will most likely be affected if the ulcer perforates?","o":["A. Hepatic portal vein","B. Superior mesenteric artery","C. Bile duct","D. Proper hepatic artery","E. Gastroduodenal artery"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Gastroduodenal artery","a":"E","why":"<b>Gastroduodenal artery.</b> The duodenal cap is the first part of the duodenum. The gastroduodenal artery runs <b>directly against its back wall</b>, so a perforating posterior ulcer hits it first.<br>A. Hepatic portal vein: also behind the first part, but deeper than the artery.<br>B. Superior mesenteric artery: much lower; it crosses the <b>third</b> part.<br>C. Bile duct: also behind the first part, but off to the right of the artery and rarely the structure hit.<br>D. Proper hepatic artery: runs <b>above</b> the duodenum, in the hepatoduodenal ligament.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 11 (Superior  (First) part of duodenum)","Small & Large Intestines slide 87 (Duodenal ulcer)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":264,"f":"F1180"},{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":207,"f":"F1178"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3901-22-1","s":"A 45 years-old female patient has been experiencing stomach pain that radiates to the medial aspect of their scapula. They also experience heartburn and discomfort. The patient has difficulty digesting their food. On examination their abdominal region feel tender to palpation. Endoscopy examination shows duodenal ulcers in the first part of the duodenum. The patient is in risk of hemorrhage. What artery is at risk?","o":["A. Superior pancreaticoduodenal artery","B. Inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery","C. Gastroduodenal artery","D. Right gastro-omental artery","E. Supraduodenal artery"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Gastroduodenal artery","a":"C","why":"<b>Gastroduodenal artery.</b> Ulcers of the first part of the duodenum sit mostly on its <b>posterior wall</b>, and the gastroduodenal artery runs right behind that wall. Pain spreading to the back fits a posterior ulcer.<br>A. Superior pancreaticoduodenal: a gastroduodenal branch lower down, beside the <b>second</b> part.<br>B. Inferior pancreaticoduodenal: comes from the SMA (superior mesenteric artery), near the <b>third</b> part.<br>D. Right gastro-omental: runs along the greater curvature of the stomach.<br>E. Supraduodenal: a tiny branch on the upper surface; not a source of big bleeds.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 11 (Superior  (First) part of duodenum)","Small & Large Intestines slide 87 (Duodenal ulcer)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":22,"f":"F3901"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":90,"label":"Duodenal ulcer: posterior bleeds GDA, anterior perforates","slides":[{"src":"sl/G005-87.jpg","cap":"Duodenal ulcers: common on posterior wall of the first part","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 87"},{"src":"sl/G005-88.jpg","cap":"Gastroduodenal artery descending behind the first part of the duodenum","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 88"},{"src":"sl/G005-11.jpg","cap":"First part of duodenum, behind: bile duct, gastroduodenal artery, portal vein, inferior vena cava","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 11"}],"q":[{"id":"F0008-121-1","s":"A 42-year-old woman is admitted to the hospital with an acutely painful abdomen and vomiting bright red blood. Radiographic examination reveals penetration of a posterior duodenal ulcer resulting in intraabdominal bleeding. Which of the following arteries is most commonly eroded by this type of ulcer?","o":["A. Gastroduodenal artery","B. Superior mesenteric","C. Posterior inferior pancreaticoduodenal","D. Right gastric","E. Right gastroepiploic"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Gastroduodenal artery","a":"A","why":"<b>Gastroduodenal artery (GDA).</b> Most duodenal ulcers are on the posterior wall of the first part of the duodenum. The GDA runs right behind that wall, so a deep ulcer erodes into it and bleeds.<br>B. Superior mesenteric: lower, behind the neck of the pancreas.<br>C. Posterior inferior pancreaticoduodenal: near the third part of the duodenum.<br>D. Right gastric: along the lesser curvature of the stomach.<br>E. Right gastroepiploic: along the greater curvature.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 87 (Duodenal ulcer)","Small & Large Intestines slide 11 (Superior  (First) part of duodenum)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":121,"f":"F0008"}],"t":1},{"id":"F1180-259-1","s":"A 37-year-old female court stenographer is admitted to the hospital with a complaint of intense pain in her abdomen. Radiologic examination reveals penetration of an anterior duodenal ulcer. Which of the following conditions will most probably occur?","o":["A. Bleeding from gastroduodenal artery","B. Bleeding from superior mesenteric artery","C. Bleeding from posterior superior pancreaticoduodenal artery","D. Bleeding from posterior inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery","E. Peritonitis"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Peritonitis","a":"E","why":"<b>Peritonitis.</b> The front of the first part of the duodenum is covered by peritoneum and faces the peritoneal cavity. An anterior ulcer that perforates spills gut contents there, causing peritonitis.<br>A. Gastroduodenal artery: behind the duodenum; posterior ulcers erode it.<br>B. Superior mesenteric artery: lower, behind the neck of the pancreas.<br>C. Posterior superior pancreaticoduodenal: posterior.<br>D. Posterior inferior pancreaticoduodenal: posterior and lower.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 87 (Duodenal ulcer)","Small & Large Intestines slide 11 (Superior  (First) part of duodenum)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":259,"f":"F1180"},{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":202,"f":"F1178"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":91,"label":"Third duodenal part lies on IVC and aorta","slides":[{"src":"sl/G005-21.jpg","cap":"Third part relations: IVC and aorta behind, SMA and SMV in front","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 21"},{"src":"sl/G005-23.jpg","cap":"Behind the third part: right psoas, IVC, aorta, right ureter","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 23"}],"q":[{"id":"F1180-303-1","s":"Which portion of the duodenum is directly anterior to the vena cava and aorta?","o":["A. Superior","B. Descending","C. Inferior","D. Ascending"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Inferior (third)","a":"C","why":"<b>Inferior (third, horizontal) part.</b> It crosses the midline at the third lumbar vertebra, lying <b>directly on the IVC (inferior vena cava) and the aorta</b>.<br>A. Superior (first) part: higher, at the first lumbar vertebra; it is in front of the IVC but not the aorta.<br>B. Descending (second) part: lies to the <b>right</b> of the IVC, on the right kidney.<br>D. Ascending (fourth) part: lies to the <b>left</b> of the aorta.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 21 (Inferior (third) part)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":303,"f":"F1180"},{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":242,"f":"F1178"},{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":144,"f":"F3900"}],"t":1},{"id":"F1228-153-1","s":"A 68 y/o male with a history of HTN presents with a pulsatile mass in his abdomen. You suspect an abdominal aortic aneurysm. What part of the duodenum is most at risk of being compressed?","o":["A. 1st part","B. 2nd part","C. 3rd part","D. 4th part"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"3rd part","a":"C","why":"<b>Third part.</b> The third (horizontal) part lies <b>directly on the aorta</b> and the IVC (inferior vena cava). A pulsating AAA (abdominal aortic aneurysm) pushes into it from behind.<br>A. First part: higher, at the first lumbar vertebra, in front of the bile duct and portal vein.<br>B. Second part: to the <b>right</b> of the aorta, on the right kidney.<br>D. Fourth part: to the <b>left</b> of the aorta, not on top of it.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 21 (Inferior (third) part)","Small & Large Intestines slide 23 (Posterior relations of third part of duodenum)"],"src":[{"t":"2019 review","n":"old Exam 2 review: abdomen part only","sl":153,"f":"F1228"},{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":10,"f":"F3902"}]},{"id":"F3903-375-1","s":"A 62-year-old man complains of abdominal pain just superior to his umbilicus. On CT examination, a mass is found anterior to the IVC at the L3 or L4 level. Which of the following structures would most likely be compressed by this mass at this level?","o":["A. Third part of the duodenum","B. The portal vein","C. Right sympathetic trunk","D. Left sympathetic trunk","E. Left renal artery"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Third part of the duodenum","a":"A","why":"<b>Third part of the duodenum.</b> At the third lumbar vertebra (L3) the horizontal part crosses <b>in front of the IVC (inferior vena cava)</b> and aorta, so a mass right there presses on it.<br>B. Portal vein: forms higher, behind the neck of the pancreas.<br>C. Right sympathetic trunk: lies on the vertebral bodies <b>behind</b> the IVC.<br>D. Left sympathetic trunk: on the left side of the vertebrae, by the aorta.<br>E. Left renal artery: higher and on the left, behind the left renal vein.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 21 (Inferior (third) part)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":375,"f":"F3903"},{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":111,"f":"F0008"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":91,"label":"Pancreatic cancer → retroperitoneal third part of duodenum","slides":[{"src":"sl/G005-24.jpg","cap":"Pancreatic head sits directly above the third part of duodenum","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 24"},{"src":"sl/G007-7.jpg","cap":"Pancreatic head cradled by the C-shaped retroperitoneal duodenum","where":"2026 lecture: Pancreas & Spleen, slide 7"}],"q":[{"id":"F3114-47-1","s":"Pancreatic cancer is considered one of the deadliest cancers in humans due to the retroperitoneal location of the pancreas. This allows the cancer to spread rapidly without having to cross the peritoneal barrier. Which of the following organs would be at risk in the case of a metastatic pancreatic cancer patient?","o":["A. Stomach","B. 3rd part of the duodenum","C. Jejunum","D. Transverse colon","E. Cecum"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"3rd part of the duodenum","a":"B","why":"<b>Third part of the duodenum.</b> Like the pancreas it is <b>retroperitoneal</b>, and it lies just below the pancreatic head. A tumor can grow straight into it without crossing peritoneum.<br>A. Stomach: intraperitoneal; the lesser sac (omental bursa) separates it from the pancreas.<br>C. Jejunum: intraperitoneal, hanging on the mesentery.<br>D. Transverse colon: intraperitoneal, on the transverse mesocolon.<br>E. Cecum: in the right lower quadrant, far from the pancreas.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Pancreas & Spleen slide 7 (Pancreas)","Small & Large Intestines slide 21 (Inferior (third) part)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"Small & large intestines interactive","sl":47,"f":"F3114"},{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":359,"f":"F3903"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":92,"label":"Jejunum: LUQ, thick wall, long vasa recta","slides":[{"src":"sl/G005-41.jpg","cap":"Table: jejunum vs ileum wall, vasa recta, arcades, fat, folds","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 41"},{"src":"sl/G005-39.jpg","cap":"Jejunum: left upper quadrant, thick wall, circular folds, few arcades","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 39"},{"src":"sl/F3114-33.jpg","cap":"Jejunum: long vasa recta, few arcades; ileum: many arcades, fat","where":"2025 Middletown clicker, slide 33"}],"q":[{"id":"D042-131-1","s":"Which of the following parts of the small intestine is mainly found in the left upper quadrant of the abdomen?","o":["A. Ileum","B. Jejunum","C. Transverse colon","D. Cecum"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Jejunum","a":"B","why":"<b>Jejunum.</b> It is the first two-fifths of the jejunum-ileum and lies mainly in the <b>LUQ (left upper quadrant)</b>.<br>A. Ileum: mainly in the <b>right lower</b> quadrant.<br>C. Transverse colon: large intestine, not small; it crosses the upper abdomen.<br>D. Cecum: large intestine in the right lower quadrant.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 39 (Jejunum)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI module TA review","sl":131,"f":"D042"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3114-31-1","s":"What are the differences between the jejunum and ileum in the following: Plicae circularis; Vasa recta; Arcades; Adipose tissue in mesentery; Absorption","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Plicae circularis: jejunum many (wrinkled), ileum few (almost smooth). Vasa recta: jejunum long, ileum short. Arcades: jejunum one row, ileum 2-3 rows (honeycomb). Mesenteric fat: jejunum less, ileum more. Absorption of nutrients: jejunum more, ileum less.","a":null,"why":"<b>Jejunum: many tall circular folds, long vasa recta, one row of a few large arcades, little mesenteric fat, more absorption. Ileum: few low folds, short vasa recta, two to three rows of many small arcades, more fat, less absorption.</b><br>Common mix-up: the ileum has <b>more</b> arcades but <b>shorter</b> vasa recta; the jejunum has <b>fewer</b> arcades but <b>longer</b> vasa recta.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 39 (Jejunum)","Small & Large Intestines slide 40 (Ileum)","Small & Large Intestines slide 44 (Superior mesenteric artery)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"Small & large intestines interactive","sl":31,"f":"F3114"}]},{"id":"F3914-29-1","s":"What features of the jejunum differentiate it from the ileum?","o":["A. Deeper red color, thin-walled, long vasa recta, less prominent arterial arcades","B. Thin and light wall, long vasa recta, many short arterial arcades, more fat","C. More plicae circularis, long vasa recta, few large arterial arcades, less fat","D. Peyers patches, thick-walled, greater vascularity, more fat"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"More plicae circularis, long vasa recta, few large arterial arcades, less fat","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> The jejunum has <b>more circular folds</b> (plicae circulares), <b>long vasa recta</b>, a <b>few large arterial loops</b> and <b>less fat</b> in its mesentery.<br>A. Wrong because the jejunum is <b>thick</b>-walled, not thin.<br>B. Describes the ileum: thin wall, many short arcades, more fat.<br>D. Peyer patches and more fat are ileum features.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 39 (Jejunum)","Small & Large Intestines slide 40 (Ileum)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 Middletown TA weekly","n":"09-28-23 TA session","sl":29,"f":"F3914"},{"t":"2022 Middletown TA weekly","n":"10-06-2022 TA session (foregut viscera)","sl":9,"f":"F3907"}]},{"id":"D045-273-1","s":"A 28-year-old woman undergoes CT evaluation for chronic abdominal pain. Imaging shows bowel loops in the left upper quadrant that are thick-walled, have prominent circular folds, and fewer arterial arcades. Which bowel segment is most likely affected?","o":["A. Ileum","B. Duodenum","C. Jejunum","D. Cecum"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Jejunum","a":"C","why":"<b>Jejunum.</b> Loops in the LUQ (left upper quadrant) with a <b>thick wall</b>, tall closely packed circular folds and <b>few arterial arcades</b> are jejunum.<br>A. Ileum: thin wall, low folds, many arcades, mostly right lower quadrant.<br>B. Duodenum: a fixed retroperitoneal C-loop around the pancreatic head, not mobile loops.<br>D. Cecum: large intestine in the right lower quadrant, with teniae and haustra instead of circular folds.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 39 (Jejunum)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":273,"f":"D045"}],"t":1},{"id":"D041-200-1","s":"During trauma surgery, a 30 cm segment of bowel with few arterial arcades, long vasa recta, and prominent plicae is found injured near the left side of the umbilicus. Loss of this segment would most likely lead to which deficiency over time?","o":["A. Vitamin B12","B. Bile salts","C. Iron","D. Intrinsic factor"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Iron","a":"C","why":"<b>Iron.</b> Few arcades, long vasa recta and tall folds left of the umbilicus mean <b>jejunum</b>. Iron is absorbed in the <b>duodenum and proximal jejunum</b>, so it is the best answer here.<br>A. Vitamin B12: absorbed in the <b>terminal ileum</b>.<br>B. Bile salts: reabsorbed in the <b>terminal ileum</b>.<br>D. Intrinsic factor: made by parietal cells of the <b>stomach</b>; it travels with B12 to the ileum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 39 (Jejunum)","Small & Large Intestines slide 44 (Superior mesenteric artery)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":200,"f":"D041"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":93,"label":"Ileum: RLQ, many arcades, fat, Peyer patches","slides":[{"src":"sl/G005-41.jpg","cap":"Table: jejunum vs ileum wall, vasa recta, arcades, fat, folds","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 41"},{"src":"sl/G005-40.jpg","cap":"Ileum: right lower quadrant, thin wall, Peyer patches, more arcades","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 40"},{"src":"sl/F3114-33.jpg","cap":"Ileum: short vasa recta, stacked arcades, encroaching fat","where":"2025 Middletown clicker, slide 33"}],"q":[{"id":"F3903-347-1","s":"A 62-year old patient schedules a visit with his physician due to increasing pain in his abdomen and a steady decrease in weight regardless of his diet. An MRI shows an inflamed small intestine. To determine which part of the small intestine is most likely affected, the physician must differentiate between the jejunum and ileum. Which of the following characteristics are typical of the ileum?","o":["(A) Less plicae circulares","(B) Less mesenteric arterial arcades","(C) More absorption nutrients","(D) Longer vasa recta","(E) Less fat in its mesentery"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Less plicae circulares","a":"A","why":"<b>Less plicae circulares (fewer circular folds).</b> The ileum's folds are low and sparse, and gone near its end.<br>B. Fewer arterial arcades: jejunum. The ileum has <b>more</b>.<br>C. More nutrient absorption: jejunum.<br>D. Longer vasa recta: jejunum. The ileum's are <b>short</b>.<br>E. Less fat in the mesentery: jejunum. The ileum has <b>more</b> fat.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 39 (Jejunum)","Small & Large Intestines slide 40 (Ileum)","Small & Large Intestines slide 44 (Superior mesenteric artery)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":347,"f":"F3903"},{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":320,"f":"F1180"},{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"Small & large intestines interactive","sl":30,"f":"F3114"},{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":259,"f":"F1178"}],"t":1},{"id":"D045-275-1","s":"A surgeon identifies thin-walled bowel in the right lower quadrant with numerous arterial arcades, abundant fat, and Peyer’s patches. Which segment is this?","o":["A. Jejunum","B. Ileum","C. Transverse colon","D. Sigmoid colon"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Ileum","a":"B","why":"<b>Ileum.</b> A thin wall, <b>many arterial arcades</b>, lots of mesenteric fat and <b>Peyer patches</b> in the RLQ (right lower quadrant) mean ileum.<br>A. Jejunum: thick wall, few arcades, little fat, left upper quadrant.<br>C. Transverse colon: large intestine with teniae and haustra, across the upper abdomen.<br>D. Sigmoid colon: large intestine in the left lower quadrant.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 40 (Ileum)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":275,"f":"D045"}],"t":1},{"id":"D041-202-1","s":"A patient with chronic ileal inflammation develops recurrent respiratory infections and decreased IgA levels. Which structural feature of the affected region explains this immune dysfunction?","o":["A. Loss of tall villi and dense plicae that normally increase absorption","B. Damage to Peyer patches that sample antigens entering the gut","C. Destruction of Brunner glands protecting the mucosa from acid","D. Loss of long vasa recta reducing mucosal immune perfusion"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Damage to Peyer patches that sample antigens entering the gut","a":"B","why":"<b>Damage to Peyer patches.</b> These lymphoid nodules cluster in the <b>ileum</b>. They sample gut antigens and drive IgA (immunoglobulin A) production, so ileal disease can lower IgA.<br>A. Tall villi and dense folds: jejunum features for absorption, not immunity.<br>C. Brunner glands: in the <b>duodenum</b>; their mucus neutralizes acid.<br>D. Long vasa recta: a jejunum feature that carries blood, not immunity.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 40 (Ileum)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":202,"f":"D041"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":94,"label":"SMA arises from aorta at L1","slides":[{"src":"sl/G005-42.jpg","cap":"SMA arises from the front of the aorta at lower L1","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 42"},{"src":"sl/G012-25.jpg","cap":"Sagittal CT: celiac trunk, then SMA just below, off the aorta","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Abdomen III, slide 25"}],"q":[{"id":"F1252-130-1","s":"At what vertebral body level does the SMA arise off the abdominal aorta?","o":["A. T11","B. T12","C. L1","D. L2","E. L3"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"L1","a":"C","why":"<b>L1 (first lumbar vertebra).</b> The SMA (superior mesenteric artery) leaves the front of the aorta at the <b>lower part of L1</b>, about one centimeter below the celiac trunk.<br>A. Eleventh thoracic: no major gut artery arises here.<br>B. Twelfth thoracic: the <b>celiac trunk</b>.<br>D. Second lumbar: the gonadal arteries.<br>E. Third lumbar: the <b>IMA</b> (inferior mesenteric artery).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 42 (SMA)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":130,"f":"F1252"}],"t":1},{"id":"F1252-56-1","s":"As a fourth year medical student, your attending physician asks you at what level does the artery that supplies the jejunum, ileum, and cecum branch off of the abdominal aorta?","o":["A. T11","B. T12","C. L1","D. L2","E. L3"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"L1","a":"C","why":"<b>L1 (first lumbar vertebra).</b> The artery feeding the jejunum, ileum and cecum is the SMA (superior mesenteric artery). It arises from the front of the aorta at the lower part of L1.<br>A. Eleventh thoracic: no major gut artery.<br>B. Twelfth thoracic: the celiac trunk (foregut).<br>D. Second lumbar: the gonadal arteries.<br>E. Third lumbar: the IMA (inferior mesenteric artery, hindgut).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 42 (SMA)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":56,"f":"F1252"}]},{"id":"F3902-121-1","s":"At what level can you find the SMA and what organ does it supply?","o":["a) T12; pancreas","b) L4: jejunum","c) L1: jejunum","d) L3: pancreas"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"L1: jejunum","a":"C","why":"<b>L1 (first lumbar vertebra): jejunum.</b> The SMA (superior mesenteric artery) arises at L1 and supplies the midgut, including the <b>jejunum</b>.<br>A. Twelfth thoracic, pancreas: that is the <b>celiac trunk</b> level.<br>B. Fourth lumbar: where the aorta splits into the common iliac arteries.<br>D. Third lumbar: the <b>IMA</b> (inferior mesenteric artery), which supplies the hindgut, not the pancreas.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 42 (SMA)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":121,"f":"F3902"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":94,"label":"IMA arises at L3: aneurysm at L3–L4","slides":[{"src":"sl/G005-79.jpg","cap":"Inferior mesenteric artery arises in front of L2–L3","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 79"}],"q":[{"id":"F0003-12-1","s":"A 70 year old man with a history of tobacco use is visiting his primary care physician. During the physical exam, his physician detects a pulsating mass in the lower abdomen. An ultrasound reveals an aortic aneurism at the level of L3-L4. Blood supply to which of the following vessels will be affected by this aneurysm?","o":["A. Celiac trunk","B. SMA","C. Renal artery","D. Gonadal artery","E. IMA"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"IMA","a":"E","why":"<b>IMA (inferior mesenteric artery).</b> The IMA leaves the aorta at about L3 (third lumbar vertebra); the lecture says in front of L2–L3. An aneurysm at L3–L4 involves its origin.<br>A. Celiac trunk: arises at T12 (twelfth thoracic vertebra).<br>B. SMA (superior mesenteric artery): arises at L1 (first lumbar vertebra).<br>C. Renal artery: arises at L1–L2.<br>D. Gonadal artery: arises at about L2.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 79 (Inferior mesenteric artery)"],"src":[{"t":"? Middletown TA review","n":"MT Abdomen Review","sl":12,"f":"F0003"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":95,"label":"SMA supplies midgut: jejunum to two-thirds transverse colon","slides":[{"src":"sl/G001-22.jpg","cap":"Midgut: duodenum past bile duct to two-thirds transverse colon; SMA","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 22"},{"src":"sl/G005-45.jpg","cap":"Table: SMA branches and the gut segment each one supplies","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 45"},{"src":"sl/G005-80.jpg","cap":"SMA and IMA branches with the marginal artery along the colon","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 80"}],"q":[{"id":"D045-292-1","s":"A patient with mesenteric ischemia has compromised blood flow to the jejunum and ileum. Which artery normally supplies these structures?","o":["A. Inferior mesenteric artery","B. Celiac trunk","C. Superior mesenteric artery","D. Internal iliac artery"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Superior mesenteric artery","a":"C","why":"<b>Superior mesenteric artery (SMA).</b> It supplies the <b>midgut</b>, including all of the jejunum and ileum, through fifteen to eighteen jejunal and ileal branches.<br>A. Inferior mesenteric artery: hindgut (distal transverse colon to upper rectum).<br>B. Celiac trunk: foregut (stomach, upper duodenum, liver, pancreas, spleen).<br>D. Internal iliac artery: pelvic organs.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 44 (Superior mesenteric artery)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":292,"f":"D045"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3114-37-1","s":"What does the SMA provide blood to? Name the organs from proximal to distal along with their respective arterial branches. Where is the transition point between the celiac trunk and the SMA? Where is the transition point between the SMA and the IMA?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"SMA supplies: lower pancreatic head and duodenum distal to the major papilla (inferior pancreaticoduodenal), jejunum (jejunal arteries), ileum (ileal arteries; ileocolic for the terminal ileum), cecum (ileocolic), appendix (appendicular), ascending colon (right colic and ileocolic), proximal two-thirds of transverse colon (middle colic). Celiac→SMA transition: in the second (descending) part of the duodenum, just distal to the major duodenal papilla where the bile duct opens. SMA→IMA transition: on the transverse colon, at the junction of its proximal two-thirds and distal one-third. The marginal artery links all parts of the colon.","a":null,"why":"<b>SMA (superior mesenteric artery) = midgut:</b> distal duodenum and lower pancreatic head, jejunum, ileum, cecum, appendix, ascending colon and the proximal <b>two-thirds of the transverse colon</b>. The celiac–SMA border is in the <b>second part of the duodenum, just past the major papilla</b> (bile-duct opening), not between parts one and two. The SMA–IMA (inferior mesenteric artery) border is <b>two-thirds of the way along the transverse colon</b>.<br>Common mix-up: the marginal artery runs along the whole colon and joins SMA and IMA blood.","ex":"","fl":["The source put the celiac–SMA transition between duodenal parts 1 and 2; it actually lies in the second (descending) part, just past the major duodenal papilla where the bile duct opens."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 44 (Superior mesenteric artery)","Small & Large Intestines slide 81 (Marginal Artery)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"Small & large intestines interactive","sl":37,"f":"F3114"}]},{"id":"F3114-36-1","s":"An arteriogram of a 34-year old female reveals a blood clot which is blocking the proximal part of her SMA. She is rushed to the OR. Her physician fears that which of the following portion of her intestines might have become ischemic?","o":["A. Duodenum","B. Jejunum","C. Transverse colon","D. Sigmoid colon","E. Rectum"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Jejunum","a":"B","why":"<b>Jejunum.</b> It gets <b>all</b> its blood from the SMA (superior mesenteric artery) and has no other source, so a proximal SMA clot makes it ischemic.<br>A. Duodenum: only the lower half is SMA-fed, and the celiac also fills it through the pancreaticoduodenal arcade.<br>C. Transverse colon: only the proximal two-thirds is SMA; the marginal artery brings IMA (inferior mesenteric artery) blood.<br>D. Sigmoid colon: IMA territory.<br>E. Rectum: IMA plus internal iliac branches.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 44 (Superior mesenteric artery)","Small & Large Intestines slide 22 (Anteriorly: SMA,SMV, small intestines)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"Small & large intestines interactive","sl":36,"f":"F3114"},{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":351,"f":"F3903"}],"t":1},{"id":"D042-140-1","s":"A 65-year-old man presents with severe postprandial abdominal pain and unintentional weight loss over the past 6 months. He reports that the pain begins about 20–30 minutes after eating and has led to a fear of eating. A CT angiogram reveals significant stenosis of an artery arising from the abdominal aorta at the L1 vertebral level. The artery is noted to pass anterior to the left renal vein and over the third part of the duodenum. Which of the following parts of the gastrointestinal tract is most likely affected by the compromised blood flow?","o":["A. Duodenum distal to the major duodenal papilla","B. Ascending colon","C. Sigmoid colon","D. Descending colon"],"g":["A","B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Ascending colon","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Ascending colon</b> (A also counts). An artery from the aorta at the first lumbar vertebra, in front of the left renal vein and third part of the duodenum, is the <b>SMA</b> (superior mesenteric artery). It feeds the <b>midgut</b>: duodenum past the major papilla through two-thirds of the transverse colon. B is the keyed answer; the distal duodenum also gets celiac blood through the pancreaticoduodenal arcade.<br>C. Sigmoid colon: IMA (inferior mesenteric artery).<br>D. Descending colon: IMA.","ex":"","fl":["Option A (duodenum distal to the major papilla) is also SMA (midgut) territory via the inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery, so two options are SMA-supplied. B is the intended answer; A is protected by the superior/inferior pancreaticoduodenal anastomosis with the celiac, which is presumably why the TA keyed B."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 42 (SMA)","Small & Large Intestines slide 63 (Ascending colon)","Small & Large Intestines slide 44 (Superior mesenteric artery)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI module TA review","sl":140,"f":"D042"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":95,"label":"Postprandial pain + weight loss → SMA stenosis","slides":[{"src":"sl/G012-25.jpg","cap":"Sagittal CT: SMA leaving front of aorta just below celiac trunk","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Abdomen III, slide 25"},{"src":"sl/G005-42.jpg","cap":"SMA: front branch of aorta at L1 supplying the midgut","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 42"}],"q":[{"id":"F0008-123-1","s":"Your 55-year-old patient complains of slowly progressive postprandial abdominal pain and weight loss. A sagittal image from a CT arteriogram is shown. The red arrows show stenosis of which artery?","o":["A. Superior mesenteric artery","B. Inferior mesenteric artery","C. Right renal artery","D. Inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery","E. Right external iliac artery"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Superior mesenteric artery","a":"A","why":"<b>Superior mesenteric artery (SMA).</b> Slowly worsening pain after meals plus weight loss is <b>chronic mesenteric ischemia</b> (intestinal angina), usually from SMA narrowing. On a sagittal CT (computed tomography) scan the SMA leaves the front of the aorta just below the celiac trunk.<br>B. IMA (inferior mesenteric artery): narrowing is usually silent, as the marginal artery fills in.<br>C. Right renal artery: causes high blood pressure, not meal pain.<br>D. Inferior pancreaticoduodenal: a small SMA branch.<br>E. Right external iliac: causes leg pain on walking.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"The CT arteriogram referred to is not present in the deck; answerable from the vignette (postprandial pain + weight loss = chronic mesenteric ischemia).","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 42 (SMA)","Radiology Abd III slide 25 (Superior Mesenteric Artery)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":123,"f":"F0008"},{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":369,"f":"F3903"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":96,"label":"Duodenal arteries vs greater pancreatic artery","slides":[{"src":"sl/G005-32.jpg","cap":"Duodenal arteries: supraduodenal, superior and inferior pancreaticoduodenal branches","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 32"},{"src":"sl/G007-15.jpg","cap":"Splenic artery branches feed pancreatic body and tail, not duodenum","where":"2026 lecture: Pancreas & Spleen, slide 15"}],"q":[{"id":"F1228-155-1","s":"The following are arteries that supply the duodenum except:","o":["A. Superior anterior pancreaticoduodenal artery","B. Posterior inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery","C. Greater pancreatic artery","D. Superior mesenteric artery","E. Posterior superior pancreaticoduodenal artery"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Greater pancreatic artery","a":"C","why":"<b>Greater pancreatic artery.</b> It is a branch of the <b>splenic</b> artery that feeds the body and tail of the pancreas, not the duodenum.<br>A. Superior anterior pancreaticoduodenal: from the gastroduodenal; supplies the duodenum.<br>B. Posterior inferior pancreaticoduodenal: from the SMA (superior mesenteric artery); supplies the lower duodenum.<br>D. Superior mesenteric artery: supplies the duodenum below the bile-duct opening, through the inferior pancreaticoduodenals.<br>E. Posterior superior pancreaticoduodenal: from the gastroduodenal; supplies the duodenum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 31 (duodenum)"],"src":[{"t":"2019 review","n":"old Exam 2 review: abdomen part only","sl":155,"f":"F1228"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":96,"label":"Posterior pancreaticoduodenal arcade behind pancreatic head","slides":[{"src":"sl/G005-33.jpg","cap":"Back view: posterior superior and inferior pancreaticoduodenal arteries on pancreatic head","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 33"}],"q":[{"id":"F3902-14-1","s":"Where does the superior posterior pancreaticoduodenal artery and inferior posterior pancreaticoduodenal artery anastamose?","o":["A. Superior to the greater omentum","B. Posterior to the head of the pancreas","C. Medial to the spleen","D. Lateral to the descending part of the duodenum"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Posterior to the head of the pancreas","a":"B","why":"<b>Posterior to the head of the pancreas.</b> The superior and inferior pancreaticoduodenal arteries each split into anterior and posterior branches. The <b>posterior</b> pair joins on the <b>back of the pancreatic head</b>; the anterior pair joins on its front.<br>A. Superior to the greater omentum: the omentum hangs from the stomach, far from this arcade.<br>C. Medial to the spleen: that is the splenic artery and pancreatic tail.<br>D. Lateral to the descending duodenum: the arcade lies <b>medial</b>, between duodenum and pancreatic head.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 31 (duodenum)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":14,"f":"F3902"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":97,"label":"Celiac–SMA collateral: superior ↔ inferior pancreaticoduodenal","slides":[{"src":"sl/G005-32.jpg","cap":"Superior (gastroduodenal) and inferior (SMA) pancreaticoduodenal arteries joining around duodenum","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 32"},{"src":"sl/F1180-263.jpg","cap":"Arcade: superior pancreaticoduodenal from celiac meets inferior from SMA","where":"2022 Harlem TA review, slide 263"},{"src":"sl/G007-15.jpg","cap":"Pancreas blood: gastroduodenal, both pancreaticoduodenal arcades, splenic branches","where":"2026 lecture: Pancreas & Spleen, slide 15"}],"q":[{"id":"F3902-2-1","s":"Which is an important anastomosis between the celiac trunk and superior mesenteric artery?","o":["A. Superior pancreaticoduodenal artery + gastroduodenal artery","B. Superior pancreaticoduodenal artery + inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery","C. Anterior gastroduodenal artery + posterior gastroduodenal artery","D. Left gastric artery + right gastro-omental artery"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Superior pancreaticoduodenal artery + inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery","a":"B","why":"<b>Superior + inferior pancreaticoduodenal arteries.</b> The superior ones come from the gastroduodenal (celiac trunk); the inferior ones come from the SMA (superior mesenteric artery). They join around the pancreatic head and link the two trunks.<br>A. Superior pancreaticoduodenal + gastroduodenal: branch and parent, both celiac.<br>C. Anterior and posterior gastroduodenal arteries: these do not exist.<br>D. Left gastric + right gastro-omental: both celiac branches.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 31 (duodenum)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":2,"f":"F3902"}]},{"id":"F1180-261-1","s":"A 65-year-old man is admitted to the emergency department with complaints of nonspecific abdominal pain. Physical and radiographic examinations reveal mild intestinal ischemia due to atherosclerotic occlusion of the midproximal part of the superior mesenteric artery, but collateral blood supply has delayed the onset of necrosis. What vessels provide collateral channels between the celiac trunk and the superior mesenteric artery?","o":["A. Superior and inferior pancreaticoduodenal","B. Left gastric and hepatic","C. Cystic and gastroduodenal","D. Right and left colic","E. Right and left gastro-omental"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Superior and inferior pancreaticoduodenal","a":"A","why":"<b>Superior and inferior pancreaticoduodenal.</b> The superior ones (gastroduodenal, celiac trunk) join the inferior ones (SMA, superior mesenteric artery) around the pancreatic head. If the SMA narrows, celiac blood flows through this arcade.<br>B. Left gastric and hepatic: both celiac branches.<br>C. Cystic and gastroduodenal: both from the hepatic artery (celiac).<br>D. Right and left colic: link the SMA to the IMA (inferior mesenteric artery), not the celiac.<br>E. Right and left gastro-omental: both celiac branches.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 31 (duodenum)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":261,"f":"F1180"},{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":204,"f":"F1178"},{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI module TA review","sl":148,"f":"D042"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3902-4-1","s":"A man was stabbed and was noted to have complete loss of blood supply from the superior mesenteric artery, what is the route that blood can take to supply the duodenum?","o":["A. Celiac trunk -> common hepatic artery -> gastroduodenal artery -> superior anterior and posterior pancreaticoduodenal arteries -> inferior anterior and posterior pancreaticoduodenal arteries","B. Celiac trunk -> splenic artery -> short gastric arteries -> gastroduodenal artery","C. Superior mesenteric artery -> inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery -> inferior anterior and posterior pancreaticoduodenal arteries","D. Celiac trunk -> gastroduodenal artery -> inferior anterior and posterior pancreaticoduodenal arteries"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Celiac trunk -> common hepatic artery -> gastroduodenal artery -> superior anterior and posterior pancreaticoduodenal arteries -> inferior anterior and posterior pancreaticoduodenal arteries","a":"A","why":"<b>A.</b> Celiac trunk → common hepatic → gastroduodenal → <b>superior pancreaticoduodenal</b> arteries, which join and fill the <b>inferior pancreaticoduodenal</b> arteries backward. This arcade links the celiac to the SMA (superior mesenteric artery) territory.<br>B. Short gastric arteries go to the gastric fundus; they never reach the gastroduodenal.<br>C. Starts at the SMA, which has no flow.<br>D. Skips steps: the gastroduodenal comes from the common hepatic and reaches the inferior arteries only through the superior pancreaticoduodenals.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 31 (duodenum)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 111 (Gasroduodenal:)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":4,"f":"F3902"},{"t":"2022 Middletown TA weekly","n":"10-06-2022 TA session (foregut viscera)","sl":12,"f":"F3907"}],"t":1},{"id":"D041-224-1","s":"A 62-year-old man has severe atherosclerotic narrowing of the celiac trunk, but he has no signs of foregut ischemia. Which arterial anastomosis is MOST responsible for maintaining adequate blood flow to his foregut organs?","o":["A. Right gastroepiploic ↔ Left gastroepiploic artery","B. Superior pancreaticoduodenal ↔ Inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery","C. Middle colic ↔ Left colic artery","D. Superior rectal ↔ Middle rectal artery","E. Gastroduodenal ↔ Right gastroepiploic artery"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Superior pancreaticoduodenal ↔ Inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery","a":"B","why":"<b>Superior ↔ inferior pancreaticoduodenal.</b> This arcade around the pancreatic head is the main link between the celiac trunk (foregut) and the SMA (superior mesenteric artery, midgut). With the celiac narrowed, SMA blood flows backward into celiac branches.<br>A. Right ↔ left gastroepiploic: both celiac.<br>C. Middle colic ↔ left colic: SMA to IMA (inferior mesenteric artery), midgut to hindgut.<br>D. Superior ↔ middle rectal: IMA to internal iliac, in the pelvis.<br>E. Gastroduodenal ↔ right gastroepiploic: parent and branch, both celiac.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 31 (duodenum)","Radiology Abd III slide 22 (Celiac Trunk: MIP)","Radiology Abd III slide 23 (Celiac Trunk: MIP)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":224,"f":"D041"}],"t":1},{"id":"F1252-127-1","s":"A patient presents with a thrombus occluding the superior mesenteric artery, causing intestinal ischemia to the midgut. How is the pancreas still receiving blood supply?","o":["A. Inferior pancreaticoduodenal","B. Superior pancreaticoduodenal","C. Middle colic","D. Right colic","E. Left colic"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Superior pancreaticoduodenal","a":"B","why":"<b>Superior pancreaticoduodenal artery.</b> It comes from the gastroduodenal artery, a <b>celiac</b> branch, so it keeps flowing when the SMA (superior mesenteric artery) is blocked. Splenic artery branches also feed the pancreatic body and tail.<br>A. Inferior pancreaticoduodenal: an SMA branch, cut off at its source.<br>C. Middle colic: SMA branch to the transverse colon.<br>D. Right colic: SMA branch to the ascending colon.<br>E. Left colic: IMA (inferior mesenteric artery) branch to the descending colon.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 31 (duodenum)","Pancreas & Spleen slide 14 (Pancreas)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":127,"f":"F1252"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":98,"label":"Jejunal/ileal arteries → arcades → vasa recta","slides":[{"src":"sl/G005-44.jpg","cap":"Fifteen to eighteen jejunal and ileal branches form arcades giving vasa recta","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 44"},{"src":"sl/G005-80.jpg","cap":"Inset: jejunal and ileal arteries forming arcades along the small bowel","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 80"}],"q":[{"id":"D045-310-1","s":"An angiogram shows long straight arteries (vasa recta) branching from arterial arcades supplying the small bowel. Which SMA branches give rise to these?","o":["A. Middle colic arteries","B. Jejunal and ileal arteries","C. Right colic artery","D. Ileocolic artery"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Jejunal and ileal arteries","a":"B","why":"<b>Jejunal and ileal arteries.</b> Fifteen to eighteen of these SMA (superior mesenteric artery) branches run in the mesentery, join into loops (<b>arcades</b>), and the arcades send straight <b>vasa recta</b> to the gut wall.<br>A. Middle colic: supplies the transverse colon.<br>C. Right colic: supplies the ascending colon.<br>D. Ileocolic: the end branch to the terminal ileum, cecum and appendix.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"slide 310 has a picture; question answerable from text","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 44 (Superior mesenteric artery)","Radiology Abd III slide 35 (Jejunal Branches)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":310,"f":"D045"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":98,"label":"Small-bowel veins → SMV → portal vein","slides":[{"src":"sl/G005-49.jpg","cap":"Jejunal and ileal veins draining into the SMV and hepatic portal vein","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 49"},{"src":"sl/G005-35.jpg","cap":"SMV joins the splenic vein to form the portal vein","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 35"}],"q":[{"id":"D045-294-1","s":"Venous drainage from the jejunum and ileum ultimately contributes to formation of which major vein?","o":["A. Inferior vena cava","B. Portal vein","C. Renal vein","D. Azygos vein"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Portal vein","a":"B","why":"<b>Portal vein.</b> Jejunal and ileal veins drain into the SMV (superior mesenteric vein). The SMV joins the splenic vein <b>behind the pancreatic neck</b> to form the portal vein, which takes gut blood to the liver.<br>A. IVC (inferior vena cava): gut blood reaches it only after the liver.<br>C. Renal vein: drains the kidney.<br>D. Azygos vein: drains the back of the chest wall.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 48 (Jejunum and ileum)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":294,"f":"D045"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":98,"label":"SMV runs to the right of SMA","slides":[{"src":"sl/G005-43.jpg","cap":"SMV (blue) running to the right of the SMA (red)","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 43"},{"src":"sl/G005-22.jpg","cap":"SMV and SMA side by side over the uncinate and third duodenum","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 22"}],"q":[{"id":"F1252-69-1","s":"While studying a donor, during Anatomy lab, you find an artery that gives off multiple branches to the jejunum and ileum and notice that these branches unite to form loops you that you recall are named arterial arcades. Nearby that artery is a vein that unites with the splenic vein posterior to to the neck of the pancreas to form the hepatic portal vein. What is the relationship of this artery and vein?","o":["A. The vein lies anterior and to the left of the artery","B. The vein lies posterior and to the left of the artery","C. The vein lies anterior and to the right of the artery","D. The vein lies posterior and to the right of the artery","E. The vein lies the to left of the artery in the same A-P plain"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"The vein lies anterior and to the right of the artery","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Anterior and to the right.</b> The artery is the SMA (superior mesenteric artery); the vein joining the splenic vein behind the pancreatic neck is the SMV (superior mesenteric vein). The SMV runs on the <b>right</b> of the SMA, slightly in front.<br>A, B. Wrong side: the SMV is not on the left.<br>D. The SMV is not behind the SMA.<br>E. Wrong side, and the vein sits slightly in front.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 48 (Jejunum and ileum)","Radiology Abd III slide 26 (Superior Mesenteric Artery)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":69,"f":"F1252"}]},{"id":"F3903-377-1","s":"The superior mesenteric artery:","o":["A) arises from the aorta at the T10 level.","B) is accompanied by the superior mesenteric vein on its right.","C) gives rise to a series of arterial arcades called vasa recta.","D) passes posterior to the horizontal part of the duodenum.","E) is accompanied by sympathetic fibers derived from lumbar splanchnic nerves."],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"is accompanied by the superior mesenteric vein on its right","a":"B","why":"<b>Accompanied by the SMV (superior mesenteric vein) on its right.</b> The vein runs beside the SMA (superior mesenteric artery) on its right side, up to join the splenic vein.<br>A. It arises at the first lumbar vertebra, not the tenth thoracic.<br>C. Its branches form arcades; vasa recta are the straight arteries that <b>leave</b> the arcades.<br>D. It passes <b>in front of</b> the horizontal part.<br>E. Its sympathetic fibers come from <b>thoracic</b> splanchnic nerves; lumbar splanchnics serve the inferior mesenteric plexus.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 42 (SMA)","Radiology Abd III slide 26 (Superior Mesenteric Artery)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":377,"f":"F3903"},{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":113,"f":"F0008"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":99,"label":"Uncinate process lies behind the SMA","slides":[{"src":"sl/G005-22.jpg","cap":"Uncinate process tucked behind the SMV and SMA","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 22"},{"src":"sl/D041-222.jpg","cap":"Behind the SMA: left renal vein, uncinate process, third duodenum","where":"2025 TA review, slide 222"}],"q":[{"id":"D041-220-1","s":"The superior mesenteric artery (SMA) passes anterior to which of the following structures?","o":["A. Uncinate process of the pancreas","B. First part of the duodenum","C. Head of the pancreas","D. Stomach"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Uncinate process of the pancreas","a":"A","why":"<b>Uncinate process of the pancreas.</b> Three things lie behind the SMA (superior mesenteric artery): the <b>left renal vein, the uncinate process and the third part of the duodenum</b>.<br>B. First part of the duodenum: higher and to the right; the SMA crosses the <b>third</b> part.<br>C. Head of the pancreas: lies to the right of the SMV (superior mesenteric vein); only its uncinate hook goes behind the vessels.<br>D. Stomach: lies <b>in front of</b> the SMA.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 42 (SMA)","Small & Large Intestines slide 4 (Objectives)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":220,"f":"D041"}],"t":1},{"id":"D045-304-1","s":"During pancreatic surgery, the surgeon notes a structure lying posterior to the SMA. Which part of the pancreas is in this location?","o":["A. Tail","B. Body","C. Uncinate process","D. Neck"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Uncinate process","a":"C","why":"<b>Uncinate process.</b> This hook from the lower pancreatic head slips <b>behind the SMV and SMA</b> (superior mesenteric vein and artery).<br>A. Tail: far left, at the splenic hilum.<br>B. Body: crosses <b>in front of</b> the aorta and SMA.<br>D. Neck: lies <b>in front of</b> the SMV and SMA; the portal vein forms behind it.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 42 (SMA)","Pancreas & Spleen slide 7 (Pancreas)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":304,"f":"D045"}],"t":1},{"id":"F1228-175-1","s":"Which of the following is correct?","o":["A. The inferior mesenteric artery lies in front of uncinate process of pancreas, and crosses 3rd part of duodenum","B. The superior mesenteric artery lies in front of uncinate process of pancreas, and crosses 3rd part of duodenum","C. The SMA runs downward and to left","D. The SMA is the artery of foregut"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"The superior mesenteric artery lies in front of uncinate process of pancreas, and crosses 3rd part of duodenum","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> The SMA (superior mesenteric artery) runs <b>in front of the uncinate process</b>, then crosses <b>in front of the third part</b> of the duodenum into the mesentery.<br>A. The IMA (inferior mesenteric artery) starts lower, below the duodenum, and runs to the left.<br>C. The SMA runs down and to the <b>right</b>, toward the ileocecal junction.<br>D. The SMA is the <b>midgut</b> artery; the celiac trunk is the foregut artery.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 42 (SMA)","Small & Large Intestines slide 43 (Crossed anteriorly by the splenic vein and the neck of the pancreas.)"],"src":[{"t":"2019 review","n":"old Exam 2 review: abdomen part only","sl":175,"f":"F1228"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":99,"label":"SMA/SMV cross in front of third duodenal part","slides":[{"src":"sl/G005-22.jpg","cap":"SMA and SMV crossing in front of the third part of duodenum","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 22"},{"src":"sl/G005-99.jpg","cap":"SMA syndrome: no fat pad, SMA compresses the duodenum on the aorta","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 99"},{"src":"sl/D042-147.jpg","cap":"Table: uncinate, third duodenum, left renal vein all behind the SMA","where":"2025 TA review, slide 147"}],"q":[{"id":"F1252-118-1","s":"What vessel(s) are BOTH superior and anterior to the 3rd part of the duodenum?","o":["A. IMA/IMV","B. SMA/SMV","C. Abdominal Aorta and branches","D. SVC","E. IVC"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"SMA/SMV","a":"B","why":"<b>SMA/SMV (superior mesenteric artery and vein).</b> They come down from behind the pancreatic neck, sit <b>above</b> the third part, then cross <b>in front of</b> it into the mesentery.<br>A. IMA/IMV (inferior mesenteric artery and vein): the artery starts below the duodenum; the vein runs up on the left.<br>C. Abdominal aorta: lies <b>behind</b> the third part.<br>D. SVC (superior vena cava): is in the chest.<br>E. IVC (inferior vena cava): lies <b>behind</b> the third part.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 21 (Inferior (third) part)","Small & Large Intestines slide 24 (Superiorly: SMA,SMV, head of pancreas)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":118,"f":"F1252"}]},{"id":"F3907-5-1","s":"A man presents to the emergency department with a stab wound just inferior to the head of the pancreas. He is bleeding extensively, and the knife is found to have penetrated the superior mesenteric artery. What other organ would be at risk of being penetrated?","o":["A. Superior part of duodenum","B. Descending part of duodenum","C. Inferior part of duodenum","D. Ascending part of duodenum"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Inferior part of duodenum","a":"C","why":"<b>Inferior (third) part of the duodenum.</b> Just below the pancreatic head, the SMA (superior mesenteric artery) crosses <b>in front of the third part</b>. A knife through the SMA here hits the duodenum right behind it.<br>A. Superior part: higher, at the first lumbar vertebra, above the pancreas.<br>B. Descending part: on the right, beside the pancreatic head, away from the SMA.<br>D. Ascending part: left of the aorta, rising to the duodenojejunal flexure.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 21 (Inferior (third) part)","Small & Large Intestines slide 22 (Anteriorly: SMA,SMV, small intestines)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA weekly","n":"10-06-2022 TA session (foregut viscera)","sl":5,"f":"F3907"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3114-18-1","s":"SMA Syndrome: Which part of the duodenum is compressed? Does anything else get compressed?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"The 3rd (inferior/horizontal) part of the duodenum; the left renal vein can also be compressed between the SMA and aorta (nutcracker syndrome, with backup into the left gonadal vein).","a":null,"why":"<b>The third (horizontal) part of the duodenum.</b> It lies in the narrow angle between the SMA (superior mesenteric artery) in front and the aorta behind. Rapid weight loss shrinks the fat pad there and closes the angle. The <b>left renal vein</b> crosses the same angle, so it can be squeezed too (nutcracker syndrome).<br>Common mix-up: SMA syndrome blocks the duodenum (vomiting, fear of eating); nutcracker blocks the left renal vein (blood in urine).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 42 (SMA)","Small & Large Intestines slide 4 (Objectives)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"Small & large intestines interactive","sl":18,"f":"F3114"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":100,"label":"SMA syndrome: third duodenum between SMA, aorta","slides":[{"src":"sl/G005-99.jpg","cap":"Side view: fat lost, duodenum pinched between superior mesenteric artery and aorta","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 99"},{"src":"sl/G005-97.jpg","cap":"Superior mesenteric vessels crossing in front of the third part of duodenum","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 97"},{"src":"sl/D042-147.jpg","cap":"Table: uncinate process, third duodenum, left renal vein relations to SMA","where":"2025 TA review, slide 147"}],"q":[{"id":"F1228-157-1","s":"In SMA syndrome, what structures are pinching the duodenum?","o":["A. Superior mesenteric artery and inferior mesenteric artery","B. Left kidney and spleen","C. Abdominal aorta and superior mesenteric artery","D. Abdominal aorta and inferior mesenteric artery","E. Pancreas and abdominal aorta"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Abdominal aorta and superior mesenteric artery","a":"C","why":"<b>Abdominal aorta and SMA (superior mesenteric artery).</b> The third part of the duodenum lies in the angle between the SMA in front and the aorta behind.<br>A. SMA and IMA (inferior mesenteric artery): the IMA arises lower, at L3 (third lumbar vertebra).<br>B. Left kidney and spleen: far to the left.<br>D. Aorta and IMA: the IMA arises below the duodenum.<br>E. Pancreas and aorta: the uncinate process sits behind the SMA, but the jaws are the SMA and aorta.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 96 (SMA syndrome)","Small & Large Intestines slide 21 (Inferior (third) part)"],"src":[{"t":"2019 review","n":"old Exam 2 review: abdomen part only","sl":157,"f":"F1228"}]},{"id":"D045-302-1","s":"A patient with SMA syndrome experiences severe postprandial pain due to compression of which structure between the SMA and aorta?","o":["A. First part of duodenum","B. Appendix","C. Third part of duodenum","D. Ascending colon"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Third part of duodenum","a":"C","why":"<b>Third part of the duodenum.</b> The third (horizontal) part crosses in front of the aorta and behind the SMA (superior mesenteric artery), right in the angle between them.<br>A. First part of duodenum: at L1 (first lumbar vertebra), above the SMA origin.<br>B. Appendix: right lower quadrant.<br>D. Ascending colon: right posterior abdominal wall.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 96 (SMA syndrome)","Small & Large Intestines slide 97 (Anteriorly: SMA,SMV, small intestines)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":302,"f":"D045"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3902-129-1","s":"A 54 yr old male presents with nausea, vomiting, and feelings of fullness early. He reports a stabbing pain after he eats and has begun to develop a fear of eating as a result. Upon presentation you suspect SMA syndrome. Which GI structure is compressed in this disorder?","o":["A. Large Intestine","B. Pancreas","C. Duodenum","D. Stomach","E. Sigmoid Colon"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Duodenum","a":"C","why":"<b>Duodenum.</b> In SMA (superior mesenteric artery) syndrome the third part of the duodenum is pinched between the SMA in front and the aorta behind. This gives early fullness, vomiting and stabbing pain after meals.<br>A. Large intestine: not between the SMA and aorta.<br>B. Pancreas: its uncinate process lies behind the SMA, but it is not the blocked tube.<br>D. Stomach: upstream; it fills up but is not squeezed.<br>E. Sigmoid colon: in the pelvis.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 96 (SMA syndrome)","Small & Large Intestines slide 21 (Inferior (third) part)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":129,"f":"F3902"}],"t":1},{"id":"F1180-305-1","s":"You have a very skinny patient that you think is missing a very important fat pad resulting in SMA Disorder. Which part of the duodenum is being crushed?","o":["A. Superior (first)","B. Descending (second)","C. Inferior (third)","D. Ascending (fourth)"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Inferior (third)","a":"C","why":"<b>Inferior (third) part.</b> A fat pad holds the SMA (superior mesenteric artery) away from the aorta. With weight loss the angle narrows and the third part, lying in that angle, is crushed.<br>A. Superior (first): at L1 (first lumbar vertebra), above the SMA.<br>B. Descending (second): on the right, beside the IVC (inferior vena cava).<br>D. Ascending (fourth): left of the aorta, past the SMA–aorta angle.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 96 (SMA syndrome)","Small & Large Intestines slide 21 (Inferior (third) part)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":305,"f":"F1180"},{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":244,"f":"F1178"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3907-25-1","s":"A 44 yo patient presents to the ED with extreme stabbing pain in their upper left quadrant immediately following a meal. This has been going on for about 4 weeks and they have since lost the desire to eat out of fear of the pain. Past medical history is notable for sudden weight loss over the course of 3 weeks. What is the correct diagnosis?","o":["A. SMA syndrome @ the 3rd portion of the duodenum","B. SMA syndrome @ the 2nd portion of the duodenum","C. SMA syndrome @ the 1st portion of the duodenum","D. Nutcracker syndrome","E. TMP (too much pizza)"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"SMA syndrome @ the 3rd portion of the duodenum","a":"A","why":"<b>SMA (superior mesenteric artery) syndrome at the third part of the duodenum.</b> Stabbing pain right after meals, fear of eating and fast weight loss are classic. Weight loss shrinks the fat pad, so the SMA pinches the third part against the aorta.<br>B. Second part: right of the IVC (inferior vena cava), not under the SMA.<br>C. First part: at L1 (first lumbar vertebra), above the SMA.<br>D. Nutcracker syndrome: the left renal vein is pinched instead.<br>E. Joke option.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 96 (SMA syndrome)","Small & Large Intestines slide 21 (Inferior (third) part)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA weekly","n":"10-06-2022 TA session (foregut viscera)","sl":25,"f":"F3907"}]},{"id":"D042-143-1","s":"A 19-year-old woman presents to the clinic with a 6-month history of early satiety, nausea, and significant weight loss. She reports worsening abdominal pain after meals, described as sharp and stabbing in the mid-abdomen that has now made her “afraid to eat”. A contrast-enhanced CT scan reveals compression of the third portion of the duodenum between two major vascular structures. Which of the following best describes the mechanism underlying this patient’s condition?","o":["A. Compression of the duodenum between the SMA and the aorta due to loss of mesenteric fat","B. Obstruction of the duodenum by hypertrophy of the uncinate process of the pancreas","C. Compression of the left renal vein leading to ischemia of the duodenal wall","D. Aberrant origin of the SMA from the celiac trunk leading to intestinal ischemia"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Compression of the duodenum between the SMA and the aorta due to loss of mesenteric fat","a":"A","why":"<b>The duodenum is squeezed between the SMA (superior mesenteric artery) and the aorta after loss of mesenteric fat.</b> The fat pad holds the SMA away from the aorta. Weight loss closes the angle and pinches the third part.<br>B. Uncinate process: lies behind the SMA but does not grow to block the duodenum.<br>C. Left renal vein squeeze is nutcracker syndrome, a kidney problem.<br>D. An SMA arising from the celiac trunk is a rare variant, not this mechanism.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 96 (SMA syndrome)","Small & Large Intestines slide 97 (Anteriorly: SMA,SMV, small intestines)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI module TA review","sl":143,"f":"D042"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":101,"label":"Nutcracker: SMA compresses left renal vein","slides":[{"src":"sl/G007-8.jpg","cap":"Left renal vein crossing the aorta just below the SMA origin","where":"2026 lecture: Pancreas & Spleen, slide 8"},{"src":"sl/D042-147.jpg","cap":"Table: left renal vein squeezed between SMA and aorta (nutcracker)","where":"2025 TA review, slide 147"}],"q":[{"id":"D045-300-1","s":"Bonus: What is this syndrome? (left renal vein compressed from anterior to posterior by the superior mesenteric artery)","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Nutcracker Syndrome","a":null,"why":"<b>Nutcracker syndrome.</b> The SMA (superior mesenteric artery) and the aorta act like the two arms of a nutcracker, squeezing the <b>left renal vein</b> that crosses between them. It causes blood in the urine, flank pain and sometimes a left varicocele.<br>Common mix-up: SMA syndrome uses the same angle but compresses the <b>third part of the duodenum</b>.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 42 (SMA)","Small & Large Intestines slide 4 (Objectives)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":300,"f":"D045"}]},{"id":"F3900-59-1","s":"Which of the following statements is correct regarding nutcracker syndrome?","o":["A. The superior mesenteric artery compresses the left renal artery against the aorta","B. The superior mesenteric artery compresses the right renal artery against the spine","C. The superior mesenteric artery compresses the left renal vein against the aorta","D. The superior mesenteric artery compresses the right renal vein against the aorta","E. The superior mesenteric artery compresses the right renal artery against the aorta"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"The superior mesenteric artery compresses the left renal vein against the aorta","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> In nutcracker syndrome the SMA (superior mesenteric artery) squeezes the <b>left renal vein against the aorta</b>. Only the left renal vein crosses the midline between them.<br>A. Left renal artery: comes off the aorta itself, behind the vein; it is not trapped.<br>B. Right renal artery: runs behind the IVC (inferior vena cava), away from the SMA.<br>D. Right renal vein: short; it enters the IVC directly and never crosses the aorta.<br>E. Right renal artery: runs behind the IVC, not between SMA and aorta.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 42 (SMA)","Small & Large Intestines slide 43 (Crossed anteriorly by the splenic vein and the neck of the pancreas.)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":59,"f":"F3900"}],"t":1},{"id":"D045-299-1","s":"A vascular mass compresses the left renal vein from anterior to posterior. What artery is most likely responsible?","o":["A. Celiac trunk","B. Superior mesenteric artery","C. Inferior mesenteric artery","D. Common iliac artery"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Superior mesenteric artery","a":"B","why":"<b>Superior mesenteric artery (SMA).</b> The left renal vein crosses the aorta <b>just below the SMA origin</b>, trapped between the SMA in front and the aorta behind. This is nutcracker syndrome.<br>A. Celiac trunk: arises higher, at the twelfth thoracic vertebra, above the renal veins.<br>C. Inferior mesenteric artery: arises lower, at the third lumbar vertebra.<br>D. Common iliac artery: begins at the fourth lumbar vertebra, far below.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 42 (SMA)","Small & Large Intestines slide 4 (Objectives)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":299,"f":"D045"}],"t":1},{"id":"D042-145-1","s":"While walking through the streets of Middletown on a dark night, you run into an evil anime villain (See pictured). In a panic, you recall a random anatomy fact to distract them. You blurt out: “With excessive weight loss, a patient could be at risk for nutcracker syndrome. This condition is caused by compression of the renal vein by…” Which structure/s complete your sentence?","o":["A. The celiac trunk and the IVC","B. The superior mesenteric artery and the aorta","C. The splenic artery and the aorta","D. The inferior mesenteric artery and the IVC"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"The superior mesenteric artery and the aorta","a":"B","why":"<b>The superior mesenteric artery and the aorta.</b> The left renal vein crosses in the narrow angle between the SMA (superior mesenteric artery) and the aorta. Weight loss shrinks the fat in this angle, so the vein gets pinched.<br>A. Celiac trunk and IVC (inferior vena cava): the celiac arises above the renal veins.<br>C. Splenic artery and aorta: the splenic artery runs along the top of the pancreas.<br>D. Inferior mesenteric artery and IVC: the IMA (inferior mesenteric artery) arises lower, below the renal veins.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"slide picture is a decorative cartoon, not needed","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 42 (SMA)","Small & Large Intestines slide 4 (Objectives)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI module TA review","sl":145,"f":"D042"}]},{"id":"F3902-132-1","s":"A 67 yr old female presents to your office with blood in her urine. She tells you that she has had lower quadrant abdominal pain, as well as nausea and vomiting. Upon further examination, you recognize that the Superior Mesenteric Artery is compressing a structure against the abdominal Aorta, causing these symptoms. What blood supply is being directly compressed in this syndrome?","o":["A. Superior Mesenteric Vein","B. Left Renal Vein","C. Hepatic Portal Vein","D. Left Renal Artery","E. Inferior Vena Cava"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Left Renal Vein","a":"B","why":"<b>Left renal vein.</b> In nutcracker syndrome the SMA (superior mesenteric artery) pins the <b>left renal vein against the aorta</b>. Back-pressure in the kidney causes blood in the urine and pain.<br>A. Superior mesenteric vein: runs to the right of the SMA, not between SMA and aorta.<br>C. Hepatic portal vein: forms behind the pancreatic neck and runs up to the liver.<br>D. Left renal artery: comes off the aorta itself, behind the vein.<br>E. Inferior vena cava: lies to the right of the aorta, outside the angle.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 42 (SMA)","Small & Large Intestines slide 4 (Objectives)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":132,"f":"F3902"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":102,"label":"Pelvic large intestine: lower sigmoid, rectum, anal canal","slides":[{"src":"sl/G005-56.jpg","cap":"Abdominal versus pelvic parts of the large intestine","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 56"},{"src":"sl/G005-72.jpg","cap":"Sigmoid colon runs to S3, where the rectum begins in the pelvis","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 72"}],"q":[{"id":"F3907-15-1","s":"The large intestines have parts in both the abdomen and the pelvis. Which of the following are included in the pelvic portion of the large intestines?","o":["A. Descending colon","B. Upper sigmoid colon","C. Lower sigmoid colon","D. Appendix","E. Cecum"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Lower sigmoid colon","a":"C","why":"<b>Lower sigmoid colon.</b> The pelvic parts of the large intestine are the lower sigmoid colon, the rectum and the anal canal. Everything above them is abdominal.<br>A. Descending colon: abdominal, on the left posterior abdominal wall.<br>B. Upper sigmoid colon: abdominal, in the left iliac fossa; only the lower part dips into the pelvis.<br>D. Appendix: abdominal, though it hangs over the pelvic brim in about one-third of people.<br>E. Cecum: abdominal, in the right iliac fossa.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 56 (Large intestines)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA weekly","n":"10-06-2022 TA session (foregut viscera)","sl":15,"f":"F3907"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":102,"label":"Descending colon: splenic flexure to sigmoid colon","slides":[{"src":"sl/G005-69.jpg","cap":"Descending colon: splenic flexure to sigmoid colon, secondarily retroperitoneal","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 69"}],"q":[{"id":"D042-133-1","s":"Which of the following parts of the large intestine travels from the splenic flexure to the sigmoid colon?","o":["A. Ascending colon","B. Transverse colon","C. Cecum","D. descending colon"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"descending colon","a":"D","why":"<b>Descending colon.</b> It runs from the splenic (left colic) flexure down the left posterior wall to the sigmoid colon. It is secondarily retroperitoneal.<br>A. Ascending colon: from the cecum up to the hepatic (right colic) flexure.<br>B. Transverse colon: from the hepatic flexure across to the splenic flexure.<br>C. Cecum: the blind pouch below the ileocecal junction, the start of the colon.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 69 (Descending colon)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI module TA review","sl":133,"f":"D042"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":102,"label":"Appendix lacks teniae, haustra, epiploic appendages","slides":[{"src":"sl/G005-57.jpg","cap":"Teniae coli, haustra, epiploic appendages; teniae absent from appendix and rectum","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 57"}],"q":[{"id":"D045-284-1","s":"A pathology specimen from the colon shows teniae coli, haustra, and epiploic appendages. Which structure lacks these features?","o":["A. Transverse colon","B. Descending colon","C. Appendix","D. Sigmoid colon"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Appendix","a":"C","why":"<b>Appendix.</b> Teniae coli (three longitudinal muscle bands), haustra (pouches) and epiploic appendages (fat tags) mark the colon. The appendix has a complete outer muscle layer instead, so it has none of the three. The rectum also lacks teniae.<br>A. Transverse colon: has all three features.<br>B. Descending colon: has all three.<br>D. Sigmoid colon: has all three; the teniae spread out only where it becomes the rectum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 57 (Large intestines)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":284,"f":"D045"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":103,"label":"Ileocecal valve: common site of obstruction","slides":[{"src":"sl/G005-60.jpg","cap":"Terminal ileum entering the cecum through the ileocecal valve","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 60"}],"q":[{"id":"F1228-159-1","s":"A 5 y/o boy comes in with bloating and abdominal cramping. You find an intestinal block. What area most commonly causes intestinal blockages?","o":["A. Splenic flexure","B. Duodenum","C. Ileocecal valves","D. Appendix","E. Jejunum"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Ileocecal valves","a":"C","why":"<b>Ileocecal valve.</b> The terminal ileum is the narrowest part of the bowel, and it pokes into the cecum as the ileocecal valve. Blockage is most common here: in children, the ileum telescopes into the cecum (ileocolic intussusception); in adults, gallstones lodge here.<br>A. Splenic flexure: prone to poor blood flow, not blockage.<br>B. Duodenum: blockage there is mainly a newborn problem (atresia).<br>D. Appendix: a blind pouch; blocking it causes appendicitis.<br>E. Jejunum: wide lumen, rarely blocked.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 58 (Cecum)"],"src":[{"t":"2019 review","n":"old Exam 2 review: abdomen part only","sl":159,"f":"F1228"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":103,"label":"Appendix usually lies retrocecal","slides":[{"src":"sl/G005-60.jpg","cap":"Appendix positions: retrocecal 64%, pelvic 32%, others rare","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 60"},{"src":"sl/G005-59.jpg","cap":"Appendix arises posteromedially from cecum; usually retrocecal; mesoappendix","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 59"}],"q":[{"id":"D045-289-1","s":"During appendectomy, the surgeon finds the appendix located behind the cecum. What is this position called?","o":["A. Pelvic","B. Subhepatic","C. Retrocecal","D. Pre-ileal"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Retrocecal","a":"C","why":"<b>Retrocecal.</b> An appendix behind the cecum is called retrocecal. It is the most common position, about two-thirds of people in the lecture figure.<br>A. Pelvic: hangs down over the pelvic brim; second most common (about one-third).<br>B. Subhepatic: up under the liver, usually with a cecum that failed to descend; rare.<br>D. Pre-ileal: in front of the terminal ileum; about one percent.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 59 (Vermiform appendix)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":289,"f":"D045"}],"t":1},{"id":"F1228-165-1","s":"You wake up in the middle of the night, diaphoretic and feverish, with a stabbing pain in your RLQ. You’re 99% sure it’s appendicitis based on your professional medical expertise. In what position would you most likely find your appendix in relation to the rest of your intestines?","o":["A. Anterior to terminal ileum","B. Medial to cecum","C. Posterior to terminal ileum","D. Retrocecal","E. Lateral to cecum"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Retrocecal","a":"D","why":"<b>Retrocecal.</b> The appendix most often lies behind the cecum (about two-thirds of people). Its base is always on the posteromedial cecum; only the free tip varies.<br>A. Anterior to terminal ileum: the pre-ileal position, about one percent.<br>B. Medial to cecum: describes where the base attaches, not the usual position of the appendix.<br>C. Posterior to terminal ileum: the post-ileal position, the rarest (about half a percent).<br>E. Lateral to cecum: not one of the usual positions.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 59 (Vermiform appendix)"],"src":[{"t":"2019 review","n":"old Exam 2 review: abdomen part only","sl":165,"f":"F1228"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":104,"label":"Cecum and appendix: ileocolic artery (from SMA)","slides":[{"src":"sl/G005-61.jpg","cap":"Appendicular artery from the ileocolic artery, running in the mesoappendix","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 61"},{"src":"sl/G005-80.jpg","cap":"Superior mesenteric branches: ileocolic, appendicular, right colic, middle colic","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 80"},{"src":"sl/G005-62.jpg","cap":"Cecum and appendix nerves: vagus and sympathetics via superior mesenteric plexus","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 62"}],"q":[{"id":"F3114-43-1","s":"Where does the appendix stem from? What artery is nearby? What is the artery that provides blood to the appendix called?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Cecum; ileocolic artery; appendicular artery.","a":null,"why":"<b>Cecum → ileocolic artery → appendicular artery.</b> The appendix comes off the posteromedial wall of the cecum, below the ileocecal junction. The ileocolic artery, the last branch of the SMA (superior mesenteric artery), runs to this corner and gives the appendicular artery, which travels in the mesoappendix.<br>Common mix-up: the appendicular artery is the vessel tied in an appendectomy; the ileocolic is its parent and the SMA its grandparent.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 59 (Vermiform appendix)","Small & Large Intestines slide 61 (Cecum and Vermiform appendix)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"Small & large intestines interactive","sl":43,"f":"F3114"}]},{"id":"F3903-381-1","s":"A 19-year-old man with a ruptured appendix is sent to the emergency department for surgery. To cut off the blood supply to the appendix (if collateral circulation is discounted), a surgeon should ligate which of the following arteries?","o":["A. Ileocolic artery","B. Middle colic artery","C. Right colic artery","D. Inferior mesenteric artery","E. Common iliac artery"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Ileocolic artery","a":"A","why":"<b>Ileocolic artery.</b> The appendix's only artery, the appendicular artery, branches from the ileocolic artery. Tying the ileocolic cuts off the appendix.<br>B. Middle colic: supplies the transverse colon.<br>C. Right colic: supplies the ascending colon.<br>D. Inferior mesenteric: supplies the colon from the distal transverse colon to the upper rectum.<br>E. Common iliac: supplies the pelvis and leg, not the gut.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 61 (Cecum and Vermiform appendix)","Small & Large Intestines slide 90 (Appendicitis)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":381,"f":"F3903"},{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":115,"f":"F0008"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3114-42-1","s":"An 13-year-old female is rushed to the emergency room complaining of severe pain located in her lower right hand quadrant. She is diagnosed with appendicitis and is sent to the OR. To prevent any extra bleeding during the procedure, the surgeon has to remember to ligate which of the following arteries?","o":["A. Right colic","B. Left colic","C. Ileocolic","D. Inferior mesenteric","E. Superior mesenteric"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Ileocolic","a":"C","why":"<b>Ileocolic artery.</b> The appendix is supplied by the appendicular artery, a branch of the ileocolic artery. The appendicular artery is not listed, so its parent, the ileocolic, is the answer.<br>A. Right colic: supplies the ascending colon.<br>B. Left colic: an IMA (inferior mesenteric artery) branch to the descending colon.<br>D. Inferior mesenteric: supplies the hindgut, far from the appendix.<br>E. Superior mesenteric: the main trunk to the whole midgut; tying it would kill the small bowel.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 61 (Cecum and Vermiform appendix)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"Small & large intestines interactive","sl":42,"f":"F3114"},{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":355,"f":"F3903"},{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":309,"f":"F1180"},{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":248,"f":"F1178"},{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":170,"f":"F3902"}],"t":1},{"id":"D045-115-1","s":"A 12-year-old boy is brought to the emergency room with a fever, nausea, and pain in his abdomen. Acute appendicitis is the preliminary diagnosis due to his symptoms and an elevated white blood cell count (leukocytosis). The given axial CT reveals an inflamed radiodense appendix in the retrocecal position, exhibiting a thickened wall and surrounding edema. The boy is taken to the operating room for a laparoscopic appendectomy. Branches of what artery will need to be ligated during the procedure to alleviate excessive bleeding?","o":["A. Celiac","B. Superior epigastric","C. Superior mesenteric","D. Inferior epigastric","E. Inferior mesenteric"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Superior mesenteric","a":"C","why":"<b>Superior mesenteric artery (SMA).</b> The appendix is midgut, so its blood comes from the SMA: SMA → ileocolic artery → appendicular artery, the vessel tied in an appendectomy.<br>A. Celiac: supplies the foregut, down to the second part of the duodenum.<br>B. Superior epigastric: from the internal thoracic artery; supplies the upper rectus abdominis.<br>D. Inferior epigastric: from the external iliac artery; supplies the lower rectus abdominis.<br>E. Inferior mesenteric: supplies the hindgut, distal transverse colon to upper rectum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"slide 115 has a picture; question answerable from text","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 61 (Cecum and Vermiform appendix)","Small & Large Intestines slide 44 (Superior mesenteric artery)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":115,"f":"D045"}]},{"id":"F3114-24-1","s":"A 58-year-old male is admitted to the hospital with complaints of abdominal pain in his lower right hand quadrant. Radiographic examination reveals diffuse ulcerations in his cecum. His physician determines that the involved area of the bowel needs to be removed. If the patient's anatomy follows the most typical patterns, which vessels and nerves will be cut during the operation?","o":["A. Superior mesenteric plexus and middle colic artery","B. Vagus nerve and the right colic artery","C. Inferior mesenteric plexus and sigmoid artery","D. Vagus nerve and the ileocolic artery","E. Pelvic splanchnic nerves and the left colic artery"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Vagus nerve and the ileocolic artery","a":"D","why":"<b>Vagus nerve and ileocolic artery.</b> The cecum is midgut. Its arteries are the anterior and posterior cecal branches of the ileocolic artery. Its parasympathetic nerves are vagal fibers in the superior mesenteric plexus.<br>A. The superior mesenteric plexus fits, but the middle colic supplies the transverse colon.<br>B. The vagus fits, but the right colic supplies the ascending colon.<br>C. Inferior mesenteric plexus and sigmoid artery: hindgut, on the left.<br>E. Pelvic splanchnic nerves and left colic artery: hindgut, on the left.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 61 (Cecum and Vermiform appendix)","Small & Large Intestines slide 62 (Cecum and Vermiform appendix)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"Small & large intestines interactive","sl":24,"f":"F3114"},{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":343,"f":"F3903"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":105,"label":"McBurney's point: one-third from ASIS → appendicitis","slides":[{"src":"sl/G005-91.jpg","cap":"McBurney's point: one-third of the way from right ASIS to umbilicus","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 91"},{"src":"sl/G003-24.jpg","cap":"McBurney's point: two-thirds from umbilicus to ASIS; appendectomy incision site","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 24"}],"q":[{"id":"D041-212-1","s":"A patient with suspected appendicitis has focal tenderness at McBurney’s point. This point is located at which surface landmark?","o":["A. Midpoint between the costal margin and umbilicus","B. One-third of the distance from the right ASIS to the umbilicus","C. Midway between both ASIS’s","D. At the intersection of the midclavicular line and subcostal plane"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"One-third of the distance from the right ASIS to the umbilicus","a":"B","why":"<b>One-third of the way from the right ASIS (anterior superior iliac spine) to the umbilicus.</b> This is McBurney's point, which lies over the base of the appendix.<br>A. Midpoint between costal margin and umbilicus: upper abdomen, far from the appendix.<br>C. Midway between both ASISs: the midline, above the bladder.<br>D. Midclavicular line at the subcostal plane: upper abdomen, far above the appendix.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 91 (Mc Burney’s point)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":212,"f":"D041"}],"t":1},{"id":"D045-287-1","s":"A 19-year-old patient presents with periumbilical pain that localizes to the RLQ. Tenderness is greatest two-thirds from the umbilicus to the ASIS. What structure is most likely inflamed?","o":["A. Terminal ileum","B. Cecum","C. Sigmoid colon","D. Appendix"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Appendix","a":"D","why":"<b>Appendix.</b> Two-thirds of the way from the umbilicus to the ASIS (anterior superior iliac spine) is the same spot as one-third from the ASIS: McBurney's point. Pain that starts at the umbilicus and moves to the RLQ (right lower quadrant) is classic appendicitis.<br>A. Terminal ileum: can cause RLQ pain, but McBurney's point marks the appendix.<br>B. Cecum: nearby, but the point marks the appendix base.<br>C. Sigmoid colon: left lower quadrant.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 91 (Mc Burney’s point)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 24 (Clinical Correlates)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":287,"f":"D045"}],"t":1},{"id":"D042-135-1","s":"A 15-year-old presents to the emergency department with a 12-hour history of lower abdominal pain. She initially felt a vague discomfort around her umbilicus, but the pain has since localized to the right lower quadrant. A low-grade fever is present, and her WBC count is mildly elevated. On examination, she has rebound tenderness at a point located one-third of the distance from the anterior superior iliac spine to the umbilicus. What is the most likely explanation for the patients pain?","o":["A. Crohn’s Disease","B. SMA Syndrome","C. Appendicitis","D. Megacolon"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Appendicitis","a":"C","why":"<b>Appendicitis.</b> Vague pain around the umbilicus (midgut visceral pain, T10, the tenth thoracic skin band) that moves to McBurney's point when the parietal peritoneum is irritated is classic.<br>A. Crohn's disease: RLQ (right lower quadrant) pain, but chronic and recurring, with diarrhea.<br>B. SMA (superior mesenteric artery) syndrome: upper abdominal pain after meals, weight loss.<br>D. Megacolon: a hugely dilated colon with constipation.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 91 (Mc Burney’s point)","Small & Large Intestines slide 90 (Appendicitis)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI module TA review","sl":135,"f":"D042"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":106,"label":"Ascending colon is secondarily retroperitoneal","slides":[{"src":"sl/G005-63.jpg","cap":"Ascending colon on right posterior wall, secondarily retroperitoneal","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 63"},{"src":"sl/D041-210.jpg","cap":"Cross-section: retroperitoneal ascending colon versus jejunum on its mesentery","where":"2025 TA review, slide 210"}],"q":[{"id":"D041-208-1","s":"Which portion of the colon is classified as secondarily retroperitoneal?","o":["A. Transverse colon","B. Sigmoid colon","C. Cecum","D. Ascending colon"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Ascending colon","a":"D","why":"<b>Ascending colon.</b> It starts out with a mesentery in the embryo. That mesentery then fuses to the posterior wall, so the colon becomes secondarily retroperitoneal. The descending colon does the same.<br>A. Transverse colon: intraperitoneal, hanging on the transverse mesocolon.<br>B. Sigmoid colon: intraperitoneal, on the sigmoid mesocolon.<br>C. Cecum: intraperitoneal; it has no mesentery but is almost fully covered by peritoneum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 63 (Ascending colon)","Small & Large Intestines slide 69 (Descending colon)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 13 (Peritoneal organs)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":208,"f":"D041"}],"t":1},{"id":"D045-280-1","s":"A 63-year-old man has inflammation involving a bowel segment that is secondarily retroperitoneal and lies along the right posterior abdominal wall. Which structure is affected?","o":["A. Cecum","B. Ascending colon","C. Transverse colon","D. Sigmoid colon"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Ascending colon","a":"B","why":"<b>Ascending colon.</b> Two clues: secondarily retroperitoneal, and on the right posterior abdominal wall. Only the ascending colon fits both. The descending colon is the left-side match.<br>A. Cecum: on the right, but intraperitoneal, in the iliac fossa.<br>C. Transverse colon: intraperitoneal, on the transverse mesocolon.<br>D. Sigmoid colon: intraperitoneal, on the left, on the sigmoid mesocolon.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 63 (Ascending colon)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":280,"f":"D045"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":106,"label":"Transverse colon: most mobile, on transverse mesocolon","slides":[{"src":"sl/G005-66.jpg","cap":"Transverse colon from hepatic to splenic flexure, on transverse mesocolon","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 66"}],"q":[{"id":"D045-282-1","s":"A bowel obstruction is found in the most mobile segment of the large intestine, which is suspended by the transverse mesocolon. Which structure is involved?","o":["A. Descending colon","B. Transverse colon","C. Ascending colon","D. Rectum"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Transverse colon","a":"B","why":"<b>Transverse colon.</b> It hangs on the transverse mesocolon from the hepatic flexure to the splenic flexure. It is the largest and most mobile part of the colon.<br>A. Descending colon: secondarily retroperitoneal, fixed to the left posterior wall.<br>C. Ascending colon: secondarily retroperitoneal, fixed on the right.<br>D. Rectum: in the pelvis, with no mesentery; fixed.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 66 (Transverse colon)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":282,"f":"D045"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":107,"label":"Ascending colon and hepatic flexure: right colic (SMA)","slides":[{"src":"sl/G005-84.jpg","cap":"Superior mesenteric angiogram: right colic, middle colic, ileocolic arteries labelled","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 84"},{"src":"sl/G005-64.jpg","cap":"Ascending colon supply: ileocolic and right colic arteries and veins","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 64"}],"q":[{"id":"D045-312-1","s":"A vessel supplying the ascending colon is seen on angiography. It most likely represents which branch of the SMA?","o":["A. Left colic artery","B. Middle colic artery","C. Right colic artery","D. Sigmoid artery"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Right colic artery","a":"C","why":"<b>Right colic artery.</b> The ascending colon is supplied by the ileocolic and right colic arteries, both branches of the SMA (superior mesenteric artery). The right colic runs straight to the right, behind the peritoneum, to the ascending colon.<br>A. Left colic: an IMA (inferior mesenteric artery) branch to the descending colon.<br>B. Middle colic: an SMA branch to the transverse colon.<br>D. Sigmoid artery: an IMA branch to the sigmoid colon.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 63 (Ascending colon)","Radiology Abd III slide 38 (Right Colic Artery)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":312,"f":"D045"}],"t":1},{"id":"F1252-75-1","s":"While studying for the practical portion of your Anatomy exam, you decide to review some written material as well by correlating viscera, their blood supply, and the ribs they are associated with. You find the right colic flexure on a plastinated specimen.  What ribs does it lie deep to and what is its blood supply?","o":["A. 7th and 8th ribs, branch of the celiac artery","B. 7th and 8th ribs, branch of the superior mesenteric artery","C. 9th and 10th ribs, branch of the superior mesenteric artery","D. 11th and 12 ribs, branch of the superior mesenteric artery","E. 11th and 12th ribs, branch of the inferior mesenteric artery"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"9th and 10th ribs, branch of the superior mesenteric artery","a":"C","why":"<b>Ninth and tenth ribs; a branch of the SMA (superior mesenteric artery).</b> The right colic (hepatic) flexure sits under the liver, deep to the ninth and tenth ribs. It is midgut, supplied mainly by the right colic artery, an SMA branch.<br>A. Seventh and eighth ribs are too high, and the celiac supplies the foregut.<br>B. Right artery, but the ribs are too high.<br>D. Right artery, but the ribs are too low.<br>E. Wrong ribs, and the IMA (inferior mesenteric artery) supplies only the left colon.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 63 (Ascending colon)","Small & Large Intestines slide 64 (Ascending colon)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":75,"f":"F1252"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":107,"label":"Middle colic artery: SMA branch to transverse colon","slides":[{"src":"sl/G005-84.jpg","cap":"Superior mesenteric angiogram: middle colic artery rising toward transverse colon","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 84"},{"src":"sl/G012-34.jpg","cap":"Angiogram with arrow on the middle colic artery","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Abdomen III, slide 34"}],"q":[{"id":"D041-227-1","s":"On an abdominal angiogram, a branch of the superior mesenteric artery is seen traveling superiorly toward the transverse colon and giving off right and left branches. Which artery is this?","o":["A. Right colic artery","B. Middle colic artery","C. Ileocolic artery","D. Inferior mesenteric artery","E. Jejunal artery"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Middle colic artery","a":"B","why":"<b>Middle colic artery.</b> It leaves the SMA (superior mesenteric artery) just below the pancreas, runs up into the transverse mesocolon, and splits into right and left branches for the transverse colon.<br>A. Right colic artery: runs to the right, toward the ascending colon.<br>C. Ileocolic artery: runs down and right to the cecum and terminal ileum.<br>D. Inferior mesenteric artery: a separate branch of the aorta, not an SMA branch.<br>E. Jejunal artery: runs left to the jejunum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Radiology Abd III slide 34 (Middle Colic Artery)","Small & Large Intestines slide 67 (Transverse colon)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":227,"f":"D041"}],"t":1},{"id":"D045-314-1","s":"During angiography, an artery is visualized supplying the transverse colon near the hepatic flexure. Which artery is this?","o":["A. Middle colic artery","B. Left colic artery","C. Ileocolic artery","D. Superior rectal artery"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Middle colic artery","a":"A","why":"<b>Middle colic artery.</b> This SMA (superior mesenteric artery) branch is the artery of the transverse colon. Its right branch reaches the transverse colon near the hepatic flexure and joins the right colic.<br>B. Left colic: an IMA (inferior mesenteric artery) branch to the splenic flexure and descending colon.<br>C. Ileocolic: goes to the cecum, appendix and terminal ileum.<br>D. Superior rectal: an IMA branch to the upper rectum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"slide 314 has a picture; question answerable from text","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 67 (Transverse colon)","Radiology Abd III slide 34 (Middle Colic Artery)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":314,"f":"D045"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":108,"label":"SMA vs IMA: split at distal transverse colon","slides":[{"src":"sl/G005-80.jpg","cap":"Superior mesenteric branches on the right colon, inferior mesenteric on the left","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 80"},{"src":"sl/G001-29.jpg","cap":"Hindgut: distal third of transverse colon to rectum, supplied by IMA","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 29"},{"src":"sl/D042-142.jpg","cap":"Table: blood supply of each gut segment, superior vs inferior mesenteric","where":"2025 TA review, slide 142"}],"q":[{"id":"D041-216-1","s":"The hindgut portion of the large intestine (distal 1/3 of transverse colon → rectum) receives blood from which artery?","o":["A. Celiac trunk","B. Superior mesenteric artery","C. Inferior mesenteric artery","D. Middle colic artery"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Inferior mesenteric artery","a":"C","why":"<b>Inferior mesenteric artery (IMA).</b> The IMA is the hindgut artery: the distal one-third of the transverse colon, the descending colon, the sigmoid colon and the upper rectum.<br>A. Celiac trunk: foregut, from the lower esophagus to the second part of the duodenum.<br>B. Superior mesenteric artery: midgut, up to the proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon.<br>D. Middle colic artery: a branch of the superior mesenteric artery to the proximal transverse colon.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 79 (Inferior mesenteric artery)","GI Development slide 29 (Hindgut)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":216,"f":"D041"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3901-146-1","s":"Describe the blood supply to the transverse colon","o":["A. Proximal ⅓ supplied by the middle colic artery (branch of SMA), and distal ⅔ supplied by the left colic artery (branch of SMA)","B. Proximal ⅔ supplied by the middle colic artery (branch of the SMA), and distal ⅓ supplied by the left colic artery (branch of IMA)","C. Proximal ⅔ supplied by the right colic artery (branch of SMA), and distal ⅓ supplied by the middle colic artery (branch of the SMA)","D. Entire transverse colon supplied by the middle colic artery (branch of SMA)","E. Entire transverse colon supplied by the middle colic artery (branch of IMA)"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Proximal ⅔ supplied by the middle colic artery (branch of the SMA), and distal ⅓ supplied by the left colic artery (branch of IMA)","a":"B","why":"<b>Proximal two-thirds by the middle colic (SMA); distal one-third by the left colic (IMA).</b> The transverse colon is where the midgut, supplied by the SMA (superior mesenteric artery), meets the hindgut, supplied by the IMA (inferior mesenteric artery).<br>A. Fractions flipped, and the left colic comes from the IMA, not the SMA.<br>C. The right colic supplies the ascending colon and hepatic flexure, not two-thirds of the transverse colon.<br>D. Leaves out the left colic's distal one-third.<br>E. The middle colic comes from the SMA, not the IMA.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 67 (Transverse colon)","Small & Large Intestines slide 79 (Inferior mesenteric artery)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":146,"f":"F3901"}]},{"id":"F1180-307-1","s":"During surgery, the inferior mesenteric artery is severed. Which portion of the colon will still have full blood perfusion?","o":["A. Ascending colon","B. Transverse colon","C. Descending colon","D. Sigmoid colon"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Ascending colon","a":"A","why":"<b>Ascending colon.</b> It is midgut, fed only by branches of the SMA (superior mesenteric artery): the ileocolic and right colic arteries. Cutting the IMA (inferior mesenteric artery) does not touch it.<br>B. Transverse colon: its distal one-third gets the left colic, an IMA branch, so part of it loses supply.<br>C. Descending colon: left colic and superior sigmoid arteries, both from the IMA.<br>D. Sigmoid colon: sigmoid arteries from the IMA.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 64 (Ascending colon)","Small & Large Intestines slide 67 (Transverse colon)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":307,"f":"F1180"},{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":172,"f":"F3902"},{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":246,"f":"F1178"}]},{"id":"F3900-57-1","s":"A 45-year-old female presents to the emergency department with sudden-onset severe abdominal pain, particularly in the left lower quadrant. She describes the pain as crampy and sharp and mentions that it began after a large meal. On examination, there is tenderness and guarding over the left lower abdomen, and laboratory tests show an elevated white blood cell count. Imaging studies reveal a segment of the sigmoid colon with a compromised blood supply. Which of the following arteries is most likely affected in this patient's condition?","o":["A. Celiac trunk","B. Superior mesenteric artery","C. Inferior mesenteric artery","D. Renal artery","E. Gonadal artery"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Inferior mesenteric artery","a":"C","why":"<b>Inferior mesenteric artery (IMA).</b> The sigmoid colon is supplied by the sigmoid arteries, which branch from the IMA. Left lower quadrant pain fits the sigmoid colon.<br>A. Celiac trunk: foregut (stomach, liver, spleen, upper duodenum).<br>B. Superior mesenteric artery: midgut, up to the proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon.<br>D. Renal artery: supplies the kidney only.<br>E. Gonadal artery: supplies the testis or ovary only.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 74 (Sigmoid colon)","Small & Large Intestines slide 79 (Inferior mesenteric artery)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":57,"f":"F3900"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3903-379-1","s":"A 21-year-old man receives a penetrating knife wound in the abdomen and is injured in both the superior mesenteric artery and the vagus nerve. Which portion of the colon would most likely be impaired by this injury?","o":["A. Ascending and transverse colons","B. Ascending and descending colons","C. Transverse and sigmoid colons","D. Descending and sigmoid colons","E. Transverse and descending colons"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Ascending and transverse colons","a":"A","why":"<b>Ascending and transverse colons.</b> The SMA (superior mesenteric artery) and the vagus nerve both serve the midgut, which ends at the proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon. So the ascending colon and most of the transverse colon are hit.<br>B. The descending colon is hindgut: IMA (inferior mesenteric artery) and pelvic splanchnic nerves.<br>C. The sigmoid colon is hindgut.<br>D. Descending and sigmoid: both hindgut, spared.<br>E. The descending colon is hindgut, spared.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 64 (Ascending colon)","Small & Large Intestines slide 67 (Transverse colon)","Small & Large Intestines slide 62 (Cecum and Vermiform appendix)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":379,"f":"F3903"},{"t":"? tutor review","n":"old Exam 2: abdomen part only","sl":107,"f":"F1243"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":108,"label":"Ileocolic artery does not supply sigmoid colon","slides":[{"src":"sl/G005-45.jpg","cap":"Table of superior mesenteric branches and the gut segment each supplies","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 45"},{"src":"sl/G005-80.jpg","cap":"Ileocolic to cecum and appendix; sigmoid arteries from inferior mesenteric","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 80"}],"q":[{"id":"F1228-161-1","s":"Pick the incorrect match regarding vascular supply and the area it supplies:","o":["A. Intestinal arteries – branches to ileum and jejunum","B. Middle colic artery – transverse colon","C. Right colic artery – ascending colon","D. Ileocolic artery – sigmoid colon","E. Inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery – head of pancreas"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Ileocolic artery – sigmoid colon","a":"D","why":"<b>Ileocolic artery – sigmoid colon is the wrong match.</b> The ileocolic artery, the last branch of the SMA (superior mesenteric artery), supplies the terminal ileum, cecum, appendix and start of the ascending colon. The sigmoid colon gets sigmoid arteries from the IMA (inferior mesenteric artery).<br>A. Intestinal (jejunal and ileal) arteries to the jejunum and ileum: correct.<br>B. Middle colic to the transverse colon: correct.<br>C. Right colic to the ascending colon: correct.<br>E. Inferior pancreaticoduodenal to the head of the pancreas: correct.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 64 (Ascending colon)","Small & Large Intestines slide 44 (Superior mesenteric artery)"],"src":[{"t":"2019 review","n":"old Exam 2 review: abdomen part only","sl":161,"f":"F1228"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":109,"label":"Descending colon: left colic artery (IMA)","slides":[{"src":"sl/G005-79.jpg","cap":"Inferior mesenteric branches: left colic (ascending, descending branches), sigmoid, superior rectal","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 79"},{"src":"sl/G005-85.jpg","cap":"Inferior mesenteric angiogram: left colic artery and its two branches labelled","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 85"}],"q":[{"id":"D045-317-1","s":"An angiogram demonstrates a branch of the IMA supplying the descending colon with superior and inferior branches. Which artery is this?","o":["A. Left colic artery","B. Sigmoid artery","C. Middle colic artery","D. Ileocolic artery"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Left colic artery","a":"A","why":"<b>Left colic artery.</b> It is the first branch of the IMA (inferior mesenteric artery). It runs left to the descending colon and splits into ascending and descending branches.<br>B. Sigmoid artery: two or three IMA branches that run down to the sigmoid colon.<br>C. Middle colic artery: an SMA (superior mesenteric artery) branch to the transverse colon.<br>D. Ileocolic artery: an SMA branch to the cecum and ileum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 79 (Inferior mesenteric artery)","Radiology Abd III slide 43 (Left Colic Artery)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":317,"f":"D045"}],"t":1},{"id":"D041-230-1","s":"On an abdominal angiogram, a branch of the inferior mesenteric artery is seen traveling upward and laterally toward the descending colon. Which artery is this?","o":["A. Left colic artery","B. Sigmoidal artery","C. Superior rectal artery","D. Middle colic artery","E. Ileocolic artery"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Left colic artery","a":"A","why":"<b>Left colic artery.</b> The first branch of the IMA (inferior mesenteric artery) runs up and to the left to the descending colon and joins the marginal artery.<br>B. Sigmoidal arteries: run down and left to the sigmoid colon.<br>C. Superior rectal artery: the IMA's continuation straight down to the rectum.<br>D. Middle colic: an SMA (superior mesenteric artery) branch to the transverse colon.<br>E. Ileocolic: an SMA branch to the cecum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Radiology Abd III slide 43 (Left Colic Artery)","Small & Large Intestines slide 79 (Inferior mesenteric artery)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":230,"f":"D041"}]},{"id":"D042-138-1","s":"A 60-year-old man presents with severe, crampy abdominal pain that began 2 hours ago. Physical examination reveals an abdomen that is tender to palpation in the LLQ, and there are decreased bowel sounds. A CT angiogram is performed and reveals reduced blood flow to the descending colon. Which of the following arteries is most likely occluded in this patient?","o":["A. Middle Colic Artery & Superior Epigastric Artery","B. Sigmoid Artery & Superior Rectal Artery","C. Left Colic Artery & Superior Sigmoid Artery","D. Right Colic Artery & Peroneal Artery"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Left Colic Artery & Superior Sigmoid Artery","a":"C","why":"<b>Left colic artery and superior sigmoid artery.</b> The 2026 lecture lists these two IMA (inferior mesenteric artery) branches as the supply of the descending colon. Pain in the LLQ (left lower quadrant) fits.<br>A. Middle colic supplies the transverse colon; superior epigastric supplies the abdominal wall.<br>B. Sigmoid and superior rectal: supply the sigmoid colon and rectum, lower down.<br>D. Right colic supplies the ascending colon; peroneal is a leg artery.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 70 (Descending colon)","Small & Large Intestines slide 79 (Inferior mesenteric artery)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI module TA review","sl":138,"f":"D042"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":109,"label":"Sigmoid arteries come from the IMA","slides":[{"src":"sl/G005-85.jpg","cap":"Inferior mesenteric angiogram with the sigmoid arteries labelled","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 85"},{"src":"sl/G012-44.jpg","cap":"Angiogram with arrow on a sigmoid artery","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Abdomen III, slide 44"}],"q":[{"id":"D045-319-1","s":"Multiple small branches arising from the IMA are seen supplying the sigmoid colon. What are these branches called?","o":["A. Jejunal arteries","B. Sigmoid arteries","C. Vasa recta","D. Pelvic splanchnic arteries"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Sigmoid arteries","a":"B","why":"<b>Sigmoid arteries.</b> The IMA (inferior mesenteric artery) gives two or three sigmoid arteries. They run down and left in the sigmoid mesocolon to the sigmoid colon.<br>A. Jejunal arteries: SMA (superior mesenteric artery) branches to the jejunum.<br>C. Vasa recta: the small straight end-vessels into the bowel wall, not named IMA branches.<br>D. Pelvic splanchnic arteries: do not exist; the pelvic splanchnics are nerves.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 73 (Sigmoid colon)","Radiology Abd III slide 44 (Sigmoid Artery)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":319,"f":"D045"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":109,"label":"Superior rectal artery joins middle rectal arteries","slides":[{"src":"sl/G005-79.jpg","cap":"Superior rectal artery splits along the rectum, joins middle rectal arteries","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 79"},{"src":"sl/F3114-14.jpg","cap":"Inferior mesenteric diagram: superior rectal meets middle and inferior rectal","where":"2025 Middletown clicker, slide 14"}],"q":[{"id":"D045-321-1","s":"A pelvic angiogram shows a vessel supplying the rectum and containing an anastomosis with middle rectal arteries. What artery is shown?","o":["A. Inferior rectal artery","B. Superior rectal artery","C. Middle colic artery","D. Internal pudendal artery"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Superior rectal artery","a":"B","why":"<b>Superior rectal artery.</b> It is the end of the IMA (inferior mesenteric artery). It splits into two branches on the sides of the rectum, and these join the middle rectal arteries.<br>A. Inferior rectal artery: from the internal pudendal artery; supplies the anal canal below the pectinate line.<br>C. Middle colic artery: an SMA (superior mesenteric artery) branch to the transverse colon.<br>D. Internal pudendal artery: the artery of the perineum; it gives the inferior rectal artery.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 79 (Inferior mesenteric artery)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":321,"f":"D045"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":110,"label":"Behind sigmoid mesocolon apex: ureter, iliac split","slides":[{"src":"sl/G005-73.jpg","cap":"Sigmoid mesocolon; left ureter and common iliac division lie behind it","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 73"}],"q":[{"id":"F0008-147-1","s":"A 37-year-old male comes into the clinic with an abdominal lump and pain in the left hypogastric region. He mentions the lump seems to have been developing for the past four months. A CT scan shows a tumor of the sigmoid colon. Which of the following structures, deep to the apex of the sigmoid mesocolon, could be at risk during the excising of the tumor?","o":["A. Testicular artery","B. Inferior mesenteric artery","C. Obturator artery","D. Superior rectal artery","E. External iliac artery"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"External iliac artery","a":"E","why":"<b>External iliac artery.</b> Deep to the apex of the root of the sigmoid mesocolon lie the left ureter and the spot where the left common iliac artery splits into internal and external iliac arteries. Both are at risk when the sigmoid is removed.<br>A. Testicular artery: runs down on the psoas, lateral to this spot.<br>B. Inferior mesenteric artery: arises much higher, at L3 (third lumbar vertebra).<br>C. Obturator artery: deep in the pelvis, on the side wall.<br>D. Superior rectal artery: runs inside the mesocolon, not behind its apex.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 73 (Sigmoid colon)","Small & Large Intestines slide 72 (Sigmoid colon)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":147,"f":"F0008"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":110,"label":"Diverticulitis: inflamed sigmoid outpouchings","slides":[{"src":"sl/G005-94.jpg","cap":"Diverticulosis versus diverticulitis: colon outpouchings, inflamed in diverticulitis","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 94"},{"src":"sl/G005-95.jpg","cap":"Diverticula in cross-section and on colonoscopy","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 95"}],"q":[{"id":"F3907-23-1","s":"A 75 yo man presents to your clinic with lower left quadrant abdominal pain and fever. He has no significant past medical history except for constipation. You perform a colonoscopy and find a condition that is most common in the sigmoid colon and characterized by increased luminal pressure causing an ‘outpocket’ of the intestinal lumen. It seems that this outpocketing has become infected. Which of the following diseases have you correctly diagnosed in this patient?","o":["A. SMA syndrome","B. Ulcerative colitis","C. Crohn's disease","D. Appendicitis","E. Diverticulitis"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Diverticulitis","a":"E","why":"<b>Diverticulitis.</b> Diverticula are outpouchings of the colon wall, most common in the sigmoid colon, pushed out by high pressure from constipation. When one gets infected, you get LLQ (left lower quadrant) pain and fever.<br>A. SMA (superior mesenteric artery) syndrome: duodenum squeezed; pain after meals.<br>B. Ulcerative colitis: autoimmune, rectum and sigmoid; no outpouchings.<br>C. Crohn's disease: autoimmune, mouth to anus, usually RLQ (right lower quadrant) pain.<br>D. Appendicitis: RLQ pain.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 93 (Diverticulosis)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA weekly","n":"10-06-2022 TA session (foregut viscera)","sl":23,"f":"F3907"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":111,"label":"Marginal artery of Drummond: main SMA–IMA link","slides":[{"src":"sl/G005-81.jpg","cap":"Marginal artery of Drummond along the colon, joining superior and inferior mesenteric","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 81"},{"src":"sl/G005-80.jpg","cap":"Marginal artery labelled along the whole colon","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 80"}],"q":[{"id":"D042-149-1","s":"Bonus Question: What provides the main collateral circulation between the SMA and IMA?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Marginal artery","a":null,"why":"<b>Marginal artery (of Drummond).</b> It runs along the inner (mesenteric) edge of the whole colon. It links the colic branches of the SMA (superior mesenteric artery) with those of the IMA (inferior mesenteric artery), and it is almost always present.<br>Common mix-up: the arc of Riolan also links the SMA and IMA, but it is often absent and runs near the root of the mesentery, so it is not the main collateral.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 81 (Marginal Artery)","Small & Large Intestines slide 82 (Arc of Riolan)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI module TA review","sl":149,"f":"D042"}]},{"id":"F3907-18-1","s":"It is the morning of the anatomy exam and a fellow students runs into you in the hallway and is visibly upset. You ask “what is bothering you?” to which they reply “I cannot remember the name of the really big artery that is an anastomosis between the terminal branches of the SMA and the IMA is! I even know it’s almost always present!” You quickly reassure them that they’re thinking of the…","o":["A. Middle colic artery","B. Marginal artery of drummond","C. Arc of riolan","D. Ileocolic artery","E. Sigmoid arteries"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Marginal artery of drummond","a":"B","why":"<b>Marginal artery of Drummond.</b> It joins the terminal branches of the SMA (superior mesenteric artery) and IMA (inferior mesenteric artery) along the bowel edge, and it is almost always present.<br>A. Middle colic artery: one SMA branch, not the link itself.<br>C. Arc of Riolan: links middle colic to left colic near the root of the mesentery, and is often absent.<br>D. Ileocolic artery: an SMA branch to the cecum.<br>E. Sigmoid arteries: IMA branches to the sigmoid colon.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 81 (Marginal Artery)","Small & Large Intestines slide 82 (Arc of Riolan)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA weekly","n":"10-06-2022 TA session (foregut viscera)","sl":18,"f":"F3907"}],"t":1},{"id":"D045-307-1","s":"A surgeon notes that perfusion to the colon is maintained after ligation of a colic artery due to a vessel running along the bowel margin. What artery is this?","o":["A. Sigmoid artery","B. Middle colic artery","C. Marginal artery of Drummond","D. Appendicular artery"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Marginal artery of Drummond","a":"C","why":"<b>Marginal artery of Drummond.</b> It runs along the bowel edge and joins all the colic arteries into one chain, so tying one colic artery does not starve that segment.<br>A. Sigmoid artery: one IMA (inferior mesenteric artery) branch; it feeds the chain but is not the chain.<br>B. Middle colic artery: one SMA (superior mesenteric artery) branch, same idea.<br>D. Appendicular artery: an end artery to the appendix only.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 81 (Marginal Artery)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":307,"f":"D045"}],"t":1},{"id":"F1156-12-1","s":"A 45 year old transitioning male presents to your clinic complaining of abdominal pain. They have a past history of type 1 diabetes, as well as a family history of high cholesterol. They say that they haven’t been going to the bathroom as much recently and after searching on WebMD they are convinced that they cut off all circulation to their transverse colon, especially because the pain is in the epigastric region of the abdomen. After assuring them that sometimes WebMD isn’t a great tool for self-diagnosing, you tell them that ischemia of the transverse colon is unlikely because:","o":["A. It shares an anastomoses with the celiac trunk","B. It is directly connected to the ileocolic artery as well","C. The transverse colon is retroperitoneal which means it gets additional blood from venous return","D. The marginal artery of Drummond connects most of the large intestine which gives extensive anastomoses to all of the colon"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"The marginal artery of Drummond connects most of the large intestine which gives extensive anastomoses to all of the colon","a":"D","why":"<b>Marginal artery of Drummond.</b> It links the colic arteries of the SMA (superior mesenteric artery) and IMA (inferior mesenteric artery) along the whole colon. One blocked vessel rarely starves the transverse colon.<br>A. Celiac trunk: no direct link to the transverse colon; its link to the SMA is at the duodenum.<br>B. Ileocolic artery: reaches the cecum, not the transverse colon directly.<br>C. Wrong twice: the transverse colon is intraperitoneal, and veins carry blood away, not in.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 81 (Marginal Artery)","Small & Large Intestines slide 67 (Transverse colon)"],"src":[{"t":"? TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":12,"f":"F1156"}]},{"id":"F3901-143-1","s":"You are in dissection lab and are doing a prosection of the large intestine. You are having trouble finding the right colic artery in the body, but you do not see a branch that has been cut, so you assume the cadaver has a variation and does not have a right colic artery. If this cadaver is missing the right colic artery, what is the best answer for how was the ascending colon able to be perfused?","o":["A. Branches of the middle colic artery supplied the ascending colon","B. You must be incorrect and there is no way this is possible because blood vessels are always the same as the textbook","C. Branches of the ileocolic artery supplied the ascending colon","D. Sigmoidal branches supplied the ascending colon","E. The marginal artery allowed for blood to supply all areas of the large intestine through a large anastomosis"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"The marginal artery allowed for blood to supply all areas of the large intestine through a large anastomosis","a":"E","why":"<b>The marginal artery.</b> It links the ileocolic, right colic, middle colic, left colic and sigmoid arteries into one chain along the colon. So the ascending colon still fills when the right colic is missing.<br>A. Middle colic branches can help, but only through the marginal artery; E is the fuller answer.<br>B. Wrong: arterial patterns vary a lot between bodies.<br>C. Ileocolic branches can help, again through the marginal artery.<br>D. Sigmoid branches come from the IMA (inferior mesenteric artery), far away on the left.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 81 (Marginal Artery)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":143,"f":"F3901"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":112,"label":"Marginal artery built from colic branches only","slides":[{"src":"sl/G005-80.jpg","cap":"Marginal artery fed by ileocolic, right, middle, left colic and sigmoid arteries","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 80"},{"src":"sl/F3114-17.jpg","cap":"Colon anastomoses: mesenteric colic branches versus internal iliac rectal branches","where":"2025 Middletown clicker, slide 17"}],"q":[{"id":"F3114-54-1","s":"What is the marginal artery? Tributaries of what two arteries create the marginal artery? What is the course of the marginal artery?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"An anastomosis that connects all of the individual arteries of the colon; formed by branches of the superior and inferior mesenteric arteries; runs from the ileocolic artery all the way around to the sigmoidal branches.","a":null,"why":"<b>A continuous artery along the colon's mesenteric edge, built from branches of the SMA (superior mesenteric artery) and IMA (inferior mesenteric artery).</b> SMA feeders: ileocolic, right colic, middle colic. IMA feeders: left colic and sigmoid arteries. It runs from the ileocolic around the colon to the sigmoid branches.<br>Common mix-up: the middle and inferior rectal arteries (from the internal iliac system) are not part of it.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 81 (Marginal Artery)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"Small & large intestines interactive","sl":54,"f":"F3114"}]},{"id":"F3114-53-1","s":"A 27-year-old male is rushed to the emergency room complaining of pain and discomfort located on the left side of his abdomen. An arteriogram shows a blockage of the IMA. He is rushed to the OR before the descending colon becomes necrotic. The marginal artery has been able to keep the descending artery supplied with minimum blood supply. Which of the following arteries is not a tributary to the marginal artery?","o":["A. Right colic","B. Left colic","C. Ileocolic","D. Middle colic","E. Middle rectal"],"g":["E"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Middle colic","a":"D","why":"<b>Middle rectal.</b> The marginal artery is made from the colic branches: ileocolic, right colic and middle colic from the SMA (superior mesenteric artery), plus left colic and sigmoid from the IMA (inferior mesenteric artery). The middle rectal comes from the internal iliac and supplies the rectum.<br>A. Right colic: feeds it.<br>B. Left colic: feeds it.<br>C. Ileocolic: feeds its start.<br>D. Middle colic: a major feeder (an older key wrongly chose this).","ex":"","fl":["The marginal artery is the arcade formed by the colic branches of the SMA (ileocolic, right colic, middle colic) and IMA (left colic, sigmoid) (G005:81; the source's own slides 11, 14, 55). The middle colic is a major contributor. The middle rectal is a branch of the internal iliac and is not part of the marginal artery, so E is the one that is NOT a contributor."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 81 (Marginal Artery)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"Small & large intestines interactive","sl":53,"f":"F3114"},{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":363,"f":"F3903"},{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":318,"f":"F1180"},{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":257,"f":"F1178"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":112,"label":"Arc of Riolan: SMA–IMA link at splenic flexure","slides":[{"src":"sl/G005-82.jpg","cap":"Arc of Riolan: short loop, middle colic to left colic, near mesentery root","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 82"},{"src":"sl/G005-83.jpg","cap":"Arc of Riolan matters when superior or inferior mesenteric artery is blocked","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 83"}],"q":[{"id":"D045-296-1","s":"A patient with ischemia at the splenic flexure is found to have collateral flow between the SMA and IMA. Which artery forms this connection?","o":["A. Arc of Riolan","B. Right gastric artery","C. Inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery","D. Gastroduodenal artery"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Arc of Riolan","a":"A","why":"<b>Arc of Riolan.</b> It is a short loop near the root of the mesentery that joins the middle colic, a branch of the SMA (superior mesenteric artery), to the left colic, a branch of the IMA (inferior mesenteric artery). It matters most at the splenic flexure, where the two territories meet.<br>B. Right gastric: supplies the lesser curvature of the stomach.<br>C. Inferior pancreaticoduodenal: links the SMA with the celiac trunk at the duodenum.<br>D. Gastroduodenal: a celiac-side branch behind the first part of the duodenum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 82 (Arc of Riolan)","Small & Large Intestines slide 83 (arc of Riolan)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":296,"f":"D045"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":112,"label":"Blocked IMA or SMA: collateral from the other","slides":[{"src":"sl/G005-83.jpg","cap":"Arc of Riolan: superior–inferior mesenteric bypass in occlusion or stenosis","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 83"},{"src":"sl/G005-81.jpg","cap":"Marginal artery links superior and inferior mesenteric territories","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 81"}],"q":[{"id":"F3903-383-1","s":"A 70-year-old man is admitted to the emergency department with severe diarrhea. An arteriogram reveals 90% blockage at the origin of the inferior mesenteric artery from the aorta. Which of the following arteries would most likely provide collateral supply to the descending colon?","o":["A. Left gastroepiploic artery","B. Middle colic artery","C. Sigmoid artery","D. Splenic artery","E. Superior rectal artery"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Middle colic artery","a":"B","why":"<b>Middle colic artery.</b> With the IMA (inferior mesenteric artery) blocked, SMA (superior mesenteric artery) blood flows from the middle colic through the marginal artery and arc of Riolan into the left colic territory, reaching the descending colon.<br>A. Left gastroepiploic: supplies the greater curvature of the stomach.<br>C. Sigmoid artery: an IMA branch, cut off by the same block.<br>D. Splenic artery: supplies the spleen, pancreas and stomach.<br>E. Superior rectal artery: also an IMA branch.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 81 (Marginal Artery)","Small & Large Intestines slide 67 (Transverse colon)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":383,"f":"F3903"},{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":311,"f":"F1180"},{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":174,"f":"F3902"},{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":117,"f":"F0008"},{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":250,"f":"F1178"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3900-36-1","s":"In cases of proximal occlusion of the superior mesenteric artery (SMA), blood flow to the transverse colon can be partially maintained through anastomoses. Which artery contributes to this collateral circulation?","o":["A. Inferior mesenteric artery (IMA)","B. Celiac artery","C. Inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery","D. Middle colic artery"],"g":["A"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Middle colic artery","a":"D","why":"<b>Inferior mesenteric artery (IMA).</b> With the SMA (superior mesenteric artery) blocked near its origin, the transverse colon must get blood from outside the SMA. The IMA sends it up through the left colic, the marginal artery and the arc of Riolan.<br>B. Celiac artery: its link to the SMA is the arcade around the duodenum, not the colon.<br>C. Inferior pancreaticoduodenal: an SMA branch in that duodenal arcade.<br>D. Middle colic: an SMA branch beyond the block; it receives the collateral flow.","ex":"","fl":["With the SMA blocked proximally, the middle colic artery (an SMA branch) is itself downstream of the block and is the vessel being refilled; the collateral SOURCE for the transverse colon is the IMA via the left colic artery and the marginal artery/arc of Riolan (G005:81-83). Among the options only A (IMA) supplies collateral flow. (Pancreaticoduodenal collaterals from the celiac reach the proximal SMA territory, not the transverse colon.)"],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 81 (Marginal Artery)","Small & Large Intestines slide 67 (Transverse colon)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":36,"f":"F3900"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":113,"label":"Absorbed fat enters lacteals in villi","slides":[{"src":"sl/G005-52.jpg","cap":"Lacteals in villi absorb fat as chyle and drain to mesenteric nodes","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 52"}],"q":[{"id":"D045-324-1","s":"Fat absorbed from the small intestine enters which initial lymphatic structure?","o":["A. Paracolic nodes","B. Lacteals","C. Cisterna chyli","D. Thoracic duct"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Lacteals","a":"B","why":"<b>Lacteals.</b> These blind lymph vessels in the center of each villus take up absorbed fat as milky <b>chyle</b>.<br>A. Paracolic nodes: lymph nodes along the colon.<br>C. Cisterna chyli: a later stop, after the mesenteric nodes.<br>D. Thoracic duct: the last channel, carrying chyle to the neck veins.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 52 (Lymphatics)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":324,"f":"D045"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":113,"label":"Jejunal lymph → superior mesenteric nodes → cisterna chyli","slides":[{"src":"sl/G005-52.jpg","cap":"Small-bowel lymph: mesenteric nodes, superior mesenteric nodes, intestinal trunk, cisterna chyli","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 52"},{"src":"sl/G005-53.jpg","cap":"Superior mesenteric nodes, intestinal trunk, cisterna chyli and thoracic duct","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 53"}],"q":[{"id":"D041-233-1","s":"Lymph from the jejunum drains primarily into which of the following lymph node groups before reaching the cisterna chyli?","o":["A. Inferior mesenteric nodes","B. Superior mesenteric nodes","C. Internal iliac nodes","D. Celiac nodes","E. Para-aortic lumbar nodes"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Superior mesenteric nodes","a":"B","why":"<b>Superior mesenteric nodes.</b> Jejunal lymph passes juxta-intestinal and mesenteric nodes, then the <b>superior mesenteric nodes</b> at the root of the SMA (superior mesenteric artery). From there the intestinal trunk carries it to the cisterna chyli.<br>A. Inferior mesenteric nodes: drain the hindgut.<br>C. Internal iliac nodes: drain pelvic organs.<br>D. Celiac nodes: drain foregut organs such as the stomach.<br>E. Para-aortic (lumbar) nodes: drain kidneys, gonads and body wall.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 52 (Lymphatics)","Small & Large Intestines slide 51 (Epiploic nodes: nodes on the gut)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":233,"f":"D041"}]},{"id":"D045-326-1","s":"Lymph from the jejunum travels through mesenteric lymph nodes before draining into which major structure?","o":["A. Azygos vein","B. Portal vein","C. Cisterna chyli","D. Superior vena cava"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Cisterna chyli","a":"C","why":"<b>Cisterna chyli.</b> Mesenteric nodes drain to superior mesenteric nodes, then by the intestinal trunk into the <b>cisterna chyli</b>, the lymph sac at the start of the thoracic duct.<br>A. Azygos vein: a vein of the back chest wall.<br>B. Portal vein: carries absorbed nutrients in <b>blood</b>, not lymph.<br>D. SVC (superior vena cava): the thoracic duct ends at the left subclavian-jugular junction, not in the SVC.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 52 (Lymphatics)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":326,"f":"D045"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":113,"label":"Duodenal lymph → celiac and superior mesenteric nodes","slides":[{"src":"sl/G005-37.jpg","cap":"Pancreaticoduodenal, pyloric, superior mesenteric and celiac lymph nodes","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 37"},{"src":"sl/G005-51.jpg","cap":"Colic node groups: epicolic, paracolic, ileocolic, middle colic, mesenteric","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 51"}],"q":[{"id":"F1156-90-1","s":"To where do the duodenum lymphatics drain into?","o":["A. Celiac & Superior mesenteric","B. Superior mesenteric & Inferior mesenteric","C. Inferior mesenteric & Celiac","D. Inguinal & Superior mesenteric"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Celiac & Superior mesenteric","a":"A","why":"<b>Celiac and superior mesenteric nodes.</b> Duodenal lymph follows its arteries. It passes pancreaticoduodenal and pyloric nodes, then reaches <b>celiac</b> nodes (upper duodenum) and <b>superior mesenteric</b> nodes (lower duodenum).<br>B. Superior + inferior mesenteric: inferior mesenteric nodes drain the hindgut (distal transverse colon to upper rectum).<br>C. Inferior mesenteric + celiac: inferior mesenteric nodes are hindgut nodes.<br>D. Inguinal + superior mesenteric: inguinal nodes drain the leg, perineum and lower anal canal.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 36 (duodenum)"],"src":[{"t":"? TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":90,"f":"F1156"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3901-189-1","s":"Where do the intestines drain their lymph? Duodenum → ? / Cecum + Appendix → ? / Ascending Colon → ?","o":["A. Ileocolic nodes","B. Pancreaticoduodenal, Pyloric, SMA and Celiac nodes","C. Epicolic and Paracolic nodes"],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Duodenum -> B (Pancreaticoduodenal, Pyloric, SMA and Celiac nodes); Cecum + Appendix -> A (Ileocolic nodes); Ascending Colon -> C (Epicolic and Paracolic nodes)","a":null,"why":"<b>Duodenum → B; cecum + appendix → A; ascending colon → C.</b> Lymph follows the arteries. The duodenum drains to <b>pancreaticoduodenal and pyloric</b> nodes, then <b>celiac and superior mesenteric</b> nodes. The cecum and appendix (ileocolic artery) drain to <b>ileocolic</b> nodes. The ascending colon drains to <b>epicolic and paracolic</b> nodes on and beside its wall.<br>Common mix-up: epicolic and paracolic nodes are the first stop for every part of the colon; ileocolic nodes belong to the cecum and appendix.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 36 (duodenum)","Small & Large Intestines slide 61 (Cecum and Vermiform appendix)","Small & Large Intestines slide 65 (Ascending colon)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":189,"f":"F3901"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":114,"label":"Descending/sigmoid lymph → intermediate colic → inferior mesenteric nodes","slides":[{"src":"sl/G005-75.jpg","cap":"Descending and sigmoid lymph: epicolic, paracolic, intermediate colic, inferior mesenteric","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 75"},{"src":"sl/G005-51.jpg","cap":"Colic lymph node groups from gut wall to inferior mesenteric nodes","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 51"}],"q":[{"id":"F1180-315-1","s":"Which of the following is the correct order of lymphatic drainage from the descending/sigmoid colon?","o":["A. Paracolic & epicolic, intermediate colic, inferior mesenteric","B. Paracolic & epicolic, intermediate colic, ileocolic, superior mesenteric","C. Paracolic & epicolic, intermediate colic, superior mesenteric, celiac","D. Paracolic & epicolic, ileocolic, celiac"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Paracolic & epicolic, intermediate colic, inferior mesenteric","a":"A","why":"<b>A.</b> Descending and sigmoid colon lymph runs <b>epicolic and paracolic</b> nodes → <b>intermediate colic</b> nodes (along the left colic and sigmoid arteries) → <b>inferior mesenteric</b> nodes. It follows the IMA (inferior mesenteric artery).<br>B. Ileocolic and superior mesenteric: the route for the cecum and ascending colon.<br>C. Superior mesenteric then celiac: a midgut-foregut route, not hindgut.<br>D. Ileocolic then celiac: not a colon pathway.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 51 (Epiploic nodes: nodes on the gut)","Small & Large Intestines slide 68 (Transverse colon)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":315,"f":"F1180"},{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":254,"f":"F1178"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":114,"label":"Colon lymph: epicolic/paracolic first, transverse to middle colic","slides":[{"src":"sl/G005-51.jpg","cap":"Colon node chain: epiploic, paracolic, intermediate colic, principal nodes","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 51"},{"src":"sl/G005-76.jpg","cap":"Colon lymph nodes colour-coded, including middle colic and left colic nodes","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 76"}],"q":[{"id":"D045-328-1","s":"Cancer in the descending colon would first metastasize to which lymph nodes?","o":["A. Epicolic and paracolic nodes","B. Celiac nodes","C. Hepatic nodes","D. Apical axillary nodes"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Epicolic and paracolic nodes","a":"A","why":"<b>Epicolic and paracolic nodes.</b> Colon lymph moves in steps: epicolic nodes on the bowel wall → paracolic nodes along the marginal artery → intermediate colic nodes along the colic arteries → inferior mesenteric nodes. Cancer reaches the first step first.<br>B. Celiac nodes: drain the foregut (stomach, liver, pancreas).<br>C. Hepatic nodes: drain the liver and gallbladder area.<br>D. Apical axillary nodes: drain the arm and breast.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 65 (Ascending colon)","Small & Large Intestines slide 51 (Epiploic nodes: nodes on the gut)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":328,"f":"D045"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3901-191-1","s":"Where do the intestines drain their lymph? (more...sorry) Transverse Colon → ? / Sigmoid Colon → ? / Descending Colon → ?","o":["A. Epicolic and Paracolic nodes → Intermediate colic → and finally IMA nodes","B. Middle colic nodes Epicolic and Paracolic nodes","C. Epicolic and Paracolic nodes"],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Transverse Colon -> Middle colic nodes; Sigmoid Colon -> Epicolic and Paracolic nodes; Descending Colon -> Epicolic and Paracolic nodes → Intermediate colic → and finally IMA nodes","a":null,"why":"<b>Transverse colon → middle colic nodes. Descending and sigmoid colon → epicolic and paracolic nodes → intermediate colic nodes → inferior mesenteric nodes.</b> Lymph follows the arteries. Transverse colon lymph runs along the middle colic artery. Left-colon lymph runs along the IMA (inferior mesenteric artery) branches.<br>Common mix-up: the sigmoid and descending colon use the same chain. The sigmoid answer gives only the first stop; the descending answer gives the full route.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 68 (Transverse colon)","Small & Large Intestines slide 75 (Descending colon and Sigmoid colon)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":191,"f":"F3901"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":115,"label":"Jejunum/ileum parasympathetics: posterior vagal trunk","slides":[{"src":"sl/G005-54.jpg","cap":"Superior mesenteric plexus; jejunum and ileum parasympathetics from posterior vagal trunk","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 54"}],"q":[{"id":"D045-331-1","s":"Parasympathetic innervation to the jejunum and ileum arises from which source?","o":["A. Pelvic splanchnic nerves","B. Anterior vagal trunk","C. Posterior vagal trunks","D. Lumbar splanchnic nerves"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Posterior vagal trunks","a":"C","why":"<b>Posterior vagal trunk.</b> The parasympathetic fibers to the jejunum and ileum come from the posterior vagal trunk and reach the gut in the superior mesenteric plexus.<br>A. Pelvic splanchnic nerves: parasympathetic to the hindgut (distal transverse colon onward), not the small intestine.<br>B. Anterior vagal trunk: goes mainly to the stomach and liver; the lecture names the posterior trunk.<br>D. Lumbar splanchnic nerves: sympathetic, to the hindgut and pelvis.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 54 (Innervation)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":331,"f":"D045"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":115,"label":"Jejunum/ileum sympathetics: T8–T10 spinal segments","slides":[{"src":"sl/G005-54.jpg","cap":"Sympathetics from T8–T10 segments via greater, lesser, least splanchnic nerves","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 54"}],"q":[{"id":"D045-333-1","s":"Sympathetic pain fibers from the jejunum and ileum originate from which spinal levels?","o":["A. T4–T6","B. T8–T10","C. T12–L1","D. L2–L4"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"T8–T10","a":"B","why":"<b>T8–T10 (thoracic spinal cord segments eight to ten).</b> Sympathetic fibers to the jejunum and ileum start here and run in the greater, lesser and least splanchnic nerves. That is why small-bowel pain is felt around the umbilicus (the T10 skin band).<br>A. T4–T6: too high; these levels do not serve the midgut.<br>C. T12–L1 (twelfth thoracic to first lumbar): too low; closer to hindgut and kidney levels.<br>D. L2–L4 (second to fourth lumbar): sympathetic outflow stops at L2, so L3–L4 send none.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 54 (Innervation)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":333,"f":"D045"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":115,"label":"Vagal fibers synapse in the enteric plexuses","slides":[{"src":"sl/G005-54.jpg","cap":"Vagal preganglionic fibers end on intrinsic enteric neurons in the gut wall","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 54"},{"src":"sl/G002-12.jpg","cap":"Gut wall: submucosal (Meissner) and myenteric (Auerbach) plexuses labelled","where":"2026 Middletown clicker, slide 12"}],"q":[{"id":"F1228-163-1","s":"You are running a science experiment on humans to study the innervation to jejunum and ileum. What would you have to cut to destroy parasympathetic innervation to the small intestine?","o":["A. Preganglionic fibers from the phrenic nerve","B. Myenteric and submucosal plexus","C. Celiac and superior mesenteric ganglia","D. Preganglionic fibers from T5-T9"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Myenteric and submucosal plexus","a":"B","why":"<b>Myenteric and submucosal plexuses.</b> Vagal (parasympathetic) fibers are preganglionic. They synapse on postganglionic neurons inside the gut wall, in these two enteric plexuses. Destroy them and parasympathetic control is gone.<br>A. Phrenic nerve: supplies the diaphragm; it carries no gut parasympathetics.<br>C. Celiac and superior mesenteric ganglia: sympathetic synapse sites; vagal fibers pass through without synapsing.<br>D. T5–T9 (thoracic segments five to nine) fibers: sympathetic outflow in the greater splanchnic nerve.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 54 (Innervation)"],"src":[{"t":"2019 review","n":"old Exam 2 review: abdomen part only","sl":163,"f":"F1228"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":116,"label":"Hindgut parasympathetics: pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2–S4)","slides":[{"src":"sl/G005-78.jpg","cap":"Pelvic splanchnic nerves from sacral cord versus thoracic and lumbar splanchnics","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 78"},{"src":"sl/G008-13.jpg","cap":"Splanchnic nerve types: only pelvic splanchnics are parasympathetic","where":"2026 lecture: Autonomic NS, slide 13"}],"q":[{"id":"D041-236-1","s":"Parasympathetic innervation to the hindgut (distal 1/3 of transverse colon through rectum) is supplied by which nerves?","o":["A. Vagus nerves","B. Pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2–S4)","C. Lumbar splanchnic nerves","D. Glossopharyngeal nerve"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2–S4)","a":"B","why":"<b>Pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2–S4, sacral segments two to four).</b> They carry the parasympathetic supply of the hindgut, from the distal one-third of the transverse colon to the rectum.<br>A. Vagus nerves: parasympathetic only to the foregut and midgut.<br>C. Lumbar splanchnic nerves: sympathetic to the hindgut.<br>D. Glossopharyngeal nerve: head and neck only (for example, the parotid gland).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 77 (Descending colon and sigmoid colon)","ANS slide 13 (Splanchnic nerve: nerve that conveys innervation to and from the viscera of the )"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":236,"f":"D041"}]},{"id":"D042-159-1","s":"A surgeon is performing a resection of the sigmoid colon. To prevent postoperative autonomic dysfunction, they carefully avoid injury to nerves providing parasympathetic innervation to this region. Which parasympathetic nerves primarily supply the sigmoid colon?","o":["A. Vagus nerves","B. Greater splanchnic nerves","C. Lesser splanchnic nerves","D. Pelvic splanchnic nerves","E. Least splanchnic nerves"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Pelvic splanchnic nerves","a":"D","why":"<b>Pelvic splanchnic nerves.</b> They are the only splanchnic nerves that carry parasympathetic fibers, from S2–S4 (sacral segments two to four). They supply the sigmoid colon.<br>A. Vagus nerves: parasympathetic, but they stop at the midgut.<br>B. Greater splanchnic nerves: sympathetic.<br>C. Lesser splanchnic nerves: sympathetic.<br>E. Least splanchnic nerves: sympathetic.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 77 (Descending colon and sigmoid colon)","ANS slide 13 (Splanchnic nerve: nerve that conveys innervation to and from the viscera of the )"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI module TA review","sl":159,"f":"D042"}]},{"id":"D045-335-1","s":"Loss of parasympathetic regulation of the sigmoid colon would most likely result from injury to which nerves?","o":["A. Greater splanchnic nerves","B. Posterior vagal trunk","C. Pelvic splanchnic nerves","D. Lumbar splanchnic nerves"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Pelvic splanchnic nerves","a":"C","why":"<b>Pelvic splanchnic nerves.</b> The sigmoid colon is hindgut. Its parasympathetic supply comes from S2–S4 (sacral segments two to four) through the pelvic splanchnic nerves and the inferior hypogastric plexus.<br>A. Greater splanchnic nerves: sympathetic, to the foregut.<br>B. Posterior vagal trunk: parasympathetic to the midgut, which ends before the distal transverse colon.<br>D. Lumbar splanchnic nerves: sympathetic, to the hindgut.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 77 (Descending colon and sigmoid colon)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":335,"f":"D045"}],"t":1},{"id":"F1228-167-1","s":"You’re late to class, but in dire need to empty your colon. To speed up this process, you try to tell your brain to increase nerve impulses to which nerves to activate your sigmoid colon?","o":["A. Superior hypogastric plexus","B. Pelvic splanchnic nerves","C. Inferior mesenteric plexus","D. Gastrocolic nerve"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Pelvic splanchnic nerves","a":"B","why":"<b>Pelvic splanchnic nerves.</b> Parasympathetic activity drives gut movement and emptying. For the sigmoid colon and rectum, that drive comes from S2–S4 (sacral segments two to four) through the pelvic splanchnic nerves.<br>A. Superior hypogastric plexus: mostly sympathetic fibers.<br>C. Inferior mesenteric plexus: mainly sympathetic (lumbar splanchnic) fibers.<br>D. Gastrocolic nerve: not a real nerve; the gastrocolic reflex is a reflex.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 77 (Descending colon and sigmoid colon)","ANS slide 23 (Parasympathetic Division)"],"src":[{"t":"2019 review","n":"old Exam 2 review: abdomen part only","sl":167,"f":"F1228"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":116,"label":"Hirschsprung: no ganglion cells in myenteric plexus","slides":[{"src":"sl/G001-32.jpg","cap":"Hirschsprung megacolon: missing parasympathetic ganglion cells, dilated colon above","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 32"},{"src":"sl/G005-103.jpg","cap":"Hirschsprung disease: no ganglion cells in the myenteric (Auerbach) plexus","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 103"}],"q":[{"id":"F1228-328-1","s":"8 month old Infant comes to the office with symptoms of constipation, abdominal distention and vomiting. Barium Enema was done which showed prestenotic dilatation. You suspect congenital magacolon. The cause of the disease is what?","o":["A. Irregular development of smooth muscle","B. Absence of enteric ganglion in the lower part of the colon","C. Over stimulation of sympathetic nervous system","D. Over stimulation of parasympathetic nervous system"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Absence of enteric ganglion in the lower part of the colon","a":"B","why":"<b>No enteric ganglion cells in the lower colon.</b> In Hirschsprung disease (congenital megacolon), neural crest cells fail to reach the distal colon, so its myenteric (Auerbach) plexus has no ganglion cells. That segment cannot relax, and the colon above it swells.<br>A. Smooth muscle forms normally; the nerves are the problem.<br>C. Not too much sympathetic drive; enteric neurons are missing.<br>D. Not too much parasympathetic drive; the postganglionic parasympathetic neurons are absent.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 103 (Hirschsprung’s disease)","Small & Large Intestines slide 104 (Hirschsprung’s disease)"],"src":[{"t":"2019 review","n":"old Exam 2 review: abdomen part only","sl":328,"f":"F1228"},{"t":"? tutor review","n":"old Exam 2: abdomen part only","sl":224,"f":"F1243"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G4","u":116,"label":"Colon nerves and arteries, segment by segment","slides":[{"src":"sl/G005-80.jpg","cap":"Colon arteries: ileocolic, right, middle, left colic, sigmoid, marginal","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 80"},{"src":"sl/G005-68.jpg","cap":"Transverse colon nerves: superior mesenteric vs inferior mesenteric plexus","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 68"},{"src":"sl/G005-77.jpg","cap":"Descending and sigmoid colon: pelvic splanchnic parasympathetics, lumbar splanchnic sympathetics","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 77"}],"q":[{"id":"F3114-25-1","s":"What is the sympathetic innervation of the colon? What is the parasympathetic innervation of the colon? What arteries provide blood to the colon? Specify which part of the colon they reach.","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Sympathetic: superior mesenteric ganglia (distal duodenum to proximal 2/3 of transverse colon); inferior mesenteric ganglia (distal 1/3 of transverse colon to superior rectum). Parasympathetic: vagus (distal duodenum to proximal 2/3 transverse colon); pelvic splanchnic nerves (distal 1/3 transverse colon to superior rectum). Arteries: ileocolic – ileocolic junction, cecum, appendix; right colic – ascending colon; middle colic – transverse colon; left colic – descending colon; sigmoid – sigmoid colon.","a":null,"why":"<b>The colon switches supply where the proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon meets the distal one-third.</b> Before that point (midgut): SMA (superior mesenteric artery) branches, the vagus nerve and the superior mesenteric plexus. After it (hindgut): IMA (inferior mesenteric artery) branches, pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2–S4, sacral segments two to four) and lumbar splanchnic nerves through the inferior mesenteric plexus.<br>Common mix-up: the vagus never reaches the descending or sigmoid colon; the pelvic splanchnic nerves do.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 54 (Innervation)","Small & Large Intestines slide 77 (Descending colon and sigmoid colon)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"Small & large intestines interactive","sl":25,"f":"F3114"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G5","u":117,"label":"Liver makes bile; gallbladder stores and concentrates it","slides":[{"src":"sl/G006-8.jpg","cap":"Liver makes bile continuously; gallbladder stores, concentrates, releases it","where":"2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary & Portal Vein, slide 8"},{"src":"sl/G006-4.jpg","cap":"Organ positions: gallbladder fundus under the right costal margin, spleen left","where":"2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary & Portal Vein, slide 4"}],"q":[{"id":"D041-241-1","s":"Which of the following correctly matches the organ with its position and primary function?","o":["A. Spleen – Right upper quadrant; bile production","B. Liver – Left upper quadrant; stores and concentrates bile","C. Gallbladder – Right upper quadrant; stores bile","D. Pancreas – Right lower quadrant; produces amylase and lipas"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Gallbladder – Right upper quadrant; stores bile","a":"C","why":"<b>Gallbladder: right upper quadrant, stores bile.</b> It sits under the right lobe of the liver. It stores and concentrates bile, then squeezes it out when fatty food reaches the duodenum.<br>A. Spleen: left upper quadrant; it filters blood and makes no bile.<br>B. Liver: mostly right upper quadrant; it makes bile but does not store it.<br>D. Pancreas: does make amylase and lipase, but it lies across the upper abdomen behind the stomach, not in the right lower quadrant.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 39 (Gallbladder)","Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 32 (Biliary ducts)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":241,"f":"D041"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3907-29-1","s":"Which of the following is NOT true?","o":["A. Liver produces bile between meals and is stored in the gallbladder","B. Gallbladder concentrates bile by absorbing water and salts","C. Gallbladder lies on the visceral surface of the liver"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Liver produces bile between meals and is stored in the gallbladder","a":"A","why":"<b>A is the false statement.</b> The liver makes bile <b>continuously</b>, not only between meals. What happens between meals is storage: bile backs up into the gallbladder and waits there.<br>B. True: the gallbladder concentrates bile by absorbing water and salts.<br>C. True: the gallbladder lies in a fossa on the visceral (under) surface of the liver.<br>Key idea: the liver makes bile all the time; the gallbladder stores it, concentrates it, and releases it when food reaches the duodenum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 8 (Liver)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA weekly","n":"10-06-2022 TA session (foregut viscera)","sl":29,"f":"F3907"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G5","u":117,"label":"Liver lies deep to right ribs 7–11","slides":[{"src":"sl/G006-5.jpg","cap":"Liver surface projection: deep to right ribs 7–11, mostly right upper quadrant","where":"2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary & Portal Vein, slide 5"},{"src":"sl/F1180-136.jpg","cap":"Rib relations: liver right ribs 7–11, spleen left ribs 9–11","where":"2022 Harlem TA review, slide 136"}],"q":[{"id":"F1180-139-1","s":"Your patient returns for a follow up 2 days after an automobile accident. On your general exam, you notice he has a slight yellow tint to the skin. He mentions to you that he has also noticed a constant upper right quadrant pain, that doesn’t seem to go away with medications or rest. Physical exam of the thorax and abdomen reveals diffuse bruising and tenderness along the right side. Your attending physician wants you to explain what the likely lead to this clinical presentation and you respond:","o":["A. Trauma to the right side, injuring ribs 5-9","B. Trauma to the left side, injuring ribs 7-11","C. Trauma to the right side, injuring ribs 7-11","D. Trauma to the left side, injuring ribs 4-6"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Trauma to the right side, injuring ribs 7-11","a":"C","why":"<b>Right side, ribs 7–11.</b> The liver sits in the right upper quadrant, deep to ribs 7 to 11. Yellow skin (jaundice) plus constant right upper quadrant pain after a right-sided blow points to a liver injury.<br>A. Right side, ribs 5–9: too high; ribs 5 and 6 overlie the lung and pleura, not the liver.<br>B. Left side, ribs 7–11: a left-sided blow hits the stomach and spleen.<br>D. Left side, ribs 4–6: wrong side and too high; this is over the heart and lung.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 5 (The liver lies mainly in the right upper quadrant of the abdomen, where it is pr)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":139,"f":"F1180"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":46,"f":"F3901"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G5","u":118,"label":"Falciform ligament: liver to anterior abdominal wall","slides":[{"src":"sl/G006-12.jpg","cap":"Anterior liver: falciform ligament to abdominal wall, round ligament in its edge","where":"2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary & Portal Vein, slide 12"},{"src":"sl/G001-19.jpg","cap":"Ventral mesentery becomes the falciform ligament plus lesser omentum","where":"2026 lecture: GI Development, slide 19"}],"q":[{"id":"F3907-32-1","s":"Peritoneal ligaments are not true ligaments; however, they consist of a double layer of peritoneum that connects an organ with another organ or to the abdominal wall. The liver originates from the hepatic diverticulum, which sprouts from the foregut into the ventral mesentery. Which part of the ventral mesentery connects the liver to the anterior abdominal wall?","o":["A. Falciform ligament","B. Lesser omentum","C. Hepatogastric ligament","D. Hepatoduodenal ligament"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Falciform ligament","a":"A","why":"<b>Falciform ligament.</b> The ventral mesentery becomes two things: the falciform ligament (liver to the anterior abdominal wall and diaphragm) and the lesser omentum (liver to stomach and duodenum).<br>B. Lesser omentum: joins the liver to the lesser curvature of the stomach and the first part of the duodenum, not to the abdominal wall.<br>C. Hepatogastric ligament: the part of the lesser omentum that goes to the stomach.<br>D. Hepatoduodenal ligament: the part of the lesser omentum that goes to the duodenum; it carries the portal triad.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 11 (Anterior surface of liver)","GI Development slide 19 (Ventral Mesentery)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA weekly","n":"10-06-2022 TA session (foregut viscera)","sl":32,"f":"F3907"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G5","u":118,"label":"Ligamentum teres: remnant of the umbilical vein","slides":[{"src":"sl/G006-12.jpg","cap":"Round ligament (obliterated left umbilical vein) in falciform ligament's free edge","where":"2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary & Portal Vein, slide 12"},{"src":"sl/G006-15.jpg","cap":"Round ligament (umbilical vein) versus ligamentum venosum (ductus venosus)","where":"2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary & Portal Vein, slide 15"}],"q":[{"id":"F1228-203-1","s":"Which of the following is the remnant of the umbilical vein?","o":["A. Falciform Ligament","B. Ligamentum teres hepatis","C. Ligamentum venosum","D. Right triangular ligament"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Ligamentum teres hepatis","a":"B","why":"<b>Ligamentum teres hepatis (round ligament).</b> It is the closed-off left umbilical vein. It runs in the free lower edge of the falciform ligament from the navel to the liver.<br>A. Falciform ligament: a peritoneal fold from the ventral mesentery; it only carries the round ligament in its edge.<br>C. Ligamentum venosum: the remnant of the ductus venosus, the fetal shunt from the umbilical vein to the IVC (inferior vena cava).<br>D. Right triangular ligament: a peritoneal fold at the right end of the coronary ligament.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 15 (Visceral surface of liver)","Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 11 (Anterior surface of liver)"],"src":[{"t":"2019 review","n":"old Exam 2 review: abdomen part only","sl":203,"f":"F1228"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G5","u":118,"label":"Left triangular ligament: falciform plus lesser omentum","slides":[{"src":"sl/G006-14.jpg","cap":"Visceral surface: left triangular ligament at the tip of the left lobe","where":"2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary & Portal Vein, slide 14"},{"src":"sl/G006-13.jpg","cap":"Superior view: falciform splits into coronary ligament, ending as left triangular","where":"2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary & Portal Vein, slide 13"}],"q":[{"id":"F1228-201-1","s":"Which structures form the left triangular ligament?","o":["A. Falciform ligament and greater omentum","B. Ligamentum venosum and lesser omentum","C. Falciform ligament and lesser omentum","D. Ligamentum teres and greater omentum"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Falciform ligament and lesser omentum","a":"C","why":"<b>Falciform ligament and lesser omentum.</b> Near the tip of the left lobe, the left layer of the falciform ligament meets the left layer of the lesser omentum. That fold is the left triangular ligament, tying the left lobe to the diaphragm.<br>A. Greater omentum: hangs from the greater curvature of the stomach; it never reaches the liver.<br>B. Ligamentum venosum: a fibrous cord (old ductus venosus) inside a fissure, not a peritoneal fold.<br>D. Ligamentum teres: the old umbilical vein in the free edge of the falciform ligament.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 11 (Anterior surface of liver)"],"src":[{"t":"2019 review","n":"old Exam 2 review: abdomen part only","sl":201,"f":"F1228"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G5","u":119,"label":"Porta hepatis transmits the portal triad","slides":[{"src":"sl/G006-15.jpg","cap":"Portal triad (portal vein, hepatic artery, bile passages) enters at porta hepatis","where":"2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary & Portal Vein, slide 15"},{"src":"sl/G006-16.jpg","cap":"Portal triad running up the hepatoduodenal ligament to the liver","where":"2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary & Portal Vein, slide 16"}],"q":[{"id":"F3907-34-1","s":"What consists of the porta hepatis?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"The portal triad: portal vein, hepatic artery (proper, right and left branches) and bile passages (hepatic ducts) - plus hepatic nerve plexus and lymphatics","a":null,"why":"<b>The porta hepatis is the doorway for the portal triad.</b> It is a crosswise fissure on the visceral surface of the liver. Through it pass the <b>hepatic portal vein</b>, the <b>hepatic artery proper</b> (right and left branches) and the <b>hepatic ducts</b>, plus nerves and lymphatics.<br>Common mix-up: the hepatic veins do not use the porta hepatis. They leave the back of the liver and empty straight into the IVC (inferior vena cava).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 15 (Visceral surface of liver)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA weekly","n":"10-06-2022 TA session (foregut viscera)","sl":34,"f":"F3907"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G5","u":119,"label":"Quadrate lobe: between gallbladder and round ligament","slides":[{"src":"sl/G006-18.jpg","cap":"Quadrate lobe between gallbladder fossa and round ligament, below porta hepatis","where":"2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary & Portal Vein, slide 18"},{"src":"sl/G006-15.jpg","cap":"Visceral fissures: round ligament, ligamentum venosum, gallbladder fossa, IVC groove","where":"2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary & Portal Vein, slide 15"}],"q":[{"id":"F1228-169-1","s":"The quadrate lobe is bound by:","o":["A. Left lobe, IVC, and ligamentum venosum","B. Gallbladder, right lobe, and IVC","C. Falciform ligament, IVC, and gallbladder","D. Gallbladder, round ligament, and caudate lobe","E. Falciform ligament, caudate process, and IVC"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Gallbladder, round ligament, and caudate lobe","a":"D","why":"<b>Gallbladder, round ligament, and caudate lobe.</b> The quadrate lobe lies between the gallbladder fossa (right) and the round ligament fissure (left). Behind it is the porta hepatis, and just past that is the caudate lobe.<br>A. Left lobe, IVC (inferior vena cava), ligamentum venosum: the IVC and ligamentum venosum frame the caudate lobe.<br>B. Right lobe and IVC: the IVC groove is up at the caudate lobe.<br>C. Falciform ligament and IVC: neither touches the quadrate lobe.<br>E. Caudate process and IVC: caudate-lobe landmarks.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 15 (Visceral surface of liver)","Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 18 (Lobes of Liver)"],"src":[{"t":"2019 review","n":"old Exam 2 review: abdomen part only","sl":169,"f":"F1228"}]},{"id":"F0008-145-1","s":"A 65-year-old man, with a history of cirrhosis of the liver, presents with pain in his upper right hand quadrant. After physical examination, the doctor suspects a tumor in the liver and sends him to a radiologist for a CT scan. The scan confirms a tumor in the quadrate lobe of the liver. What structures border the diseased lobe?","o":["A. Fossa of gall bladder and ligamentum teres hepatis","B. Fossa of gall bladder and ligamentum venosum","C. Ligamentum teres hepatis and groove for inferior vena cava","D. Ligamentum venosum and groove for inferior vena cava","E. Ligamentum venosum and ligamentum teres hepatis"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Fossa of gall bladder and ligamentum teres hepatis","a":"A","why":"<b>Gallbladder fossa and ligamentum teres hepatis.</b> On the visceral surface, the quadrate lobe is the square block between the gallbladder fossa (on its right) and the fissure for the round ligament (on its left). The porta hepatis lies just behind it.<br>B. Ligamentum venosum: borders the caudate lobe, not the quadrate lobe.<br>C. Groove for the IVC (inferior vena cava): borders the caudate lobe.<br>D. Ligamentum venosum and IVC groove: these two frame the caudate lobe.<br>E. Ligamentum venosum and ligamentum teres: together they mark off the left lobe.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 15 (Visceral surface of liver)","Radiology Abd II slide 17 (Quadrate lobe)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":145,"f":"F0008"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G5","u":120,"label":"Hepatoduodenal ligament carries the portal triad","slides":[{"src":"sl/G006-16.jpg","cap":"Lesser omentum: hepatogastric part versus hepatoduodenal part enclosing the portal triad","where":"2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary & Portal Vein, slide 16"}],"q":[{"id":"F1178-269-1","s":"The hepatogastric ligament encloses the portal triad","o":["A. True","B. False"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"False, hepatoduodenal ligament","a":"B","why":"<b>False.</b> The portal triad (portal vein, hepatic artery proper, bile duct) runs in the <b>hepatoduodenal ligament</b>. This is the thick right free edge of the lesser omentum, at the front of the omental foramen.<br>Common mix-up: the hepatogastric ligament is the thin part of the lesser omentum that goes to the stomach. It carries the left and right gastric vessels along the lesser curvature, not the triad.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Peritoneum/Stomach slide 22 (Parts of lesser omentum)","Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 16 (Review of lesser omentum)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":269,"f":"F1178"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G5","u":120,"label":"Liver lymph: hepatic nodes, then celiac nodes","slides":[{"src":"sl/G006-24.jpg","cap":"Liver lymph: hepatic nodes along hepatic artery, phrenic, celiac, left gastric","where":"2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary & Portal Vein, slide 24"},{"src":"sl/G006-23.jpg","cap":"Extra liver lymph routes: phrenic, left gastric, parasternal, abdominal wall","where":"2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary & Portal Vein, slide 23"}],"q":[{"id":"F1180-335-1","s":"Which of the following is incorrect pertaining to lymph drainage of the liver?","o":["A) There are both superficial and deep lymphatic vessels.","B) Some drainage is via vessels passing directly to phrenic nodes.","C) Some drainage is to vessels within the falciform ligament.","D) Hepatic nodes are located in the greater omentum.","E) Metastases spread via the lymph system will eventually reach the celiac nodes"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Hepatic nodes are located in the greater omentum","a":"D","why":"<b>D is false.</b> The hepatic lymph nodes lie along the hepatic vessels and ducts in the <b>lesser omentum</b>, near the porta hepatis. They are not in the greater omentum.<br>A. True: superficial lymphatics run in the capsule; deep ones follow the portal triad and hepatic veins.<br>B. True: the back of the liver drains to phrenic nodes, then posterior mediastinal nodes.<br>C. True: lymph along the falciform ligament goes to parasternal nodes.<br>E. True: hepatic nodes drain to celiac nodes, then to the cisterna chyli.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 22 (Lymphatic drainage)","Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 23 (Lymphatic drainage)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":335,"f":"F1180"},{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":271,"f":"F1178"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G5","u":121,"label":"Bile duct: common hepatic plus cystic duct","slides":[{"src":"sl/G006-40.jpg","cap":"Hepatic ducts, common hepatic duct, cystic duct joining to form bile duct","where":"2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary & Portal Vein, slide 40"},{"src":"sl/G006-35.jpg","cap":"Bile route: right and left hepatic, common hepatic, cystic, bile duct","where":"2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary & Portal Vein, slide 35"}],"q":[{"id":"F1228-207-1","s":"The common bile duct is formed by…","o":["A. Common Hepatic Duct and Pancreatic Duct","B. Cystic Duct and Common Hepatic Duct","C. Cystic Duct and Pancreatic Duct","D. Pancreatic Duct and Cystic Duct"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Cystic Duct and Common Hepatic Duct","a":"B","why":"<b>Cystic duct and common hepatic duct.</b> The common hepatic duct (from the liver) meets the cystic duct (from the gallbladder). Below that junction the tube is the bile duct, also called the common bile duct.<br>A. Common hepatic and pancreatic ducts: the pancreatic duct joins much lower, at the ampulla.<br>C. Cystic and pancreatic ducts: these two never meet.<br>D. Pancreatic and cystic ducts: the same pair as C, reordered; they never meet.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 35 (Biliary ducts)"],"src":[{"t":"2019 review","n":"old Exam 2 review: abdomen part only","sl":207,"f":"F1228"}]},{"id":"D041-245-1","s":"Bile produced in the liver flows through which structure immediately before entering the common bile duct?","o":["A. Cystic duct","B. Right and left hepatic ducts","C. Common hepatic duct","D. Main pancreatic duct"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Common hepatic duct","a":"C","why":"<b>Common hepatic duct.</b> The path is: right and left hepatic ducts, then the common hepatic duct, which is joined by the cystic duct to become the CBD (common bile duct). So bile coming from the liver is in the common hepatic duct just before the CBD.<br>A. Cystic duct: carries bile to and from the gallbladder, not straight from the liver.<br>B. Right and left hepatic ducts: one step earlier; they form the common hepatic duct.<br>D. Main pancreatic duct: meets the CBD at the far end, at the ampulla.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 35 (Biliary ducts)","Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 31 (Biliary ducts)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":245,"f":"D041"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G5","u":121,"label":"Bile duct ends at ampulla, second duodenum","slides":[{"src":"sl/G007-13.jpg","cap":"Bile duct plus main pancreatic duct form hepatopancreatic ampulla at major papilla","where":"2026 lecture: Pancreas & Spleen, slide 13"},{"src":"sl/G005-16.jpg","cap":"Descending duodenum: bile and pancreatic ducts open at the major papilla","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 16"}],"q":[{"id":"F3907-52-1","s":"Which two ducts form the Ampulla of Vater?","o":["A. Pancreatic Duct and Hepatic Bile Duct","B. Cystic Duct and Lymphatic Duct","C. Duodenal Duct and Renal Duct","D. Intestinal Duct and Renal Duct"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Pancreatic Duct and Hepatic Bile Duct","a":"A","why":"<b>Main pancreatic duct and bile duct.</b> They join in the duodenal wall to form the hepatopancreatic ampulla (ampulla of Vater). It opens on the major duodenal papilla in the second part of the duodenum. The sphincter of Oddi surrounds it.<br>B. Cystic duct and lymphatic duct: the cystic duct joins the common hepatic duct, far above; lymph ducts carry no bile.<br>C. Duodenal duct and renal duct: not real structures here.<br>D. Intestinal duct and renal duct: not real structures here.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 35 (Biliary ducts)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA weekly","n":"10-06-2022 TA session (foregut viscera)","sl":52,"f":"F3907"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3902-185-1","s":"A 41-year-old woman is brought to the emergency room because of acute onset of right upper quadrant pain, nausea and vomiting. Prior to going into the operating room, the medical student briefly considers reminding the surgeon it is important to remember that the bile duct:","o":["A. Drains bile into the second part of the duodenum","B. Can be blocked by cancer in the body of the pancreas","C. Joins the main pancreatic duct, which carries hormones","D. Is formed by the union of the left and right hepatic ducts","E. Lies posterior to the portal vein in the right free edge of the lesser omentum"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Drains bile into the second part of the duodenum","a":"A","why":"<b>It drains bile into the second part of the duodenum.</b> The bile duct joins the main pancreatic duct to form the hepatopancreatic ampulla, which opens at the major duodenal papilla.<br>B. The bile duct runs through the <b>head</b> of the pancreas, not the body.<br>C. The main pancreatic duct carries digestive enzymes; hormones go into the blood.<br>D. The right and left hepatic ducts form the common hepatic duct, not the bile duct.<br>E. The bile duct lies <b>in front</b> and to the right; the portal vein is behind.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Small & Large Intestines slide 16 (Descending (second) part of duodenum)","Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 33 (Biliary ducts)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":185,"f":"F3902"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G5","u":121,"label":"Cystic duct stone: cholecystitis without jaundice","slides":[{"src":"sl/G006-40.jpg","cap":"Cystic duct joins common hepatic duct; liver bile bypasses a cystic stone","where":"2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary & Portal Vein, slide 40"},{"src":"sl/G006-35.jpg","cap":"Biliary tree: gallbladder on the cystic duct side branch off the main route","where":"2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary & Portal Vein, slide 35"}],"q":[{"id":"D042-163-1","s":"A 49-year-old woman is admitted to the hospital with abdominal pain. Physical examination reveals an epigastric pain that migrates toward the patient’s right side and posteriorly toward the scapula. Radiographic examination reveals cholecystitis with a large gallstone and no jaundice. Which of the following structures is most likely obstructed by the gallstone?","o":["A. Common bile duct","B. Cystic duct","C. Left hepatic duct","D. Pancreatic duct","E. Hepatopancreatic ampulla","F. Right hepatic duct"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Cystic duct","a":"B","why":"<b>Cystic duct.</b> A stone in the cystic duct traps bile in the gallbladder, which becomes inflamed (cholecystitis). Bile from the liver still flows down the common hepatic duct and bile duct to the gut, so there is <b>no jaundice</b>.<br>A. Common bile duct: blocks all bile, so jaundice appears.<br>C. Left hepatic duct: above the cystic duct, so the gallbladder is not involved.<br>D. Pancreatic duct: gives pancreatitis, not cholecystitis.<br>E. Hepatopancreatic ampulla: blocks bile and pancreatic juice, giving jaundice.<br>F. Right hepatic duct: above the cystic duct, so the gallbladder is not involved.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 35 (Biliary ducts)","Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 39 (Gallbladder)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI module TA review","sl":163,"f":"D042"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G5","u":122,"label":"Gallbladder fundus at right 9th costal cartilage","slides":[{"src":"sl/G006-4.jpg","cap":"Fundus of gallbladder peeking below the right costal margin","where":"2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary & Portal Vein, slide 4"},{"src":"sl/G003-9.jpg","cap":"Transpyloric plane: gallbladder fundus and 9th costal cartilage on same level","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 9"}],"q":[{"id":"F1180-325-1","s":"A 47-year-old female was rushed to the ER due to complaints of excruciating pain in her right upper quadrant. On examination, the physician noted that the patient experienced pain while inhaling and the pain was localized at the junction of the linea semilunaris and the right ninth costal cartilage. Which of the following organs is the most likely culprit for this pain?","o":["A. Duodenum","B. Gall bladder","C. Kidney","D. Liver","E. Transverse colon"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Gall bladder","a":"B","why":"<b>Gallbladder.</b> Its fundus touches the front wall where the right linea semilunaris (outer edge of the rectus sheath) meets the 9th costal cartilage. Pain there that stops a deep breath is Murphy's sign.<br>A. Duodenum: lies deeper, curved around the head of the pancreas near the midline.<br>C. Kidney: behind the peritoneum; its pain is felt in the back and flank.<br>D. Liver: covered by ribs 7–11; this exact spot is the gallbladder fundus.<br>E. Transverse colon: lies lower, hanging across the abdomen.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 39 (Gallbladder)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 9 (Transpyloric planes)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":325,"f":"F1180"},{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":264,"f":"F1178"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G5","u":122,"label":"Gallbladder sensory nerve: right phrenic","slides":[{"src":"sl/G006-37.jpg","cap":"Gallbladder nerves: right phrenic sensory, celiac sympathetic, vagal parasympathetic","where":"2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary & Portal Vein, slide 37"},{"src":"sl/G006-25.jpg","cap":"Liver and biliary nerves: celiac plexus sympathetic, vagal trunks parasympathetic","where":"2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary & Portal Vein, slide 25"}],"q":[{"id":"F1228-209-1","s":"The gallbladder receives sensory information from the...","o":["A. Vagus","B. Celiac Plexus","C. Left Phrenic","D. Right Phrenic"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Right Phrenic","a":"D","why":"<b>Right phrenic nerve.</b> The lecture's nerve figure draws the right phrenic nerve as the gallbladder's sensory nerve. The pattern to learn: sympathetic from the celiac plexus, parasympathetic from the vagus, sensory from the right phrenic.<br>A. Vagus: the parasympathetic supply; it makes the gallbladder contract.<br>B. Celiac plexus: the sympathetic supply. Dull visceral pain fibers also ride back through it, but the course's 'sensory' answer is the right phrenic.<br>C. Left phrenic: supplies the left half of the diaphragm, not the gallbladder.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 25 (Nerve supply)","Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 37 (FIGURE 5.72. Nerves and veins of the liver and biliary system. Nerves are promin)"],"src":[{"t":"2019 review","n":"old Exam 2 review: abdomen part only","sl":209,"f":"F1228"}],"t":1},{"id":"F1228-173-1","s":"Your gallbladder is angry, inflamed, and not having it. You have pain radiating to your shoulder. What nerve or ganglia is responsible for carrying sensory information from your gallbladder?","o":["A. Left recurrent laryngeal","B. Right phrenic","C. Celiac plexus","D. Vagus","E. Inferior mesenteric"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Right phrenic","a":"B","why":"<b>Right phrenic nerve.</b> It carries sensory fibers from the gallbladder and the peritoneum under the right diaphragm. It comes from spinal segments C3 to C5 (third to fifth cervical), which also supply the skin over the shoulder. So the pain is felt in the right shoulder (referred pain).<br>A. Left recurrent laryngeal: supplies the larynx, not the abdomen.<br>C. Celiac plexus: the sympathetic supply.<br>D. Vagus: the parasympathetic supply.<br>E. Inferior mesenteric: hindgut nerves, far from the gallbladder.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 25 (Nerve supply)","Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 37 (FIGURE 5.72. Nerves and veins of the liver and biliary system. Nerves are promin)"],"src":[{"t":"2019 review","n":"old Exam 2 review: abdomen part only","sl":173,"f":"F1228"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G5","u":123,"label":"Calot's triangle: borders and contents","slides":[{"src":"sl/G006-46.jpg","cap":"Calot's triangle: cystic duct, common hepatic duct, liver; contains cystic artery","where":"2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary & Portal Vein, slide 46"},{"src":"sl/G006-42.jpg","cap":"Cystic artery origin and its variations around the cystohepatic triangle","where":"2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary & Portal Vein, slide 42"}],"q":[{"id":"F1180-330-1","s":"What are the contents of Calot’s Triangle","o":["A. Cystic duct, common hepatic duct","B. Cystic artery and Right Hepatic artery","C. cystic duct and cystic artery"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Cystic artery and Right Hepatic artery","a":"B","why":"<b>Cystic artery and right hepatic artery.</b> Calot's (cystohepatic) triangle is bordered by the cystic duct, the common hepatic duct, and the underside of the liver. Inside it run the cystic artery, often the right hepatic artery, and the cystic lymph node.<br>A. Cystic duct and common hepatic duct: these are borders, not contents.<br>C. Cystic duct and cystic artery: the artery is inside, but the cystic duct is a border.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 46 (Calot's triangle)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":330,"f":"F1180"}],"t":1},{"id":"F1228-211-1","s":"Which structure is not included in Calot’s Triangle?","o":["A. Cystic Duct","B. Common Hepatic Duct","C. Cystic Artery","D. Lund’s lymph node","E. Portal Vein"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Portal Vein","a":"E","why":"<b>Portal vein.</b> It lies deeper, at the back of the hepatoduodenal ligament, outside Calot's (cystohepatic) triangle.<br>A. Cystic duct: forms the lower border.<br>B. Common hepatic duct: forms the inner (medial) border.<br>C. Cystic artery: runs inside the triangle.<br>D. Lund's node (the cystic lymph node, also called Mascagni's or Calot's node): sits inside the triangle near the gallbladder neck.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 46 (Calot's triangle)"],"src":[{"t":"2019 review","n":"old Exam 2 review: abdomen part only","sl":211,"f":"F1228"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3902-183-1","s":"You are observing a cholecystectomy on your surgery rotation and the surgeon asks you what we look out for in calot’s triangle, what could you say without looking like you forgot your anatomy?","o":["A. The borders of the triangle are the visceral surface of the liver, common bile duct and common hepatic duct","B. We need to make sure there isn't a rogue branch of the right hepatic artery","C. We should look out for a variation in the common bile duct that forms a border of the triangle","D. This area can be found near the caudate lobe of the liver"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"We need to make sure there isn't a rogue branch of the right hepatic artery","a":"B","why":"<b>Look out for a rogue branch of the right hepatic artery.</b> Calot's (cystohepatic) triangle often holds an aberrant or accessory right hepatic artery near the cystic artery. Cutting the wrong vessel is the classic error.<br>A. Wrong border: the borders are the cystic duct, common hepatic duct, and liver, not the common bile duct.<br>C. The common bile duct is not a border, so its variation is not the point.<br>D. The triangle sits by the gallbladder, between the right and quadrate lobes, not by the caudate lobe.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 46 (Calot's triangle)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":183,"f":"F3902"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3907-40-1","s":"What is the most common surgical procedure that you can think of? How does Calot’s triangle play into this procedure?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Cholecystectomy (gallbladder removal); Calot's (cystohepatic) triangle - bounded by the cystic duct, common hepatic duct and visceral surface of the liver - contains the cystic artery, which must be identified and can be injured during the operation (branching is highly variable).","a":null,"why":"<b>Cholecystectomy (gallbladder removal).</b> The surgeon opens Calot's (cystohepatic) triangle. Its borders are the cystic duct, the common hepatic duct and the underside of the liver. Inside lies the <b>cystic artery</b>, which is clipped and cut. Its branching varies a lot, and the right hepatic artery may loop into the triangle.<br>Common mix-up: the common bile duct is not a border. Careless dissection in this triangle is the top cause of bile duct injury.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 46 (Calot's triangle)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA weekly","n":"10-06-2022 TA session (foregut viscera)","sl":40,"f":"F3907"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G5","u":124,"label":"Cholecystectomy: clip cystic artery from right hepatic","slides":[{"src":"sl/G006-42.jpg","cap":"Cystic artery usually arises from right hepatic artery inside Calot's triangle","where":"2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary & Portal Vein, slide 42"},{"src":"sl/G006-46.jpg","cap":"Calot's triangle with cystic artery branching off the right hepatic artery","where":"2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary & Portal Vein, slide 46"}],"q":[{"id":"F3903-334-1","s":"During a laparoscopic cholecystectomy on a 61-year-old male, which of the following arteries must be clamped to remove the gallbladder safely?","o":["A. Common hepatic","B. Proper hepatic","C. Right hepatic","D. Left hepatic","E. Gastroduodenal"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Right hepatic","a":"C","why":"<b>Right hepatic artery</b> is the best choice here. The vessel actually clipped is the <b>cystic artery</b>, which usually branches off the right hepatic artery inside Calot's (cystohepatic) triangle. The right hepatic artery itself must be found and protected, because it feeds the right liver.<br>A. Common hepatic: a celiac trunk branch, far from the gallbladder.<br>B. Proper hepatic: splits into right and left hepatic before the cystic artery arises.<br>D. Left hepatic: goes to the left liver, away from the gallbladder.<br>E. Gastroduodenal: runs behind the first part of the duodenum.","ex":"","fl":["Key is the best available option, but the stem is flawed: in a cholecystectomy the cystic artery (a branch of the right hepatic, found in the cystohepatic triangle of Calot) is clamped, not the right hepatic artery itself, which also supplies the right liver."],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 36 (Blood supply to the Bile duct)","Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 46 (Calot's triangle)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":334,"f":"F3903"},{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":104,"f":"F0008"}],"t":1},{"id":"F1180-338-1","s":"Which of the following is incorrect pertaining to the gall bladder?","o":["A) Some venous drainage is directly into the visceral surface of the liver.","B) Cholecystitis can result from an impacted gallstone.","C) A gallstone lodged in the cystic duct causes intense spasmodic pain.","D) During cholecystectomy surgeons typically ligate the left hepatic artery.","E) The infundibulum of the gall bladder is a pouch that appears between the neck and the cystic duct in diseased states."],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"During cholecystectomy surgeons typically ligate the left hepatic artery","a":"D","why":"<b>D is false.</b> In a cholecystectomy (gallbladder removal) surgeons tie off the <b>cystic artery</b>, which usually comes from the <b>right</b> hepatic artery. The left hepatic artery goes to the left liver and is left alone.<br>A. True: veins from the fundus and body pass straight into the liver.<br>B. True: a stuck stone blocks outflow and inflames the gallbladder.<br>C. True: a cystic duct stone causes biliary colic, an intense cramping pain.<br>E. True: this pouch (Hartmann's pouch) is a bulge at the neck in a diseased gallbladder.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 43 (The venous drainage from the neck of the gallbladder and cystic duct flows via t)","Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 36 (Blood supply to the Bile duct)","Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 46 (Calot's triangle)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":338,"f":"F1180"},{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":141,"f":"F0008"},{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":274,"f":"F1178"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G5","u":125,"label":"Portal vein: superior mesenteric plus splenic veins","slides":[{"src":"sl/G006-48.jpg","cap":"Portal vein formed by superior mesenteric and splenic veins behind pancreas","where":"2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary & Portal Vein, slide 48"},{"src":"sl/G007-9.jpg","cap":"Union of splenic vein with superior mesenteric vein posterior to pancreatic neck","where":"2026 lecture: Pancreas & Spleen, slide 9"}],"q":[{"id":"F1228-215-1","s":"The portal vein is formed by the…","o":["A. Superior mesenteric vein and splenic vein","B. Superior mesenteric vein and pancreaticoduodenal veins","C. Inferior mesenteric vein and middle colic vein","D. Gastric vein and cystic vein"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Superior mesenteric vein and splenic vein","a":"A","why":"<b>Superior mesenteric vein and splenic vein.</b> They join behind the neck of the pancreas, at about the first lumbar vertebra.<br>B. Pancreaticoduodenal veins: small tributaries; the superior one drains straight into the portal vein, the inferior one into the SMV (superior mesenteric vein).<br>C. Inferior mesenteric and middle colic: the middle colic drains to the SMV, and the IMV (inferior mesenteric vein) to the splenic vein.<br>D. Gastric and cystic veins: both empty directly into the portal vein; they don't form it.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 48 (It is formed anterior to the IVC and posterior to the neck of the pancreas (clos)"],"src":[{"t":"2019 review","n":"old Exam 2 review: abdomen part only","sl":215,"f":"F1228"}]},{"id":"F1228-205-1","s":"The splenic vein and superior mesenteric vein unite to form the…","o":["A. Inferior Mesenteric Vein","B. Pancreatic Vein","C. Left Gastroepiploic Vein","D. Short Gastric Veins","E. Portal Vein"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Portal Vein","a":"E","why":"<b>Portal vein.</b> The splenic vein and SMV (superior mesenteric vein) unite behind the neck of the pancreas to form it.<br>A. Inferior mesenteric vein: a tributary that usually empties into the splenic vein.<br>B. Pancreatic veins: small veins draining into the splenic vein.<br>C. Left gastroepiploic vein: drains the greater curvature into the splenic vein.<br>D. Short gastric veins: drain the fundus of the stomach into the splenic vein.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 48 (It is formed anterior to the IVC and posterior to the neck of the pancreas (clos)"],"src":[{"t":"2019 review","n":"old Exam 2 review: abdomen part only","sl":205,"f":"F1228"}]},{"id":"D041-249-1","s":"The portal vein is formed by the union of which two major vessels?","o":["A. Splenic vein and inferior vena cava","B. Superior mesenteric vein and splenic vein","C. Inferior mesenteric vein and renal vein","D. Hepatic veins and splenic vein"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Superior mesenteric vein and splenic vein","a":"B","why":"<b>Superior mesenteric vein and splenic vein.</b> They join behind the neck of the pancreas, in front of the IVC (inferior vena cava), at about the first lumbar vertebra.<br>A. Splenic vein and IVC: the IVC is a systemic (caval) vein and never forms the portal vein.<br>C. IMV (inferior mesenteric vein) and renal vein: the IMV usually empties into the splenic vein; the renal vein goes to the IVC.<br>D. Hepatic veins: they carry blood out of the liver into the IVC.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 48 (It is formed anterior to the IVC and posterior to the neck of the pancreas (clos)","Small & Large Intestines slide 48 (Jejunum and ileum)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":249,"f":"D041"}],"t":1},{"id":"D042-166-1","s":"You are in the anatomy lab and observing the portal venous circulation in the cadaver with Mr. Walker. You are able to correctly identify the portal vein within the portal tried, but Mr. Walker then asks you to name the two veins that join to form the portal vein. You respond:","o":["A. IMV and splenic","B. Left hepatic and right hepatic","C. Left gastric and SMV","D. SMV and splenic","E. Left gastric and splenic"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"SMV and splenic","a":"D","why":"<b>SMV (superior mesenteric vein) and splenic vein.</b> They join behind the neck of the pancreas to form the portal vein.<br>A. IMV (inferior mesenteric vein) and splenic: the IMV usually empties into the splenic vein before the junction.<br>B. Left and right hepatic: the portal vein splits into right and left branches at the porta hepatis; it is not formed by them.<br>C. Left gastric and SMV: the left gastric vein drains straight into the portal vein.<br>E. Left gastric and splenic: same mistake; the left gastric is a tributary, not a source.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 48 (It is formed anterior to the IVC and posterior to the neck of the pancreas (clos)","Small & Large Intestines slide 48 (Jejunum and ileum)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI module TA review","sl":166,"f":"D042"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G5","u":125,"label":"Portal vein: most posterior in hepatoduodenal ligament","slides":[{"src":"sl/G006-16.jpg","cap":"Portal triad in hepatoduodenal ligament: portal vein behind artery and bile duct","where":"2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary & Portal Vein, slide 16"},{"src":"sl/G006-48.jpg","cap":"Portal vein formation behind the neck of the pancreas","where":"2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary & Portal Vein, slide 48"}],"q":[{"id":"F0008-137-1","s":"Which of the following is incorrect pertaining to the portal vein?","o":["A) It is typically formed by the superior mesenteric and splenic veins posterior to the neck of the pancreas.","B) It carries more of the total blood volume reaching the liver than any other vessel.","C) It is typically the most anterior of the structures within the hepatoduodenal ligament.","D) It divides into right and left branches at the porta hepatis.","E) It may spread cancer to the liver."],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"It is typically the most anterior of the structures within the hepatoduodenal ligament (it is the most posterior)","a":"C","why":"<b>C is false.</b> The portal vein is the most <b>posterior</b> structure in the hepatoduodenal ligament. The bile duct is in front on the right, and the hepatic artery proper is in front on the left.<br>A. True: superior mesenteric plus splenic veins, behind the pancreatic neck.<br>B. True: it brings about seventy percent of the liver's blood; the hepatic artery brings about thirty.<br>D. True: it splits into right and left branches at the porta hepatis.<br>E. True: gut cancers spread to the liver through it.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 48 (It is formed anterior to the IVC and posterior to the neck of the pancreas (clos)","Pancreas & Spleen slide 9 (Pancreas)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":137,"f":"F0008"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G5","u":126,"label":"Portal system versus caval system veins","slides":[{"src":"sl/G006-52.jpg","cap":"Portal system: splenic, inferior and superior mesenteric veins feeding portal vein","where":"2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary & Portal Vein, slide 52"},{"src":"sl/G006-56.jpg","cap":"Portal tributaries and caval veins side by side, with the inferior vena cava","where":"2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary & Portal Vein, slide 56"}],"q":[{"id":"F3900-24-1","s":"During a routine abdomen, a radiologist identifies a dilated vein in the abdomen of a patient. Which of the following veins is most likely part of the caval system and not part of the portal system?","o":["A. Hepatic portal vein","B. Superior vena cava","C. Splenic vein","D. Inferior mesenteric vein"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Superior vena cava","a":"B","why":"<b>Superior vena cava.</b> The caval system is the two venae cavae and the veins that empty into them. The portal system is the hepatic portal vein and the gut veins that feed it.<br>A. Hepatic portal vein: the trunk of the portal system.<br>C. Splenic vein: joins the SMV (superior mesenteric vein) to form the portal vein, so it is portal.<br>D. Inferior mesenteric vein: drains into the splenic vein, so it is portal.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 49 (Portal vein)","Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 54 (Porto systemic anastomosis)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":24,"f":"F3900"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G5","u":126,"label":"Left gastroepiploic vein drains to splenic vein","slides":[{"src":"sl/G004-115.jpg","cap":"Left gastro-omental vein into splenic vein; right gastro-omental into superior mesenteric","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 115"},{"src":"sl/G006-48.jpg","cap":"Superior mesenteric vein tributaries: middle colic, right colic, ileocolic, jejunal","where":"2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary & Portal Vein, slide 48"}],"q":[{"id":"F1228-171-1","s":"The following are tributaries to the superior mesenteric vein except:","o":["A. Right colic vein","B. Ileocolic vein","C. Jejunal vein","D. Inferior pancreatidocuodenal vein","E. Left gastroepiploic vein"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Left gastroepiploic vein","a":"E","why":"<b>Left gastroepiploic vein.</b> It drains the left part of the greater curvature into the <b>splenic vein</b>, not the SMV (superior mesenteric vein).<br>A. Right colic vein: an SMV tributary.<br>B. Ileocolic vein: an SMV tributary.<br>C. Jejunal veins: SMV tributaries.<br>D. Inferior pancreaticoduodenal vein: an SMV tributary.<br>Mix-up to avoid: the <b>right</b> gastroepiploic vein does drain to the SMV.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 50 (Tributaries of superior mesenteric vein)","Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 51 (Tributaries of the splenic vein)"],"src":[{"t":"2019 review","n":"old Exam 2 review: abdomen part only","sl":171,"f":"F1228"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G5","u":126,"label":"Venogram: inferior mesenteric vein joins splenic vein","slides":[{"src":"sl/G006-48.jpg","cap":"Inferior mesenteric vein rising to join the splenic vein left of the confluence","where":"2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary & Portal Vein, slide 48"},{"src":"sl/G006-56.jpg","cap":"MR angiogram: splenic vein, superior and inferior mesenteric veins labelled","where":"2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary & Portal Vein, slide 56"}],"q":[{"id":"F0008-128-1","s":"In the portal venogram shown below, the structure labeled “6” is:","o":["A. Hepatic veins","B. Inferior mesenteric vein","C. Right gastric vein","D. Right gastroepiploic vein","E. Splenic vein"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Inferior mesenteric vein","a":"B","why":"<b>Inferior mesenteric vein (IMV).</b> In this venogram, 3 is the portal vein trunk and 4 is the splenic vein running to the patient's left. Label 5 is the SMV (superior mesenteric vein) coming up from below. Label 6 is a thinner vein rising from below into the splenic vein, as the IMV usually does.<br>A. Hepatic veins: inside the liver, draining up into the IVC (inferior vena cava).<br>C. Right gastric vein: a small vein entering the portal vein directly.<br>D. Right gastroepiploic vein: joins the SMV.<br>E. Splenic vein: that is label 4.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0008-128.jpg","in":"Portal venogram (catheter from the right) with numbered veins 1-6: 1-2 right/left portal branches, 3 portal vein trunk, 4 splenic vein running to the left, 5 superior mesenteric vein descending, 6 a thin vein joining from below near the splenic-SMV confluence. Asked: label 6.","tg":["Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 51 (Tributaries of the splenic vein)","Radiology Abd III slide 54 (Portal Vein)","Radiology Abd III slide 57 (Splenic Vein)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":128,"f":"F0008"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G5","u":127,"label":"Esophageal varices: left gastric with azygos veins","slides":[{"src":"sl/G006-56.jpg","cap":"Portocaval sites: A esophageal, B rectal, C umbilical, D retroperitoneal","where":"2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary & Portal Vein, slide 56"},{"src":"sl/G006-54.jpg","cap":"Table: left gastric with azygos causes esophageal varices; rectal causes hemorrhoids","where":"2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary & Portal Vein, slide 54"}],"q":[{"id":"F3900-20-1","s":"What anastomosis is at risk in esophageal varices?","o":["A. Esophageal branches off of the left gastric vein and esophageal branches off of the azygous vein","B. Esophageal branches off of the right gastric vein and esophageal branches off of the hemi-azygous vein","C. Esophageal branches off of the Superior vena cava and esophageal branches off of the azygous vein","D. Esophageal branches off of the left gastric vein and esophageal branches off of the accessory azygous vein"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Esophageal branches off of the left gastric vein and esophageal branches off of the azygous vein","a":"A","why":"<b>Esophageal branches of the left gastric vein and of the azygos vein.</b> They meet in the lower esophagus. The left gastric side is portal; the azygos side is systemic. High portal pressure swells this link into varices.<br>B. Right gastric and hemiazygos: the right gastric vein drains the lesser curvature near the pylorus, not the esophagus.<br>C. Superior vena cava: has no esophageal branches; the azygos empties into it.<br>D. Accessory azygos: drains upper left chest-wall veins; the partner named in the lecture is the azygos itself.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 54 (Porto systemic anastomosis)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":20,"f":"F3900"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3900-22-1","s":"Going back to the previous question. Which on is from the portal system and which is from the caval system? Fill in the blank Esophageal branch of the left gastric is part of the ________ Esophageal branches off of the Azygous vein is part of the ______","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Esophageal branch of the left gastric is part of the Portal system; Esophageal branches off of the Azygous vein is part of the Caval system","a":null,"why":"<b>Left gastric is portal; azygos is caval (systemic).</b> The left gastric vein empties into the hepatic portal vein. The azygos vein empties into the superior vena cava. Esophageal varices form where these two systems meet in the lower esophagus.<br>Common mix-up: in every pair, the portal partner (left gastric, superior rectal, paraumbilical, colic veins) drains to the liver. The caval partner (azygos, middle and inferior rectal, epigastric, retroperitoneal veins) drains to a vena cava.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 54 (Porto systemic anastomosis)","Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 49 (Portal vein)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":22,"f":"F3900"}]},{"id":"F3901-70-1","s":"A medical student was cramming the night before his Anatomy exam and was running on 2 hours of sleep so he was mixing up everything. He was trying to recall which parts of the porto-caval anasatomes were from the caval system and which were from the portal system. Which of the following statements regarding the porto-caval anastomosis is correct?","o":["A. The left gastric vein forms an anastomosis with the azygos vein","B. The left gastric veins forms an anastomosis with the small epigastric veins","C. The paraumbilical veins forms an anastomosis with the middle and inferior rectal veins","D. The superior rectal veins form an anastomosis with the azygos veins","E. The left gastric vein forms an anastomosis with the middle and inferior rectal veins"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"The left gastric vein forms an anastomosis with the azygos vein","a":"A","why":"<b>The left gastric vein meets the azygos vein.</b> This is the esophageal portocaval link. The four pairs: left gastric with azygos (esophagus); superior rectal with middle and inferior rectal (rectum); paraumbilical with epigastric (navel); colic with retroperitoneal (back wall).<br>B. Left gastric does not meet the epigastric veins; the paraumbilical veins do.<br>C. Paraumbilical veins meet epigastric veins, not rectal veins.<br>D. Superior rectal veins meet the middle and inferior rectal veins, not the azygos.<br>E. Left gastric meets the azygos, not rectal veins.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 54 (Porto systemic anastomosis)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":70,"f":"F3901"},{"t":"? TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":76,"f":"F1156"}]},{"id":"F3902-95-1","s":"Portal hypertension puts the anastomoses of the esophageal branches of the left gastric vein and the azygous vein at risk for…","o":["A. Hemorrhoids","B. Acid reflux","C. Esophageal varices","D. Pulmonary embolism","E. Interstitial fibrosis"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Esophageal varices","a":"C","why":"<b>Esophageal varices.</b> The left gastric (portal) and azygos (systemic) veins meet in the lower esophagus. High portal pressure swells these veins, and they can burst and bleed.<br>A. Hemorrhoids: from the superior rectal with middle and inferior rectal link.<br>B. Acid reflux: a sphincter problem, not a venous one.<br>D. Pulmonary embolism: a clot, usually from deep leg veins; unrelated.<br>E. Interstitial fibrosis: a lung scarring disease; unrelated.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 54 (Porto systemic anastomosis)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":95,"f":"F3902"}],"t":1},{"id":"D041-253-1","s":"A 54-year-old man with a history of chronic alcohol use presents to the clinic with progressive abdominal distension and painless hematemesis. On physical exam, he has jaundice, shifting dullness, and dilated, tortuous subcutaneous veins radiating from the umbilicus. Endoscopy shows dilated veins in the distal esophagus. Which of the following portosystemic anastomoses is most responsible for the life-threatening complication seen in this patient?","o":["A. Superior rectal vein ↔ middle and inferior rectal veins","B. Paraumbilical veins ↔ superficial epigastric veins","C. Left gastric vein ↔ azygos vein","D. Colic veins ↔ retroperitoneal lumbar veins"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Left gastric vein ↔ azygos vein","a":"C","why":"<b>Left gastric vein with azygos vein.</b> This link causes esophageal varices. Vomiting blood from burst varices is the deadly problem in portal hypertension (high pressure in the portal vein).<br>A. Superior rectal with middle and inferior rectal veins: anorectal varices; they bleed far less dangerously.<br>B. Paraumbilical with superficial epigastric veins: the caput medusae he has, but it rarely bleeds badly.<br>D. Colic with retroperitoneal (lumbar) veins: hidden and usually silent.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 54 (Porto systemic anastomosis)","Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 55 (Porto systemic anastamosis)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":253,"f":"D041"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G5","u":127,"label":"Inferior vena cava is not itself an anastomosis","slides":[{"src":"sl/G006-56.jpg","cap":"Anastomoses are small-vein links (A–D), not the inferior vena cava itself","where":"2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary & Portal Vein, slide 56"},{"src":"sl/G006-54.jpg","cap":"Lecture list of portal and systemic partners at esophagus and rectum","where":"2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary & Portal Vein, slide 54"}],"q":[{"id":"F3900-32-1","s":"Which of the following vessels is NOT DIRECTLY part of the connections between the portal vein and the systemic circulation in the porto-caval system?","o":["A. Left gastric vein","B. Inferior vena cava","C. Esophageal veins","D. Superior mesenteric vein","E. Splenic vein"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Inferior vena cava","a":"B","why":"<b>Inferior vena cava.</b> The anastomoses link small veins, such as the left gastric with esophageal veins, or paraumbilical with epigastric veins. The IVC (inferior vena cava) only collects the systemic partners' blood later; it never touches a portal vein itself.<br>A. Left gastric vein: the portal partner at the esophagus.<br>C. Esophageal veins: the systemic partner at the esophagus.<br>D. Superior mesenteric vein: portal trunk whose colic branches meet retroperitoneal veins.<br>E. Splenic vein: a portal trunk fed by portal-side tributaries; it is also the vein joined to the left renal vein in a splenorenal shunt.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 54 (Porto systemic anastomosis)","Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 55 (Porto systemic anastamosis)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":32,"f":"F3900"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G5","u":128,"label":"Left gastric vein feeds esophageal varices","slides":[{"src":"sl/G006-56.jpg","cap":"Site A: left gastric vein meeting esophageal veins draining to azygos","where":"2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary & Portal Vein, slide 56"},{"src":"sl/G004-115.jpg","cap":"Left gastric vein draining the lesser curvature into the portal vein","where":"2026 lecture: Peritoneum, Stomach & Celiac, slide 115"}],"q":[{"id":"F1243-96-1","s":"A 58-year-old male alcoholic is admitted to the hospital after vomiting dark red blood (hematemesis). Endoscopy reveals ruptured esophageal varices, most likely resulting from portal hypertension. Which of following venous tributaries to the portal system anastomoses with caval veins to cause the varices?","o":["A. Splenic","B. Left gastro-omental","C. Left gastric","D. Left Hepatic","E. Right gastric"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Left gastric","a":"C","why":"<b>Left gastric vein.</b> This portal tributary sends esophageal branches up the lower esophagus. There they meet azygos (caval) veins. In portal hypertension this link swells into varices.<br>A. Splenic: helps form the portal vein; it has no esophageal branches.<br>B. Left gastro-omental: drains the greater curvature into the splenic vein.<br>D. Left hepatic: drains liver blood into the IVC (inferior vena cava); it is not portal.<br>E. Right gastric: drains the lesser curvature near the pylorus, far from the esophagus.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 54 (Porto systemic anastomosis)"],"src":[{"t":"? tutor review","n":"old Exam 2: abdomen part only","sl":96,"f":"F1243"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3900-34-1","s":"In patients with portal hypertension, esophageal varices can develop due to increased pressure within the portal venous system. Which of the following vessels is primarily responsible for supplying blood to esophageal varices in this condition?","o":["A. Inferior vena cava","B. Hepatic artery","C. Left gastric vein","D. Splenic artery","E. Superior mesenteric vein"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Left gastric vein","a":"C","why":"<b>Left gastric vein.</b> Its esophageal branches drain the lower esophagus into the portal vein. When portal pressure rises, blood backs up through it into the small veins under the esophageal lining, and they swell into varices.<br>A. Inferior vena cava: a systemic vein, not under portal pressure.<br>B. Hepatic artery: an artery; varices are veins.<br>D. Splenic artery: an artery.<br>E. Superior mesenteric vein: portal, but it drains the small intestine and right colon, not the esophagus.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 54 (Porto systemic anastomosis)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":34,"f":"F3900"}],"t":1},{"id":"F0003-10-1","s":"The emergency services bring a 68-year-old man who is vomiting blood to the ER. The man has a history of hepatic cirrhosis due to chronic Hepatitis C infection. Endoscopy reveals bleeding varicose veins in the lower esophagus. The bleeding vessels NORMALLY drain blood preferentially into which of the following veins?","o":["A. Azygos","B. Hepatic","C. Splenic","D. Hepatic Portal","E. Hemiazygos"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Hepatic Portal","a":"D","why":"<b>Hepatic portal vein.</b> The lower esophagus normally drains through esophageal branches of the left gastric vein into the portal vein. In cirrhosis, portal pressure rises and blood is pushed backward up these veins toward the azygos. They swell into varices.<br>A. Azygos: drains the middle esophagus; here it is only the escape route.<br>B. Hepatic veins: drain the liver into the IVC (inferior vena cava).<br>C. Splenic: drains the spleen and the stomach fundus, not the esophagus.<br>E. Hemiazygos: a systemic vein of the left chest wall; not the normal drain of the lower esophagus.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 54 (Porto systemic anastomosis)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 115 (right and left gastric veins drain into the hepatic portal vein; the short gastr)"],"src":[{"t":"? Middletown TA review","n":"MT Abdomen Review","sl":10,"f":"F0003"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G5","u":129,"label":"Caput medusae: paraumbilical with epigastric veins","slides":[{"src":"sl/G006-56.jpg","cap":"Site C: paraumbilical veins meeting epigastric veins around the umbilicus","where":"2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary & Portal Vein, slide 56"},{"src":"sl/G006-55.jpg","cap":"Paraumbilical (portal) with epigastric (systemic) veins: caput medusae","where":"2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary & Portal Vein, slide 55"}],"q":[{"id":"F3902-91-1","s":"Kacper is drawing one of his famous line diagrams on the white board in Z-Space. He is bravely attempting to tackle the porto-caval anastomoses. Upon drawing the anastomoses occuring at the anterior abdominal wall, his mind goes blank. He turns to Justin and asks “What is the vein that anastamoses with the epigastric veins?” Justin replies “Ummmm I think it’s the…”","o":["A. IMV","B. SMV","C. Portal vein","D. Paraumbilical vein","E. Colonic veins"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Paraumbilical vein","a":"D","why":"<b>Paraumbilical vein.</b> On the front abdominal wall, the paraumbilical veins (portal) run along the round ligament. At the navel they meet the epigastric veins (systemic).<br>A. IMV (inferior mesenteric vein): its superior rectal branch meets the rectal veins.<br>B. SMV (superior mesenteric vein): its colic branches meet retroperitoneal veins.<br>C. Portal vein: the trunk itself; it doesn't reach the abdominal wall.<br>E. Colonic veins: meet retroperitoneal veins on the back wall.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 55 (Porto systemic anastamosis)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":91,"f":"F3902"}]},{"id":"F3902-93-1","s":"Justin then turns to Kacper and goes “I think the clinical correlate associated with that anastamoses is hemorrhoids.” Kacper tells Justin he is…","o":["A. Correct","B. Incorrect, the answer is esophageal varices","C. Incorrect, the answer is varicocele","D. Incorrect, the answer is caput medusae","E. Incorrect, the answer is SMA syndrome"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Incorrect, the answer is caput medusae","a":"D","why":"<b>Incorrect: the answer is caput medusae.</b> The link from the last question is paraumbilical with epigastric veins at the navel. When it swells, veins radiate from the umbilicus (caput medusae).<br>A. 'Correct': no; hemorrhoids come from the superior rectal with middle and inferior rectal veins.<br>B. Esophageal varices: from the left gastric with azygos link.<br>C. Varicocele: swollen pampiniform plexus in the scrotum; not portocaval.<br>E. SMA (superior mesenteric artery) syndrome: the artery pinches the third part of the duodenum; not venous.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 55 (Porto systemic anastamosis)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":93,"f":"F3902"}]},{"id":"F1180-341-1","s":"This disorder is caused by an expansion of which anastomosis?","o":["a. Left gastric vein and azygos vein","b. Superior rectal vein and inferior rectal vein","c. Retroperitoneal and colonic veins","d. Paraumbilical and epigastric veins"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Paraumbilical and epigastric veins","a":"D","why":"<b>Paraumbilical and epigastric veins.</b> The photo shows caput medusae: swollen, winding veins spreading from the navel. Paraumbilical veins (portal) meet the abdominal-wall epigastric veins (systemic) there. Portal hypertension forces blood out through them.<br>a. Left gastric and azygos: esophageal varices.<br>b. Superior and inferior rectal: hemorrhoids (anorectal varices).<br>c. Retroperitoneal and colonic: deep veins on the back wall; no visible sign.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F1180-341.jpg","in":"Photo of a distended abdomen with dilated tortuous veins radiating around the umbilicus, captioned 'CAPUT MEDUSAE' on the slide.","tg":["Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 55 (Porto systemic anastamosis)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 21 (vasculature)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":341,"f":"F1180"},{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":277,"f":"F1178"},{"t":"? TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":62,"f":"F1156"}],"t":1},{"id":"D042-168-1","s":"A 59 yo M comes to you complaining of symptoms related to an obstruction in the portal venous system. Which of the following pairs of veins describes a point of connection between the portal and caval system to shunt venous blood?","o":["A. Splenic and Left Renal","B. Left Gastric and Right Gastric","C. Paraumbilical and Inferior epigastric","D. Superior epigastric and musculophrenic","E. Superior rectal and Middle colic"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Paraumbilical and Inferior epigastric","a":"C","why":"<b>Paraumbilical and inferior epigastric veins.</b> The paraumbilical veins (portal) run with the round ligament to the navel. There they meet the epigastric veins (caval) of the abdominal wall.<br>A. Splenic and left renal: one of each, but not one of the four natural anastomoses; it is the classic surgical (splenorenal) shunt.<br>B. Left and right gastric: both portal.<br>D. Superior epigastric and musculophrenic: both caval, from the internal thoracic veins.<br>E. Superior rectal and middle colic: both portal.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 55 (Porto systemic anastamosis)","Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 54 (Porto systemic anastomosis)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI module TA review","sl":168,"f":"D042"}],"t":1},{"id":"D042-170-1","s":"What clinical sign would you expect the previous patient to present with if that specific anastomosis is being overfilled with blood?","o":["A. Caput Medusae","B. Esophageal varices","C. Kidney failure","D. Hemorrhoids"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Caput Medusae","a":"A","why":"<b>Caput medusae.</b> The previous answer was the paraumbilical with epigastric veins at the navel. When this link overfills, swollen veins spread out from the umbilicus like snakes (caput medusae).<br>B. Esophageal varices: from the left gastric with azygos link.<br>C. Kidney failure: not a sign of a portocaval link.<br>D. Hemorrhoids: from the superior rectal with middle and inferior rectal link.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 55 (Porto systemic anastamosis)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI module TA review","sl":170,"f":"D042"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3901-123-1","s":"While taking a history of your patient they tell you they are taking Lisinopril 10mg po 1/day to control their high blood pressure. You begin the abdominal exam and expose their abdomen and see what looks similar to the image below. This is caused by an anastomosis of what vessels?","o":["A. Splenic vein and inferior mesenteric vein","B. Splenic artery and inferior mesenteric artery","C. Esophageal branches off left gastric vein and esophageal branches off azygos vein","D. Paraumbilical veins and epigastric veins","E. Superior rectal vein and middle rectal vein"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Paraumbilical veins and epigastric veins","a":"D","why":"<b>Paraumbilical veins and epigastric veins.</b> The picture is caput medusae: swollen veins radiating from the navel. It comes from portal hypertension, not the ordinary high blood pressure that lisinopril treats.<br>A. Splenic and IMV (inferior mesenteric vein): both portal; the IMV normally drains into the splenic vein.<br>B. Arteries: portocaval anastomoses are between veins only.<br>C. Left gastric with azygos: esophageal varices.<br>E. Superior with middle rectal: hemorrhoids.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F3901-123.jpg","in":"Photo of an abdomen with tortuous distended veins radiating from the umbilicus (caput medusae)","tg":["Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 55 (Porto systemic anastamosis)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":123,"f":"F3901"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G5","u":130,"label":"Portocaval shunt joins a portal and caval vein","slides":[{"src":"sl/G006-56.jpg","cap":"MR angiogram: splenic vein running near the left renal vein","where":"2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary & Portal Vein, slide 56"},{"src":"sl/G006-53.jpg","cap":"Splenic vein behind the pancreas, receiving the inferior mesenteric vein","where":"2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary & Portal Vein, slide 53"}],"q":[{"id":"F0008-130-1","s":"A 62-year-old female patient has chronic liver disease and portal hypertension. To relieve the pressure in the portal system, a portocaval shunt is performed. Which of the following pairs of veins may be anastomoses to perform this shunt?","o":["A. Left ovarian-left renal","B. Right renal-right suprarenal","C. Splenic- left renal","D. Superior mesenteric-inferior mesenteric","E. Superior mesenteric-splenic"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Splenic- left renal","a":"C","why":"<b>Splenic vein to left renal vein.</b> A portocaval shunt must join a <b>portal</b> vein to a <b>caval</b> (systemic) vein, so portal blood can bypass the liver. The splenic vein (portal) lies close to the left renal vein (caval) behind the pancreas.<br>A. Left ovarian and left renal: both caval.<br>B. Right renal and right suprarenal: both caval.<br>D. Superior and inferior mesenteric: both portal.<br>E. Superior mesenteric and splenic: both portal; they already join to form the portal vein.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 54 (Porto systemic anastomosis)","Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 55 (Porto systemic anastamosis)","Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 51 (Tributaries of the splenic vein)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":130,"f":"F0008"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3901-126-1","s":"After a thorough workup of your patient, you diagnose them with portal hypertension. To relieve the increased pressure on the portal system you decide to do a portocaval shunt. Your attending pimps you on portocaval shunts, but instead of panicking you realize that all of the following are possible examples of portocaval shunts except:","o":["A. Splenic vein and left renal vein","B. Right gastric vein and right ovarian vein","C. Superior pancreaticoduodenal vein and supraduodenal vein","D. Right renal vein and right gastroomental vein","E. Left testicular vein and inferior mesenteric vein"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Superior pancreaticoduodenal vein and supraduodenal vein","a":"C","why":"<b>Superior pancreaticoduodenal and supraduodenal veins.</b> Both drain into the portal vein. Joining two portal veins cannot bypass the liver, so this is not a portocaval shunt.<br>A. Splenic (portal) with left renal (caval): the classic real shunt.<br>B. Right gastric (portal) with right ovarian (caval): one of each, so it qualifies.<br>D. Right renal (caval) with right gastro-omental (portal): one of each.<br>E. Left testicular (caval) with inferior mesenteric (portal): one of each.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 54 (Porto systemic anastomosis)","Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 49 (Portal vein)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":126,"f":"F3901"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G5","u":130,"label":"Portal hypertension: varices, caput medusae, hemorrhoids","slides":[{"src":"sl/G006-56.jpg","cap":"Four portocaval sites that swell in portal hypertension","where":"2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary & Portal Vein, slide 56"},{"src":"sl/G006-55.jpg","cap":"Paraumbilical with epigastric veins gives caput medusae; colonic with retroperitoneal","where":"2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary & Portal Vein, slide 55"}],"q":[{"id":"F1252-106-1","s":"A 54-year-old man with a long history of alcohol abuse presents to the emergency department with rapidly increasing abdominal distention most likely resulting from an alteration in portal systemic blood flow. Which of the following characteristics is associated with the portal vein or the portal venous system?","o":["A. Lower blood pressure than in the IVC","B. Least risk of venous varices because of portal hypertension","C. Distention of the portal vein resulting from its numerous valves","D. Caput medusae and hemorrhoids caused by portal hypertension","E. Less blood flow than in the hepatic artery"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Caput medusae and hemorrhoids caused by portal hypertension","a":"D","why":"<b>Caput medusae and hemorrhoids from portal hypertension.</b> High portal pressure pushes blood out through portocaval links: paraumbilical with epigastric veins (caput medusae) and superior rectal with middle and inferior rectal veins (hemorrhoids).<br>A. Wrong: portal pressure is normally higher than IVC (inferior vena cava) pressure.<br>B. Wrong: portal hypertension is the main cause of varices.<br>C. Wrong: the portal vein has no valves, which is why blood can flow backward.<br>E. Wrong: the portal vein brings about seventy percent of the liver's blood, more than the hepatic artery.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 54 (Porto systemic anastomosis)","Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 55 (Porto systemic anastamosis)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":106,"f":"F1252"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G5","u":131,"label":"Pancreatic tail is the intraperitoneal part","slides":[{"src":"sl/G007-11.jpg","cap":"Retroperitoneal head and body; tail enters the splenorenal ligament","where":"2026 lecture: Pancreas & Spleen, slide 11"},{"src":"sl/G007-27.jpg","cap":"Axial diagram: pancreatic tail wrapped in the lienorenal ligament","where":"2026 lecture: Pancreas & Spleen, slide 27"}],"q":[{"id":"F1252-35-1","s":"Which part of the pancreas is NOT retroperitoneal?","o":["A. Head","B. Body","C. Tail"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Tail","a":"C","why":"<b>Tail.</b> The tail runs into the splenorenal ligament toward the splenic hilum, so it is wrapped in peritoneum (intraperitoneal). The rest of the pancreas stays behind the peritoneum.<br>A. Head: retroperitoneal, inside the C-loop of the duodenum.<br>B. Body: retroperitoneal; it lies across the aorta in the floor of the lesser sac (omental bursa).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Pancreas & Spleen slide 11 (Pancreas)","Pancreas & Spleen slide 4 (Pancreas)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":35,"f":"F1252"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G5","u":131,"label":"SMA and SMV lie behind the pancreatic neck","slides":[{"src":"sl/G007-9.jpg","cap":"Neck overlies superior mesenteric vessels; portal vein forms behind it","where":"2026 lecture: Pancreas & Spleen, slide 9"},{"src":"sl/G007-10.jpg","cap":"Body touches aorta and superior mesenteric artery posteriorly","where":"2026 lecture: Pancreas & Spleen, slide 10"}],"q":[{"id":"F0008-132-1","s":"Which of the following is incorrect pertaining to the pancreas?","o":["A) The superior mesenteric artery lies anterior to its neck.","B) Its head is embraced mainly by the descending part of the duodenum.","C) It is retroperitoneal.","D) Its main duct joins with the common bile duct before entering the duodenum.","E) It is partially supplied by branches from the splenic artery."],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"The superior mesenteric artery lies anterior to its neck (it lies posterior)","a":"A","why":"<b>A is false.</b> The SMA (superior mesenteric artery) lies <b>behind</b> the neck of the pancreas, not in front. It leaves the aorta behind the neck, then passes forward over the uncinate process.<br>B. True: the C-shaped duodenum hugs the head, mostly by its descending part.<br>C. True: the pancreas is retroperitoneal, except the tail.<br>D. True: the main duct joins the bile duct at the hepatopancreatic ampulla.<br>E. True: the splenic artery sends pancreatic branches to the body and tail.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Pancreas & Spleen slide 9 (Pancreas)","Small & Large Intestines slide 42 (SMA)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":132,"f":"F0008"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G5","u":131,"label":"Pancreatic head overlies IVC and renal vessels","slides":[{"src":"sl/G007-7.jpg","cap":"Head of pancreas lying on the inferior vena cava, bile duct embedded","where":"2026 lecture: Pancreas & Spleen, slide 7"},{"src":"sl/G007-8.jpg","cap":"Head outline over inferior vena cava, both renal veins, right renal artery","where":"2026 lecture: Pancreas & Spleen, slide 8"}],"q":[{"id":"F1228-213-1","s":"Which of the following is not overlaid by the pancreatic head?","o":["A. IVC","B. Left renal vein","C. Right renal vein","D. Right renal artery","E. Superior mesenteric vein"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Superior mesenteric vein","a":"E","why":"<b>Superior mesenteric vein.</b> The SMV (superior mesenteric vein) lies behind the <b>neck</b> of the pancreas and in front of the uncinate process, so the head does not cover it. The head lies on the IVC (inferior vena cava), both renal veins and the right renal artery.<br>A. IVC: directly behind the head.<br>B. Left renal vein: ends in the IVC behind the head.<br>C. Right renal vein: behind the head.<br>D. Right renal artery: passes behind the IVC and the head.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Pancreas & Spleen slide 7 (Pancreas)","Pancreas & Spleen slide 8 (Overlies IVC, left and right renal veins and right renal artery.)"],"src":[{"t":"2019 review","n":"old Exam 2 review: abdomen part only","sl":213,"f":"F1228"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G5","u":131,"label":"Distal pancreatectomy: aorta and splenic vessels","slides":[{"src":"sl/G007-10.jpg","cap":"Pancreatic body crosses L2 and aorta; contacts superior mesenteric artery","where":"2026 lecture: Pancreas & Spleen, slide 10"},{"src":"sl/G007-11.jpg","cap":"Tail runs in splenorenal ligament with splenic vessels toward splenic hilum","where":"2026 lecture: Pancreas & Spleen, slide 11"}],"q":[{"id":"F3907-50-1","s":"You are scheduled to perform a distal pancreatectomy, a surgery that requires removal of the body and tail of the pancreas. What two major structures do you need to watch out for?","o":["A. IVC and Splenic Vessels","B. IVC and Aorta","C. Aorta and Splenic Vessels","D. Superior Mesenteric Artery and IVC"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Aorta and Splenic Vessels","a":"C","why":"<b>Aorta and splenic vessels.</b> The body of the pancreas crosses in front of the aorta (and the SMA, superior mesenteric artery) at the second lumbar vertebra. The splenic artery and vein run along the body and tail, and the tail lies with them in the splenorenal ligament.<br>A. IVC (inferior vena cava): lies behind the head, which is not removed here.<br>B. IVC and aorta: the aorta is right, but the IVC belongs to the head.<br>D. SMA and IVC: the IVC again belongs to the head, not the body and tail.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Pancreas & Spleen slide 10 (Pancreas)","Pancreas & Spleen slide 11 (Pancreas)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA weekly","n":"10-06-2022 TA session (foregut viscera)","sl":50,"f":"F3907"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G5","u":132,"label":"Pancreatic head tumor blocks bile duct, duodenum","slides":[{"src":"sl/G007-7.jpg","cap":"Pancreatic head inside the duodenal C-loop with the bile duct embedded","where":"2026 lecture: Pancreas & Spleen, slide 7"},{"src":"sl/G006-35.jpg","cap":"Bile duct passing behind the pancreatic head to the second duodenum","where":"2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary & Portal Vein, slide 35"}],"q":[{"id":"F1252-36-1","s":"In case of cancer/CA of the head of the pancreas which two structures can be blocked?","o":["A. Bile duct and esophagus","B. Bile duct and Duodenum","C. Bile duct and part of the small intestine","D. Bile duct and part of the large intestine"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Bile duct and Duodenum","a":"B","why":"<b>Bile duct and duodenum.</b> The bile duct runs embedded in the back of the pancreatic head, so a tumor there causes jaundice. The duodenum curves around the head, so the tumor can also block it.<br>A. Esophagus: in the chest and upper abdomen, far from the pancreas.<br>C. Only loosely true: the duodenum is part of the small intestine, but B names the exact structure.<br>D. Large intestine: the transverse mesocolon attaches along the pancreas, but the colon is not the structure normally blocked.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Pancreas & Spleen slide 7 (Pancreas)","Small & Large Intestines slide 16 (Descending (second) part of duodenum)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":36,"f":"F1252"}],"t":1},{"id":"D042-161-1","s":"A 49-year-old man presents with acute abdominal pain and jaundice. Radiographic studies reveal a tumor in the head of the pancreas. Which of the following structures is most likely being obstructed?","o":["A. Common bile duct","B. Common hepatic duct","C. Cystic duct","D. Accessory pancreatic duct","E. Proper hepatic artery"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Common bile duct","a":"A","why":"<b>Common bile duct.</b> The bile duct runs embedded in the back of the pancreatic head on its way to the duodenum. A head tumor squeezes it, bile backs up, and the patient turns yellow (jaundice).<br>B. Common hepatic duct: above the pancreas, near the porta hepatis.<br>C. Cystic duct: up by the gallbladder neck.<br>D. Accessory pancreatic duct: carries only pancreatic juice, so blocking it causes no jaundice.<br>E. Proper hepatic artery: runs above the pancreas in the lesser omentum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 35 (Biliary ducts)","Pancreas & Spleen slide 7 (Pancreas)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI module TA review","sl":161,"f":"D042"},{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":348,"f":"F1180"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3902-12-1","s":"45 y/o F presents to ER complaining of nausea, vomiting and diarrhea. Imaging reveals a tumor at the head of the pancreas. Which structure is the tumor most likely compressing?","o":["A. 2nd part of duodenum","B. Spleen","C. R kidney","D. Azygos vein"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"2nd part of duodenum","a":"A","why":"<b>Second part of the duodenum.</b> The C-shaped duodenum wraps the pancreatic head, and its descending (second) part hugs the head's right side. A head tumor can squeeze it shut, causing nausea and vomiting.<br>B. Spleen: next to the tail, on the far left.<br>C. Right kidney: lies behind and to the side; pressing it would not cause vomiting.<br>D. Azygos vein: in the chest, beside the spine.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Pancreas & Spleen slide 7 (Pancreas)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":12,"f":"F3902"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G5","u":133,"label":"Main pancreatic blood supply: splenic artery","slides":[{"src":"sl/G007-15.jpg","cap":"Splenic artery branches along body and tail; pancreaticoduodenal arcades at head","where":"2026 lecture: Pancreas & Spleen, slide 15"},{"src":"sl/G007-31.jpg","cap":"Netter: dorsal, greater and tail pancreatic arteries from the splenic artery","where":"2026 lecture: Pancreas & Spleen, slide 31"}],"q":[{"id":"F1228-217-1","s":"What is the main blood supply to the pancreas?","o":["A. Superior pancreaticoduodenal arteries","B. Inferior pancreaticoduodenal arteries","C. Splenic artery","D. Superior mesenteric artery"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Splenic artery","a":"C","why":"<b>Splenic artery.</b> As it runs along the top of the pancreas, it gives many pancreatic branches (such as the great pancreatic artery) to the neck, body and tail.<br>A. Superior pancreaticoduodenal arteries: supply only the head, from the gastroduodenal artery.<br>B. Inferior pancreaticoduodenal arteries: supply only the head, from the SMA (superior mesenteric artery).<br>D. Superior mesenteric artery: helps only through the inferior pancreaticoduodenal arteries.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Pancreas & Spleen slide 14 (Pancreas)"],"src":[{"t":"2019 review","n":"old Exam 2 review: abdomen part only","sl":217,"f":"F1228"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G5","u":133,"label":"Pancreatic head: gastroduodenal and SMA arcades","slides":[{"src":"sl/G007-15.jpg","cap":"Superior pancreaticoduodenals from gastroduodenal meet inferior ones from superior mesenteric","where":"2026 lecture: Pancreas & Spleen, slide 15"}],"q":[{"id":"F3907-55-1","s":"Branches of which two arteries anastomose to provide blood to the head of the pancreas?","o":["A. Splenic Artery and Superior Mesenteric Artery","B. Right Gastric Artery and Hepatic Artery","C. Right Colic Artery and Middle Colic Artery","D. Gastroduodenal Artery and Superior Mesenteric Artery"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Gastroduodenal Artery and Superior Mesenteric Artery","a":"D","why":"<b>Gastroduodenal artery and superior mesenteric artery.</b> The gastroduodenal artery (from the celiac trunk) gives the superior pancreaticoduodenal arteries. The SMA (superior mesenteric artery) gives the inferior pancreaticoduodenal arteries. The two sets join in loops around the head.<br>A. Splenic and SMA: the splenic artery feeds the body and tail, not the head.<br>B. Right gastric and hepatic: they supply the lesser curvature and the liver.<br>C. Right colic and middle colic: they supply the colon.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Pancreas & Spleen slide 14 (Pancreas)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA weekly","n":"10-06-2022 TA session (foregut viscera)","sl":55,"f":"F3907"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3901-130-1","s":"You are on rotations and are presented with a patient who has a rupture of their celiac trunk. Obviously, there are a lot of organs that you should be worried about due to loss of blood supply, but you tell your attending that you are concerned with the loss of blood supply to the pancreas. Later, the attending patiently and nicely explains why this is not an immediate fear by saying:","o":["A. The pancreas is completely supplied by branches of the pancreatic branches of the inferior mesenteric artery","B. The pancreas also receives blood from an anastomosis of the inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery which is a branch of the superior mesenteric artery","C. The pancreas receives blood from pancreatic branches coming directly off of the aorta","D. The pancreas receives blood from the pancreatic branches and superior pancreaticoduodenal artery which are branches of the superior mesenteric artery, plus the inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery which is a branch of the inferior mesenteric artery","E. The attending is wrong and you are correct to be worried, all of the blood supply of the pancreas comes from branches off of the celiac trunk!!!"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"The pancreas also receives blood from an anastomosis of the inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery which is a branch of the superior mesenteric artery","a":"B","why":"<b>The inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery from the SMA (superior mesenteric artery).</b> It joins the superior pancreaticoduodenal artery, which comes from the gastroduodenal artery (a celiac branch). So the head still gets blood if the celiac trunk fails.<br>A. The IMA (inferior mesenteric artery) supplies the hindgut, not the pancreas.<br>C. No pancreatic branches come straight off the aorta.<br>D. The superior one comes from the gastroduodenal artery, and the inferior one from the SMA, not the IMA.<br>E. Wrong: the SMA gives a backup supply.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Pancreas & Spleen slide 14 (Pancreas)","Small & Large Intestines slide 31 (duodenum)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":130,"f":"F3901"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G5","u":134,"label":"Spleen: ribs 9–11, splenic artery from celiac","slides":[{"src":"sl/G007-22.jpg","cap":"Spleen related posteriorly to left ribs 9–11","where":"2026 lecture: Pancreas & Spleen, slide 22"},{"src":"sl/G007-15.jpg","cap":"Celiac trunk giving the splenic artery, which runs to the spleen","where":"2026 lecture: Pancreas & Spleen, slide 15"}],"q":[{"id":"F1252-77-1","s":"While studying for the practical portion of your Anatomy exam, you decide to review some written material as well by correlating viscera, their blood supply, and the ribs they are associated with. You find the spleen on a plastinated specimen.  What ribs lie posteriorly and what is its blood supply?","o":["A. 7th - 9th ribs, branch of the celiac artery","B. 9th - 11th ribs, branch of the celiac artery","C. 9th -11th ribs, branch of the superior mesenteric artery","D. 11th and 12th ribs, branch of the superior mesenteric artery","E. 11th and 12th ribs, branch of the inferior mesenteric artery"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"9th - 11th ribs, branch of the celiac artery","a":"B","why":"<b>Ribs 9–11; a branch of the celiac artery.</b> The spleen lies behind left ribs 9 to 11. Its splenic artery is the largest branch of the celiac trunk.<br>A. Ribs 7–9: too high.<br>C. Superior mesenteric artery: supplies the midgut, not the spleen.<br>D. Ribs 11–12 and SMA (superior mesenteric artery): kidney level and the wrong artery.<br>E. Ribs 11–12 and IMA (inferior mesenteric artery): kidney level and a hindgut artery.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Pancreas & Spleen slide 22 (Largest lymphatic organ, very vascular Located in left upper quadrant (LUQ))","Pancreas & Spleen slide 29 (Spleen)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":77,"f":"F1252"}]},{"id":"F3902-135-1","s":"A medical student is trying to find the organ located in relation to ribs 9 through 11 on the left side of the body. Name the organ and its arterial blood supply.","o":["A. Pancreas, Inferior Pancreaticoduodenal Artery","B. Spleen, Right Gastric Artery","C. Liver, Hepatic Artery","D. Spleen, Splenic Artery","E. Pancreas, Superior Mesenteric Artery"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Spleen, Splenic Artery","a":"D","why":"<b>Spleen, splenic artery.</b> The spleen lies behind left ribs 9 to 11. It is fed by the splenic artery, a celiac trunk branch that winds along the top of the pancreas.<br>A. Pancreas: deep near the midline, not under ribs 9–11.<br>B. Spleen is right, but the right gastric artery supplies the lesser curvature of the stomach.<br>C. Liver: on the right, under ribs 7–11.<br>E. Pancreas: wrong organ for ribs 9–11.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Pancreas & Spleen slide 22 (Largest lymphatic organ, very vascular Located in left upper quadrant (LUQ))","Pancreas & Spleen slide 29 (Spleen)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":135,"f":"F3902"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G5","u":134,"label":"Pancreatic tail tumor threatens splenic blood supply","slides":[{"src":"sl/G007-15.jpg","cap":"Splenic artery running along the upper pancreas to the splenic hilum","where":"2026 lecture: Pancreas & Spleen, slide 15"},{"src":"sl/G007-11.jpg","cap":"Tail closely related to the splenic hilum, with the splenic vessels","where":"2026 lecture: Pancreas & Spleen, slide 11"}],"q":[{"id":"F1180-350-1","s":"A 48 year old man is admitted to the hospital with severe abdominal pain. Radiological examination reveals a tumor in the tail of the pancreas. A diagnostic arteriogram shows that the tumor has compromised the blood supply to another organ. Which of the following organs is most likely to have its blood supply compromised by this tumor?","o":["A. Duodenum","B. Gallbladder","C. Kidney","D. Liver","E. Spleen"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Spleen","a":"E","why":"<b>Spleen.</b> The splenic artery runs along the upper border of the pancreas to the splenic hilum, and the tail reaches that hilum. A tail tumor can therefore squeeze the splenic artery and vein.<br>A. Duodenum: fed by the gastroduodenal and pancreaticoduodenal arteries, near the head.<br>B. Gallbladder: fed by the cystic artery, far from the pancreas.<br>C. Kidney: fed by renal arteries straight from the aorta.<br>D. Liver: fed by the hepatic artery and portal vein, on the head side.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Pancreas & Spleen slide 11 (Pancreas)","Pancreas & Spleen slide 29 (Spleen)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":350,"f":"F1180"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G5","u":135,"label":"Spleen lies deep to left ribs 9–11","slides":[{"src":"sl/G007-22.jpg","cap":"Spleen in left upper quadrant against ribs 9–11, lateral view numbered","where":"2026 lecture: Pancreas & Spleen, slide 22"},{"src":"sl/G007-25.jpg","cap":"Spleen in situ under the diaphragm beside the 9th rib","where":"2026 lecture: Pancreas & Spleen, slide 25"},{"src":"sl/F1180-136.jpg","cap":"Rib relations: spleen left ribs 9–11, liver right 7–11, kidneys 11–12","where":"2022 Harlem TA review, slide 136"}],"q":[{"id":"F3900-125-1","s":"A teenage girl is rushed to the hospital due to left sided abdominal pain, shoulder pain, confusion, lightheadedness and distended abdomen. Upon arrival it turns out she was in a serious accident is suspected to have many broken ribs. You suspect a splenic rupture given her symptoms and past history of a serious viral infection. Which ribs does the spleen normally have a relationship with and should be evaluated for fracture?","o":["A. Ribs 7-8 on left","B. Ribs 9-11 on left","C. Ribs 11-12 on the right","D. Rib 12 on the left"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Ribs 9-11 on left","a":"B","why":"<b>Ribs 9–11 on the left.</b> The spleen lies in the left upper quadrant behind the stomach. Behind it, the diaphragm separates it from ribs 9 to 11, so broken ribs there can tear it. Left shoulder pain (Kehr's sign) comes from blood irritating the diaphragm.<br>A. Ribs 7–8, left: too high and forward; over the stomach.<br>C. Ribs 11–12, right: the right kidney area.<br>D. Rib 12, left: the left kidney area.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Pancreas & Spleen slide 22 (Largest lymphatic organ, very vascular Located in left upper quadrant (LUQ))","Pancreas & Spleen slide 28 (Ribs 9-11)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":125,"f":"F3900"}],"t":1},{"id":"F1156-88-1","s":"Someone was standing next to his friend that was swinging a baseball bat that hit him on the left side of his back. He was rushed to the emergency room. At what rib levels is the organ that the doctors are most concerned about?","o":["A. Ribs 7-11","B. Ribs 9-11","C. Ribs 11-12","D. Ribs 10-12"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Ribs 9-11","a":"B","why":"<b>Ribs 9–11.</b> A hit on the left lower back puts the spleen most at risk. It sits behind left ribs 9 to 11 and bleeds heavily when torn.<br>A. Ribs 7–11: the liver's ribs, on the right side.<br>C. Ribs 11–12: the left kidney; it can be hurt too, but bleeds less dangerously.<br>D. Ribs 10–12: not the range of any key organ.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Pancreas & Spleen slide 22 (Largest lymphatic organ, very vascular Located in left upper quadrant (LUQ))","Pancreas & Spleen slide 28 (Ribs 9-11)"],"src":[{"t":"? TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":88,"f":"F1156"}]},{"id":"F1180-137-1","s":"A patient presents to the emergency room after suffering a snowboarding accident. His partner says he lost control on a patch of ice and struck a tree. You notice his breathing is labored and his blood pressure is rapidly dropping. You suspect a splenic rupture, and immediately rush him to imaging (after stabilizing his blood pressure!) What ribs were likely fractured to cause the splenic rupture?","o":["A. Ribs 6, 7, & 8, right side","B. Ribs 7 & 8, left side","C. Ribs 10 & 11, right side","D. Ribs 10 & 11, left side"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Ribs 10 & 11, left side","a":"D","why":"<b>Ribs 10 and 11, left side.</b> The spleen lies against left ribs 9 to 11, so fractures of ribs 10 and 11 can tear it. Labored breathing and falling blood pressure fit heavy internal bleeding.<br>A. Ribs 6–8, right: over the liver.<br>B. Ribs 7–8, left: above the spleen, over the stomach.<br>C. Ribs 10–11, right: the liver and right kidney side.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Pancreas & Spleen slide 22 (Largest lymphatic organ, very vascular Located in left upper quadrant (LUQ))","Pancreas & Spleen slide 28 (Ribs 9-11)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":137,"f":"F1180"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":44,"f":"F3901"}]},{"id":"F1180-327-1","s":"A 19-year-old male is rushed to the emergency room after being tackled during football practice. A physical exam reveals a bruise on the left side between the 9th and 10th ribs posterior to the axillary line. Which of the following organs most likely has been injured?","o":["A. Descending colon","B. Pancreas","C. Left kidney","D. Left lobe of the liver","E. Spleen"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Spleen","a":"E","why":"<b>Spleen.</b> A blow to left ribs 9 and 10 behind the axillary line lands right over the spleen, which lies against ribs 9 to 11.<br>A. Descending colon: lower down in the left flank.<br>B. Pancreas: deep near the midline, behind the stomach.<br>C. Left kidney: lower, at ribs 11 and 12.<br>D. Left lobe of the liver: at the front of the upper abdomen near the midline, not back at the axillary line.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Pancreas & Spleen slide 22 (Largest lymphatic organ, very vascular Located in left upper quadrant (LUQ))","Pancreas & Spleen slide 28 (Ribs 9-11)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":327,"f":"F1180"},{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":266,"f":"F1178"}],"t":1},{"id":"F1243-70-1","s":"A 13-year old girl is brought to the ER after falling from a horse onto her left side. Her pulse is 120/min, BP is 90/60 mmHg, and respirations are 30/min. Physical examination reveals cold and clammy skin, bruising, and fractured 9th and 10th ribs on her left side at the posterior axillary line. Which of the following organs is most likely damaged in this patient?","o":["A. Stomach","B. Kidney","C. Lung","D. Liver","E. Spleen"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Spleen","a":"E","why":"<b>Spleen.</b> Left ribs 9 and 10 at the posterior axillary line lie right over the spleen. A fast pulse, low blood pressure and cold, clammy skin mean shock from internal bleeding, which is typical of a torn spleen.<br>A. Stomach: front of the left upper quadrant; rarely bleeds like this after a fall.<br>B. Kidney: lower, at ribs 11 and 12.<br>C. Lung: injury here mainly causes breathing trouble or a collapsed lung, not shock.<br>D. Liver: on the right side.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Pancreas & Spleen slide 22 (Largest lymphatic organ, very vascular Located in left upper quadrant (LUQ))","Pancreas & Spleen slide 28 (Ribs 9-11)"],"src":[{"t":"? tutor review","n":"old Exam 2: abdomen part only","sl":70,"f":"F1243"}]},{"id":"D045-111-1","s":"A 65-year-old woman is injured in an automobile accident. Examination indicates she suffers fractures of left ribs 8 to 10 and profuse internal bleeding. Which of the structures indicated in the given CT scan of the abdomen will likely need to be surgically removed?","o":["A. A","B. B","C. C","D. D","E. E"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"C (spleen)","a":"C","why":"<b>C, the spleen.</b> On a CT (computed tomography) image, the patient's left is on the screen's right. C is the solid organ at the back of the left upper quadrant, behind the air-filled stomach. Fractured left ribs 8–10 can tear it, and heavy bleeding often means splenectomy.<br>A. Liver: the large organ on the patient's right.<br>B. Stomach: the black air bubble in front.<br>D. Back muscles (erector spinae) beside the spine.<br>E. Abdominal aorta: the round bright vessel in front of the vertebra.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/D045-111.jpg","in":"Axial contrast CT of the upper abdomen with letters A-E: A = liver, B = stomach (air-filled), C = spleen, D = erector spinae, E = abdominal aorta (per the notes' choice explanations)","tg":["Pancreas & Spleen slide 28 (Ribs 9-11)","Pancreas & Spleen slide 23 (Spleen)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":111,"f":"D045"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G5","u":136,"label":"Gastrosplenic ligament: spleen to greater curvature","slides":[{"src":"sl/G007-26.jpg","cap":"Axial section: gastrosplenic ligament to stomach, splenorenal ligament to left kidney","where":"2026 lecture: Pancreas & Spleen, slide 26"},{"src":"sl/G007-27.jpg","cap":"Gastrosplenic and lienorenal ligaments with the vessels each one carries","where":"2026 lecture: Pancreas & Spleen, slide 27"}],"q":[{"id":"F3900-38-1","s":"The gastrosplenic ligament has attachments from the ______ to the __________.","o":["A. Jejunum and the greater curvature of the stomach","B. Spleen and the greater curvature of the stomach","C. Spleen and the lesser curvature of the stomach","D. Ileum and the lesser curvature of the stomach"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Spleen and the greater curvature of the stomach","a":"B","why":"<b>Spleen and greater curvature of the stomach.</b> The gastrosplenic ligament runs from the splenic hilum to the greater curvature. It carries the short gastric and left gastro-omental vessels.<br>A. Jejunum: hangs on the mesentery, far from the spleen.<br>C. Lesser curvature: tied to the liver by the hepatogastric ligament.<br>D. Ileum and lesser curvature: neither is tied to the spleen.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Pancreas & Spleen slide 23 (Spleen)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":38,"f":"F3900"}],"t":1},{"id":"F1228-219-1","s":"Which of the following is not a ligamentous attachment of the spleen?","o":["A. Splenorenal ligament","B. Gastrosplenic ligament","C. Hepatosplenic Ligament"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Hepatosplenic Ligament","a":"C","why":"<b>Hepatosplenic ligament.</b> No such ligament exists; the liver and spleen are not tied together. The spleen's two main ligaments both attach at its hilum: the gastrosplenic (to the greater curvature) and the splenorenal (to the left kidney). The spleen also rests on the phrenicocolic ligament.<br>A. Splenorenal: real; it holds the splenic vessels and the pancreatic tail.<br>B. Gastrosplenic: real; it holds the short gastric and left gastro-omental vessels.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Pancreas & Spleen slide 23 (Spleen)"],"src":[{"t":"2019 review","n":"old Exam 2 review: abdomen part only","sl":219,"f":"F1228"}],"t":1},{"id":"F1156-14-1","s":"The spleen is a very important yet fragile organ, due to the fact that it contains so much blood. When injured it lead to serious complications. The body therefore provides support to the spleen so that it moves around inside the body. What anatomical structure limits the movement of the spleen anteriorly?","o":["A. Left kidney","B. Diaphragm","C. Gastrosplenic ligament","D. Splenocolic ligament","E. Phrenicocolic ligament"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Gastrosplenic ligament","a":"C","why":"<b>Gastrosplenic ligament.</b> The stomach lies in front of and medial to the spleen. The gastrosplenic ligament ties the splenic hilum to the greater curvature, holding the spleen from the front.<br>A. Left kidney: behind and medial to the spleen.<br>B. Diaphragm: above and behind it.<br>D. Splenocolic ligament: holds the lower pole to the left colic flexure.<br>E. Phrenicocolic ligament: the shelf the spleen sits on, from below.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Pancreas & Spleen slide 23 (Spleen)"],"src":[{"t":"? TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":14,"f":"F1156"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G5","u":136,"label":"Short gastric arteries run in gastrosplenic ligament","slides":[{"src":"sl/G007-27.jpg","cap":"Gastrosplenic ligament carries short gastric and left gastro-epiploic arteries","where":"2026 lecture: Pancreas & Spleen, slide 27"},{"src":"sl/G007-25.jpg","cap":"Short gastric vessels running in the gastrosplenic ligament to the stomach","where":"2026 lecture: Pancreas & Spleen, slide 25"}],"q":[{"id":"F3907-58-1","s":"A surgeon is asked to perform on the spleen and specifically ligate the short gastric arteries. As the genius med student you are, you tell them you can easily find those arteries if they find which ligament?","o":["A. Splenorenal ligament","B. Gastrosplenic Ligament","C. Suspensory Ligament","D. Peritoneal Ligament"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Gastrosplenic Ligament","a":"B","why":"<b>Gastrosplenic ligament.</b> The short gastric arteries leave the splenic artery near the hilum. They run in this ligament to the fundus of the stomach, along with the left gastro-omental vessels.<br>A. Splenorenal ligament: holds the splenic artery and vein and the pancreatic tail.<br>C. Suspensory ligament: not a splenic ligament; the suspensory ligament of the duodenum is the ligament of Treitz.<br>D. 'Peritoneal ligament': a general term, not one specific structure.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Pancreas & Spleen slide 23 (Spleen)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA weekly","n":"10-06-2022 TA session (foregut viscera)","sl":58,"f":"F3907"}],"t":1},{"id":"F0003-6-1","s":"After being hit by a speeding vehicle, a 35-year-old male is rushed to the ER. His ribs were crushed on his left side and his spleen was perforated. The damage is severe and a splenectomy (removal of the spleen) has to be performed. All is going well during the surgery until the surgeon accidentally tears the gastrosplenic ligament. Which of the following structures is most at risk of having been damaged by this?","o":["A. Splenic artery","B. Tail of the pancreas","C. Right gastro-omental artery","D. Short gastric arteries","E. Kidney"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Short gastric arteries","a":"D","why":"<b>Short gastric arteries.</b> They run from the splenic artery to the fundus of the stomach inside the gastrosplenic ligament, so tearing it rips them.<br>A. Splenic artery: runs in the splenorenal ligament.<br>B. Tail of the pancreas: lies in the splenorenal ligament.<br>C. Right gastro-omental artery: runs along the right greater curvature, from the gastroduodenal artery; the left one is in this ligament.<br>E. Kidney: behind the peritoneum, linked to the spleen by the splenorenal ligament.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Pancreas & Spleen slide 23 (Spleen)"],"src":[{"t":"? Middletown TA review","n":"MT Abdomen Review","sl":6,"f":"F0003"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G5","u":137,"label":"Splenorenal ligament: pancreatic tail and splenic vessels","slides":[{"src":"sl/G007-27.jpg","cap":"Lienorenal ligament holds splenic artery, vein, pancreatic tail; gastrosplenic holds short gastrics","where":"2026 lecture: Pancreas & Spleen, slide 27"},{"src":"sl/G007-25.jpg","cap":"Spleen in situ: splenorenal ligament with splenic vessels and tail of pancreas","where":"2026 lecture: Pancreas & Spleen, slide 25"},{"src":"sl/G007-11.jpg","cap":"Pancreatic tail passes between the layers of the splenorenal ligament","where":"2026 lecture: Pancreas & Spleen, slide 11"}],"q":[{"id":"F3902-20-1","s":"A 32 year-old male was stabbed in the ligament that connects the left kidney and spleen. What vessels are in this ligament and are at risk for injury?","o":["A. Portal vein, hepatic artery, common bile duct","B. Right and left gastric arteries","C. Short gastric artery, short gastric vein, left gastroepiploic artery, left gastroepiploic vein","D. Tail of pancreas, splenic artery, splenic vein"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Tail of pancreas, splenic artery, splenic vein","a":"D","why":"<b>Tail of pancreas, splenic artery, splenic vein.</b> The splenorenal ligament runs from the left kidney to the splenic hilum. It carries the splenic vessels and the tail of the pancreas.<br>A. Portal vein, hepatic artery, common bile duct: the portal triad, in the hepatoduodenal ligament.<br>B. Right and left gastric arteries: in the hepatogastric ligament, along the lesser curvature.<br>C. Short gastric and left gastroepiploic vessels: in the gastrosplenic ligament.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Pancreas & Spleen slide 11 (Pancreas)","Pancreas & Spleen slide 23 (Spleen)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":20,"f":"F3902"}]},{"id":"F3112-49-1","s":"Due to his fluctuating levels of insulin, a 62 year old male undergoes a series of tests. A tumor is detected in the tail of the pancreas. The tumor's definite borders would make it easy to excise. However, before the surgeon can reach the tumor, which of the following ligaments needs to be dissected?","o":["A. Splenorenal","B. Gastrosplenic","C. Gastrocolic","D. Hepatorenal","E. Hepatogastric"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Splenorenal","a":"A","why":"<b>Splenorenal ligament.</b> The tail of the pancreas runs inside this ligament, with the splenic artery and vein, toward the hilum of the spleen. To reach a tail tumor, the surgeon opens this ligament.<br>B. Gastrosplenic: spleen to greater curvature; holds the short gastric and left gastro-omental vessels.<br>C. Gastrocolic: greater curvature to transverse colon (part of the greater omentum).<br>D. Hepatorenal: liver to right kidney, on the far right.<br>E. Hepatogastric: liver to lesser curvature (part of the lesser omentum).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Pancreas & Spleen slide 11 (Pancreas)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"Peritoneum & celiac trunk interactive (JL)","sl":49,"f":"F3112"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3900-98-1","s":"A 42-year-old female is admitted to the hospital after a traumatic landing while skydiving. Radiographic examination reveals a ruptured spleen. An emergency splenectomy is performed. Which of the following peritoneal structures must be carefully manipulated to prevent intraperitoneal bleeding?","o":["A. Coronary ligament","B. Gastrocolic ligament","C. Splenorenal ligament","D. Phrenocolic ligament","E. Falciform ligament"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Splenorenal ligament","a":"C","why":"<b>Splenorenal ligament.</b> It carries the splenic artery and vein (and the pancreatic tail) to the hilum. During splenectomy the surgeon must handle it carefully and tie these vessels, or the abdomen fills with blood.<br>A. Coronary ligament: attaches the liver to the diaphragm.<br>B. Gastrocolic ligament: joins the stomach to the transverse colon.<br>D. Phrenicocolic ligament: a shelf under the spleen with no large vessels.<br>E. Falciform ligament: joins the liver to the anterior abdominal wall.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Pancreas & Spleen slide 23 (Spleen)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":98,"f":"F3900"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":3,"f":"F3901"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3112-50-1","s":"Which two organs do the following ligaments connect? Splenorenal; Gastrosplenic; Gastrocolic; Hepatorenal; Hepatogastric. What is another name for the splenorenal ligament? Which portions of the pancreas are retroperitoneal and which are intraperitoneal?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Splenorenal – spleen and left kidney; gastrosplenic – spleen and stomach; gastrocolic – greater curvature of stomach to transverse colon (part of greater omentum); hepatorenal – liver to right kidney; hepatogastric – liver to lesser curvature (part of lesser omentum). Splenorenal = lienorenal ligament. Head, neck and body of pancreas are retroperitoneal; the tail is intraperitoneal.","a":null,"why":"<b>Each ligament is named for the two organs it joins.</b> Splenorenal: spleen to left kidney. Gastrosplenic: spleen to greater curvature. Gastrocolic: greater curvature to transverse colon. Hepatorenal: liver to right kidney. Hepatogastric: liver to lesser curvature. The pancreatic head, neck and body are retroperitoneal; the <b>tail</b> is intraperitoneal, inside the splenorenal ligament.<br>Common mix-up: 'lieno-' just means spleen, so the lienorenal and splenorenal ligaments are the same ligament.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Pancreas & Spleen slide 11 (Pancreas)","Pancreas & Spleen slide 23 (Spleen)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Middletown clicker","n":"Peritoneum & celiac trunk interactive (JL)","sl":50,"f":"F3112"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G6","u":138,"label":"Withdrawal reflex is somatic, not autonomic","slides":[{"src":"sl/G008-6.jpg","cap":"Somatic vs autonomic table: voluntary skeletal muscle vs smooth muscle, heart, glands","where":"2026 lecture: Autonomic NS, slide 6"},{"src":"sl/G008-3.jpg","cap":"Divisions of nervous system: somatic voluntary, autonomic involuntary","where":"2026 lecture: Autonomic NS, slide 3"}],"q":[{"id":"F2936-420-1","s":"The following reflexes are all autonomic Except:","o":["A. Salivary secretion.","B. Micturition.","C. Flexion of the arm following a painful stimulus.","D. Acceleration of the heart"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Flexion of the arm following a painful stimulus.","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Flexion of the arm after a painful stimulus.</b> This withdrawal reflex runs through somatic nerves to skeletal muscle, so it is not autonomic.<br>A. Salivary secretion: a gland, run by autonomic nerves.<br>B. Micturition (emptying the bladder): a parasympathetic reflex contracts the bladder's smooth muscle.<br>D. Speeding up the heart: sympathetic nerves act on heart muscle.<br>Rule: autonomic nerves supply smooth muscle, heart muscle and glands; somatic nerves supply skeletal muscle.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["ANS slide 3 (Divisions of the nervous system)","ANS slide 6 (Difference between the Somatic and Autonomic Nervous systems)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 clicker","n":"combined clickers for the final: abdomen/GI-embryo clickers only","sl":420,"f":"F2936"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G6","u":138,"label":"Preganglionic body in brain/cord; postganglionic in ganglion","slides":[{"src":"sl/G008-7.jpg","cap":"Two-neuron chain: preganglionic body in spinal cord, postganglionic body in ganglion","where":"2026 lecture: Autonomic NS, slide 7"},{"src":"sl/G008-10.jpg","cap":"Paravertebral chain and prevertebral ganglia hold postganglionic sympathetic cell bodies","where":"2026 lecture: Autonomic NS, slide 10"}],"q":[{"id":"D042-176-1","s":"Which statement correctly describes a preganglionic fiber?","o":["A. It originates in a ganglion and is unmyelinated","B. It originates in the CNS and synapses in a ganglion","C. It innervates the target organ directly and is myelinated","D. It is only found in the parasympathetic system"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"It originates in the CNS and synapses in a ganglion","a":"B","why":"<b>B. It starts in the central nervous system and synapses in a ganglion.</b> The preganglionic cell body sits in the gray matter of the brainstem or spinal cord; its axon ends in an autonomic ganglion.<br>A. Starts in a ganglion and is unmyelinated: that is the postganglionic fiber.<br>C. Reaches the target organ directly and is myelinated: the postganglionic fiber reaches the target; a single direct neuron is somatic.<br>D. Only parasympathetic: both divisions have preganglionic fibers.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["ANS slide 7 (Pre/Postganglionic system)","ANS slide 6 (Difference between the Somatic and Autonomic Nervous systems)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI module TA review","sl":176,"f":"D042"}],"t":1},{"id":"F2936-2-1","s":"Which of the following will be seen in a ‘ganglion’?","o":["A. Cell bodies of somatic motor neurons","B. Cell bodies of spinal efferent neurons","C. Postganglionic sympathetic neuronal cell body","D. Preganglionic parasympathetic neuronal cell body","E. Preganglionic sympathetic neuronal cell body"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Postganglionic sympathetic neuronal cell body","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Postganglionic sympathetic cell body.</b> A ganglion is a cluster of nerve cell bodies outside the brain and spinal cord. Autonomic ganglia hold the cell bodies of the second (postganglionic) neuron.<br>A. Somatic motor neurons: cell bodies in the anterior horn of the spinal cord.<br>B. Spinal efferent neurons: also inside the spinal cord.<br>D. Preganglionic parasympathetic: brainstem and sacral 2 to 4 cord segments.<br>E. Preganglionic sympathetic: lateral horn of thoracic 1 to lumbar 2.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["ANS slide 7 (Pre/Postganglionic system)","ANS slide 10 (Cell bodies of postganglionic fibers will be located in 2 locations)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 clicker","n":"combined clickers for the final: abdomen/GI-embryo clickers only","sl":2,"f":"F2936"}]},{"id":"F2936-445-1","s":"Prevertebral and paravertebral ganglia","o":["A. Cell bodies of the presynaptic neurons of the sympathetic nervous system","B. Cell bodies of the presynaptic neurons of the parasympathetic neurons","C. Cell bodies of the postynaptic neurons of the sympathetic nervous system","D. Cell bodies of the potsynaptic neurons of the parasympathetic nervous system"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Cell bodies of the postsynaptic neurons of the sympathetic nervous system","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Cell bodies of postsynaptic (postganglionic) sympathetic neurons.</b> Paravertebral ganglia (the chain beside the vertebrae) and prevertebral ganglia (celiac and mesenteric, in front of the aorta) are both sympathetic, and both hold the second neuron.<br>A. Presynaptic sympathetic: lateral horn of thoracic 1 to lumbar 2.<br>B. Presynaptic parasympathetic: brainstem and sacral 2 to 4 cord.<br>D. Postsynaptic parasympathetic: the 4 head ganglia or small ganglia in the organ walls.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["ANS slide 10 (Cell bodies of postganglionic fibers will be located in 2 locations)","ANS slide 8 (Pre/Postganglionic system)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 clicker","n":"combined clickers for the final: abdomen/GI-embryo clickers only","sl":445,"f":"F2936"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G6","u":139,"label":"Sympathetic short pre/long post; parasympathetic reverse","slides":[{"src":"sl/G008-8.jpg","cap":"Ganglia locations and fiber lengths: sympathetic short-long, parasympathetic long-short","where":"2026 lecture: Autonomic NS, slide 8"},{"src":"sl/G008-26.jpg","cap":"Summary table: origin, fiber length, ganglia, transmitters for both divisions","where":"2026 lecture: Autonomic NS, slide 26"}],"q":[{"id":"D042-178-1","s":"Long or Short? Preganglionic sympathetic nerve fiber – long or short; Postganglionic sympathetic nerve fiber – long or short; Preganglionic parasympathetic nerve fiber – long or short; Postganglionic parasympathetic nerve fiber – long or short","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Preganglionic sympathetic – short; postganglionic sympathetic – long; preganglionic parasympathetic – long; postganglionic parasympathetic – short.","a":null,"why":"<b>Sympathetic: short pre, long post. Parasympathetic: long pre, short post.</b> Fiber length follows where the ganglion sits. Sympathetic ganglia lie close to the spinal cord (the chain beside the vertebrae, or in front of the aorta), so the first fiber is short and the second travels far. Parasympathetic ganglia lie next to or inside the target organ, so the first fiber is long and the second is tiny.<br>Common mix-up: flipping the two divisions. Picture where each ganglion lives.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["ANS slide 8 (Pre/Postganglionic system)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI module TA review","sl":178,"f":"D042"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G6","u":139,"label":"Head ganglia are parasympathetic, not sympathetic","slides":[{"src":"sl/G008-8.jpg","cap":"Sympathetic ganglia beside vertebrae or aorta; four head ganglia are parasympathetic","where":"2026 lecture: Autonomic NS, slide 8"},{"src":"sl/G008-26.jpg","cap":"Summary table: sympathetic ganglia lateral to vertebrae or in front of aorta","where":"2026 lecture: Autonomic NS, slide 26"}],"q":[{"id":"F2936-423-1","s":"Which of the below does NOT describe the Sympathetic division?","o":["A. Ganglion in the head","B. Short preganglionic neurons","C. Thoracolumbar output","D. Synapse with blood vessels","E. Stimulate sweat glands"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Ganglion in the head","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Ganglion in the head.</b> The four autonomic ganglia in the head (ciliary, pterygopalatine, otic, submandibular) are parasympathetic. Sympathetic ganglia lie beside the vertebrae or in front of the aorta; the highest one, the superior cervical ganglion, is in the neck.<br>B. Short preganglionic neurons: true of sympathetic.<br>C. Thoracolumbar output (thoracic 1 to lumbar 2): true.<br>D. Synapse with blood vessels: true; sympathetic fibers supply all vessels.<br>E. Stimulate sweat glands: true; only sympathetic fibers reach them.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["ANS slide 8 (Pre/Postganglionic system)","ANS slide 10 (Cell bodies of postganglionic fibers will be located in 2 locations)","ANS slide 16 (Sympathetic Division)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 clicker","n":"combined clickers for the final: abdomen/GI-embryo clickers only","sl":423,"f":"F2936"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G6","u":140,"label":"Sympathetic preganglionic cell bodies: lateral horn T1–L2","slides":[{"src":"sl/G008-9.jpg","cap":"Lateral horn of T1–L2 holds sympathetic preganglionic cell bodies, by body region","where":"2026 lecture: Autonomic NS, slide 9"},{"src":"sl/G008-16.jpg","cap":"Spinal segments: head, neck and upper limb T1–T4; sweat glands sympathetic","where":"2026 lecture: Autonomic NS, slide 16"}],"q":[{"id":"D042-180-1","s":"Where are sympathetic preganglionic neuron cell bodies located?","o":["A. Dorsal horn of the spinal cord","B. Lateral horn of T1-L2 spinal cord","C. Cranial Nerve III, VII, IX and X","D. Prevertebral ganglia","E. Paravertebral ganglia"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Lateral horn of T1-L2 spinal cord","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Lateral horn of the T1–L2 (thoracic 1 to lumbar 2) cord.</b> Sympathetic preganglionic cell bodies sit in the lateral horn gray matter of these segments, which is why the system is called thoracolumbar.<br>A. Dorsal horn: sensory neurons.<br>C. Cranial nerves III, VII, IX, X (oculomotor, facial, glossopharyngeal, vagus): parasympathetic.<br>D. Prevertebral and E. paravertebral ganglia: hold postganglionic, not preganglionic, sympathetic cell bodies.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["ANS slide 9 (Sympathetic Division)","ANS slide 11 (Course of Sympathetic fibers)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI module TA review","sl":180,"f":"D042"}],"t":1},{"id":"F2936-16-1","s":"Almost all the cell bodies of sympathetic fibers are located in which part of central nervous system?","o":["A. Cervical segment of spinal cord","B. Sacral segment of spinal cord","C. Thoracic segment of spinal cord","D. Coccygeal segment of spinal cord","E. Grey matter of brain"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Thoracic segment of spinal cord","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Thoracic segments of the spinal cord.</b> Sympathetic preganglionic cell bodies lie in the lateral horn from thoracic 1 to lumbar 2. Twelve of those fourteen segments are thoracic.<br>A. Cervical cord: no sympathetic outflow; the neck gets fibers that climb the chain from the upper thoracic cord.<br>B. Sacral cord: sacral 2 to 4 holds parasympathetic cell bodies.<br>D. Coccygeal cord: no autonomic outflow.<br>E. Brain gray matter: brainstem autonomic nuclei are parasympathetic.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["ANS slide 9 (Sympathetic Division)","ANS slide 5 (Divisions of the ANS)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 clicker","n":"combined clickers for the final: abdomen/GI-embryo clickers only","sl":16,"f":"F2936"}]},{"id":"F2936-6-1","s":"A lesion in which of the following will abolish sweating from the upper limb?","o":["A. Dorsal rami of the first four thoracic spinal nerves","B. Lateral grey horn in the first four thoracic segments of the spinal cord","C. Posterior grey horn in the first four thoracic segments of the spinal cord","D. Posterior roots of the first four thoracic spinal nerves","E. The first four thoracic spinal ganglia"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Lateral grey horn in the first four thoracic segments of the spinal cord","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Lateral gray horn of the first four thoracic segments.</b> Sweat glands get only sympathetic fibers. For the upper limb, the preganglionic cell bodies sit in the lateral horn of thoracic 1 to 4; destroy them and the arm stops sweating.<br>A. Dorsal rami of thoracic 1 to 4: supply the skin of the back, not the arm.<br>C. Posterior gray horn: sensory neurons.<br>D. Posterior roots: sensory fibers only.<br>E. First four thoracic spinal ganglia: dorsal root ganglia, which hold sensory cell bodies.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["ANS slide 16 (Sympathetic Division)","ANS slide 9 (Sympathetic Division)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 clicker","n":"combined clickers for the final: abdomen/GI-embryo clickers only","sl":6,"f":"F2936"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G6","u":141,"label":"White ramus in, gray ramus out","slides":[{"src":"sl/G008-11.jpg","cap":"Sympathetic course: lateral horn, ventral root, white ramus, ganglion, gray ramus","where":"2026 lecture: Autonomic NS, slide 11"}],"q":[{"id":"F2936-4-1","s":"Which of the following are found in grey rami communicans?","o":["A. Postganglionic parasympathetic axons","B. Postganglionic sympathetic axons","C. Preganglionic parasympathetic axons","D. Preganglionic sympathetic axons","E. Somatic motor axons"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Postganglionic sympathetic axons","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Postganglionic sympathetic axons.</b> After synapsing in a chain ganglion, unmyelinated postganglionic fibers return to the spinal nerve through the gray ramus. Gray = unmyelinated = postganglionic.<br>A. Postganglionic parasympathetic: start in ganglia near or in the organ, never in the chain.<br>C. Preganglionic parasympathetic: travel in cranial nerves and pelvic splanchnic nerves.<br>D. Preganglionic sympathetic: enter the chain through the white ramus (myelinated).<br>E. Somatic motor axons: stay in the spinal nerve.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["ANS slide 11 (Course of Sympathetic fibers)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 clicker","n":"combined clickers for the final: abdomen/GI-embryo clickers only","sl":4,"f":"F2936"}]},{"id":"D042-184-1","s":"Which one is the right order of sympathetic signal pathway?","o":["A. Preganglionic fibers originate in the lateral horn of T1-L2→ exit via ventral root → communicate with white rami communicans → synapse in pre- or paravertebral ganglion → fibers from paravertebral ganglion re-enter to the spinal cord through grey rami → distributed by both ventral and dorsal rami","B. Preganglionic fibers originate in the lateral horn of T1-L2 →exit via ventral root → communicate with grey rami communicans → synapse in pre- or paravertebral ganglion → fibers from paravertebral ganglion re-enter to the spinal cord through white rami →distributed by both ventral and dorsal rami","C. Preganglionic fibers originate in the lateral horn of T1-L2 → exit via dorsal root → communicate with white rami communicans → synapse in pre- or paravertebral ganglion → fibers from paravertebral ganglion re-enter to the spinal cord through grey rami →distributed by both ventral and dorsal rami","D. Preganglionic fibers originate in the lateral horn of T1-L2 → exit via dorsal root → communicate with grey rami communicans → synapse in pre- or paravertebral ganglion → fibers from paravertebral ganglion re-enter to the spinal cord through grey rami →distributed by both ventral and dorsal rami"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Preganglionic fibers originate in the lateral horn of T1-L2→ exit via ventral root → communicate with white rami communicans → synapse in pre- or paravertebral ganglion → fibers from paravertebral ganglion re-enter to the spinal cord through grey rami → distributed by both ventral and dorsal rami","a":"A","why":"<b>A.</b> Lateral horn of thoracic 1 to lumbar 2 → <b>ventral</b> (anterior) root → <b>white</b> ramus (myelinated, preganglionic) → synapse in a paravertebral or prevertebral ganglion → postganglionic fibers return through the <b>gray</b> ramus (unmyelinated) → spread by dorsal and ventral rami. (Strictly, they rejoin the spinal nerve, not the spinal cord.)<br>B. Swaps the rami: white goes in, gray comes back.<br>C. Dorsal root: carries only sensory fibers.<br>D. Wrong twice: dorsal root and gray ramus on the way in.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["ANS slide 11 (Course of Sympathetic fibers)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI module TA review","sl":184,"f":"D042"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G6","u":141,"label":"Four routes of a sympathetic preganglionic fiber","slides":[{"src":"sl/G008-12.jpg","cap":"Four routes: ascend, descend, synapse at same level, or pass through as splanchnic","where":"2026 lecture: Autonomic NS, slide 12"}],"q":[{"id":"D042-182-1","s":"Sympathetic preganglionic fiber may:","o":["A. Synapse in the paravertebral ganglion at the same level","B. Ascend the sympathetic chain before synapsing","C. Descend the sympathetic chain before synapsing","D. Pass through the chain without synapsing to treach prevertebral ganglia","E. All of the above"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"All of the above","a":"E","why":"<b>E. All of the above.</b> Once in the sympathetic chain, a preganglionic fiber can take any of four routes:<br>A. Synapse in the ganglion at its own level.<br>B. Climb the chain first (how thoracic 1 fibers reach the head and neck).<br>C. Run down the chain first (how lumbar 2 fibers reach the pelvis and leg).<br>D. Pass through without synapsing, as a splanchnic nerve, to a prevertebral ganglion (the route to abdominal and pelvic organs).<br>Every option is a real route, so none is wrong.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["ANS slide 12 (Course of Sympathetic fibers)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI module TA review","sl":182,"f":"D042"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G6","u":142,"label":"Horner syndrome: broken sympathetic path to head","slides":[{"src":"sl/G008-17.jpg","cap":"T1–T2 fibers climb to superior cervical ganglion; carotid plexus to eye, eyelid","where":"2026 lecture: Autonomic NS, slide 17"},{"src":"sl/G008-18.jpg","cap":"Horner syndrome: ptosis, miosis, anhidrosis from cervical chain lesion or Pancoast tumor","where":"2026 lecture: Autonomic NS, slide 18"}],"q":[{"id":"F2936-429-1","s":"A 45-year-old woman is admitted to the hospital with severe dyspnea. Radiographic examination confirms the presence of a Pancoast tumor. Physical examination reveals that the patient has miosis of the pupil, partial ptosis of the eyelid, and anhydrosis of the face. Which of the following structures has most likely been injured?","o":["A. Sympathetic chain","B. Vagus nerve","C. Phrenic nerve","D. Arch of aorta","E. Cardiopulmonary plexus"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Sympathetic chain","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Sympathetic chain.</b> Miosis (small pupil), partial ptosis (droopy upper lid) and anhidrosis (no sweating) of the face are Horner syndrome: loss of sympathetic supply to the head. A Pancoast tumor at the lung apex presses on the cervicothoracic sympathetic chain.<br>B. Vagus nerve: parasympathetic; injury causes hoarseness, not Horner.<br>C. Phrenic nerve: paralyzes the diaphragm.<br>D. Arch of aorta: an artery, not a nerve.<br>E. Cardiopulmonary plexus: goes to heart and lungs, not the face.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["ANS slide 18 (Horner’s Syndrome)","ANS slide 17 (Sympathetic Innervation of the Head and Neck)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 clicker","n":"combined clickers for the final: abdomen/GI-embryo clickers only","sl":429,"f":"F2936"}],"t":1},{"id":"F2936-12-1","s":"A 45-year-old man presents with loss of sympathetic innervations to the left side of his head, characteristic of Horner syndrome. This could occur due to damage of which of the following structure?","o":["A. Cervical anterior roots","B. Thoracic posterior rami","C. Thoracic posterior roots","D. Thoracic anterior roots","E. Thoracic posterior rootlets"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Thoracic anterior roots","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Thoracic anterior (ventral) roots.</b> Sympathetic fibers to the head start in the lateral horn of thoracic 1 to 2, leave through the anterior roots, then climb the chain to the superior cervical ganglion. Cutting those roots gives Horner syndrome.<br>A. Cervical anterior roots: the cervical cord has no sympathetic outflow.<br>B. Thoracic posterior rami: carry postganglionic fibers to the back, not the head.<br>C. Thoracic posterior roots and E. posterior rootlets: sensory fibers only.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["ANS slide 11 (Course of Sympathetic fibers)","ANS slide 17 (Sympathetic Innervation of the Head and Neck)","ANS slide 18 (Horner’s Syndrome)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 clicker","n":"combined clickers for the final: abdomen/GI-embryo clickers only","sl":12,"f":"F2936"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G6","u":143,"label":"Thoracic splanchnics carry preganglionic sympathetic fibers","slides":[{"src":"sl/G008-27.jpg","cap":"Table: greater T5–T9, lesser T10–T11, least T12, all presynaptic sympathetic","where":"2026 lecture: Autonomic NS, slide 27"},{"src":"sl/G008-14.jpg","cap":"Splanchnic diagram: abdominopelvic splanchnics preganglionic, synapse in prevertebral ganglia","where":"2026 lecture: Autonomic NS, slide 14"}],"q":[{"id":"F2936-426-1","s":"Lesser splanchnic","o":["A. Carry preganglionic sympathetic fibers","B. Carry post ganglionic sympathetic fibers","C. Carry preganglionic parasympathetic fibers","D. Carry postganglionic parasympathetic fibers"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Carry preganglionic sympathetic fibers","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Preganglionic sympathetic fibers.</b> The greater, lesser and least splanchnic nerves are preganglionic sympathetic fibers that pass through the chain without synapsing. The lesser splanchnic (thoracic 10 to 11) synapses in prevertebral ganglia (aorticorenal and superior mesenteric).<br>B. Postganglionic sympathetic: only the cardiopulmonary splanchnics are.<br>C. Preganglionic parasympathetic: that is the pelvic splanchnics (sacral 2 to 4).<br>D. Postganglionic parasympathetic: short fibers inside organ walls, not named nerves.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["ANS slide 13 (Splanchnic nerve: nerve that conveys innervation to and from the viscera of the )","ANS slide 14 (Splanchnic nerve: nerve that conveys innervation to and from the viscera of the )","ANS slide 27 (| Splanchnic Nerves | Autonomic Fiber Type | System | Origin | Destination |)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 clicker","n":"combined clickers for the final: abdomen/GI-embryo clickers only","sl":426,"f":"F2936"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G6","u":143,"label":"Superior hypogastric plexus: in front of L5","slides":[{"src":"sl/G008-23.jpg","cap":"Pelvic plexus diagram: superior hypogastric plexus at aortic bifurcation, hypogastric nerves","where":"2026 lecture: Autonomic NS, slide 23"}],"q":[{"id":"F2936-440-1","s":"This plexus is located anterior to the fifth lumbar vertebra and supplies pelvic viscera.","o":["A. Superior hypogastric plexus","B. Superior mesenteric plexus","C. Inferior mesenteric plexus","D. Renal plexus","E. Celiac plexus"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Superior hypogastric plexus","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Superior hypogastric plexus.</b> It lies at the aortic bifurcation, in front of the fifth lumbar vertebra and sacral promontory, and splits into right and left hypogastric nerves that run to the pelvis.<br>B. Superior mesenteric plexus: around the superior mesenteric artery at the first lumbar vertebra.<br>C. Inferior mesenteric plexus: around the inferior mesenteric artery at the third lumbar vertebra.<br>D. Renal plexus: around the renal arteries, first to second lumbar.<br>E. Celiac plexus: around the celiac trunk at the twelfth thoracic vertebra.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["ANS slide 23 (Parasympathetic Division)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 clicker","n":"combined clickers for the final: abdomen/GI-embryo clickers only","sl":440,"f":"F2936"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G6","u":144,"label":"Parasympathetic preganglionic cell bodies: brainstem and S2–S4","slides":[{"src":"sl/G008-20.jpg","cap":"Parasympathetic preganglionic bodies: brainstem (cranial nerves III, VII, IX, X) and S2–S4","where":"2026 lecture: Autonomic NS, slide 20"},{"src":"sl/G008-26.jpg","cap":"Summary table: parasympathetic preganglionic neurons in brainstem and S2–S4 lateral horn","where":"2026 lecture: Autonomic NS, slide 26"}],"q":[{"id":"F2936-447-1","s":"Gray matter of S2-S4","o":["A. Cell bodies of the presynaptic neurons of the sympathetic nervous system","B. Cell bodies of the presynaptic neurons of the parasympathetic neurons","C. Cell bodies of the postynaptic neurons of the sympathetic nervous system","D. Cell bodies of the potsynaptic neurons of the parasympathetic nervous system"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Cell bodies of the presynaptic neurons of the parasympathetic neurons","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Cell bodies of presynaptic (preganglionic) parasympathetic neurons.</b> Parasympathetic outflow is craniosacral: brainstem nuclei plus the lateral horn of sacral 2 to 4.<br>A. Presynaptic sympathetic: lateral horn of thoracic 1 to lumbar 2.<br>C. Postsynaptic sympathetic: paravertebral and prevertebral ganglia.<br>D. Postsynaptic parasympathetic: small ganglia in or next to the organs, and the 4 head ganglia.<br>Rule: a cell body inside the spinal cord is always presynaptic.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["ANS slide 20 (Parasympathetic Division)","ANS slide 26 (Summary)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 clicker","n":"combined clickers for the final: abdomen/GI-embryo clickers only","sl":447,"f":"F2936"}],"t":1},{"id":"F2936-8-1","s":"Which of the following best describes the parasympathetic nervous system?","o":["A. It is part of the autonomic nervous system that is mainly active during stress related activity","B. Its preganglionic axons are located in the white rami communicans","C. Parts of its preganglionic neurons are located in the sacral segments of the spinal cord","D. Some of its postganglionic neurons are located in paravertebral ganglia","E. The ganglia that are located in front of the vertebral column are parasympathetic in function"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Parts of its preganglionic neurons are located in the sacral segments of the spinal cord","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Part of its preganglionic neurons lie in the sacral spinal cord.</b> Parasympathetic outflow is craniosacral: cranial nerves III, VII, IX, X (oculomotor, facial, glossopharyngeal, vagus) and sacral 2 to 4.<br>A. Active in stress: sympathetic ('fight or flight').<br>B. Axons in the white rami: those are sympathetic preganglionic fibers.<br>D. Neurons in paravertebral ganglia: the sympathetic chain.<br>E. Ganglia in front of the vertebral column (prevertebral: celiac, mesenteric): sympathetic.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["ANS slide 20 (Parasympathetic Division)","ANS slide 5 (Divisions of the ANS)","ANS slide 26 (Summary)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 clicker","n":"combined clickers for the final: abdomen/GI-embryo clickers only","sl":8,"f":"F2936"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G6","u":145,"label":"Vagus constricts bronchial smooth muscle","slides":[{"src":"sl/G008-22.jpg","cap":"Vagus joins sympathetic fibers to form cardiac, pulmonary and esophageal plexuses","where":"2026 lecture: Autonomic NS, slide 22"},{"src":"sl/G008-28.jpg","cap":"Effects table, lungs row: parasympathetic constricts bronchi, sympathetic dilates them","where":"2026 lecture: Autonomic NS, slide 28"}],"q":[{"id":"F2936-433-1","s":"A 17-year-old girl is admitted to the hospital with severe dyspnea. Physical examination reveals that the patient is suffering from an asthma attack, with associated bronchospasm. Which of the following nerves is responsible for the innervation of the bronchial smooth muscle cells?","o":["A. Greater thoracic splanchnic","B. Phrenic","C. Vagus","D. Intercostal","E. Lesser thoracic splanchnic"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Vagus","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Vagus.</b> Bronchial smooth muscle gets parasympathetic fibers from the vagus through the pulmonary plexus. Vagal action constricts the bronchi, as in the bronchospasm of asthma; sympathetic action dilates them.<br>A. Greater thoracic splanchnic and E. lesser thoracic splanchnic: go to the abdomen (celiac and superior mesenteric ganglia).<br>B. Phrenic: motor nerve to the diaphragm.<br>D. Intercostal: chest wall muscles and skin.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["ANS slide 22 (Will provide preganglionic parasympathetic fibers to plexi around the viscera up)","ANS slide 28 (| Organ, Tract, or System |  | Effect of Sympathetic Stimulation | Effect of Par)","ANS slide 21 (CN III – innervate the eye, dilate the pupil)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 clicker","n":"combined clickers for the final: abdomen/GI-embryo clickers only","sl":433,"f":"F2936"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G6","u":145,"label":"Vagal preganglionics reach gut; postganglionics in wall","slides":[{"src":"sl/G008-20.jpg","cap":"Vagus sends preganglionic fibers to gut up to left colic flexure; synapse in walls","where":"2026 lecture: Autonomic NS, slide 20"},{"src":"sl/G005-54.jpg","cap":"Small bowel nerves: parasympathetic to jejunum and ileum from posterior vagal trunk","where":"2026 lecture: Small & Large Intestines, slide 54"}],"q":[{"id":"D042-191-1","s":"A 22-year-old woman undergoes abdominal surgery, and the surgeon accidentally damages fibers that provide parasympathetic innervation to the small intestine but did not injure the small intestines. Based on your knowledge of parasympathetic pathways, which neuronal structure was most likely injured?","o":["A. Postganglionic neuron in the celiac ganglion","B. Preganglionic neuron in the lateral horn of S2–S4","C. Preganglionic neuron traveling with the vagus nerve","D. Postganglionic neuron in the sympathetic chain","E. Preganglionic neuron in T5–T9 splanchnic nerves"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Preganglionic neuron traveling with the vagus nerve","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Preganglionic neuron traveling with the vagus nerve.</b> The vagus carries parasympathetic preganglionic fibers to the gut up to the left colic flexure. They synapse on postganglionic neurons inside the intestinal wall. The intestine itself was not injured, so the damage must be preganglionic.<br>A. Celiac ganglion: sympathetic.<br>B. Sacral 2 to 4: supplies the gut beyond the left colic flexure.<br>D. Sympathetic chain: sympathetic.<br>E. Thoracic 5 to 9 splanchnic (the greater splanchnic): sympathetic.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["ANS slide 20 (Parasympathetic Division)","ANS slide 8 (Pre/Postganglionic system)","Small & Large Intestines slide 54 (Innervation)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI module TA review","sl":191,"f":"D042"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G6","u":146,"label":"Pelvic splanchnics: S2–S4 preganglionic parasympathetic","slides":[{"src":"sl/G008-23.jpg","cap":"S2–S4 pelvic splanchnics join inferior hypogastric plexus; gut beyond left colic flexure","where":"2026 lecture: Autonomic NS, slide 23"},{"src":"sl/G008-27.jpg","cap":"Splanchnic table: pelvic splanchnics presynaptic parasympathetic from S2–S4 anterior rami","where":"2026 lecture: Autonomic NS, slide 27"}],"q":[{"id":"F2936-443-1","s":"Pelvic splanchnics arising from levels ______ bring ______ to the inferior hypogastric plexus.","o":["A. S2-S4, preganglionic parasympathetic fibers","B. S1-S2, postganglionic sympathetic fibers","C. S3-S4, preganglionic sympatehtic fibers","D. T1-L2/L3, postganglionic sympathetic fibers","E. T1-L2/L3, preganglionic parasympathetic fibers"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"S2-S4, preganglionic parasympathetic fibers","a":"A","why":"<b>A. S2–S4 (sacral 2 to 4), preganglionic parasympathetic fibers.</b> Pelvic splanchnic nerves leave the anterior rami of sacral 2 to 4 and join the inferior hypogastric plexus, where they mix with sympathetic fibers.<br>B. Sacral 1 to 2, postganglionic sympathetic: wrong level and wrong type.<br>C. Sacral 3 to 4, preganglionic sympathetic: pelvic splanchnics are never sympathetic (the similar-named sacral splanchnics are).<br>D and E. Thoracic 1 to lumbar 2 or 3: sympathetic outflow, which reaches the plexus through the hypogastric nerves.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["ANS slide 23 (Parasympathetic Division)","ANS slide 27 (| Splanchnic Nerves | Autonomic Fiber Type | System | Origin | Destination |)","ANS slide 13 (Splanchnic nerve: nerve that conveys innervation to and from the viscera of the )"],"src":[{"t":"2022 clicker","n":"combined clickers for the final: abdomen/GI-embryo clickers only","sl":443,"f":"F2936"}]},{"id":"D042-189-1","s":"Bonus Question: Is this a preganglionic or postganglionic nerve injury? (pelvic fracture injuring the pelvic splanchnic nerves)","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Preganglionic – pelvic splanchnic nerves are preganglionic parasympathetic nerves.","a":null,"why":"<b>Preganglionic.</b> Pelvic splanchnic nerves carry preganglionic parasympathetic fibers from sacral 2 to 4. They synapse later, in small ganglia in the wall of the colon, rectum and bladder.<br>Common mix-up: the other abdominopelvic splanchnics (greater, lesser, least, lumbar, sacral) are also preganglionic, but they are sympathetic. Only the cardiopulmonary splanchnics are postganglionic.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["ANS slide 13 (Splanchnic nerve: nerve that conveys innervation to and from the viscera of the )","ANS slide 14 (Splanchnic nerve: nerve that conveys innervation to and from the viscera of the )"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI module TA review","sl":189,"f":"D042"}]},{"id":"F3900-90-1","s":"Which of the following are true regarding the innervation of the abdominal organs?","o":["A. Pelvic splanchnics are the sympathetic innervation to the ascending colon","B. Pelvic splanchnics are the parasympathetic innervation to the stomach","C. Pelvic splanchnics are the sympathetic innervation to the stomach","D. Pelvic splanchnics are the parasympathetic innervation to the descending colon","E. Pelvic splanchnics are the parasympathetic innervation to the ascending colon"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Pelvic splanchnics are the parasympathetic innervation to the descending colon","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Pelvic splanchnics are the parasympathetic supply to the descending colon.</b> The vagus covers the gut up to the left colic (splenic) flexure. Below that point, the descending colon, sigmoid and rectum get parasympathetic fibers from the pelvic splanchnic nerves (sacral 2 to 4).<br>A and C. Pelvic splanchnics are never sympathetic.<br>B. Stomach: parasympathetic supply is the vagus.<br>E. Ascending colon: midgut, so the vagus supplies it.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["ANS slide 23 (Parasympathetic Division)","ANS slide 22 (Will provide preganglionic parasympathetic fibers to plexi around the viscera up)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":90,"f":"F3900"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":67,"f":"F3901"}],"t":1},{"id":"D042-186-1","s":"A patient present with urinary retention and constipation following a spinal cord injury at the sacral level (S2-4). Which of the following nerves carries parasympathetic fibers to the bladder and the rectum?","o":["A. Greater splanchnic nerve","B. Lesser splanchnic nerve","C. Least splanchnic nerve","D. Pelvic splanchnic nerve","E. Lumbar splanchnic nerve"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Pelvic splanchnic nerve","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Pelvic splanchnic nerves.</b> They carry the sacral 2 to 4 preganglionic parasympathetic fibers that contract the bladder and rectum, so a sacral cord injury causes urinary retention and constipation.<br>A. Greater (thoracic 5 to 9), B. lesser (thoracic 10 to 11) and C. least (thoracic 12) splanchnics: preganglionic sympathetic, to the celiac, superior mesenteric and renal ganglia.<br>E. Lumbar splanchnics: sympathetic, to the inferior mesenteric and superior hypogastric plexuses.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["ANS slide 13 (Splanchnic nerve: nerve that conveys innervation to and from the viscera of the )","ANS slide 5 (Divisions of the ANS)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI module TA review","sl":186,"f":"D042"}],"t":1},{"id":"D042-188-1","s":"A pelvic fracture injures pelvic splanchnic nerves. Which of the following GI functions would most likely be impaired?","o":["A. Peristalsis of the jejunum","B. Peristalsis of the descending colon","C. Contraction of the internal anal sphincter","D. Secretion of digestive enzymes in the stomach","E. Vasoconstriction of colonic blood vessels"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Peristalsis of the descending colon","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Peristalsis of the descending colon.</b> Pelvic splanchnic nerves give parasympathetic supply to the gut beyond the left colic (splenic) flexure, so losing them weakens descending colon motility.<br>A. Jejunum: parasympathetic supply from the vagus.<br>C. Contraction of the internal anal sphincter: done by sympathetic fibers; parasympathetic fibers relax it.<br>D. Stomach enzyme secretion: vagus.<br>E. Vasoconstriction of colon vessels: sympathetic.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["ANS slide 13 (Splanchnic nerve: nerve that conveys innervation to and from the viscera of the )","Small & Large Intestines slide 77 (Descending colon and sigmoid colon)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI module TA review","sl":188,"f":"D042"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G6","u":147,"label":"Sympathetic vs parasympathetic effects on organs","slides":[{"src":"sl/G008-28.jpg","cap":"Effects table: sympathetic vs parasympathetic on eye, heart, lungs, gut, bladder","where":"2026 lecture: Autonomic NS, slide 28"},{"src":"sl/G008-16.jpg","cap":"Sympathetic actions: dilates pupils, bronchodilation, contracts sphincters, sweating","where":"2026 lecture: Autonomic NS, slide 16"},{"src":"sl/G008-24.jpg","cap":"Parasympathetic actions: constricts pupil, slows heart, constricts bronchi, increases peristalsis","where":"2026 lecture: Autonomic NS, slide 24"}],"q":[{"id":"D042-174-1","s":"A 26 year old medical student is studying for her GI anatomy practical. In order to stay awake enough to study, she’s had 3 celsius energy drinks and overactivated her sympathetic nervous system. Which of the following would be occurring in her body as a result?","o":["A. Bradycardia","B. Narrowing of the bronchi","C. Decreased peristalsis","D. Constriction of the pupils"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Decreased peristalsis","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Decreased peristalsis.</b> The sympathetic system is 'fight or flight': it slows the gut and sends blood to skeletal muscle.<br>A. Bradycardia (slow heart rate): parasympathetic; sympathetic speeds the heart.<br>B. Narrowing of the bronchi: parasympathetic; sympathetic widens them.<br>D. Constriction of the pupils: parasympathetic (oculomotor nerve); sympathetic dilates them.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["ANS slide 5 (Divisions of the ANS)","ANS slide 28 (| Organ, Tract, or System |  | Effect of Sympathetic Stimulation | Effect of Par)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI module TA review","sl":174,"f":"D042"}],"t":1},{"id":"F2936-14-1","s":"Stimulation of parasympathetic nervous system produces which of the following action","o":["A. Dilatation of pupil","B. Constriction of anal sphinctor","C. Decrease gastric enzymes secretions","D. Decrease heart rate","E. Decrease gastrointestinal motility"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Decrease heart rate","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Decrease heart rate.</b> The parasympathetic system ('rest and digest') slows the heart through the vagus nerve.<br>A. Dilation of the pupil: sympathetic; parasympathetic constricts the pupil.<br>B. Constriction of the anal sphincter: sympathetic fibers contract the internal sphincter; parasympathetic fibers relax it for defecation.<br>C. Less gastric enzyme secretion: sympathetic; parasympathetic increases it.<br>E. Less gut motility: sympathetic; parasympathetic increases peristalsis.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["ANS slide 24 (Parasympathetic Division)","ANS slide 28 (| Organ, Tract, or System |  | Effect of Sympathetic Stimulation | Effect of Par)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 clicker","n":"combined clickers for the final: abdomen/GI-embryo clickers only","sl":14,"f":"F2936"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G6","u":147,"label":"Norepinephrine at postganglionic sympathetic endings","slides":[{"src":"sl/G008-26.jpg","cap":"Summary: sympathetic postganglionic ending releases norepinephrine, except sweat glands","where":"2026 lecture: Autonomic NS, slide 26"},{"src":"sl/G008-16.jpg","cap":"Transmitters: acetylcholine at ganglia, norepinephrine at sympathetic targets","where":"2026 lecture: Autonomic NS, slide 16"}],"q":[{"id":"F2936-10-1","s":"A research scientist discovers a drug that selectively blocks the release of norepinephrine (NE) from nerve endings. At which of the following sites would this drug have the greatest effect on normal synaptic transmission?","o":["A. Preganglionic parasympathetic nerve terminals","B. Preganglionic sympathetic nerve terminals","C. Postganglionic parasympathetic nerve terminals","D. Postganglionic sympathetic nerve terminals","E. None"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Postganglionic sympathetic nerve terminals","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Postganglionic sympathetic nerve terminals.</b> These endings release NE (norepinephrine) onto the target organ, so blocking NE release silences them.<br>A. Preganglionic parasympathetic and B. preganglionic sympathetic terminals: both release ACh (acetylcholine) onto the ganglion cell.<br>C. Postganglionic parasympathetic terminals: release ACh.<br>E. None: wrong, because sympathetic postganglionic endings depend on NE. (Exception: sympathetic fibers to sweat glands release ACh.)","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["ANS slide 16 (Sympathetic Division)","ANS slide 26 (Summary)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 clicker","n":"combined clickers for the final: abdomen/GI-embryo clickers only","sl":10,"f":"F2936"}],"t":1},{"id":"F2936-435-1","s":"A research scientist at a pharmaceutical company discovers a new drug that selectively blocks the release of norepinephrine from nerve endings. At which of the following sites would this drug have the greatest effect on normal synaptic transmission?","o":["A. Somatic neuron motor end plates","B. Postsynaptic parasympathetic nerve terminals","C. Postsynaptic sympathetic nerve terminals","D. Synapses between presynaptic and postsynaptic parasympathetic neurons","E. Synapses between presynaptic and postsynaptic sympathetic neurons"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Postsynaptic sympathetic nerve terminals","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Postsynaptic (postganglionic) sympathetic nerve terminals.</b> They release NE (norepinephrine), so a drug that blocks NE release hits them.<br>A. Somatic motor end plates: release ACh (acetylcholine) onto skeletal muscle.<br>B. Postsynaptic parasympathetic terminals: release ACh.<br>D and E. Synapses between pre- and postsynaptic neurons, in both divisions: the preganglionic fiber releases ACh in every autonomic ganglion.<br>Exception: sympathetic fibers to sweat glands release ACh, not NE.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["ANS slide 16 (Sympathetic Division)","ANS slide 24 (Parasympathetic Division)","ANS slide 26 (Summary)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 clicker","n":"combined clickers for the final: abdomen/GI-embryo clickers only","sl":435,"f":"F2936"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G6","u":148,"label":"Visceral pain: greater (liver) vs lesser (appendix) splanchnic","slides":[{"src":"sl/G008-27.jpg","cap":"Splanchnic levels: greater T5–T9 to celiac, lesser T10–T11, least T12","where":"2026 lecture: Autonomic NS, slide 27"},{"src":"sl/G003-7.jpg","cap":"Referred pain regions: foregut epigastric, midgut umbilical, hindgut pubic","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 7"},{"src":"sl/G006-25.jpg","cap":"Liver nerve supply: sympathetic fibers from the celiac plexus","where":"2026 lecture: Liver, Biliary & Portal Vein, slide 25"}],"q":[{"id":"F1156-2-1","s":"A 56 year old male presents to your clinic feeling pain just below their right costal margin. When pressing just beneath the ribs, the patient gasps in pain, and says they feel pain ”on the inside, right there”. As a star anatomy student you deduce that what organ is giving off these pain signals and through what nerve that is part of which plexus?","o":["A. Liver, least splanchnic, superior mesenteric plexus","B. Appendix, greater splanchnics, Inferior mesenteric plexus","C. Spleen, lesser splanchnics, Superior Mesenteric plexus","D. Liver, greater splanchnics, celiac plexus","E. Descending colon, lesser splanchnics, inferior mesenteric plexus"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Liver, greater splanchnics, celiac plexus","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Liver, greater splanchnic nerves, celiac plexus.</b> Pain just under the right costal margin, 'on the inside', is visceral pain from the liver. Its pain fibers run with sympathetic fibers through the celiac plexus to the greater splanchnic nerves (thoracic 5 to 9).<br>A. Least splanchnic and superior mesenteric plexus: wrong nerve and plexus for the liver.<br>B. Appendix: pain starts around the umbilicus.<br>C. Spleen: left upper quadrant.<br>E. Descending colon: left side, hindgut.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 25 (Nerve supply)","ANS slide 27 (| Splanchnic Nerves | Autonomic Fiber Type | System | Origin | Destination |)","ANS slide 13 (Splanchnic nerve: nerve that conveys innervation to and from the viscera of the )"],"src":[{"t":"? TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":2,"f":"F1156"}],"t":1},{"id":"F0003-8-1","s":"A 22-year-old man presents to the clinic with a three-day history of diffuse, periumbilical pain associated with vomiting. The pain has now become sharp, constant, and localized to the right lower quadrant. During physical examination, the right lower quadrant pain is aggravated by movement, and the patient is running a temperature. Acute appendicitis is suspected and he is treated with intravenous antibiotics and referred for further evaluation. The original diffuse pain in this patient was most likely transmitted via which of the following nerves ?","o":["A. Greater splanchnic","B. Lumbar splanchnic","C. Least splanchnic","D. Lesser splanchnic","E. Superior hypogastric"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Lesser splanchnic","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Lesser splanchnic.</b> Early appendicitis pain is visceral. The appendix is midgut; its pain fibers run with sympathetic fibers back to the tenth thoracic segment through the lesser splanchnic nerve (thoracic 10 to 11), so the pain is felt around the umbilicus. Later right lower quadrant pain comes from the parietal peritoneum.<br>A. Greater splanchnic (thoracic 5 to 9): foregut, epigastric pain.<br>B. Lumbar splanchnic: hindgut and pelvis.<br>C. Least splanchnic (thoracic 12): kidney.<br>E. Superior hypogastric plexus: pelvic organs.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["ANS slide 27 (| Splanchnic Nerves | Autonomic Fiber Type | System | Origin | Destination |)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 7 (Referred Pain and Palpations)"],"src":[{"t":"? Middletown TA review","n":"MT Abdomen Review","sl":8,"f":"F0003"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G7","u":149,"label":"Left pulmonary artery: bump below aortic knob","slides":[{"src":"sl/G009-10.jpg","cap":"Labeled frontal chest film: aortic arch, then pulmonary artery bulge on left border","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Thorax, slide 10"},{"src":"sl/G009-49.jpg","cap":"Axial CT: left main pulmonary artery curving backward on the left","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Thorax, slide 49"}],"q":[{"id":"D045-236-1","s":"A PA chest radiograph demonstrates a left mediastinal border formed superiorly by a prominent bulge just below the aortic knob. Which structure most likely forms this contour?","o":["A. Left ventricle","B. Left pulmonary artery","C. Left atrium","D. Main bronchi","E. Superior vena cava"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Left pulmonary artery","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Left pulmonary artery.</b> On a PA (posteroanterior) chest film, the left mediastinal border has bumps from top to bottom: aortic knob, then the pulmonary trunk and left pulmonary artery, then the left atrial appendage, then the left ventricle. The bulge just below the aortic knob is the pulmonary artery.<br>A. Left ventricle: the lowest, largest curve, at the apex.<br>C. Left atrium: only bulges, lower down, when enlarged.<br>D. Main bronchi: air-filled, inside the mediastinum.<br>E. Superior vena cava: on the right side.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Radiology Thorax slide 49 (CT of the chest: Axial)","Radiology Thorax slide 47 (CT of the chest: Axial)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":236,"f":"D045"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G7","u":149,"label":"Right atrium forms the right heart border","slides":[{"src":"sl/G009-11.jpg","cap":"Frontal chest film outline: right atrium forms right border, left ventricle the left","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Thorax, slide 11"},{"src":"sl/G009-10.jpg","cap":"Labeled frontal chest film: right atrium on right border; arch, pulmonary artery on left","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Thorax, slide 10"}],"q":[{"id":"D045-231-1","s":"A 55-year-old woman receives a chest X-ray. The right heart border appears sharply defined. Which structure provides the majority of the right heart border on PA radiograph?","o":["A. Right ventricle","B. Right atrium","C. Left ventricle","D. Superior vena cava","E. Ascending aorta"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Right atrium","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Right atrium.</b> On a PA (posteroanterior, back-to-front) chest film, the right heart border is made almost entirely by the right atrium.<br>A. Right ventricle: faces forward; it forms the front of the heart, not a side border.<br>C. Left ventricle: forms the left heart border and apex.<br>D. Superior vena cava: forms the straight upper right mediastinal border, above the heart.<br>E. Ascending aorta: sits above the right atrium, mostly hidden in the mediastinum.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"slide 231 has a picture; question answerable from text","tg":["Radiology Thorax slide 10 (Chest Radiography)","Radiology Thorax slide 11 (Chest Radiography)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":231,"f":"D045"}]},{"id":"D045-240-1","s":"A 61-year-old patient’s chest X-ray shows a very straight right mediastinal border extending downward to the diaphragm. This border is largely created by which structure?","o":["A. Right atrium","B. Esophagus","C. Inferior vena cava","D. Right ventricle","E. Azygos vein"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Right atrium","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Right atrium.</b> The right heart border that runs down to the diaphragm on a frontal chest film is made by the right atrium.<br>B. Esophagus: midline, behind the heart; it makes no border.<br>C. Inferior vena cava: at most a small piece at the right cardiophrenic angle, not the whole border.<br>D. Right ventricle: anterior; it forms the front of the heart on the lateral film.<br>E. Azygos vein: a small bump high up, at the right tracheobronchial angle.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Radiology Thorax slide 10 (Chest Radiography)","Radiology Thorax slide 11 (Chest Radiography)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":240,"f":"D045"}],"t":1},{"id":"F1252-201-1","s":"Which chamber of the heart is being indicated below by the arrow?","o":["A. Right ventricle","B. Left ventricle","C. Right atrium","D. Left atrium"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Right atrium","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Right atrium.</b> The arrow points to the chamber at the far right side of the heart, which is on the image's left because axial images are viewed from the feet. The rightmost chamber, forming the right heart border, is the right atrium.<br>A. Right ventricle: the front chamber, just behind the sternum.<br>B. Left ventricle: the thick-walled chamber toward the patient's left and the apex.<br>D. Left atrium: the back chamber, just in front of the esophagus.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F1252-201.jpg","in":"Axial chest CT at heart level, labels blanked; green arrow points to the chamber forming the right heart border (right atrium)","tg":["Radiology Thorax slide 66 (CT of the chest: Axial)","Radiology Thorax slide 67 (CT of the chest: Axial)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":201,"f":"F1252"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3900-182-1","s":"Which of the following is the BLUE square","o":["A. Left Ventricle","B. Right Ventricle","C. Left Atrium","D. Right Atrium","E. Arch of the Aorta"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Right Atrium","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Right atrium.</b> In this four-chamber view, the blue square sits in the chamber on the patient's right (image left), behind the right ventricle. That is the right atrium.<br>A. Left ventricle: the long chamber running to the apex, on the image right.<br>B. Right ventricle: the front chamber, nearest the chest wall.<br>C. Left atrium: the back chamber, in front of the descending aorta.<br>E. Arch of the aorta: not in this slice; the round vessel behind the heart is the descending aorta.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F3900-182.jpg","in":"Four-chamber cardiac MRI; blue square on the chamber at the patient's right, posterior-lateral to the RV (right atrium); red squares on the other chambers and lung; arrow on the descending aorta","tg":["Radiology Thorax slide 78 (Cardiac CT)","Radiology Thorax slide 79 (Cardiac CT)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":182,"f":"F3900"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3901-164-1","s":"IDENTIFY RED & BLUE! Do the others too if you want, I cant stop you","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Red = Right atrium; Blue = Left ventricle (other labels: Brachiocephalic trunk, Arch of aorta, Superior vena cava, Pulmonary trunk)","a":null,"why":"<b>Red = right atrium; blue = left ventricle.</b> On a coronal (front-view) CT (computed tomography) slice, the chamber forming the lower right heart border, on the patient's right (image left), is the right atrium. The large chamber running to the apex on the patient's left is the left ventricle. The upper boxes, top to bottom: brachiocephalic trunk, arch of aorta, superior vena cava, pulmonary trunk.<br>Common mix-up: calling the right border the right ventricle; the right ventricle faces forward.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F3901-164.jpg","in":"Coronal contrast CT of the chest with six blank label boxes; red box points to the right-heart border chamber (right atrium), blue box to the chamber at the apex (left ventricle); upper boxes point to brachiocephalic trunk, arch of aorta, SVC, pulmonary trunk","tg":["Radiology Thorax slide 44 (CT of the chest: Coronal)","Radiology Thorax slide 45 (CT of the chest: Axial)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":164,"f":"F3901"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G7","u":150,"label":"Right ventricle is the most anterior chamber","slides":[{"src":"sl/G009-13.jpg","cap":"Lateral chest film outline: right ventricle forms anterior border behind sternum","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Thorax, slide 13"},{"src":"sl/G009-66.jpg","cap":"Axial CT: right ventricle, the most anterior chamber behind the sternum","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Thorax, slide 66"}],"q":[{"id":"F0008-150-1","s":"The most anterior aspect of the heart on a lateral chest X-Ray is:","o":["A. Right Atrium","B. Right Ventricle","C. Left Atrium","D. Left Ventricle","E. If you had a heart, you wouldn’t ask…"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Right Ventricle","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Right ventricle.</b> On a lateral chest film, the front (anterior) heart border, right behind the sternum, is the right ventricle.<br>A. Right atrium: forms the right heart border on the frontal film.<br>C. Left atrium: forms the upper back border on the lateral film.<br>D. Left ventricle: forms the lower back border on the lateral film and the left border on the frontal film.<br>E. A joke option.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Lateral chest radiograph shown beside the question; not needed to answer.","tg":["Radiology Thorax slide 13 (Chest Radiography)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":150,"f":"F0008"}],"t":1},{"id":"D041-264-1","s":"What structure is indicated by the arrow?","o":["A. Left atrium","B. Right atrium","C. Right ventricle","D. Left ventricle"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Right ventricle","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Right ventricle.</b> The arrow is on the most anterior chamber, directly behind the sternum and in front of and left of the aortic root. The right ventricle is the front chamber of the heart.<br>A. Left atrium: the most posterior chamber, in front of the esophagus.<br>B. Right atrium: forms the right heart border, on the image's left.<br>D. Left ventricle: lies to the left and back, toward the apex.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/D041-264.jpg","in":"Axial contrast chest CT; arrow points to the most anterior cardiac chamber just behind the sternum (right ventricle/outflow)","tg":["Radiology Thorax slide 66 (CT of the chest: Axial)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":264,"f":"D041"}],"t":1},{"id":"F1156-34-1","s":"A 33 years old female comes to the office complaining of chest pain. Your attending tells you there is a problem with her moderator band. In which of the following chamber would you find the moderator band?","o":["A. A","B. B","C. C","D. D"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"B (right ventricle, the anterior chamber)","a":"B","why":"<b>B (right ventricle).</b> The moderator band (septomarginal trabecula) runs from the interventricular septum to the anterior papillary muscle of the right ventricle and carries part of the right bundle branch. B is the front chamber, behind the sternum: the right ventricle.<br>A. Right atrium, on the patient's right (image left).<br>C. Left ventricle, the thick-walled chamber toward the apex.<br>D. Left atrium, the back chamber.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F1156-34.jpg","in":"Axial contrast CT of the heart (four-chamber level) with labels A (right atrium), B (anterior chamber = right ventricle), C (left ventricle), D (left atrium)","tg":["Radiology Thorax slide 66 (CT of the chest: Axial)","Radiology Thorax slide 78 (Cardiac CT)"],"src":[{"t":"? TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":34,"f":"F1156"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G7","u":151,"label":"Sternum: midline bone at the front","slides":[{"src":"sl/G009-28.jpg","cap":"Axial CT: sternum, the flat midline bone at the front of the chest","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Thorax, slide 28"}],"q":[{"id":"D041-258-1","s":"Identify the structure indicated by the arrow.","o":["A. Vertebra","B. Sternum","C. Rib","D. Clavicle"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Sternum","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Sternum.</b> The arrow points to the flat bone in the midline at the very front of the chest, in front of the mediastinum. At this upper level it is the manubrium of the sternum.<br>A. Vertebra: midline but at the back, behind the trachea and esophagus.<br>C. Rib: curved bones along the sides of the chest wall.<br>D. Clavicle: higher and to each side, meeting the manubrium at the sternoclavicular joints.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/D041-258.jpg","in":"Axial chest CT (lung window, upper mediastinum/aortic arch level); arrow points down onto the midline anterior bone (sternum)","tg":["Radiology Thorax slide 28 (CT of the chest: Axial)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":258,"f":"D041"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G7","u":151,"label":"Thoracic inlet structures on axial CT","slides":[{"src":"sl/G009-25.jpg","cap":"Axial CT at thoracic inlet: trachea, black air-filled tube in the midline","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Thorax, slide 25"},{"src":"sl/G009-30.jpg","cap":"Axial CT: brachiocephalic trunk, in front and right of the trachea","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Thorax, slide 30"},{"src":"sl/G009-32.jpg","cap":"Axial CT: left subclavian artery, left of and behind the trachea","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Thorax, slide 32"}],"q":[{"id":"F3900-175-1","s":"Which of the following is the arrow pointing to?","o":["A. Aortic Arch","B. Trachea","C. Left Subclavian Artery","D. Left Common Carotid Artery","E. Brachiocephalic Trunk"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Trachea","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Trachea.</b> The arrow points to the round black (air-filled) tube in the midline, in front of the vertebra and esophagus. Air is black on CT (computed tomography).<br>A. Aortic arch: a curved vessel, slightly lower, left of the trachea.<br>C. Left subclavian artery: a small round vessel left of and behind the trachea.<br>D. Left common carotid artery: small round vessel left of the trachea, in front of the subclavian.<br>E. Brachiocephalic trunk: in front of and right of the trachea.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F3900-175.jpg","in":"Axial chest CT (lung window) at the thoracic inlet; red arrow on the round air-filled tube anterior to the vertebra (trachea)","tg":["Radiology Thorax slide 25 (CT of the chest: Axial)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":175,"f":"F3900"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3901-231-1","s":"CT of superior mediastinum. Name as many structures as you can.","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Labels on answer slide: Right brachiocephalic vein, Brachiocephalic artery, Manubrium, Left brachiocephalic vein, Left common carotid artery, Left subclavian artery, Trachea, Esophagus, Right lung, Left lung, Vertebral body, Rib, Spinal cord","a":null,"why":"<b>Superior mediastinum, front to back.</b> The left brachiocephalic vein (9) crosses in front, behind the manubrium, to meet the right brachiocephalic vein (1). Behind it lie the three arch branches: brachiocephalic trunk (10) in the middle, then left common carotid (8) and left subclavian (7) on the left, the subclavian furthest back. The black trachea (2) is midline, the esophagus (5) behind it, then the vertebral body (3) and spinal cord (4).<br>Tip: veins are in front of arteries here.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F3901-231.jpg","in":"Axial contrast CT of the superior mediastinum with numbered pointers 1-10 (right brachiocephalic vein, trachea, vertebral body, spinal cord, esophagus, left lung, left subclavian artery, left common carotid artery, left brachiocephalic vein, brachiocephalic artery/manubrium)","tg":["Radiology Thorax slide 29 (CT of the chest: Axial)","Radiology Thorax slide 30 (CT of the chest: Axial)","Radiology Thorax slide 65 (CT of the chest: Axial)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":231,"f":"F3901"}]},{"id":"D045-221-1","s":"Radiology Practice: Name the Numbered Structures (axial chest CT at the level of the thoracic inlet, structures numbered 1-10).","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Trachea; Rib; Brachiocephalic artery; Brachiocephalic vein; Pectoralis major; Pectoralis minor; Common carotid; Subclavian artery; Esophagus; Scapula (listed in the speaker notes; the notes do not tie each name to its number).","a":null,"why":"<b>1 trachea, 2 rib, 3 brachiocephalic artery, 4 brachiocephalic vein, 5 pectoralis major, 6 pectoralis minor, 7 common carotid, 8 subclavian artery, 9 esophagus, 10 scapula.</b> At the thoracic inlet, the black trachea is midline with the esophagus behind it. The brachiocephalic artery sits front-right of the trachea; the left common carotid and left subclavian sit on its left, the subclavian further back. The brachiocephalic vein lies in front of the arteries. Pectoralis major is superficial to pectoralis minor; the scapula is at the back.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/D045-221.jpg","in":"Axial contrast chest CT at the thoracic inlet with red numbers 1-10 (1-7 along the anterior/lateral chest, 8-10 posterior); labeled versions of this level are on slides 223-224","tg":["Radiology Thorax slide 25 (CT of the chest: Axial)","Radiology Thorax slide 30 (CT of the chest: Axial)","Radiology Thorax slide 33 (CT of the chest: Axial)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":221,"f":"D045"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G7","u":152,"label":"Esophagus: behind trachea, in front of spine","slides":[{"src":"sl/G009-65.jpg","cap":"Axial CT: esophagus directly in front of the vertebral body","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Thorax, slide 65"}],"q":[{"id":"D045-238-1","s":"On an axial CT at the level of the carina, the structure located posterior to the trachea and anterior to the vertebral body is identified. Which mediastinal structure is this?","o":["A. Thoracic duct","B. Esophagus","C. Azygos vein","D. Left main bronchus","E. Aorta"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Esophagus","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Esophagus.</b> At the carina, the esophagus sits directly behind the trachea and its split, and directly in front of the vertebral body.<br>A. Thoracic duct: a tiny duct behind the esophagus, between the aorta and azygos vein.<br>C. Azygos vein: right of the esophagus, against the spine.<br>D. Left main bronchus: air-filled, left of the carina.<br>E. Aorta: the descending aorta sits left of the spine, not straight behind the trachea.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"slide 238 has a picture; question answerable from text","tg":["Radiology Thorax slide 65 (CT of the chest: Axial)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":238,"f":"D045"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G7","u":152,"label":"Bronchus intermedius on axial CT","slides":[{"src":"sl/G009-63.jpg","cap":"Axial CT: bronchus intermedius behind the right pulmonary artery","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Thorax, slide 63"},{"src":"sl/G009-22.jpg","cap":"Coronal CT: bronchus intermedius continuing below the right upper lobe bronchus","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Thorax, slide 22"}],"q":[{"id":"D041-261-1","s":"What structure is indicated by the arrow?","o":["A. Trachea","B. Right main bronchus","C. Left main bronchus","D. Bronchus intermedius"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Bronchus intermedius","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Bronchus intermedius.</b> This slice is below the carina. On the patient's right (image left), the air tube just behind the right pulmonary artery, below the right upper lobe bronchus, is the bronchus intermedius; it continues to the middle and lower lobes.<br>A. Trachea: a single midline tube above the carina.<br>B. Right main bronchus: very short, ending where the upper lobe bronchus leaves.<br>C. Left main bronchus: on the patient's left (image right).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/D041-261.jpg","in":"Axial contrast chest CT below the carina; arrow points to the air-filled airway on the patient's right just behind the right pulmonary artery (bronchus intermedius)","tg":["Radiology Thorax slide 22 (CT of the chest: Coronal)","Radiology Thorax slide 63 (CT of the chest: Axial)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":261,"f":"D041"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G7","u":152,"label":"Inhaled object → right main bronchus","slides":[{"src":"sl/G009-20.jpg","cap":"Coronal CT: right mainstem bronchus, short, wide and steep","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Thorax, slide 20"},{"src":"sl/G009-19.jpg","cap":"Coronal CT: left mainstem bronchus, longer and more horizontal","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Thorax, slide 19"}],"q":[{"id":"F0008-152-1","s":"A 7 year old boy comes into the ER choking. Mom says her son was seen playing with army men just prior to the choking fit. Where is the most likely place the toy is stuck?","o":["A. Oropharynx","B. Trachea","C. Right main stem bronchus","D. Left main stem bronchus","E. Esophagus"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Right main stem bronchus","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Right main bronchus.</b> It is wider, shorter and more vertical than the left, so inhaled objects usually drop into it.<br>A. Oropharynx: small objects pass through it into the larynx and trachea.<br>B. Trachea: an object small enough to pass the larynx usually continues into a bronchus.<br>D. Left main bronchus: longer, narrower and more horizontal, pushed sideways by the aortic arch and heart.<br>E. Esophagus: swallowed, not inhaled, objects go there; they cause trouble swallowing, not choking.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Radiology Thorax slide 20 (CT of the chest: Coronal)","Radiology Thorax slide 19 (CT of the chest: Coronal)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":152,"f":"F0008"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G7","u":153,"label":"Superior vena cava sits right of ascending aorta","slides":[{"src":"sl/G009-45.jpg","cap":"Axial CT: ascending aorta; round vessel just to its right is superior vena cava","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Thorax, slide 45"},{"src":"sl/G009-11.jpg","cap":"Frontal chest film outline labels the superior vena cava on the upper right","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Thorax, slide 11"}],"q":[{"id":"D045-229-1","s":"A 64-year-old man undergoes a chest CT for evaluation of dyspnea. On an axial slice through the superior mediastinum, a round vascular structure is seen posterior and slightly to the right of the ascending aorta. Which structure is most likely identified?","o":["A. Left atrium","B. Pulmonary trunk","C. Superior vena cava","D. Right pulmonary artery","E. Descending aorta"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Superior vena cava","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Superior vena cava.</b> In the upper chest, the SVC (superior vena cava) is the round vessel just to the right of, and slightly behind, the ascending aorta.<br>A. Left atrium: a heart chamber, lower down at the back of the heart.<br>B. Pulmonary trunk: in front of and to the left of the ascending aorta.<br>D. Right pulmonary artery: a horizontal tube passing behind the ascending aorta and SVC, not a round dot beside it.<br>E. Descending aorta: left of the spine, at the back.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"slide 229 has a picture; question answerable from text","tg":["Radiology Thorax slide 45 (CT of the chest: Axial)","Radiology Thorax slide 68 (CT of the chest: Axial)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":229,"f":"D045"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G7","u":153,"label":"Ascending aorta in front, descending aorta by spine","slides":[{"src":"sl/G009-45.jpg","cap":"Axial CT: ascending thoracic aorta, right of the pulmonary trunk","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Thorax, slide 45"},{"src":"sl/G009-46.jpg","cap":"Axial CT: descending thoracic aorta, left and in front of the vertebra","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Thorax, slide 46"}],"q":[{"id":"F3900-179-1","s":"Which of the following is the arrow pointing to?","o":["A. Ascending Aorta","B. Left Main Pulmonary Artery","C. Right Main Pulmonary Artery","D. Trachea","E. Descending Thoracic Aorta"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Ascending Aorta","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Ascending aorta.</b> At the level of the pulmonary trunk, the round bright vessel in the middle, just right of the pulmonary trunk, is the ascending aorta.<br>B. Left main pulmonary artery: curves backward from the pulmonary trunk on the patient's left.<br>C. Right main pulmonary artery: runs horizontally behind the ascending aorta.<br>D. Trachea: black (air), and already split into bronchi at this level.<br>E. Descending thoracic aorta: the round vessel beside the spine at the back.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F3900-179.jpg","in":"Axial contrast chest CT at the pulmonary-trunk level; red arrow on the round vessel to the right of the pulmonary trunk (ascending aorta)","tg":["Radiology Thorax slide 45 (CT of the chest: Axial)","Radiology Thorax slide 47 (CT of the chest: Axial)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":179,"f":"F3900"}]},{"id":"D041-267-1","s":"What structure is indicated by the arrow on this axial chest CT?","o":["A. Pulmonary trunk","B. Ascending aorta","C. Superior vena cava","D. Descending thoracic aorta"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Descending thoracic aorta","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Descending thoracic aorta.</b> The arrow points to the round bright vessel just left of and in front of the vertebral body, at the back of the chest.<br>A. Pulmonary trunk: in front, leaving the heart toward the patient's left.<br>B. Ascending aorta: in front, in the middle of the mediastinum, right of the pulmonary trunk.<br>C. Superior vena cava: front-right, beside the ascending aorta.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/D041-267.jpg","in":"Axial contrast chest CT; arrow points to the round vessel left-anterior to the vertebral body (descending thoracic aorta)","tg":["Radiology Thorax slide 46 (CT of the chest: Axial)","Radiology Thorax slide 69 (CT of the chest: Axial)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":267,"f":"D041"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G7","u":153,"label":"Coarctation: high arm pressure, weak leg pulses","slides":[{"src":"sl/F0010-171.jpg","cap":"Coarctation of aorta: narrowing past left subclavian; high arm, low leg pressure","where":", slide 171"}],"q":[{"id":"F1180-365-1","s":"A pediatrician is unable to detect a pulse in the groin or legs of an infant and notes the lower extremities are cold to the touch. The pulse and blood pressure of the upper limbs are significantly elevated from normal. A pediatric cardiologist performs an echocardiogram and other imaging. The given sagittal MRI verifies the presence of what specific anomaly, as indicated by the white arrowhead?","o":["A. Tetrology of Fallot","B. Atrial septal defect","C. Coarctation of aorta","D. Esophageal atresia","E. Hiatal hernia"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Coarctation of aorta","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Coarctation of the aorta.</b> The aorta is narrowed just past the left subclavian artery. Blood pressure is high in the arms (supplied before the narrowing) and low in the legs, with weak femoral pulses and cold legs. The arrowhead marks the narrowing on the sagittal MRI (magnetic resonance imaging).<br>A. Tetralogy of Fallot: cyanosis, no arm-leg pressure gap.<br>B. Atrial septal defect: a hole between the atria; pulses are normal.<br>D. Esophageal atresia: feeding problems.<br>E. Hiatal hernia: stomach through the diaphragm.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Sagittal MRI of the thoracic aorta with a white arrowhead at a narrowing of the descending aorta just past the arch; the vignette alone gives the answer.","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":365,"f":"F1180"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":233,"f":"F3901"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G7","u":154,"label":"Four chambers on cardiac CT","slides":[{"src":"sl/G009-78.jpg","cap":"Cardiac CT: left ventricle, long thick-walled chamber running to apex","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Thorax, slide 78"},{"src":"sl/G009-79.jpg","cap":"Cardiac CT: left atrium at the back of the heart","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Thorax, slide 79"},{"src":"sl/G009-66.jpg","cap":"Axial CT: right ventricle at the front, behind the sternum","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Thorax, slide 66"}],"q":[{"id":"F1180-374-1","s":"Name the Structures","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Right atrium (RA), right ventricle (RV), left ventricle (LV), left atrium (LA)","a":null,"why":"<b>Right atrium, right ventricle, left ventricle, left atrium.</b> In a four-chamber view: the box on the image left (patient's right) is the right atrium; the top box, nearest the chest wall, is the right ventricle; the box on the image right, the long thick-walled chamber running to the apex, is the left ventricle; the bottom (back) box is the left atrium.<br>Common mix-up: the image's left is the patient's right.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F1180-374.jpg","in":"Two contrast cardiac CT images in the 4-chamber view with four blank boxes each (upper-left chamber, top chamber, right-sided chamber of the image, bottom chamber). Same item also appears in the Week 7 block (slides 126-127); kept once here.","tg":["Radiology Thorax slide 78 (Cardiac CT)","Radiology Thorax slide 79 (Cardiac CT)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":374,"f":"F1180"},{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":303,"f":"F1178"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G7","u":154,"label":"Left atrium lies directly in front of esophagus","slides":[{"src":"sl/G009-65.jpg","cap":"Axial CT: esophagus directly behind the left atrium","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Thorax, slide 65"},{"src":"sl/G009-79.jpg","cap":"Cardiac CT: left atrium, the most posterior chamber","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Thorax, slide 79"}],"q":[{"id":"D045-233-1","s":"A trauma patient undergoes CT. On an axial slice through the lower mediastinum, a large chamber is seen directly anterior to the esophagus and posterior to the sternum. Which chamber is being visualized?","o":["A. Left ventricle","B. Right atrium","C. Right ventricle","D. Left atrium","E. Coronary sinus"],"g":["D"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Right ventricle","a":"C","why":"<b>D. Left atrium.</b> The chamber lying directly in front of the esophagus is the left atrium; that is why an enlarged left atrium pushes the esophagus backward. Every chamber is 'posterior to the sternum', so that clue does not narrow it down.<br>A. Left ventricle: toward the left and the apex.<br>B. Right atrium: forms the right heart border.<br>C. Right ventricle: directly behind the sternum, but far from the esophagus.<br>E. Coronary sinus: a vein in the back groove, not a chamber.","ex":"","fl":["The two clues conflict: the chamber 'directly anterior to the esophagus' on an axial chest CT is the left atrium (D), while the chamber just behind the sternum is the right ventricle (key C). No explanation is given in the source. On an exam, 'directly anterior to the esophagus' = left atrium.","The source keyed right ventricle (C), but the chamber directly anterior to the esophagus is the left atrium (D); 'posterior to the sternum' fits every chamber."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Radiology Thorax slide 66 (CT of the chest: Axial)","Radiology Thorax slide 67 (CT of the chest: Axial)","Radiology Thorax slide 65 (CT of the chest: Axial)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":233,"f":"D045"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3901-166-1","s":"A Horizon resident lovingly cooked a potent smelling fish stew in the school kitchenette.  All his neighbors came out, drooling over the smell and wanting a taste.  However, the master chef did not remove all the fishbones and a huge one gets stuck in his friends esophagus, piercing directly anterior through all esophageal layers at the level of T7.  Using the CT provided, which number correctly labels the affected heart chamber?","o":["A. 1.","B. 2.","C. 3.","D. 6.","E. 7."],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"2. Left Atrium","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Label 2 (left atrium).</b> The left atrium is the most posterior chamber and sits directly in front of the esophagus (label 3). A fishbone piercing straight forward through the esophagus at this level enters the left atrium.<br>A. 1 = right atrium, on the patient's right.<br>C. 3 = the esophagus itself, not a heart chamber.<br>D. 6 = left ventricle, toward the apex.<br>E. 7 = right ventricle, the most anterior chamber.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F3901-166.jpg","in":"Axial chest CT with numbered labels: 1 right atrium, 2 left atrium (directly anterior to 3), 3 esophagus, 6 left ventricle, 7 right ventricle","tg":["Radiology Thorax slide 79 (Cardiac CT)","Radiology Thorax slide 65 (CT of the chest: Axial)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":166,"f":"F3901"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G7","u":154,"label":"Heart-level axial CT: chambers and great vessels","slides":[{"src":"sl/G009-68.jpg","cap":"Heart-level axial CT: ascending aorta (aortic root) marked","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Thorax, slide 68"},{"src":"sl/G009-67.jpg","cap":"Same level: auricle of the left atrium","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Thorax, slide 67"},{"src":"sl/G009-69.jpg","cap":"Same level: descending thoracic aorta beside the spine","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Thorax, slide 69"}],"q":[{"id":"D045-222-1","s":"Radiology Practice: Name the Numbered Structures (axial chest CT at the level of the heart, structures numbered 1-5).","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Right atrium; Aortic root; Pulmonary artery root; Left atrium; Descending thoracic aorta (listed in the speaker notes; the notes do not tie each name to its number).","a":null,"why":"<b>1 right atrium, 2 aortic root, 3 pulmonary artery root, 4 left atrium, 5 descending thoracic aorta.</b> At this level the aortic root (2) sits in the middle of the heart, with the root of the pulmonary trunk (3) in front and to its left. The right atrium (1) wraps the right side. The left atrium (4) is the back chamber, and the round descending aorta (5) sits left of the spine behind it.<br>Common mix-up: aortic root vs pulmonary root; the pulmonary root is more anterior and to the left.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/D045-222.jpg","in":"Axial contrast chest CT through the heart with red numbers 1-5 around the cardiac chambers and great vessels","tg":["Radiology Thorax slide 66 (CT of the chest: Axial)","Radiology Thorax slide 67 (CT of the chest: Axial)","Radiology Thorax slide 47 (CT of the chest: Axial)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":222,"f":"D045"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G7","u":155,"label":"Left ventricle on angiogram","slides":[{"src":"sl/G009-78.jpg","cap":"Cardiac CT: left ventricle, the thick-walled chamber leading to the aorta","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Thorax, slide 78"}],"q":[{"id":"F3901-169-1","s":"You are in your cardiology residency (wow time goes fast) and your attending is performing an angiogram.  Which chamber of the heart is highlighted (#15)?","o":["A. Right atrium","B. Left atrium","C. Right ventricle","D. Left ventricle"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Left ventricle","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Left ventricle.</b> Number 15 is the large contrast-filled chamber at the lower left of the heart shadow, at the apex, from which the ascending aorta rises.<br>A. Right atrium: forms the right heart border.<br>B. Left atrium: sits high and at the back, behind the aortic root.<br>C. Right ventricle: the front chamber; it leads to the pulmonary trunk, not the aorta.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F3901-169.jpg","in":"Thoracic aortogram/angiogram with numbered vessels; #15 is the large opacified chamber at the lower left of the heart shadow (left ventricle). Only one slide exists, and it already shows the answer in red","tg":["Radiology Thorax slide 78 (Cardiac CT)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":169,"f":"F3901"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G7","u":155,"label":"Catheter tip in left main coronary artery","slides":[{"src":"sl/G009-92.jpg","cap":"Coronary angiogram: catheter tip in the left main coronary artery","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Thorax, slide 92"},{"src":"sl/G009-98.jpg","cap":"Labeled left coronary angiogram: left main, anterior descending, circumflex, diagonals, marginals","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Thorax, slide 98"}],"q":[{"id":"F1180-376-1","s":"What is the yellow arrow pointing to?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Left Main Coronary Artery","a":null,"why":"<b>Left main coronary artery.</b> In a left coronary angiogram, the catheter comes up the aorta and its tip sits in the short first segment of the left coronary artery: the left main. The left main then splits into the LAD (left anterior descending artery) and the circumflex artery.<br>Common mix-up: calling it the LAD; the LAD is the branch running down the front of the heart after the split.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F1180-376.jpg","in":"Coronary angiogram (faint contrast); yellow arrow at the catheter tip engaged in the first short segment of the left coronary system.","tg":["Radiology Thorax slide 92 (Coronary Angiography)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":376,"f":"F1180"},{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":378,"f":"F1180"},{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":379,"f":"F1180"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G7","u":155,"label":"Left anterior descending artery: the widow maker","slides":[{"src":"sl/G009-94.jpg","cap":"Coronary angiogram: left anterior descending artery marked","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Thorax, slide 94"},{"src":"sl/G009-98.jpg","cap":"Labeled left coronary angiogram: left main, anterior descending, circumflex, diagonals, marginals","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Thorax, slide 98"}],"q":[{"id":"F1252-93-1","s":"You are rounding with your attending and he tells you the patient you are about to see just had a MI from occlusion of the artery that would have made his poor wife a widow. Being the smart medical student that you are you identified the vessel on imaging as: What is the vessel?","o":["A. A","B. B","C. C"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"B. Left Anterior Descending or Anterior Interventricular Artery","a":"B","why":"<b>B. LAD (left anterior descending, or anterior interventricular) artery.</b> Blocking it causes a large anterior heart attack, the 'widow maker'. On this angiogram it is the vessel running straight down the middle of the heart shadow, in the interventricular groove toward the apex.<br>A. A smaller branch heading off toward the image's left edge, not the vessel in the groove.<br>C. The vessels along the outer heart border: the circumflex artery and its marginal branches.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F1252-93.jpg","in":"Left coronary angiogram with arrows A (branch running to the lower left: circumflex/marginal side), B (the artery running down the anterior interventricular groove = LAD), C (diagonal/lateral branches)","tg":["Radiology Thorax slide 94 (Coronary Angiography)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":93,"f":"F1252"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G7","u":156,"label":"Splenic flexure: left upper quadrant, beside spleen","slides":[{"src":"sl/G010-95.jpg","cap":"Colon diagram: left colic (splenic) flexure high on the left","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Abdomen I, slide 95"},{"src":"sl/G010-103.jpg","cap":"Axial CT: splenic flexure of the colon next to the spleen","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Abdomen I, slide 103"}],"q":[{"id":"D042-198-1","s":"A patient presents with pain in the left upper quadrant. A radiograph may show an abnormality/obstruction at. . .","o":["A. Right (hepatic) flexure","B. Left (splenic) flexure","C. Duodenum","D. Cecum"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Left (splenic) flexure","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Left (splenic) flexure.</b> The bend between the transverse and descending colon sits high in the LUQ (left upper quadrant), next to the spleen.<br>A. Right (hepatic) flexure: right upper quadrant, under the liver.<br>C. Duodenum: right of midline, around the pancreatic head.<br>D. Cecum: right lower quadrant.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Radiology Abd I slide 103 (Splenic Flexure)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI module TA review","sl":198,"f":"D042"}]},{"id":"D045-261-1","s":"CT colonography reveals haustra and a sharp turn adjacent to the spleen. Which colonic region is visualized?","o":["A. Cecum","B. Hepatic flexure","C. Splenic flexure","D. Sigmoid colon","E. Rectum"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Splenic flexure","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Splenic flexure.</b> Haustra mean colon; the sharp turn beside the spleen is the left colic (splenic) flexure, where the transverse colon turns down into the descending colon.<br>A. Cecum: right lower quadrant.<br>B. Hepatic flexure: the right-sided bend, under the liver.<br>D. Sigmoid colon: left lower quadrant and pelvis.<br>E. Rectum: in the pelvis.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Radiology Abd I slide 103 (Splenic Flexure)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":261,"f":"D045"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G7","u":156,"label":"Ascending colon on plain film: right flank","slides":[{"src":"sl/G010-12.jpg","cap":"Plain film: arrows on mottled stool of the ascending colon, right flank","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Abdomen I, slide 12"},{"src":"sl/G010-95.jpg","cap":"Colon diagram: cecum, ascending, transverse, descending, sigmoid, both flexures","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Abdomen I, slide 95"}],"q":[{"id":"D041-276-1","s":"What are these arrows pointing to?","o":["A. Duodenum","B. Pancreas","C. Ileum","D. Ascending colon","E. liver"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Ascending colon","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Ascending colon.</b> The arrows point to mottled gas and stool running up the patient's right flank, lateral to the spine. Mottled stool in the right flank = ascending colon.<br>A. Duodenum: near the midline, around the head of the pancreas; rarely seen on plain film.<br>B. Pancreas: soft tissue, not seen on plain film.<br>C. Ileum: smaller loops, lower right and pelvis, without stool.<br>E. Liver: a large solid shadow in the right upper quadrant.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/D041-276.jpg","in":"Frontal abdominal plain film (child); four red arrows point to mottled stool/gas-filled bowel along the patient's right flank lateral to the spine (ascending colon)","tg":["Radiology Abd I slide 12 (Ascending Colon)","Radiology Abd I slide 100 (Ascending Colon)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":276,"f":"D041"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G7","u":157,"label":"Thoracic esophagus on barium esophagram","slides":[{"src":"sl/G010-52.jpg","cap":"Lateral esophagram: barium-filled thoracic esophagus behind trachea and heart","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Abdomen I, slide 52"}],"q":[{"id":"D045-254-1","s":"A barium esophagram reveals a structure descending posterior to the trachea, coursing toward the stomach. What is it?","o":["A. Descending duodenum","B. Thoracic esophagus","C. Gastric fundus","D. Pancreatic tail","E. Splenic flexure"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Thoracic esophagus","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Thoracic esophagus.</b> On a barium esophagram, the barium-filled tube running down behind the trachea and heart to the stomach is the esophagus; in the chest it is the thoracic esophagus.<br>A. Descending duodenum: in the abdomen, beside the pancreatic head.<br>C. Gastric fundus: the top of the stomach, under the left diaphragm.<br>D. Pancreatic tail: a solid organ, never filled by swallowed barium.<br>E. Splenic flexure: colon, not reached by a swallow.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Radiology Abd I slide 52 (Lateral Esophogram/UGI)","Radiology Abd I slide 47 (Lateral Esophogram/UGI)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":254,"f":"D045"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G7","u":157,"label":"Gastric fundus on barium swallow","slides":[{"src":"sl/G010-56.jpg","cap":"Barium study: stomach parts outlined: cardia, fundus, body, antrum, pylorus","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Abdomen I, slide 56"}],"q":[{"id":"F3901-221-1","s":"A 23 year old female underwent a barium swallow test after complaining of difficulty swallowing for 3 months. Her test results are provided in the image attached. A high school student shadowing you asks what the structure seen in #3 is. You correctly tell them that it is the:","o":["A. Duodenum","B. Fundus of the stomach","C. Pylorus of the stomach","D. Left colic flexure","E. Transverse colon"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Fundus of the stomach","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Fundus of the stomach.</b> Label 3 points to the barium-filled dome just below the left hemidiaphragm, lateral to where the esophagus enters the stomach. The part of the stomach above that entry level is the fundus.<br>A. Duodenum: past the pylorus, right of the spine.<br>C. Pylorus: the narrow outlet on the patient's right, much lower.<br>D. Left colic flexure and E. transverse colon: colon, not filled by swallowed barium.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F3901-221.jpg","in":"Barium swallow (esophagram) with labels 1-4; #3 points to the barium-filled sac below the left hemidiaphragm, lateral to the gastroesophageal junction (gastric fundus)","tg":["Radiology Abd I slide 56 (Coronal Esophogram/UGI)","Radiology Abd I slide 43 (Coronal Esophogram/UGI)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":221,"f":"F3901"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G7","u":158,"label":"Duodenal C-loop wraps the pancreatic head","slides":[{"src":"sl/G007-7.jpg","cap":"Pancreas head encircled by the C-shaped duodenum","where":"2026 lecture: Pancreas & Spleen, slide 7"},{"src":"sl/G010-58.jpg","cap":"Barium study: duodenum parts one to four forming the C-loop","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Abdomen I, slide 58"}],"q":[{"id":"D042-196-1","s":"A CT obtained at the level of L1 shows a C-shaped hollow structure wrapping around the head of the pancreas. What structure is this?","o":["A. Stomach","B. Duodenum","C. Jejunum","D. Ileum","E. Gallbladder"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Duodenum","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Duodenum.</b> Its C-shaped loop (first to third parts) wraps around the head of the pancreas, at about the first to third lumbar vertebrae.<br>A. Stomach: left upper abdomen, in front of the pancreas.<br>C. Jejunum: loops in the left upper abdomen, after the duodenojejunal flexure.<br>D. Ileum: loops in the right lower abdomen and pelvis.<br>E. Gallbladder: a fluid sac under the liver, not a C-shaped loop.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Radiology Abd I slide 72 (2nd & 3rd Part of the Duodenum)","Radiology Abd I slide 57 (Doudenal C-Loop)","Pancreas & Spleen slide 7 (Pancreas)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI module TA review","sl":196,"f":"D042"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G7","u":158,"label":"Jejunum left upper, ileum right lower","slides":[{"src":"sl/G010-59.jpg","cap":"Jejunum in left upper quadrant; ileum continues to right lower quadrant","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Abdomen I, slide 59"},{"src":"sl/G010-85.jpg","cap":"Coronal CT: jejunum with feathery folds in the left upper abdomen","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Abdomen I, slide 85"},{"src":"sl/G010-86.jpg","cap":"Coronal CT: ileum in the right lower abdomen","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Abdomen I, slide 86"}],"q":[{"id":"D045-257-1","s":"A CT enterography shows bowel with thick, prominent valvulae conniventes throughout the lumen and location mainly in the left upper abdomen. What segment is this?","o":["A. Ileum","B. Cecum","C. Jejunum","D. Ascending colon","E. Distal sigmoid"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Jejunum","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Jejunum.</b> The jejunum sits mainly in the left upper abdomen and has tall, closely packed circular folds (valvulae conniventes, or plicae circulares) that look feathery.<br>A. Ileum: lower right abdomen and pelvis, with fewer, lower folds.<br>B. Cecum: right lower quadrant; colon has haustra, not valvulae.<br>D. Ascending colon: right flank, haustra.<br>E. Distal sigmoid: in the pelvis, colon.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Radiology Abd I slide 85 (Coronal View: Jejunem)","Radiology Abd I slide 16 (Small Bowel Obstruction)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":257,"f":"D045"}],"t":1},{"id":"D045-259-1","s":"On abdominal plain film, loops of bowel are identified with fewer and more widely spaced folds, located in the RLQ. Which structure is most consistent?","o":["A. Jejunum","B. Ileum","C. Transverse colon","D. Descending colon","E. Stomach"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Ileum","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Ileum.</b> The ileum lies mostly in the RLQ (right lower quadrant) and pelvis, and its circular folds are fewer and farther apart than in the jejunum.<br>A. Jejunum: left upper abdomen, many tall folds.<br>C. Transverse colon: crosses the upper abdomen, with haustra.<br>D. Descending colon: left flank, with haustra.<br>E. Stomach: left upper quadrant, with rugae.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Radiology Abd I slide 86 (Coronal View: Ileum)","Radiology Abd I slide 10 (Small Bowel)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":259,"f":"D045"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G7","u":159,"label":"External oblique: from lower ribs, runs inferomedially","slides":[{"src":"sl/G003-14.jpg","cap":"External oblique: from ribs 5–12, fibers run down and forward","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 14"}],"q":[{"id":"D045-247-1","s":"A stab wound patient undergoes CT. The muscle originating from the lower ribs and running inferomedially is identified. Which muscle is this?","o":["A. Internal oblique","B. External oblique","C. Transversus abdominis","D. Rectus abdominis","E. Pyramidalis"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"External oblique","a":"B","why":"<b>B. External oblique.</b> It arises from the outer surfaces of ribs 5 to 12, and its fibers run inferomedially, like hands in front pockets.<br>A. Internal oblique: arises from the thoracolumbar fascia, iliac crest and inguinal ligament; fibers run superomedially, at right angles to the external oblique.<br>C. Transversus abdominis: fibers run horizontally.<br>D. Rectus abdominis: vertical fibers, pubis to ribs 5 to 7.<br>E. Pyramidalis: a small triangle above the pubis.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 14 (External Oblique)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":247,"f":"D045"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G7","u":159,"label":"Transversus abdominis: innermost flat muscle","slides":[{"src":"sl/G003-16.jpg","cap":"Transversus abdominis: innermost flat muscle, horizontal fibers","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 16"},{"src":"sl/G003-13.jpg","cap":"Wall layers, superficial to deep: external oblique, internal oblique, transversus","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 13"}],"q":[{"id":"D045-245-1","s":"On axial imaging of the abdomen, a thin muscle layer is seen deep to the internal oblique and wrapping circumferentially around the abdomen. This muscle is:","o":["A. Rectus abdominis","B. External oblique","C. Transversus abdominis","D. Iliacus","E. Quadratus lumborum"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Transversus abdominis","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Transversus abdominis.</b> It is the deepest (innermost) flat muscle, deep to the internal oblique, with fibers running around the abdomen like a belt.<br>A. Rectus abdominis: a vertical strap muscle in front, inside the rectus sheath.<br>B. External oblique: the outermost layer.<br>D. Iliacus: in the iliac fossa of the pelvis.<br>E. Quadratus lumborum: in the posterior wall, beside the spine.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 16 (Transversus Abdominis)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":245,"f":"D045"}]},{"id":"D041-270-1","s":"Identify the muscle the red arrows are pointing to","o":["A. rectus abdominis","B. external oblique","C. internal oblique","D. transversus abdominis"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"transversus abdominis","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Transversus abdominis.</b> The red arrows end on the innermost of the three flat muscle layers in the side abdominal wall, the layer right next to the abdominal cavity.<br>A. Rectus abdominis: the paired strap muscle in front, beside the midline.<br>B. External oblique: the outermost of the three side layers.<br>C. Internal oblique: the middle layer.<br>(The big round muscles beside the vertebra are the psoas major muscles.)","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/D041-270.jpg","in":"Axial anatomical (cadaver) section of the abdomen; red arrows point to the innermost of the three lateral flat muscle layers (transversus abdominis)","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 16 (Transversus Abdominis)","Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 12 (Anterolateral Abdominal Wall)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":270,"f":"D041"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G7","u":159,"label":"Rectus abdominis: paramedian, flexes the trunk","slides":[{"src":"sl/G003-17.jpg","cap":"Rectus abdominis: vertical, beside midline, in rectus sheath; flexes trunk","where":"2026 lecture: Ant. Abdominal Wall, slide 17"}],"q":[{"id":"D045-243-1","s":"During CT review, a radiologist points to a paired, longitudinal muscle immediately adjacent to the midline, enclosed within a rectus sheath. Which abdominal wall muscle is this?","o":["A. Internal oblique","B. External oblique","C. Transversus abdominis","D. Rectus abdominis","E. Psoas major"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Rectus abdominis","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Rectus abdominis.</b> It is the paired vertical muscle right next to the midline (linea alba), wrapped in the rectus sheath.<br>A. Internal oblique and B. external oblique: flat side-wall muscles, lateral to the rectus.<br>C. Transversus abdominis: the deepest side-wall layer.<br>E. Psoas major: posterior, beside the lumbar vertebrae.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 17 (Rectus Abdominis and pyramidalis)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":243,"f":"D045"}]},{"id":"D042-194-1","s":"A patient presents with a complaint of pain during cough and when they try to do sit ups at the gym. Which muscle is most likely affected?","o":["A. External Oblique","B. Internal Oblique","C. Transversus Abdominis","D. Rectus Abdominis"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Rectus Abdominis","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Rectus abdominis.</b> Its main action is flexing the trunk, the movement of a sit-up, and it also tightens during a cough. Pain with both points to the rectus.<br>A. External oblique and B. internal oblique: mainly rotate and side-bend the trunk, though they help flex it.<br>C. Transversus abdominis: mainly compresses the abdomen; it does not flex the trunk.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Ant. Abdominal Wall slide 17 (Rectus Abdominis and pyramidalis)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI module TA review","sl":194,"f":"D042"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G7","u":160,"label":"Inferior vena cava: right of aorta and spine","slides":[{"src":"sl/G010-81.jpg","cap":"Axial CT: inferior vena cava circled, right of the spine","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Abdomen I, slide 81"},{"src":"sl/G011-12.jpg","cap":"Axial CT at liver dome: inferior vena cava in the back of liver","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Abdomen II, slide 12"},{"src":"sl/G012-59.jpg","cap":"Coronal CT: inferior vena cava running up the right side","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Abdomen III, slide 59"}],"q":[{"id":"D045-264-1","s":"A CT abdomen demonstrates a large vessel coursing along the right side of the spine. What vessel is this?","o":["A. Aorta","B. IVC","C. Portal vein","D. Splenic artery","E. Superior mesenteric artery"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"IVC","a":"B","why":"<b>B. IVC (inferior vena cava).</b> The large vessel on the right side of the spine is the IVC; the aorta runs on the left side.<br>A. Aorta: left of midline, in front of the spine.<br>C. Portal vein: in front, entering the liver at the porta hepatis.<br>D. Splenic artery: small, wavy, along the top of the pancreas to the spleen.<br>E. SMA (superior mesenteric artery): a small vessel in front of the aorta.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Radiology Abd I slide 81 (IVC)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":264,"f":"D045"}],"t":1},{"id":"F1156-86-1","s":"42 Identify","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"IVC (inferior vena cava)","a":null,"why":"<b>IVC (inferior vena cava).</b> The asterisk marks the round vessel set into the back of the liver, to the right of the esophagus and aorta. At the liver dome the IVC runs in its groove on the back of the liver, about to pierce the diaphragm and enter the right atrium.<br>Common mix-up: the aorta, which lies on the left, directly in front of the spine.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F1156-86.jpg","in":"Axial contrast CT at the level of the liver dome and heart; a blue asterisk marks the vessel passing through the posterior liver to the right of the esophagus/aorta (IVC)","tg":["Radiology Abd II slide 12 (IVC)","Radiology Abd II slide 20 (IVC)","Radiology Abd I slide 81 (IVC)"],"src":[{"t":"? TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":86,"f":"F1156"}]},{"id":"F3901-172-1","s":"*This is image is HARD to read!* Lets walk through it. It is two cuts in a coronal MRI. Identify Both","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Blue arrow = IVC; green arrow = Abdominal Aorta","a":null,"why":"<b>Blue = IVC (inferior vena cava); green = abdominal aorta.</b> On a coronal MRI (magnetic resonance imaging), both vessels run vertically in front of the spine. The IVC is on the patient's right (image left) and is wider; the aorta is on the patient's left (image right).<br>Common mix-up: forgetting that the image's left is the patient's right.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F3901-172.jpg","in":"Two coronal MRI cuts of the upper abdomen; blue arrow on the vertical vessel to the patient's right (IVC), green arrow on the vessel to its left (abdominal aorta)","tg":["Radiology Abd III slide 59 (Inferior Vena Cava)","Radiology Abd III slide 3 (Abdominal Aorta)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":172,"f":"F3901"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G7","u":160,"label":"Kidney sides: patient's left is image right","slides":[{"src":"sl/G010-62.jpg","cap":"Orientation: liver on image left (patient's right), spleen on image right","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Abdomen I, slide 62"},{"src":"sl/G012-60.jpg","cap":"Coronal CT: renal veins; right kidney beside the inferior vena cava","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Abdomen III, slide 60"}],"q":[{"id":"D041-279-1","s":"Which letter corresponds to the left kidney?","o":["A. A","B. B","C. C","D. D","E. E","F. F"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"E (the kidney on the image's right side)","a":"E","why":"<b>E.</b> CT (computed tomography) images are viewed from the patient's feet, so the patient's left is on the image's right. E is the bean-shaped kidney on the image right: the left kidney.<br>A. The right kidney (image left, under the liver).<br>B. Liver.<br>C and D. The psoas major muscles beside the vertebral body.<br>F. A loop of bowel in the left abdomen, in front of the kidney.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/D041-279.jpg","in":"Axial contrast abdominal CT at renal level with red letters A-F: A = kidney on image left (patient's right), B = liver, C/D = vessels anterior to the spine (IVC/aorta), E = kidney on image right (patient's left), F = spleen/colon region","tg":["Radiology Abd II slide 76 (Kidneys)","Radiology Abd II slide 83 (Normal Kidney: NE-CT)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":279,"f":"D041"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3901-173-1","s":"*This is image is HARD to read!* Lets walk through it. It is two cuts in a coronal MRI. What organ?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"RIGHT Kidney","a":null,"why":"<b>Right kidney.</b> On this coronal MRI (magnetic resonance imaging), the arrowed oval organ lies lateral to the IVC (inferior vena cava), on the patient's right, which is the image's left. A bean-shaped organ beside the IVC, below the liver, is the right kidney.<br>Common mix-up: reading the image's left as the patient's left.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F3901-173.jpg","in":"Same coronal MRI; red arrow points to the oval organ lateral to the IVC (right kidney)","tg":["Radiology Abd II slide 76 (Kidneys)","Radiology Abd II slide 77 (Kidney Anatomy)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":173,"f":"F3901"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G7","u":161,"label":"Gastric air bubble marks the stomach","slides":[{"src":"sl/G010-9.jpg","cap":"Plain film: gastric air bubble outlined in the left upper abdomen","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Abdomen I, slide 9"},{"src":"sl/G010-66.jpg","cap":"Axial CT: stomach with black air pocket in the left upper abdomen","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Abdomen I, slide 66"}],"q":[{"id":"D041-273-1","s":"What clues you in the MOST to thinking the arrow points to the stomach?","o":["A. It’s on the left side of the body","B. It has an air bubble","C. It’s gray","D. The presence of the liver"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"It has an air bubble","a":"B","why":"<b>B. It has an air bubble.</b> The stomach is the one organ in the left upper abdomen that normally holds a pocket of air (the gastric bubble), which is black on CT (computed tomography) and X-ray.<br>A. Left side: helpful, but the spleen, colon and kidney are also on the left.<br>C. It is gray: almost every soft tissue is gray on CT.<br>D. Liver present: the liver is huge and shows up on many slices, so it proves nothing.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Axial upper-abdominal CT; arrow points to air-containing stomach in the left upper abdomen","tg":["Radiology Abd I slide 9 (Gastric Bubble)","Radiology Abd I slide 66 (Stomach)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":273,"f":"D041"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G7","u":161,"label":"Spleen: posterolateral left upper quadrant","slides":[{"src":"sl/G010-62.jpg","cap":"Axial CT: star on the spleen, far left behind the stomach","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Abdomen I, slide 62"},{"src":"sl/G007-22.jpg","cap":"Spleen in the left upper quadrant, against ribs 9 to 11","where":"2026 lecture: Pancreas & Spleen, slide 22"}],"q":[{"id":"F0008-134-1","s":"In the following enhanced CT scan of the abdomen, the arrow points to the:","o":["A) spleen.","B) liver.","C) stomach.","D) left kidney.","E) inferior lobe of the left lung."],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"spleen","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Spleen.</b> The arrow points to the solid organ at the far left, posterolateral, behind the stomach and under ribs 9 to 11. In the left upper quadrant (image right), that is the spleen.<br>B. Liver: the large organ on the patient's right (image left).<br>C. Stomach: the contrast- and air-filled organ in the middle-left, in front of the spleen.<br>D. Left kidney: lower and closer to the spine.<br>E. Lower lobe of the left lung: black (air), above the diaphragm.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0008-134.jpg","in":"Axial contrast CT of the upper abdomen (liver on the right, contrast-filled stomach in the middle); blue arrow points to the solid organ at the far left posterolateral side behind the stomach. Slide 136 shows the same CT labeled (Spleen).","tg":["Pancreas & Spleen slide 28 (Ribs 9-11)","Pancreas & Spleen slide 22 (Largest lymphatic organ, very vascular Located in left upper quadrant (LUQ))"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":134,"f":"F0008"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G7","u":161,"label":"Upper abdomen axial CT: label the structures","slides":[{"src":"sl/G010-62.jpg","cap":"Axial CT: stars on liver (image left), stomach and spleen (image right)","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Abdomen I, slide 62"},{"src":"sl/G011-11.jpg","cap":"Axial CT: caudate lobe beside the inferior vena cava","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Abdomen II, slide 11"},{"src":"sl/G011-15.jpg","cap":"Axial CT: portal vein marked as it enters the liver","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Abdomen II, slide 15"}],"q":[{"id":"D042-200-1","s":"Label the structures indicated on this axial contrast CT of the upper abdomen (labels hidden).","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Falciform ligament; left lobe of the liver; pyloric antrum of the stomach; body of the stomach; caudate lobe of the liver; right lobe of the liver; portal vein; inferior vena cava; right adrenal gland; right crus of the diaphragm; aorta; left crus of the diaphragm; tail of the pancreas; spleen.","a":null,"why":"<b>Upper abdomen, key landmarks.</b> The falciform ligament is the fat-filled cleft at the front of the liver, between the big right lobe and the left lobe. The stomach (body on the left, pyloric antrum crossing the midline) lies in front. The caudate lobe sits between the portal vein and the IVC (inferior vena cava). The IVC is on the right and the aorta on the left, hugged by the right and left diaphragm crura. The right adrenal is the thin line behind the IVC. The pancreatic tail points to the spleen at the far left.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/D042-200.jpg","in":"Axial contrast CT at the level of the upper abdomen (liver/stomach/spleen) with 14 blank tan label boxes; slide 201 shows the labels","tg":["Radiology Abd II slide 8 (CT: Liver)","Radiology Abd II slide 11 (Caudate Lobe)","Radiology Abd II slide 12 (IVC)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI module TA review","sl":200,"f":"D042"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G7","u":162,"label":"Gallbladder body on imaging","slides":[{"src":"sl/G011-29.jpg","cap":"Ultrasound: gallbladder body, the main fluid-filled sac","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Abdomen II, slide 29"},{"src":"sl/G011-24.jpg","cap":"Diagram: gallbladder fundus, body, neck and cystic duct","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Abdomen II, slide 24"}],"q":[{"id":"D045-250-1","s":"A 42-year-old woman has RUQ pain. CT shows a fluid-filled sac in the gallbladder fossa connected via a duct to the biliary tree. Which segment is being identified?","o":["A. Gallbladder body","B. Gallbladder neck","C. Pancreatic head","D. Stomach antrum","E. Splenic hilum"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Gallbladder body","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Gallbladder body.</b> The gallbladder is a pear-shaped, fluid-filled sac in the gallbladder fossa under the liver, linked to the biliary tree by the cystic duct. The body is its main sac, between the fundus (rounded tip) and the neck.<br>B. Neck: the narrow end tapering into the cystic duct, not the sac.<br>C. Pancreatic head: solid, beside the duodenum.<br>D. Stomach antrum: muscular tube near the pylorus.<br>E. Splenic hilum: left upper quadrant.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"slide 250 has a picture; question answerable from text","tg":["Radiology Abd II slide 29 (Gallbladder: Body)","Radiology Abd II slide 23 (Gallbladder)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":250,"f":"D045"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G7","u":162,"label":"Pancreatic body: behind stomach, front of splenic vein","slides":[{"src":"sl/G011-56.jpg","cap":"Axial CT: pancreatic body and tail outlined behind the stomach","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Abdomen II, slide 56"},{"src":"sl/G011-21.jpg","cap":"Axial CT: splenic vein running along the back of the pancreas","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Abdomen II, slide 21"}],"q":[{"id":"D045-252-1","s":"An axial CT at the level of the pancreas shows a structure posterior to the stomach and anterior to the splenic vein. Which organ is this?","o":["A. Gallbladder","B. Pancreatic body","C. Duodenal bulb","D. Left lobe of liver","E. Spleen"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Pancreatic body","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Pancreatic body.</b> The pancreas lies behind the stomach (across the lesser sac), and the splenic vein runs along its back surface. So the organ between the stomach and the splenic vein is the pancreas, here its body.<br>A. Gallbladder: right side, under the liver.<br>C. Duodenal bulb: right of midline, after the pylorus.<br>D. Left lobe of the liver: in front of the stomach.<br>E. Spleen: far left and back, where the splenic vein begins.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Radiology Abd II slide 55 (Pancreas: Body and Tail)","Radiology Abd II slide 56 (Pancreas: Body and Tail)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":252,"f":"D045"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G7","u":162,"label":"Sphincter of Oddi at the major papilla","slides":[{"src":"sl/G011-69.jpg","cap":"Magnetic resonance cholangiopancreatography: arrow at major papilla, sphincter of Oddi","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Abdomen II, slide 69"},{"src":"sl/G011-66.jpg","cap":"Same image: arrow on the common bile duct","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Abdomen II, slide 66"}],"q":[{"id":"F0008-158-1","s":"An MRCP 3D image is shown. What structure is indicated by the arrow?","o":["A. Sphincter of Oddi","B. Minor Papilla","C. CBD","D. Cystic Duct","E. Pancreatic Duct"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Sphincter of Oddi","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Sphincter of Oddi (at the major duodenal papilla).</b> On this MRCP (magnetic resonance cholangiopancreatography), the arrow is at the very bottom of the common bile duct, where it enters the duodenal wall with the pancreatic duct.<br>B. Minor papilla: higher up, where the accessory pancreatic duct drains.<br>C. CBD (common bile duct): the long tube above the arrow.<br>D. Cystic duct: joins the gallbladder neck to the bile duct, high up.<br>E. Pancreatic duct: runs across from the patient's left to meet the CBD.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0008-158.jpg","in":"3D MRCP: gallbladder top left, bile duct descending to the contrast-filled duodenum; blue arrow points to the lowest end of the common bile duct where it enters the duodenal wall.","tg":["Radiology Abd II slide 69 (MRCP: Major Papilla/Sphincter of Oddi)","Radiology Abd II slide 66 (MRCP: Common Bile Duct)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":158,"f":"F0008"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G7","u":163,"label":"Aorta bifurcates at L4 into common iliacs","slides":[{"src":"sl/G012-48.jpg","cap":"Aortogram: arrows on the common iliac arteries arising at the bifurcation","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Abdomen III, slide 48"},{"src":"sl/G012-7.jpg","cap":"Aortogram: aorta splitting into the two common iliac arteries","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Abdomen III, slide 7"}],"q":[{"id":"F3902-123-1","s":"At what level can you find the bifurcation of the aorta?","o":["a) T12","b) L1","c) L4","d) L3"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"L4","a":"C","why":"<b>c) L4 (fourth lumbar vertebra).</b> The abdominal aorta bifurcates into the common iliac arteries at the fourth lumbar vertebra, near the level of the umbilicus and iliac crests.<br>a) T12 (twelfth thoracic): where the aorta enters the abdomen and gives off the celiac trunk.<br>b) L1 (first lumbar): level of the superior mesenteric and renal arteries.<br>d) L3 (third lumbar): level of the inferior mesenteric artery.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Radiology Abd III slide 7 (Common Illiac Arteries)","Radiology Abd III slide 3 (Abdominal Aorta)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":123,"f":"F3902"}]},{"id":"D045-268-1","s":"Axial CT at the level of the L4 vertebra shows the aorta dividing into two major branches. What vessels are these?","o":["A. Internal iliac arteries","B. Renal arteries","C. Lumbar arteries","D. Common iliac arteries","E. Superior mesenteric artery and celiac trunk"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Common iliac arteries","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Common iliac arteries.</b> At the fourth lumbar vertebra the aorta ends by splitting into the right and left common iliac arteries.<br>A. Internal iliac arteries: arise lower, when each common iliac splits, in front of the sacroiliac joint.<br>B. Renal arteries: higher, at the first to second lumbar vertebrae.<br>C. Lumbar arteries: small back-wall branches, not an end split.<br>E. SMA (superior mesenteric artery) and celiac trunk: single front branches, much higher.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Radiology Abd III slide 7 (Common Illiac Arteries)","Radiology Abd III slide 48 (Common Iliac Arteries)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":268,"f":"D045"}],"t":1},{"id":"D041-282-1","s":"Which blood vessel gives off the vessels the arrows are pointing to?","o":["A. internal iliac artery","B. Superior mesenteric artery","C. Left renal artery","D. Descending aorta","E. Lumbar artery"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Descending aorta","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Descending (abdominal) aorta.</b> The arrows point to the two common iliac arteries, the end branches of the aorta at its bifurcation, at about the fourth lumbar vertebra.<br>A. Internal iliac artery: a branch of the common iliac, not its source.<br>B. SMA (superior mesenteric artery): supplies the midgut; gives no iliac branches.<br>C. Left renal artery: goes to the kidney.<br>E. Lumbar artery: a small body-wall branch of the aorta.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/D041-282.jpg","in":"Aortogram of the distal aorta and pelvis/lower limbs; blue arrows point to the two vessels arising at the aortic bifurcation (common iliac arteries)","tg":["Radiology Abd III slide 7 (Common Illiac Arteries)","Radiology Abd III slide 48 (Common Iliac Arteries)","Radiology Abd III slide 3 (Abdominal Aorta)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":282,"f":"D041"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G7","u":163,"label":"Aortogram branches: renal (sideways) vs lumbar (posterior)","slides":[{"src":"sl/G012-5.jpg","cap":"Aortogram: renal arteries running sideways to each kidney","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Abdomen III, slide 5"},{"src":"sl/G012-3.jpg","cap":"Three-dimensional aorta: its branches from the top down to the iliac split","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Abdomen III, slide 3"}],"q":[{"id":"D045-266-1","s":"An abdominal aortogram shows paired lateral branches coursing toward each kidney. These branches are:","o":["A. Lumbar arteries","B. Gonadal arteries","C. Renal arteries","D. Common iliac arteries","E. Inferior phrenic arteries"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Renal arteries","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Renal arteries.</b> The large paired branches leaving the sides of the aorta at about the first to second lumbar vertebrae and running to each kidney are the renal arteries.<br>A. Lumbar arteries: small, to the posterior body wall.<br>B. Gonadal arteries: thin, from the front, run down to the gonads.<br>D. Common iliac arteries: the end split, lower, at the fourth lumbar vertebra.<br>E. Inferior phrenic arteries: small, run up to the diaphragm.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Radiology Abd III slide 5 (Renal Arteries)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":266,"f":"D045"}],"t":1},{"id":"D045-269-1","s":"A catheter study demonstrates small posterior branches of the abdominal aorta that supply the posterior body wall. They are:","o":["A. Renal arteries","B. Lumbar arteries","C. Celiac trunk","D. Common iliac arteries","E. Inferior mesenteric artery"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Lumbar arteries","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Lumbar arteries.</b> Four pairs of small lumbar arteries leave the back of the abdominal aorta and supply the posterior body wall.<br>A. Renal arteries: large, paired, run sideways to the kidneys at the first to second lumbar vertebrae.<br>C. Celiac trunk: single, from the front at the twelfth thoracic vertebra.<br>D. Common iliac arteries: the end split at the fourth lumbar vertebra.<br>E. IMA (inferior mesenteric artery): single, from the front at the third lumbar vertebra.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["Radiology Abd III slide 3 (Abdominal Aorta)","Radiology Abd III slide 4 (Abdominal Aorta)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI exam TA review","sl":269,"f":"D045"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G7","u":163,"label":"Abdominal aortogram: label the branches","slides":[{"src":"sl/G012-5.jpg","cap":"Aortogram: arrows on the right and left renal arteries","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Abdomen III, slide 5"},{"src":"sl/G012-7.jpg","cap":"Aortogram: arrows on the two common iliac arteries at the bifurcation","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Abdomen III, slide 7"},{"src":"sl/G012-3.jpg","cap":"Three-dimensional aorta: its branches from the top down to the iliac split","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Abdomen III, slide 3"}],"q":[{"id":"D042-202-1","s":"Label the vessels indicated on this abdominal aortogram (labels hidden; L4 vertebra labeled).","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"1st pair of lumbar arteries; right renal artery; left renal artery; abdominal aorta; 4th pair of lumbar arteries; inferior mesenteric artery; median sacral artery; right common iliac artery; left common iliac artery; right internal iliac artery; left internal iliac artery; right external iliac artery; left external iliac artery.","a":null,"why":"<b>Abdominal aortogram branches.</b> The renal arteries are the big side branches near the top, running to the kidneys. Lumbar arteries are small paired side branches at each lumbar level. The IMA (inferior mesenteric artery) leaves the front-left at the third lumbar vertebra. The aorta splits at the fourth lumbar vertebra into the common iliacs, and the thin median sacral artery continues down the midline. Each common iliac splits into an internal iliac (medial, into the pelvis) and an external iliac (lateral, toward the leg).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/D042-202.jpg","in":"Abdominal aortogram (renal level to pelvis) with 13 blank tan label boxes (L4 vertebra labeled); slide 203 shows the labels","tg":["Radiology Abd III slide 3 (Abdominal Aorta)","Radiology Abd III slide 5 (Renal Arteries)","Radiology Abd III slide 7 (Common Illiac Arteries)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI module TA review","sl":202,"f":"D042"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G7","u":164,"label":"Celiac trunk first, superior mesenteric artery below","slides":[{"src":"sl/G012-9.jpg","cap":"Sagittal CT: celiac trunk, the first front branch of the aorta","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Abdomen III, slide 9"},{"src":"sl/G012-25.jpg","cap":"Sagittal CT: superior mesenteric artery just below the celiac trunk","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Abdomen III, slide 25"}],"q":[{"id":"F1156-58-1","s":"Which of the following is superior mesenteric artery?","o":["A. A","B. B","C. C"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"B","a":"B","why":"<b>B. SMA (superior mesenteric artery).</b> On a sagittal CT (computed tomography), two front branches leave the aorta close together. The upper one (A), short and pointing forward, is the celiac trunk. The lower one (B), about a centimeter below, runs downward in front of the aorta: the SMA.<br>A. Celiac trunk.<br>C. The abdominal aorta itself, in front of the spine.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F1156-58.jpg","in":"Sagittal contrast CT of the abdominal aorta with arrows A (vessel just above, the celiac trunk), B (anterior branch below it = SMA) and C (aorta)","tg":["Radiology Abd III slide 24 (Celiac Trunk: MIP)","Radiology Abd III slide 25 (Superior Mesenteric Artery)","Radiology Abd I slide 88 (Coronal View: SMA)"],"src":[{"t":"? TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":58,"f":"F1156"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3900-153-1","s":"What is 6, 5 and 2 in that order?","o":["A. SMA, Abdominal aorta, Celiac a.","B. Celiac a., Abdominal aorta, SMA","C. Celiac a., Abdominal aorta, IMA","D. Common Hepatic, Abdominal aorta, Celiac a."],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Celiac a., Abdominal aorta, SMA","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Celiac trunk, abdominal aorta, SMA (superior mesenteric artery).</b> On a sagittal (side) view the aorta (5) runs down in front of the spine. Its first front branch, short and just below the diaphragm at the twelfth thoracic vertebra, is the celiac trunk (6). The next, about a centimeter lower, running downward, is the SMA (2).<br>A. Swaps the celiac trunk and SMA.<br>C. The IMA (inferior mesenteric artery) arises much lower, at the third lumbar vertebra.<br>D. The common hepatic is a celiac branch, not an aortic branch.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F3900-153.jpg","in":"Sagittal contrast CT/MRI of the abdomen with numbered pointers: 6 = upper anterior aortic branch (celiac), 5 = aorta, 2 = lower anterior branch (SMA)","tg":["Radiology Abd III slide 9 (Celiac Trunk)","Radiology Abd III slide 24 (Celiac Trunk: MIP)","Radiology Abd I slide 88 (Coronal View: SMA)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":153,"f":"F3900"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3901-219-1","s":"A 16 year old high school quarterback comes to your office and you diagnose him with mononucleosis. You are worried that his spleen may be inflamed and instruct him to avoid playing contact sports for 6 weeks. The artery that supplies the spleen is a branch of which structure labeled on the provided angiography?","o":["A. 1","B. 2","C. 3","D. 4","E. 5"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"5 (Celiac Artery/Trunk)","a":"E","why":"<b>E. 5 (celiac trunk).</b> The spleen is supplied by the splenic artery, the largest branch of the celiac trunk. On this side-view aortogram, 5 is the first branch off the front of the aorta: the celiac trunk.<br>A. 1: the aorta itself, above its branches.<br>B. 2: the renal artery level.<br>C. 3: the lower abdominal aorta.<br>D. 4: the next front branch, the SMA (superior mesenteric artery), which supplies the midgut, not the spleen.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F3901-219.jpg","in":"Lateral aortogram (posterior left, anterior right) with labels 1-5; 5 = first anterior branch of the aorta (celiac trunk), 4 = branch just below it (SMA), 1/3 = aorta, 2 = renal level","tg":["Radiology Abd III slide 9 (Celiac Trunk)","Radiology Abd III slide 17 (Celiac Trunk: MIP)","Radiology Abd III slide 24 (Celiac Trunk: MIP)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":219,"f":"F3901"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G7","u":165,"label":"Celiac trunk branches on angiogram","slides":[{"src":"sl/G012-11.jpg","cap":"Normal celiac trunk: left gastric, common hepatic and its branches, splenic","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Abdomen III, slide 11"},{"src":"sl/F1180-367.jpg","cap":"Labeled celiac angiogram: common hepatic, splenic, left gastric, hepatic, right gastroepiploic","where":"2022 Harlem TA review, slide 367"},{"src":"sl/G012-20.jpg","cap":"Celiac CT angiogram: left and right hepatic arteries marked","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Abdomen III, slide 20"}],"q":[{"id":"F1228-324-1","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Pancreatic Artery","B. Left Gastric Artery","C. Gastroduodenal Artery","D. Celiac Artery","E. Splenic Artery"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Splenic Artery","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Splenic artery.</b> The arrow is on the large, wavy (tortuous) vessel running from the celiac trunk to the patient's left (image right), toward the spleen.<br>A. Pancreatic arteries: small branches off the splenic artery into the pancreas.<br>B. Left gastric artery: the smallest celiac branch, runs up toward the esophagus.<br>C. Gastroduodenal artery: runs down from the common hepatic behind the duodenum, on the right.<br>D. Celiac artery: the short trunk where the catheter sits, before it branches.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F1228-324.jpg","in":"Same celiac angiogram; green arrow points to the large tortuous vessel running to the patient's left toward the spleen = splenic artery.","tg":["Radiology Abd III slide 17 (Celiac Trunk: MIP)","Radiology Abd III slide 11 (“Normal” Celiac Trunk)"],"src":[{"t":"2019 review","n":"old Exam 2 review: abdomen part only","sl":324,"f":"F1228"},{"t":"? tutor review","n":"old Exam 2: abdomen part only","sl":220,"f":"F1243"}],"t":1},{"id":"F1180-370-1","s":"What is this structure?","o":["A. Common Hepatic Artery","B. Right Ureter","C. Right Hepatic Artery","D. Splenic Artery","E. Pancreatic Artery"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Common Hepatic Artery","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Common hepatic artery.</b> The arrow is on the branch leaving the celiac trunk toward the patient's right (image left), heading for the liver before it splits into the proper hepatic and gastroduodenal arteries.<br>B. Right ureter: not filled on an arteriogram.<br>C. Right hepatic artery: further on, at the liver hilum.<br>D. Splenic artery: runs to the patient's left (image right).<br>E. Pancreatic artery: small branches off the splenic artery.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F1180-370.jpg","in":"Celiac-trunk angiogram (catheter in the celiac trunk, bright contrast pool); the green arrow points to the artery leaving the trunk toward the patient’s right (toward the liver) before it divides.","tg":["Radiology Abd III slide 18 (Celiac Trunk: MIP)","Radiology Abd III slide 11 (“Normal” Celiac Trunk)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":370,"f":"F1180"},{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":299,"f":"F1178"},{"t":"2019 review","n":"old Exam 2 review: abdomen part only","sl":322,"f":"F1228"},{"t":"? tutor review","n":"old Exam 2: abdomen part only","sl":218,"f":"F1243"}],"t":1},{"id":"D042-125-1","s":"You are on your radiology rotation and your preceptor shows you this normal angiogram. Which arrow is Pointing to the right gastro-omental artery?","o":["A. Green arrow","B. Gold","C. Tan","D. White"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Tan","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Tan arrow.</b> The right gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery continues the GDA (gastroduodenal artery). The GDA runs straight down behind the first part of the duodenum; at its lower end it turns left along the greater curvature as the right gastro-omental artery, where the tan arrow points.<br>A. Green: the catheter.<br>B. Gold: the left gastric artery, running up toward the esophagus.<br>D. White: the common hepatic artery, running right toward the liver.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/D042-125.jpg","in":"Celiac angiogram with four colored arrows: green (catheter), gold (left gastric), white (common hepatic), tan (points up along the vessel descending to the right of the spine = gastroduodenal/right gastro-omental artery)","tg":["Radiology Abd III slide 9 (Celiac Trunk)","Radiology Abd III slide 21 (Celiac Trunk: MIP)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 111 (Gasroduodenal:)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI module TA review","sl":125,"f":"D042"}],"t":1},{"id":"D041-180-1","s":"A patient develops sudden epigastric pain. Angiography shows reduced contrast filling in the artery labeled 6. Which region would most likely show ischemic changes if this artery were acutely blocked?","o":["A. Lesser curvature of the stomach","B. Fundus of the stomach via short gastric branches","C. First part of the duodenum","D. Right lobe of the liver"],"g":["D"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Fundus of the stomach via short gastric branches","a":"B","why":"<b>D. Right lobe of the liver.</b> On this angiogram's legend, label 6 is the right hepatic artery (a branch of the proper hepatic), so blocking it starves the right lobe. The old deck keyed B, which fits label 9 (splenic artery), not 6.<br>A. Lesser curvature: right and left gastric arteries, which form an arcade.<br>B. Fundus via short gastrics: branches of the splenic artery.<br>C. First part of the duodenum: gastroduodenal artery (label 4) and its branches.","ex":"","fl":["Per the source's own legend for this angiogram (slide 182 legend (same angiogram): 1 = celiac axis, 2 = common hepatic artery, 3 = right gastric artery, 4 = gastroduodenal artery, 5 = proper hepatic artery, 6 = right hepatic artery, 7 = left hepatic artery, 8 = left gastric artery, 9 = splenic artery), label 6 is the right hepatic artery, whose block would cause ischemia of the right lobe of the liver (D). The keyed fundus/short-gastric answer would fit label 9 (splenic artery)."],"ch":true,"img":"img/D041-180.jpg","in":"Same celiac axis angiogram (numbered arrows 1-9); asks about label 6. slide 182 legend (same angiogram): 1 = celiac axis, 2 = common hepatic artery, 3 = right gastric artery, 4 = gastroduodenal artery, 5 = proper hepatic artery, 6 = right hepatic artery, 7 = left hepatic artery, 8 = left gastric artery, 9 = splenic artery.","tg":["Radiology Abd III slide 9 (Celiac Trunk)","Liver/Biliary/Portal slide 20 (LIVER)","Peritoneum/Stomach slide 110 (Left gastric:)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI Block exam TA review","sl":180,"f":"D041"}],"t":1},{"id":"F1180-372-1","s":"Name the following branches","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Upper-left arrow = middle hepatic artery (MHA); upper-right arrow = left hepatic artery (LHA); right lower arrow = common hepatic artery (CHA); lower-left arrow = gastroduodenal artery (GDA); left arrow = right hepatic artery (RHA)","a":null,"why":"<b>Middle hepatic, left hepatic, common hepatic, gastroduodenal, right hepatic.</b> Follow the flow: the common hepatic artery (lower-right arrow) leaves the celiac trunk toward the liver. It gives off the gastroduodenal artery (lower-left arrow), which dives behind the duodenum, then continues as the proper hepatic. That splits into the right hepatic (left arrow, to the patient's right lobe) and the left hepatic (upper-right arrow). The middle hepatic (upper-left arrow) supplies segment four.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F1180-372.jpg","in":"Coronal CT angiogram (MIP) of the celiac/hepatic arteries with five arrows and blacked-out labels: two pointing to branches going up into the liver (middle and left hepatic), one to the right-sided hepatic branch, one to the vessel descending behind the duodenum, and one to the trunk coming from the celiac axis.","tg":["Radiology Abd III slide 18 (Celiac Trunk: MIP)","Radiology Abd III slide 19 (Celiac Trunk: MIP)","Radiology Abd III slide 20 (Celiac Trunk: MIP)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":372,"f":"F1180"},{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":301,"f":"F1178"},{"t":"2019 review","n":"old Exam 2 review: abdomen part only","sl":326,"f":"F1228"},{"t":"? tutor review","n":"old Exam 2: abdomen part only","sl":222,"f":"F1243"}]}]},{"l":"ANAT-G7","u":166,"label":"Ileocolic artery: lowest right superior mesenteric branch","slides":[{"src":"sl/G012-39.jpg","cap":"Superior mesenteric angiogram: ileocolic artery, lowest right-sided branch","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Abdomen III, slide 39"},{"src":"sl/D042-154.jpg","cap":"Line diagram plus labeled superior mesenteric angiogram: ileocolic, right colic, middle colic","where":"2025 TA review, slide 154"}],"q":[{"id":"F3903-385-1","s":"The image following is an arteriogram of the superior mesenteric artery and its branches. The artery indicated by the arrow is the:","o":["A) Inferior mesenteric.","B) Middle colic.","C) Inferior pancreaticoduodenal.","D) First jejunal branch.","E) Ileocolic."],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Ileocolic","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Ileocolic.</b> On an SMA (superior mesenteric artery) arteriogram, the arrow marks the lowest branch from the right side of the SMA, running toward the cecum in the right lower quadrant: the ileocolic artery.<br>A. Inferior mesenteric: a separate aortic branch, not filled by an SMA injection.<br>B. Middle colic: the first right-sided branch, high up, to the transverse colon.<br>C. Inferior pancreaticoduodenal: the first small branch, near the SMA origin.<br>D. First jejunal branch: leaves the left side, high up.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F3903-385-1.jpg","in":"Slide 386: SMA digital-subtraction arteriogram (Figure F3); orange arrow points to the lowest right-sided branch running toward the right lower quadrant (ileocecal region) = ileocolic artery. Slide 367 is the same question without its image; merged here.","tg":["Radiology Abd III slide 39 (Ileocolic Artery)","Radiology Abd III slide 29 (Superior Mesenteric Artery DSA)","Radiology Abd III slide 37 (Ileocolic trunk)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 MT+Harlem clicker","n":"old Anatomy I Exam II (thorax+abdomen): abdomen part only","sl":385,"f":"F3903"},{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":125,"f":"F0008"}],"t":1},{"id":"D042-151-1","s":"20 year old female presents to the ER with abdominal pain and hypotension 2 days after an appendectomy. There are large amounts of blood in the peritoneal cavity due to injury to a vessel during the appendectomy. Which of the following vessels must be ligated to stop the bleeding?","o":["A. 1","B. 2","C. 3","D. 4","E. 5","F. 6","G. 7"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"1 – Ileocolic artery","a":"A","why":"<b>A. 1, the ileocolic artery.</b> The appendicular artery, tied during appendectomy, is a branch of the ileocolic artery. The ileocolic is the lowest branch from the right side of the SMA (superior mesenteric artery), heading to the cecum in the right lower quadrant.<br>B. 2 = right colic artery.<br>C. 3 = SMA trunk.<br>D. 4 = ileal branches.<br>E. 5 = arterial arcades.<br>F. 6 = jejunal branches.<br>G. 7 = middle colic artery.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/D042-151.jpg","in":"SMA angiogram with numbered leader lines 1-7; key on slide 153: 1 ileocolic a., 2 right colic a., 3 SMA, 4 ileal branch of SMA, 5 arterial arcade, 6 jejunal branch of SMA, 7 middle colic a.","tg":["Radiology Abd III slide 39 (Ileocolic Artery)","Radiology Abd III slide 37 (Ileocolic trunk)","Small & Large Intestines slide 61 (Cecum and Vermiform appendix)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI module TA review","sl":151,"f":"D042"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G7","u":167,"label":"Left colic artery supplies descending colon","slides":[{"src":"sl/G012-43.jpg","cap":"Inferior mesenteric angiogram: left colic artery marked","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Abdomen III, slide 43"},{"src":"sl/D042-158.jpg","cap":"Line diagram plus labeled inferior mesenteric angiogram: left colic, sigmoid, superior rectal","where":"2025 TA review, slide 158"}],"q":[{"id":"D042-155-1","s":"50 year old male presents to the ER with severe abdominal pain. It is determined that there is significant bleeding from the descending colon. Which of the following arteries is most likely the source of the bleeding?","o":["A. 1","B. 2","C. 3","D. 4","E. 5","F. 6"],"g":["F"],"k":["F"],"rv":false,"at":"6 – Left colic artery","a":"F","why":"<b>F. 6, the left colic artery.</b> The descending colon is supplied by the left colic artery, the first branch of the IMA (inferior mesenteric artery), running up and to the patient's left (image right).<br>A. 1 = catheter.<br>B. 2 = IMA trunk.<br>C. 3 = sigmoid arteries.<br>D. 4 = superior rectal artery.<br>E. 5 = marginal artery, the linking channel along the colon edge, fed by the colic arteries.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/D042-155.jpg","in":"IMA angiogram with numbered leader lines 1-6; key on slide 157: 1 catheter, 2 IMA, 3 sigmoid a., 4 superior rectal a., 5 marginal a., 6 left colic a.","tg":["Radiology Abd III slide 43 (Left Colic Artery)","Radiology Abd III slide 41 (Inferior Mesenteric Artery DSA)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"Fall 2025 GI module TA review","sl":155,"f":"D042"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G7","u":167,"label":"Marginal artery of Drummond along colon edge","slides":[{"src":"sl/G012-46.jpg","cap":"Inferior mesenteric angiogram: arrow on marginal artery of Drummond along colon","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Abdomen III, slide 46"},{"src":"sl/G012-45.jpg","cap":"Collaterals diagram: marginal artery along colon edge, arc of Riolan inside","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Abdomen III, slide 45"}],"q":[{"id":"F0008-154-1","s":"Identify the structure indicated by the arrow","o":["A. Left colic artery","B. Left colic vein","C. Marginal artery of Drummond","D. Arc of Riolan","E. Arc of Buehler"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Marginal artery of Drummond","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Marginal artery of Drummond.</b> On this IMA (inferior mesenteric artery) angiogram, the arrow is on a vessel running close along the edge of the colon near the splenic flexure. The marginal artery runs along the mesenteric border of the colon and links SMA (superior mesenteric artery) and IMA branches.<br>A. Left colic artery: runs from the IMA toward the colon, not along it.<br>B. Left colic vein: veins do not fill on an arterial injection.<br>D. Arc of Riolan: a central loop, nearer the mesentery root.<br>E. Arc of Buehler: links the celiac trunk and SMA.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0008-154.jpg","in":"IMA digital-subtraction angiogram; blue arrow points to a vessel running along the edge of the colon near the left colic (splenic) flexure, parallel to the bowel wall.","tg":["Radiology Abd III slide 46 (Marginal Artery of Drummond)","Small & Large Intestines slide 81 (Marginal Artery)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":154,"f":"F0008"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G7","u":168,"label":"Portal vein on venogram","slides":[{"src":"sl/G012-56.jpg","cap":"Portal venogram: arrows on the portal vein trunk and its split","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Abdomen III, slide 56"},{"src":"sl/G012-54.jpg","cap":"Diagram: superior mesenteric and splenic veins join to form the portal vein","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Abdomen III, slide 54"}],"q":[{"id":"F0008-156-1","s":"Name the structure indicated by the arrow:","o":["A. Superior mesenteric vein","B. Inferior vena cava","C. Hepatic vein","D. Portal vein","E. Splenic Vein"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Portal vein","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Portal vein.</b> The arrows point to the big trunk that enters the liver and splits into right and left branches. It forms behind the pancreatic neck, where the SMV (superior mesenteric vein) joins the splenic vein.<br>A. SMV: the vertical vein rising from below to join it.<br>B. IVC (inferior vena cava): behind the liver; it does not branch inside the liver.<br>C. Hepatic veins: drain out of the top of the liver into the IVC.<br>E. Splenic vein: runs horizontally from the patient's left.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0008-156.jpg","in":"Coronal contrast MR/CT venogram; two blue arrows point to the large trunk entering the liver and branching into right and left intrahepatic branches, fed from below by the confluence of the SMV and splenic vein.","tg":["Radiology Abd II slide 14 (Portal Vein)","Radiology Abd III slide 54 (Portal Vein)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Harlem clicker","n":"old Exam II clickers: abdomen part only","sl":156,"f":"F0008"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G7","u":168,"label":"Superior mesenteric vein on portal venogram","slides":[{"src":"sl/G012-58.jpg","cap":"Portal venogram: arrows on the superior mesenteric vein rising from below","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Abdomen III, slide 58"},{"src":"sl/G012-54.jpg","cap":"Diagram: superior mesenteric and splenic veins form the portal vein","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Abdomen III, slide 54"}],"q":[{"id":"F1180-368-1","s":"A 53 year-old-male notices several tortuous veins on his anterior abdominal wall around his umbilicus. His physician suspects hepatic portal hypertension and orders a portal venogram. Which structure is indicated by the red arrow?","o":["A. Hepatic veins","B. Inferior mesenteric vein","C. Right gastric vein","D. Superior mesenteric vein","E. Splenic vein"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Superior mesenteric vein","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Superior mesenteric vein.</b> On this portal venogram, the red arrow is on the large vein rising vertically from below. The SMV (superior mesenteric vein) joins the splenic vein behind the pancreatic neck to form the portal vein.<br>A. Hepatic veins: drain the top of the liver into the IVC (inferior vena cava).<br>B. Inferior mesenteric vein: smaller, usually drains into the splenic vein on the left.<br>C. Right gastric vein: small, drains straight into the portal vein.<br>E. Splenic vein: runs horizontally from the patient's left.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F1180-368.jpg","in":"Portal venogram (catheters from the right); the red arrow points to the large vein ascending from below into the portal confluence, below where the splenic vein joins from the left.","tg":["Radiology Abd III slide 58 (Superior Mesenteric Vein)","Radiology Abd II slide 14 (Portal Vein)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":368,"f":"F1180"},{"t":"2021 Harlem TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":297,"f":"F1178"},{"t":"2019 review","n":"old Exam 2 review: abdomen part only","sl":320,"f":"F1228"},{"t":"? tutor review","n":"old Exam 2: abdomen part only","sl":216,"f":"F1243"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"ANAT-G7","u":168,"label":"Right renal vein drains into inferior vena cava","slides":[{"src":"sl/G012-60.jpg","cap":"Coronal CT: arrows on renal veins draining into the inferior vena cava","where":"2026 lecture: Radiology of the Abdomen III, slide 60"}],"q":[{"id":"F3901-174-1","s":"*This is image is HARD to read!* Lets walk through it. It is two cuts in a coronal MRI. Identify A or V?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Right Renal VEIN (upper vessel entering the IVC) and Right Renal Artery","a":null,"why":"<b>Vein: the right renal vein.</b> On this coronal MRI (magnetic resonance imaging), the arrowed vessel leaves the right kidney's hilum and runs straight into the IVC (inferior vena cava), so it is a vein. The right renal artery comes from the aorta and passes behind the IVC.<br>Common mix-up: the right renal vein is short; the left renal vein is long and crosses in front of the aorta.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F3901-174.jpg","in":"Second coronal MRI cut; two pink arrows on the vessels running from the right kidney hilum medially (right renal vein to the IVC, right renal artery from the aorta)","tg":["Radiology Abd III slide 60 (Renal veins)","Radiology Abd III slide 5 (Renal Arteries)"],"src":[{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam II: abdomen part only","sl":174,"f":"F3901"}]}]}],"sum":{},"units":[{"l":"ANAT-G1","h":"Folding makes the gut tube","p":"<p>In week 4 the flat, three-layered embryo folds two ways, and its inner layer (endoderm) becomes the gut tube. <b>Head-to-tail folding</b> tucks both ends under. The head fold closes the front end into the <b>foregut</b>. The tail fold closes the back end into the hindgut, which ends in the cloaca. The midgut between them stays open to the yolk sac; it will make the far duodenum, the small intestine, the appendix and much of the colon. <b>Lateral folding</b> swings the side edges down to meet in the midline. This closes the endoderm into a tube inside the new body cavity and builds the front body wall. It also narrows the yolk sac link to a thin stalk, the vitelline duct. If lateral folding fails, the yolk sac does not pinch off, and the midgut and front wall stay open. Other structures form other ways: the neural tube from ectoderm folding on the back, the notochordal canal from the primitive node, and the allantois as a small pouch off the hindgut end. Midgut rotation comes weeks later.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G1","h":"Mesenteries hang the tube","p":"<p>Lateral folding also splits the side (lateral plate) mesoderm into two sheets. The somatic sheet lines the body wall. The visceral (splanchnic) sheet wraps the endoderm and makes the gut wall's muscle, connective tissue and peritoneum; the lining itself stays endoderm. The space between the sheets, the intraembryonic coelom, becomes the peritoneal cavity. Behind the gut, the visceral sheets of both sides meet as the <b>dorsal mesentery</b>: a double layer of peritoneum that hangs the whole tube, lower esophagus to rectum, from the back wall. Aortic branches, nerves and lymph vessels reach the gut between its layers. Its parts are named for what they hold: dorsal mesogastrium (stomach), mesoduodenum, mesentery proper (jejunum and ileum) and mesocolon. A <b>ventral mesentery</b> exists only along the foregut (lower esophagus, stomach, upper duodenum). It comes from the septum transversum, a block of mesoderm below the heart, not from somatic mesoderm. It becomes the lesser omentum and falciform ligament. Paraxial mesoderm makes the somites.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G1","h":"Intraperitoneal or retroperitoneal","p":"<p>Whether an organ keeps its mesentery decides how peritoneum covers it in the adult. An <b>intraperitoneal</b> organ keeps its mesentery, so it hangs into the peritoneal cavity, wrapped almost all around by visceral peritoneum: stomach, jejunum, ileum, appendix (on its mesoappendix), transverse and sigmoid colon, liver, spleen. A primary retroperitoneal organ never had a mesentery; it formed behind the peritoneum: kidneys, ureters, suprarenal glands, uterine cervix. In the lecture, plain \"retroperitoneal\" means this group. A <b>secondary retroperitoneal</b> organ began on a mesentery. As the gut rotated, it was pressed against the back wall, its mesentery fused there, and it lost its peritoneum behind. Now peritoneum covers only its front. The group: duodenum parts 2 to 4, the pancreas except its tail, and the ascending and descending colon. The <b>tail of the pancreas</b> stays intraperitoneal, in the splenorenal ligament. The pancreas is a foregut organ. Extraperitoneal is a general word for lying outside the peritoneal sac. A subperitoneal organ, like the bladder, lies below it, covered only on top.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G1","h":"Three gut parts, three arteries","p":"<p>The gut tube has three parts, and each has its own artery. The dorsal aorta runs behind the tube. Below the diaphragm it sends one unpaired branch to each part: the <b>celiac trunk</b> to the foregut, the superior mesenteric artery (SMA) to the midgut, and the inferior mesenteric artery (IMA) to the hindgut. The foregut ends in the <b>duodenum</b>. The pylorus and the first (upper) part of the duodenum are foregut, fed by the celiac trunk. The border is the opening of the bile duct, in the second part. Past it the duodenum is midgut, fed by the SMA. So the duodenum is the switch point twice: foregut to midgut, and celiac trunk to SMA. The midgut then runs to two-thirds of the way along the transverse colon, where the hindgut takes over. The bile duct opening also explains vomit color. A block above it, as at the pylorus, gives vomit without bile; a block below it gives green, bile-stained vomit. The allantois is not a gut part; it becomes the urachus.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G1","h":"What the foregut makes","p":"<p>Above the diaphragm the foregut makes the pharynx and the respiratory tract. Below, it makes the <b>esophagus</b> (all of it, upper part included), the stomach and the first part of the duodenum. It also makes the organs that bud off the duodenum at the bile duct opening: the hepatic diverticulum (liver bud) gives the liver, gallbladder and bile ducts, and two pancreatic buds give the pancreas. All are endoderm, fed by the celiac trunk. Duodenal atresia, the cause of bile-stained vomiting in a newborn, usually sits in that same stretch of duodenum, so the pancreas grows from the very piece of gut that is blocked. The <b>spleen</b> is a trap. It sits by the stomach and gets celiac blood, but it is not gut tube: it forms from mesoderm in the dorsal mesogastrium. The jejunum, ileum, cecum, appendix and ascending colon are midgut; the descending colon is hindgut. A duodenal ulcer in foregut territory lies just before the bile duct opening (the major papilla); the lecture puts the duodenum past that opening in the midgut.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G1","h":"Plug, reopen, and amniotic fluid","p":"<p>The tracheoesophageal septum splits the foregut into the trachea in front and the esophagus behind. The esophageal lining then grows so fast that the tube becomes an almost solid rod. It reopens by programmed cell death (apoptosis) by about week 8. This is <b>recanalization</b>. The duodenum goes through the same plug-and-reopen cycle. No reopening leaves a full block (atresia); partial reopening leaves a narrow lumen (stenosis). Either can cause <b>polyhydramnios</b>, too much amniotic fluid. Fetal urine adds amniotic fluid; fetal swallowing removes it, and the upper intestine absorbs it. A blocked or narrowed esophagus or duodenum stops this, so fluid builds up. More fetal urine (maternal diabetes) also raises it. A low block (imperforate anus, Hirschsprung disease) does not, because absorption happens higher up. <b>Oligohydramnios</b>, too little fluid, follows when no urine is made, as when both kidneys never form (bilateral renal agenesis). The embryologic cause to name for excess fluid is a gut tube that failed to recanalize. Surfactant and maternal hormones do not set the volume; pleuroperitoneal fold failure gives a diaphragmatic hernia.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G1","h":"Esophageal atresia and fistula types","p":"<p>If the tracheoesophageal septum deviates backward (posteriorly), the upper esophagus ends in a blind pouch: <b>esophageal atresia</b>. The esophagus normally reopens by week 8. Atresia usually comes with a <b>tracheoesophageal fistula (TEF)</b>, an abnormal channel to the trachea, and often with other defects, such as anal or duodenal atresia. The mother often had polyhydramnios. From the first feed the baby drools and chokes, coughs or turns blue (cyanosis). To sort the types, ask: does the lower esophagus connect to the trachea? If yes, breathing pushes air into the stomach, the belly swells, and acid can reflux into the lungs. The commonest type, about 85%, is a blind upper pouch plus a fistula from the trachea to the lower esophagus. An H-type fistula (esophagus open, side channel) and fistulas at both ends also put air in the stomach. Pure atresia (both ends blind) and an upper-pouch fistula leave it airless; the upper-pouch fistula sends feeds into the trachea, while pure atresia gives froth without cyanosis. Respiratory distress syndrome and diaphragmatic hernia (sunken belly) cause breathing trouble without drooling.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G1","h":"The stomach grows and turns","p":"<p>The stomach starts as a widening of the foregut. It widens front to back, and its <b>dorsal (back) wall grows faster</b> than its ventral (front) wall. Then the stomach <b>rotates 90° to the left</b> (clockwise seen from above) around its long axis. After the turn, the fast-growing back wall faces left and becomes the greater curvature. The slower front wall becomes the lesser curvature. The turn drags the mesenteries along. The stomach's dorsal mesentery, the <b>dorsal mesogastrium</b>, is pulled to the left. It keeps growing, hangs down from the greater curvature like an apron, and folds back up on itself as the greater omentum. A two-layer mesentery folded double gives four layers of peritoneum. The space trapped behind the stomach is the omental bursa (lesser sac); it is a space, not a mesentery. The lesser omentum (liver to stomach) comes from the ventral mesentery instead. The mesoduodenum mostly disappears as the duodenum fuses to the back wall, and the mesentery proper holds the jejunum and ileum.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G1","h":"The spleen forms behind the stomach","p":"<p>The <b>spleen</b> is not a gut-tube organ and is not endoderm. It starts in week 5, when a cluster of <b>mesenchymal cells</b> (loose embryonic connective tissue cells, from mesoderm) collects between the two layers of the dorsal mesogastrium. That spot is in the dorsal mesentery immediately behind (posterior to) the stomach. When the stomach rotates 90° to the left, it carries the dorsal mesogastrium, and the spleen inside it, to the left upper quadrant. So stomach rotation sets the spleen's position, not the reverse. The spleen is a major blood-forming (hematopoietic) organ in the fetal period, not in adolescence. Other sources make other things. Foregut endoderm makes the gut lining, liver and pancreas. Neural crest makes the gut's nerve cells. Intermediate mesoderm makes kidneys and gonads. Septum transversum mesoderm makes the liver's fibrous tissue and the diaphragm's central tendon. The ventral mesentery and falciform ligament lie in front, by the liver. The pancreas grows into the mesoduodenum, and duodenal rotation moves the pancreatic bud, not the spleen.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G1","h":"Pyloric stenosis","p":"<p><b>Hypertrophic pyloric stenosis</b> is one of the most common gut blockages in infancy. The pylorus, the stomach's muscular outlet, is foregut. Its circular muscle thickens and narrows the outlet. The 2026 lecture calls the cause unsettled: failed recanalization, or overgrowth (hypertrophy) of the sphincter muscle after development; textbooks describe muscle hypertrophy. The baby, usually a boy, is well at birth. At about 2 to 6 weeks he starts vomiting after feeds, more forcefully each time, until the vomiting is <b>projectile</b>. The block is above the bile duct opening, so the vomit has <b>no bile</b>. He gains little weight and gets dehydrated. The thick pylorus is felt as a small, firm, olive-shaped mass at the right costal margin. Surgery cures it. Sort the lookalikes by timing and bile. Esophageal atresia and tracheoesophageal fistula: drooling, choking and blue spells from the first feed. Duodenal atresia: bilious vomiting on day one. Annular pancreas: duodenal narrowing from birth, no olive. Biliary atresia: jaundice and pale stools. Esophageal stenosis: trouble swallowing, no mass.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G1","h":"Duodenal atresia and stenosis","p":"<p>The duodenal lining overgrows and plugs the lumen, then reopens by cell death; <b>recanalization</b> is finished by week 8. If it never reopens, an epithelial plug blocks the lumen: <b>duodenal atresia</b>. If it reopens only partly, the lumen stays narrow: duodenal stenosis. Most blocks lie just past the bile duct opening, so bile enters above them. The newborn vomits green, <b>bile-stained</b> fluid from day one, has a swollen upper belly and passes little meconium (first stool). An X-ray shows a \"double bubble\": air in the stomach and in the first part of the duodenum. Before birth, swallowed amniotic fluid could not pass on, so the mother had polyhydramnios. Duodenal atresia and stenosis go with Down syndrome (trisomy 21); clues are an older mother and thick nuchal translucency (extra fluid behind the fetal neck) on ultrasound. Other failures: a vitelline duct that does not regress, Meckel diverticulum; neural crest that does not migrate, Hirschsprung disease; pancreatic ducts that do not fuse, pancreas divisum; a cloacal membrane that does not rupture, imperforate anus. Rotation sets position, not the lumen.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G1","h":"Liver and gallbladder from one bud","p":"<p>The <b>hepatic diverticulum</b> sprouts from the ventral (front) wall of the lowest foregut, at the future duodenum. It grows into the <b>septum transversum</b>, a thick plate of mesoderm below the heart, and branches there. Its stalk narrows into the bile duct. A small outgrowth of the stalk forms the gallbladder and cystic duct. The rest, which the lecture calls the hepatic duct, forms the liver cells (parenchyma) and bile capillaries. If the bud fails to grow into the septum and branch, the gallbladder can be missing. Stomach rotation later moves the bud from ventral to lateral, which changes only its position. So the liver has two sources. Endoderm from the bud makes hepatocytes, bile ducts and the gallbladder. <b>Septum transversum mesoderm</b> makes the liver's fibrous tissue and its Kupffer cells, the macrophages in the sinusoids that break down old red blood cells. So Kupffer cells are mesoderm, not endoderm. Surface ectoderm makes skin and lens; neuroectoderm, brain and spinal cord; neural crest, ganglia and melanocytes. The ventral mesentery forms ligaments, not liver.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G1","h":"The liver splits the ventral mesentery","p":"<p>The ventral mesentery is the thin part of the septum transversum mesoderm between the foregut and the front body wall. The liver grows into it and splits it in two. The part from the liver to the front abdominal wall becomes the <b>falciform ligament</b>. The umbilical vein runs in its free edge and, after birth, closes into the ligamentum teres (round ligament of the liver). The part from the liver to the stomach and duodenum becomes the <b>lesser omentum</b>. It has two named parts: the hepatogastric ligament (to the stomach) and the hepatoduodenal ligament (to the duodenum), whose free edge carries the portal triad. Lecture rule: ventral mesentery = falciform ligament + lesser omentum. The greater omentum, gastrosplenic ligament, mesentery proper and mesocolons are all dorsal mesentery. The coronary ligament, around the liver's bare area, is not on the lecture's list. The septum transversum is the source of the ventral mesentery, not its product; most of it becomes the diaphragm's central tendon.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G1","h":"Two pancreatic buds, and a ring","p":"<p>The pancreas grows from a dorsal and a ventral bud of foregut <b>endoderm</b> on the duodenum; the ventral bud arises beside the bile duct, where the hepatic diverticulum starts. Endoderm makes all the gland's own cells: ducts, enzyme-making acini and hormone-making islet cells. Visceral mesoderm adds only the surrounding connective tissue. The stomach's rotation turns the duodenum into a C-shape to the right, and the turning duodenum swings the ventral bud, with the bile duct, around its back. The ventral bud ends up <b>posterior and inferior</b> to the dorsal bud, and the two fuse. The ventral bud forms the uncinate process and lower head; the dorsal bud forms the rest of the head, the neck, body and tail. In <b>annular pancreas</b> the ventral bud has two lobes. In rotation one lobe swings behind the duodenum and one in front. They meet as a ring that squeezes or blocks the second part of the duodenum; a ring above the bile duct opening gives vomit without bile. Failed duct fusion gives pancreas divisum; a missing dorsal bud means no body or tail.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G1","h":"What the midgut makes","p":"<p>The <b>midgut</b> starts in the duodenum just past the opening of the bile duct. It makes the rest of the duodenum, the jejunum, the ileum, the cecum, the appendix, the ascending colon and the first (proximal) two-thirds of the transverse colon. Every midgut organ is fed by the <b>superior mesenteric artery (SMA)</b>, the artery the midgut loop later turns around. The duodenum before the bile duct opening, including its first part, is foregut, fed by the celiac trunk. So a duodenal block just past that opening lies in midgut, with bile entering above it. The last third of the transverse colon is hindgut, fed by the inferior mesenteric artery (IMA), and so are the descending colon and rectum. The liver and pancreas are foregut, fed by the celiac trunk. The urinary bladder comes from the urogenital sinus, the front part of the cloaca. The proctodeum is the ectoderm pit that forms the lower third of the anal canal.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G1","h":"The midgut loop leaves and returns","p":"<p>The midgut grows into a U-shaped loop. Its tip is still tied to the yolk sac by the vitelline duct, and the superior mesenteric artery (SMA) runs down its middle. The lecture's rule: the <b>cranial (upper) limb</b> becomes small intestine and grows fast into coils; the caudal (lower) limb becomes large intestine and grows a cecal swelling. (Textbooks add that the lowest ileum also comes from the caudal limb.) The abdomen is too small, so at the start of week 6 the loop pushes through the umbilical ring into the cord. This is normal <b>physiologic umbilical herniation</b>; loops in the cord at week 8 are expected. Outside, the loop lengthens and turns 90° counterclockwise (seen from the front), with the SMA as its axis. In week 10 it returns (retraction) and turns another 180° counterclockwise, <b>270°</b> in all. Order: herniation, 90° turn, return, 180° turn. Clockwise turning is abnormal. Too much turning twists the mesentery around the SMA and kinks it, cutting off the loop's blood. The foregut and hindgut, with their own arteries, stay inside the abdomen.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G1","h":"Settling in, and rotation errors","p":"<p>The cecum returns last. It settles low on the right, and the appendix grows from it as a pouch (diverticulum), not straight off a midgut limb. Once the midgut is in place, the mesenteries of the ascending and descending colon <b>fuse with the back wall</b>. These colons had a mesentery and lost it, so they are secondary retroperitoneal, covered by peritoneum only in front. The appendix and sigmoid colon keep their mesenteries, the transverse mesocolon fuses with the greater omentum, and the mesentery proper attaches diagonally. The kidney never had a mesentery; the stomach, spleen and liver keep theirs. In <b>nonrotation</b> the loop makes only its first 90° turn and skips the last 180°. So the small intestine lies on the right, the colon on the left, and the cecum low in the midline above the pubis. In reversed rotation the first turn is clockwise, and the transverse colon ends up behind the superior mesenteric artery. In mixed rotation the cecum stops below the pylorus, held by bands. Volvulus is a twist that cuts off blood, not a layout.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G1","h":"Omphalocele: the loop never returns","p":"<p>Normally the herniated midgut loop is back in the abdomen by week 10. In an <b>omphalocele</b> it never returns: the hernia persists, and the bowel stays out at the umbilicus, in the base of the cord. It is covered by a thin, shiny sac of <b>amnion</b> (with peritoneum inside), with no skin and no muscle. On a photo it is a large midline bulge inside a see-through membrane, with the cord coming off it. A large one can hold liver as well as bowel. Omphalocele often comes with a trisomy (such as 13 or 18) and other defects; how many babies die depends on those other defects. The sac is what sets it apart from gastroschisis, where the bowel lies bare. Other errors give other defects: a leftover vitelline duct gives Meckel diverticulum, a thick pyloric muscle gives pyloric stenosis, an anal membrane that never ruptures gives imperforate anus, and abnormal turning gives malrotation. In an umbilical hernia the bowel did return and pushes out later, under skin.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G1","h":"Gastroschisis: the wall never closes","p":"<p><b>Gastroschisis</b> is a body-wall defect, not a failure of the loop to return. The front abdominal wall forms when the <b>lateral body folds</b> carry the somatic lateral plate mesoderm forward from both sides to fuse in the midline. If they fail to fuse, a full-thickness gap stays open beside the umbilicus, usually on the right. Bowel spills out through it and floats freely in the amniotic fluid. Nothing covers it: no amnion sac, no peritoneum, no skin. The cord itself is normal and lies next to the gap. Usually only bowel comes out; the liver stays inside. Unlike omphalocele, it is not usually linked to a trisomy. It is obvious at birth, and the lecture puts its death rate at 5 to 10%. Failure of the other folds gives other defects: the head fold, a heart outside the chest (ectopia cordis); the tail fold, bladder exstrophy. The neural folds make the neural tube, not the belly wall. In short: bare bowel beside the cord is gastroschisis; bowel in a sac at the umbilicus is omphalocele.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G1","h":"Umbilical hernia","p":"<p>An <b>umbilical hernia</b> is not a failure of gut development. The midgut loop came back normally. The weak spot is the umbilical ring, the opening in the wall where the cord attached, which has not closed firmly. When pressure in the belly rises, as when a baby cries, strains or coughs, omentum and small bowel push out through it, so the bulge comes and goes. It is covered by <b>subcutaneous tissue and skin</b>. That covering is the contrast with omphalocele, which is present at birth, covered only by amnion (no skin, no muscle), and made of bowel that never returned. Gastroschisis is bare bowel beside the umbilicus at birth. Adults can get umbilical hernias too, especially women and people with obesity; omphalocele is only congenital. Other hernias sit elsewhere: an epigastric hernia passes through the linea alba above the umbilicus, mostly in adults, and an indirect inguinal hernia passes through the deep inguinal ring toward the scrotum.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G1","h":"Meckel diverticulum","p":"<p>The tip of the midgut loop is tied to the yolk sac by the <b>vitelline duct</b> (yolk stalk). Normally the duct shrinks and disappears. If its gut end stays, it leaves a finger-like outgrowth of the ileum: <b>Meckel diverticulum</b>. Lecture numbers: about 2 inches long, about 2 feet from the ileocecal junction, in about 2 to 3% of people. As the small intestine returns, the diverticulum can stay anchored to the front abdominal wall. If it stays open to the umbilicus, it forms a fistula, an open channel that can carry stool to the umbilicus. The pouch often holds stomach lining. Its acid ulcerates the nearby ileum and causes <b>painless rectal bleeding</b>, bright to dark red, in a child. Other errors give other diseases: failed recanalization gives atresia; a midgut that never returns, omphalocele; a wall that never fuses, gastroschisis; a fetus that cannot swallow, polyhydramnios. Rathke's pouch makes the front pituitary, and the umbilical vein becomes the round ligament of the liver.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G1","h":"Drainage from the umbilicus","p":"<p>A vitelline duct remnant takes several forms: a free pouch on the ileum, a fibrous cord from the ileum to the umbilicus, a cyst along that cord, or a duct that stays open from end to end. The open duct is a <b>vitelline (omphaloenteric) fistula</b>. It joins the ileum to the umbilicus, so intestinal secretions, and at times stool, drain out of the umbilicus. The baby is weeks to months old. The umbilicus stays swollen, will not heal and may smell foul, and pressing a lump in the right lower quadrant can push out discharge. The lecture treats this fistula as one form of <b>Meckel diverticulum</b>, so stool at the umbilicus means Meckel. The lookalikes are present at birth and look different: omphalocele is bowel in a sac at the umbilicus, and gastroschisis is bare bowel beside it. Hirschsprung disease (megacolon) gives constipation and a swollen belly. Intestinal atresia or stenosis gives vomiting and bloating. Anal agenesis means no anus; stool may exit by a fistula to the perineum or urinary tract, never the umbilicus.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G1","h":"What the hindgut makes","p":"<p>The <b>hindgut</b> makes the last (distal) third of the transverse colon, the descending colon, the sigmoid colon, the rectum and the upper anal canal. Its end, the cloaca, also gives the urogenital sinus (bladder and urethra). All of it is fed by the <b>inferior mesenteric artery (IMA)</b>. The border with the midgut is two-thirds of the way along the transverse colon, near the splenic (left colic) flexure. So disease running from the rectum up to the splenic flexure stays inside hindgut, and a problem in the rectum points to other hindgut organs, such as the descending colon. Before that border nothing is hindgut. The duodenum is foregut above the bile duct opening and midgut below it. The jejunum, cecum, ascending colon and first two-thirds of the transverse colon are midgut. The liver is foregut, and the spleen is mesoderm, not gut tube. The allantois becomes the urachus. In Hirschsprung disease, a dilated colon sits above a narrow segment without nerve cells, usually the rectum and sigmoid colon, which are hindgut.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G1","h":"Arteries and nerves of each part","p":"<p>Each gut part has its own artery and nerves. T, L and S mean thoracic, lumbar and sacral spinal cord segments. Foregut: celiac trunk; sympathetics in the greater splanchnic nerve (T5 to T9) to the celiac ganglion. Midgut: superior mesenteric artery (SMA); sympathetics classically in the <b>lesser splanchnic nerve</b> (T10 to T11) to the superior mesenteric ganglion. Hindgut: inferior mesenteric artery (IMA); lumbar splanchnic nerves (L1 to L2) to the inferior mesenteric ganglion. Parasympathetics are the vagus (foregut and midgut) and the pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2 to S4, hindgut). The 2026 small-intestine lecture adds that jejunal and ileal sympathetics come from T8 to T10 through the greater, lesser and least splanchnic nerves, so the greater splanchnic counts too; the lesser is the best single answer. At the <b>midgut–hindgut junction</b>, in the last third of the transverse colon, the middle colic branch of the SMA meets the left colic branch of the IMA. A tumor there draws on both arteries; the distal third alone is IMA. The internal iliac artery supplies the pelvis.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G1","h":"The urorectal septum divides the cloaca","p":"<p>In week 4, tail folding bends the end of the hindgut into a pouch, the <b>cloaca</b>, shared by the gut and the urinary tract. The allantois runs from its front into the cord and later becomes the urachus. A cloacal membrane closes it below. A wedge of mesoderm, the <b>urorectal septum</b>, grows down between the allantois and the hindgut. It divides the cloaca into the urogenital sinus in front (bladder, urethra) and the anorectal canal behind (rectum, upper anal canal). It also splits the cloacal membrane into a urogenital membrane in front and an anal membrane behind. If the septum does not fully divide the cloaca, the rectum stays joined to the front part. The result is a <b>fistula</b>: rectourethral (urorectal) in boys; rectovaginal or rectovestibular in girls, with meconium leaving through the vagina or its vestibule and no normal anus. An anal membrane that fails to rupture, or a faulty proctodeum, gives a closed or narrow anus, not a fistula; the anus opens by rupture, not recanalization. Neural crest failure gives Hirschsprung disease.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G1","h":"Opening the anus","p":"<p>After the urorectal septum divides the cloacal membrane, its back part, the <b>anal membrane</b>, must rupture at the end of week 7 or start of week 8. Surface ectoderm sinks in as a pit, the proctodeum, to meet the endoderm of the rectum and complete the canal. The 2026 lecture simply says the cloacal membrane must rupture; textbooks name the ruptured part the anal membrane. Both names mean the same event. The septum divides and the proctodeum sinks in; neither ruptures. If the membrane stays intact, ectoderm and endoderm cannot join: <b>imperforate anus</b>. The newborn feeds normally and does not vomit, but passes no meconium at all, and the belly slowly swells. A finger exam finds no anal opening. Compare: anal stenosis lets some meconium out; a urorectal fistula sends meconium out through the urethra; Hirschsprung disease has a normal anus. Incomplete division of the cloaca causes a fistula, not a closed anus. The hindgut does not rotate. Midgut rotation, esophageal recanalization and the vitelline duct are unrelated.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G1","h":"Two origins of the anal canal","p":"<p>The anal canal is built from two sources that meet at the <b>pectinate line</b>. The upper two-thirds comes from the cloaca, which is hindgut <b>endoderm</b>. Like the rest of the gut, it is lined by columnar epithelium. The lower third comes from the proctodeum, the pit of surface <b>ectoderm</b> that sank in when the membrane ruptured. It is lined by stratified squamous epithelium. So any columnar lining above the pectinate line is endoderm; only the zone below the line is ectoderm. Mesoderm makes the muscle and connective tissue of the wall, not the lining. The allantois becomes the urachus. Some other true statements from this lecture often appear next to this one in true-or-false lists: the stomach widens front to back and turns 90° to the left; the ventral pancreatic bud makes the uncinate process and lower head; the ventral mesentery becomes the falciform ligament and lesser omentum; and the midgut loop turns 270° in all.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G1","h":"Hemorrhoids: painless above, painful below","p":"<p>The two halves of the anal canal have different nerves, vessels and lymph. Above the pectinate line (hindgut endoderm), the nerves are <b>visceral (autonomic)</b>: they sense stretch, not sharp pain. The artery is the superior rectal, from the inferior mesenteric artery (IMA). Its vein drains to the portal system, and lymph goes to the internal iliac nodes. Below the line (proctodeum ectoderm), the nerves are <b>somatic</b>, from the inferior rectal nerve, so pain is sharp, as in skin. The artery is the inferior rectal, from the internal pudendal. Its vein drains to the caval (systemic) system, and lymph goes to the superficial inguinal nodes. Hemorrhoids follow the line. Internal hemorrhoids lie above it, in endoderm, and give painless bright red bleeding after straining. They stay painless unless they prolapse or clot. External hemorrhoids lie below it, in ectoderm, and hurt. The anal canal is hindgut, never midgut. Mesoderm makes muscle and vessels, not the lining. The two venous systems meet here (a portocaval anastomosis), and cancers spread along each zone's lymph.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G1","h":"Hirschsprung disease","p":"<p>The gut wall has its own nerve networks: the myenteric (Auerbach) plexus between the muscle layers and the submucosal (Meissner) plexus under the lining. Their nerve cells come from <b>neural crest</b> cells that migrate down the gut from head to tail. If migration stops short, the last segment of colon, usually rectum and sigmoid, has no ganglion cells: <b>Hirschsprung disease</b> (aganglionic megacolon). The missing cells are the <b>postganglionic parasympathetic cell bodies</b>. Preganglionic fibers (vagus, pelvic splanchnics) and sympathetic fibers, whose postganglionic cells sit in ganglia outside the gut wall, are still there. Without the local neurons the segment cannot relax, the internal anal sphincter included, so stool backs up and the colon above dilates. The newborn feeds normally but passes no meconium. The anus, anal canal and rectum look normal, but the canal is tight, and pulling out the examining finger releases a gush of gas and stool. Imperforate anus, anal agenesis and rectal atresia are structural blocks instead. The sphincter's smooth muscle is mesoderm; endoderm makes the lining.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G2","h":"Four quadrants from two lines","p":"<p>Doctors map the front of the abdomen in two ways. The simpler map has <b>four quadrants</b> and uses only two lines. The <b>median (midsagittal) plane</b> runs straight down the midline and splits right from left. The <b>transumbilical plane</b> runs across through the umbilicus and splits upper from lower. So the right lower quadrant (RLQ) is the patient's right side below the umbilicus, and the left lower quadrant (LLQ) is the left side below it. Above the umbilicus lie the right upper and left upper quadrants. Right and left always mean the patient's sides. The finer map has nine regions and uses other lines: two vertical midclavicular lines, the subcostal plane (below the 10th costal cartilage) and the transtubercular, or intertubercular, plane (through the iliac tubercles). Some doctors swap in the transpyloric and interspinous planes. None of these nine-region lines, and no midaxillary or midsternal line, marks a quadrant.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G2","h":"Which organs sit in each quadrant","p":"<p>The right upper quadrant (RUQ) holds the right lobe of the liver, the <b>gallbladder</b>, the pylorus, the head of the pancreas and the right kidney. The left upper quadrant (LUQ) holds most of the <b>stomach</b>, the spleen, the left lobe of the liver, the body and tail of the pancreas and the left kidney. The spleen, behind ribs 9–11, is the organ most often torn by a blunt blow; its bleeding causes pallor and low blood pressure. The right lower quadrant (RLQ) holds the cecum and appendix. The left lower quadrant (LLQ) holds the sigmoid colon, the left ovary and uterine tube, and in men the abdominal part of the left <b>spermatic cord</b> heading for the groin. The bladder lies low in the midline. An inflamed gallbladder irritates the diaphragm. Its phrenic nerve shares spinal levels with the shoulder skin, so the pain reaches the right shoulder tip. A horizontal line drawn too high can mislabel only an upper organ just above the true line, such as the gallbladder at the lower edge of the right ribs.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G2","h":"Nine regions and where gut pain is felt","p":"<p>The <b>nine regions</b> form a grid. Top row, from the patient's right: right hypochondrium (under the right ribs), epigastric, left hypochondrium. Middle row: right lateral (flank or lumbar), umbilical, left lateral. Bottom row: right inguinal (groin), pubic (hypogastric), left inguinal. Gut pain is first felt in the midline, by gut segment. <b>Foregut</b> organs (stomach, first part of the duodenum, liver, gallbladder, pancreas) refer pain to the epigastric region. Midgut pain is felt around the umbilicus, hindgut pain in the pubic region. So a peptic ulcer is tender in the epigastrium, and stomach pain can also be felt in the middle of the upper back, between the shoulder blades. Other spots: gallbladder in the right hypochondrium (not the right lateral region below it), appendicitis moving from the umbilicus to the right inguinal region, a big spleen in the left hypochondrium, a kidney stone in the flank spreading to the groin. Surgeons cut over the region that gives the best access. The stomach lies mainly in the epigastric region, so even an ulcer on its back wall is reached there.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G2","h":"The transpyloric plane at L1","p":"<p>The <b>transpyloric plane</b> is a horizontal line halfway between the top of the manubrium (the jugular notch) and the pubic symphysis. It lies at the first lumbar vertebra (L1). Many structures sit on it: the pylorus (when the patient lies down), the gallbladder fundus, the neck of the pancreas, the origins of the superior mesenteric artery (SMA) and the hepatic portal vein, the root of the transverse mesocolon, the duodenojejunal junction, the hila of the kidneys, the hilum of the spleen and the 9th costal cartilages. Textbooks add the end of the spinal cord. The <b>celiac trunk</b> is not on it; it leaves the aorta one level higher, at the twelfth thoracic vertebra (T12). The gallbladder fundus touches the front wall where the lateral edge of rectus abdominis (the linea semilunaris) meets the right 9th costal cartilage. Pain at that spot when the patient breathes in is <b>Murphy sign</b> of an inflamed gallbladder. The duodenum lies deeper, near the midline; the kidney is felt in the flank and back; the transverse colon hangs lower.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G2","h":"Layers of the wall, outside to inside","p":"<p>The anterior abdominal wall is a stack of layers. Under the skin lies the subcutaneous tissue, also called superficial fascia. In the lower wall it has two layers. <b>Camper fascia</b> is the outer, fatty layer. <b>Scarpa fascia</b> is the deeper, membranous layer, distinct mainly below the umbilicus. Both lie between the skin and the muscles, far outside the peritoneum. In the perineum the membranous layer takes another name, Colles fascia. Next come the three flat muscles, each in a thin investing fascia: external oblique, then internal oblique, then transversus abdominis. Deep to all three lies the <b>transversalis fascia</b>, the innermost fascia of the wall. Then come extraperitoneal fat and the parietal peritoneum. Near the midline the three muscles have turned into flat tendons (aponeuroses) that form the rectus sheath, but the layers keep the same order. The rectus sheath is built from those aponeuroses, not from Camper or Scarpa fascia.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G2","h":"The flat muscles and what they do","p":"<p>Three flat muscles wrap the side of the abdomen. They continue the three layers of intercostal muscles. The <b>external oblique</b> is the outermost and largest. It runs from ribs 5–12 to the linea alba, the pubic tubercle and the iliac crest. Its fibers run down and toward the midline (inferomedially), like hands slid into front pockets. The slide's word is \"posteroinferiorly\", but its hands-in-pockets picture shows this same slope. Its aponeurosis joins the linea alba, and its free lower edge folds back on itself as the inguinal ligament. The internal oblique runs at right angles to it. The transversus abdominis runs horizontally. The obliques flex and rotate the trunk. Together the muscles compress and support the viscera and raise <b>intra-abdominal pressure</b> for coughing, forced breathing out, defecation, childbirth and the Valsalva maneuver. When braced, they make the abdomen a stiff cylinder that steadies the spine for lifting. Rectus abdominis also controls pelvic tilt. Other muscles flex the hip (iliopsoas) and extend the trunk (erector spinae).</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G2","h":"Rectus abdominis, its sheath and the arcuate line","p":"<p><b>Rectus abdominis</b> is the vertical \"six-pack\" muscle, from the pubic symphysis to the xiphoid process and costal cartilages 5–7. The two recti meet at a midline seam, the linea alba. The lateral edge of each is the linea semilunaris. The muscle sits in the <b>rectus sheath</b>, a sleeve built from the aponeuroses of the three flat muscles plus transversalis fascia. The sheath covers the muscle in front and behind. But its back layer stops a little below the umbilicus, about a third of the way down to the pubis. Its curved lower edge is the <b>arcuate line</b>. Below it, all three aponeuroses pass in front of the muscle. So there the back of rectus lies directly on transversalis fascia, then extraperitoneal fat and peritoneum. A low transverse (suprapubic) cut, as in a Caesarean section, never meets a posterior sheath. Look-alike landmarks: the intercristal line joins the tops of the iliac crests; the pectineal line is a ridge on the pubic bone; the conjoint tendon (falx inguinalis) inserts on the pubic crest.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G2","h":"Sheath layers above and below the arcuate line","p":"<p>Above the arcuate line, the internal oblique aponeurosis <b>splits</b> into two leaflets that pass in front of and behind rectus abdominis. The anterior sheath there is the external oblique aponeurosis plus the anterior leaflet of the internal oblique. The posterior sheath is the posterior leaflet of the internal oblique plus the transversus abdominis aponeurosis, with transversalis fascia behind them. So the muscle itself touches only the two internal oblique leaflets, and the layer lying directly on its front is the anterior leaflet. Below the arcuate line, all three aponeuroses run in front of the muscle: external oblique outermost, <b>transversus abdominis</b> deepest. There is no posterior sheath. Behind the muscle lie the inferior epigastric vessels, resting on the transversalis fascia. Transversalis fascia is a single sheet; it has no anterior or posterior division.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G2","h":"Where the wall's arteries come from","p":"<p>The wall gets blood from above, from below and from the sides. From above, the internal thoracic artery, a branch of the subclavian artery, runs down behind the costal cartilages. At the sixth intercostal space it splits into the musculophrenic artery, along the costal margin, and the <b>superior epigastric artery</b>. The superior epigastric enters the rectus sheath behind the upper rectus abdominis. It and its vein supply and drain the wall above the umbilicus. From below, the external iliac artery gives the inferior epigastric artery, behind the lower rectus, and the deep circumflex iliac artery, deep along the iliac crest. The femoral artery gives two small arteries in the subcutaneous fat: the superficial epigastric, to the lower wall, and the <b>superficial circumflex iliac</b>, toward the anterior superior iliac spine (ASIS). From the sides, the lower posterior intercostal and subcostal arteries come from the aorta. The lateral thoracic artery, from the axillary artery, feeds the side of the chest and the breast, not the abdominal wall.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G2","h":"The inferior epigastric artery","p":"<p>The <b>inferior epigastric artery</b> branches from the external iliac artery just above the inguinal ligament. It climbs the inner surface of the wall just medial to the deep inguinal ring and raises a ridge of peritoneum, the lateral umbilical fold. Below the arcuate line it runs up directly behind rectus abdominis, lying on (anterior to) the transversalis fascia. The transversus abdominis aponeurosis passes in front of the muscle there, so the artery is also posterior to that aponeurosis. At the <b>arcuate line</b> it passes into the rectus sheath, and the lateral umbilical fold ends. Because it runs vertically behind the lower rectus, any cut across the lower muscle, such as taking a suprapubic rectus graft or a transverse cut at the arcuate line, crosses it. Neighbors lie elsewhere: the superficial epigastric artery in the subcutaneous fat, the musculophrenic along the ribs, the deep circumflex iliac out along the iliac crest, the thoracoepigastric vein in the fat of the side wall, the ilioinguinal nerve lower, in the inguinal canal. Wall nerves run roughly sideways, parallel to a transverse cut.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G2","h":"Two epigastric arteries meet in the sheath","p":"<p>Inside the rectus sheath, on the back of rectus abdominis, the superior and inferior epigastric arteries meet and join. Such a link between two arteries is an <b>anastomosis</b>. The superior epigastric is the end branch of the internal thoracic artery, which comes from the subclavian. The inferior epigastric comes from the external iliac. The change in the posterior sheath at the arcuate line, below the umbilicus, is where the inferior artery enters the sheath to reach its partner. This matters in heart surgery. Surgeons often cut the internal thoracic artery near the lower sternum and use it to bypass a blocked coronary artery. The superior epigastric then loses its source, and rectus abdominis lives on blood flowing up from the <b>inferior epigastric</b>. The superficial epigastric artery runs in the subcutaneous fat, not in the muscle, so it is not part of this link. The circumflex iliac and musculophrenic arteries supply the side wall.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G2","h":"Veins and lymph split at the umbilicus","p":"<p>Veins and lymph vessels follow the arteries. In the skin and fat of the wall, both drain away from a horizontal line at the <b>umbilicus</b>. Above it they run up toward the armpit: veins reach the axillary vein, and lymph goes to the <b>axillary lymph nodes</b>. Below it they run down toward the groin: veins reach the femoral vein, and lymph goes to the superficial inguinal nodes. Skin lymph passes through these regional nodes first, not straight into the thoracic duct or cisterna chyli. Other nodes have other territories: internal iliac nodes take pelvic organs, para-aortic nodes the testis, deep inguinal nodes the glans and deep thigh. A long superficial vein, the <b>thoracoepigastric vein</b>, runs up the side of the trunk. It links the superficial epigastric vein (draining to the femoral vein) with the lateral thoracic vein (draining to the axillary vein). When the inferior vena cava (IVC) is blocked, blood detours through it and it swells. The superior and inferior epigastric veins, by contrast, lie deep inside the rectus sheath.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G2","h":"Nerves and dermatomes of the wall","p":"<p>The wall is supplied by the front branches (anterior rami) of spinal nerves: the thoracoabdominal nerves (lower intercostals, seventh to eleventh thoracic, T7–T11), the subcostal nerve (twelfth thoracic, T12) and the iliohypogastric and ilioinguinal nerves (first lumbar, L1). In these labels T means thoracic, L lumbar, and the number the spinal level. The lecture gives the whole range as T5–L1. They carry both motor and sensory fibers and are not sympathetic trunk branches. Like intercostal nerves in the chest, they run <b>between internal oblique and transversus abdominis</b>, outside the peritoneum. A <b>dermatome</b> is the strip of skin supplied by one spinal nerve. Landmarks: T7 at the xiphoid process (lecture; some textbooks say T6), <b>T10 at the umbilicus</b>, and L1 at the groin and the skin just above the pubis. T12 lies just below the umbilicus; T5 is on the chest. Because the L1 nerves run between the oblique muscles, strain there can send pain into the groin. The femoral nerve (L2–L4) and the lateral femoral cutaneous nerve belong to the thigh.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G2","h":"The inguinal ligament","p":"<p>The <b>inguinal ligament</b> is not a separate band. It is the free lower edge of the external oblique aponeurosis, folded back on itself. It runs from the <b>anterior superior iliac spine (ASIS)</b> to the pubic tubercle, two bony points you can feel through the skin. The anterior inferior iliac spine lies lower and gives origin to rectus femoris. The pubic symphysis is the midline joint, medial to the tubercle. At its medial end, fibers fan back to the pectineal line of the pubis as the lacunar ligament. On its deep side, a thickening of transversalis fascia, the iliopubic tract, reinforces it. The ligament forms most of the floor of the inguinal canal. Beneath it, the external iliac artery and vein pass into the thigh and become the femoral artery and vein; the femoral nerve also passes under it. Structures bound for the testis run above the ligament, through the canal. The obturator nerve leaves the pelvis through its own obturator canal.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G2","h":"How the canal forms, and its two rings","p":"<p>The <b>inguinal canal</b> is the oblique tunnel, about 1.5 inches long, that the testis made as it pushed down through every layer of the wall. The ovary, by contrast, attaches to the uterus and stays inside. The canal starts at the <b>deep (internal) ring</b>, a round opening in the transversalis fascia, the innermost layer. Peritoneum covers it but has no hole. The ring lies lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels, about half an inch above the mid-inguinal point, where the midclavicular line crosses the inguinal ligament (textbooks: above the midpoint of the ligament). Indirect hernias start here, so pressure on this spot holds them in. The canal ends at the <b>superficial (external) ring</b>, a triangular gap between the medial and lateral crura of the external oblique aponeurosis, just above and lateral to the pubic tubercle. The internal oblique and transversus abdominis arch over the canal and form neither ring; Scarpa fascia has no ring. Below the ligament, medial to the femoral vein and beside the lacunar ligament, lies the femoral ring.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G2","h":"Walls of the inguinal canal","p":"<p>Because the canal runs obliquely, its walls change along its length. The anterior wall is the external oblique aponeurosis, helped laterally by internal oblique fibers. The floor is the inguinal ligament, with the lacunar ligament medially. The roof is the arching lower fibers of the internal oblique and transversus abdominis. The <b>posterior wall</b> is transversalis fascia. Medially it is reinforced by the <b>conjoint tendon</b> (falx inguinalis): the aponeuroses of the internal oblique and transversus abdominis fused together, inserting on the pubic crest. The pectineal ligament lies lower, on the pubic bone. The back wall gets its strength from the transversus abdominis aponeurosis lying on the transversalis fascia. When that backing weakens with age and strain, abdominal contents can push straight forward through the back wall without using the deep ring. Faults at the deep ring itself, such as peritoneum that never closed there, give a different kind of hernia. Extraperitoneal fat is padding, not a supporting layer.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G2","h":"What passes through the canal","p":"<p>In males the canal carries the <b>spermatic cord</b>. It enters at the deep ring, leaves at the superficial ring and descends into the scrotum. Its parts include the ductus deferens, the testicular artery and the pampiniform plexus of veins; any one of these is only part of the content. In females the canal carries the <b>round ligament of the uterus</b>. It also enters at the deep ring and ends in the labium majus. A small nerve, the genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve, may run beside it. In both sexes the ilioinguinal nerve lies in the canal too, but it enters from the side through the internal oblique, not through the deep ring. Nearby structures that are not contents: the iliohypogastric nerve runs above the canal; the inferior epigastric artery lies just medial to the deep ring, outside it; the ovarian ligament and the suspensory ligament of the ovary stay in the pelvis; the urachus and the obliterated umbilical arteries lie on the inner wall near the midline. The femoral and obturator nerves leave by other routes.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G2","h":"Folds and fossae on the inner wall","p":"<p>Seen from inside, the lower front wall shows five ridges of peritoneum running up to the umbilicus. Each covers a cord or vessel. The single <b>median umbilical fold</b> covers the urachus, the closed-off remnant of the allantois, running from the top of the bladder. The two medial umbilical folds cover the obliterated umbilical arteries. The two lateral umbilical folds cover the inferior epigastric vessels, which stay open. The umbilical vein ends differently, as the round ligament of the liver; the ductus venosus becomes the ligamentum venosum. Between the folds lie shallow pits. The supravesical fossa lies between the median and medial folds. The <b>medial inguinal fossa</b> lies between the medial and lateral folds; direct hernias push through it. The lateral inguinal fossa lies lateral to the lateral fold and holds the deep ring. So the inferior epigastric vessels hug the deep ring's medial edge. A hernia that skips the deep ring has them on its lateral side and rectus abdominis on its medial side.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G2","h":"Hesselbach's triangle","p":"<p>The <b>inguinal (Hesselbach) triangle</b> is the weakest part of the lower front wall. It sits in the medial inguinal fossa and has three borders. Medially: the lateral edge of rectus abdominis. Laterally: the <b>inferior epigastric vessels</b>. Inferiorly: the inguinal ligament. Its floor is the back wall of the canal, transversalis fascia backed medially by the conjoint tendon. Direct inguinal hernias push through this triangle, so they lie medial to the inferior epigastric vessels. There is no \"inferior iliac\" artery. Nearby landmarks are not borders. The femoral vein, femoral artery, lacunar ligament and pectineal line belong to the femoral region below the inguinal ligament. The superior epigastric vessels are in the upper wall. The deep and superficial rings are the two ends of the canal. The spermatic cord runs through the canal, and the internal oblique arches over it as its roof.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G2","h":"Direct hernias","p":"<p>A hernia is tissue, often a loop of bowel, pushing out through a weak spot. The inferior epigastric vessels divide groin hernias into two kinds. A <b>direct inguinal hernia</b> comes straight forward through Hesselbach's triangle, medial to these vessels, and does not use the deep ring. It pushes peritoneum and transversalis fascia ahead of it, so those are its only coverings. It stays outside the spermatic cord, so it rarely reaches the scrotum. It is <b>acquired</b>: the back wall of the canal weakens with age and strain, so it is typical of older men who lift or strain. It may be one-sided or bilateral. The triangle lies just above the medial part of the inguinal ligament, near the pubis. An indirect hernia is the opposite: lateral, through the deep ring, inside the cord with all three cord coats, and congenital, from an open peritoneal pouch (the processus vaginalis). A femoral hernia lies below the inguinal ligament. Hernias at the linea alba are epigastric or umbilical. When a lump appears does not settle its type; its route does.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G2","h":"Recognizing a direct hernia at the bedside","p":"<p>The usual patient with a <b>direct hernia</b> is a man past middle age. The bulge grew slowly over months or appeared after heavy lifting, and it grows when he stands, coughs or strains. It sits medially, above the inguinal ligament, and does not drop into the scrotum. An indirect hernia is more typical of younger patients and often reaches the scrotum. To examine, push a finger up through the scrotal skin into the superficial ring and ask him to cough. An indirect hernia comes down the canal from the deep ring and taps the <b>fingertip</b>. A direct hernia pushes straight forward through the back wall, so it taps the side of the finger, medially, and the finger can seem to pass straight back into the abdomen. Look-alikes: a femoral hernia bulges below the inguinal ligament, usually in women; a swollen lymph node is firm and has no cough impulse; torsion of the testis causes sudden severe pain; epididymitis is a tender swelling behind the testis; a hydrocele is fluid inside the scrotum; testicular cancer is a hard scrotal mass.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G2","h":"Indirect hernias","p":"<p>An <b>indirect inguinal hernia</b> enters the deep inguinal ring. The ring lies in the lateral inguinal fossa, lateral to the lateral umbilical fold and the inferior epigastric vessels inside it. So an indirect hernia always lies <b>lateral</b> to those vessels. From the ring it runs inside the spermatic cord along the canal and out the superficial ring. If large, it reaches the scrotum or labium majus. Its entrance and neck are at the deep ring; the superficial ring is only its exit. It is common in children but occurs at any age. Because it travels in the cord, repair can injure the genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve, which runs in the cord and drives the cremaster muscle. Other hernias lie elsewhere: direct ones medial to the vessels, femoral ones below the inguinal ligament and medial to the femoral vein, umbilical ones at the navel, incisional ones through a scar, ventral ones through the front wall away from the groin, lumbar ones in the back wall. The femoral, lateral femoral cutaneous and obturator nerves lie far from its path.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G2","h":"Why indirect hernias are congenital","p":"<p>As the testis descends, a finger of peritoneum, the <b>processus vaginalis</b>, goes ahead of it through the deep ring and down to the scrotum. Normally its upper part seals off, and only its lower end stays, as the tunica vaginalis around the testis. If the pouch stays open (patent), the deep ring opens straight into it and bowel can slide down it: a congenital indirect hernia. So groin hernias in babies are almost always indirect. They are soft and push back into the abdomen. If only fluid fills the open pouch, the result is a hydrocele, which lets light through; it is a sister condition, not the cause. A weak transversalis fascia gives direct hernias in adults. An indirect hernia's neck sits at the deep ring. Because it runs inside the cord, it is covered by the cord's <b>three coats</b>: external spermatic fascia, cremaster muscle and internal spermatic fascia. It slips through the hole already present in the transversalis fascia; pushing peritoneum and transversalis fascia out ahead of the bowel is what a direct hernia does.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G2","h":"Femoral and obturator hernias","p":"<p>Some hernias leave the abdomen below the inguinal ligament. A <b>femoral hernia</b> enters the femoral ring, medial to the femoral vein, and comes out in the thigh. Its lump lies below the inguinal ligament, inferior and lateral to the pubic tubercle; inguinal hernias lie above the ligament, superior and medial to the tubercle. Women get femoral hernias more often than men do, because of their wider pelvis. The ring is narrow and its medial edge, the lacunar ligament, is sharp, so the bowel is often trapped, causing pain and vomiting. Still, the most common hernia in women, as in men, is the <b>indirect inguinal hernia</b>. An <b>obturator hernia</b> pushes through the obturator canal and presses on the obturator nerve. That gives sharp pain down the inner thigh, worse when the hip is extended and turned inward. It is typical of thin elderly women after weight loss. There is no groin lump; it shows up as small bowel obstruction. Umbilical hernias bulge at the navel, mostly in infants, women and obese adults.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G2","h":"What the spermatic cord carries","p":"<p>The testis forms on the back wall of the abdomen and drags its vessels and nerves with it as it descends. At the deep ring the ductus deferens joins them, and the bundle becomes the <b>spermatic cord</b>. The cord holds the ductus (vas) deferens; three arteries (the testicular artery from the aorta, the cremasteric artery from the inferior epigastric, and the small artery of the ductus); the <b>pampiniform plexus</b> of veins; lymph vessels; the genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve; sympathetic fibers; and the remnant of the processus vaginalis. Two nerves nearby stay out. The femoral branch of the genitofemoral nerve passes under the inguinal ligament to the skin of the upper front thigh. The ilioinguinal nerve lies in the canal but outside the cord. The cord has three coats, from outside in: external spermatic fascia, cremaster muscle and fascia, internal spermatic fascia. Do not confuse them with the scrotal layers (dartos, tunica vaginalis), the testis capsule (tunica albuginea) or the epididymis, which sits on the testis.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G2","h":"Each cord coat comes from a wall layer","p":"<p>On its way down, the testis took a sleeve from each wall layer it crossed, and each coat matches the layer at the same depth. The deep ring is a hole in the <b>transversalis fascia</b>, so the cord picks up its innermost coat there: the internal spermatic fascia. The lowest fibers of the <b>internal oblique</b> are pulled down around the cord as the cremaster muscle and its fascia. At the superficial ring, the external oblique aponeurosis adds the outer coat, the external spermatic fascia. The transversus abdominis gives no coat, because its lower fibers arch above the deep ring. Below the canal the scrotum adds layers from the subcutaneous tissue: Scarpa fascia gives the dartos muscle and fascia, while fatty Camper fascia does not enter (there is no fat in the scrotum). Peritoneum gives the tunica vaginalis. A newborn's scrotal hernia is indirect. It passes the deep ring, so transversalis fascia is the deepest wall layer it crosses, with only peritoneum deeper. Rectus abdominis lies medial to the canal and is not involved.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G2","h":"Layers in order, and the dartos","p":"<p>The cord coats keep the wall's outside-to-inside order: external oblique, internal oblique, transversalis fascia. So from superficial to deep the cord has the external spermatic fascia, then the cremaster muscle and fascia (with the genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve), then the internal spermatic fascia, and inside them the vessels, duct and nerves. The cremaster comes from the middle muscle, so it sits in the middle. The scrotal wall adds layers on the outside. Right under the skin lies the <b>dartos muscle</b>, a sheet of smooth muscle formed from Scarpa fascia. Next comes the membranous Colles fascia, then the three spermatic layers in their usual order, then the tunica vaginalis around the testis. Being smooth muscle, the dartos is run by <b>sympathetic fibers</b>, which reach it inside scrotal skin nerves such as the genital branch of the genitofemoral. In the cold it contracts and wrinkles the skin. Its fascia thickens in the midline into the scrotal septum. The cremaster, by contrast, is skeletal muscle with a somatic motor nerve.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G2","h":"The cremaster and its nerve","p":"<p>The <b>cremaster</b> is skeletal muscle derived from the internal oblique. It wraps the cord and lifts the testis, in the cold and for protection. Its motor nerve is the <b>genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve</b> (first and second lumbar, L1–L2). That branch runs in the cord and also supplies the skin of the front of the scrotum. The cremasteric reflex tests it: stroke the upper inner thigh and the testis on that side rises. The sensory (afferent) limb is the ilioinguinal nerve, which feels the stroke. The motor (efferent) limb is the genital branch. Other nerves do other jobs. The femoral branch of the genitofemoral supplies upper thigh skin, the iliohypogastric the skin above the pubis, the femoral nerve the front thigh muscles. The pudendal nerve (second to fourth sacral, S2–S4) and its branches serve the perineum, the penis, the back of the scrotum and the bulbospongiosus. The dartos, a separate smooth muscle, wrinkles the skin under sympathetic control. So skin that still wrinkles while the testis stays down points to the cremaster or its nerve.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G2","h":"Using the reflex to test the spinal cord","p":"<p>Each reflex checks particular spinal cord segments. The cremasteric reflex checks the first and second lumbar segments (L1–L2): the ilioinguinal nerve carries the stroke in, and the genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve carries the command out. The normal response is <b>testicular retraction</b>: the cremaster contracts and pulls the testis up. Other reflexes test other levels. The anocutaneous reflex (\"anal wink\") tests the second to fourth sacral segments (S2–S4). The patellar reflex (knee jerk) tests the second to fourth lumbar (L2–L4). The plantar reflex tests the fifth lumbar and first sacral (L5–S1). The calcaneal reflex (ankle jerk) tests the first and second sacral (S1–S2). Some responses look similar but are different. Stroking the abdominal skin makes the abdominal muscles twitch (the abdominal reflex). The <b>dartos</b> contracts slowly in the cold under sympathetic control. Erection is a parasympathetic response.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G2","h":"Testis, epididymis and ductus deferens","p":"<p>The testis makes sperm and testosterone. A tough white capsule, the <b>tunica albuginea</b>, covers it. Inside, seminiferous tubules drain into a network of channels, the rete testis, and then through efferent ductules to the head of the epididymis. The <b>tunica vaginalis</b> is a closed, two-layer serous sac left from the processus vaginalis. Its visceral layer sticks to the tunica albuginea. Fluid between its layers is a hydrocele, and light shone through the scrotum passes through it; blood there (a hematocele) blocks the light. The epididymis is a coiled tube on the back of the testis, outside the tunica albuginea: head on top, body behind, tail behind and below. Sperm mature there; seminal fluid comes mainly from the seminal glands and prostate. The <b>ductus deferens</b> begins at the tail, climbs in the spermatic cord and enters the abdomen through the deep ring on its way to the prostate. So infection climbing up the ductus reaches the tail first. The testicular arteries come from the aorta, and a varicocele is a widened pampiniform plexus (next step).</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G2","h":"Blood supply and cooling of the testis","p":"<p>The testis formed beside the kidneys. So its <b>testicular artery</b> leaves the abdominal aorta just below the renal arteries and runs down in the spermatic cord. Iliac arteries do not supply it; the inferior epigastric gives only the small cremasteric artery. Its veins form the <b>pampiniform plexus</b>, 8–12 veins wrapped around the artery. Cool blood leaving the testis takes heat from warm blood coming in (countercurrent heat exchange), keeping the testis cooler than the body. The cremaster and dartos only help, by moving the testis. Near the deep ring the plexus becomes one testicular vein. The right vein enters the inferior vena cava (IVC). The <b>left vein enters the left renal vein</b> at a right angle, and that vein crosses to the IVC. Pressure is higher on the left, so a varicocele (widened plexus veins, a \"bag of worms\" worse on standing) is mostly left-sided; the slide calls it inflammation of the plexus. In nutcracker syndrome, fat loss lets the superior mesenteric artery (SMA) and aorta pinch the left renal vein, and blood backs up into the left testis.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G2","h":"Lymph: testis versus scrotum","p":"<p>Lymph follows the blood vessels. The testis formed beside the kidneys, so its lymph runs up along the testicular vessels to the <b>para-aortic nodes</b>, beside the aorta and inferior vena cava. \"Lumbar\", \"aortic\" and \"lateral aortic\" nodes are other names for this same group. Pre-aortic nodes, in front of the aorta, mainly drain the gut; some textbooks say a little testicular lymph reaches them, but the lecture's answer is para-aortic. The cisterna chyli is a collecting sac further on, not a first node. The scrotum is different. It is skin of the lower body wall, and skin below the umbilicus drains to the <b>superficial inguinal nodes</b> in the groin. So a tumor of the testis and a tumor of the scrotal skin spread to different first nodes. Other groups: deep inguinal nodes take the glans of the penis and the deep lower limb, external iliac nodes the lower limb and part of the bladder, internal iliac nodes the pelvic organs, sacral nodes the rectum.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G2","h":"When the testis does not descend","p":"<p>The testis descends along a fixed path: from the back wall of the abdomen, over the pelvic brim, to the deep ring, through the inguinal canal, out the superficial ring and into the scrotum. <b>Cryptorchidism</b> (cryptorchism, \"hidden testis\") means it stopped somewhere on that normal path. A testis found off the path, for example in the perineum or thigh, is an <b>ectopic testis</b>, a different condition. Undescended testes carry a much higher risk of <b>testicular cancer</b>, and they make fewer sperm. In androgen insensitivity syndrome, a person with male chromosomes (XY) has testes, but the body's cells cannot respond to testosterone. The outer body develops as female, with a short, blind-ending vagina. There is no uterus and there are no ovaries, because the testes still release anti-Müllerian hormone. The testes stay in the abdomen or groin and are usually removed because of the cancer risk. Unrelated terms: chordee is a downward bend of the penis; congenital adrenal hyperplasia is an adrenal enzyme defect; true hermaphroditism means both ovarian and testicular tissue.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G3","h":"Two layers and the space between","p":"<p>The peritoneum is a thin serous membrane: one continuous sheet folded into two layers. The <b>parietal peritoneum</b> lines the inner surface of the abdominal and pelvic walls. The <b>visceral peritoneum</b> covers the organs. The gap between them is the <b>peritoneal cavity</b>. It is only a potential space, holding a thin film of fluid that lets organs glide; it contains no organs and no lymph nodes. Outside the parietal layer lie extraperitoneal fat and the body wall, and the space behind it at the back is the retroperitoneum. In males the cavity is closed, because the pouch that followed the testis seals off. In females it opens to the outside through the uterine tubes, uterus and vagina. The layers feel pain differently. Parietal peritoneum shares the somatic nerves of the wall it lines, so pain, heat, cold and cutting are felt sharply and well localized. Visceral peritoneum lacks somatic sensory nerves, so its pain is vague and poorly localized. Both come from lateral plate mesoderm: parietal from its somatic layer, visceral from its splanchnic layer. Endoderm forms only the gut lining.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G3","h":"Intraperitoneal or retroperitoneal","p":"<p>An <b>intraperitoneal</b> organ is wrapped in visceral peritoneum almost all round and usually hangs on a mesentery, so it moves. It is not inside the cavity; it pushes into the sac like a fist into a balloon. Examples: stomach, first 2 cm of duodenum, jejunum, ileum, appendix, transverse and sigmoid colon, spleen and the pancreatic tail. A <b>retroperitoneal</b> organ lies behind the parietal peritoneum, against the posterior wall. Peritoneum covers only its front and it has no mesentery, so it cannot be lifted. Primarily retroperitoneal organs never had a mesentery: kidneys, ureters, suprarenal glands, aorta and inferior vena cava (IVC). <b>Secondarily retroperitoneal</b> organs grew in a mesentery, then fused to the posterior wall and lost it: duodenum parts 2 to 4, pancreas except its tail, and ascending and descending colon. Gonads, uterine cervix and rectum are not on that list. The bladder is subperitoneal, covered on top only. Retroperitoneal organs spell SAD PUCKER: Suprarenals, Aorta and IVC, Duodenum, Pancreas, Ureters, Colon, Kidneys, Esophagus (abdominal), Rectum (upper two-thirds; the lower third lies below the peritoneum).</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G3","h":"Ligaments, omenta and mesenteries","p":"<p>Peritoneal formations are <b>double layers</b> of peritoneum, with vessels running between them. A <b>peritoneal ligament</b> joins an organ to another organ or to the body wall. An omentum runs from the stomach and first part of the duodenum to nearby organs. A <b>mesentery</b> joins an intraperitoneal organ to the body wall, usually the posterior wall; retroperitoneal organs have none. Omenta and mesenteries are both visceral peritoneum, which turns into parietal peritoneum at their roots. The classic ligament is the falciform ligament, a remnant of the ventral mesentery. It joins the front of the liver to the anterior abdominal wall and diaphragm, and its free edge holds the round ligament of the liver (the closed-off umbilical vein). It is separate from the greater omentum. The free edge of the lesser omentum is a different ligament, the hepatoduodenal. The root of the mesentery proper, about 15 cm long, starts at the duodenojejunal junction, left of the second lumbar vertebra (L2), and runs obliquely down and to the right to the ileocolic junction and right sacroiliac joint. The transverse mesocolon's root runs across, along the lower border of the pancreas.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G3","h":"Greater omentum and the splenorenal ligament","p":"<p>The <b>greater omentum</b> hangs from the greater curvature of the stomach and the first part of the duodenum. It drapes down in front of the small intestine, folds back up and attaches to the front of the transverse colon. Its parts all start on the greater curvature, and each is named for its two ends. The <b>gastrocolic ligament</b>, greater curvature to transverse colon, is the big apron. The gastrosplenic ligament runs from the greater curvature to the splenic hilum and carries the short gastric vessels. The gastrophrenic ligament runs from the fundus to the diaphragm. There is no gastrointestinal ligament. Ligaments that start on the liver (hepatogastric, hepatoduodenal) form the lesser omentum, and the hepatorenal ligament joins the liver to the right kidney. The <b>splenorenal ligament</b> joins the spleen to the left kidney and is not omentum. Between its layers run the splenic vessels and the tail of the pancreas, so a surgeon must open it to reach a tumor in the tail. The falciform ligament belongs to the liver; the ovarian ligament and round ligament of the uterus are pelvic.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G3","h":"Lesser omentum: two ligaments","p":"<p>The <b>lesser omentum</b> is a double layer of peritoneum running from the liver to the lesser curvature of the stomach and the first part of the duodenum. It has two continuous parts. Both start on the liver and are named for their two ends. The <b>hepatogastric ligament</b> runs from the liver to the lesser curvature. It is the thin, membranous left part, and it holds no portal triad. The <b>hepatoduodenal ligament</b> runs from the liver to the first part of the duodenum. It is the thick right part and forms the free edge of the lesser omentum, which holds the portal triad. Sort the neighbors by where they start. Ligaments from the greater curvature (gastrophrenic, gastrosplenic, gastrocolic) make the greater omentum. The splenorenal ligament joins spleen to left kidney. The falciform ligament joins the liver to the anterior abdominal wall, and the coronary and triangular ligaments join the top of the liver to the diaphragm.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G3","h":"The ligament that carries the portal triad","p":"<p>The <b>portal triad</b> is the three tubes that serve the liver at its hilum: an artery, a vein and a bile duct. They reach the liver inside the <b>hepatoduodenal ligament</b>, the thick right part of the lesser omentum. This ligament joins the liver to the first part of the duodenum, and its right border is the <b>free edge</b> of the lesser omentum. Behind that edge is an opening into the lesser sac, so a surgeon can pinch the whole ligament between two fingers. The thin left part of the lesser omentum, the hepatogastric (gastrohepatic) ligament, runs to the lesser curvature of the stomach and carries no triad. A simple rule: the duodenal edge holds the triad. The lesser omentum is the whole sheet; the triad lies in one named part of it. Don't confuse it with the falciform ligament (liver to anterior abdominal wall), the round ligament of the liver in the falciform's free edge (the closed umbilical vein), the lienorenal (splenorenal) ligament far to the left, or the greater omentum hanging from the greater curvature.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G3","h":"The three members of the portal triad","p":"<p>The portal triad has three members. Inside the hepatoduodenal ligament, the <b>proper hepatic artery</b> lies on the left and brings oxygen-rich blood. The <b>common bile duct</b> lies on the right and carries bile down to the duodenum. The <b>hepatic portal vein</b>, the largest, lies behind both and brings blood from the gut and spleen. The classic trap is the hepatic veins. They are not in the triad: they leave the back of the liver straight into the inferior vena cava (IVC), so they never run in the lesser omentum. The IVC is not in the ligament either; it lies behind it. The cystic duct and cystic artery belong to the gallbladder, not the triad. The celiac trunk and its common hepatic and splenic branches lie lower, along the pancreas. Any operation on the lesser omentum must find and protect these three tubes in its free edge. Directly behind that free edge is the epiploic (omental) foramen, the only opening between the greater sac and the lesser sac.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G3","h":"Free-edge artery and vein: the Pringle maneuver","p":"<p>Follow the hepatic artery from the celiac trunk. The common hepatic artery runs right along the top of the pancreas. It gives off the gastroduodenal artery, which drops behind the first part of the duodenum. Beyond that branch it is called the <b>proper hepatic artery</b>, the only artery that climbs in the hepatoduodenal ligament. It splits into right and left hepatic arteries, and the right hepatic gives the cystic artery to the gallbladder. The right gastric artery, another hepatic branch, turns left along the lesser curvature and is not in the triad. The only large vein in the ligament is the <b>hepatic portal vein</b>. Pinching the free edge, with a finger behind it in the epiploic foramen and a thumb in front, is the <b>Pringle maneuver</b>. It stops inflow through the proper hepatic artery and portal vein, so it controls bleeding in liver surgery, liver transplants, or a gallbladder operation with a torn cystic artery. The left gastric runs on the lesser curvature, the splenic goes left, and the celiac trunk lies behind the lesser sac.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G3","h":"Greater sac and lesser sac","p":"<p>The peritoneal cavity has two parts. The <b>greater sac</b> is most of it, from the diaphragm to the pelvic floor; the infracolic spaces, the paracolic gutters and Morison pouch (between liver and right kidney) all belong to it. The <b>lesser sac (omental bursa)</b> is a pocket behind the liver, lesser omentum, stomach and upper greater omentum, and in front of the pancreas. It is closed except for one opening into the greater sac, the <b>epiploic (omental) foramen</b>, just behind the hepatoduodenal ligament. Its front wall is the lesser omentum, the back of the stomach and the gastrocolic ligament. Its recesses are superior, inferior and splenic. Because it lies between stomach and pancreas, a perforated ulcer of the posterior stomach wall, or an injured, leaking pancreas, spills into it first; pancreatic fluid trapped there forms a pseudocyst. Surgeons enter it widely and safely by cutting the broad gastrocolic ligament. The gastrosplenic ligament is narrow and carries short gastric vessels, the splenorenal holds the splenic vessels, the hepatoduodenal holds the portal triad, and the coronary ligament ties the liver to the diaphragm.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G3","h":"Borders of the epiploic foramen","p":"<p>The <b>epiploic foramen</b> (omental foramen, foramen of Winslow) is the only doorway between the greater sac and the lesser sac. Picture a finger slipped into it from the right. Above the finger is peritoneum on the <b>caudate lobe</b> of the liver. Below it is peritoneum on the <b>first (superior) part of the duodenum</b>. In front is the hepatoduodenal ligament, the free edge of the lesser omentum, with the portal vein, proper hepatic artery and bile duct inside. Behind is peritoneum covering the inferior vena cava (IVC). The quadrate lobe and the gastroduodenal artery are not borders. The aorta is not one either; it lies to the left, behind the lesser sac. The second and third parts of the duodenum lie lower. These four borders are sometimes asked as the borders of the lesser sac; they are really the borders of its doorway. Rarely, a loop of small intestine slips through and is strangled. No border can be cut to free it, because each carries large vessels, so the loop is emptied with a needle and pushed back.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G3","h":"Behind the foramen: the inferior vena cava","p":"<p>The back wall of the epiploic foramen is a thin layer of peritoneum lying on the <b>inferior vena cava (IVC)</b>. So a finger, probe, knife or scissors passing just behind the hepatoduodenal ligament through the foramen lands on the IVC, and a tear floods the field with blood at once. Know where the neighbors are, to rule them out. In front of the foramen is the ligament, with the bile duct, proper hepatic artery and portal vein. Above is the caudate lobe of the liver. The <b>aorta</b> lies to the left of the IVC, behind the lesser sac, not directly behind the foramen; the celiac trunk leaves it there. The common hepatic artery runs along the top of the pancreas, below the foramen. The head of the pancreas lies lower, inside the C-shaped curve of the duodenum. The superior mesenteric artery and vein lie lower still, behind the neck of the pancreas. The right renal artery is lower too, near the first lumbar vertebra (L1), and passes behind the IVC.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G3","h":"Recesses above and below the liver","p":"<p>The greater sac has recesses, pockets where fluid and pus collect. Fluid runs to whichever pocket is lowest (<b>gravity-dependent</b>) for the patient's position. The <b>subphrenic (suprahepatic) recess</b> lies above the liver, between the diaphragm and the front and top of the liver. The falciform ligament runs through it and splits it into right and left; the coronary ligaments only form its back edge. Upside down, it is the lowest point. The <b>hepatorenal recess (Morison pouch)</b> lies below the liver (subhepatic), between the liver in front and the right kidney and suprarenal gland behind. It is part of the greater sac, inside the peritoneal cavity, not a space in a ligament. It opens into the lesser sac through the epiploic foramen and into the pelvis through the right paracolic gutter. Supine, it is the lowest point. So a posterior gastric ulcer leaks first into the lesser sac, then out through the foramen into Morison pouch. Upright, the pelvic pouches are lowest. The costodiaphragmatic recess at the lung base is pleural, and the superior and splenic recesses belong to the lesser sac.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G3","h":"Lying supine: fluid pools in Morison pouch","p":"<p>When a patient lies flat on the back (<b>supine</b>), the lowest part of the peritoneal cavity is the <b>hepatorenal recess</b> (Morison pouch), between the liver and the right kidney. Blood, pus, leaked gut contents and ascites all collect there first, so ultrasound looks there first. Ascites is excess free fluid in the abdomen, often from portal hypertension in alcoholic liver disease. A perforated duodenal ulcer has a second reason to fill it: the first part of the duodenum lies right beside the pouch. A patient on a stretcher, in an ambulance, in bed or on the operating table is supine. The right paracolic gutter, beside the ascending colon, is only a channel linking Morison pouch with the pelvis; fluid flows along it but does not settle. When supine, the subphrenic recess lies higher than Morison pouch. The splenic recess and the rest of the lesser sac are not dependent spaces. The pelvic pouches are lowest only when sitting or standing: the <b>rectouterine pouch</b> in a woman and the rectovesical pouch in a man.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G3","h":"Upright: fluid sinks to the pelvis","p":"<p>When a patient sits or stands (upright or ambulant), the pelvis becomes the lowest part of the peritoneal cavity, so free fluid sinks there. In a <b>woman</b>, the deepest pocket is the <b>rectouterine pouch</b> (pouch of Douglas), between the rectum and the back of the uterus. The vesicouterine pouch, between the bladder and the front of the uterus, is shallower, so fluid gathers behind the uterus first. A <b>man</b> has no uterus, so his pelvic pocket is the rectovesical pouch, between the rectum and the bladder. Check the patient's sex before naming the pouch. Lying supine, the lowest point is the hepatorenal recess (Morison pouch) instead. The paracolic gutters beside the ascending and descending colon are channels that carry fluid between the upper abdomen and the pelvis, not places where it pools. The lesser sac is closed except for the epiploic foramen and is not a low point. So when a patient gets out of bed, fluid that pooled in Morison pouch runs down the right paracolic gutter into the pelvis.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G3","h":"Esophagus: course, nerve and arteries","p":"<p>The esophagus runs from the pharynx to the stomach. It starts at the lower border of the cricoid cartilage, at the sixth cervical vertebra (C6), and descends in front of the spine through neck and thorax. In the neck the trachea lies in front, the vertebral column behind, and the common carotid arteries and thyroid lobes at the sides. The <b>recurrent laryngeal nerves</b>, branches of the vagus, climb in the groove between esophagus and trachea, so they lie in front of the esophagus and behind the trachea. They supply the muscles of the larynx, so damage, for example from a swallowed fish bone, causes hoarseness. The phrenic nerve lies far to the side, on the anterior scalene, and supplies the diaphragm. The esophagus gets its arteries in <b>thirds</b>. Upper (cervical) third: the inferior thyroid artery, from the thyrocervical trunk. Middle third: esophageal branches straight off the descending thoracic aorta. Lower third and abdominal part: esophageal branches of the <b>left gastric artery</b>, which climb through the hiatus. The splenic, right gastric and superior mesenteric arteries give no esophageal branches.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G3","h":"Where the esophagus meets the stomach","p":"<p>The esophagus enters the abdomen through the esophageal hiatus, an opening in the <b>right crus</b> of the diaphragm. The 2026 lecture says it enters opposite the eleventh thoracic vertebra (T11); textbooks place the hiatus itself at T10. Its short abdominal part widens into a cone on the stomach's upper opening, the antrum cardiacum. The esophagogastric junction lies left of T11, below the hiatus, so it is not where the esophagus pierces the diaphragm. There a jagged line, the <b>Z-line</b>, marks the abrupt change from the esophagus's stratified squamous lining to the stomach's columnar lining. The lower esophageal sphincter (LES) is a <b>physiological</b> sphincter, not a thick anatomical ring. Just above the junction, right crus fibers around the hiatus pinch the esophagus involuntarily and stop stomach contents flowing back. A thick smooth-muscle ring describes the pyloric sphincter; the cricopharyngeus forms the upper sphincter in the neck. When this barrier fails, as in gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD), acid washes up into the lowest esophagus. The antrum cardiacum is burned first, and the squamous lining just above the Z-line is where ulcers form.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G3","h":"Stomach: regions, bed and outlet","p":"<p>The stomach is the J-shaped widening of the gut. The cardia surrounds the opening from the esophagus. The fundus is the dome above and left of it, holding the swallowed-air bubble. The body is the main part. The pyloric part (antrum, canal and sphincter) leads to the duodenum. The short right border is the lesser curvature; the long left border is the greater curvature. The back of the stomach faces the lesser sac, and behind that sac lies the <b>stomach bed</b>. From top to bottom: left dome of the diaphragm, spleen, left kidney and suprarenal gland, <b>splenic artery</b>, pancreas and transverse mesocolon. It is the left kidney, not the right, and the greater omentum hangs in front, not behind. The pyloric sphincter is a thickened middle (circular) muscle layer, closed except during peristalsis. In <b>hypertrophic pyloric stenosis</b>, this muscle grows too thick and narrows the canal. Emptying slows, so food piles up and the stomach above the outlet stretches. The antrum fills rather than collapses, and the duodenum beyond gets less. Babies vomit forcefully, dehydrate, lose weight and seem always hungry.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G3","h":"Celiac trunk: level and branch map","p":"<p>The <b>celiac trunk</b> is the artery of the foregut. This short trunk leaves the front of the aorta at the twelfth thoracic vertebra (T12), just after the aorta passes through the diaphragm. Keep the levels apart: T10 esophageal hiatus, T11 esophagogastric junction, T12 celiac trunk, first lumbar vertebra (L1) superior mesenteric artery. The kidneys get their own renal arteries. The trunk splits into three. The <b>left gastric</b> runs up to the cardia, then along the lesser curvature. The <b>splenic</b> runs, wavy, to the left along the top of the pancreas to the spleen, feeding the pancreatic body and tail on the way; it gives the short gastric and left gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) arteries. On an angiogram it is the long, tortuous branch heading to the patient's left. The common hepatic runs right. It gives the right gastric artery (per the lecture; textbooks often say the proper hepatic gives it) and the gastroduodenal artery, which gives the right gastro-omental and superior pancreaticoduodenal arteries. It then continues as the proper hepatic, whose right hepatic branch gives the cystic artery. Suprarenal arteries are not celiac branches.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G3","h":"Two arcades along the curvatures","p":"<p>Two arterial arcades ring the stomach, one on each curvature. In each, two arteries arrive from opposite ends and join in the middle. On the <b>lesser curvature</b> run the gastric arteries. The left gastric, a direct celiac branch, takes the left part. The right gastric, from the hepatic artery (the common hepatic in the lecture; often the proper hepatic in textbooks), takes the right part near the pylorus. On the <b>greater curvature</b> run the gastro-omental arteries, also called gastroepiploic. The left gastro-omental comes from the splenic artery, the right gastro-omental from the gastroduodenal artery. Because partners join, cutting one leaves the other to keep that curvature supplied. An ulcer erodes the artery beside it: on the lesser curvature a gastric artery, on the greater curvature a <b>gastro-omental artery</b>. Short gastric arteries feed the fundus. The splenic artery runs behind the stomach, the gastroduodenal behind the duodenum, and the proper hepatic up to the liver. Gastric arteries never run on the greater curvature. The right and left gastric veins run with their arteries and drain straight into the hepatic portal vein.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G3","h":"Splenic artery and the fundus","p":"<p>The <b>splenic artery</b> is the largest celiac branch. It runs left in a wavy course along the upper border of the pancreas, behind the stomach, to the spleen, so it is exposed in pancreatic surgery. It gives pancreatic branches to the pancreatic body and tail, and a posterior gastric artery. Near the spleen it gives four or five <b>short gastric arteries</b>, which run in the gastrosplenic ligament to the <b>fundus</b>, and the left gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery to the left greater curvature. Cutting the splenic artery stops flow in all of these, but not in its sister's territory: the common hepatic, proper hepatic, right hepatic and cystic arteries. The fundus is the dome above the cardia. It holds the gas bubble and relaxes to receive a meal (receptive relaxation). A fundic ulcer bleeds from the short gastrics. There is no long gastric artery. Other regions have other arteries: cardia and lower esophagus, the left gastric; pylorus, the right gastric and right gastro-omental; first part of the duodenum, the gastroduodenal; pancreatic head, the pancreaticoduodenal arteries. The antrum makes gastrin.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G3","h":"The gastroduodenal artery","p":"<p>The <b>gastroduodenal artery</b> is a branch of the common hepatic artery. It runs down directly behind the first part of the duodenum and has three branches. The small supraduodenal artery feeds the top of the first part. The <b>superior pancreaticoduodenal arteries</b> (anterior and posterior) run between the duodenum and the pancreatic head. The <b>right gastro-omental</b> (gastroepiploic) artery runs left along the greater curvature. The upper duodenum, down to where the bile duct opens, is foregut, fed by the gastroduodenal and superior pancreaticoduodenal arteries. Below that, the inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery from the superior mesenteric artery (SMA) takes over. The left gastric and splenic arteries give no duodenal branches. Cutting the gastroduodenal artery stops flow to its three branches only. The common hepatic is upstream, and it continues as the proper hepatic, which gives the right hepatic and then the cystic artery, so these are spared. If the gastroduodenal artery is blocked, collaterals partly rescue its territory: the inferior pancreaticoduodenal for the pancreatic head and duodenum, and the left gastro-omental along the greater curvature. The right gastric artery comes from the hepatic artery, not the gastroduodenal.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G3","h":"The duodenum and its neighbors","p":"<p>The <b>duodenum</b> is the first part of the small intestine: a C-shaped tube, mostly right of the midline, curved around the head of the pancreas. It has four parts: superior (first), descending (second), inferior or horizontal (third) and ascending (fourth). Only the first 2 cm is intraperitoneal; the rest is secondarily retroperitoneal. Its jobs are to neutralize the acid chyme arriving from the stomach, to continue chemical digestion and to absorb, and it has villi like the rest of the small intestine. The bile duct and main pancreatic duct join and open together on the <b>major duodenal papilla</b>, in the back inner wall of the descending part. The <b>inferior vena cava (IVC)</b> runs up behind the first three parts. The gastroduodenal artery lies behind the first part only. The transverse colon crosses in front of the second part, and the superior mesenteric artery and vein cross in front of the third. The head of the pancreas sits inside the C.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G3","h":"Posterior ulcers: which artery bleeds","p":"<p>An ulcer that eats through the wall erodes whatever artery lies right behind or beside it. The back wall of the stomach body faces the lesser sac and the stomach bed, where the large <b>splenic artery</b> runs along the upper border of the pancreas. So a perforating <b>posterior gastric ulcer</b> erodes the splenic artery. The back of the first (superior) part of the duodenum lies directly on the <b>gastroduodenal artery</b>, so a posterior duodenal ulcer erodes that artery. The first part of the duodenum receives acid chyme from the stomach, and its job is to neutralize it. Other jobs belong elsewhere: the stomach mixes chyme, bile emulsifies fat, and the stomach's parietal cells make intrinsic factor. Other ulcer sites have other arteries: lesser curvature, the gastric arteries; greater curvature, the gastro-omental arteries; fundus, the short gastrics. The common hepatic runs right toward the liver, the small supraduodenal artery sits on top of the duodenum, and the superior pancreaticoduodenal arteries lie on the pancreatic head beside the second part. Front-wall ulcers tend to perforate into the greater sac and cause peritonitis.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G3","h":"Veins and lymph follow the arteries","p":"<p>The stomach's veins and lymph vessels run with its arteries. The right and left gastric veins drain straight into the <b>hepatic portal vein</b>. The short gastric and left gastro-omental veins drain into the splenic vein, and the right gastro-omental vein into the superior mesenteric vein (SMV); the splenic vein and SMV then form the portal vein. No stomach vein drains to the inferior vena cava or the hepatic veins. Lymph first passes small nodes along the arteries: left gastric nodes for the lesser curvature, pancreaticosplenic nodes at the splenic hilum and along the pancreas for the fundus and left greater curvature, gastro-omental nodes, and pyloric nodes. All of it then reaches the <b>celiac nodes</b> around the celiac trunk, so they are the nodes most likely to receive a stomach cancer from any site. Next comes the <b>cisterna chyli</b>, a lymph sac behind the right crus that starts the thoracic duct. If it is compressed, lymph backs up into enlarged celiac nodes, and the duct above does not swell. Stomach lymph never goes to mesenteric or lumbar nodes, the azygos vein or the right lymphatic duct.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G3","h":"The vagus: parasympathetic supply","p":"<p>The stomach's parasympathetic nerve is the <b>vagus</b>. Below the esophageal plexus, the left vagus continues as the anterior vagal trunk and the right vagus as the posterior vagal trunk; both reach the stomach. Vagal action <b>increases peristalsis and relaxes the pyloric sphincter</b>, so chyme moves on into the duodenum. Sympathetic action does the opposite: less peristalsis and a contracted pylorus. Vagal fibers are preganglionic. They pass through the celiac plexus without stopping and synapse in small ganglia in or near the organ itself. The anterior trunk mainly serves the stomach and liver. The <b>posterior vagal trunk</b> sends fibers through the celiac plexus to ganglia in and around the pancreas, and drives its endocrine secretion, such as insulin. The celiac ganglion is a sympathetic relay, and the splanchnic nerves carry sympathetic fibers, not vagal ones. The phrenic nerve is a motor nerve to the diaphragm, not to the stomach.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G3","h":"Sympathetic supply and foregut pain","p":"<p>The stomach's sympathetic supply starts in spinal cord segments T6 to T9 (sixth to ninth thoracic). These preganglionic fibers leave in the <b>greater splanchnic nerve</b> and synapse in the <b>celiac ganglion</b>, beside the celiac trunk. Postganglionic fibers then travel along the arteries to the stomach. Sympathetic action slows peristalsis and tightens the pylorus. Visceral pain fibers run back along the same route. So pain from foregut organs (stomach, duodenum, liver, pancreas) travels in the greater splanchnic nerves to T6 to T9 and is felt in the epigastrium, the upper middle abdomen; pancreatic pain can spread to the back. The other splanchnic nerves carry pain from lower gut: the lesser splanchnic nerves (T10 to T11) from the midgut, and the lumbar splanchnic nerves (first and second lumbar, L1 to L2) from the hindgut. Vagal sensory fibers carry fullness and reflex signals, not pain. Spinal nerves carry pain from the body wall. The dorsal root, geniculate and trigeminal ganglia are sensory ganglia, not sympathetic relays.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G4","h":"The small intestine and its mesentery","p":"<p>The small intestine runs from the pylorus of the stomach to the ileocecal junction, where the large intestine begins. It has three parts. The duodenum is a short C-shaped loop around the head of the pancreas. Apart from its first 2 cm it is retroperitoneal: it lies behind the peritoneum, fixed to the back wall. The jejunum and ileum follow, about 6 m together. They hang free from the posterior abdominal wall on the <b>mesentery</b>, a double-layered fold of peritoneum that carries their arteries, veins, lymph vessels and nerves. Other folds have their own names. The mesoappendix holds the appendix, the transverse and sigmoid mesocolons hold those parts of the colon, and the omenta hang from the stomach. Because the jejunum and ileum hang on a stalk, a loop can twist on its own mesentery. This is a <b>volvulus</b>. It blocks the bowel and pinches its blood supply. The lecture names sigmoid, cecal and midgut (small-bowel) volvulus. The cecum, appendix and transverse colon are large intestine, not small.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G4","h":"First part of the duodenum: its neighbors","p":"<p>The duodenum has four parts. The <b>superior (first) part</b> lies anterolateral to the body of the first lumbar vertebra (L1). Its first 2 cm, the duodenal cap, is mobile; the rest of the duodenum is fixed. Learn this part by its neighbors on each side. In front: peritoneum, the <b>gallbladder</b> and the quadrate lobe of the liver. So an ulcer in its front wall can erode into the gallbladder. Behind: the <b>bile duct</b>, the gastroduodenal artery, the portal vein and the inferior vena cava (IVC). So a knife that passes through the duodenum and then these vessels went through the first part. Above: the neck of the gallbladder, with the cystic duct, and the hepatoduodenal ligament. Below: the neck of the pancreas and the greater omentum. The common hepatic artery runs above it, along the top of the pancreas, and the stomach joins it at the pylorus. Other organs touch other parts: the right kidney lies behind the second part, and the left lobe of the liver lies over the stomach.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G4","h":"Duodenal ulcers: bleeding or perforation","p":"<p>Duodenal ulcers (open sores in the lining) are most common on the <b>posterior wall of the first part</b>. What happens next depends on which wall is eaten through. Directly behind the first part, the <b>gastroduodenal artery</b> (GDA), a branch of the common hepatic artery, runs straight down. A deep posterior ulcer erodes it, and the patient vomits bright-red blood. The bile duct and portal vein also lie behind the first part, but deeper or off to the side, so the GDA is hit first. The proper hepatic artery runs above the duodenum, in the hepatoduodenal ligament. The GDA's own branches lie elsewhere: the superior pancreaticoduodenal arteries run lower, on the pancreatic head; the right gastro-omental runs along the greater curvature of the stomach; the supraduodenal is a tiny twig. The inferior pancreaticoduodenal and superior mesenteric arteries lie lower still, near the third part. Stomach arteries (left gastric, short gastric, left gastro-omental, splenic) bleed from gastric ulcers, not duodenal ones. The anterior wall faces the open peritoneal cavity. An anterior ulcer that perforates spills gut contents there and causes <b>peritonitis</b>.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G4","h":"The lower duodenum and its neighbors","p":"<p>Past its first 2 cm, the duodenum is <b>retroperitoneal</b>: it lies behind the peritoneum, like the pancreas it wraps around. The descending (second) part runs down the right side of the first to third lumbar vertebrae (L1–L3), to the right of the inferior vena cava (IVC) and on the right kidney. The <b>inferior (third, horizontal) part</b> crosses the midline at L3. Behind it lie the right psoas, the IVC, the aorta and the right ureter. In front lie the superior mesenteric vessels and jejunum; above it is the pancreatic head. So a pulsating abdominal aortic aneurysm (AAA), or a mass in front of the IVC at L3, presses on this part. The ascending (fourth) part lies left of the aorta. The first part, at L1, is in front of the IVC but not the aorta. The portal vein and left renal artery lie higher, and the sympathetic trunks lie deeper, on the vertebral bodies. Because the third part sits just below the pancreatic head, pancreatic cancer can grow straight into it without crossing peritoneum. The stomach, jejunum and transverse colon are intraperitoneal, so peritoneum separates them from the pancreas.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G4","h":"Jejunum versus ileum","p":"<p>The jejunum is the first two-fifths of the free small intestine and lies mainly in the left upper quadrant (LUQ), left of the umbilicus. The ileum is the last three-fifths and lies mainly in the right lower quadrant (RLQ). Their blood supply looks different. Jejunal and ileal arteries join into loops in the mesentery called <b>arterial arcades</b>, and straight arteries, the <b>vasa recta</b>, run from the last loop to the gut wall. The jejunum has one row of a few large arcades, so its vasa recta are long. The ileum has two or three rows of many small arcades, so its vasa recta are short. The jejunum also has a thick, red, very vascular wall, tall closely packed circular folds (plicae circulares), little mesenteric fat and the most absorption. Iron is absorbed in the duodenum and proximal jejunum, so losing jejunum risks <b>iron deficiency</b>. Vitamin B12 and bile salts are absorbed in the terminal ileum; intrinsic factor is made by the stomach. The duodenum is a fixed C-loop, and the cecum is large bowel, with muscle bands (teniae) and pouches instead of circular folds.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G4","h":"The ileum and Peyer patches","p":"<p>The ileum ends the small intestine at the ileocecal junction, mainly in the right lower quadrant (RLQ). Compared with the jejunum, it has less of almost everything, except arcades and fat. Its wall is thin and pale. Its circular folds (plicae circulares) are low and fewer, and they disappear near its end. It has more arterial arcades, in two or three rows (the lecture says a double row), but shorter vasa recta, and more fat in its mesentery. It absorbs fewer nutrients. Its special feature is <b>Peyer patches</b>: large clusters of lymphoid nodules in the wall. They sample antigens coming down the gut and drive the making of <b>immunoglobulin A (IgA)</b>, the antibody of the gut lining. So chronic ileal disease can lower IgA and lead to repeated infections. Do not confuse them with Brunner glands, which are mucous glands in the duodenum that neutralize stomach acid. Tall villi and dense folds serve absorption, mainly in the jejunum. The transverse and sigmoid colon are large bowel, with teniae and haustra.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G4","h":"Where the gut arteries leave the aorta","p":"<p>The gut is divided into three regions, and each gets one unpaired artery from the front of the abdominal aorta. The <b>foregut</b> runs from the lower esophagus to the second part of the duodenum. Its artery is the celiac trunk, at the twelfth thoracic vertebra (T12), which also feeds the liver, pancreas and spleen. The <b>midgut</b> runs from the second part of the duodenum to two-thirds of the way along the transverse colon, including the jejunum, ileum and cecum. Its artery is the superior mesenteric artery (SMA), from the lower part of the first lumbar vertebra (L1), about 1 cm below the celiac trunk. The <b>hindgut</b> runs from the last third of the transverse colon to the upper anal canal. Its artery is the inferior mesenteric artery (IMA). The 2026 lecture places its origin in front of L2–L3; textbooks say L3. Either way, an aneurysm at L3–L4 involves the IMA. Other aortic levels: renal arteries L1–L2, gonadal arteries L2, and the split into the common iliac arteries at L4. No gut artery arises at T11.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G4","h":"The SMA feeds the midgut","p":"<p>The superior mesenteric artery (SMA) is the artery of the midgut. Its branches: the inferior pancreaticoduodenal to the lower pancreatic head and the duodenum past the major duodenal papilla; jejunal and ileal arteries to the jejunum and ileum; the ileocolic to the terminal ileum and cecum, with the appendicular artery to the appendix; the right colic to the ascending colon; and the middle colic to the proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon. The <b>celiac–SMA border</b> lies in the second part of the duodenum, just past the papilla where the bile duct opens, not between parts one and two. The SMA–IMA border lies two-thirds of the way along the transverse colon; the inferior mesenteric artery (IMA) feeds the colon beyond it. The jejunum and ileum have no other source, so a proximal SMA clot hits them hardest. The duodenum also gets celiac blood, and the transverse colon gets IMA blood. In <b>chronic mesenteric ischemia</b>, an atherosclerotic SMA cannot deliver extra blood after meals. Pain starts 20–30 minutes after eating, so the patient fears food and loses weight. IMA narrowing alone is usually silent.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G4","h":"Two arterial sources for the duodenum","p":"<p>Because the foregut–midgut border falls in the second part of the duodenum, the duodenum gets blood from both the celiac trunk and the superior mesenteric artery (SMA). From above come the <b>superior pancreaticoduodenal arteries</b>, branches of the gastroduodenal artery (from the common hepatic artery, a celiac branch). From below comes the <b>inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery</b>, the first branch of the SMA. Each splits into an anterior and a posterior branch. The two anterior branches meet on the front of the pancreatic head; the two posterior branches meet behind the head of the pancreas. So the arcades lie in the curve between the descending duodenum and the pancreatic head, medial to the duodenum, and feed both organs. The SMA itself therefore supplies the lower duodenum. The greater pancreatic artery is different: it is a branch of the splenic artery that feeds the body and tail of the pancreas, not the duodenum. Duodenal veins follow the arteries and drain into the portal vein.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G4","h":"The celiac–SMA collateral","p":"<p>The superior pancreaticoduodenal arteries (from the celiac trunk) join the inferior pancreaticoduodenal arteries (from the superior mesenteric artery, SMA) around the pancreatic head. This arcade is the <b>main link between the celiac trunk and the SMA</b>, and blood can flow through it either way. If the SMA is narrowed or blocked, blood runs celiac trunk → common hepatic artery → gastroduodenal artery → superior pancreaticoduodenal arteries → inferior pancreaticoduodenal arteries, backward into SMA territory. This can delay bowel death. The pancreas keeps its supply too: its head from the superior pancreaticoduodenals, its body and tail from splenic branches. If the celiac trunk is narrowed, SMA blood flows the other way and feeds the foregut organs. Other loops do not link these two trunks. Right with left gastro-omental (gastroepiploic), gastroduodenal with right gastro-omental, left gastric with hepatic, and cystic with gastroduodenal all join celiac branches to each other. The short gastrics run from the splenic artery to the fundus. Colic links join the SMA to the inferior mesenteric artery (IMA), and superior with middle rectal joins the IMA to the internal iliac.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G4","h":"SMA branches, arcades and the SMV","p":"<p>The superior mesenteric artery (SMA) leaves the aorta at the first lumbar vertebra (L1), passes in front of the third (horizontal) part of the duodenum, and runs down and right in the root of the mesentery. From its left side come 15–18 <b>jejunal and ileal arteries</b> (BRS Gross Anatomy gives 12–15). They join into loops, the arcades, and the arcades send straight vasa recta into the gut wall. From its right side come the middle colic (to the transverse colon), right colic (to the ascending colon) and ileocolic (to the cecum and terminal ileum). Its sympathetic fibers come from thoracic splanchnic nerves; lumbar splanchnics serve the hindgut. The <b>superior mesenteric vein (SMV)</b> runs beside the artery, on its right and slightly in front. Jejunal and ileal veins drain into it. Behind the neck of the pancreas, the SMV joins the splenic vein to form the <b>portal vein</b>, which carries gut blood to the liver. Only after the liver does that blood reach the inferior vena cava. The renal vein drains the kidney, and the azygos vein drains the back of the chest wall.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G4","h":"What lies behind the SMA","p":"<p>The superior mesenteric artery (SMA) leaves the aorta behind the pancreas and runs down through a sandwich. In front of it lie the splenic vein, the neck and body of the pancreas and, further forward, the stomach. Behind it, from top to bottom, lie the left renal vein, the <b>uncinate process</b> and the third part of the duodenum. The uncinate process is a hook from the lower pancreatic head that slips behind the superior mesenteric vessels; the pancreatic tail is far left, at the spleen. Below the pancreas, the SMA and superior mesenteric vein sit just above the <b>third part of the duodenum</b>, then cross in front of it into the mesentery. So they are both superior and anterior to it, and a knife through the SMA just below the pancreatic head reaches this part. The aorta and inferior vena cava lie behind it; the first part is higher and to the right. The SMA runs down and to the right and is the midgut artery; the inferior mesenteric artery starts lower and runs left. The SMA–aorta angle can squeeze both this part of the duodenum and the left renal vein.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G4","h":"SMA syndrome","p":"<p>The superior mesenteric artery (SMA) leaves the aorta at a narrow angle. Normally a pad of mesenteric fat props it away from the aorta. After rapid weight loss, the fat pad shrinks and the angle closes like a pair of jaws. The <b>third part of the duodenum</b> crosses inside that angle, so it is pinched between the SMA in front and the <b>aorta</b> behind. This is SMA syndrome. The blocked duodenum causes early fullness, nausea, vomiting and sharp, stabbing pain soon after meals, made worse by reversed peristalsis. Patients come to fear food (\"food fear\"), which deepens the weight loss. Only the third part lies in the angle. The first part is higher, at the first lumbar vertebra (L1); the second lies on the right beside the inferior vena cava; the fourth lies left of the aorta. The inferior mesenteric artery arises lower, at the third lumbar vertebra (L3), so it cannot form the jaws. The uncinate process also sits behind the SMA, but it does not grow to block the duodenum. When the left renal vein is pinched instead, the condition is nutcracker syndrome.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G4","h":"Nutcracker syndrome","p":"<p>The <b>left renal vein</b> runs from the left kidney across the front of the aorta, just below the origin of the superior mesenteric artery (SMA), to the inferior vena cava (IVC) on the right. So it passes through the narrow angle between the SMA in front and the aorta behind. If the angle closes, as after heavy weight loss, the two arteries squeeze the vein like the arms of a nutcracker: <b>nutcracker syndrome</b>. Blood backs up in the left kidney, causing blood in the urine and flank pain. The left gonadal vein drains into the left renal vein, so a left varicocele can appear. Only the left renal vein crosses this angle. The left renal artery comes off the aorta behind the vein. The right renal vein is short and enters the IVC directly; the right renal artery runs behind the IVC. The superior mesenteric vein lies right of the SMA, and the portal vein forms higher, behind the pancreatic neck. The celiac trunk arises above the renal veins, and the inferior mesenteric and common iliac arteries arise below them.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G4","h":"The large intestine: parts and markers","p":"<p>The large intestine runs from the end of the ileum to the anus. In order: the <b>cecum</b>, a blind pouch in the right iliac fossa with the appendix hanging from it; the ascending colon, up the right side to the right colic (hepatic) flexure under the liver; the transverse colon, across to the left colic (splenic) flexure; the descending colon, down the left side from the splenic flexure to the sigmoid colon; the sigmoid colon, from the left iliac fossa to the third sacral vertebra; then the rectum and anal canal. The abdominal parts run from the cecum to the upper sigmoid colon. The <b>pelvic parts</b> are the lower sigmoid colon, the rectum and the anal canal. The appendix may hang over the pelvic brim but counts as abdominal. Three markers identify colon: <b>teniae coli</b>, three ribbons of longitudinal muscle; haustra, the pouches the teniae gather the wall into; and epiploic appendages, small fat tags. The appendix and rectum have a complete longitudinal muscle coat instead, so they lack all three.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G4","h":"Cecum, ileocecal valve and appendix position","p":"<p>The cecum is a blind pouch about 7.5 cm wide in the right iliac fossa. It has no mesentery, yet it is covered by peritoneum. The ileum pokes into it, forming the <b>ileocecal valve</b>, which does not stop backflow. The terminal ileum is the narrowest part of the small intestine, so this is a common site of blockage: in children the ileum telescopes into the cecum (intussusception), and in adults a gallstone can lodge there. The wide jejunum rarely blocks, duodenal blockage is mainly a newborn problem, and the splenic flexure suffers from poor blood flow rather than blockage. The appendix arises from the posteromedial wall of the cecum, below the ileocecal junction, on a short mesentery, the mesoappendix. Its base is fixed; its tip varies. The lecture says it is usually <b>retrocecal</b>, behind the cecum: 64% in its figure. Then come pelvic (32%), subcecal (2%), pre-ileal, in front of the terminal ileum (1%), and post-ileal, behind it (0.5%). A subhepatic appendix, under the liver, is rare and goes with a cecum that failed to descend.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G4","h":"Blood supply of the cecum and appendix","p":"<p>The cecum and appendix are midgut, so all their supply comes through the superior mesenteric system. The last branch of the superior mesenteric artery (SMA), the <b>ileocolic artery</b>, runs down and right to the corner where the ileum meets the cecum. There it gives ileal branches, a colic branch to the start of the ascending colon, anterior and posterior cecal arteries to the cecum, and the <b>appendicular artery</b>, which runs in the mesoappendix to the appendix. The appendicular artery is the appendix's only artery and is tied in an appendectomy. If it is not among the choices, pick its parent, the ileocolic; if only main trunks are offered, pick the SMA. The right colic feeds the ascending colon and the middle colic the transverse colon. The left colic, the sigmoid arteries and their parent, the inferior mesenteric artery, feed the left colon. Venous blood leaves through the ileocolic vein, and lymph goes to ileocolic nodes. Nerves come from the superior mesenteric plexus: vagal parasympathetic fibers and lower thoracic sympathetic fibers. Pelvic splanchnic nerves and the inferior mesenteric plexus serve only the hindgut.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G4","h":"Appendicitis and McBurney's point","p":"<p>Appendicitis is acute inflammation of the appendix, usually in a young person. Its pain moves. At first the swollen appendix stretches its own wall. That pain travels with visceral (organ) nerves to the tenth thoracic spinal segment (T10), so it is felt as a vague ache <b>around the umbilicus</b>, the T10 skin band. Hours later the inflammation reaches the parietal peritoneum, whose somatic nerves give sharp, well-placed pain. The pain then settles in the right lower quadrant (RLQ), with rebound tenderness, low fever and a raised white cell count. The surface spot is <b>McBurney's point</b>: one-third of the way from the right anterior superior iliac spine (ASIS) to the umbilicus. That is the same spot as two-thirds of the way from the umbilicus to the ASIS. It lies over the base of the appendix, not the terminal ileum or cecum. Rupture causes peritonitis. Look-alikes: Crohn disease also gives RLQ pain, but it is chronic and relapsing, with diarrhea. Superior mesenteric artery syndrome gives upper pain after meals and weight loss. Megacolon is a hugely dilated colon with constipation.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G4","h":"Which parts of the colon are fixed","p":"<p>In the embryo every part of the colon hangs on a mesentery. Some parts keep it; others lose it. The <b>ascending colon</b> and the descending colon lose theirs: the mesentery fuses to the posterior abdominal wall, pinning them flat behind the peritoneum. Organs that become retroperitoneal this way are called <b>secondarily retroperitoneal</b>. The ascending colon lies on the right posterior wall, from the cecum up to the hepatic flexure; the descending colon is its match on the left (one person in three keeps a short mesentery there). The <b>transverse colon</b> keeps a mesentery, the transverse mesocolon, and runs from the hepatic flexure to the splenic flexure. It is the largest and most mobile part of the colon. The sigmoid colon also keeps a long mesentery, the sigmoid mesocolon, so it is intraperitoneal and very mobile. The cecum has no mesentery but is almost fully covered by peritoneum, so it counts as intraperitoneal. The rectum, in the pelvis, has no mesentery and is fixed.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G4","h":"Right colic and middle colic arteries","p":"<p>Three branches of the superior mesenteric artery (SMA) feed the right half of the colon, and on an angiogram each is known by its direction. The ileocolic runs down and to the right, to the cecum and terminal ileum. The <b>right colic artery</b> runs straight to the right, behind the peritoneum, to the ascending colon and the right colic (hepatic) flexure; the lecture lists the ileocolic and right colic for the ascending colon. The hepatic flexure lies under the liver, deep to the ninth and tenth ribs. It is midgut, so its blood comes from the SMA, not the celiac trunk. The <b>middle colic artery</b> leaves the SMA just below the pancreas and runs up into the transverse mesocolon. There it splits into a right branch, which reaches the transverse colon near the hepatic flexure and joins the right colic, and a left branch, which joins the left colic. Jejunal arteries fan out to the left. The left colic, sigmoid and superior rectal arteries come from the inferior mesenteric artery (IMA) and feed the left colon and rectum.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G4","h":"The SMA–IMA split on the colon","p":"<p>The colon is split between two arteries, and the split falls on the transverse colon. Its <b>proximal two-thirds</b> is midgut and gets the middle colic artery, from the superior mesenteric artery (SMA). Its <b>distal one-third</b> is hindgut and gets the left colic artery, from the inferior mesenteric artery (IMA). From there to the upper rectum, everything is IMA: the left colic and superior sigmoid arteries feed the descending colon, and the sigmoid arteries feed the sigmoid colon in the left lower quadrant. Everything before the split is SMA. The ileocolic feeds the terminal ileum, cecum, appendix and start of the ascending colon, never the sigmoid. The right colic feeds the ascending colon, the jejunal and ileal (intestinal) arteries the small bowel, and the inferior pancreaticoduodenal the pancreatic head. So cutting the IMA leaves the ascending colon fully perfused but costs part of the transverse colon. Nerves split at the same point: in the colon, the vagus serves only the SMA territory. So injury to both the SMA and the vagus impairs the ascending and transverse colon. The celiac trunk stops at the duodenum.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G4","h":"IMA branches: left colic, sigmoid, superior rectal","p":"<p>The inferior mesenteric artery (IMA) runs down and to the left behind the peritoneum. Its first branch is the <b>left colic artery</b>. It runs up and to the left toward the descending colon and splits into ascending and descending branches. Next come two or three <b>sigmoid arteries</b>, which run down and left in the sigmoid mesocolon to the sigmoid colon. The 2026 lecture names the left colic and the superior (highest) sigmoid artery as the supply of the descending colon. The IMA ends as the <b>superior rectal artery</b>, which continues straight down into the pelvis. It splits into two branches along the sides of the rectum, and these join the middle rectal arteries, branches of the internal iliac. The inferior rectal artery comes from the internal pudendal artery and supplies the anal canal below the pectinate line. The middle colic and ileocolic arteries are superior mesenteric branches. Vasa recta are the tiny straight end-vessels into the gut wall, not named branches. The pelvic splanchnics are nerves; there are no pelvic splanchnic arteries.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G4","h":"The sigmoid colon: surgery and diverticula","p":"<p>The sigmoid colon is an S-shaped loop from the left iliac fossa to the third sacral vertebra (S3), where the rectum begins. It hangs on a long sigmoid mesocolon, so it is very mobile and prone to volvulus. The root of this mesocolon is shaped like an upside-down V. <b>Deep to its apex</b>, behind the peritoneum, lie the left ureter and the point where the left common iliac artery divides into the internal and external iliac arteries. Both are at risk when the sigmoid colon is removed. The testicular artery runs on the psoas, lateral to this spot; the inferior mesenteric artery arises much higher; the superior rectal artery runs inside the mesocolon; the obturator artery lies deep in the pelvis. The sigmoid is also the usual site of <b>diverticulosis</b>. High pressure inside the colon, as in chronic constipation, pushes the lining out through weak spots in the wall as small pouches. They are painless until one becomes inflamed and infected (diverticulitis), giving left lower quadrant pain and fever. Ulcerative colitis also involves the sigmoid, but it forms no pouches.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G4","h":"The marginal artery of Drummond","p":"<p>Along the inner (mesenteric) edge of the whole colon runs one continuous vessel, the <b>marginal artery of Drummond</b>. It is the colic branches joined end to end: the ileocolic, right colic and middle colic from the superior mesenteric artery (SMA), and the left colic and sigmoid arteries from the inferior mesenteric artery (IMA). It runs in the mesentery close to the bowel and is <b>almost always present</b>; its absence is a variant. So it is the main link between the terminal branches of the SMA and IMA. Because every colic artery feeds this chain, one tied, blocked or missing colic artery rarely starves its segment; the neighbors fill it through the marginal artery. A body without a right colic artery still perfuses its ascending colon this way, and arterial patterns do vary. The arc of Riolan also links the SMA and IMA, but it is a short loop near the root of the mesentery and is often absent. The celiac trunk's link to the SMA is at the duodenum. The transverse colon is intraperitoneal, and veins carry blood away, not in.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G4","h":"Arc of Riolan and rerouted flow","p":"<p>Only colic branches build the marginal artery: ileocolic, right colic and middle colic from the superior mesenteric artery (SMA), and left colic and sigmoid from the inferior mesenteric artery (IMA). It runs from the ileocolic all the way round to the sigmoid branches. The rectal arteries are a separate system; the middle rectal comes from the internal iliac and does not feed it. A second SMA–IMA link is the <b>arc of Riolan</b> (meandering mesenteric artery), a short loop near the root of the mesentery joining the middle colic to the left colic. When present, it matters most at the <b>splenic flexure</b>, the watershed where the two territories meet. Blood flows toward the blocked side. IMA blocked: SMA blood runs from the middle colic through the marginal artery and arc of Riolan to the descending colon; the sigmoid and superior rectal arteries are cut off with the IMA. SMA blocked near its origin: the middle colic is downstream and starved, so the IMA feeds the transverse colon up through the left colic. Stomach arteries play no part, and the celiac trunk links to the SMA only at the duodenum.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G4","h":"Lymph from the small intestine","p":"<p>Lymph from the gut follows the arteries back toward the aorta. It starts in the villi. Each villus has a blind lymph vessel in its center, the <b>lacteal</b>, which takes up absorbed fat as milky lymph called chyle. From the wall, lymph runs between the layers of the mesentery to mesenteric nodes and then to the <b>superior mesenteric nodes</b> at the root of the superior mesenteric artery. The intestinal trunk then carries it to the <b>cisterna chyli</b>, a sac at the start of the thoracic duct, which ends at the junction of the left subclavian and internal jugular veins. The portal vein carries absorbed nutrients in blood, not lymph. The duodenum drains to pancreaticoduodenal and pyloric nodes, then to celiac nodes (upper part) and superior mesenteric nodes (lower part), matching its two arteries. The cecum and appendix drain to ileocolic nodes. The colon's first stop is the epicolic and paracolic nodes on and beside its wall. Inferior mesenteric nodes drain the hindgut. Internal iliac nodes drain the pelvis, para-aortic nodes the kidneys and gonads, and inguinal nodes the leg and lower anal canal.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G4","h":"Lymph from the colon","p":"<p>Lymph from the colon follows the arteries through four stations. First come the epicolic (epiploic) nodes on the bowel wall. Second, the paracolic nodes along the mesenteric border, beside the marginal artery. Third, the <b>intermediate colic nodes</b> along the named colic arteries. Fourth, the principal nodes at the origin of the artery: the superior or <b>inferior mesenteric nodes</b>. From there lymph goes on through the intestinal trunks to the cisterna chyli. Cancer spreads in the same order, so it reaches the epicolic and paracolic nodes first. Which principal group depends on the artery. The descending and sigmoid colon (left colic and sigmoid arteries) drain epicolic and paracolic → intermediate colic → inferior mesenteric nodes. The transverse colon drains to the middle colic nodes, along its middle colic artery. The cecum and ascending colon drain through ileocolic and right colic nodes to the superior mesenteric nodes. Celiac nodes drain foregut organs, hepatic nodes the liver, and axillary nodes the arm and breast; none of them drain the colon.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G4","h":"Nerves of the jejunum and ileum","p":"<p>The jejunum and ileum get their nerves from the <b>superior mesenteric plexus</b>, a net of fibers around the superior mesenteric artery that carries both sympathetic and parasympathetic fibers. The sympathetic fibers start in spinal cord segments <b>T8–T10</b> (the eighth to tenth thoracic segments, not spinal nerves). They run in the greater, lesser and least splanchnic nerves and synapse in the celiac and superior mesenteric ganglia. Pain fibers travel back along this path, so small-bowel pain is felt around the umbilicus, the T10 skin band. Sympathetic outflow stops at the second lumbar segment. The parasympathetic fibers come from the vagus nerve, through the posterior vagal trunk; the anterior trunk goes mainly to the stomach and liver. Vagal fibers are preganglionic. They pass through the celiac and superior mesenteric ganglia without synapsing and synapse instead on postganglionic neurons inside the gut wall, in the <b>myenteric and submucosal plexuses</b>. Destroy those plexuses and parasympathetic control is gone. Pelvic splanchnic nerves (parasympathetic) and lumbar splanchnic nerves (sympathetic) serve the hindgut, and the phrenic nerve supplies the diaphragm.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G4","h":"Nerves of the hindgut, and Hirschsprung disease","p":"<p>The vagus nerve stops at the end of the midgut, two-thirds of the way along the transverse colon. The hindgut, from there to the rectum, gets its parasympathetic fibers from the <b>pelvic splanchnic nerves</b>, from the second to fourth sacral segments (S2–S4), through the inferior hypogastric (pelvic) plexus. They are the only parasympathetic splanchnic nerves, and they make the sigmoid colon and rectum contract and empty. All other splanchnics (greater, lesser, least, lumbar) are sympathetic; the hindgut's come from lumbar splanchnics through the inferior mesenteric plexus, and the superior hypogastric plexus is mostly sympathetic too. Parasympathetic fibers synapse on ganglion cells in the gut wall. In <b>Hirschsprung disease</b> (congenital megacolon), neural crest cells never reach the distal colon, so its myenteric (Auerbach) plexus has no ganglion cells. These ganglion cells are the postganglionic parasympathetic neurons. That segment cannot relax, and the colon above it swells: constipation, distention, vomiting. The muscle is normal; neurons are missing, not overactive. Whole-colon summary: up to the two-thirds point, ileocolic, right colic and middle colic arteries, the vagus and the superior mesenteric ganglia; beyond it, left colic and sigmoid arteries, pelvic splanchnics and the inferior mesenteric ganglia.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G5","h":"The upper abdominal organs at a glance","p":"<p>This lecture covers four organs of the upper abdomen. The liver, the largest gland in the body, lies mainly in the right upper quadrant deep to <b>right ribs 7–11</b>, and it reaches across the midline toward the left nipple. Ribs 5 and 6 lie higher, over the lung and pleura. The liver <b>makes bile all the time</b>, not only between meals. Between meals the bile backs up into the gallbladder, a sac in a fossa on the liver's underside (visceral surface), also in the right upper quadrant. The gallbladder <b>stores and concentrates</b> the bile by absorbing water and salts, then squeezes it out when food reaches the duodenum. The spleen lies in the left upper quadrant; it filters blood and makes no bile. The pancreas lies across the upper abdomen behind the stomach and makes enzymes such as amylase and lipase. A blow that breaks the right lower ribs can tear the liver, giving constant right upper quadrant pain and yellow skin (jaundice).</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G5","h":"Folds that hold the liver","p":"<p>Peritoneal \"ligaments\" are double layers of peritoneum that join an organ to another organ or to the body wall. The liver grows from a foregut bud into the <b>ventral mesentery</b>, the sheet between the gut and the front body wall, and splits it in two. The part in front of the liver becomes the <b>falciform ligament</b>, from the liver to the anterior abdominal wall and diaphragm. The part behind becomes the lesser omentum, running to the lesser curvature of the stomach (hepatogastric ligament) and the first part of the duodenum (hepatoduodenal ligament). The falciform's free lower edge carries the <b>round ligament (ligamentum teres hepatis)</b>, the closed-off left umbilical vein. The ligamentum venosum is a different remnant: the closed ductus venosus, a fetal shunt to the inferior vena cava, lying in a fissure. Near the tip of the left lobe, the left layers of the falciform ligament and the lesser omentum meet to form the left triangular ligament. The right triangular ligament ends the coronary ligament. The greater omentum hangs from the stomach and never reaches the liver.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G5","h":"The H on the liver's underside","p":"<p>Turn the liver up, and its underside (visceral surface) shows fissures shaped like an <b>H</b>. The left limb holds two fetal remnants: the round ligament fissure in front and the ligamentum venosum fissure behind. The right limb is the gallbladder fossa in front and the groove for the inferior vena cava (IVC) behind. The crossbar is the <b>porta hepatis</b>, the liver's doorway. Through it pass the portal vein, the hepatic artery proper (right and left branches) and the hepatic ducts, plus nerves and lymph vessels. The hepatic veins never use it; they leave the back of the liver straight into the IVC. The <b>quadrate lobe</b> is the square block in front of the crossbar, between the gallbladder fossa (right) and the round ligament fissure (left). Behind it lie the porta hepatis and then the caudate lobe. The caudate lobe sits behind the crossbar, between the IVC groove and the ligamentum venosum fissure. The left lobe lies left of the whole left limb.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G5","h":"The lesser omentum and liver lymph","p":"<p>The lesser omentum has two parts. The thin <b>hepatogastric ligament</b> runs from the liver to the lesser curvature of the stomach and carries the left and right gastric vessels. Its thick right free edge is the <b>hepatoduodenal ligament</b>, running to the first part of the duodenum, in front of the omental foramen. It carries the portal triad to the porta hepatis: bile duct in front on the right, hepatic artery proper in front on the left, portal vein behind. Liver lymph follows the same path. Superficial lymph vessels run in the liver's capsule; deep ones follow the portal triad and the hepatic veins. Both drain to the <b>hepatic nodes</b>, which lie along the hepatic vessels and ducts in the lesser omentum, then to the celiac nodes and the cisterna chyli. So cancer spreading by lymph ends up in celiac nodes. Side routes: the back of the liver drains to phrenic and then posterior mediastinal nodes, and vessels along the falciform ligament reach parasternal nodes. The greater omentum plays no part.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G5","h":"Bile's path from liver to duodenum","p":"<p>Bile leaves the liver in the right and left <b>hepatic ducts</b>, which join to form the common hepatic duct. The cystic duct from the gallbladder joins next; below that junction the tube is the <b>bile duct</b> (common bile duct, CBD). So liver bile passes through the common hepatic duct just before the CBD. The bile duct runs in front of and to the right of the portal vein, passes behind the first part of the duodenum, and lies embedded in the back of the pancreatic head, not the body. In the duodenal wall it joins the main pancreatic duct, which carries enzymes (pancreatic hormones go into the blood). Together they form the <b>hepatopancreatic ampulla (of Vater)</b>, which opens in the second part of the duodenum. A cystic duct stone traps bile in the gallbladder and inflames it (cholecystitis), but liver bile still reaches the gut, so there is no jaundice. A bile duct stone blocks all bile and causes jaundice. An ampulla stone adds pancreatitis. Hepatic duct stones lie above the cystic duct and spare the gallbladder.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G5","h":"The gallbladder and its referred pain","p":"<p>The gallbladder sits in a fossa under the liver, between the right and quadrate lobes, and its neck bends into the cystic duct. Its <b>fundus</b> touches the front wall at the tip of the right 9th costal cartilage, where the linea semilunaris (the outer edge of the rectus sheath) meets the rib margin. Pressing there on an inflamed gallbladder stops a deep breath (Murphy's sign). The liver lies under ribs 7–11, the duodenum lies deeper near the midline, the transverse colon hangs lower, and kidney pain is felt in the back. Sympathetic fibers reach the gallbladder from the celiac plexus; parasympathetic fibers from the vagus make it contract. The lecture names the <b>right phrenic nerve</b> as its sensory nerve. That nerve comes from spinal segments C3–C5 (third to fifth cervical), which also supply the skin of the shoulder, so gallbladder pain is referred to the right shoulder. Textbooks add that dull visceral pain also travels back through the celiac plexus. The left phrenic nerve serves the left half of the diaphragm.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G5","h":"Calot's triangle","p":"<p>Removing the gallbladder (<b>cholecystectomy</b>) is one of the most common operations. Its key landmark is the <b>cystohepatic triangle of Calot</b>. The borders are the cystic duct, the common hepatic duct and the underside (visceral surface) of the liver. The common bile duct is not a border; it begins below the triangle. Inside run the <b>cystic artery</b>, often the right hepatic artery, and the cystic lymph node (Calot's, Lund's or Mascagni's node) near the gallbladder neck. The triangle may also hold a \"rogue\" (aberrant or accessory) right hepatic artery or unusual bile ducts, and cutting the wrong vessel is the classic mistake. The portal vein lies deeper, at the back of the hepatoduodenal ligament, outside the triangle. The triangle lies by the gallbladder neck, between the right and quadrate lobes, not near the caudate lobe. The lecture's safety rule: identify the cystic duct for certain before dissecting here, because careless dissection in the triangle is the number one cause of common bile duct injury.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G5","h":"Which artery the surgeon ties","p":"<p>The <b>cystic artery</b> feeds the gallbladder. In about three of four people it branches off the <b>right hepatic artery</b> inside Calot's triangle. Upstream, the common hepatic artery (from the celiac trunk) gives off the gastroduodenal artery, which runs behind the duodenum, and continues as the hepatic artery proper. That splits into right and left hepatic arteries before the cystic artery arises. In a cholecystectomy the surgeon clips the cystic duct and the cystic artery. The right hepatic artery itself is protected, because it feeds the right half of the liver; it is the large artery whose branch gets clamped. The left hepatic artery goes to the left liver and is never tied. Veins from the gallbladder's fundus and body pass straight into the liver. A stone stuck (impacted) in the neck or cystic duct causes intense cramping pain (biliary colic) and inflammation (cholecystitis). A diseased neck can bulge into a pouch, <b>Hartmann's pouch</b>, where stones lodge.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G5","h":"How the portal vein forms","p":"<p>The <b>portal vein</b> carries blood from the gut, spleen, pancreas and gallbladder to the liver. It forms where the <b>superior mesenteric vein (SMV)</b>, coming up from the small intestine, meets the <b>splenic vein</b>, running in from the left. They join behind the neck of the pancreas, in front of the inferior vena cava (IVC), at about the first lumbar vertebra (L1). The inferior mesenteric vein (IMV) usually empties into the splenic vein first. Small veins such as the left gastric, cystic and pancreaticoduodenal veins are only tributaries; they do not form the trunk. The portal vein climbs in the hepatoduodenal ligament as its most posterior structure, behind the bile duct (front right) and the hepatic artery proper (front left). At the porta hepatis it splits into right and left branches. It brings about 70% of the liver's blood (textbooks say 75–80%); the hepatic artery brings the rest. It can also carry gut cancer to the liver. The hepatic veins, by contrast, drain the liver into the IVC.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G5","h":"Portal versus caval veins","p":"<p>Abdominal veins belong to one of two systems. The <b>caval system</b> is the superior and inferior venae cavae and the veins that empty into them, such as the azygos, renal, gonadal, epigastric, and middle and inferior rectal veins. The <b>portal system</b> is the hepatic portal vein and the gut veins that feed it. The superior mesenteric vein (SMV) receives the jejunal, ileal, ileocolic, right and middle colic, inferior pancreaticoduodenal and <i>right</i> gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) veins. The splenic vein receives the short gastric, pancreatic and <i>left</i> gastro-omental veins, and the <b>inferior mesenteric vein (IMV)</b>. The small right gastric vein enters the portal vein directly. The hepatic veins are not portal; they drain the liver into the inferior vena cava. On a portal venogram (an X-ray with dye in the portal veins), the portal trunk runs up and to the right and splits into branches in the liver. The splenic vein runs across to the patient's left. The SMV rises from below into the junction, and the thinner IMV rises from below into the splenic vein.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G5","h":"Where portal veins meet caval veins","p":"<p>In cirrhosis, scarring makes flow through the liver hard, so pressure in the portal vein rises (<b>portal hypertension</b>). The portal vein has no valves, so blood flows backward and escapes wherever small portal veins meet small caval (systemic) veins. These are the four <b>portocaval anastomoses</b>. Lower esophagus: esophageal branches of the left gastric vein (portal; it drains to the portal vein) meet esophageal branches of the azygos vein (caval; it drains to the superior vena cava). Swollen, they become <b>esophageal varices</b>. Rectum: the superior rectal vein (portal) meets the middle and inferior rectal veins (caval), giving hemorrhoids. Navel: paraumbilical veins (portal) meet the epigastric veins of the abdominal wall (caval), giving caput medusae. Back wall: colic veins (portal) meet retroperitoneal veins (caval), with no visible sign. Varices are the deadly one: they burst, and the patient vomits blood. Each link joins small partner veins. The inferior vena cava only collects the caval partners' blood later and never touches a portal vein.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G5","h":"Esophageal varices and the left gastric vein","p":"<p>The esophagus drains two ways. Its middle part drains to the azygos vein, a systemic vein in the chest. Its lower part normally drains down through esophageal branches of the <b>left gastric vein</b> into the <b>hepatic portal vein</b>. In portal hypertension (high portal pressure, often from cirrhosis due to alcohol or hepatitis C), blood is pushed backward up the left gastric vein into small veins under the lining of the lower esophagus. They swell into varices that carry blood up toward the azygos. So the left gastric vein feeds the varices, and the azygos is only the escape route. Varices are veins, so no artery feeds them. The look-alikes drain elsewhere. The right gastric vein drains the lesser curvature near the pylorus. The left gastro-omental vein drains the greater curvature into the splenic vein, which has no esophageal branches. The superior mesenteric vein drains the small intestine. The hepatic veins and the inferior vena cava are systemic, as is the hemiazygos, a vein of the left chest wall.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G5","h":"Caput medusae at the navel","p":"<p>The <b>paraumbilical veins</b> are small portal veins that run with the round ligament from the liver to the navel. There they meet the <b>epigastric veins</b> of the abdominal wall (superficial and inferior epigastric), which are caval (systemic). When portal pressure rises, blood flows backward along the paraumbilical veins into these skin veins, which swell and radiate from the navel like snakes: <b>caput medusae</b>. It signals portal hypertension, usually from cirrhosis, not the ordinary high blood pressure that drugs like lisinopril treat. Keep the other pairs apart. Left gastric with azygos gives esophageal varices. Superior rectal (from the inferior mesenteric vein) with middle and inferior rectal gives hemorrhoids. Colic veins (from the superior mesenteric vein) with retroperitoneal veins give no visible sign. These links join veins, never arteries. Two portal veins (such as left and right gastric) or two caval veins (such as superior epigastric and musculophrenic) cannot form one. Splenic vein to left renal vein is a surgical shunt, not a natural link.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G5","h":"Portocaval shunts and portal pressure","p":"<p>A surgeon can relieve portal hypertension with a <b>portocaval shunt</b>, a new connection that lets portal blood bypass the liver. It must join <b>one portal vein to one caval vein</b>. The classic one joins the splenic vein (portal) to the left renal vein (caval), which lie close together behind the pancreas. Two portal veins, such as the superior mesenteric and splenic, or the superior pancreaticoduodenal and supraduodenal (both drain into the portal vein), bypass nothing. Neither do two caval veins, such as an ovarian or suprarenal vein with a renal vein. To sort them: gastric, gastro-omental, mesenteric, pancreaticoduodenal and supraduodenal veins are portal; renal, suprarenal and gonadal (ovarian, testicular) veins and the venae cavae are caval. The portal system has three key traits. Its pressure is normally higher than in the inferior vena cava. It has <b>no valves</b>, so blood can flow backward. It brings more blood to the liver (about 70%) than the hepatic artery. So portal hypertension is the main cause of varices, hemorrhoids and caput medusae.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G5","h":"Pancreas: parts and neighbors","p":"<p>The pancreas lies across the back wall of the abdomen behind the stomach, at the first and second lumbar vertebrae (L1–L2). It is <b>retroperitoneal</b> except for its tail. The <b>head</b> sits in the C-shaped loop of the duodenum, hugged mainly by the descending (second) part. It lies on the inferior vena cava (IVC), both renal veins and the right renal artery, and the bile duct is embedded in its back. Its uncinate process hooks left from the lower head. The <b>neck</b> lies in front of the superior mesenteric vein (SMV) and superior mesenteric artery (SMA), and the portal vein forms behind it. So these vessels lie behind the neck and in front of the uncinate process, not under the head. The body crosses the aorta at L2, with the splenic vessels running along it. The tail runs with the splenic artery and vein in the splenorenal ligament to the splenic hilum, so it is the one intraperitoneal part. The splenic artery sends branches to the body and tail, and the main duct joins the bile duct at the ampulla.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G5","h":"Tumors of the pancreatic head","p":"<p>Two tubes wrap closely around the <b>head of the pancreas</b>. The C-shaped duodenum curves around it, and its descending (second) part hugs the head most tightly. The bile duct runs <b>embedded in the back of the head</b> on its way to the duodenal wall. So a tumor in the head squeezes the bile duct (common bile duct). Bile backs up into the blood and the patient turns yellow (<b>jaundice</b>). A larger tumor can also squeeze the second part of the duodenum, so food cannot pass, causing nausea and vomiting. Structures higher up are spared: the common hepatic duct lies above the pancreas near the porta hepatis, the cystic duct is up at the gallbladder neck, and the hepatic artery proper runs above the pancreas. The accessory pancreatic duct carries only pancreatic juice, so blocking it causes no jaundice. The spleen lies by the tail, the right kidney lies behind the head, and the transverse colon only attaches along the pancreas by its mesocolon.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G5","h":"Blood supply of the pancreas","p":"<p>The pancreas draws blood from the celiac trunk (the foregut artery) and the superior mesenteric artery (SMA, the midgut artery). The neck, body and tail are fed by the <b>splenic artery</b>, a celiac branch that runs along the upper border of the pancreas and gives many pancreatic branches. That makes it the main supply of the pancreas overall. The head gets two sets of arteries that join in loops (arcades) around it. The superior pancreaticoduodenal arteries come from the <b>gastroduodenal artery</b>, a celiac branch. The inferior pancreaticoduodenal arteries come from the SMA. Because these loops link the two territories, the head still gets blood if the celiac trunk is torn or blocked. No pancreatic arteries come straight off the aorta, and the inferior mesenteric artery (IMA) supplies the hindgut, not the pancreas. The right gastric and hepatic arteries go to the stomach and liver, and the colic arteries to the colon.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G5","h":"Spleen: position and splenic artery","p":"<p>The spleen is the largest lymphatic organ and is full of blood. It lies in the left upper quadrant, behind the stomach. Behind it, the diaphragm separates it from the pleura, the lung and <b>left ribs 9–11</b>. The left kidney lies medial to it, and the left colic flexure below. Compare the other landmarks: the liver lies under right ribs 7–11, the kidneys at about ribs 11–12, and the pancreas deep near the midline. The spleen is fed by the <b>splenic artery</b>, the largest branch of the celiac trunk, which twists along the upper border of the pancreas to the splenic hilum. The superior and inferior mesenteric arteries supply the midgut and hindgut instead, and the right gastric artery supplies the lesser curvature of the stomach. The <b>tail of the pancreas</b> reaches the splenic hilum, so a tumor there can squeeze the splenic vessels and cut off the spleen's blood. Other nearby organs have their own arteries: pancreaticoduodenal for the duodenum, cystic for the gallbladder, renal for the kidney, hepatic for the liver.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G5","h":"A torn spleen","p":"<p>The spleen lies against <b>left ribs 9–11</b>, at the side and back of the left lower chest. A blow or fall that breaks these ribs can tear it. The spleen is full of blood, so a tear bleeds heavily and causes shock: fast pulse, low blood pressure, and cold, clammy skin. Blood under the diaphragm irritates phrenic nerve fibers (C3–C5, the third to fifth cervical segments), so pain is felt in the left shoulder (<b>Kehr's sign</b>). An enlarged spleen, as after mononucleosis, tears more easily. The liver lies under right ribs 7–11. The left kidney sits lower, at ribs 11–12. Left ribs 7–8 lie above the spleen, over the stomach. The descending colon lies lower in the flank. On an axial computed tomography (CT) image, the patient's left is on the viewer's right. The spleen is the solid organ at the back of the left upper quadrant, behind the air-filled stomach. The liver fills the patient's right side, the aorta sits in front of the spine, and back muscles lie behind it.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G5","h":"The spleen's gastrosplenic ligament","p":"<p>The spleen is held by two folds of dorsal mesentery, both attached at its hilum. The <b>gastrosplenic ligament</b> runs from the hilum to the greater curvature of the stomach. The stomach lies in front of the spleen, so this ligament holds the spleen from the front. It carries the <b>short gastric</b> arteries and veins, which leave the splenic vessels near the hilum for the fundus of the stomach, plus the left gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) vessels. The <b>splenorenal ligament</b> runs back from the hilum to the left kidney and carries the splenic artery and vein and the tail of the pancreas. Below, the spleen rests on the phrenicocolic ligament, and the splenocolic ligament ties its lower pole to the left colic flexure. The diaphragm lies above and behind. No ligament joins the liver and the spleen; the lesser curvature is tied to the liver. The right gastro-omental artery comes from the gastroduodenal artery, along the right greater curvature. The suspensory ligament of the duodenum (ligament of Treitz) is unrelated.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G5","h":"The splenorenal ligament","p":"<p>The <b>splenorenal ligament</b> runs from the splenic hilum to the left kidney; it is also called the lienorenal ligament, since \"lien\" means spleen. Between its layers run the <b>splenic artery and vein</b> and the <b>tail of the pancreas</b>. The tail is wrapped in peritoneum here, so it is intraperitoneal; the head, neck and body are retroperitoneal. A surgeon must open this ligament to reach a tail tumor. In a splenectomy (removal of the spleen) the splenic vessels are tied inside it; rough handling floods the abdomen with blood. The phrenicocolic ligament, the shelf under the spleen, carries no large vessels. Each nearby fold is named for the two organs it joins. Gastrosplenic: spleen to greater curvature, with the short gastric and left gastro-omental vessels. Gastrocolic: greater curvature to transverse colon, part of the greater omentum. Hepatorenal: liver to right kidney. Hepatogastric: liver to lesser curvature, with the gastric vessels. Hepatoduodenal: the portal triad. The coronary ligament ties the liver to the diaphragm, and the falciform ligament ties it to the front wall.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G6","h":"Somatic or autonomic, and the two-neuron chain","p":"<p>The <b>somatic</b> nervous system is voluntary. It runs skeletal muscle through a single motor neuron, whose cell body sits in the anterior horn of the spinal cord. So a reflex that pulls the arm away from pain is somatic. The <b>autonomic nervous system (ANS)</b> is involuntary. It runs smooth muscle, heart muscle and glands, so salivating, emptying the bladder (micturition) and speeding up the heart are autonomic. The ANS always uses two neurons in a row. The first, the <b>preganglionic</b> neuron, has its cell body in the gray matter of the brainstem or spinal cord. Its myelinated axon ends in a ganglion: a cluster of nerve cell bodies outside the brain and cord. The second, the postganglionic neuron, has its cell body in that ganglion and sends an unmyelinated axon to the organ. Both divisions, sympathetic and parasympathetic, work this way. The sympathetic ganglia are the paravertebral chain beside the spine and the prevertebral ganglia (celiac, mesenteric) in front of the aorta; both hold postganglionic cell bodies. Rule: an autonomic cell body inside the brain or cord is preganglionic, and one in a ganglion is postganglionic. Dorsal root ganglia hold sensory cell bodies.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G6","h":"Where the ganglia sit sets fiber length","p":"<p>The two divisions put their ganglia in different places, and that decides how long each fiber is. <b>Sympathetic</b> ganglia lie close to the spinal cord: the paravertebral chain beside the vertebral bodies, and the prevertebral ganglia in front of the aorta. So the sympathetic preganglionic fiber is <b>short</b> and its postganglionic fiber is long. <b>Parasympathetic</b> ganglia lie next to or inside the target: four small ganglia in the head (ciliary, pterygopalatine, otic and submandibular) and tiny ganglia in the walls of the organs. So the parasympathetic preganglionic fiber is long and its postganglionic fiber is short. Any autonomic ganglion in the head is parasympathetic. The highest sympathetic ganglion, the superior cervical ganglion, sits in the neck just below the skull. The sympathetic division has three more signatures. It leaves the cord only from thoracic 1 to lumbar 2 (thoracolumbar outflow). It reaches every blood vessel in the body. And it is the only supply to the sweat glands.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G6","h":"Where sympathetic outflow starts","p":"<p>Every sympathetic preganglionic cell body lies in the <b>lateral horn</b>, a small wedge of gray matter between the anterior (motor) and posterior (sensory) horns of the spinal cord. The lateral horn exists only from thoracic 1 to lumbar 2 (T1–L2). Twelve of those 14 segments are thoracic, so almost all sympathetic cell bodies are in the thoracic cord. The cervical, sacral and coccygeal segments have no sympathetic outflow. Sacral 2 to 4 hold parasympathetic cells instead, and so do the autonomic nuclei of the brainstem. Within T1–L2 the cells are arranged by target: head, neck and upper limb T1–T4; chest organs T1–T5; abdomen T5–T12; pelvis T11–L2; lower limb T12–L2. <b>Sweat glands</b> get only sympathetic fibers, so destroying the lateral horn of T1–T4 stops the arm from sweating. Don't pick the sensory parts. The posterior horn, the posterior roots and the dorsal root (spinal) ganglia hold or carry sensory neurons only. The dorsal rami supply the skin and muscles of the back, not the arm. The paravertebral and prevertebral ganglia hold postganglionic, not preganglionic, cells.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G6","h":"White ramus in, gray ramus out","p":"<p>A sympathetic preganglionic axon leaves the spinal cord through the <b>ventral (anterior) root</b>; the dorsal root carries only sensory fibers. Just past the root, it leaves the spinal nerve through the <b>white ramus communicans</b> and enters the chain ganglion beside the vertebra. The ramus is white because preganglionic axons are myelinated. White rami exist only from thoracic 1 to lumbar 2 (T1–L2). After the synapse, the unmyelinated postganglionic axon goes back to the spinal nerve through the <b>gray ramus communicans</b>, which exists at every spinal nerve. Dorsal and ventral rami then carry it onward. Once in the chain, a preganglionic fiber has four options. It can synapse at its own level. It can climb the chain first, which is how fibers that start at T1 reach the head. It can run down first, which is how fibers that stop at L2 reach the pelvis and leg. Or it can pass straight through without synapsing, as a splanchnic nerve, to a prevertebral ganglion in front of the aorta. Parasympathetic fibers never use the chain or the rami; they travel in cranial nerves and pelvic splanchnic nerves. Somatic motor axons simply stay in the spinal nerve.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G6","h":"Sympathetics to the head: Horner syndrome","p":"<p>The head has no sympathetic outflow of its own. Its fibers start in the lateral horn of thoracic 1 and 2 (<b>T1–T2</b>). They leave through the thoracic anterior (ventral) roots and white rami, then climb the cervical part of the sympathetic chain to synapse in the <b>superior cervical ganglion</b>. Postganglionic fibers wrap the internal and external carotid arteries as carotid plexuses and ride their branches. Their main targets are the sweat glands of the face, the dilator muscle of the pupil, and the superior tarsal muscle, a small smooth muscle that helps hold up the upper eyelid. Cut this path on one side and you get <b>Horner syndrome</b> on that side: miosis (a small pupil), partial ptosis (a mildly drooping lid) and anhidrosis (no sweating on that half of the face). Causes include a lesion of the cervical chain, a Pancoast tumor at the lung apex pressing on the chain, carotid artery injury, and damage to the upper thoracic anterior roots. Look-alikes: cervical roots carry no sympathetic fibers, posterior roots are sensory, and posterior rami go to the back. Vagus injury causes hoarseness, phrenic injury paralyzes the diaphragm, and the cardiopulmonary plexus serves the heart and lungs.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G6","h":"Splanchnic nerves and the aortic plexuses","p":"<p>A <b>splanchnic nerve</b> is any nerve that carries fibers to or from the organs of a body cavity; the name alone does not mean sympathetic. The cardiopulmonary splanchnics, to the heart, lungs and esophagus, are the only postganglionic ones: they have already synapsed in the chain. The abdominopelvic splanchnics carry <b>preganglionic sympathetic</b> fibers that pass through the chain without synapsing. The greater splanchnic (thoracic 5 to 9, T5–T9) goes to the celiac ganglion. The lesser (T10–T11) goes to the aorticorenal and superior mesenteric ganglia. The least (T12) goes to the renal plexus. The lumbar splanchnics go to the inferior mesenteric and superior hypogastric plexuses. They synapse in these prevertebral ganglia, and postganglionic fibers then wrap the aorta's branches to reach the organs. (One slide line says \"paravertebral\"; the next slide says prevertebral, which is correct.) Each plexus sits at its artery's level: celiac at T12, superior mesenteric at lumbar 1 (L1), renal at L1–L2, inferior mesenteric at L3. The <b>superior hypogastric plexus</b> lies at the aortic bifurcation, in front of L5 and the sacral promontory, and sends right and left hypogastric nerves down to the pelvic organs. The pelvic splanchnics are the exception: preganglionic parasympathetic.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G6","h":"Parasympathetic outflow is craniosacral","p":"<p>The parasympathetic division is <b>craniosacral</b>. Its preganglionic cell bodies sit in two places: the brainstem nuclei of four cranial nerves (CN III oculomotor, VII facial, IX glossopharyngeal and X vagus), and the lateral horn of <b>sacral 2 to 4 (S2–S4)</b>. A cell body inside the spinal cord is always preganglionic, so the gray matter of S2–S4 holds preganglionic parasympathetic cells. The postganglionic cells sit in the four head ganglia or in tiny ganglia in the organ walls. Everything else in the trunk belongs to the sympathetic side. The lateral horn of thoracic 1 to lumbar 2 (T1–L2) holds sympathetic preganglionic cells, and their myelinated axons travel in the white rami. The paravertebral chain and the prevertebral ganglia in front of the vertebral column and aorta (celiac, mesenteric) hold sympathetic postganglionic cells. And the response to stress, \"fight or flight\", is sympathetic; the parasympathetic side is \"rest and digest\".</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G6","h":"The vagus nerve","p":"<p>The <b>vagus nerve</b> (cranial nerve X) carries the parasympathetic supply of the neck, the chest organs and the gut as far as the left colic (splenic) flexure, the border between midgut and hindgut. Its fibers stay preganglionic the whole way. They mix with sympathetic fibers in plexuses around the organs (cardiac, pulmonary, esophageal, and those on the aorta's branches), pass through them, and synapse only in tiny ganglia inside the organ wall. So if the parasympathetic fibers to an intact intestine are cut, the cut fibers are preganglionic vagal fibers. The celiac ganglion, the sympathetic chain and the greater splanchnic nerve (thoracic 5 to 9) are sympathetic, and sacral 2 to 4 serve only the gut beyond the flexure. In the lungs, vagal fibers from the pulmonary plexus <b>constrict bronchial smooth muscle</b> and increase mucus, as in the bronchospasm of asthma; sympathetic fibers widen the bronchi. Other chest nerves don't reach the bronchi. The phrenic nerve moves the diaphragm, the intercostal nerves supply the chest wall, and the greater and lesser thoracic splanchnic nerves pass through to the abdomen.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G6","h":"Pelvic splanchnic nerves","p":"<p>The sacral part of the parasympathetic outflow leaves through the <b>pelvic splanchnic nerves</b>. They branch from the anterior rami of sacral 2 to 4 (S2–S4) and carry preganglionic parasympathetic fibers. They join the <b>inferior hypogastric plexus</b>, a mixed plexus that also gets sympathetic fibers through the hypogastric nerves, and they synapse later, in small ganglia in the organ walls. So damage to them, as in a pelvic fracture or a sacral cord injury, is a preganglionic injury. They supply the gut beyond the left colic (splenic) flexure: the descending colon, sigmoid colon and rectum. They also supply the bladder and the erectile tissue. The vagus supplies everything above the flexure, including the stomach, the jejunum and the ascending colon. Parasympathetic action contracts the bladder wall and the rectum, drives peristalsis, and relaxes the internal sphincters. Losing it gives urinary retention, constipation and weak peristalsis of the descending colon. Contracting the internal anal sphincter and narrowing gut vessels are sympathetic jobs. Watch the names: the <b>sacral splanchnics</b> come from the sacral chain and are sympathetic, like the greater, lesser, least and lumbar splanchnics. Pelvic splanchnics are never sympathetic.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G6","h":"What each division does, and its messengers","p":"<p>The sympathetic division is \"fight or flight\". It speeds and strengthens the heart, widens the bronchi and the pupils, slows peristalsis, and narrows gut vessels so blood goes to skeletal muscle. It contracts the internal anal and bladder sphincters and cuts the secretion of every gland except the sweat glands. The parasympathetic division is \"rest and digest\" and does the opposite. Through the vagus it slows the heart. It narrows the bronchi and, through cranial nerve III (oculomotor), the pupil. It speeds peristalsis, raises gastric enzyme and other gut secretions, contracts the rectum, and relaxes the internal anal sphincter for defecation. (One 2026 slide says cranial nerve III \"dilates\" the pupil; that is a slip, and the lecture's own effects table says it constricts.) The chemical messengers follow one rule. <b>Acetylcholine (ACh)</b> is released by every preganglionic ending in every autonomic ganglion, by parasympathetic postganglionic endings, and by somatic motor end plates. <b>Norepinephrine (NE)</b> is released only by sympathetic postganglionic endings onto their targets, so a drug that blocks NE release acts there. The exception: sympathetic fibers to sweat glands release ACh.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G6","h":"Visceral pain follows the sympathetic path","p":"<p>Pain fibers from the abdominal organs run back along the <b>sympathetic</b> route in reverse: organ, prevertebral plexus, splanchnic nerve, sympathetic chain, white ramus, and the dorsal root ganglion of the matching cord segments. The brain then reads the pain as coming from the skin of those segments. So visceral pain is dull, poorly localized and felt near the midline. Foregut organs (stomach, liver, gallbladder, pancreas) send pain through the celiac plexus and the <b>greater splanchnic</b> nerve (thoracic 5 to 9, T5–T9). It is felt in the epigastrium, and liver pain is felt deep under the right costal margin. Midgut organs such as the appendix send pain through the superior mesenteric plexus and the <b>lesser splanchnic</b> nerve (T10–T11), so it is felt around the umbilicus, the skin band of T10. The least splanchnic (T12) serves the kidney, and the lumbar splanchnics serve the hindgut and pelvis. In appendicitis the early pain is this vague periumbilical visceral pain. Later the inflamed appendix irritates the parietal peritoneum, which has somatic nerves, so the pain turns sharp and moves to the right lower quadrant.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G7","h":"Heart borders on the frontal chest film","p":"<p>A frontal chest film is usually taken <b>posteroanterior (PA)</b>: the beam passes from back to front. The heart is a white shadow between the black, air-filled lungs, and you see an edge only where a structure touches lung. On the right side, the straight upper edge is the superior vena cava (SVC). Below it, the <b>right atrium</b> makes almost the whole right heart border, down to the diaphragm. The azygos vein (high up) and the inferior vena cava (lowest corner) add only small bits. On the left side, from top to bottom: the aortic knob, a bulge from the pulmonary trunk and left pulmonary artery, the left atrial appendage (flat unless enlarged), and the <b>left ventricle</b>, the largest curve, ending at the apex. The right ventricle faces forward, so it makes no side border, and the esophagus is hidden behind the heart. Computed tomography (CT) shows the same layout in slices. Axial slices are viewed from the feet and coronal slices from the front, so the patient's right is on the image's left. The right atrium is the chamber farthest to the image's left. The left ventricle is the long, thick-walled chamber running to the apex.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G7","h":"The lateral film: right ventricle in front","p":"<p>On a lateral chest film you see the heart from the side, so you see its front and back edges. The front edge, right behind the sternum, is the <b>right ventricle</b>. It is the most anterior chamber, because the heart is turned so that its right side faces forward. The back edge is the left atrium above and the left ventricle below. On the frontal film, by contrast, the right atrium makes the right border and the left ventricle the left border. Axial computed tomography (CT) shows the same arrangement. The right ventricle is the chamber directly behind the sternum, in front of and to the left of the aortic root. The right atrium is on the patient's right, which is the image's left. The left ventricle is the thick-walled chamber toward the patient's left and the apex. The left atrium is the back chamber, just in front of the esophagus. Inside the right ventricle runs the <b>moderator band</b> (septomarginal trabecula): a muscle bar from the interventricular septum to the anterior papillary muscle. It carries part of the right bundle branch of the conduction system.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G7","h":"Top of the chest on CT","p":"<p>Computed tomography (CT) shows the body in thin slices. Air is black, fat is dark gray, muscle and organs are mid-gray, and bone and contrast-filled vessels are white. Start an upper chest slice with its bony frame. The <b>sternum</b> is the flat bone in the midline at the very front; at this level it is the manubrium, which the clavicles join on each side. The vertebra is the midline bone at the back, the ribs curve around the sides, and the scapula lies behind the ribs. Next find the <b>trachea</b>, the round black tube in the midline, with the esophagus behind it against the vertebra. Then the vessels, where the veins lie in front of the arteries. The left brachiocephalic vein crosses in front, behind the manubrium, to meet the right one. Behind it sit the three branches of the aortic arch: the brachiocephalic trunk front-right of the trachea, the left common carotid artery to the left of the trachea, and the left subclavian artery furthest left and back. The aortic arch itself appears a little lower, as a curved tube left of the trachea. In the chest wall, pectoralis major lies over pectoralis minor.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G7","h":"At the carina: bronchi and esophagus","p":"<p>The trachea splits at the <b>carina</b> into two main bronchi that are not alike. The <b>right main bronchus</b> is wider, shorter and more vertical, almost in line with the trachea. The left main bronchus is longer, narrower and more horizontal, because the aortic arch and the heart push it sideways. So an inhaled object small enough to pass the larynx usually drops into the right main bronchus. A swallowed object goes to the esophagus instead and causes trouble swallowing, not choking. The right main bronchus is short: it ends where the right upper lobe bronchus leaves it. The tube that continues downward is the <b>bronchus intermedius</b>, which feeds the middle and lower lobes. On axial computed tomography (CT) it is a round black tube on the patient's right (the image's left), just behind the right pulmonary artery, below the upper lobe bronchus. Directly behind the carina, and directly in front of the vertebral body, lies the esophagus. The azygos vein lies on the spine to its right, the tiny thoracic duct lies behind it between the aorta and the azygos, and the descending aorta sits left of the spine.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G7","h":"Great vessels above the heart","p":"<p>Just above the heart, contrast makes the great vessels bright. The <b>ascending aorta</b> is a large round vessel in the middle of the front of the mediastinum. The pulmonary trunk lies in front of it and to its left. The trunk splits into a left pulmonary artery, which curves backward on the left, and a right pulmonary artery, which runs horizontally behind the ascending aorta. The superior vena cava (SVC) is the round vessel just to the right of the ascending aorta and slightly behind it. The <b>descending aorta</b> is the round vessel at the back, just left of and in front of the vertebral body. The black trachea has already split into bronchi at this level. Higher up, the arch joins the two aortas. Its classic narrowing, <b>coarctation of the aorta</b>, lies just past the origin of the left subclavian artery. The arms get blood from branches before the narrowing, so arm pulses and blood pressure are high. The legs get blood only past it, so femoral pulses are weak or absent and the legs are cold. A sagittal (side-view) magnetic resonance image shows the narrowed segment. Tetralogy of Fallot causes cyanosis instead, and an atrial septal defect leaves the pulses normal.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G7","h":"The four chambers on axial CT","p":"<p>On axial computed tomography (CT), name each heart chamber by its position. The <b>right ventricle</b> is the most anterior chamber, right behind the sternum. The right atrium wraps the patient's right side, on the image's left. The left ventricle has the thickest wall and runs toward the apex on the patient's left. The <b>left atrium</b> is the most posterior chamber. It lies directly in front of the esophagus at about the seventh thoracic vertebra (T7), with the descending aorta behind it, just left of the spine. So a sharp object driven straight forward through the esophagus at T7 enters the left atrium, and an enlarged left atrium pushes the esophagus backward. Every chamber lies behind the sternum, so that clue does not single one out; lying directly in front of the esophagus does. In a four-chamber view, the right atrium is on the image's left, the right ventricle at the top near the chest wall, the left ventricle on the image's right, and the left atrium at the bottom. A little higher, the <b>aortic root</b> sits in the middle of the heart, and the root of the pulmonary trunk lies in front of it and to its left. The coronary sinus is a vein in the back groove, not a chamber.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G7","h":"Angiograms of the heart and coronaries","p":"<p>In an angiogram, contrast is injected through a catheter while X-ray images are taken, so the filled chamber or vessel stands out. In a left ventriculogram, the large chamber at the lower left of the heart shadow, at the apex, with the ascending aorta rising from it, is the left ventricle. The right ventricle leads to the pulmonary trunk, not the aorta, and the left atrium sits high and at the back. In a left coronary angiogram, the catheter comes up the aorta and its tip sits in the first, short segment: the <b>left main coronary artery</b>. It soon splits in two. The <b>left anterior descending (LAD)</b> artery runs straight down the anterior interventricular groove, between the two ventricles, toward the apex, giving diagonal branches. The circumflex artery curves around the left border of the heart, giving marginal branches. The LAD feeds most of the front of the left ventricle and the front of the septum. Blocking it causes a large anterior heart attack, which is why it is called the <b>\"widow maker\"</b>. The right coronary artery is injected separately and usually gives the posterior descending artery.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G7","h":"Abdominal plain film: the colon frame","p":"<p>Read an abdominal plain film in order: normal anatomy, the bowel gas pattern, any air outside the bowel, abnormal densities such as stones, then the lungs and bones. Gas is black. The gastric bubble sits in the left upper quadrant (LUQ) under the left hemidiaphragm. Small bowel gas lies in the middle of the abdomen, in loops without stool. The colon forms a frame around the edge, and it is known by its <b>mottled stool</b>, a bubbly mix of gas and feces. Follow the frame. The cecum sits in the right lower quadrant. The <b>ascending colon</b> runs up the right flank, lateral to the spine. The hepatic (right colic) flexure turns under the liver, and the transverse colon crosses the top. The <b>splenic (left colic) flexure</b> is the sharp turn high in the LUQ, beside the spleen; it sits higher than the hepatic flexure. The descending colon runs down the left flank, and the sigmoid colon and rectum lie in the pelvis. The colon wall shows haustra, pouch folds that cross only part of its width. The liver is a large solid shadow in the right upper quadrant. The pancreas and duodenum lie near the midline and are not seen.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G7","h":"Barium swallow: esophagus and stomach","p":"<p>Fluoroscopy is a live X-ray movie. In a barium esophagram or upper gastrointestinal (UGI) series, the patient swallows barium, which shows white and outlines only the inside of the tube it passes through. Solid organs, such as the pancreas, never fill. The <b>thoracic esophagus</b> is the barium-filled tube running down behind the trachea and the heart to the gastroesophageal (GE) junction, just below the diaphragm. The stomach then has five parts. The cardia surrounds the GE junction. The <b>fundus</b> is the dome above the level of the junction, tucked under the left hemidiaphragm and lateral to where the esophagus enters. Below it come the body and the antrum. The <b>pylorus</b> is the narrow outlet, much lower and on the patient's right. Past it, the duodenum runs to the right of the spine and curves around the head of the pancreas. A swallow study does not fill the colon, so the transverse colon and the left colic flexure are not seen.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G7","h":"Duodenal loop, jejunum and ileum","p":"<p>The duodenum has four parts and forms a <b>C-shaped loop</b> around the head of the pancreas, at about the first to third lumbar vertebrae (L1–L3). The first (superior) part continues from the pylorus. The second (descending) part runs down beside the pancreatic head, and the common bile duct opens into it. The third (horizontal) part crosses the midline between the superior mesenteric artery in front and the aorta behind. The fourth (ascending) part rises to join the jejunum. The small bowel then fills the middle of the abdomen. Its inner circular folds, the valvulae conniventes (plicae circulares), cross the full width of the lumen. The <b>jejunum</b> lies mainly in the left upper abdomen and has tall, closely packed folds that look feathery. The <b>ileum</b> lies mainly in the right lower quadrant (RLQ) and the pelvis, and its folds are fewer, lower and farther apart. Folds that cross only part of the width are colon haustra, and the stomach has rugae. The stomach lies in front of the pancreas, and the gallbladder is a fluid sac under the liver.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G7","h":"Abdominal wall muscles on CT","p":"<p>An axial computed tomography (CT) slice shows four muscles in the front and side abdominal wall. In front, on each side of the midline (the linea alba), lies the <b>rectus abdominis</b>, a paired vertical strap wrapped in the rectus sheath. It runs from the pubis to the lower ribs, so its main job is to flex the trunk, as in a sit-up; it also tightens hard with a cough. Lateral to it, the side wall has three flat layers. The <b>external oblique</b> is outermost. It arises from the outer surfaces of ribs 5 to 12, and its fibers run inferomedially, like hands in your front pockets. The internal oblique is the middle layer. It arises from the thoracolumbar fascia, iliac crest and inguinal ligament, and its fibers run superomedially. The two obliques mainly rotate and side-bend the trunk. The <b>transversus abdominis</b> is innermost, right next to the abdominal cavity. Its fibers run horizontally around the abdomen like a belt, and it compresses the abdomen; it does not flex the trunk. Further back, the psoas major lies beside the lumbar vertebrae, the quadratus lumborum lateral to it, and the iliacus in the iliac fossa. The small pyramidalis sits just above the pubis.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G7","h":"Right from left: vena cava, aorta, kidneys","p":"<p>Axial computed tomography (CT) and magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) slices are viewed from the patient's feet, and coronal slices from the front. Either way, the <b>patient's right is on the image's left</b>. Use the two big vessels in front of the spine to orient yourself. The <b>inferior vena cava (IVC)</b> runs on the right side of the spine and is usually the wider, flatter one. High up it sits in a groove on the back of the liver, then passes through the diaphragm into the right atrium. The <b>aorta</b> runs just left of the midline, directly on the spine, and is round. On a coronal image both run vertically, with the IVC on the image's left. Smaller vessels lie in front: the portal vein enters the liver, the superior mesenteric artery is a small vessel in front of the aorta, and the splenic artery is a small wavy vessel along the top of the pancreas. The kidneys are bean-shaped organs beside the spine at the back. The right kidney lies below the liver, lateral to the IVC. The left kidney lies on the image's right, lateral to the aorta. The large round muscles hugging the lumbar vertebral bodies are the psoas major muscles.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G7","h":"Reading an upper-abdomen CT slice","p":"<p>For any abdominal computed tomography (CT) slice, first work out the level, whether contrast was given, and why that slice is being shown. The stomach is the hollow organ in the front of the upper abdomen; its body lies on the left and its pyloric antrum crosses the midline. Its best clue is its <b>air bubble</b>, because it is the only organ there that normally holds black air. Being gray or being on the left proves little: most soft tissues are gray, and several organs lie on the left. The liver is the huge organ on the right. The falciform ligament is a fat-filled cleft at its front, between the large right lobe and the left lobe. The <b>caudate lobe</b> sits between the portal vein in front and the inferior vena cava (IVC) behind. The right adrenal gland is a thin line behind the IVC. The aorta lies on the spine, hugged by the right and left crura of the diaphragm. The pancreatic tail points to the <b>spleen</b>, the solid organ at the far left and back, behind the stomach, under ribs 9 to 11. The left kidney lies lower and nearer the spine, and the black lung base lies above the diaphragm.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G7","h":"Gallbladder, pancreas and bile ducts","p":"<p>The <b>gallbladder</b> is a pear-shaped sac of bile in the gallbladder fossa on the underside of the liver. It has three parts: the rounded fundus at the tip, the body (the main sac), and the neck, which narrows into the cystic duct that links it to the biliary tree. The <b>pancreas</b> is a lobulated gland outlined by fat. Its head sits in the duodenal C-loop. Its body crosses the spine behind the stomach, and the splenic vein runs along its back. So the organ between the stomach in front and the splenic vein behind is the pancreatic body. Its tail reaches the splenic hilum. Nearby, the duodenal bulb lies right of midline past the pylorus, and the left lobe of the liver lies in front of the stomach. <b>Magnetic resonance cholangiopancreatography (MRCP)</b> is a T2-weighted scan, in which fluid is bright, so the ducts light up. It shows the cystic duct joining high up, the common bile duct (CBD) running down, and the pancreatic duct coming across from the patient's left. They meet at the bottom of the CBD, in the duodenal wall at the major papilla, where the sphincter of Oddi sits. The minor papilla lies higher and drains the accessory pancreatic duct.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G7","h":"The abdominal aorta, level by level","p":"<p>The aorta enters the abdomen at the twelfth thoracic vertebra (T12) and runs down in front of the spine, a little left of midline. Single branches leave the front: the celiac trunk at T12, the superior mesenteric artery (SMA) at the first lumbar vertebra (L1), and the inferior mesenteric artery (IMA) at the third (L3). Paired branches leave the sides. The <b>renal arteries</b> are large and run sideways to each kidney at L1–L2. The gonadal arteries are thin and run down from the front at about L2, and the small inferior phrenic arteries run up to the diaphragm. Four pairs of small <b>lumbar arteries</b> leave the back at L1–L4 and supply the posterior body wall. The aorta ends at the fourth lumbar vertebra (<b>L4</b>), near the level of the umbilicus and the iliac crests, by splitting into the right and left common iliac arteries. The thin median sacral artery continues down the midline. Each common iliac then splits, in front of the sacroiliac joint, into an internal iliac (medial, into the pelvis) and an external iliac (lateral, toward the leg). On an aortogram, the iliacs form an upside-down Y at the bottom.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G7","h":"Celiac trunk and superior mesenteric artery","p":"<p>A sagittal (side) view, on computed tomography (CT) or an aortogram, is the best way to tell apart the two upper front branches of the aorta. The aorta runs down in front of the spine. Its first front branch, just below the diaphragm at the twelfth thoracic vertebra (T12), is the <b>celiac trunk</b>. It is short and points straight forward, then splits into the left gastric, splenic and common hepatic arteries, which supply the foregut, liver and spleen. About a centimeter lower, at the first lumbar vertebra (L1), comes the <b>superior mesenteric artery (SMA)</b>. It runs downward in front of the aorta to supply the midgut. The inferior mesenteric artery leaves much lower, at the third lumbar vertebra. The renal arteries leave from the sides. The spleen gets its blood from the <b>splenic artery</b>, the largest celiac branch. This matters in infectious mononucleosis, when the spleen swells and can rupture with a blow, so contact sports are avoided for weeks. The common hepatic artery is a branch of the celiac trunk, not of the aorta.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G7","h":"Celiac branches on an angiogram","p":"<p>In a celiac angiogram, the catheter sits in the short celiac trunk, which splits into three branches. The <b>left gastric artery</b> is the smallest. It runs up toward the esophagus, then along the lesser curvature. The <b>splenic artery</b> is the largest. It is long and wavy (tortuous) and runs to the patient's left (the image's right) along the top of the pancreas to the spleen. On the way it gives small pancreatic branches, short gastric arteries to the fundus, and the left gastro-omental artery. The <b>common hepatic artery</b> runs to the patient's right (the image's left), toward the liver. It gives off the gastroduodenal artery (GDA), which dives straight down behind the first part of the duodenum and supplies it. At its lower end the GDA turns left along the greater curvature as the right gastro-omental (gastroepiploic) artery. The common hepatic then continues as the proper hepatic artery. This gives the right gastric artery to the lesser curvature, then splits into the right hepatic artery, to the right lobe, and the left hepatic artery, to the left lobe. A middle hepatic artery supplies segment 4. So blocking the right hepatic artery starves the right lobe of the liver.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G7","h":"Branches of the superior mesenteric artery","p":"<p>A superior mesenteric artery (SMA) arteriogram shows the midgut's blood supply. The SMA trunk runs down and slightly to the right, with branches from both sides. From its left side come many jejunal and ileal branches, which form loops (arcades) in the mesentery before reaching the bowel. The first jejunal branch leaves high up on the left. The right side gives the branches to the colon. The first, small branch near the origin is the inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery. The <b>middle colic artery</b> leaves high up and runs to the transverse colon. The right colic artery runs to the ascending colon. The <b>ileocolic artery</b> is the lowest branch on the right. It runs toward the cecum in the right lower quadrant and gives the <b>appendicular artery</b>, the vessel tied off in an appendectomy. So bleeding from the appendix stump after surgery is stopped by tying the ileocolic artery. The inferior mesenteric artery is a separate branch of the aorta and does not fill when contrast is injected into the SMA.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G7","h":"Inferior mesenteric artery and the marginal artery","p":"<p>The inferior mesenteric artery (IMA) leaves the front of the aorta at the third lumbar vertebra (L3) and supplies the hindgut. On an IMA angiogram its trunk runs down and to the patient's left. Its first branch, the <b>left colic artery</b>, runs up and to the left (the image's right) to the descending colon. Next come several sigmoid arteries to the sigmoid colon. The trunk ends as the superior rectal artery, running down to the rectum. The colic arteries do not reach the bowel directly. They feed a continuous channel that runs close along the mesenteric edge of the whole colon: the <b>marginal artery of Drummond</b>. It links the superior mesenteric artery (SMA) and IMA territories, so on an angiogram it is the thin vessel hugging the edge of the colon. The <b>arc of Riolan</b> is a different link: a more central loop, near the root of the mesentery, that joins the middle colic and left colic arteries. The arc of Buehler links the celiac trunk and SMA. Veins do not fill on an arterial injection.</p>"},{"l":"ANAT-G7","h":"Portal vein and renal veins","p":"<p>The gut's veins drain to the liver, not straight to the heart. The <b>superior mesenteric vein (SMV)</b> rises vertically from below, just right of the superior mesenteric artery. The splenic vein runs horizontally from the patient's left, along the back of the pancreas. They join behind the neck of the pancreas to form the <b>portal vein</b>, a large trunk that runs up to the porta hepatis and splits into right and left branches inside the liver. The inferior mesenteric vein is smaller and usually drains into the splenic vein; the right gastric vein drains straight into the portal vein. Blood leaves the liver another way: the hepatic veins leave its top and empty into the inferior vena cava (IVC), which runs behind the liver and does not branch inside it. When liver disease blocks portal flow (portal hypertension), blood detours through veins around the umbilicus, which swell. The kidneys drain straight into the IVC. The <b>right renal vein</b> is short and runs directly from the kidney's hilum into the IVC; the right renal artery comes from the aorta and passes behind the IVC. The left renal vein is long and crosses in front of the aorta.</p>"}]};